{"@context":"https://www.openphar.org/ontology/context/pharmacopoeia.jsonld","@graph":[{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/abri-herba-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Abri Herba v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Official Name: Abri Herba\n\nChinese Name: 雞骨草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jigucao"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Abri Herba\n\nChinese Name: 雞骨草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jigucao"},{"name":"13","text":"Abri Herba is the dried whole plant of Abrus cantoniensis Hance (Fabaceae). The whole plant is collected all year round, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Abri Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Root externally greyish-brown to purplish-brown, rough, with fine longitudinal wrinkles, tapering to the end, usually branched and varying in size. Stem caespitose, extremely slender, greyish-brown to purplish-brown, with sparse and short pubescences. Leaves pinnately compound, alternate; leaflets almost fallen off, petiole short; lamina subrectangular, 0.5-1.5 cm long and 0.3-0.6 cm wide, apex truncate and mucronulate, margins entire. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Cork consists of several layers of cells, cells subsquare or rectangular, reddish-brown. Cortex narrow, stone cells and prisms of calcium oxalate arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem relatively thin. Cambium in a ring. Xylem vessels scattered, arranged radially; ray distinct, 2 to several rows of cells wide [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nStem: Cork consists of several layers of brown cells, cells subsquare or rectangular, arranged in order. Cortex relatively thin, prisms of calcium oxalate and stone cells visible. Phloem fibres numerous, arranged in a ring. Phloem narrow. Xylem relatively broad, vessels radially arranged; ray distinct. Pith broad, parenchymatous cells subrounded, mostly broken, hollow in centre [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 4, 1974, 262])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells subsquare or rectangular, prisms of calcium oxalate occasionally found under the epidermis. Palisade tissue consists of 2 layers of palisade cells. Spongy tissue cells subrounded, arranged loosely. Vascular bundles collateral, xylem vessels arranged radially. Numerous fibres visible around the vascular bundles. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, irregular in shape [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green. Cork cells brown, rectangular, elliptical or irregular. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, 6-43 $ \\mu $m in diameter; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, apex gradually acuminate, with fine warty protuberance on the surface. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 10-53 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Stone cells elliptical or subsquare, 11-74 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls slightly thick. Epidermal cells and stomata of leaf occasionally visible, cell walls slightly curved, stomata diacytic. Fibres single or in bundles, 8-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls relative thick, fibre bundles sometimes surrounded by cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 521, 1703, 2053])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Abri Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Stone cells 5. Phloem\n\n6. Cambium 7. Ray 8. Xylem\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nAbri Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 412, 1733, 2098])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Abri Herba A. Sketch B. Sections illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate and stone cells D. Prisms of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem fibres 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Ray 7. Pith\n\n8. Prisms of calcium oxalate 9. Stone cells\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[238, 417, 1730, 2126])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Abri Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Fibres 4. Palisade tissue\n\n5. Spongy tissue 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Lower epidermis\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\n沙苑子 Astragali Complanati Semen\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n彯金 Curcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nAbri Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[257, 675, 1708, 2134])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Abri Herba\n\n1. Cork cells 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Non-glandular hair\n\n4. Bordered-pitted vessel 5. Stone cell 6. Epidermal cells of leaf 7. Fibre bundle 8. Crystal Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 15, 1902, 247])"},{"name":"13","text":"Abrine (N-Methyl-L-tryptophan) standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of abrine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, n-butanol and glacial acetic acid (5:4:1, v/v).\n\nWeigh 2 g of ninhydrin and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol and then filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately abrine standard solution and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 5 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 4 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[551, 97, 717, 155])\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1073, 161, 1127, 211])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1427, 101, 1593, 162])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 366, 684, 653])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of abrine (N-methyl-L-tryptophan)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[381, 831, 859, 1647])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Abri Herba extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Abrine standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of abrine (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 9, 1916, 250])"},{"name":"13","text":"Abrine (N-Methyl-L-tryptophan) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (13 mg/L) Weigh 0.13 mg of abrine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (30%)"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (30%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>95→87</td><td>5→13</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>87→81</td><td>13→19</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of abrine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of abrine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of abrine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from abrine peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject abrine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of abrine peak in the chromatogram of abrine Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify abrine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of abrine Std-FP. The retention\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 8, 1962, 262])\n\ntimes of abrine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Abri Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Abri Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,abrine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[390, 1145, 1643, 1736])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Abri Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[764, 119, 870, 172])\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B).\n\nAbrine (N-Methyl-L-tryptophan) standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of abrine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (30%).\n\nAbrine standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the abrine Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (30%) to produce a series of solutions of 2.5, 7.5, 15, 30, 60 mg/L for abrine."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh accurately 0.8 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (30%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with ethanol (30%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol (30%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (278 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[42, 8, 1966, 262])\n\nthe separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 3) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 3 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>95→87</td><td>5→13</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-25</td><td>87→85.5</td><td>13→14.5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/abri-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Abri Herba","la":"Abri Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/acanthopanacis-cortex-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acanthopanacis Cortex v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[615, 978, 988, 1095])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1062, 965, 1327, 1097])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[561, 1225, 1436, 1846])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Acanthopanacis Cortex A. Acanthopanacis Cortex B. Magnified outer surface of root bark C. Magnified inner surface of root bark"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[615, 978, 988, 1095])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1062, 965, 1327, 1097])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[561, 1225, 1436, 1846])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Acanthopanacis Cortex A. Acanthopanacis Cortex B. Magnified outer surface of root bark C. Magnified inner surface of root bark"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Acanthopanacis Cortex\n\nChinese Name: 五加皮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Wujiapi"},{"name":"11","text":"Acanthopanacis Cortex is the dried root bark of Acanthopanax gracilistylus W. W. Smith (Araliaceae). The root is collected in summer and autumn, washed clean, stripped off the root bark, then baked or dried under the sun to obtain Acanthopanacis Cortex."},{"name":"11","text":"Irregularly quilled, 3.5-20.5 cm long, 3-16 mm in diameter, 1-3 mm thick. Externally greyish-brown, with slightly twisted longitudinal wrinkles and transverse lenticel-like scars; inner surface pale yellow or greyish yellow, with fine longitudinal striations. Texture fragile and light in weight, easily broken. Fracture irregular, greyish-white. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly pungent and bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of suberized cells. Cortex narrow, scattered with a few secretory canals. Phloem broad; phloem rays 1-5 rows of cells wide; secretory canals scattered in phloem, abundant, surrounded by 4-15 secretory cells. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, located in the parenchymatous cells. Phloem fibres occasionally found at old root bark, scattered singly or 2-4 in bundles. Secretory canals subrounded; pale yellow oil droplets observed before stained (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[60, 8, 1960, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-white. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered or present in parenchymatous cells, 10-72 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, angles large and blunt, some short and sharp; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Secretory cells and canal fragments contain pale yellow secretions and colourless oil droplets. Cork cells pale yellow or pale yellowish-brown, subpolygonal or subsquare in the surface view, walls thick. Phloem fibres present at old root bark, few, scattered singly or 2-4 in bundles, linear, straight, margin slightly wavy, endings blunt, short pointed or truncate, lignified, pits sparse (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus 水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[282, 489, 1740, 2053])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Acanthopanacis Cortex A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate D. Phloem fibre E. Secretory canal 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Clusters of calcium oxalate 5. Phloem fibre\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[253, 755, 1707, 2109])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Acanthopanacis Cortex\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Secretory canal 3. Cork cells 4. Phloem fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[22, 11, 1922, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Syringin (eleutheroside B) standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of syringin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (10:2:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately syringin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[552, 98, 717, 155])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 367, 923, 633])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of syringin (eleutheroside B)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 799, 875, 1648])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Acanthopanacis Cortex extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Syringin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of syringin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Syringin (eleutheroside B) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of syringin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-13</td><td>11</td><td>89</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>13-18</td><td>11→17</td><td>89→83</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>18-60</td><td>17</td><td>83</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of syringin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of syringin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of syringin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from syringin peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject syringin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of syringin peak in the chromatogram of syringin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify syringin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of syringin Std-FP. The retention times of syringin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 7, 1959, 263])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Acanthopanacis Cortex extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Acanthopanacis Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, syringin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2 (chlorogenic acid)</td><td>1.26</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.01</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>4.44</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>4.61</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[360, 1096, 1745, 1720])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Acanthopanacis Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[767, 119, 870, 173])\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 12.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 18.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Syringin (eleutheroside B) standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of syringin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nSyringin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the syringin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 1,4,8,40,80 mg/L for syringin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/acanthopanacis-cortex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acanthopanacis Cortex","la":"Acanthopanacis Cortex"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/acanthopanacis-senticosi-radix-et-rhizoma-seu-caulis-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[725, 902, 943, 1059])\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1055, 902, 1216, 1056])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[406, 1128, 1448, 1882])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis*\n\nA. Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis\n\nB. Magnified transverse section of root C. Magnified transverse section of rhizome\n\n* Remarks: Magnified transverse section of the stem of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis is not shown, as this plant part is found only occasionally in the market samples."},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[725, 902, 943, 1059])\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1055, 902, 1216, 1056])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[406, 1128, 1448, 1882])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis*\n\nA. Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis\n\nB. Magnified transverse section of root C. Magnified transverse section of rhizome\n\n* Remarks: Magnified transverse section of the stem of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis is not shown, as this plant part is found only occasionally in the market samples."},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 刺五加\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Ciwujia"},{"name":"14","text":"Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis is the dried root and rhizome or stem of Acanthopanax senticosus (Rupr. et Maxim.) Harms (Araliaceae). The root, rhizome and stem are collected in spring or autumn, washed clean, dried under the sun or dried by baking to obtain Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis."},{"name":"14","text":"Root cylindrical, sometimes twisted, 2-57 mm in diameter, externally greyish-brown to brown, with longitudinal wrinkles; fracture yellowish-white. Rhizomes knotty, irregularly cylindrical or cylindrical, 4-60 mm in diameter, with pith in the centre. Texture hard, fibrous. Odour distinctively aromatic; taste slightly bitter and astringent. Stem occasionally found, cylindrical, externally yellowish-brown to brown. Old stem inermous; young stem with small prickles. Odour slight; taste slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Root: Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex narrow, scattered with numerous clusters of calcium oxalate. Phloem with fibre bundles scattered at the outer side, some cells contain clusters of calcium oxalate; phloem ray distinct. Cambium in a ring. Xylem occupies the majority of root, consisting of vessels and fibres; xylem rays consist of 1-3 rows of cells. Secretory canals scattered in cortex and phloem, mainly in cortex [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[53, 7, 1956, 261])\n\nRhizome: Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex narrow, scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate. Phloem with fibre bundles scattered at the outer side, some cells contain clusters of calcium oxalate; phloem ray distinct. Cambium in a ring. Xylem consists of vessels and fibres; xylem rays consist of 1-3 rows of cells. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells. Secretory canals scattered in cortex and phloem, mainly in cortex [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-white. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered or present in parenchymatous cells, some arranged in a row, 10-74 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Phloem fibres usually scattered singly, 9-47 $ \\mu $m in diameter, pit canals distinct; bright yellowishwhite under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres scattered or in bundles, 8-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter, lumens with septum, some with sparse oblique pits; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Secretory canals mostly broken, filling with yellow or brownish-yellow secretions. Cork cells yellowish-brown, subpolygonal or subrectangular in surface view. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, often broken, 18-109 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 17, 1902, 252])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[246, 451, 1734, 2106])"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory canal\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cluster of calcium oxalate 4. Secretory canal\n\n5. Fibre bundles 6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Phloem ray 10. Xylem ray\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1426, 101, 1599, 165])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1070, 158, 1234, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[256, 637, 1696, 2098])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizoma of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory canal\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cluster of calcium oxalate 4. Secretory canal\n\n5. Fibre bundles 6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n10. Phloem ray 11. Xylem ray\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[22, 16, 1901, 252])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[282, 615, 1710, 2113])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Phloem fibre 3. Xylem fibres\n\n4. Secretory canal 5. Cork cells 6. Vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[553, 97, 714, 155])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1426, 102, 1600, 164])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1073, 158, 1232, 210])"},{"name":"14","text":"Isofraxidin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isofraxidin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nLiriodendrin (eleutheroside E) standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of liriodendrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 50% ).\n\nSyringin (eleutheroside B) standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of syringin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (10:2:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 10 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately isofraxidin standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), liriodendrin standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), syringin standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value of isofraxidin by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A). Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values of liriodendrin and syringin by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[437, 370, 841, 613])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[424, 651, 1139, 1496])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[437, 1532, 1133, 1855])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) isofraxidin (ii) liriodendrin (eleutheroside E) and (iii) syringin (eleutheroside B)\n\n</div>\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1070, 158, 1235, 211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[454, 373, 1025, 1028])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[454, 1102, 1019, 1743])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis extract observed (i) under UV light (366 nm) and (ii) under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Isofraxidin standard solution 2. Syringin standard solution\n\n3. Liriodendrin standard solution 4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of isofraxidin, liriodendrin and syringin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Liriodendrin (eleutheroside E) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of liriodendrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nSyringin (eleutheroside B) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of syringin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (50%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>89</td><td>11</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-35</td><td>89→82</td><td>11→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-50</td><td>82</td><td>18</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>82→70</td><td>18→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of liriodendrin Std-FP and syringin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of liriodendrin and syringin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of liriodendrin and syringin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from liriodendrin and syringin peaks should not be less than 100000 and 10000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[552, 99, 717, 155])"},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject liriodendrin Std-FP, syringin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of liriodendrin and syringin peaks in the chromatograms of liriodendrin Std-FP, syringin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify liriodendrin and syringin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of liriodendrin Std-FP and syringin Std-FP. The retention times of liriodendrin and syringin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(syringin)</td><td>0.35</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.39</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, liriodendrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>1.49</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[380, 1662, 1903, 2279])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[50, 16, 1891, 252])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/acanthopanacis-senticosi-radix-et-rhizoma-seu-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis","la":"Acanthopanacis Senticosi Radix et Rhizoma seu Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aconiti-kusnezoffii-radix-unprocessed-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix unprocessed v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1043, 889, 1227, 1069])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[431, 1182, 1551, 1825])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) A. Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of transverse section of root tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 24, 1873, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1043, 889, 1227, 1069])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[431, 1182, 1551, 1825])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) A. Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of transverse section of root tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 24, 1873, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed)\n\nChinese Name: 草鳥（生）\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Caowu (Sheng)"},{"name":"13","text":"Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) is the unprocessed dried root tuber of Aconitum kusnezoffii Reichb. (Ranunculaceae). The root tuber is collected in autumn when the stem and leaves withered, rootlets and soil removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed)."},{"name":"13","text":"Irregularly long-conical or conical, slightly curved, 2-9 cm long, 7-25 mm in diameter. Apex usually with remnants of stem and a few remnant bases of adventitious roots, sometimes apex bearing a withered bud on one side and a circular or oblate remnant base of adventitious root on the other side. Externally blackish-brown, shrunken, with longitudinal wrinkles, dotted rootlet scars and several tubercular lateral roots. Texture hard. Fracture greyish-white or dark grey, sometimes with fissures; cambium ring subrounded, the pith relatively large or hollow. Odour slight (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Metaderm consists of 1-9 layers of brownish-yellow suberized cells. Cortex narrow. Stone cells subretangular, square or elliptical, lumen large, scattered singly or 2-5 in groups in cortex. Endodermis mostly indistinct. Phloem broad, with groups of sieve tubes and irregular clefts scattered throughout. Sclerenchyma occasionally present near the xylem vessels across the cambium layer. Cambium ring subrounded or subpolygonal. Xylem vessels several to dozens in groups, located inside each angle of the cambium, some containing brownish-yellow contents. Pith relatively large. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 4, 1946, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Single starch granules subrounded, 2-26 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-6 units. Metaderm cells brown, subsquared or elongated polygonal in surface view, walls unevenly thickened, some appeared tuberculate and projected into lumen. Sclerenchymatous cells long stripe-shaped or long fusiform, 21-37 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bright white under the polarized microscope. Stone cells colourless, brown when adhered to the metaderm cells, subsquare, subrectangular, subrounded, fusiform or long stripe-shaped, 22-155 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls varied in thickness, the thick-walled stone cells distinctly striated and finely pitted, some contained brown contents; bright white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate, annular vessels occasionally found, 4-59 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 21, 1891, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 402, 960, 950])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[277, 1035, 961, 1480])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1030, 559, 1705, 1241])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[279, 1579, 926, 2017])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1030, 1274, 1743, 2026])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed)\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Sieve tubes in phloem D. Stone cells in cortex\n\n1. Metaderm 2.Cortex 3. Stone cells in cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Cambium 6. Phloem\n\n7. Xylem vessels 8. Sclerenchyma 9. Sieve tube group 10. Pith 11. Starch granules\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\nFritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1065, 164, 1113, 209])\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1727, 58, 1832, 116])\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n上柏\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed)\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[285, 607, 779, 921])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[793, 606, 1280, 922])\n\n20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1303, 609, 1699, 915])\n\n20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[287, 1054, 842, 1408])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[856, 1049, 1406, 1409])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1425, 1053, 1700, 1412])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[289, 1440, 841, 1817])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[856, 1440, 1407, 1815])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1422, 1440, 1702, 1819])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed)\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Metaderm cells 3. Sclerenchymatous cells 4. Stone cells 5. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[41, 24, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Aconitine standard solution\n\nWeigh 3.5 mg of aconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nHypaconitine standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of hypaconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.5 mg of mesaconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-hexane and methanol (6.8:6.4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth oxynitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent\n\nTransfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid into a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 2 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1%, w/v) and 20 mL of diethyl ether. Cap the flask. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min in an ice water bath. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately aconitine standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), hypaconitine standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), mesaconitine standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 3, 1944, 256])\n\nthe developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and add ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) to the other trough. Hang the HPTLC plate in the chamber, cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully place the HPTLC plate in the trough containing the developing solvent for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[451, 849, 933, 1307])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[454, 1381, 898, 1840])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[452, 1915, 924, 2366])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) aconitine (ii) hypaconitine and (iii) mesaconitine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[385, 374, 987, 1065])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Mesaconitine standard solution 2. Aconitine standard solution\n\n3. Hypaconitine standard solution 4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Aconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (180 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.9 mg of aconitine CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nHypaconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (320 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.6 mg of hypaconitine CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nMesaconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (420 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.1 mg of mesaconitine CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 9, 1944, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 2 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) and 20 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Cap the tube. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min in an ice water bath. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue for three times each with 10 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Combine the solutions. Evaporate the solvent to dryness below $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (235 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2%Acetic acid*(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-44</td><td>79→69</td><td>21→31</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>44-65</td><td>69→65</td><td>31→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n*Adjust the pH to 6.2 with triethylamine"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP and mesaconitine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP, mesaconitine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatograms of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP, mesaconitine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP and mesaconitine Std-FP. The retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.31</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(mesaconitine)</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,hypaconitine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(aconitine)</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[384, 1771, 1775, 2308])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 13, 1944, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aconiti-kusnezoffii-radix-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix unprocessed","la":"Aconiti Kusnezoffii Radix unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aconiti-lateralis-radix-unprocessed-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aconiti Lateralis Radix unprocessed v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 863, 1253, 1169])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[642, 1223, 1401, 1823])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed)\n\nA. Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed)\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of branch root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 17, 1873, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 863, 1253, 1169])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[642, 1223, 1401, 1823])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed)\n\nA. Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed)\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of branch root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 17, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed)\n\nChinese Name: 附子（生）\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Fuzi (Sheng)"},{"name":"13","text":"Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed) is the unprocessed dried branch root of Aconitum carmichaelii Debx. (Ranunculaceae). The root is collected in late June to early August, primary root tubers, rootlets and soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed)."},{"name":"13","text":"Conical, polygonal conical or deeply wrinkled conical, 1-7 cm long, 8-50 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown to brown, with dented bud scars at apex, and surrounded with protuberant tuberculate rootlets or rootlets scars. Texture heavy in weight. Transverse section greyish-white to pale yellowishbrown, cambium ring slightly subrounded or polygonal, and vascular bundles arranged irregularly inside the ring. Odour slight (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Metaderm consists of 1-7 layers of brown suberized cells. Stone cells subrectangular, square or elongated-elliptical, with relatively large lumen, singly scattered or in groups, occasionally found in cortical parenchyma. Endodermis distinct. Phloem broad, scattered with sieve tube groups. Cambium ring slightly subrounded or subpolygonal. Xylem vessels consist of several rows of cells, arranged radially or V-shaped. Pith distinct. Some parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-white to greyish-brown. Single starch granules spherical, oblong or reniform, 3-22 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 4, 1944, 259])\n\nstarch granules composed of 2-15 units. Stone cells nearly colourless or pale yellowish-green, subrectangular, subsquare, polygonal or tapering at one side, 68-280 $ \\mu m $ long, 45-117 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls 4-13 $ \\mu m $ thick, the thick-walled stone cells distinctly striated, pits relatively sparse. Metaderm cells brown, some walls tuberculate thickened and projected into lumen. Vessels nearly colourless or pale yellow, mainly bordered-pitted, 27-70 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, endings truncate or mucronate, perforated at the end walls or side walls, some vessels thick and short, tortuous or connected in crisscross pattern (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 17, 1893, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[208, 406, 1732, 2077])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed)\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells in cortex D. Sieve tube in phloem\n\n1. Metaderm 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells in cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Sieve tube group\n\n6. Starch granules 7. Phloem 8. Cambium 9. Xylem 10. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 11, 1946, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[371, 449, 980, 930])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[996, 447, 1610, 934])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[370, 958, 981, 1452])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[996, 958, 1610, 1451])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[369, 1488, 979, 1979])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[998, 1488, 1609, 1982])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Stone cell (2-1 subsquare stone cell, 2-2 polygonal stone cell)\n\n3. Metaderm cells 4. Vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 25, 1881, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Aconitine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of aconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nHypaconitine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hypaconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 mg of mesaconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and methanol (6.4:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth oxynitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent\n\nTransfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid into a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 3 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) and 25 mL of diethyl ether. Cap the flask. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min in an ice water bath. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.5 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately aconitine standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), hypaconitine standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), mesaconitine standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 4, 1944, 255])\n\ndeveloping solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and add ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) to the other trough. Hang the HPTLC plate in the chamber, cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 10 min. Carefully place the HPTLC plate in the trough containing the developing solvent for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[451, 849, 933, 1307])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[451, 1381, 898, 1841])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[454, 1915, 923, 2365])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) aconitine (ii) hypaconitine and (iii) mesaconitine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[386, 378, 955, 1111])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed) extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Mesaconitine standard solution 2. Aconitine standard solution\n\n3. Hypaconitine standard solution 4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Aconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of aconitine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nHypaconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of hypaconitine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nMesaconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of mesaconitine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 8, 1945, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 3 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) and 30 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Cap the tube. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min in an ice water bath. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue for three times each with 10 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Combine the solutions. Evaporate the solvent to dryness below $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (235 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2%Acetic acid*(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-44</td><td>79→69</td><td>21→31</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>44-65</td><td>69→65</td><td>31→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n*Adjust the pH to 6.2 with triethylamine"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP and mesaconitine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[37, 18, 1856, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP, mesaconitine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatograms of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP, mesaconitine Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP and mesaconitine Std-FP. The retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.34</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(mesaconitine)</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,hypaconitine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(aconitine)</td><td>1.06</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[381, 1833, 1813, 2296])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Aconiti Lateralis Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 12, 1944, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aconiti-lateralis-radix-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aconiti Lateralis Radix unprocessed","la":"Aconiti Lateralis Radix unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aconiti-radix-unprocessed-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aconiti Radix unprocessed v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1053, 892, 1277, 1120])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[553, 1261, 1099, 1721])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1138, 1259, 1426, 1735])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) A. Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of transverse section of primary root tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 21, 1873, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1053, 892, 1277, 1120])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[553, 1261, 1099, 1721])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1138, 1259, 1426, 1735])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) A. Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of transverse section of primary root tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 21, 1873, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Aconiti Radix (unprocessed)\n\nChinese Name: 川烏（生）\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chuanwu (Sheng)"},{"name":"13","text":"Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) is the unprocessed dried primary root tuber of Aconitum carmichaelii Debx. (Ranunculaceae). The root tuber is collected from late June to early August, branch roots, rootlets and soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Aconiti Radix (unprocessed)."},{"name":"13","text":"Irregularly conical, slightly curved, usually with remnants of stem at apex, mostly swollen towards one side in the middle, 2-7.5 cm long, 12-30 mm in diameter. Externally brown or greyish-brown, shrunken, with tuberculate lateral roots and scars of branch roots . Texture hard and compact. Fracture whitish to greyish-yellow, cambium ring polygonal. Odour slight (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Metaderm consists of brown suberized cells. Stone cells subrectangular, squared, elongated-elliptical or subtriangular, with a relatively large lumen, occurring scattered singly or in groups, occasionally found in cortical parenchyma. Endodermis indistinct. Sieve tube groups scattered in the phloem, with fibre bundles occasionally found on the inner side of phloem. Cambium in a ring, subpolygonal. 1 or several anomalous vascular bundles occasionally found on the inner side of the cambium. Xylem vessels consist of several rows of cells, arranged radially or in V-shaped. Pith distinct. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 3, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale brown to brown. Single starch granules spherical, oblong or reniform, 3-25 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-15 units. Stone cells nearly colourless or pale yellowish-green, subrectangular, subsquare, polygonal or tapering at one side, 62-280 $ \\mu m $ long, 49-117 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls 4-13 $ \\mu m $ thick, the thick-walled stone cells distinctly striated, pits relatively sparse. Metaderm cells brown, some walls tuberculate thickened and projected into lumen. Vessels nearly colourless or pale yellow, mainly bordered-pitted, 12-70 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, endings truncate or mucronate, perforated at the end walls or side walls, some vessels thick and short, tortuous or connected in crisscross pattern (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 448, 1742, 2106])\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells in cortex D. Sieve tube in phloem\n\n1. Metaderm 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells in cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Sieve tube group 6. Phloem\n\n7. Starch granules 8. Cambium 9. Xylem 10. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 12, 1947, 256])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[455, 468, 984, 944])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1005, 470, 1528, 947])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[456, 969, 982, 1446])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1006, 976, 1526, 1451])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[455, 1477, 982, 1964])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1006, 1470, 1527, 1966])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Stone cell (2-1 subsquare stone cell, 2-2 polygonal stone cell)\n\n3. Metaderm cells 4. Vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 25, 1881, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Aconitine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of aconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nHypaconitine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hypaconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 mg of mesaconitine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and methanol (6.4:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth oxynitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent\n\nTransfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid into a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 3 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) and 25 mL of diethyl ether. Cap the flask. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min in an ice water bath. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.5 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately aconitine standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), hypaconitine standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), mesaconitine standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 4, 1944, 256])\n\ndeveloping solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and add ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) to the other trough. Hang the HPTLC plate in the chamber, cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 10 min. Carefully place the HPTLC plate in the trough containing the developing solvent for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[451, 849, 933, 1307])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[454, 1381, 898, 1841])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[453, 1915, 923, 2365])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) aconitine (ii) hypaconitine and (iii) mesaconitine\n\n</div>\n\n金銀花\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[385, 381, 954, 1130])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Mesaconitine standard solution 2. Aconitine standard solution\n\n3. Hypaconitine standard solution 4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Aconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of aconitine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nHypaconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of hypaconitine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nMesaconitine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of mesaconitine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 9, 1945, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 3 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (9.1% , w/v) and 30 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Cap the tube. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min in an ice water bath. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue for three times each with 10 mL of a mixture of isopropanol and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v). Combine the solutions. Evaporate the solvent to dryness below $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with 0.01% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (235 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2%Acetic acid*(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-44</td><td>79→69</td><td>21→31</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>44-65</td><td>69→65</td><td>31→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n*Adjust the pH to 6.2 with triethylamine"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP and mesaconitine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP, mesaconitine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatograms of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP, mesaconitine Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of aconitine Std-FP, hypaconitine Std-FP and mesaconitine Std-FP. The retention times of aconitine, hypaconitine and mesaconitine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(mesaconitine)</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,hypaconitine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(aconitine)</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.18</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[383, 1830, 1800, 2288])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Aconiti Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 12, 1944, 261])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aconiti-radix-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aconiti Radix unprocessed","la":"Aconiti Radix unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/acori-tatarinowii-rhizoma-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[479, 856, 1543, 1897])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma A. Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma B. Transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[30, 9, 1902, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[479, 856, 1543, 1897])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma A. Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma B. Transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[30, 9, 1902, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 石菖蒲\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shichangpu"},{"name":"12","text":"Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Acorus tatarinowii Schott (Araceae). The rhizome is collected in autumn and winter, fibrous roots and soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Compressed-cylindrical, frequently tortuous, normally branched, 3-31 cm long, 3-16 mm in diameter. Externally brown or greyish-brown, rough, with uneven annulations, internodes 1-15 mm long, with fine longitudinal wrinkles, occasionally with remnants of fibrous roots or rounded root scars; leaf scars triangular, arranged alternately, some with hairy and scaly remnants of leaf bases. Texture hard. Fracture fibrous, white or pale red, an endodermis ring distinct, numerous dotted vascular bundles and brown oil spots visible. Odour aromatic; taste bitter and slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of brown cells with thickened outer wall. Cortex broad, scattered with numerous fibre bundles and leaf-trace vascular bundles. Endodermis distinct. Stele vascular bundles amphivasal or collateral, densely lined up near the endodermis, gradually larger and sparse inward, vascular bundles sheath distinct. Parenchyma scattered with subrounded secretory cells, the latter filled with secretions (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Fibres mostly in bundle, colourless or pale yellow, 6-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, tapering towards the end, wall 2-7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ thick, lignified, pit canals distinct. Fibre bundles surrounded by cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres. Prisms of calcium oxalate polyhedral, subpolygonal or polyconelike, 4-13 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Simple starch granules ellipsoid, spheroidal or long-ovoid, 2-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum pointed, V-shaped or short slit-shaped, striation indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-20 (even more) units. Secretory cells abundant, subrounded or elongated-rounded, 14-43 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, filled with orangish-yellow secretions. Epidermal cells of leaf sheath greyish-green or pale yellowish-brown, rectangular, 26-62 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 9-31 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide. Vessels mainly reticulate, 9-32 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[33, 6, 1909, 263])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 406, 1740, 2135])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibre bundle D. Secretory cell E. Stele vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Fibre bundle 4. Secretory cell\n\n5. Leaf-trace vascular bundle 6. Endodermis 7. Stele vascular bundle\n\n8. Vascular bundle sheath 9. Xylem 10. Phloem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[293, 459, 985, 931])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[999, 461, 1693, 928])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[294, 951, 988, 1394])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[995, 949, 1693, 1411])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[286, 1427, 747, 2016])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[757, 1434, 1284, 2012])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1294, 1433, 1687, 2011])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Crystal fibres 2. Starch granules 3. Secretory cells\n\n4. Epidermal cells of leaf sheath 5. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 7, 1909, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\alpha $ -Asarone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of $ \\alpha $ -asarone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (5:1, v/v).\n\nStaining reagent\n\nIodine."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately $ \\alpha $ -asarone standard solution and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapor chamber for about 30 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of $ \\alpha $ - asarone.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[67, 11, 1965, 259])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 369, 817, 662])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of $ \\alpha $ -asarone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\alpha $ -Asarone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of $ \\alpha $ -asarone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>70→55</td><td>30→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>55→25</td><td>45→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>25</td><td>75</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 5, 1904, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of $ \\alpha $ -asarone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of $ \\alpha $ -asarone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of $ \\alpha $ -asarone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from $ \\alpha $ -asarone peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates."},{"name":"12","text":"The R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5).\n\nSeparately inject $ \\alpha $ -asarone Std-FP and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of $ \\alpha $ -asarone peak in the chromatogram of $ \\alpha $ -asarone Std-FP and the retention times of the two characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify $ \\alpha $ -asarone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of $ \\alpha $ -asarone Std-FP. The retention times of $ \\alpha $ -asarone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the two characteristic peaks of Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the two characteristic peaks of Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(β-asarone)</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,α-asarone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n香附\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nAcori Tatarinowii Rhizoma Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[294, 367, 1847, 947])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above two characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nToluene distillation method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 8, 1900, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\alpha $ - Asarone standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of $ \\alpha $ -asarone CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol.\n\n$ \\alpha $ -Asarone standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the $ \\alpha $ -asarone Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 0.5, 1, 30, 60, 90 mg/L for $ \\alpha $ -asarone."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh accurately 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/acori-tatarinowii-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma","la":"Acori Tatarinowii Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/agastaches-herba-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Agastaches Herba v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Agastaches Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[370, 783, 1567, 2057])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Agastaches Herba\n\nA. Agastaches Herba B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of stem E. Transverse section of stem F. Magnified image of spike\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[50, 24, 1920, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Agastaches Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[370, 783, 1567, 2057])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Agastaches Herba\n\nA. Agastaches Herba B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of stem E. Transverse section of stem F. Magnified image of spike\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[50, 24, 1920, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Agastaches Herba\n\nChinese name: 藿香\n\nChinese phonetic name: Huoxiang"},{"name":"14","text":"Agastaches Herba is the dried aerial part of Agastache rugosa (Fisch. & C.A.Mey.) Kuntze (Lamiaceae). The aerial part is collected when foliage branch growing luxuriantly or at the beginning of flowering period in summer and autumn, dried under the sun or in a shaded area to obtain Agastaches Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem square-cylindrical, frequently with opposite branches, 30-110 cm in length, 0.2-1.3 cm in width, externally green to yellowish-green, texture fragile, easily broken, fracture surface white, hollow in the centre. Leaves opposite, thin lamina, almost shrunken and broken; the intact ones ovate or long-ovate when flattened, 2-9 cm in length, 1-5 cm in width, acute or short-acuminate at the apex, rounded or cordate at the base, and with obtuse serrate margin; the upper surface dark green to brown, the lower surface pale green to brown; petioles 1-5 cm in length. Terminal spikes compact, cylindrical. Odour aromatic; taste bland and giving a slightly cooling sensation in mouth (Fig.1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Leaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, sometimes with non-glandular hairs and glandular hairs. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cylindrical cells, arranged in order. Spongy tissue consists of 4-6 layers of cells, arranged loosely. Vascular bundle 1, collateral, xylem vessels arranged radially. Collenchymatous cells sometimes located in the inner side of lower epidermis of the midrib. Lower epidermis also consists of 1 layer of cells, sometimes with glandular hairs, non-glandular hairs and glandular scales [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[50, 12, 1934, 259])\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle, occasionally with glandular hairs and non-glandular hairs. Cortex relatively narrow, collenchymatous cells observed at four angles of stem. Pericyclic fibres interrupted arranged in a ring, lignified. Phloem narrow. Cambium distinct, arranged in a ring. Xylem relatively wide, consists of lignified vessels and fibres. Pith broad, consisting of lots of large polygonal cells, occasionally with prisms of calcium oxalate, usually hollow in the middle [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-green to yellowish-brown. The walls of upper and lower epidermal cells of leaf slightly curved; stomata diacytic type. Multicellular non-glandular hair two types, the first type short conical, consisting of 1-2 cells, 15-80 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length, 20-35 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter at base, with fine warty protuberance on the surface; the second type slender, consisting of 1-4 cells, 80-420 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length, 18-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter at base. Glandular hair consists of 1-2 celled head, subrounded, stalk single cell. Glandular scale consists of 4-8 celled head, oblate spherical, stalk single cell. Fibres often in bundles, 10-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with fine pits in cell wall. Bordered-pitted vessels and spiral vessels frequently visible (Fig. 3).\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nCampsis Flos\n\n凌霄花\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[235, 407, 1739, 1595])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Agastaches Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C-D. Magnified image of mesophyll\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Lower epidermis 6. Glandular hair 7. Vascular bundle 8. Glandular scale\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁 菊花\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[234, 408, 1723, 1573])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Agastaches Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Crystal of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Crystal of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Glandular hair F. Non-glandular hair\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Pericyclic fibres 4. Phloem 5. Cambium\n\n6. Glandular hair 7. Xylem 8. Pith 9. Non-glandular hair\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\nUncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[249, 426, 1752, 2133])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Upper epidermal cells of leaf 2. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Multicellular non-glandular hair (3-1 short conical, 3-2 long slender) 4. Glandular hairs\n\n5. Glandular scale 6. Pericyclic fibres\n\n7. Vessels (7-1 bordered-pitted vessel, 7-2 spiral vessels)\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[48, 32, 1942, 255])"},{"name":"14","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 13 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol ( 75% ).\n\nTilianin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of tilianin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol ( 75% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (15:1:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (75%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture at below $ 2 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately rosmarinic acid standard solution, tilianin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[11, 9, 1939, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 425, 1053, 711])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 816, 1145, 1144])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) rosmarinic acid and (ii) tilianin\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1402, 203, 1440, 256])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 376, 1054, 1210])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Agastaches Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Tilianin standard solution 2. Rosmarinic acid standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of rosmarinic acid and tilianin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (75%).\n\nTilianin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (16 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.8 mg of tilianin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (75%).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[48, 15, 1920, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (75%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture at below $ 2 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 8 0 0 0 \\times g $ at $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (75%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $"},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>90→80</td><td>10→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-12</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>12-20</td><td>80→75</td><td>20→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-25</td><td>75→72</td><td>25→28</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-40</td><td>72→58</td><td>28→42</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-45</td><td>58→34</td><td>42→66</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of rosmarinic acid Std-FP and tilianin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of rosmarinic acid and tilianin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of rosmarinic acid and tilianin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from rosmarinic acid and tilianin peaks should not be less than 40000 and 50000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject rosmarinic acid Std-FP, tilianin Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of rosmarinic acid and tilianin peaks in the chromatograms of rosmarinic acid Std-FP, tilianin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify rosmarinic acid and tilianin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of rosmarinic acid Std-FP and tilianin Std-FP. The retention times of rosmarinic acid and tilianin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Agastaches Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Agastaches Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(rosmarinic acid)</td><td>0.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,tilianin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.30</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.77</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[330, 1655, 1820, 2202])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Agastaches Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[41, 33, 1938, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.81%."},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 9.1%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.3%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.7%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/agastaches-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Agastaches Herba","la":"Agastaches Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/agrimoniae-herba-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Agrimoniae Herba v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Agrimoniae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[473, 1034, 826, 1284])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[871, 910, 966, 1061])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1002, 909, 1088, 1062])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[867, 1192, 970, 1286])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1005, 1185, 1096, 1287])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1107, 861, 1236, 1286])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nG\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[643, 1374, 1333, 1681])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Agrimoniae Herba\n\nA. Agrimoniae Herba B. A branch with leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of upper part of stem\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of upper part of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of lower part of stem\n\nF. Magnified image of transverse section of lower part of stem\n\nG. Magnified image of inflorescence\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Agrimoniae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[473, 1034, 826, 1284])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[871, 910, 966, 1061])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1002, 909, 1088, 1062])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[867, 1192, 970, 1286])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1005, 1185, 1096, 1287])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1107, 861, 1236, 1286])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nG\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[643, 1374, 1333, 1681])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Agrimoniae Herba\n\nA. Agrimoniae Herba B. A branch with leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of upper part of stem\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of upper part of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of lower part of stem\n\nF. Magnified image of transverse section of lower part of stem\n\nG. Magnified image of inflorescence\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Agrimoniae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 仙鹤草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xianhecao"},{"name":"12","text":"Agrimoniae Herba is the dried aerial part of Agrimonia pilosa Ledeb. (Rosaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn when foliage branch growing luxuriantly, foreign matter removed, then dried to obtain Agrimoniae Herba."},{"name":"12","text":"Up to 93 cm long, covered with white pubescence. The lower part of stem cylindrical, 2-7 mm in diameter, reddish-brown; the upper part squared, the four sides slightly sunken, greenish-brown, with longitudinal furrows and ridges, the nodes distinct; texture hard and light in weight, easily broken; fracture hollowed. Leaves odd-pinnately compound, alternate, dark green, crumpled and rolled; texture fragile, easily broken; leaflets of two sizes, the small leaflets interposed between the large ones along the rachis, the leaflet at the apex relatively large, when intact flattened out, ovate to elongated-elliptic, apex acute, base cuneate, margin serrate; stipules 2, amplexicaul, oblique-ovate. Racemes disposed on terminal, slender, pedicel short, the lower part of calyx tubular, 5-lobed at the apex, petals 5, yellow. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem: Non-glandular hairs occur commonly on the surface of the epidermis. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquared cells, arranged orderly. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells. Pericyclic fibres consist of 6-8 layers of cells, arranged in ring; fibres polygonal, lignified. Phloem relatively narrow, arranged in ring. Cambium indistinct. Xylem relatively broad, arranged radically, arranged in an undulated ring. Pith broad, occupying about 2/3 of the stem. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in the cortex and pith [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 4, 1946, 258])\n\nLeaf: Non-glandular hairs occur on the surface of the upper and lower epidermis. Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged orderly. Palisade tissue consists of 2 layers of cells, not passing through the midvein. Spongy tissue consists of 2-4 layers of cells. Phloem narrow. Xylem broad. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged orderly. Collenchyma located distinctly at prominent regions underneath the lower epidermis. Clusters of calcium oxalate located in the palisade tissue and parenchymatous cells of the midvein [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour dark green. Upper epidermal cells polygonal. The cell walls of lower epidermal cells sinuous, stomata anomocytic or anisocytic. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, varying in length, walls thickened and lignified, with warty protuberances and sometimes with spiral striations. Multicellular glandular hairs consist of 1- to 4-celled head, ovoid, with a 1- to 2-celled stalk. Glandular hairs occur occasionally, consisting of a unicellular head, 43-74 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, containing volatile oil droplet, locating on the top of a unicellular stalk. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, 8-46 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 16, 1892, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 457, 1730, 2041])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Agrimoniae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Pericyclic fibres 6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 4, 1947, 253])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[239, 465, 1727, 2022])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Agrimoniae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate D. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Clusters of calcium oxalate 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Collenchyma 9. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[33, 25, 1890, 258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[255, 626, 1710, 2115])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Agrimoniae Herba\n\n1. Upper epidermal cells 2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata\n\n3. Non-glandular hair (3-1 with warty protuberances, 3-2 with spiral striations)\n\n4. Multicellular glandular hair 5. Glandular hair 6. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 13, 1946, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Quercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of quercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (25:2:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercitrin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 1680, 833, 2165])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of quercitrin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 18, 1885, 260])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 376, 867, 1217])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Agrimoniae Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Quercitrin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercitrin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Quercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of quercitrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[39, 7, 1948, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>90→65</td><td>10→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of quercitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of quercitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of quercitrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from quercitrin peak should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject quercitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of quercitrin peak in the chromatogram of quercitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify quercitrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of quercitrin Std-FP. The retention times of quercitrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Agrimoniae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Agrimoniae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.99</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, quercitrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 22, 1875, 262])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[382, 372, 1811, 880])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Agrimoniae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 12, 1947, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/agrimoniae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Agrimoniae Herba","la":"Agrimoniae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ailanthi-cortex-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ailanthi Cortex v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[576, 1006, 1018, 1209])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1083, 1035, 1432, 1210])\n\nB 1mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[604, 1284, 976, 1514])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1110, 1344, 1441, 1445])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[842, 1579, 1150, 1893])\n\nE 5 mm"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[576, 1006, 1018, 1209])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1083, 1035, 1432, 1210])\n\nB 1mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[604, 1284, 976, 1514])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1110, 1344, 1441, 1445])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[842, 1579, 1150, 1893])\n\nE 5 mm"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Outer surface view (with coarse bark)\n\nB. Fracture surface (with coarse bark)\n\nC. Outer surface view (without coarse bark)\n\nD. Fracture surface (without coarse bark)\n\nE. Enlarged inner surface view\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 23, 1901, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Ailanthi Cortex\n\nChinese name: 椿皮\n\nChinese phonetic name: Chunpi"},{"name":"13","text":"Ailanthi Cortex is the stem bark of Ailanthus altissima (Mill.) Swingle (Simaroubaceae). The stem bark is collected all year round, usually in spring, sometimes the coarse bark removed, cut longitudinally, dried under the sun to obtain Ailanthi Cortex."},{"name":"13","text":"Irregular plates or pieces, varying in size, 0.3-2 cm thick. Outer surface greyish-black, very rough and deeply fissured, coarse bark sometimes flaking or peeling off. Inner surface pale yellow, relatively even, densely covered with small spindle-shaped holes or spots. Fracture surface shows granular features at the outer part while inner part shows fibrous characteristics. Texture hard and fragile. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Cork consists of many layers of cells, the cell elongated tangentially, arranged orderly, with several groups of stone cell scattered on the inside part of cork, arranged in an interrupted ring. Stone cells yellow, wall thickened and lignified, some containing prisms of calcium oxalate, clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres numerous, lignified and cell wall extremely thickened. Stone cells and fibres scattered in cortex and phloem. Phloem relatively broad, scattered fibres and groups of stone cells. Phloem rays 2-4 rows of cells wide (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale greyish-yellow. Stone cells numerous, subrounded, subsquare or irregular in shape, 23-97 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls thickened, or thicker on three sides and thinner on one side, some containing prisms of calcium oxalate; bright yellow under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate polyhedral or biconical shape, 11-49 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, 12-49 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells light brownish-yellow, rectangular to square. Fibres 18-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls extremely thickened and lignified (Fig. 3).\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[306, 425, 1733, 2118])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Ailanthi Cortex\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate in stone cells (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate in stone cells (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Fibres\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cells (clusters of calcium oxalate $ \\rightarrow $ ; prisms of calcium oxalate $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Fibre 4. Phloem 5. Phloem ray\n\n---\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n菊花\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 459, 1751, 2118])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Ailanthi Cortex\n\n1. Stone cells (prism of calcium oxalate $ \\rightarrow $ ） 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n3. Cluster of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate 4. Cork cells 5. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[45, 33, 1938, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Ailanthone standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of ailanthone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nCanthin-6-one standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of canthin-6-one CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and formic acid (20:1.5:0.3, v/v)"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the combined filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol (70%). Transfer the extract to a 25-mL separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 6 mL of dichloromethane. Combine the dichloromethane extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ailanthone standard solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), canthin-6-one standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 10 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm and 366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1400, 196, 1501, 256])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 436, 754, 752])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 878, 662, 1076])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) ailanthone and (ii) canthin-6-one\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 1263, 640, 2094])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[671, 1264, 1099, 2089])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ailanthi Cortex extract observed under UV light (left: 254 nm for visualization of ailanthone; right: 366 nm for visualization of canthin-6-one)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Ailanthone standard solution 2. Canthin-6-one standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of ailanthone and canthin-6-one (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[46, 15, 1938, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Standard solutions\n\nAilanthone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of ailanthone CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nCanthin-6-one standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (1.5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of canthin-6-one CRS and dissolve in 200 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Wash the residue with 10 mL of methanol (70%). Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and combine the filtrates. Evaporate the combined filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (70%). Transfer the extract to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Pipette 2 mL of the extract to a 25-mL separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 6 mL of dichloromethane. Combine the dichloromethane extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (70%). Transfer the extract to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (244 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>0.05% Formic acid in acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-15</td><td>88→78</td><td>12→22</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-20</td><td>78→68</td><td>22→32</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-25</td><td>68→64</td><td>32→36</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-28</td><td>64</td><td>36</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[33, 24, 1952, 253])"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ailanthone Std-FP and canthin-6-one Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of ailanthone and canthin-6-one should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of ailanthone and canthin-6-one peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from ailanthone and canthin-6-one peaks should not be less than 7000 and 100000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject ailanthone Std-FP, canthin-6-one Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of ailanthone and canthin-6-one peaks in the chromatograms of ailanthone Std-FP, canthin-6-one Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ailanthone and canthin-6-one peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of ailanthone Std-FP and canthin-6-one Std-FP. The retention times of ailanthone and canthin-6-one peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Ailanthi Cortex extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Ailanthi Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, ailanthone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.67</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3 (canthin-6-one)</td><td>3.30</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[48, 20, 1924, 256])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[331, 375, 1825, 919])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ailanthi Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 11%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.9%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 32, 1918, 255])"},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.9%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.9%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ailanthi-cortex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ailanthi Cortex","la":"Ailanthi Cortex"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/akebiae-caulis-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Akebiae Caulis v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[403, 798, 1498, 2009])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Lianoid stems\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAkebiae Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[373, 513, 1643, 1789])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz.\n\nA. Lianoid stem\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁 Akebiae Caulis 菊花\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[371, 535, 1639, 1785])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu\n\n</div>\n\nA. Lianoid stems\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[42, 31, 1912, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"20","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[403, 798, 1498, 2009])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Lianoid stems\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAkebiae Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[373, 513, 1643, 1789])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz.\n\nA. Lianoid stem\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁 Akebiae Caulis 菊花\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[371, 535, 1639, 1785])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu\n\n</div>\n\nA. Lianoid stems\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[42, 31, 1912, 257])"},{"name":"20","text":"Official name: Akebiae Caulis\n\nChinese name: 木通\n\nChinese phonetic name: Mutong"},{"name":"20","text":"Akebiae Caulis is the dried lianoid stem of Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne., Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. or Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu (Lardizabalaceae). The lianoid stem is collected in autumn, freed from leaves and small branches, cut into long sections, dried under the sun to obtain Akebiae Caulis."},{"name":"20","text":"Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.: Cylindrical, often slightly twisted, 9-70 cm in length, 3-32 mm in diameter. Externally light grey to greyish-brown, rough, with irregular cracks and longitudinal furrows. Lenticels distinct. Nodes sometimes swollen, with scars of lateral branches. Light in weight and texture compact, uneasily broken. Fracture surface uneven, bark relatively broad and yellowish-brown, with pale yellowish granular dots, easily distinguished from the wood, sometimes with arched hollows around the outside of the wood; the wood yellowish-white, with distinct radial striations. Pith small, occasionally hollowed, yellowish-white to yellowish-brown. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and astringent [Figure 1 (i)].\n\nAkebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz.: 17-77 cm in length, 2-28 mm in diameter. Externally brown to dark brown [Figure 1 (ii)].\n\nAkebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu: 14-73 cm in length, 2-26 mm in diameter. Externally brown to dark brown [Figure 1 (iii)].\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[38, 20, 1943, 257])"},{"name":"20","text":"Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.: Cork consists of several layers of rectangular or subsquare cells, densely arranged. Cortex consists of several layers of polygonal cells. Pericycle appearing as a continuous shallow wavy ring formed by crystal fibre bundles and crystal stone cell groups alternately arranging. Vascular bundles 11-29. Phloem consists of several layers of cells. Cambium distinct, cells polygonal. Xylem broad, vessels variable in size, mostly singly scattered. Medullary rays distinct, outer parts of phloem rays containing crystals stone cells, which connected with those of pericycle; cells of xylem rays around the pith often containing small prisms of calcium oxalate. Pith sometimes hollow in the centre, cells surrounding pith rounded, small, walls thickened [Figure 2 (i)].\n\nAkebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz.: There is no significant difference in features as compared with Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne. [Figure 2 (ii)].\n\nAkebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu: There is no significant difference in features as compared with Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne. [Figure 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.: Colour yellowish-white to greyish-brown. Cork cells pale yellow, polygonal in surface view, sometimes with yellowish-brown content. Stone cells numerous, squared, elongated or polygonal, 10-63 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; lumen filled with one or several prisms of calcium oxalates, 3-47 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pericyclic fibres (crystal fibre) abundant, single or in groups, frequently broken, 4-34 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls thickened and lignified, narrow lumen densely scattered with fine prisms of calcium oxalates; usually surrounding with crystal stone cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Medullary ray cells more or less rectangular, with thick, ligneous, pitted walls, 6-31 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Xylem fibres long fusiform, frequently broken, wall thickened, with slit-like single pits or fine bordered pits. Vessels often fragmented, mostly bordered-pitted [Figure 3 (i)].\n\nAkebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz.: Cork cells yellow or dark brown, without content. Stone cells 9-51 $ \\mu $m in diameter, prisms of calcium oxalate 1-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Pericyclic fibres 4-31 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Medullary ray cells 5-47 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Figure 3 (ii)].\n\nAkebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu: Cork cells yellow or dark brown. Stone cells 8-62 $ \\mu $m in diameter, prisms of calcium oxalate 3-30 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Pericyclic fibres 4-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Medullary ray cells 5-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Figure 3 (iii)].\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAkebiae Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[240, 411, 1747, 2122])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of dried lianoid stem of Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibres and stone cells D. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n7. Ray 8. Pith 9. Fibre 10. Stone cell 11. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁 Akebiae Caulis 菊花\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[242, 426, 1741, 2106])\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibres and stone cells D. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n7. Ray 8. Pith 9. Fibre 10. Stone cell 11. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAkebiae Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[242, 396, 1744, 2105])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibres and stone cells D. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n7. Ray 8. Pith 9. Fibre 10. Stone cell 11. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁\n\n菊花\n\nAkebiae Caulis\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[239, 440, 1749, 2124])\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of lianoid stem of Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.\n\n1. Cork cells 2. Stone cells (prisms of calcium oxalate $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n3. Pericyclic fibres (crystal fibres) (crystal stone cells $ \\rightarrow $ ） 4. Medullary ray cells ( $ \\rightarrow $ ）\n\n5. Xylem fibre 6. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAkebiae Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n苦杏仁\n\nChrysanthemi Flos菊花\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[364, 443, 841, 828])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[879, 443, 1614, 812])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[368, 854, 842, 1449])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[865, 852, 1615, 1234])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[862, 1263, 1614, 1650])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[368, 1474, 843, 2050])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[866, 1670, 1224, 2050])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1248, 1669, 1614, 2056])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz.\n\n1. Cork cells 2. Stone cells (prisms of calcium oxalate $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n3. Pericyclic fibres (crystal fibres) (crystal"},{"name":"20","text":"Calceolarioside B standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of calceolarioside B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"20","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol, water and glacial acetic acid (8:2:0.2:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"20","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol and dissolve 0.2 g of vanillin."},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 150-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL of round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of water. Transfer the extract to a 100-mL separating funnel. Extract the aqueous layer for three times each with 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"20","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately calceolarioside B standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 0.5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤 Sinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[1399, 196, 1499, 256])\n\nAkebiae Caulis\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[379, 366, 1089, 821])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of calceolarioside B\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[377, 1009, 1042, 1801])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2(i) 2(ii) 2(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Akebiae Caulis extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Calceolarioside B standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried lianoid stem of Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne.\n\n(ii) dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz.\n\n(iii) dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of calceolarioside B (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[45, 22, 1939, 257])"},{"name":"20","text":"Calceolarioside B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of calceolarioside B CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 15 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the extract to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction two more times. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"20","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (325 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Formic acid:Acetonitrile(0.1:99.9,v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-2</td><td>94→92</td><td>6→8</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>2-11</td><td>92</td><td>8</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>11-12</td><td>92→85</td><td>8→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>12-20</td><td>85→83</td><td>15→17</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>83→75</td><td>17→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[40, 26, 1953, 253])"},{"name":"20","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of calceolarioside B Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of calceolarioside B should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of calceolarioside B peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from calceolarioside B peak should not be less than 140000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"Separately inject calceolarioside B Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of calceolarioside B peak in the chromatogram of calceolarioside B Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify calceolarioside B peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of calceolarioside B Std-FP. The retention times of calceolarioside B peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Akebiae Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Akebiae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(5-caffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>0.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(3-caffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3(4-caffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, calceolarioside B)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[329, 373, 1825, 925])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried lianoid stem of Akebia quinata (Houtt.) Decne. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[328, 1109, 1824, 1677])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[333, 1843, 1818, 2414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried lianoid stem of Akebia trifoliata (Thunb.) Koidz. var. australis (Diels) T. Shimizu extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[41, 29, 1943, 257])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.4%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"20","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10%.\n\n5. 8 Detection of Aristolochic Acid I (Appendix XIII): not detected."},{"name":"20","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.5%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11%.\n\n![](page=17,bbox=[47, 29, 1939, 258])"},{"name":"20","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/akebiae-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Akebiae Caulis","la":"Akebiae Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/albiziae-cortex-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albiziae Cortex v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[644, 955, 866, 1228])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[913, 962, 1202, 1229])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1251, 938, 1346, 1238])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Albiziae Cortex\n\nA. Albiziae Cortex B. Outer surface of stem bark\n\nC. Inner surface of stem bark D. Transverse section of stem bark"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[644, 955, 866, 1228])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[913, 962, 1202, 1229])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1251, 938, 1346, 1238])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Albiziae Cortex\n\nA. Albiziae Cortex B. Outer surface of stem bark\n\nC. Inner surface of stem bark D. Transverse section of stem bark"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Albiziae Cortex\n\nChinese Name: 合歡皮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hehuanpi"},{"name":"12","text":"Albiziae Cortex is the dried stem bark of Albizia julibrissin Durazz. (Fabaceae). The stem bark is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Albiziae Cortex."},{"name":"12","text":"Quilled or semi-quilled pieces, 40-80 cm long, 1-3 mm thick. Externally greyish-brown, sometimes longitudinally wrinkled or shallowly fissured, with distinct transverse elliptical lenticels, brown to reddish-brown, occasionally showing protuberant transverse ridges or relatively large rounded branch scars, and usually covered with patches of lichens. The inner surface pale yellowish-brown to yellowish-white, smooth, with fine and dense longitudinal striations. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Fracture laminate-fibrous, pale yellowish-brown to yellowish-white. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly astringent and irritating to the tongue, followed by the discomfort of throat (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork cells varying in layers, up to more than 20 layers. Cortex consists of tangentially prolonged cells, scattered with groups of stone cells and prisms of calcium oxalate; most of stone cells in groups. A band of stone cell groups intermittently located around pericycle position. The cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate densely distributed around stone cells and fibre bundles. Phloem rays 1-4 rows of cells wide, slightly curved. Stone cells scattered in outer part of phloem. Phloem fibres in bundles, arranged in more than 10 rows discontinuously, surrounded by parenchymatous cells which contain prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[63, 6, 1959, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellow. Fibres usually in bundles, slender, 7-22 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, surrounding by parenchymatous cells which contain prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Phloem parenchymatous cells subrectangular or irregular, walls thickened or beaded-thickened, pits visible. Prisms of calcium oxalate up to 18 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells suboblong, subrounded, rectangular or irregular, 16-58 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls relatively thickened, pit canals distinct, some branched; bright white under the polarized microscope. Cork cells pale yellowish-brown, polygonal in surface view (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus 水紅花子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 577, 1690, 2122])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Albiziae Cortex\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells, prisms of calcium oxalate and fibres\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cell group band 4. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Stone cells 6. Phloem 7. Phloem rays 8. Fibre bundles\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\n沙苑子 Astragali Complanati Semen\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nAlbiziae Cortex\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[264, 696, 1696, 2121])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Albiziae Cortex\n\n1. Crystal fibres 2. Phloem parenchymatous cells 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Stone cells\n\n5. Cork cells (in surface view)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[23, 9, 1926, 250])"},{"name":"12","text":"(-)-Syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl-（1 $ \\rightarrow $ 2）- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution\n\n(-)-Syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution Weigh 0.5 mg of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (7:3:1, v/v). Keep in a refrigerator at a temperature below $ 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for at least 10 h. Mix 10 mL of the lower layer with 0.1 mL of formic acid."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of phosphomolybdic acid hydrate and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.5 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately (-)-syringaresinol-4 $ \\mathrm{O}-\\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution $ (1\\ \\mu\\mathrm{L}) $ and the test solution $ (2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{L}) $ to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 110^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n鬱金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nAlbiziae Cortex\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 373, 865, 1043])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl-（1 $ \\rightarrow $ 2）- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 1251, 932, 1932])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Albiziae Cortex extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. (-)-Syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl-（1 $ \\rightarrow $ 2）- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution\n\n2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of (-)-syringaresinol 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"(-)-Syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl-（1 $ \\rightarrow $ 2）- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (204 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>82→60</td><td>18→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak in the chromatogram of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP. The retention times of (-)-syringaresinol-4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-apiofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Albiziae Cortex extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Albiziae Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.56</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2[marker，(-)-syringaresinol-4-O-β-D-apiofuranosyl-(1→2)-β-D-glucopyranoside]</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.92</td><td>$\\pm$0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[382, 1705, 1827, 2346])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Albiziae Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[635, 18, 1892, 249])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 8, 1968, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/albiziae-cortex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albiziae Cortex","la":"Albiziae Cortex"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/albiziae-flos-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albiziae Flos v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[670, 886, 966, 1145])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1006, 863, 1382, 1194])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[531, 1229, 1366, 1873])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Albiziae Flos\n\nA. Albiziae Flos B. Magnified image of flowers\n\nC. Magnified image of inflorescence with flower\n\nD. Magnified image of inflorescence with flower buds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 17, 1878, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"9","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[670, 886, 966, 1145])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1006, 863, 1382, 1194])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[531, 1229, 1366, 1873])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Albiziae Flos\n\nA. Albiziae Flos B. Magnified image of flowers\n\nC. Magnified image of inflorescence with flower\n\nD. Magnified image of inflorescence with flower buds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 17, 1878, 260])"},{"name":"9","text":"Official Name: Albiziae Flos\n\nChinese Name: 合歡花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hehuanhua"},{"name":"9","text":"Albiziae Flos is the dried inflorescence of Albizia julibrissin Durazz. (Fabaceae). The inflorescence is collected in summer at flowering on sunny days, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun in time to obtain Albiziae Flos."},{"name":"9","text":"Capitulum crumpled to masses. Peduncle 3-4 cm long, sometimes detached from inflorescence, yellowish-green, with longitudinal striations, covered with sparse tomenta. Flowers densely covered with tomenta, slender and curved, pale yellow to pale yellowish-brown, sessile or short-pedicellate. Calyx and corolla 0.7-1 cm long. Calyx tubular, 5-toothed at the apex. Corolla tube about 2 times in length of calyx tube, 5-lobed at the apex; lobes lanceolate. Stamens numerous, filaments slender, yellowish-brown, connate at the lower part and separated at the upper part, extended over corolla tube. Flower buds occasionally visible, club shaped, calyx tubular, corolla unopened. Odour slightly fragrant; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"9","text":"Colour greyish-yellow. Compound pollen grains oblate, 16-polysomic, 70-105 $ \\mu $m in diameter, the central 8 monosomic arranged in cruciform and surrounded by the other 8 monosomic; individual monosomic subsquare or long-spherical. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, 81-447 $ \\mu $m long. Prisms of calcium oxalate frequently occur in parenchymatous cells, 3-21 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly spiral or bordered-pitted. Epidermal cells of filament elongated rectangular, with cuticle striations in surface view (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 4, 1953, 253])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[246, 495, 1744, 2142])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Albiziae Flos\n\n1. Compound pollen grains 2. Non-glandular hairs 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Vessels 5. Epidermal cells of filament\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 26, 1874, 260])"},{"name":"9","text":"Quercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of quercitrin CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"9","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (15:0.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercitrin standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 70^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nNelumbinis Receptaculum\n\n蓮房\n\nCirsii Japonici Herba\n\n大薊\n\n穿山龍 Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\n石上柏\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香 Inulae Radix Polyporus 猪苓\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nAlbiziae Flos\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[377, 368, 913, 936])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structure of quercitrin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[384, 1094, 968, 1898])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Albiziae Flos extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Quercitrin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercitrin (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 15, 1881, 262])"},{"name":"9","text":"Quercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of quercitrin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"9","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>85→60</td><td>15→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"9","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of quercitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of quercitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of quercitrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from quercitrin peak should not be less than 26000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"9","text":"Separately inject quercitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of quercitrin peak in the chromatogram of quercitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify quercitrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of quercitrin Std-FP.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 8, 1947, 258])\n\nThe retention times of quercitrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Albiziae Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Albiziae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.76</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, quercitrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.18</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[392, 1147, 1727, 1925])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Albiziae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"9","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 27, 1874, 262])\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Total ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 24.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/albiziae-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albiziae Flos","la":"Albiziae Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/allii-tuberosi-semen-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Allii Tuberosi Semen v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[833, 887, 1156, 1212])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[632, 1255, 1324, 1789])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[833, 887, 1156, 1212])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[632, 1255, 1324, 1789])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Allii Tuberosi Semen\n\nChinese Name: 韮菜子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jiucaizi"},{"name":"11","text":"Allii Tuberosi Semen is the dried ripe seed of Allium tuberosum Rottl. ex Spreng. (Liliaceae). The infructescence is collected in autumn when the fruit is ripe, dried under the sun, and gathered the seeds, foreign matter removed to obtain Allii Tuberosi Semen."},{"name":"11","text":"Semi-spheroidal to semi-ovoid, slightly flattened, 2-4 mm long, 1.5-3 mm wide. Externally black, one side protuberant, rough, with finely and densely reticulate wrinkles; the other side slightly dented, wrinkles relatively indistinct. Apex obtuse, base slightly acute, with a protuberant and dotted hilum. Texture hard. Odour characteristic; taste slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells slightly flattened with thickened wall, the outer wall covered with cuticle, containing dark brown substances. Parenchyma of testa contains several layers of brownish-yellow cells, underneath the epidermis, arranged in crisscross. Endosperm occupies major portion of the seed, endosperm parenchymatous cells large, arranged densely, filling with aleurone grains and oil droplets. The embryo hook-liked, separately located near the upper and lower end of the seed in the transverse section, embryo cells arranged densely, filling with aleurone grains (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[42, 7, 1965, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-black. Epidermal cells black or brownish-black, elongated stripe-shaped, polygonal, subrounded or irregular, 44-132 $ \\mu $m long, 13-45 $ \\mu $m wide, with reticulate striations. Endosperm cells large, subrounded, walls relatively thickened, filling with aleurone grains and oil droplets. Parenchymatous cells underneath the epidermis, brownish-yellow, arranged in crisscross pattern, relatively large, mostly broken. Embryo cells in groups, rectangular or square (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 451, 1734, 2121])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Allii Tuberosi Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermis, embryo and endosperm cells 1. Epidermis 2. Parenchyma of testa 3. Endosperm 4. Embryo\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nAllii Tuberosi Semen\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[264, 647, 1698, 2120])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Allii Tuberosi Semen (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Epidermal cells 2. Endosperm cells 3. Parenchymatous cells of testa 4. Embryo cells"},{"name":"11","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of linoleic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and acetone (2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linoleic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n黟金 Curcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nAllii Tuberosi Semen\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 364, 909, 776])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of linoleic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 934, 837, 1757])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Allii Tuberosi Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Linoleic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of linoleic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (700 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 7.0 mg of linoleic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>20→100</td><td>80→0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-50</td><td>100</td><td>0</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linoleic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of linoleic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of linoleic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from linoleic acid peak should not be less than 400000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject linoleic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of linoleic acid peak in the chromatogram of linoleic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify linoleic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of linoleic acid Std-FP. The retention times of linoleic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Allii Tuberosi Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Allii Tuberosi Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, linoleic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.08</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 1541, 1811, 2343])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Allii Tuberosi Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[636, 19, 1892, 249])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/allii-tuberosi-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Allii Tuberosi Semen","la":"Allii Tuberosi Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/alpiniae-katsumadai-semen-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[584, 859, 1413, 1909])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen A. Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen B. Magnified seeds C. Magnified longitudinal section of the seed"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[584, 859, 1413, 1909])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen A. Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen B. Magnified seeds C. Magnified longitudinal section of the seed"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen\n\nChinese Name: 草豆蔻\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Caodoukou"},{"name":"13","text":"Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen is the dried almost ripe seed of Alpinia katsumadai Hayata (Zingiberaceae). The fruit is collected in summer and autumn, dried under the sun until almost dry, or dipped into boiling water followed by drying under the sun until semi-dried, then the pericarp removed and the seed masses dried under the sun to obtain Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen."},{"name":"13","text":"Seed masses subspherical, with 3 obvious shallow furrows, 11-29 mm in diameter. Externally greyishbrown to yellowish-brown, with yellowish-white or pale brown septa in the middle dividing the seed mass into 3 groups, each bearing numerous seeds stuck together. Seeds ovoid-polyhedral, 1-4 mm in diameter, covered with pale brown membranous aril; raphe in the form of a longitudinal furrow, with a hilum at one end; when cutting in the longitudinal section, testa extending along the raphe into the inner part occupying about half of the surface area; endosperms greyish-white. Texture hard. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 9, 1971, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Epidermal cells of testa subrounded, aril occasionally found. Hypodermis consists of 1-3 layers of tangentially elongated cells. Pigment layer consists of several layers of cells, boundaries indistinct, with subrounded oil cells scattered. Endotesta consists of 1 layer of palisade sclerenchymatous cells, with heavily thickened inner and lateral walls; lumen small, containing silica bodies. Perisperm cells filled with tiny starch granules, some contain small prisms of calcium oxalate. Endosperm cells contain aleurone grains (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Endotesta cells orange-yellow to yellowish-brown, polygonal or subrounded in surface view, walls thickened, containing silica bodies; palisade-like in lateral view, lumens on one side, containing silica bodies; orange-yellow or brown under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of testa colourless or pale yellow, strip-shaped in surface view; bright yellow under the polarized microscope. Hypodermal cells colourless or pale yellow, usually adhere epidermal cells of testa on the lower layer. Pigment cells yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, with indistinct boundaries; oil cells often scattered in pigment cells, subrounded, 11-59 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Perisperm cells rectangular or polygonal, filling with tiny starch granules, some contain prisms of calcium oxalate of 1-13 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; pale white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[233, 430, 1723, 2126])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Epidermis of testa 2. Hypodermis 3. Pigment layer 4. Oil cell 5. Endotesta 6. Perisperm 7. Endosperm 8. Silica body 9. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\nRosae Laevigatae Fructus\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\n沙苑子 Astragali Complanati Semen\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n髂金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 102, 1591, 163])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nAlpiniae Katsumadai Semen\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[289, 542, 1696, 2005])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Endotesta cells contain silica bodies (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view, silica body\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa 3. Hypodermal cells 4. Pigment cells (oil cells $ \\longrightarrow $)\n\n5. Perisperm cells (prisms of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized light microscope"},{"name":"13","text":"Alnustone standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of alnustone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nAlpinetin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of alpinetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nCardamonin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of cardamonin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nPinocembrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of pinocembrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and methanol (15:4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 5 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately alnustone standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $), alpinetin standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $), cardamonin standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $), pinocembrin standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[550, 96, 718, 156])\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n彆金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1069, 158, 1238, 212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1422, 101, 1666, 199])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nAlpiniae Katsumadai Semen\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[428, 367, 1109, 594])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[434, 662, 939, 993])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[436, 1063, 937, 1398])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[437, 1474, 921, 1780])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) alnustone (ii) alpinetin (iii) cardamonin and (iv) pinocembrin\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[386, 377, 1002, 1311])\n\n1 2 3 4 5\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cardamonin standard solution 2. Pinocembrin standard solution\n\n3. Alpinetin standard solution 4. Alnustone standard solution 5. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of alnustone, alpinetin, cardamonin and pinocembrin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Alnustone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of alnustone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nAlpinetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of alpinetin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nCardamonin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (75 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.75 mg of cardamonin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nPinocembrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (75 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.75 mg of pinocembrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[43, 9, 1961, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (260 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>60→48</td><td>40→52</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>48→0</td><td>52→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of alnustone Std-FP, alpinetin Std-FP, cardamonin Std-FP and pinocembrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of alnustone, alpinetin, cardamonin and pinocembrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of alnustone, alpinetin, cardamonin and pinocembrin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from alnustone, alpinetin, cardamonin and pinocembrin peaks should not be less than 50000, 10000, 10000 and 10000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 7 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject alnustone Std-FP, alpinetin Std-FP, cardamonin Std-FP, pinocembrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of alnustone, alpinetin, cardamonin and pinocembrin peaks in the chromatograms of alnustone Std-FP, alpinetin Std-FP, cardamonin Std-FP, pinocembrin Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 3, 1918, 254])\n\nalnustone, alpinetin, cardamonin and pinocembrin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of alnustone Std-FP, alpinetin Std-FP, cardamonin Std-FP and pinocembrin Std-FP. The retention times of alnustone, alpinetin, cardamonin and pinocembrin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(alpinetin)</td><td>0.49</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, pinocembrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.35</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.39</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr><tr><td>5(cardamonin)</td><td>1.63</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.70</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr><tr><td>7(alnustone)</td><td>2.56</td><td>$\\pm0.11$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[359, 1609, 1839, 2118])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[38, 16, 1957, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/alpiniae-katsumadai-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen","la":"Alpiniae Katsumadai Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/alpiniae-oxyphyllae-fructus-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[580, 953, 978, 1140])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 939, 1398, 1124])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[582, 1198, 1460, 1843])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus A. Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus B. Transverse section of fruits showing seeds divided by septa into groups of seeds in 3 valves C. A group of seeds and separated seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 7, 1893, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[580, 953, 978, 1140])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 939, 1398, 1124])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[582, 1198, 1460, 1843])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus A. Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus B. Transverse section of fruits showing seeds divided by septa into groups of seeds in 3 valves C. A group of seeds and separated seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 7, 1893, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 益智\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yizhi"},{"name":"12","text":"Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Alpinia oxyphylla Miq. (Zingiberaceae). The fruit is collected in summer and autumn when ripe, then dried under the sun to obtain Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Ellipsoid, both ends slightly acute, 0.7-2.2 cm long, 4-14 mm in diameter. Externally yellowishbrown to reddish-brown to greyish-brown, with 13-20 uneven and protuberant longitudinal lines on the surface, with remnants of perianth on the apex and fruit stalk at the base. Pericarp thin and slightly tough, adhering closely to the seed. Seeds gathered in groups and divided to 3 valves by septa, 6-11 seeds in each valve. Individual seeds irregularly oblate, with slightly obtuse ribs, about 3 mm in diameter, greyish-brown to greyish-yellow externally, covered with pale brown membranous aril. Texture hard, endosperm white. Odour distinctly aromatic; taste pungent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Pericarp: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of subrectangular epidermal cells, covered with cuticle. Mesocarp consists of rectangular or tangentially elongated parenchymatous cells. Parenchymatous cells contain oil cells, vascular bundles scattered, with a fibre bundle outside the phloem. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of tangentially elongated parenchymatous cells. Septum cells relatively thin-walled [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 7, 1974, 263])\n\nSeed: Aril occasionally found, consisting of several layers of parenchymatous cells. Epidermal cells of testa subrounded, subsquare or rectangular, slightly radially elongated, with relatively thick walls. Hypodermis consists of 1 layer of parenchymatous cells with yellowish-brown contents. Oil cell layer consists of 1 layer of oil cells, cells subsquare or rectangular, with yellow oil droplets. Pigment layer consists of several layers of yellowish-brown cells, scattered with 1-3 layers of relatively large subrounded oil cells, with yellow oil droplets. Endotesta consists of 1 layer of palisade sclerenchymatous cells, yellowish-brown or reddish-brown, with heavily thickened inner and lateral walls, lumen small, containing silica bodies. Perisperm cells filled with starch granules and with scattered prisms of calcium oxalate. Endosperm cells contain aleurone grains [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Epidermal cells of testa strip-shaped in surface view, up to 29 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls slightly thickened, often vertically arranged with hypodermal cells. Hypodermal cells subrectangular or irregular, containing pale yellow or yellowish-brown contents. Oil cells scattered among pigment layer cells, subsquare or rectangular. Cells of pigment layer wrinkled, border indistinct, containing reddish-brown and dark brown contents, most cells broken into irregular fragments of pigment. Sclerenchymatous cells of endotesta yellowish-brown or brown, palisade-like cells appear in 1 layer in lateral view, inner and lateral walls extremely thickened, lumens outward eccentric, containing silica bodies; polygonal in surface view, walls thickened and unlignified, containing silica bodies. Perisperm cells filled with starch masses, aggregated from fine starch granules. Prisms of calcium oxalate 0.6-15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bright yellowish-white under polarized microscope. Oil cells of pericarp yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, subrounded. Fibres slender, 7-42 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls thickened, pits distinct. Vessels mainly scalariform, 6-46 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 497, 1737, 2135])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle 1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Fibre bundle 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Endocarp 7. Septum\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夥金 Curcumae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1070, 158, 1237, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 101, 1591, 163])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 401, 1739, 2127])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate in perisperm 1. Aril 2. Epidermis of testa 3. Hypodermis 4. Oil cell layer 5. Pigment layer 6. Endotesta 7. Perisperm 8. Prisms of calcium oxalate 9. Endosperm\n\nDryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\n綿馬貫眾\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\n益智\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\n芫花\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\nCentellae Herba\n\n積雪草\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[322, 518, 737, 856])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[789, 519, 1208, 853])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1247, 523, 1654, 850])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[323, 899, 738, 1219])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[791, 899, 1208, 1222])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1241, 900, 1654, 1226])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[326, 1266, 734, 1592])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[792, 1263, 1207, 1595])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1242, 1263, 1655, 1597])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[323, 1647, 736, 1978])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[793, 1647, 1210, 1977])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1243, 1647, 1654, 1981])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Epidermal cells of testa 2. Hypodermal cells 3. Oil cells 4. Pigment layer cells\n\n5. Sclerenchymatous cells of endotesta (silica bodies (5-1 in lateral view, 5-2 in surface view)\n\n6. Perisperm cells with starch masses 7. Prisms of calcium oxalate 8. Oil cells of pericarp (    ）\n\n9. Fibres of pericarp 10. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 9, 1967, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Nootkatone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of nootkatone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (3:2, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately nootkatone standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[387, 1754, 771, 1960])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of nootkatone\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 370, 814, 1225])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Nootkatone standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of nootkatone (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Nootkatone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of nootkatone CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 12.5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 40 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Wash the residue with 5 mL of methanol. Combine the solutions. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 8, 1969, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>36</td><td>64</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-60</td><td>36→30</td><td>64→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of nootkatone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of nootkatone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of nootkatone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from nootkatone peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject nootkatone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of nootkatone peak in the chromatogram of nootkatone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify nootkatone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of nootkatone Std-FP. The retention times of nootkatone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 7, 1898, 255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, nootkatone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[381, 885, 1788, 1368])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[38, 15, 1957, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/alpiniae-oxyphyllae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus","la":"Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/amomi-fructus-rotundus-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amomi Fructus Rotundus v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[446, 1136, 716, 1398])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[734, 1151, 944, 1390])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[464, 1443, 697, 1661])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[730, 1435, 949, 1651])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[978, 1163, 1206, 1584])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1290, 1192, 1484, 1593])\n\nB 2mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\nA. Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\nB. Transverse section of fruit and magnified image of seeds"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[446, 1136, 716, 1398])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[734, 1151, 944, 1390])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[464, 1443, 697, 1661])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[730, 1435, 949, 1651])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[978, 1163, 1206, 1584])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1290, 1192, 1484, 1593])\n\nB 2mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\nA. Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\nB. Transverse section of fruit and magnified image of seeds"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\nChinese Name: 豆蔻\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Doukou"},{"name":"14","text":"Amomi Fructus Rotundus is the dried ripe fruit of Amomum compactum Soland ex Maton (Zingiberaceae). The fruit-spike is collected mostly in July and August when the fruit is nearly ripe and turns yellow but indehiscent, persistent perianth and fruit stalk removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Amomi Fructus Rotundus."},{"name":"14","text":"Subspherical, 8-15 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to yellowish-brown, sometimes slightly showing purplish-brown, with 3 relatively deep longitudinal furrows, apex with a prominent stylopodium, base with a fruit stalk scar, both ends bearing pale brown pubescence. Texture of pericarp fragile and light in weight, easily broken longitudinally, divided into 3 loculi, each containing 4-10 seeds. Seeds irregularly polyhedral, dorsal surface slightly protuberant, 3-4 mm in diameter, externally greyish-brown or dark brown, with wrinkles, covered with whitish membranous aril. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and cool, slightly camphor-like (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Aril sometimes remnant, consisting of serveral layers of slender parenchymatous cells, some containing crystal sand of calcium oxalate. Epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of cells, cells radially elongated, covered with cuticle. Hypodermis of testa consists 1-2 layers of cells, containing yellowish-brown or reddish-brown contents (before stained). Oil cells layer consists of 1 layer of subrectangular cells, 40-75 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with thin walls. Pigment layer consists several layers of cells, containing dark reddish-brown contents (before stained). Endotesta consists 1 layer of cells, reddish-brown or yellowish-brown (before stained), 10-35 $ \\mu $m in\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[44, 35, 1932, 263])\n\ndiameter, with extremely thick inner and side walls, lumen located on the upper part of the cells, containing silica bodies. Perisperm cells filled with small starch granules, some containing prisms of calcium oxalate. Endosperm and parenchymatous cells of embryo contain aleurone granules and oil droplets (before stained). Hilum position located on cupped side of the seed, cells contain reddish-brown contents (before stained) (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of flattened rectangular parenchymatous cells, 25-60 $ \\mu m $ long, about 8 $ \\mu m $ wide. Parenchymatous cells of mesocarp subrounded to oblong. Collateral vascular bundles located on the inner side of mesocarp; crescent-shaped fibre bundles located on the outer side of vascular bundles. 1-4 layers of stone cells arranged in an interrupted band, located in between vascular bundles, stone cells subrounded to subsquare, with distinct pits. Parenchymatous cells scattered with prisms of calcium oxalate. Endocarp consists 1 layer of rectangular parenchymatous cells, mostly shrunken (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour pale brown or greyish-brown. Stone cells varied in shape, 35-150 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, pits distinct, lumens relatively large. Endotesta cells yellowish-brown or reddish-brown, polygonal in surface view, 10-35 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, containing silica bodies; 1 layer of palisade-like cells in lateral view, with extremely thick inner and side walls, lumens located on the upper part of the cells, containing silica bodies. Epidermal cells of testa pale yellow, long stripe-shaped in surface view, walls slightly thickened, usually arranged vertically with yellowish-brown hypodermal cells. Perisperm cells subrectangular, containing small starch masses, usually with prisms of calcium oxalate; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Oil cells nearly colourless to pale yellow, subsquare, 40-75 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered, 2-20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Aril cells with thin walls, the wall slightly curved, some cells contain crystal sand of calcium oxalate. Fibres mostly broken, 15-25 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, lumens distinct (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 414, 1754, 2107])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of longitudinal section of seed of Amomi Fructus Rotundus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Aril 2. Epidermis of testa 3. Hypodermis of testa 4. Oil cells layer\n\n5. Pigment layer 6. Endotesta 7. Perisperm 8. Endosperm 9. Embryo\n\n10. Prism of calcium oxalate 11. Hilum\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1295, 69, 1443, 118])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 467, 1712, 2131])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate in mesocarp\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Fibre bundle 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Stone cell 6. Endocarp 7. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[238, 462, 1689, 2110])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Microscopic features of powder of Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Endotesta cells with silica bodies (2-1 in surface view, 2-2 in lateral view)\n\n3. Epidermal cells of testa 4. Perisperm cells 5. Oil cells\n\n6. Prisms of calcium oxalate 7. Aril cells 8. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 40, 1940, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"1,8-Cineole standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of 1,8-cineole CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (19:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 5 g of vanillin and dissolve in 100 mL of sulphuric acid."},{"name":"14","text":"Kieselguhr is added to the sample before powdering in the ratio of 1:2. Weigh 1.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (320 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 1,8-cineole standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1267, 193, 1388, 241])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 368, 632, 612])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 Chemical structure of 1,8-cineole\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 778, 830, 1603])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Amomi Fructus Rotundus extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. 1,8-Cineole standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of 1,8-cineole (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"1,8-Cineole standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 10.0 mg of 1,8-cineole CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of n-hexane."},{"name":"14","text":"Kieselguhr is added to the sample before powdering in the ratio of 1:2. Weigh 1.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 500-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 200 mL of water. Connect the round-bottomed flask to a volatile oil determination tube, then add 20 mL of water and 3 mL of n-hexane. Connect to the condenser and heat the flask gently until boiling for 2 h. Cool down and allow to stand until two layers can be separated. Transfer the n-hexane layer to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Transfer the aqueous layer to a 100-mL separating funnel. Rinse the volatile oil determination tube with 5 mL of n-hexane. Transfer the solution to the separating funnel and extract with aqueous layer. Collect the n-hexane extract and filter through the funnel containing 1 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate. Transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Extract the aqueous layer with 5 mL of n-hexane. Collect the n-hexane extract and filter through the funnel containing 1 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate. Transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the filter funnel with 5 mL of n-hexane. Combine the n-hexane extracts and make up to the mark with n-hexane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (DB-1701, $ 0.32 \\mathrm{~mm} \\times 30 \\mathrm{~m} $) of which the internal wall is covered with (14%-cyanopropyl-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about $ 0.25 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. The injection temperature is at $ 210^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 230^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 20:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>40</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3-39</td><td>40→220</td><td>5</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 1,8-cineole Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 1,8-cineole should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 1,8-cineole peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 1,8-cineole peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 7)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject 1,8-cineole Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of 1,8-cineole peak in the chromatogram of 1,8-cineole Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 7) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 1,8-cineole peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 1,8-cineole Std-FP. The retention times of 1,8-cineole peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Amomi Fructus Rotundus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Amomi Fructus Rotundus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(α-pinene)</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3(limonene)</td><td>0.96</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,1,8-cineole)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(terpinen-4-ol)</td><td>1.42</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[346, 1553, 1800, 2208])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 7 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Amomi Fructus Rotundus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 7)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 11.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 12.0% Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 5.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/amomi-fructus-rotundus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amomi Fructus Rotundus","la":"Amomi Fructus Rotundus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/amomi-fructus-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amomi Fructus v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[717, 884, 1257, 1352])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[704, 1389, 993, 1604])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1005, 1375, 1270, 1635])\n\n1 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[763, 1705, 880, 1814])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[960, 1685, 1145, 1835])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1156, 1694, 1247, 1823])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n2 mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour.\n\nA. Fruits B. Outer surface of pericarp\n\nC. Inner surface of pericarp D. Seed mass\n\nE. Magnified image of seeds\n\nPotentillae Discoloris Herba翻白草\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n白花蛇舌草\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\n三棱\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\n天花粉\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nSemiagilegiae Radix\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[372, 539, 1642, 1792])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T.L. Wu et Senjen A. Fruits B. Outer surface of pericarp C. Inner surface of pericarp D. Seed mass E. Magnified image of seeds\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[925, 86, 1027, 137])\n\n天冬\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba Hoveniae Semen\n\n連錢草 枳椇子\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[371, 538, 1657, 1806])\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Amomum longiligulare T. L. Wu A. Fruits B. Outer surface of pericarp C. Inner surface of pericarp D. Seed mass E. Magnified image of seeds"},"sections":[{"name":"31","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[717, 884, 1257, 1352])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[704, 1389, 993, 1604])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1005, 1375, 1270, 1635])\n\n1 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[763, 1705, 880, 1814])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[960, 1685, 1145, 1835])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1156, 1694, 1247, 1823])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n2 mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour.\n\nA. Fruits B. Outer surface of pericarp\n\nC. Inner surface of pericarp D. Seed mass\n\nE. Magnified image of seeds\n\nPotentillae Discoloris Herba翻白草\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n白花蛇舌草\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\n三棱\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\n天花粉\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nSemiagilegiae Radix\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[372, 539, 1642, 1792])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T.L. Wu et Senjen A. Fruits B. Outer surface of pericarp C. Inner surface of pericarp D. Seed mass E. Magnified image of seeds\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[925, 86, 1027, 137])\n\n天冬\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba Hoveniae Semen\n\n連錢草 枳椇子\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[371, 538, 1657, 1806])\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Amomum longiligulare T. L. Wu A. Fruits B. Outer surface of pericarp C. Inner surface of pericarp D. Seed mass E. Magnified image of seeds"},{"name":"31","text":"Official Name: Amomi Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 砂仁\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Sharen"},{"name":"31","text":"Amomi Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour., Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen or Amomum longiligulare T. L. Wu (Zingiberaceae). The fruit is collected in summer and autumn when ripe, and then dried under the sun to obtain Amomi Fructus.\n\nPart I Dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. and Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen"},{"name":"31","text":"Amomum villosum Lour.: Ellipsoid or ovoid, slightly 3-ridged, 0.7-2.3 cm long, 5-16 mm in diameter; externally greyish-brown to dark brown, densely covered with spiny protrudings, with remnants of calyx at apex and a fruit stalk often at base. Pericarp thin and soft. Seeds aggregated into an obtusely 3-ridged mass, divided into 3 segments separated by white septa, each segment consists of 3-26 seeds. Seeds irregularly polyhedral, 2-3 mm in diameter; externally brownish-red to dark brown, finely wrinkled, covered with pale brown membranous aril; texture hard, endosperm greyish-white. Odour strongly aromatic; taste pungent, cool and slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nAmomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen: Ellipsoid or ovoid, indistinctly 3-ridged, 0.9-2.5 cm long, 6-20 mm in diameter. Seeds agglutinated into an obtusely 3-ridged mass, divided into 3 segments separated by white septa, each segment consists of 4-26 seeds [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 37, 1947, 255])"},{"name":"31","text":"Pericarp: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of flattened rectangular cells. Mesocarp broad; parenchymatous cells relatively large, subrounded to oblong; vascular bundles collateral, arranged in an interrupted ring in the inner side of mesocarp, surrounded by cresent-shaped fibre bundles; sieve tubes and vessels visible; parenchymatous cells scattered with crystals of calcium oxalate. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of rectangular cells, mostly shrunken [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nSeeds: Aril present sometimes, consisting of several layers of slender cells. Epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of cells, cells radially elongated, slightly thick-walled. Hypodermis consists of 1 layer of cells, containing brown to reddish-brown contents. Oil cell layer consists of 1 layer of subrectangular cells, with thin walls. Pigment layer consists of several layers of cells, cells polygonal, irregularly arranged, containing dark reddish-brown contents. Endotesta consists of 1 layer of small, palisade-like thick-walled cells, small, containing silica bodies, inner and lateral walls heavily thickened. Perisperm cells filled with small starch granules, some contain crystals of calcium oxalate. Endosperm and embryo cells contain fine aleurone grains. Hilum deeply indented [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nAmomum villosum Lour. Var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen: There is no significant difference in features as compared with Amomum villosum Lour. [Fig.2 (iii-iv)]."},{"name":"31","text":"Amomum villosum Lour.: Colour greyish-brown. Endotesta cells yellowish-brown to reddish brown, polygonal in surface view, with thickened and non-lignified walls, lumen contains silica bodies. Epidermal cells of testa pale yellow, elongated in surface view. Hypodermal cells rectangular to oblong, containing brown to reddish-brown contents. Oil cells nearly colourless to pale yellow, easily broken, subsquare, 16-96 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, sometimes with oil droplets. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells, 2-41 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Mainly spiral vessels, 8-41 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Pigment cells usually shrunken, boundaries unclear, containing reddish-brown to dark brown contents. Perisperm cells subrectangular or irregular in shape, filled with starch masses formed by aggregation of small starch granules [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nAmomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen: Oil cells 17-76 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Spiral vessels 11-52 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate 2-46 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 241])\n\n天葵子\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[244, 512, 1687, 2086])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Amomum villosum Lour.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Crystals of calcium oxalate in mesocarp (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Crystals of calcium oxalate in mesocarp (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Crystal of calcium oxalate 4. Vascular bundle 5. Endocarp\n\nBletillae Rhizoma 白及\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[234, 450, 1745, 2115])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Amomum villosum Lour.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Aril 2. Epidermis of testa 3. Hypodermis of testa 4. Oil cell 5. Pigment layer\n\n6. Endotesta (silica body $ \\rightarrow $ ） 7. Perisperm 8. Crystal of calcium oxalate 9. Endosperm\n\n10. Embryo 11. Hilum\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[102, 188, 203, 242])\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[239, 436, 1745, 2113])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Crystals of calcium oxalate in mesocarp (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Crystals of calcium oxalate in mesocarp (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Crystal of calcium oxalate 4. Vascular bundle 5. Endocarp\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[926, 86, 1025, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nAmomi\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[475, 537, 912, 1025])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[446, 1079, 983, 1453])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1008, 419, 1550, 1389])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[442, 1503, 980, 1966])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1005, 1400, 1547, 1721])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1012, 1731, 1565, 2021])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (iv) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Aril 2. Epidermis of testa 3. Hypodermis of testa 4. Oil cell 5. Pigment layer\n\n6. Endotesta (silica body 7. Perisperm 8. Crystal of calcium oxalate 9. Endosperm\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n白花蛇舌草\n\n三棱\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\nSemiaquilegiae Radix\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[250, 485, 1722, 2121])\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour.\n\n1. Endotesta cells with silica bodies (in surface view, silica bodies $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa 3. Hypodermal cells 4. Oil cells (oil droplets $ \\longrightarrow $)\n\n5. Clusters of calcium oxalate 6. Spiral vessels 7. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n8. Pigment 9. Perisperm cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1756, 51, 1911, 109])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nAmomi Fructus\n\n!["},{"name":"31","text":"Bornyl acetate standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.5 mg of bornyl acetate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"31","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (11:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"31","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol and dissolve 5 g of vanillin."},{"name":"31","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"31","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately bornyl acetate standard solution and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[46, 41, 235, 121])\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[374, 377, 748, 770])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[439, 803, 663, 1163])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) bornyl acetate and (ii) camphor\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[377, 1284, 983, 1931])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2(i) 2(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Amomi Fructus extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Bornyl acetate standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour.\n\n(ii) dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of bornyl acetate (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"31","text":"Bornyl acetate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of bornyl acetate CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nCamphor standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of camphor CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"31","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"31","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-1, $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{~ m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{~ m} $) of which the internal wall is covered with dimethylpolysiloxane in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{\\mu m} $ thick. The injection temperature is at $ 2 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 10:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1)\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>70</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>10-12</td><td>70→80</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>12-17</td><td>80</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>17-25</td><td>80→160</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>25-30</td><td>160</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"31","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bornyl acetate Std-FP and camphor Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of bornyl acetate and camphor should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of bornyl acetate and camphor peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from bornyl acetate and camphor peaks should not be less than 2000000 and 300000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])"},{"name":"31","text":"Separately inject bornyl acetate Std-FP, camphor Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of bornyl acetate and camphor peaks in the chromatograms of bornyl acetate Std-FP, camphor Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify bornyl acetate and camphor peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of bornyl acetate Std-FP and camphor Std-FP. The retention times of bornyl acetate and camphor peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. extract and Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. extract and Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(camphor)</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(borneol)</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,bornyl acetate)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(caryophyllene)</td><td>1.12</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[310, 1670, 1803, 2308])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[311, 377, 1808, 1061])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe fruit of Amomum villosum Lour. var. xanthioides T. L. Wu et Senjen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference GC fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"31","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"31","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[45, 36, 1952, 244])"},{"name":"31","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"31","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (C)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/amomi-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amomi Fructus","la":"Amomi Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ampelopsis-radix-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampelopsis Radix v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[510, 890, 1526, 1872])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[510, 890, 1526, 1872])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Ampelopsis Radix\n\nChinese Name: 白蘞\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Bailian"},{"name":"12","text":"Ampelopsis Radix is the dried root tuber of Ampelopsis japonica (Thunb.) Makino (Vitaceae). The root tuber is collected in spring and autumn, stems and rootlets removed, cut into longitudinal segments or oblique slices, then dried under the sun to obtain Ampelopsis Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Mainly longitudinal segments, fusiform, oblong-ellipsoid or subglobularse, 1.5-13.4 cm long, 6-37 mm in diameter. Externally reddish-brown to dark brown, with longitudinal wrinkles, fine transverse striations and transversely elongated lenticels; bark easily fallen off, revealing pale reddish-brown surface. Cut surface whitish to pale reddish-brown, edges usually rolled inwards, with a protuberant ridge in the centre. Texture hard, fragile and light in weight, easily broken and dusting on breaking. Odour slight; taste sweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex consists of parenchymatous cells, scattered with abundant mucilage cells. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered singly or in bundles in mucilage cells. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered mainly in parenchymatous cells in cortex. Phloem narrow, stripped. Cambium in a ring. Xylem vessels scattered sparsely, with few surrounded xylem fibres; mucilage cells with raphides of calcium oxalate scattered. Parenchymatous cells full of starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[56, 7, 1960, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-white to greyish-brown. Starch granules numerous, simple starch granules subrounded, reniform or ovate, 2-44 $ \\mu $m long, 2-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules occasionally found. Raphides of calcium oxalate abundant, scattered or grouped in bundles in mucilage cells, 23-179 $ \\mu $m long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate 11-69 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with broad and large angles; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells yellowish-brown, subrectangular or polygonal in surface view. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, pits often scalariform arranged (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[260, 535, 1713, 2069])\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphides of calcium oxalate in mucilage cell D. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate 3. Cortex 4. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Vessels 9. Starch granules\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[552, 98, 717, 154])\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 102, 1590, 161])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nAmpelopsis Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[312, 457, 1661, 2051])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Ampelopsis Radix\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Cork cells 5. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Gallic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of gallic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (5:5:1, v/v).\n\nStaining reagent\n\nIodine."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of water. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the upper layer to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately gallic acid standard solution (0.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapour chamber for about 3-5 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[551, 96, 717, 156])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1070, 157, 1238, 213])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[443, 368, 998, 737])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[439, 802, 1001, 1172])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[438, 1243, 871, 1559])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) (+)-catechin (ii) (-)-epicatechin and (iii) gallic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 1708, 865, 2350])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ampelopsis Radix extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Gallic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[636, 17, 1894, 247])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of gallic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"(+)-Catechin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of (+)-catechin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol (60%).\n\n(-)-Epicatechin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of (-)-epicatechin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol (60%).\n\nGallic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (2.5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of gallic acid CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol (60%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (60%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (60%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (216 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>96→90</td><td>4→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>90→85</td><td>10→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 7, 1968, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of (+)-catechin Std-FP, (-)-epicatechin Std-FP and gallic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid peaks should not be less than 20000, 100000 and 10000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject (+)-catechin Std-FP, (-)-epicatechin Std-FP, gallic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid peaks in the chromatograms of (+)-catechin Std-FP, (-)-epicatechin Std-FP, gallic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of (+)-catechin Std-FP, (-)-epicatechin Std-FP and gallic acid Std-FP. The retention times of (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Ampelopsis Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Ampelopsis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(gallic acid)</td><td>0.24</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.70</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3[marker，(+)-catechin]</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.12</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5[(-)-epicatechin]</td><td>1.41</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡寅連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[382, 364, 1825, 952])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ampelopsis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.5% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 11.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 10, 1961, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 15.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (20 mg/L for (+)-catechin, 20 mg/L for (-)-epicatechin and 10 mg/L for gallic acid)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of (+)-catechin CRS, 1.0 mg of (-)-epicatechin CRS and 0.5 mg of gallic acid, and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (60%).\n\nMixed (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed (+)-catechin, (-)-epicatechin and gallic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (60%) to produce a series of solutions of 1, 2, 5, 10, 20 mg/L for (+)-catechin, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20 mg/L for (-)-epicatechin and 0.5, 1, 2.5, 5, 10 mg/L for gallic acid."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh accurately 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (60%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (60%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (202 nm for (+)-catechin and (-)-epicatechin; 216 nm for gallic acid) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 3)\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 3 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>96→90</td><td>4→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>90→85</td><td>10→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ampelopsis-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampelopsis Radix","la":"Ampelopsis Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/anemones-raddeanae-rhizoma-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[410, 823, 1538, 1778])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma A. Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[410, 823, 1538, 1778])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma A. Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 兩頭尖\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Liangtoujian"},{"name":"12","text":"Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Anemone raddeana Regel (Ranunculaceae). The rhizome is collected in summer, rootlets removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Long subfusiform, acute and pointed at both ends, both ends sometimes broken, slightly curved and one end somewhat expanded, 1-3 cm long, 2-7 mm in diameter. Externally brown to brownish-black, with fine longitudinal wrinkles, the expanded portion usually possessing 1-3 fin-liked rootlet remnants, occasionally with 3-5 indistinct annulations. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken, fracture slightly even, whitish to greyish-brown, slightly horny. Odour slight; taste bland then slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, tangentially elongated, outer walls thickened. Cortex consists of more than 10 layers of subrounded parenchymatous cells. Vascular bundles collateral, 10 or more, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem cells shrunken. Xylem vessels 6-24. Rays broad. Pith relatively large, consisting of subrounded parenchymatous cells. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Starch granules numerous, simple starch granules subrounded to elliptic, 2-13 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum pointed or cleft-like, striations indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-4 units. Epidermal cells brownish-yellow to reddish-brown, outer walls suberized and thickened, frequently protruding into the cells to be ridge-like or tubercular. Vessels mostly reticulate, spiral or scalariform, bordered-pitted vessels few, 10-38 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Parenchymatous cells subrounded (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 412, 1734, 2077])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermis\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Xylem 5. Pith 6. Starch granules\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[242, 596, 1725, 2121])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Epidermal cells 3. Vessels 4. Parenchymatous cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Raddeanin A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of raddeanin A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, dichloromethane, methanol and water (4:3:2:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately raddeanin A standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[370, 1800, 1123, 2437])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of raddeanin A\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 370, 841, 1097])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Raddeanin A standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of raddeanin A (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Raddeanin A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of raddeanin A CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (95%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (206 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>85→40</td><td>15→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of raddeanin A Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of raddeanin A should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of raddeanin A peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from raddeanin A peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject raddeanin A Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of raddeanin A peak in the chromatogram of raddeanin A Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify raddeanin A peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of raddeanin A Std-FP. The retention times of raddeanin A peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.26</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,raddeaninA)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[343, 365, 1803, 1333])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\nNote: no water-soluble extractives is proposed due to the high viscosity of water-soluble extracts."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Raddeanin A standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of raddeanin A CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol.\n\nRaddeanin A standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the raddeanin A Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 5,100,200,300,400 mg/L for raddeanin A."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/anemones-raddeanae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma","la":"Anemones Raddeanae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/angelicae-dahuricae-radix-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Angelicae Dahuricae Radix v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[455, 1168, 1111, 1651])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1162, 1020, 1394, 1239])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1162, 1245, 1401, 1550])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1151, 1573, 1405, 1791])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.\n\nA. Root B. The section at the thicker end of root\n\nC. Magnified image of the section at the thicker end of root\n\nD. The section at the thinner end of root\n\n1. Cambium ring 2. Oil dots\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[369, 516, 1649, 1790])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried root of Angelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen A. Root B. The section at the thicker end of root C. Magnified image of the section at the thicker end of root D. The section at the thinner end of root\n\n1. Cambium ring 2. Oil dots\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 31, 1905, 254])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[455, 1168, 1111, 1651])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1162, 1020, 1394, 1239])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1162, 1245, 1401, 1550])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1151, 1573, 1405, 1791])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.\n\nA. Root B. The section at the thicker end of root\n\nC. Magnified image of the section at the thicker end of root\n\nD. The section at the thinner end of root\n\n1. Cambium ring 2. Oil dots\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[369, 516, 1649, 1790])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried root of Angelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen A. Root B. The section at the thicker end of root C. Magnified image of the section at the thicker end of root D. The section at the thinner end of root\n\n1. Cambium ring 2. Oil dots\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 31, 1905, 254])"},{"name":"17","text":"Official name: Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nChinese name: 白芷\n\nChinese phonetic name: Baizhi"},{"name":"17","text":"Angelicae Dahuricae Radix is the dried root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav. or Angelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen (Apiaceae). The root is collected in summer and autumn when leaves turn yellow, freed from rootlets and soil, dried under the sun to obtain Angelicae Dahuricae Radix."},{"name":"17","text":"Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.: Long-conical, 7-24.5 cm in length, 10-52 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to greyish-brown, root stock subrounded, with longitudinal wrinkles, rootlet scars and lenticel-like transverse protrudings. Apex with dented stem scars. Texture compact. Fracture surface greyish-white and powderly, cambium ring brown, subrounded, scattered with many brown oil dots in bark. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and bitterish [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nAngelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen: 7.5-21 cm in length, 12-45 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown, root stock subquadrangular, lenticel-like transverse protrudings and some arranged in four longitudinal rows. Fracture surface white and powderly, cambium subsquare [Fig. 1(ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[34, 25, 1936, 259])"},{"name":"17","text":"Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.: Cork consists of 5-10 layers of cells, cork cells rectangular or polygonal. Oil ducts scattered in the cortex and phloem. Phloem with clefts in the outer part and sieve tubes often compressed, sparsely arranged radially. Phloem rays consist of 2-3 rows of cells. Cambium ring circular or slightly square. Xylem occupies 1/3-1/2 of the root, and vessels arranged radially or sparse and scattered. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (before staining process). Microcrystals of calcium oxalate quasi circular or round cluster, translucent, found in cork cells and parenchymatous cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nAngelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen: Cambium ring slightly square, more rays, xylem occupies about 1/2 of the root, and vessels sparse and scattered [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.: Colour yellowish-white. Starch granules numerous, simple starch granule subrounded, polygonal, oval or helmet shaped, 3-25 $ \\mu m $ in diameter with punctate, crevicular, cruciform, trigeminal, stellate or herringbone hilum; compound starch granules mostly composed of 2-12 units. Reticulate and spiral vessels 10-85 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Cork cells polygonal or oblong, yellowish-brown. Oil ducts mostly broken, containing yellowish-brown secretions. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate tiny, polygonal or subrounded; polychromatic under the polarized microscope [Fig. 3(i)].\n\nAngelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen: There is no significant difference in features as compared with the root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav. [Fig. 3(ii)].\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦木仁 菊花\n\nAngelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[466, 498, 1030, 998])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[460, 1015, 1028, 1452])\n\n20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[333, 1581, 699, 1860])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1106, 492, 1703, 873])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[714, 1584, 1076, 1861])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1106, 886, 1704, 1240])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1107, 1253, 1706, 1627])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1109, 1640, 1703, 2006])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 $ \\overline{\\mu\\mathrm{m}} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil duct (Phloem)\n\nD. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Oil duct (Cortex) 3. Cleft 4. Oil duct (Phloem) 5. Phloem 6. Ray\n\n7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Mircocrystal of calcium oxalate\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[442, 520, 1031, 1010])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[442, 1075, 975, 1480])\n\n20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1080, 491, 1703, 877])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[310, 1601, 1069, 1977])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1079, 895, 1703, 1263])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1081, 1280, 1707, 1655])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1082, 1668, 1639, 2055])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Angelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil duct (Phloem) D. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\nE. Crystals of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Oil duct (Cortex) 3. Cleft 4. Oil duct (Phloem) 5. Phloem 6. Ray\n\n7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Mircocrystal of calcium oxalate\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[235, 436, 1757, 2135])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Vessels (2-1 spiral vessels, 2-2 reticulate vessels) 3. Cork cells\n\n4. Oil duct (4-1 oil duct, 4-2 fragments of oil duct) 5. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[243, 435, 1752, 2127])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Angelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Vessels (2-1 spiral vessels, 2-2 reticulate vessels) 3. Cork cells\n\n4. Oil duct (4-1 oil duct, 4-2 fragments of oil duct) 5. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[47, 28, 1939, 252])"},{"name":"17","text":"Imperatorin standard solution\n\nWeigh 13 mg of imperatorin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nIsoimperatorin standard solution\n\nWeigh 13 mg of isoimperatorin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and diethyl ether (3:2, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately imperatorin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) , isoimperatorin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤 Sinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum苦杏仁\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[378, 437, 743, 725])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[381, 834, 740, 1120])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) imperatorin and (ii) isoimperatorin\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[379, 380, 908, 1161])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3(i) 3(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Angelicae Dahuricae Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Imperatorin standard solution\n\n2. Isoimperatorin standard solution\n\n3. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav.\n\n(ii) dried root of Angelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of imperatorin and isoimperatorin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[43, 29, 1943, 253])"},{"name":"17","text":"Imperatorin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of imperatorin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nIsoimperatorin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of isoimperatorin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (70%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"17","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>70→60</td><td>30→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-17</td><td>60→20</td><td>40→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>17-20</td><td>20</td><td>80</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of imperatorin Std-FP and isoimperatorin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of imperatorin and isoimperatorin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of imperatorin and isoimperatorin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from imperatorin and isoimperatorin peaks should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Separately inject imperatorin Std-FP, isoimperatorin Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of imperatorin and isoimperatorin peaks in the chromatograms of imperatorin Std-FP, isoimperatorin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify imperatorin and isoimperatorin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of imperatorin Std-FP and isoimperatorin Std-FP. The retention times of imperatorin and isoimperatorin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Angelicae Dahuricae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Angelicae Dahuricae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.22</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, imperatorin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(isoimperatorin)</td><td>1.08</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum苦杏仁\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[331, 367, 1823, 931])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Angelica dahurica (Hoffm.) Benth. & Hook.f. ex Franch. & Sav. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[330, 1144, 1822, 1715])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Angelica dahurica var. formosana (Boissieu) Yen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[48, 35, 1938, 251])"},{"name":"17","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.2%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.3%."},{"name":"17","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 14%."},{"name":"17","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"17","text":"Mixed imperatorin and isoimperatorin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (2000 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 20 mg of imperatorin CRS and 20 mg of isoimperatorin CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nMixed imperatorin and isoimperatorin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed imperatorin and isoimperatorin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol ( 70% ) to produce a series of solutions of 0.4, 10, 30, 50, 100 mg/L for both imperatorin and isoimperatorin.\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[46, 16, 1936, 256])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/angelicae-dahuricae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Angelicae Dahuricae Radix","la":"Angelicae Dahuricae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/apocyni-veneti-folium-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Apocyni Veneti Folium v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# B\n\n1 cm\n\nC\n\n1 cm\n\nA\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Apocyni Veneti Folium\n\nA. Apocyni Veneti Folium B. Upper part of the leaf C. Lower part of the leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[11, 2, 1910, 265])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# B\n\n1 cm\n\nC\n\n1 cm\n\nA\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Apocyni Veneti Folium\n\nA. Apocyni Veneti Folium B. Upper part of the leaf C. Lower part of the leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[11, 2, 1910, 265])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Apocyni Veneti Folium\n\nChinese Name: 羅布麻葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Luobumaye"},{"name":"14","text":"Apocyni Veneti Folium is the dried leaf of Apocynum venetum L. (Apocynaceae). The leaf is collected in summer, foreign matters removed, then dried under the sun or in shaded area to obtain Apocyni Veneti Folium."},{"name":"14","text":"Mostly crumpled, some broken; when intact and flattened, lamina elliptic-lanceolate or ovate lanceolate, 1.3-5 cm long, 0.4-2.1 cm wide, pale green or greyish-green. Apex obtuse with small arista, base obtuse or cuneate; margin serrulate, usually recurved, glabrous on both surfaces. Veins prominent at lower surface. Petioles thin, about 1-5 mm long. Texture fragile. Odour slight; taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Upper epidermal cells flattened, with convex outer walls. Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells. Collenchyma located in the upper and lower sides of midrib. Spongy tissue consists of 2-4 layers of cells, containing brown contents. Vascular bundle of midrib bicollateral. Lower epidermis consists 1 layer of rectangular cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour pale green or greyish-green. Lower epidermal cells subpolygonal in surface view, stomata paracytic, easily observed. Upper epidermal cells with convex outer wall. Non-glandular hairs multicellular. Cells of spongy tissue subspherical, containing brown or reddish-brown content. Clusters of calcium oxalate subrounded, 8-29 $ \\mu $m in diameter, mainly with short and obtuse angles; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres mostly in bundles, pale, long. Laticifer contain milky juice. Vessels spiral or annular, 1-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[47, 9, 1914, 262])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[235, 435, 1683, 2177])"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Collenchyma 5. Phloem\n\n6. Xylem 7. Lower epidermis\n\nRubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Apocyni Veneti Folium Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[246, 681, 1717, 2180])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Apocyni Veneti Folium\n\n1. Lower epidermal cells with stomata 2. Upper epidermal cells with palisade tissue\n\n3. Non-glandular hair 4. Spongy tissue 5. Clusters of calcium oxalate 6. Fibres\n\n7. Laticifer 8. Spiral vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 5, 1910, 260])"},{"name":"14","text":"Hyperoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.5 mg of hyperoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nIsoquercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of isoquercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, butanone, formic acid and water (10:2:2:0.9, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hyperoside standard solution (0.3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), isoquercitrin standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[69, 9, 1967, 260])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of hyperoside and isoquercitrin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[434, 607, 898, 1096])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[434, 1167, 899, 1651])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hyperoside and (ii) isoquercitrin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 3, 1904, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"Hyperoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of hyperoside CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nIsoquercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of isoquercitrin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (360 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>12-17</td><td>88-83</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-23</td><td>17</td><td>83</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>23-35</td><td>17-20</td><td>83-80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-45</td><td>20-30</td><td>80-70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-50</td><td>30-70</td><td>70-30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hyperoside Std-FP and isoquercitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of hyperoside and isoquercitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of hyperoside and isoquercitrin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from hyperoside and isoquercitrin peaks should not be less than 13000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 9, 1968, 260])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject hyperoside Std-FP, isoquercitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of hyperoside and isoquercitrin peaks in the chromatograms of hyperoside Std-FP, isoquercitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hyperoside and isoquercitrin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of hyperoside Std-FP and isoquercitrin Std-FP. The retention times of hyperoside and isoquercitrin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Apocyni Veneti Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Apocyni Veneti Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(hyperoside)</td><td>0.95</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,isoquercitrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.20</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.58</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.82</td><td>$\\pm0.05$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[36, 100, 209, 201])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[296, 369, 1827, 1043])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Apocyni Veneti Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 12.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[65, 13, 1959, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/apocyni-veneti-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Apocyni Veneti Folium","la":"Apocyni Veneti Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arctii-fructus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arctii Fructus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[474, 875, 1503, 1761])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[474, 875, 1503, 1761])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 256])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Arctii Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 牛蒡子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Niubangzi"},{"name":"11","text":"Arctii Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Arctium lappa L. (Asteraceae). The infructescence is collected in autumn when fruit is ripe, and dried under the sun; the fruit is tapped out, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun again to obtain Arctii Fructus."},{"name":"11","text":"Long-obovoid, slightly flattened, somewhat curved, 5-7 mm long, 2-3 mm wide. Externally greyishbrown, purplish-black mottled, with several longitudinal ribs, usually the 1-2 middle ribs relatively distinct. Summit obtuse-rounded, slightly broad, with a circular ring at the top, and a pointed remain of style in the centre; base slightly narrowed, bearing surface pale in colour. Pericarp relatively hard, cotyledons 2, yellowish-white, oily. Odourless; taste bitter, followed by slight pungency and numbness (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of cells, wall crooked, most of wall cracked. Mesocarp varies in number of layers, up to more than 10 layers, walls slightly thickened and lignified, with small collateral vascular bundles. Prism of calcium oxalate 3-9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, occurring abundantly in mesocarp near the boundary with the endocarp. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of palisadealigned stone cells. Testa consists of several rows of decadent cells, the borders of cell indistinct. Endosperm consists of 1-2 layers of cells. Cotyledon cells filled with aleurone grains, oil droplets, some containing small crystals (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 10, 1984, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Stone cells of endocarp slightly flattened, tapering-fusiform, long-elliptic to tapering-ovate, closely mosaic in surface view, 70-224 $ \\mu $m long, 13-70 $ \\mu $m wide, the walls up to 20 $ \\mu $m thick; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3-9 $ \\mu $m in diameter, occurring abundantly in parenchymatous cells of mesocarp; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Reticulated cells of mesocarp elongated in longitudinal sectional view, their walls have fine and dense crisscross striations. Cells of cotyledon filled with aleurone grains, some containing small crystals and oil droplets. Exocarp consists of 1 layer of cells, wall crooked, most of wall cracked (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Arctiin standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of arctiin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (40:8:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately arctiin standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of arctiin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 0, 1929, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[247, 439, 1751, 2011])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Arctii Fructus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Cotyledon\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Vascular bundle 3. Mesocarp 4. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Endocarp 6. Testa 7. Endosperm 8. Crystal 9. Cotyledon\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 9, 1984, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 396, 1728, 2063])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Arctii Fructus\n\n1. Endocarp stone cells 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Reticulated cells of mesocarp\n\n4. Cotyledon cells 5. Exocarp cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 2, 1934, 258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[447, 402, 1006, 839])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[442, 957, 1097, 1279])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) arctiin and (ii) arctigenin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Arctiin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of arctiin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.4 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>75→48</td><td>25→52</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[13, 7, 1978, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of arctiin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of arctiin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of arctiin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from arctiin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject arctiin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of arctiin peak in the chromatogram of arctiin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify arctiin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of arctiin Std-FP. The retention times of arctiin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Arctii Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Arctii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,arctiin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.17</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(arctigenin)</td><td>2.19</td><td>±0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[45, 2, 1935, 259])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[375, 410, 1839, 1098])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Arctii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 14, 1976, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 11.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 18.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Mixed arctiin and arctigenin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L for arctiin and 50 mg/L for arctigenin)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of arctiin CRS and 0.5 mg of arctigenin CRS (Fig. 4), and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arctii-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arctii Fructus","la":"Arctii Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ardisiae-crenatae-radix-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ardisiae Crenatae Radix v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[386, 825, 1503, 1818])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix A. Ardisiae Crenatae Radix B. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[386, 825, 1503, 1818])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix A. Ardisiae Crenatae Radix B. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Ardisiae Crenatae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 朱砂根\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhushagen"},{"name":"12","text":"Ardisiae Crenatae Radix is the dried root of Ardisia crenata Sims (Myrsinaceae). The root is collected in autumn and winter, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Ardisiae Crenatae Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical, slightly curved, 5-30 cm long, 2-10 mm in diameter, clustered on the slightly swollen rhizomes. Externally greyish-brown to brown, longitudinal wrinkles visible, with transverse or annular cracked traces, bark easily separated from wood. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken, fracture uneven, bark thick, whitish or pink, scattered with purplish-red dots on the outside, known as \"cinnabar dots\", wood yellowish-white. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter, irritating to tongue (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of 3-10 layers of subsquare or subrectangular cells, neatly arranged, some of cork cell inner walls thickened, appearing as stone cells. Cortex consists of 10 to more than 20 layers of subrounded parenchymatous cells, some containing reddish-brown masses, sometimes scattered with stone cells. Endodermis distinct. Pericyclic stone cells arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem narrow. Cambium sometimes visible. Xylem vessels radially arranged into single row, some containing reddish-brown to brown masses. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale brown. Simple starch granules abundant, subrounded, helmet-shaped, elliptic or irregular in shape, 4-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with dotted, cleft-like or V-shaped hilum, striations indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-4 units. Vessels mostly bordered-pitted, 10-45 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Xylem fibres slender, 10-15 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Cork cells subsquare, subrectangular or subpolygonal, walls slightly thick. Stone cells subsquare, subrounded, subrectangular or irregular in shape, 40-120 $ \\mu $m in diameter, pit canals distinct, some with striations, lumens relatively large; bright blue-white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Reddish-brown to brown masses visible (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[735, 185, 854, 230])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1267, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 440, 1719, 2062])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cork cells (thickened inner walls $ \\longrightarrow $ ) D. Section magnified\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell in cortex 3. Reddish-brown masses (cinnabar dots) 4. Cortex\n\n5. Endodermis 6. Pericyclic stone cells 7. Phloem 8. Cambium 9. Xylem\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1295, 69, 1443, 119])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[290, 408, 1692, 2063])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Bordered-pitted vessels 3. Fibres 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Stone cells 6. Brown masses\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Bergenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of bergenin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol (95%) and formic acid (8:0.6:0.4, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to an evaporating dish. Evaporate the solvent to dryness on a water bath. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (70%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately bergenin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[375, 1637, 866, 2054])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of bergenin\n\n</div>\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n山銀花\n\nArdis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 378, 929, 1101])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Bergenin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of bergenin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Bergenin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of bergenin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (272 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>5→40</td><td>95→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bergenin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of bergenin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of bergenin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from bergenin peak should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject bergenin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of bergenin peak in the chromatogram of bergenin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify bergenin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of bergenin Std-FP. The retention times of bergenin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 33, 1939, 263])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.69</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,bergenin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.94</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[335, 849, 1796, 1757])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ardisiae Crenatae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[62, 37, 1920, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 16.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ardisiae-crenatae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ardisiae Crenatae Radix","la":"Ardisiae Crenatae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ardisiae-japonicae-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ardisiae Japonicae Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ardisiae-japonicae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ardisiae Japonicae Herba","la":"Ardisiae Japonicae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arecae-pericarpium-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arecae Pericarpium v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[513, 1082, 1483, 1791])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[513, 1082, 1483, 1791])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Arecae Pericarpium\n\nChinese Name: 大腹皮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Dafupi"},{"name":"12","text":"Arecae Pericarpium is the dried pericarp of Areca catechu L. (Palmae). The unripe fruit is collected in winter to next spring, dried under the sun after boiled, cut longitudinally into two valves; the pericarp is peeled off to obtain Arecae Pericarpium."},{"name":"12","text":"Slightly elliptical or elongated-ovate, gourd-shaped, 3.2-9.1 cm long, 1.6-6.6 cm wide, 2-5 mm thick. Exocarp dark brown to black, with irregular longitudinal wrinkles and prominent transverse striations on the surface; style scars at apex, a fruit stalk and remnants of calyx at the base. Mesocarp fibres present when pericarp stripped off longitudinally. Endocarp dented, brown or dark brown, smooth and hard shell-shaped. Texture hard and light in weight, easily stripped off longitudinally. Odour slight; taste slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Exocarp cells arranged relatively densely, covered with cuticle. Mesocarp relatively thick, parenchymatous cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate; stone cells scattered in mescocarp, subrounded, subrectangular or oblong. Fibre bundles scattered, lignified, scattered with vascular bundles. Endocarp cells irregularly polygonal, with thick wall, lignified (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-white or yellowish-brown. Stone cells subrounded, subrectangular or oblong, 12-76 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, 22-241 $ \\mu m $ long, sometimes clearly laminated. Exocarp cells polygonal or long polygonal. Endocarp cells irregularly polygonal, subrounded or elliptical, wall thick and lignified, pits distinct. Mesocarp fibres in bundles, fine and long, slightly lignified, pits distinct, cells surrounding the fibres containing round-clustered silica bodies. Vessels mainly spiral or scalariform, 4-30 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered singly or in bundles, bright orange, 22-109 $ \\mu m $ long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[29, 4, 1898, 265])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 440, 1729, 2167])"},{"name":"12","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells and raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Stone cells 3. Fibre bundles 4. Mesocarp\n\n5. Raphides of calcium oxalate 6. Vascular bundles 7. Endocarp\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n繢斷 Arecae Pericarpium Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[297, 524, 743, 856])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[775, 524, 1222, 853])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1246, 525, 1688, 854])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[293, 881, 736, 1209])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[768, 887, 1216, 1221])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1241, 884, 1690, 1221])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 1258, 738, 1595])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[769, 1255, 1218, 1591])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1248, 1249, 1687, 1587])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 1628, 735, 1955])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[769, 1623, 1219, 1959])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1250, 1620, 1691, 1960])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Arecae Pericarpium\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Exocarp cells 3. Endocarp cells 4. Mesocarp fibres\n\n5a-1. Spiral vessels 5a-2. Scalariform vessel\n\n6. Scattered raphides of calcium oxalate 7. Raphides of calcium oxalate in bundle\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[28, 7, 1903, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Arecoline hydrobromide standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of arecoline hydrobromide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), methanol and ethyl acetate (0.2:0.4:8.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 45 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately arecoline hydrobromide standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapor chamber for about 1 h until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[67, 10, 1964, 260])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of arecoline hydrobromide.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 611, 674, 905])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of arecoline hydrobromide\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Arecoline hydrobromide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of arecoline hydrobromide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (215 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with strong cation exchanger bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.2% phosphoric acid $ ^{*} $ and acetonitrile (35:65, v/v). The elution time is about 30 min.\n\n*Adjust the pH to 3.8 with ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v)\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 4, 1905, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of arecoline hydrobromide should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of arecoline hydrobromide peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from arecoline hydrobromide peak should not be less than 8500 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of arecoline hydrobromide peak in the chromatogram of arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify arecoline hydrobromide peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP. The retention times of arecoline hydrobromide peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Arecae Pericarpium extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Arecae Pericarpium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.43</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, arecoline hydrobromide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績断 Arecae Pericarpium Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[302, 377, 1702, 997])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Arecae Pericarpium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[27, 6, 1903, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arecae-pericarpium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arecae Pericarpium","la":"Arecae Pericarpium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arecae-semen-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arecae Semen v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[596, 960, 821, 1126])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[844, 917, 1027, 1130])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1134, 937, 1365, 1124])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[566, 1279, 802, 1508])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[866, 1275, 1086, 1487])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1149, 1252, 1397, 1506])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[586, 1524, 836, 1776])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[879, 1527, 1117, 1773])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1173, 1537, 1398, 1777])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Arecae Semen A. Arecae Semen B. Lateral view of Arecae Semen C. Longitudinal section of seed with marble-like striations"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[596, 960, 821, 1126])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[844, 917, 1027, 1130])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1134, 937, 1365, 1124])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[566, 1279, 802, 1508])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[866, 1275, 1086, 1487])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1149, 1252, 1397, 1506])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[586, 1524, 836, 1776])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[879, 1527, 1117, 1773])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1173, 1537, 1398, 1777])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Arecae Semen A. Arecae Semen B. Lateral view of Arecae Semen C. Longitudinal section of seed with marble-like striations"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Arecae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 檳榔\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Binglang"},{"name":"12","text":"Arecae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Areca catechu L. (Arecaceae). The ripe fruit is collected from late spring to early autumn; boiled in water, dried, pericarp removed, and the seed is collected, then dried under the sun to obtain Arecae Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Oblate or conical, 1.1-2.7 cm high, base 15-30 mm in diameter. Externally pale yellowish-brown or pale reddish-brown, with slightly dented reticulate furrows; a rounded concave micropyle located at the centre of the base and accompanying with an obvious scar-shaped hilum by the side. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Fracture showing marble-like striations alternating with brown testa and white endosperm. Odour slight; taste astringent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Outer layers of testa consist of several layers of flattened stone cells, elongated tangentially, containing reddish-brown contents; stone cells vary in shape and size, usually with intercellular space. Inner layers of testa consist of several layers of parenchymatous cells, containing reddishbrown contents, scattered with few vascular bundles. Perisperm relatively narrow, the inner layers of testa and perisperm usually inserted into the endosperm, forming a crisscross tissue. Endosperm cells polygonal, with thickened walls, pits large, containing aleurone grains (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour charred yellow. Endosperm cells extremely numerous, colourless, mostly broken, intact cells irregular polygonal or subsquare, 32-136 $ \\mu $m in diameter, intercellular layers indistinct, walls 5-14 $ \\mu $m thick, pits abundant, subrounded or rectangular-rounded, very large, 4-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Perisperm cells subsquare, subpolygonal or strip-shaped, 32-75 $ \\mu $m in diameter, pit canals distinct, most lumens filled with reddish-brown or dark brown masses; bright white to reddish-brown under the polarized microscope. Vessels spiral or reticulate, occasionally visible, 5-20 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Stone cells of testa subrounded, polygonal, fusiform or irregular, 11-73 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls pale yellowish-brown, 4-16 $ \\mu $m thick, pits few, pits slit-shaped, some lumens filled with reddish-brown masses (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 449, 1735, 2134])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Arecae Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Outer layers of testa 2. Inner layers of testa 3. Vascular bundle\n\n4. Perisperm (crisscross tissue) 5. Endosperm\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 102, 1591, 162])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nArecae Semen\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 404, 1755, 2150])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Arecae Semen\n\n</div>\n\n1. Endosperm cells 2. Perisperm cells 3. Vessels 4. Stone cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Arecaidine hydrochloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of arecaidine hydrochloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nArecoline hydrobromide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of arecoline hydrobromide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (28%, v/v), methanol and dichloromethane (0.5:2:6, v/v).\n\nStaining reagent\n\nIodine."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately arecaidine hydrochloride standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), arecoline hydrobromide standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapor chamber for about 20 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nBuddlejae Flos\n\n密蒙花\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium番瀉葉\n\n髂金 Curcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nArecae Semen\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[437, 368, 821, 754])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[441, 820, 821, 1195])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) arecaidine hydrochloride and (ii) arecoline hydrobromide\n\n</div>\n\n路路通 Liquidambaris Fructus\n\nAllii Tuberosi Semen\n\n韮菜子\n\n益智 荒\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nArecae Semen\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\nCentellae Herba\n\n積雪草\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[382, 372, 905, 1317])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Arecae Semen extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Arecoline hydrobromide standard solution\n\n2. Arecaidine hydrochloride standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of arecaidine and arecoline (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Arecaidine hydrochloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (13 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.65 mg of arecaidine hydrochloride CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nArecoline hydrobromide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of arecoline hydrobromide CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (50%).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[41, 9, 1975, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (50%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with strong cation exchanger bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.2 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.2% phosphoric acid $ ^{*} $ and acetonitrile (35:65, v/v). The elution time is about 35 min.\n\n*Adjust the pH to 3.8 with ammonium hydroxide solution (28%, v/v)"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of arecaidine hydrochloride Std-FP and arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of arecaidine and arecoline should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of arecaidine and arecoline peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from arecaidine and arecoline peaks should not be less than 6000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject arecaidine hydrochloride Std-FP, arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of arecaidine and arecoline peaks in the chromatograms of arecaidine hydrochloride Std-FP, arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify arecaidine and arecoline peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of arecaidine hydrochloride Std-FP and arecoline hydrobromide Std-FP. The retention times of arecaidine and arecoline peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[22, 7, 1938, 256])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Arecae Semen extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Arecae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(arecaidine)</td><td>0.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, arecoline)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[383, 995, 1815, 1481])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Arecae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 12, 1959, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed arecaidine hydrochloride and arecoline hydrobromide standard stock solution, Std-Stock (63.2 mg/L for arecaidine hydrochloride and 304 mg/L for arecoline hydrobromide)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.58 mg of arecaidine hydrochloride CRS and 7.6 mg of arecoline hydrobromide CRS, and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nMixed arecaidine hydrochloride and arecoline hydrobromide standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed arecaidine hydrochloride and arecoline hydrobromide Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.6, 1.3, 3.2, 6.3, 12.6 mg/L for arecaidine hydrochloride and 6.1, 15.2, 30.4, 60.9, 152.2 mg/L for arecoline hydrobromide."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arecae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arecae Semen","la":"Arecae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/armeniacae-semen-amarum-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Armeniacae Semen Amarum v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Armeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[647, 863, 1346, 1547])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[645, 1668, 786, 1761])\n\nB 5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[832, 1577, 952, 1770])\n\nC 5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[976, 1593, 1152, 1769])\n\nD 0.2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1188, 1581, 1329, 1773])\n\nE 5mm"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Armeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[647, 863, 1346, 1547])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[645, 1668, 786, 1761])\n\nB 5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[832, 1577, 952, 1770])\n\nC 5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[976, 1593, 1152, 1769])\n\nD 0.2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1188, 1581, 1329, 1773])\n\nE 5mm"},{"name":"27","text":"A. Seeds\n\nB. Magnified image of basal view of seed (chalaza $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of lateral view of seed (hilum $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nD. Magnified image of surface of seed E. Magnified image of cotyledon\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[378, 556, 1642, 1785])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Prunus sibirica L.\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified image of basal view of seed (chalaza $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of lateral view of seed (hilum $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nD. Magnified image of surface of seed E. Magnified image of cotyledon\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦木仁 菊花\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[378, 559, 1639, 1786])\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Prunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified image of basal view of seed (chalaza $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of lateral view of seed (hilum $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nD. Magnified image of surface of seed E. Magnified image of cotyledon\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[379, 551, 1640, 1785])\n\nFigure 1 (iv) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L.\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified image of basal view of seed (chalaza $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of lateral view of seed (hilum $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nD. Magnified image of surface of seed E. Magnified image of cotyledon\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[37, 30, 1909, 252])"},{"name":"27","text":"Official name: Armeniacae Semen Amarum\n\nChinese name: 苦杏仁\n\nChinese phonetic name: Kuxingren"},{"name":"27","text":"Armeniacae Semen Amarum is the dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim., Prunus sibirica L., Prunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne or Prunus armeniaca L. (Rosaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in summer, the seeds are freed from pulp and shell, dried under the sun to obtain Armeniacae Semen Amarum."},{"name":"27","text":"Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim.: Flattened-conic or flattened-cordate. 0.9-1.9 cm in length, 0.7-1.4 cm in width, 3-7 mm in thickness. Externally yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, with numerous granular protuberance. One end acute, the other end obtuse-rounded, plump and somewhat asymmetric. A short linear hilum occurring at the apex, and an elliptic chalaza at the round end; numerous vascular bundles radiated from the chalaza throughout the seed surface, appearing as dark brown longitudinal irregular furrows. Testa thin, cotyledons 2, white and oily. Odour slight; taste bitter [Fig.1 (i)].\n\nPrunus sibirica L.: mostly flattened-conic in shape. Externally yellow, 0.9-1.8 cm in length, 0.7-1.3 cm in width and 4-7 mm in thickness [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\nPrunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne: Mostly conic, slightly flatter. Externally yellowish-brown to dark brown. Slightly smaller in size, 0.7-1.7 cm in length, 0.6-1.3 cm in width and 4-6 mm in thickness [Fig.1 (iii)].\n\nPrunus armeniaca L.: 0.9-2.1 cm in length, 0.7-1.6 cm in width and 4-7 mm in thickness [Fig. 1 (iv)].\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[43, 27, 1935, 259])"},{"name":"27","text":"Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim.: Epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of parenchymatous cells, scattered with ovoid or trapezoid stone cells. Nutritive layer consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, scattered with vascular bundles. Perisperm consists of 1 layer of shrunken cells. Endosperm consists of 1 to several layers of rectangular or subsquared cells, containing oil droplets (before staining process) and aleurone grains. Cotyledons consist of polygonal parenchymatous cells, containing oil droplets (before staining process), aleurone grains and cluster of calcium oxalate. Cluster of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregated; polychromatic or bright white under the polarized microscope. Primary vascular bundles scattered among cotyledons [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nPrunus sibirica L.: There is no significant difference in features as compared with Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim. [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nPrunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne: There is no significant difference in features as compared with Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim. [Fig. 2 (iii)].\n\nPrunus armeniaca L.: There is no significant difference in features as compared with Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim. [Fig. 2 (iv)].\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 17, 1939, 255])"},{"name":"27","text":"Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim.: Colour yellowish-white. Stone cells subrounded or subsquared in lateral view, 20-97 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length, 20-110 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in width at the base; upper parts tabular, bow-shaped, helmet-shaped or lunate, with very thick striated walls; lower parts a more or less flat, thinner, with ramified channels; rounded to ovoid, or polygonal in basal view, walls thick, with wide channels, large and dense rounded or slit-shaped pits in the bottom of the cell walls; weak yellow or white light under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of testa subrounded to polygonal, scattered with stone cells, single or 2-7 in groups. Endosperm cells rectangular or subsquare, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets. Spiral vessels mostly in single, 3-14 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Cotyledon cells polygonal, containing numerous oil droplets, aleurone grains, small cluster of calcium oxalate and starch granules. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregated; polychromatic or bright white under the polarized microscope. Starch granules rare; simple granules subrounded, hilum punctate; showing black cruciate shape under the polarized microscope [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nPrunus sibirica L.: Stone cells conchoidal, subrounded, or subsquared in lateral view, 21-88 $ \\mu $m in length, 21-110 $ \\mu $m in width at the base; rounded to ovoid, polygonal or fusiform in surface view; usually 2-6 cells in group. Spiral vessels 4-14 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\nPrunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne: Stone cells 30-96 $ \\mu $m in length, 20-105 $ \\mu $m in width at the base; usually 2-8 cells in group. Spiral vessels 5-22 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 3 (iii)].\n\nPrunus armeniaca L.: Stone cells 30-115 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length, 24-107 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in width at the base; usually 2-5 cells in group, the rounded pits small and dense in the upper part cell walls and slit-shaped pits in the lower part. Spiral vessels 6-18 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter [Fig. 3 (iv)].\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[518, 517, 950, 1167])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[965, 442, 1530, 1169])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[513, 1232, 998, 1533])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1012, 1203, 1525, 1539])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[509, 1587, 994, 1896])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1011, 1583, 1526, 1890])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Section magnified (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Cotyledon (under the light microscope)\n\nF. Cotyledon (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Epidermis of testa 3. Nutritive layer 4. Vascular bundle 5. Perisperm\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Primary vascular bundle\n\n---\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n芍木仁 菊花\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[248, 448, 1712, 1994])"},{"name":"27","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Section magnified (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Cotyledon (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\nF. Cotyledon (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Epidermis of testa 3. Nutritive layer 4. Vascular bundle 5. Perisperm\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Primary vascular bundle\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[239, 423, 1747, 2001])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Prunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Section magnified (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Cotyledon (under the light microscope)\n\nF. Cotyledon (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Epidermis of testal 3. Nutritive layerl 4. Vascular bundle 5. Perisperm\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Primary vascular bundle\n\n---\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n芍木仁 菊花\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[239, 393, 1748, 2002])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (iv) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Section magnified (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Cotyledon (under the light microscope)\n\nF. Cotyledon (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Epidermis of testal 3. Nutritive layerl 4. Vascular bundle 5. Perisperm\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Primary vascular bundle\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[239, 415, 1750, 2084])"},{"name":"27","text":"1. Stone cells (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in basal view, 1-3 showing pits)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa (stone cells $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n3. Endosperm cells (oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n4. Spiral vessel 5. Cotyledon cells (oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ ）\n\n6. Clusters of calcium oxalate 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[1289, 53, 1927, 261])\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[419, 431, 1764, 2068])\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Prunus sibirica L.\n\n1. Stone cells (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in basal view, 1-3 showing pits)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa (stone cells $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Endosperm cells (oil droplett $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ ） 4. Spiral vessel\n\n5. Cotyledon cells (oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Clusters of calcium oxalate 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[483, 431, 1515, 2073])"},{"name":"27","text":"1. Stone cells (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in basal view, 1-3 showing pits)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa (stone cells $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n3. Endosperm cells (oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ ） 4. Spiral vessel\n\n5. Cotyledon cells (oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Clusters of calcium oxalate 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦木仁 菊花\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[482, 437, 1512, 2053])\n\nFigure 3 (iv) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L.\n\n1. Stone cells (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in basal view, 1-3 showing pits)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa (stone cells $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n3. Endosperm cells (oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ ） 4. Spiral vessel\n\n5. Cotyledon cells (oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Clusters of calcium oxalate 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[44, 35, 1944, 256])"},{"name":"27","text":"Amygdalin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of amygdalin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"27","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol, dichloromethane and water (8:4.5:3:2, v/v)."},{"name":"27","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"27","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the solution."},{"name":"27","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately amygdalin standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\nCampsis Flos\n\n凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[379, 430, 895, 1004])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[381, 1116, 896, 1520])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[384, 1627, 904, 2030])\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n苦杏仁\n\nChrysanthemi Flos菊花\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=17,bbox=[379, 406, 968, 794])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n( v )\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=17,bbox=[380, 902, 1015, 1301])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) amygdalin (ii) linoleic acid (iii) methyl linoleate (iv) methyl oleate and (v) oleic acid\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n![](page=18,bbox=[381, 375, 1117, 1213])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Armeniacae Semen Amarum extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Amygdalin standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim.\n\n(ii) dried ripe seed of Prunus sibirica L.\n\n(iii) dried ripe seed of Prunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne\n\n(iv) dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of amygdalin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=19,bbox=[50, 27, 1938, 258])"},{"name":"27","text":"Amygdalin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of amygdalin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"27","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the supernatants. Wash the residue with methanol. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol. Pipette 5 mL of the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"27","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (215 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>99</td><td>1</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-22</td><td>99→90</td><td>1→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>22-23</td><td>90→88</td><td>10→12</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>23-24</td><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>24-26</td><td>88→50</td><td>12→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>26-29</td><td>50→10</td><td>50→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>29-31</td><td>10→99</td><td>90→1</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>31-40</td><td>99</td><td>1</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=20,bbox=[43, 21, 1940, 250])"},{"name":"27","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of amygdalin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of amygdalin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of amygdalin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from amygdalin peak should not be less than 160000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)]."},{"name":"27","text":"Separately inject amygdalin Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of amygdalin peak in the chromatogram of amygdalin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify amygdalin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of amygdalin Std-FP. The retention times of amygdalin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Armeniacae Semen Amarum extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Armeniacae Semen Amarum extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.85</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.99</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, amygdalin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum苦杏仁\n\n![](page=21,bbox=[330, 378, 1823, 923])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L. var. ansu Maxim. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=21,bbox=[331, 1161, 1823, 1706])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Prunus sibirica L. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=21,bbox=[332, 1880, 1823, 2432])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Prunus mandshurica (Maxim.) Koehne extract\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n青風藤 Sinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=22,bbox=[333, 403, 1822, 917])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (iv) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Prunus armeniaca L. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)]."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/armeniacae-semen-amarum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Armeniacae Semen Amarum","la":"Armeniacae Semen Amarum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arnebiae-radix-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arnebiae Radix v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[497, 986, 1499, 1772])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[497, 986, 1499, 1772])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Arnebiae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 紫草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zicao"},{"name":"12","text":"Arnebiae Radix is the dried root of Arnebia euchroma (Royle) Johnst. (Boraginaceae). The root is collected in spring or autumn, soil removed, and dried under the sun to obtain Arnebiae Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregularly long-cylindrical, usually twisted, 7-20 cm long, 10-25 mm in diameter. Externally purplishred to purplish-brown; bark lax, striped-lamellar, usually more than 10 overlapped layers and easily exfoliated. Apex of the root sometimes bearing branched remnants of stem. Texture lax, soft and light in weight, easily broken. Fracture uneven, wood relatively small, yellowish-white to yellow. Odour characteristic; taste slightly bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Rhytidome thick, showing striped-lamellar, many layers overlapped, easily exfoliated. Phloem relatively broad, separating by lines of cell that are arranged longitudinally and transversely, forming many large gaps of tissue. Xylem occupies the larger portion of the root, with clefts; xylem vessels mostly arranged radially, the xylem sometimes off centre to one side, in which case, the phloem in the outside nearly degenerated (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[60, 9, 1963, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour dark purplish-red. Suberized cells numerous, polygonal or rounded-polygonal in surface view, walls straight or slightly curved, filling with purplish-red pigment substances, pigment substances gradually dissolved and cells became brown in colour after treatment with a solution of chloral hydrate. Parenchymatous cells relatively numerous, pale brown, mostly filled with purplish-red substances. Vessels mainly reticulate, few bordered-pitted, pale brown or brown, 14-83 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, often connected with brown parenchymatous cells. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, extremely long, straight or slightly curved, mostly broken, 13-56 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, base enlarged into trumpet-like, walls with longitudinal striations, some lumens contain purplish-red pigments (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 403, 1735, 2132])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Arnebiae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Phloem D. Xylem\n\n1. Rhytidome 2. Xylem 3. Phloem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 7, 1983, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[232, 375, 1724, 2137])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Arnebiae Radix (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Suberized cells 2. Parenchymatous cells 3. Vessels 4. Non-glandular hair\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[23, 10, 1927, 250])"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\beta $ -Acetoxyisovalerylalkannin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin CRS (Fig. 4) and place it in a 1-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with petroleum ether $ (6 0-8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}) $ ."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (9:2:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of petroleum ether."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately $ \\beta $ - acetoxyisovalerylalkannin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[551, 98, 717, 155])\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n鬆金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nArnebiae Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 369, 937, 731])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 894, 913, 1713])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Arnebiae Radix extract observed under visible light 1. $ \\beta $ -Acetoxyisovalerylalkannin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of $ \\beta $ - acetoxyisovalerylalkannin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 8, 1927, 252])"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\beta $ -Acetoxyisovalerylalkannin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (140 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.4 mg of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin CRS and place it in a 10-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of acetone. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (516 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.1% formic acid and acetonitrile (30:70, v/v). The elution time is about 30 min."},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin peak should not be less than 16000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin peak in the chromatogram of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin Std-FP. The retention times of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Arnebiae Radix extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Arnebiae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,β-acetoxyisovalerylalkannin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.25</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.67</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[381, 1599, 1841, 2093])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Arnebiae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1454, 137, 1615, 193])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 15.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 2.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\beta $ -Acetoxyisovalerylalkannin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (2000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 4.0 mg of $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin CRS and place it in a 2-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol.\n\n$ \\beta $ -Acetoxyisovalerylalkannin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 6, 30, 60, 140, 460 mg/L for $ \\beta $ -acetoxyisovalerylalkannin.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 9, 1968, 260])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arnebiae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arnebiae Radix","la":"Arnebiae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arsenicum-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arsenicum v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[632, 1002, 1373, 1849])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1927, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"6","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[632, 1002, 1373, 1849])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1927, 255])"},{"name":"6","text":"Official Name: Arsenicum\n\nChinese Name: 砒霜\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Pishuang"},{"name":"6","text":"Arsenicum is an inorganic compound with the formula $ \\mathrm{A s}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3} $ , obtained by chemical synthesis or sublimation of Arsenolite."},{"name":"6","text":"White powder. Extremely toxic. Odourless (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"6","text":"Powder\n\nColour white. Prismatic polyhedron, triangle or irregular flakes (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"6","text":"Chemical test of arsenic salt\n\nWeigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (0.2% , w/v). Heat the mixture on a hot plate for about 5 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer 1 mL of the filtrate to a test tube. Add 2 drops of silver nitrate solution (1.7% , w/v) and mix well. Yellow precipitate can be observed.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 7, 1984, 264])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[561, 585, 1421, 1432])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Arsenicum (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[28, 4, 1930, 256])"},{"name":"6","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI."},{"name":"6","text":"Finely powdered arsenic (III) oxide (0.5 g)."},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"6","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 20) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 20 values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a $ 2\\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0. 0 5^{\\circ} $ when compared to the corresponding 20 values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database."},{"name":"6","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard material and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20 values of the diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the characteristic diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample as listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Arsenicum\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>13.863</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>27.918</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>32.340</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>35.328</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>46.370</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[22, 6, 1976, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[378, 384, 1610, 900])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference XRPD pattern of Arsenicum\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic diffraction peaks (Fig. 3) each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0.2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the values stated in Table 1."},{"name":"6","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XV."},{"name":"6","text":"Iodine titrant\n\nWeigh 12.69 g of iodine and 36.0 g of potassium iodide, place in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Add 3 drops of hydrochloric acid and make up to the mark with water. Filter the solution."},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of starch and dissolve in 5 mL of water. Add the mixture slowly to 100 mL of boiling water with shaking and boil for about 2 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL brown conical flask. Freshly prepare the indicator."},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh accurately 0.075 g of arsenic (III) oxide and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4%, w/v). Warm the mixture on a hot plate at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 10 min. Add 20 mL of water and 1 drop of methyl orange indicator (1%, w/v). Neutralize the solution with sulphuric acid (5%, w/v) until the colour turns pink. Add 2.0 g of sodium hydrogen carbonate and 50 mL of water then followed by 2 mL of starch indicator. Titrate the solution with the iodine titrant until persistent purplish-blue colouration can be observed. Calculate the concentration of the iodine titrant according to the following equation:\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 6, 1927, 254])\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {C} _ {\\mathrm {I o d i n e}} = \\frac {\\mathrm {W} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}} \\times \\mathrm {P} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}} \\times 2 \\times 1 0 0 0}{\\mathrm {V} _ {\\mathrm {I o d i n e}} \\times \\mathrm {M w} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{where} \\quad C_{\\text{Iodine}} = \\text{Molarity of iodine titrant (mol/L)}\n$$\n\n$$\nV _ {\\mathrm {I o d i n e}} = \\mathrm {V o l u m e o f i o d i n e t i t r a n t u s e d (m L)}\n$$\n\n$ \\mathrm{Mw}_{\\mathrm{A s}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}=\\mathrm{M o l e c u l a r w e i g h t o f a r s e n i c (I I I) o x i d e (1 9 7. 8 4 g)} $\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {W} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}} = \\text {W e i g h t o f a r s e n i c (I I I) o x i d e u s e d (g)}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {P} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}} = \\text {Purity of arsenic (III) oxide} (\\%)\n$$"},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh accurately 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4%, w/v). Warm the mixture on a hot plate at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 10 min. Add 20 mL of water and 1 drop of methyl orange indicator (1%, w/v). Neutralize the solution with sulphuric acid (5%, w/v) until the colour turns pink. Add 2.0 g of sodium hydrogen carbonate and 50 mL of water then followed by 2 mL of starch indicator. Titrate the solution with the iodine titrant until persistent purplish-blue colouration can be observed. Measure the volume of the iodine titrant used and calculate the percentage content of arsenic (III) oxide in the sample by using the equation indicated in Appendix XV.\n\nReaction equations for Arsenicum:\n\nBefore end-point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l} \\mathrm {H} _ {3} \\mathrm {A s O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {l}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {H} _ {3} \\mathrm {A s O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {H I} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\\\ \\mathrm {I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {s t a r c h} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {I} _ {2} - \\mathrm {s t a r c h c o m p l e x} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\\\ \\end{array}\n$$\n\nAt end-point:"},{"name":"6","text":"The purity of sample is not less than 99.5%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arsenicum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arsenicum","la":"Arsenicum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arsenolite-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arsenolite v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[649, 863, 1335, 1399])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[635, 1719, 1365, 2389])\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of Arsenolite (White Pishi)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 5, 1927, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"7","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[649, 863, 1335, 1399])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[635, 1719, 1365, 2389])\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of Arsenolite (White Pishi)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 5, 1927, 256])"},{"name":"7","text":"Official Name: Arsenolite\n\nChinese Name: 砒石\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Pishi"},{"name":"7","text":"Arsenolite is a mineral of the arsenic group, containing mainly arsenic trioxide $ \\left( \\mathrm{A s}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3} \\right) $ . It is obtained from natural arsenolite ores or from the oxidation of arsenic sulphides, such as arsenopyrite, realgar or orpiment. From the natural arsenolite ores, foreign matter removed to obtain arsenolite. From the arsenopyrite, realgar or orpiment, the arsenolite is obtained through a combustion procedure to oxidatively remove the sulphide moiety."},{"name":"7","text":"Irregular lumps, varying in size. Classified into \"Red Pishi\" and \"White Pishi\". \"Red Pishi\": pale red, pale yellow or a mixture of red and yellow. \"White Pishi\": greyish-white to white. Slightly transparent or opaque, with glassy lustre or lustreless. Fragile. Odourless (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"7","text":"Powder\n\nColour white to reddish-white. Irregular flakes or polyhedron in shape, translucent (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"7","text":"Chemical test of arsenic salt"},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (0.2% , w/v). Heat the mixture on a hot plate for about 5 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer 1 mL of the filtrate to a test tube. Add 2 drops of silver nitrate solution (1.7% , w/v) and mix well. Yellow precipitate can be observed.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 8, 1977, 263])"},{"name":"7","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI."},{"name":"7","text":"Finely powdered arsenic (III) oxide (0.5 g)."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"7","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 20) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 20 values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a $ 2\\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0. 0 5^{0} $ when compared to the corresponding 20 values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database."},{"name":"7","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard material and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20 values of the diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the characteristic diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample as listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Arsenolite\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>13.850</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>27.911</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>32.335</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>35.322</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>46.367</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[19, 3, 1936, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[411, 459, 1577, 1625])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of powder of Arsenolite (Red Pishi) (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 43, 1984, 88])\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱莲\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[416, 449, 1569, 1604])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of powder of Arsenolite (White Pishi) (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 0, 1932, 256])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 387, 1530, 856])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference XRPD pattern of Arsenolite\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic diffraction peaks (Fig. 3) each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0.2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the values stated in Table 1."},{"name":"7","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XV."},{"name":"7","text":"Iodine titrant\n\nWeigh 12.69 g of iodine and 36.0 g of potassium iodide, place in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Add 3 drops of hydrochloric acid and make up to the mark with water. Filter the solution."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of starch and dissolve in 5 mL of water. Add the mixture slowly to 100 mL of boiling water with shaking and boil for about 2 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL brown conical flask. Freshly prepare the indicator."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh accurately 0.075 g of arsenic (III) oxide and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4%, w/v). Warm the mixture on a hot plate at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 10 min. Add 20 mL of water and 1 drop of methyl orange indicator (1%, w/v). Neutralize the solution with sulphuric acid (5%, w/v) until the colour turns pink. Add 2.0 g of sodium hydrogen carbonate and 50 mL of water then followed by 2 mL of starch indicator. Titrate the solution with the iodine titrant until persistent purplish-blue colouration can be observed. Calculate the concentration of the iodine titrant according to the following equation:\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[23, 14, 1984, 263])\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {C} _ {\\mathrm {I o d i n e}} = \\frac {\\mathrm {W} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}} \\times \\mathrm {P} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}} \\times 2 \\times 1 0 0 0}{\\mathrm {V} _ {\\mathrm {I o d i n e}} \\times \\mathrm {M w} _ {\\mathrm {A s} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3}}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{where} \\quad C_{\\text{Iodine}} = \\text{Molarity of iodine titrant (mol/L)}\n$$\n\n$$\nV _ {\\mathrm {I o d i n e}} = \\mathrm {V o l u m e o f i o d i n e t i t r a n t u s e d (m L)}\n$$\n\n$ \\mathrm{Mw}_{\\mathrm{A s}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}=\\mathrm{M o l e c u l a r w e i g h t o f a r s e n i c (I I I) o x i d e (1 9 7. 8 4 g)} $\n\n$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{A s}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}} $ = Weight of arsenic (III) oxide used (g)\n\n$ \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{A s}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}} $ = Purity of arsenic (III) oxide (%)"},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh accurately 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4%, w/v). Warm the mixture on a hot plate at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 10 min. Add 20 mL of water and 1 drop of methyl orange indicator (1%, w/v). Neutralize the solution with sulphuric acid (5%, w/v) until the colour turns pink. Add 2.0 g of sodium hydrogen carbonate and 50 mL of water then followed by 2 mL of starch indicator. Titrate the solution with the iodine titrant until persistent purplish-blue colouration can be observed. Measure the volume of the iodine titrant used and calculate the percentage content of arsenic (III) oxide in the sample by using the equation indicated in Appendix XV.\n\nReaction equations for Arsenolite:\n\nBefore end-point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l} \\mathrm {H} _ {3} \\mathrm {A s O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {l}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {H} _ {3} \\mathrm {A s O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {H I} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\\\ \\mathrm {I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {s t a r c h} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {I} _ {2} - \\mathrm {s t a r c h c o m p l e x} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\\\ \\end{array}\n$$\n\nAt end-point:"},{"name":"7","text":"The sample contains not less than 96.0% of arsenic (III) oxide $ \\mathrm{(A s_{2} O_{3})} $"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/arsenolite","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arsenolite","la":"Arsenolite"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-annuae-herba-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Annuae Herba v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[499, 845, 1406, 1846])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[499, 845, 1406, 1846])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Artemisiae Annuae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 青蒿\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qinghao"},{"name":"12","text":"Artemisiae Annuae Herba is the dried aerial part of Artemisia annua L. (Asteraceae). The aerial part of herb is collected in autumn during flowering stage, followed by removal of the old stem, then dried under shade to obtain Artemisiae Annuae Herba."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem cylindrical, much branched above, up to 162 cm long, 1-11 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-green to yellowish-brown, with longitudinal ridges. Texture fairly hard, easily broken; broken surface yellowish-white, with pith in the centre. Leaves alternate, dark green to brownishgreen, curled and shriveled, crumble and falling off easily; when intact, leaves are flat, tripinnatipartite, lobes and lobules oblong to oblong-elliptic, pubescent on both surfaces. Capitula globose, numerous, 1-1.5 mm in diameter, golden yellow to greenish-yellow, shortly pedunculate, diverging or drooping, arranged into paniculate inflorescences, brittle, fallen off easily. Odour fragrant and distinctive; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Transverse section of the stem roundish, with approximately 10 subrounded-acute ridges. Epidermal cells rectangle in shape, 1-2 layers, covered with cuticles. Ridge area with irregular or subrounded collenchymatous cells of up to 12 layers. Cortex narrow, composed of 2-3 layers of subrounded to oblong parenchymatous cells. Approximately 13-20 collateral vascular bundles; phloem relatively narrow, with lignified phloem fibres on the outer layer; cambium 1-2 layers; xylem relatively broad. Pith large, comprising about 2/3 of the stem (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[13, 8, 1984, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-green to yellowish-brown. Bract cells rectangle in shape, cell wall slightly sinuous. Anther cells largely dismembered, polygonal, cell wall thick, colourless. Fibrous layer of anther cells villiform on one side, slightly sinuous on the other. Vessel elements mainly spiral, 3-17 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, or bordered-pitted, 8-50 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Style epidermal cells short, villiform, pale brown to reddish-brown, mostly dismembered. Clusters of calcium oxalate small, 2-7 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains numerous, globose to ellipsoid in shape, 14-21 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with 3 apertures. Glandular hair with multiple cell stalk. Stomata of anomocytic type, 15-27 $ \\mu m $ long and 13-21 $ \\mu m $ wide. Fibres bundled or scattered, 6-20 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, thick-walled, cell cavity narrow; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Artemisinin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of artemisinin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and glacial acetic acid (80:20:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mix cautiously 25 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v) into 25 mL of ice-cold glacial acetic acid. Add 2.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde. Add further 50 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 200 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 100 mL of n-hexane. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 50 mL of acetonitrile ( 50% , v/v ). Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[36, 2, 1929, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 433, 1733, 2022])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Artemisiae Annuae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Medullary ray\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Cortex 4. Phloem fibres 5. Phloem 6. Cambium\n\n7. Xylem 8. Pith 9. Medullary ray\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 9, 1984, 262])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[256, 414, 1709, 2103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Artemisiae Annuae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n1. Bract 2. Anther cells 3. Fibrous layer of anther 4. Bordered-pitted vessel 5. Style epidermal cells\n\n6. Clusters of calcium oxalate 7. Pollen grain 8. Spiral vessels 9. Glandular hair 10. Stoma 11. Fibre bundles\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 5, 1926, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately artemisinin standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of artemisinin.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[382, 1106, 711, 1468])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of artemisinin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Artemisinin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of artemisinin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of acetonitrile."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in acetonitrile. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with acetonitrile. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 5, 1984, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5, 0.32 mm $ \\times $ 30 m) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about 0.25 $ \\mu $m thick. The injection temperature is at $ 1 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 8 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ The split injection mode at a ratio of 40:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>150→175</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>25-35</td><td>175</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of artemisinin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of artemisinin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of artemisinin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from artemisinin peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject artemisinin Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of artemisinin peak in the chromatogram of artemisinin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify artemisinin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of artemisinin Std-FP. The retention times of artemisinin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Artemisiae Annuae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 3, 1930, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Artemisiae Annuae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.24</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.69</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(marker, artemisinin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 1028, 1754, 1666])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Artemisiae Annuae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[33, 15, 1976, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%.\n\nAbsolute ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-annuae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Annuae Herba","la":"Artemisiae Annuae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-anomalae-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Anomalae Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-anomalae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Anomalae Herba","la":"Artemisiae Anomalae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-argyi-folium-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Argyi Folium v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[504, 831, 1461, 1930])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Artemisiae Argyi Folium A. Artemisiae Argyi Folium B. Upper surface of laminae C. Lower surface of laminae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 8, 1911, 264])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[504, 831, 1461, 1930])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Artemisiae Argyi Folium A. Artemisiae Argyi Folium B. Upper surface of laminae C. Lower surface of laminae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 8, 1911, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Artemisiae Argyi Folium\n\nChinese Name: 艾葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Aiye"},{"name":"13","text":"Artemisiae Argyi Folium is the dried leaf of Artemisia argyi Lévl. et Vant. (Asteraceae). The leaf is collected in summer before flowering stage, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Artemisiae Argyi Folium."},{"name":"13","text":"Mostly crumpled and broken, with short petiole. Lamina ovate-elliptical when intact and flattened, pinnatipartite, segments elliptical-lanceolate, margin dentate irregular; upper surface greyish-green to dark yellowish-green, sparsely pubescent and glandular-punctate; lower surface densely covered with greyish-white tomentose. Texture soft. Odour delicately aromatic; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticles. Non-glandular hairs and glandular hairs present abundantly on the upper and lower surface of epidermis, non-glandular hairs especially abundant on the lower epidermis. Non-glandular hairs 2 types: T-shaped and uniseriate, both frequently broken. Collenchyma consists of 2-3 layers of cells, underneath the epidermis of the midrib. Palisade tissue and spongy tissue each comprises half of the lamina. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cells, loosely packed. Midvein 1 distinctly protruded, collateral, with sclerenchymatous cells on the upper and lower parts of the vascular bundle. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in mesophyll cells, rosette-aggregated (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greenish-brown. Non-glandular hairs 2 types, numerous, first type T-shaped, with elongated and bent apical cell and 2- to 5-celled stalk, unequal arms 2, 91-422 $ \\mu $ m long; second type uniseriate, 3- to 5-celled, with very long and twisted apical cell, frequently broken. Glandular hairs consist of 4-6 oppositely overlapped cells, without stalk, paramecium-like in surface view, 32-53 $ \\mu $ m long and 19-31 $ \\mu $ m wide. Stomata anomocytic, 21-41 $ \\mu $ m long, 19-30 $ \\mu $ m wide. Oil droplets present in palisade and mesophyll tissues. Clusters of calcium oxalate 3-12 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, rosette-aggregated; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 8, 1914, 261])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 410, 1742, 2105])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Artemisiae Argyi Folium\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. D. Section magnified E. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cuticles 2. Epidermis 3. Collenchyma 4. Palisade tissue\n\n5. Spongy tissue 6. Sclerenchyma 7. Uniseriate non-glandular hair\n\n8. Xylem 9. Phloem 10. Clusters of calcium oxalate 11. T-shaped non-glandular hair\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[291, 538, 1691, 915])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 1044, 1109, 1458])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1142, 1044, 1689, 1460])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[290, 1475, 836, 1890])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[853, 1489, 1433, 1886])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1448, 1483, 1692, 1891])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Artemisiae Argyi Folium\n\n1. T-shaped non-glandular hair 2. Uniseriate non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Stomata 5. Oil droplets (occurring in palisade and mesophyll tissues)\n\n6. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 9, 1911, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Caryophyllene oxide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of caryophyllene oxide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of n-hexane."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane and acetone (20:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Mix cautiously 25 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v) into 25 mL of ice-cold glacial acetic acid. Add 2.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde. Add further 50 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of n-hexane. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and add 0.5 g of activated charcoal to the filtrate. Allow to stand for about 15 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of n-hexane."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately caryophyllene oxide standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of caryophyllene oxide.\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix紫苑\n\n繢断 Artemisiae Argyi Folium Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 368, 783, 609])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[449, 682, 773, 922])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[446, 1003, 605, 1156])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) caryophyllene oxide (ii) trans-caryophyllene and (iii) 1,8-cineole\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Caryophyllene oxide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of caryophyllene oxide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of n-hexane.\n\nTrans-caryophyllene standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of trans-caryophyllene CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of n-hexane.\n\n1,8-Cineole standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of 1,8-cineole CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of n-hexane."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of n-hexane. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with n-hexane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5, 0.32 mm $ \\times $ 30 m) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about 0.25 $ \\mu $m thick. The injection temperature is at $ 2 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 5:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 4, 1905, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>60→210</td><td>5</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of caryophyllene oxide Std-FP, trans-caryophyllene Std-FP and 1,8-cineole Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of caryophyllene oxide, trans-caryophyllene and 1,8-cineole should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of caryophyllene oxide, trans-caryophyllene and 1,8-cineole peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from caryophyllene oxide, trans-caryophyllene and 1,8-cineole peaks should not be less than 1000000, 700000 and 200000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject caryophyllene oxide Std-FP, trans-caryophyllene Std-FP, 1,8-cineole Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of caryophyllene oxide, trans-caryophyllene and 1,8-cineole peaks in the chromatograms of caryophyllene oxide Std-FP, trans-caryophyllene Std-FP, 1,8-cineole Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify caryophyllene oxide, trans-caryophyllene and 1,8-cineole peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of caryophyllene oxide Std-FP, trans-caryophyllene Std-FP and 1,8-cineole Std-FP. The retention times of caryophyllene oxide, trans-caryophyllene and 1,8-cineole peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Artemisiae Argyi Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 6, 1963, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Artemisiae Argyi Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(1,8-cineole)</td><td>0.47</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.66</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,trans-caryophyllene)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(caryophyllene oxide)</td><td>1.20</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.28</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[377, 943, 1802, 1520])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Artemisiae Argyi Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[27, 8, 1898, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 12.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%.\n\nWater-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 22.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-argyi-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Argyi Folium","la":"Artemisiae Argyi Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-scopariae-herba-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Scopariae Herba v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[681, 693, 1338, 1343])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried aerial part of Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit.\n\nA. Aerial part of Herba B. Magnified stem C. Magnified leaf\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[726, 1670, 1248, 1939])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[737, 1951, 1238, 2286])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Artemisia capillaris Thunb.\n\nA. Aerial part of Herba B. Magnified stem C. Magnified leaf"},"sections":[{"name":"18","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[681, 693, 1338, 1343])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried aerial part of Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit.\n\nA. Aerial part of Herba B. Magnified stem C. Magnified leaf\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[726, 1670, 1248, 1939])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[737, 1951, 1238, 2286])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Artemisia capillaris Thunb.\n\nA. Aerial part of Herba B. Magnified stem C. Magnified leaf"},{"name":"18","text":"Official Name: Artemisiae Scopariae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 茵陳\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yinchen"},{"name":"18","text":"Artemisiae Scopariae Herba is the dried aerial part of Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit. or Artemisia capillaris Thunb. (Asteraceae). The aerial part is collected in spring when the seedling is 6-10 cm high, foreign matter and older stem removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Artemisiae Scopariae Herba."},{"name":"18","text":"Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit.: Mostly pieces are rolled into masses, greyish-white or greyish-green, densely covered with soft, white pubescence throughout. Stem thin, small, cylindrical, 1.5-2.5 cm long, 1-2 mm in diameter, with longitudinal striations underneath the white pubescence; texture fragile, easily broken. Leaves petioled, pinnately to tripinnately compound, when intact flattened out, lamina 1.5-3.5 cm long and 1-3 cm wide; segments ovate to oblanceolate, apex acute. Odour delicately aromatic; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nArtemisia capillaris Thunb.: Stem base woody, silky pubescent or glabrescent, 1.5-4.0 cm long and 1-3 mm in diameter. Lamina 1.6-8.2 cm long and 1.0-2.6 cm wide [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Transverse section\n\nStem:\n\nArtemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit.: Non-glandular hair occurring abundantly on the epidermis. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells elliptical to polygonal, with slightly thickened walls. Cortex consists of 8-9 layers of parenchymatous cells. Vascular bundles 12-15, collateral, arranged in an interrupted ring, corresponding to phloem fibres. Resin canals distinct in between\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 11, 1965, 260])\n\ntwo vascular bundles. Pith consists of lots of large rounded parenchymatous cells, occupied nearly 2/3 of the stem [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nArtemisia capillaris Thunb.: Vascular bundles 11-14, arranged in an interrupted ring [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Non-glandular hair occurring abundantly on the surface of the epidermis. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells elliptical to polygon, arranged regularly and fragile. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cells, beneath the epidermal cells or collenchymous cells. Collenchyma consists of 1-2 layers of collenchymatous cells, beneath the epidermis, mostly located at the edges and corners of the petiole. Vascular bundles 3-5, the middle one relative large, 2 collateral at the corner. Resin canal next to the vascular bundle, containing bright yellow resin in it [Fig. 3 (i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit.: Non-glandular hairs occuring abundantly on the epidermis. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, often broken. Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells, beneath the epidermal cells. Spongy tissue presents in the middle, occurring half of the lamina. Resin canal visible right above the vascular bundle. Xylem locates in the upper of the middle vascular of the midvein, phloem in the lower correspondingly [Fig. 4 (i)].\n\nArtemisia capillaris Thunb.: Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells in the upper surface, and 1 layer in the lower surface, arranged relatively loose [Fig. 4 (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit.: Colour greyish-green. Upper epidermal cells of leaf subrectangular, walls relatively smooth, stomata visible. Lower epidermal cells of leaf nearly colourless, walls sinuately curved, stomata visible, anomocytic, 22-36 $ \\mu m $ long, 15-24 $ \\mu m $ wide, residues of non-glandular hairs infrequently visible in epidermis. Spiral vessels in bundles or irregular crossed, colourless to pale yellow, 3-15 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Glandular hairs consist of 4-8 oppositely overlapped cells, without stalk, subrounded to oblong in surface view. Non-glandular hairs T-shaped, slightly V-shaped at the centre with unequal arms, apical cell walls 2-6 $ \\mu m $ thick [Fig. 5 (i)].\n\nArtemisia capillaris Thunb.: Stomata anomocytic, 20-32 $ \\mu $m long and 12-21 $ \\mu $m wide. Vessels spiral, 5-16 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Apical cell walls of non-glandular hairs 2-5 $ \\mu $m thick [Fig. 5 (ii)].\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[271, 554, 873, 1049])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[302, 1141, 870, 1857])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[965, 601, 1684, 1839])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried stem of Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Phloem fibres 5. Phloem\n\n6. Xylem 7. Resin canal 8. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[552, 98, 717, 154])\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n彯金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 102, 1591, 162])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nArtemisiae Scopariae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 410, 1736, 1957])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried stem of Artemisia capillaris Thunb.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Phloem fibres 5. Phloem\n\n6. Xylem 7. Resin canal 8. Pith\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[238, 432, 1708, 2127])\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Collenchyma 4. Non-glandular hair\n\n5. Vascular bundle 6. Resin canal 7. Xylem 8. Phloem\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n鬱金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1070, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1427, 101, 1591, 163])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[236, 411, 1723, 2129])\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried petiole of Artemisia capillaris Thunb.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Collenchyma 4. Non-glandular hair\n\n5. Vascular bundle 6. Resin canal 7. Xylem 8. Phloem\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[234, 413, 1694, 2132])\n\nFigure 4 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried leaf of Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit. A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Resin canal 6. Xylem 7. Phloem\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1427, 102, 1591, 163])\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nArtemisiae Scopariae Herba\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[233, 396, 1706, 2129])\n\nFigure 4 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried leaf of Artemisia capillaris Thunb.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Resin canal 6. Xylem 7. Phloem\n\nGenkwa Flos芫花\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[237, 425, 1701, 2119])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried aerial part of Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Upper epidermal cells with stomata (    )\n\n2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata (    ) and residue of non-glandular hair (    )\n\n3. Spiral vessels 4. Glandular hair 5. Non-glandular hair\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n鬆金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1069, 154, 1236, 209])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1426, 100, 1591, 158])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nArtemisiae Scopariae Herba\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[233, 513, 1693, 2120])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried aerial part of Artemisia capillaris Thunb. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Upper epidermal cells with stomata (    ) 2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata (    )\n\n3. Lower epidermal cells with residue of non-glandular hair (    ) 4. Spiral vessels\n\n5. Glandular hair 6. Non-glandular hair"},{"name":"18","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 6) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butyl acetate, formic acid and water (28:13:10, v/v). Shake well and use the upper layer."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"18","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[443, 1521, 989, 1862])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[443, 1934, 695, 2115])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid and (ii) 4-hydroxyacetophenone\n\n</div>\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[1426, 101, 1592, 162])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nArtemisiae Scopariae Herba\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[375, 377, 892, 1271])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 7 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Artemisiae Scopariae Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(ii) dried aerial part of Artemisia capillaris Thunb.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of chlorogenic acid (Fig. 7)."},{"name":"18","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\n4-Hydroxyacetophenone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 4-hydroxyacetophenone CRS (Fig. 6) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"18","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (275 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The internal diameter of inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>75→55</td><td>25→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-50</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"18","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and 4-hydroxyacetophenone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid and 4-hydroxyacetophenone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid and 4-hydroxyacetophenone peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid and 4-hydroxyacetophenone peaks should not be less than 8500 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 8 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[1071, 156, 1236, 213])"},{"name":"18","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 4-hydroxyacetophenone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid and 4-hydroxyacetophenone peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 4-hydroxyacetophenone Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 8 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid and 4-hydroxyacetophenone peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and 4-hydroxyacetophenone Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid and 4-hydroxyacetophenone peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Artemisiae Scopariae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Artemisiae Scopariae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.79</td><td>$\\pm$0.06</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,4-hydroxyacetophenone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.57</td><td>$\\pm$0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[377, 1714, 1865, 2242])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 8 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried aerial part of Artemisia scoparia Waldst. et Kit. extract\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡寅連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[389, 372, 1856, 880])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 8 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried aerial part of Artemisia capillaris Thunb. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 8 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 5.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 32.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 23.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"18","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 25.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[38, 15, 1961, 263])"},{"name":"18","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/artemisiae-scopariae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisiae Scopariae Herba","la":"Artemisiae Scopariae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/asparagi-radix-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Asparagi Radix v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1 A photograph of Asparagi Radix A. Asparagi Radix B. Magnified image of root tuber C. Magnified image of cross section"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"Figure 1 A photograph of Asparagi Radix A. Asparagi Radix B. Magnified image of root tuber C. Magnified image of cross section"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Asparagi Radix\n\nChinese Name: 天冬\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tiandong"},{"name":"13","text":"Asparagi Radix is the dried root tuber of Asparagus cochinchinensis (Lour.) Merr. (Liliaceae). The root tuber is collected in autumn and winter, washed clean, steamed or boiled until the centre is devoid of core white; the velamen peeled while hot, washed, then bake to dryness at lower than $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 20 hour or dried under the sun to obtain Asparagi Radix."},{"name":"13","text":"Long-fusiform, slightly curved, 5-18 cm long, 5-20 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to yellowish-brown, translucent, smooth or with deep and shallow longitudinal wrinkles, patches of the outer peel of greyish-brown colour occasionally found. Texture hard or tough and sleek, fracture horny, mucilaginous, stele yellowish-white. Odour slight; taste sweet and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Velamen peeled off. Cortex broad, with several mucilage cells which contain raphides of calcium oxalate. Endodermis distinct, with fibre bundles scattered beneath, stone cells occasionally found. Phloem alternately arranged with xylem strands. Xylem mostly arranged in a T shape; vessels sometimes penetrated into the pith. Pith broad, parenchymatous cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[30, 21, 1955, 256])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Raphides of calcium oxalate mostly in bundles or scattered singly; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Lignified parenchymatous cells numerous, usually broken, wall slightly thickened, with distinct pits and pit canals. Fibres scattered or in bundles, walls slightly thickened, with distinct pits and pit canals; white under the polarized microscope. Bordered-pitted vessels visible, 18-109 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Stone cells rare; usually scattered singly, long-elliptical, 80-430 $ \\mu $m long, 36-88 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls 10-35 $ \\mu $m thick, with striations, distinct pits and pit canals; white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nSemiagulagina Radix\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[253, 585, 967, 1331])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1005, 585, 1704, 1466])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[320, 1463, 967, 1833])\n\n50 μm\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1011, 1572, 1298, 1801])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1326, 1571, 1617, 1802])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Asparagi Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) E. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cortex 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Pith\n\nBletillae Rhizoma 白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1025, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[242, 410, 1731, 2123])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Asparagi Radix\n\n1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Lignified parenchymatous cells\n\n3. Fibres 4. Bordered-pitted vessels 5. Stone cell\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"13","text":"Pseudoprotodioscin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of pseudoprotodioscin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (65%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, acetonitrile and tetrahydrofuran (6:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 10-mL centrifuge tube, then add 2.5 mL of methanol (65%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Pipette 1 mL of supernatant to 2 mL of water. Pre-condition a solid-phase extraction (SPE) column containing ODS packing (3 mL, 200 mg) with 3 mL of methanol and then followed by 3 mL of methanol (5%). Load the sample solution to the pre-conditioned SPE column. Add 2 mL of methanol (10%) to the column and discard the eluant. Add 0.5 mL of methanol to the column and collect the eluant."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC RP-18 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4 \\mathrm{s}} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately pseudoprotodioscin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[369, 368, 1559, 951])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of pseudoprotodioscin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 1062, 846, 1940])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Asparagi Radix extract observed under UV light (254 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Pseudoprotodioscin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of pseudoprotodioscin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Pseudoprotodioscin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of pseudoprotodioscin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (65%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (65%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>85</td><td>15</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-25</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-35</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>70→69</td><td>30→31</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of pseudoprotodioscin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of pseudoprotodioscin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of pseudoprotodioscin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from pseudoprotodioscin peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 7 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 35, 1951, 256])"},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject pseudoprotodioscin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of pseudoprotodioscin peak in the chromatogram of pseudoprotodioscin Std-FP and the retention times of the eight characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify pseudoprotodioscin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of pseudoprotodioscin Std-FP. The retention times of pseudoprotodioscin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eight characteristic peaks of Asparagi Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eight characteristic peaks of Asparagi Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.77</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.87</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.97</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7(marker,pseudoprotodioscin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[354, 377, 1809, 1151])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Asparagi Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above eight characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 16.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[46, 37, 1949, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 64.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 61.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"13","text":"Pseudoprotodioscin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of pseudoprotodioscin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (65%).\n\nPseudoprotodioscin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the pseudoprotodioscin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (65%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.5, 5, 10, 25, 50 mg/L for pseudoprotodioscin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/asparagi-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Asparagi Radix","la":"Asparagi Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/asteris-radix-et-rhizoma-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Asteris Radix et Rhizoma v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[830, 880, 1140, 1147])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[523, 1177, 1497, 1761])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Asteris Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1901, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[830, 880, 1140, 1147])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[523, 1177, 1497, 1761])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Asteris Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1901, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Asteris Radix et Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 紫菀\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Ziwan"},{"name":"13","text":"Asteris Radix et Rhizoma is the dried root and rhizome of Aster tataricus L. f. (Asteraceae). The root and rhizome are collected in spring and autumn, knotty rhizome and soil removed and washed, then braided and dried under the sun to obtain Asteris Radix et Rhizoma."},{"name":"13","text":"Rhizome in irregular masses, varying in size, apex with scar remnants of stems and leaves; texture slightly hard. Rhizomes bearing numerous, fascicular rootlets, 3-15 cm long, 1-3 mm in diameter, frequently braided, externally purplish-red to greyish-red, with longitudinal wrinkles; texture flexible. Odour slightly aromatic; taste sweet and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Epidermal cells frequently withered or sometimes sloughing off, containing purplish-red pigments. Hypodermal cells 1 layer, somewhat elongated tangentially, lateral and inner walls slightly thickened, some containing purplish-red pigments. Cortex broad, consisting 19-21 layers of cells, with intercellular spaces. Secretory ducts 4-5, located in the inner side of cortex, near the endodermis, opposite the vascular bundle. Endodermis distinct. Stele small; each phloem bundles arranged alternate with the arc of xylem respectively. Primary xylem tetrarch to polyarch, small vessels arranged closely; Metaxylem vessels up to 40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, arranged irregularly. Pith mainly consists of parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[67, 9, 1975, 259])\n\nRhizome: Cork cells rectangular or subsquare, with slightly thickened wall. Cortex broad, secretory duct subrounded, several scattered in cortex, usually is located near the corresponding phloem. Parenchymatous cells sometimes contain clusters of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale brown to reddish-brown. Inulin fan-shaped or irregular, with weak radial striation; slightly bright blue under the polarized microscope. Hypodermal cells rectangle in surface view, anticlinal wall sinuously zigzag, frequently containing purplish-red pigment. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, spiral or reticulate. Fibres mostly in bundle. Cork cells rectangular, occasionally polygonal or subsquare. Sclerenchymatous cells rectangular or oblong, wall slightly thickened. Clusters of calcium oxalate sometimes visible (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[44, 7, 1911, 262])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 402, 1742, 2153])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermis, hypodermis and cortex D. Secretory duct\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermis 3. Cortex 4. Secretory duct 5. Endodermis\n\n6. Phloem 7. Primary xylem 8. Metaxylem 9. Pith\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizon香附\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[243, 418, 1708, 2155])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory duct\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Secretory duct 4. Clusters of calcium oxalate 5. Phloem\n\n6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[27, 6, 1900, 264])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[263, 457, 1705, 1669])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma\n\n1. Inulin 2. Hypodermal cells with purplish-red pigments 3. Vessels\n\n4. Fibres 5. Cork cells 6. Sclerenchymatous cell 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[68, 13, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Shionone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of shionone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane and acetone (15:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately shionone standard solution and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of shionone.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 5, 1912, 260])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 369, 1149, 713])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of shionone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Shionone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of shionone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (200 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0-2.5 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Flow rate(mL/min)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>70→0</td><td>30→100</td><td>1.0→1.5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-25</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>1.5→2.5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-45</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>2.5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 13, 1965, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of shionone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of shionone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of shionone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from shionone peak should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject shionone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of shionone peak in the chromatogram of shionone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify shionone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of shionone Std-FP. The retention times of shionone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.23</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.51</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.67</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,shionone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[31, 5, 1914, 262])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[297, 366, 1829, 1153])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Asteris Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[66, 13, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 53.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 33.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/asteris-radix-et-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Asteris Radix et Rhizoma","la":"Asteris Radix et Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/astragali-complanati-semen-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Astragali Complanati Semen v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[435, 859, 1550, 1914])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[435, 859, 1550, 1914])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Astragali Complanati Semen\n\nChinese Name: 沙苑子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shayuanzi"},{"name":"11","text":"Astragali Complanati Semen is the dried ripe seed of Astragalus complanatus R. Br. (Fabaceae). The fruit with stalk is collected in late autumn and early winter when ripe but not dehiscent; dried under the sun; the seed is tapped out; foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Astragali Complanati Semen."},{"name":"11","text":"Reniform but slightly flattened, 2-2.5 mm long, 1.5-2 mm wide, about 1 mm thick. Externally smooth, brownish-green or greyish-brown, the rounded hilum located on the slightly dented edge. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Cotyledons 2, pale yellow, radicle curved, about 1 mm long. Odour slight; taste bland and bean-like flavor when chewed (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Epidermal palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cells, 2 layers of cells in hilum region, covered with cuticle. Palisade cells radially elongated, 15-67 $ \\mu $m in radial length, with gradually thickened wall outward, with longitudinal striation in the upper part, colourless or containing yellowish-brown contents; 1/6 part close to surface bearing a light line. 1 layer brace cells dumbbell-shaped, with longitudinally striated-thickened wall, beneath palisade cells, colourless or containing yellow contents. Endosperm consists of 5-9 layers of parenchymatous cells or depressed degenerate cells, most crimped, colourless. Cotyledons contain abundant oil droplets (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[43, 8, 1973, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Palisade cells arrange in 1 layer, colourless or pale yellow, narrow rectangular in lateral view, subpolygonal in surface view, 15-67 $ \\mu $m long. Brace cells dumbbell-shaped in lateral view, subrounded or oval in surface view, with 3 concentric circles, 7-43 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Parenchymatous cells of cotyledon contain abundant oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[284, 810, 970, 1611])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1008, 788, 1691, 1622])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of longitudinal section of Astragali Complanati Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Cuticle 2. Palisade tissue 3. Brace cells 4. Endosperm 5. Cotyledon\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[551, 98, 717, 154])\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n髂金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 101, 1591, 163])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[283, 423, 964, 925])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[998, 422, 1691, 935])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[287, 945, 962, 1471])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[999, 955, 1691, 1467])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[284, 1495, 964, 2017])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[996, 1501, 1691, 2016])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Astragali Complanati Semen (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Palisade cells (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in upper surface view, 1-3 in under surface view)\n\n2. Palisade cells and brace cells (in lateral view)\n\n3. Brace cells (in surface view) 4. Fragment of cotyledon"},{"name":"11","text":"Complanatoside A standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of complanatoside A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol, formic acid and water (5:1:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately complanatoside A standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 1-3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[377, 1969, 1103, 2517])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of complanatoside A\n\n</div>\n\n黟金 Curcumae Radix\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1427, 102, 1592, 163])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 372, 897, 1192])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Astragali Complanati Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Complanatoside A standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of complanatoside A (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Complanatoside A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of complanatoside A CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol (50%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (267 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>13→15</td><td>87→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-25</td><td>15</td><td>85</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-60</td><td>15→25</td><td>85→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of complanatoside A Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of complanatoside A should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of complanatoside A peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from complanatoside A peak should not be less than 85000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject complanatoside A Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of complanatoside A peak in the chromatogram of complanatoside A Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify complanatoside A peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of complanatoside A Std-FP. The retention times of complanatoside A peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Astragali Complanati Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[37, 12, 1959, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Astragali Complanati Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.06</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.96</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, complanatoside A)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[360, 949, 1715, 1610])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Astragali Complanati Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 24.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/astragali-complanati-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Astragali Complanati Semen","la":"Astragali Complanati Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/atractylodis-rhizoma-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atractylodis Rhizoma v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[730, 1725, 1295, 2359])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1926, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[730, 1725, 1295, 2359])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1926, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Atractylodis Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 蒼朮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Cangzhu"},{"name":"14","text":"Atractylodis Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC. or Atractylodes chinensis (DC.) Koidz. (Asteraceae). The rhizome is collected in spring and autumn, freed of aerial part, dried under the sun, followed by the removal of fibrous roots to obtain Atractylodis Rhizoma."},{"name":"14","text":"Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC. : Irregularly moniliform or nodular-cylindrical, slightly curved, occasionally branched, 2-9.3 cm long, 5-42 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown to dark brown, marked with wrinkle, remains of rootlets and stem scars. Texture compact, fracture fibrous like, pale yellow or greyish-white, scattered with many reddish-brown oil spots, and white fine needle crystals appear after exposing for a long time. Odour aromatic; taste slightly sweetish, pungent and bitter [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nAtractylodes chinensis (DC.) Koidz. : Knotty-lumpy or nodular-cylindrical, slightly curved, occasionally branched. 3.2-11.6 cm long, 6-28 mm in diameter. Externally dark brown. Texture relatively loose, fracture fibrous-like, pale yellow or greyish-white, and scattered with yellowishbrown oil spots. Odour weakly aromatic; taste pungent and bitter [Fig. 1(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC. : Cork consists of 10 or more layers of cork cells, containing stone cells bands and composing of 1-3 layers of stone cells. Cortex relatively broad. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. Fibres in bundles, mainly existing in xylem, large and mostly, usually alternate arrangement with vessels. Vessels singly or in group, arranged radially. Oil\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 7, 1984, 262])\n\ncavities scattered in cortex, phloem, ray and pith, ellipsoid or subrounded, 75-525 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Parenchymatous cells contain small raphides of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nAtractylodes chinensis (DC.) Koidz. : Cortex broad. Fibres in bundles, mainly existing in the inner of xylem, relatively few, alternate arrangement with vessels. Oil cavities relatively few and large, 125-718 $ \\mu $m in diameter scattered in parenchyma; oil cavities are large in pith [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to brown. Raphides of calcium oxalate extremely abundant, minute, 4-29 $ \\mu m $ long, irregularly filled in parenchymatous cells, often lean to one side of cell or scattered throughout; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells fairly abundant, sometimes linking up with cork cells, polygonal, subrounded or subrectangular, 11-95 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with heavily thickened walls. Inulin frequently visible, mass or fan-shaped, with radial striations on surface; slightly bright blue under the polarized microscope. Mainly reticulate vessels or bordered pits vessels, 8-66 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Fibres singly scattered or dispersed in bundles, 9-60 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, pit canals thin and dense, with rather thickened lignified walls; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells subpolygonal or subrectangular in shape, sometimes linking up with stone cells. Fragment of oil cavities occasionally visible (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"14","text":"Atractylodin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of atractylodin CRS (Fig. 4) and place it in a 5-mL brown volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with n-hexane."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane and ethyl acetate (10:0.8, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 5 g of p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde and dissolve in 100 mL of sulphuric acid (10%, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[598, 15, 695, 69])\n\nArctii Fructus牛蒡子\n\n湖北貝母\n\nAurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\n延胡索 Corydalis Rhizoma\n\n砒霜 Arsenicum\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1693, 26, 1901, 108])\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[427, 549, 988, 1017])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[360, 1189, 934, 1606])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[359, 1707, 936, 2129])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1056, 434, 1681, 849])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1057, 856, 1683, 1271])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1056, 1281, 1686, 1701])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1057, 1714, 1683, 2126])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried rhizome of Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cell band D. Oil cavity\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell band 3. Cortex 4. Oil cavity 5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Fibres\n\n8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[21, 9, 1979, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[173, 432, 1754, 2237])\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried rhizome of Atractylodes chinensis (DC.) Koidz.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cell band D. Oil cavity\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell band 3. Cortex 4. Oil cavity 5. Phloem 6. Cambium\n\n7. Fibres 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[33, 5, 1930, 257])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[277, 675, 1703, 2031])\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried rhizome of Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC.\n\n1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Stone cell 3. Inulin 4. Vessels 5. Fibre\n\n6. Cork cells 7. Fragment of oil cavity\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1892, 45, 1984, 87])\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[255, 419, 1718, 2032])\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried rhizome of Atractylodes chinensis (DC.) Koidz.\n\n1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Stone cells 3. Inulin 4. Vessel 5. Fibres\n\n6. Cork cells 7. Fragment of oil cavity\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[12, 5, 1931, 260])\n\nCarry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately atractylodin standard solution and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of atractylodin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[388, 1371, 1176, 1608])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of atractylodin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"Atractylodin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (18 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.9 mg of atractylodin CRS and place it in a 50-mL brown volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[12, 5, 1978, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>55→15</td><td>45→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of atractylodin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of atractylodin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of atractylodin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from atractylodin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject atractylodin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of atractylodin peak in the chromatogram of atractylodin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify atractylodin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of atractylodin Std-FP. The retention times of atractylodin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Atractylodis Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[28, 0, 1932, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Atractylodis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.76</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.83</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,atractylodin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[378, 880, 1681, 1479])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[381, 1723, 1744, 2379])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Atractylodes chinensis (DC.) Koidz. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[21, 17, 1976, 266])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%"},{"name":"14","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%"},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 11.0%"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 35.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 25.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/atractylodis-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atractylodis Rhizoma","la":"Atractylodis Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aurantii-fructus-immaturus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aurantii Fructus Immaturus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[702, 819, 1333, 1396])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried young fruit of Citrus aurantium\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[694, 1855, 1349, 2230])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[702, 819, 1333, 1396])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried young fruit of Citrus aurantium\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[694, 1855, 1349, 2230])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Aurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\nChinese Name: 枳實\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhishi"},{"name":"16","text":"Aurantii Fructus Immaturus is the dried young fruit of Citrus aurantium L. and its cultivated varieties or Citrus sinensis Osbeck (Rutaceae). The fallen young fruit is collected in May and June, removed from foreign matter, whole or cut into two parts or pieces, then dried under the sun or at ambient temperature to obtain Aurantii Fructus Immaturus."},{"name":"16","text":"Citrus aurantium L.: Semi-spheroidal, 8-35 mm in diameter. Exocarp dark green to dark brownish green, with granular protuberances and wrinkles; remain of style or fruit stalk scar distinct. Mesocarp in transverse section slightly prominent, 4-8 mm thick, with 1-2 rows of oil cavaties at the outer part of mesocarp. Pulp segments (carpels) 7-13, juice vesicles brown. Centre core 2-5 mm in diameter. Texture hard. Odour aromatic; taste bitter and slightly sour [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nCitrus sinensis Osbeck: Mostly spheroidal to semi-spheroidal or as a round slice, 5-25 mm in diameter. Exocarp yellowish-brown to dark brownish-green, remains of style or fruit stalk scar distinct. Texture hard. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Citrus aurantium L.: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of cells covered with thick cuticles and incrusted with stomata. Oil cavities of 1-2 layers are irregularly located in the outer portion of the mesocarp, relatively large in size, the shape subglobular to long-ellipsoid. Mesocarp consists of numerous layers of parenchymatous cells with unevenly thickened cell walls, scattered with prisms of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundles collateral, small, scattered in\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 8, 1984, 262])\n\nthe mesocarp. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of parenchymatous cells and shrinking, as is the membrane of segments which is filled with juice vesicles. Juice vesicle consists of parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nCitrus sinensis Osbeck: Ratio of mesocarp in the transverse section is smaller than that in Citrus aurantium L. Fan-shaped crystals can be found in the parenchymatous cells of the mesocarp [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Citrus aurantium L.: Colour yellowish-white. Mesocarp cells subrounded or irregular, most cell walls unevenly thickened and beaded. Epidermal cells of exocarp polygonal, subsquare to rectangular in surface view; stomata actinocytic, 22-32 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, subsidiary cells 5-9, in lateral view appearing covered with thick cuticle. Fragments of oil cavity brownish-yellow, often broken, sometimes with droplets of oil. Vessel elements are mostly spiral or reticulate, 4-24 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Prisms of calcium oxalate crystals abundant, rhombic, double-conical or irregularly polygonal, 2-35 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, occuring in cells of the mesocarp and inside the juice vesicle; crystals appear intensely white to polychromatic under the polarized microscope [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nCitrus sinensis Osbeck: Colour brownish-yellow. Fan-shaped crystals yellow, sometimes irregular in shape [Fig. 3 (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Synephrine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of synephrine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, n-butanol and acetic acid (5:4:1, v/v) in a separating funnel. Shake well and allow to stand for 30 min. Use the upper layer."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1 g of ninhydrin and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[22, 0, 1928, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 471, 1715, 2051])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried young fruit of Citrus aurantium L.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle D. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Oil cavity 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle 5. Endocarp 6. Juice vesicles\n\n7. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[30, 17, 1976, 265])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[247, 404, 1734, 2104])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried young fruit of Citrus sinensis Osbeck A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle D. Fan-shaped crystals 1. Exocarp 2. Oil cavity 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle 5. Endocarp 6. Juice vesicles 7. Fan-shaped crystals\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[170, 14, 327, 71])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[598, 15, 695, 68])\n\nArctii Fructus牛蒡子\n\n湖北貝母\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1692, 26, 1902, 109])\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[389, 457, 972, 902])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1070, 464, 1596, 909])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[387, 946, 975, 1405])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1013, 950, 1594, 1371])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[391, 1447, 972, 1894])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1012, 1444, 1598, 1899])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried young fruit of Citrus aurantium L.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Mesocarp cells 2. Epidermal cells of exocarp 3. Fragments of oil cavity 4. Spiral vessels\n\n5. Prisms of Calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\n雄黄\n\nRealgar\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱莲\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1891, 44, 1984, 89])\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba 半枝蓮\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[237, 542, 1698, 2021])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried young fruit of Citrus sinensis Osbeck (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Mesocarp cells 2. Epidermal cells of exocarp 3. Fragments of oil cavity 4. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Spiral vessels 6. Fan-shaped crystals\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 4, 1932, 260])"},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate [immerse in sodium hydroxide solution (1%, w/v) for 20s. Dry the plate at $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 h. Cool in a desiccator.], a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately synephrine standard solution and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of synephrine.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 1399, 1274, 2324])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) naringin and (ii) synephrine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[23, 13, 1972, 265])"},{"name":"16","text":"Naringin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of naringin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nSynephrine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of synephrine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (224 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid and 0.1% Sodium dodecyl sulphate(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-25</td><td>80→75</td><td>20→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-30</td><td>75→67</td><td>25→33</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>67→65</td><td>33→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of naringin Std-FP and synephrine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of naringin and synephrine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of naringin and synephrine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from naringin and synephrine peaks should not be less than 8000 and 150000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 1, 1928, 260])"},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject naringin Std-FP, synephrine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of naringin and synephrine peaks in the chromatograms of naringin Std-FP, synephrine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify naringin and synephrine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of naringin Std-FP and synephrine Std-FP. The retention times of naringin and synephrine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Aurantii Fructus Immaturus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Aurantii Fructus Immaturus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.34</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(naringin)</td><td>0.40</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(hesperidin)</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,synephrine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[379, 1700, 1762, 2250])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried young fruit of Citrus aurantium L. extract\n\n</div>\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\nSchisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\nAurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[380, 400, 1765, 950])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried young fruit of Citrus sinensis Osbeck extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nToluene distillation method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 22.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 23.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[26, 4, 1929, 260])"},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"16","text":"Synephrine standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.5 mg of synephrine CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nSynephrine standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the synephrine Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 25, 50, 75, 100, 150 mg/L for synephrine."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aurantii-fructus-immaturus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aurantii Fructus Immaturus","la":"Aurantii Fructus Immaturus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aurantii-fructus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aurantii Fructus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[593, 1038, 1393, 1727])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1927, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[593, 1038, 1393, 1727])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1927, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Aurantii Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 枳殼\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhiqiao"},{"name":"13","text":"Aurantii Fructus is the dried immature fruit of Citrus aurantium L. (Rutaceae) and its cultivated varieties. The immature fruit is collected in July while the fruit is green, cut into two parts, then dried under the sun or at ambient temperature to obtain Aurantii Fructus."},{"name":"13","text":"Semi-spheroidal, 30-60 mm in diameter. Exocarp brown to dark brown, with rigidly precise convexes and sunken oil cavity spots on the surface; remains of style at the apex and fruit stalk scar at base distinct. Mesocarp in transverse section yellowish-white, smooth and slightly prominent, 3-10 mm thick, with 1-2 rows of oil cavities on the outer part of pericarp. Pulp segments (carpels) 7-14, juice vesicles dried and shrunken, brown to dark brown, containing seeds. Centre core 5-9 mm in diameter. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Odour aromatic; taste bitter and slightly sour (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of cells covered with thick cuticles and incrusted with stomata. Oil cavities of 1-2 layers are irregularly located in the outer portion of the mesocarp, relatively large, subrounded to long-elliptic. Mesocarp consists of numerous layers of parenchymatous cells with unevenly thickened cell walls, scattered with prisms of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundles collateral, small, scattered in the mesocarp. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of parenchymatous cells and shrinking, as is the membrane of segments which is filled with juice vesicles. Juice vesicle consists of parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[14, 8, 1984, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Mesocarp fragments abundant, cells subrounded or of irregular shapes, mostly with uneven thickened walls. Epidermal cells of exocarp subpolygonal to subsquare on surface view; stomata actinocytic, 22-32 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, subsidiary cells 5-9. Fragments of oil cavity brownish-yellow, often broken, sometimes with droplets of oil. Fragments of juice vesicles pale brownish-yellow, the cells long and shrinking as lines, scattered with smaller prisms of calcium oxalate. Vessel elements mostly spiral, 4-24 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Prisms of calcium oxalate abundant, dispersed singly in the parenchymatous cells, rhombic to double-conical to irregularly polygonal, 2-35 $ \\mu $ m across; intensely white to polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"13","text":"Naringin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of naringin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nNeohesperidin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of neohesperidin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of water and acetone (3:2, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC polyamide plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately naringin standard solution, neohesperidin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the TLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the TLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[24, 0, 1922, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[254, 402, 1737, 2177])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Aurantii Fructus\n\nA. Sketch of transverse section B. Transverse section C. Vascular bundle D. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Oil cavity 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Endocarp\n\n8. Juice vesicle 9. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 45, 1984, 87])\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[301, 519, 969, 999])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1024, 515, 1681, 969])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[303, 1033, 966, 1484])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1016, 1035, 1682, 1481])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[365, 1517, 721, 2050])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[784, 1522, 1189, 2046])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1240, 1512, 1684, 2051])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Aurantii Fructus\n\n1. Mesocarp cells 2. Exocarp epidermal cells 3. Fragments of oil cavity 4. Fragments of juice vesicles\n\n5. Spiral vessels 6. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[25, 4, 1924, 260])\n\ndry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{f} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of naringin and neohesperidin.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[375, 801, 1201, 2270])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) naringin (ii) neohesperidin and (iii) synephrine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 15, 1971, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"Naringin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of naringin CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol.\n\nNeohesperidin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of neohesperidin CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol.\n\nSynephrine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of synephrine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (224 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid and0.1% Sodium dodecyl sulphate(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-25</td><td>80→75</td><td>20→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-30</td><td>75→67</td><td>25→33</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>67→65</td><td>33→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of naringin Std-FP, neohesperidin Std-FP and synephrine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of naringin, neohesperidin and synephrine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of naringin, neohesperidin and synephrine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from naringin, neohesperidin and synephrine peaks should not be less than 8000, 8000 and 70000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 1, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject naringin Std-FP, neohesperidin Std-FP, synephrine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of naringin, neohesperidin and synephrine peaks in the chromatograms of naringin Std-FP, neohesperidin Std-FP, synephrine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify naringin, neohesperidin and synephrine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of naringin Std-FP, neohesperidin Std-FP and synephrine Std-FP. The retention times of naringin, neohesperidin and synephrine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Aurantii Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Aurantii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, naringin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3 (neohesperidin)</td><td>1.29</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4 (synephrine)</td><td>2.46</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 1671, 1784, 2188])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Aurantii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 15, 1977, 265])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 32.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 25.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/aurantii-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aurantii Fructus","la":"Aurantii Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/baphicacanthis-cusiae-rhizoma-et-radix-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[416, 866, 1467, 1931])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix\n\nA. Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix B. Transverse section of rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[30, 10, 1903, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[416, 866, 1467, 1931])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix\n\nA. Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix B. Transverse section of rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[30, 10, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix\n\nChinese Name: 南板蓝根\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Nanbanlangen"},{"name":"12","text":"Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix is the dried rhizome and root of Baphicacanthus cusia (Nees) Bremek. (Acanthaceae). The rhizome and root are collected in summer and autumn, the aerial part and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Rhizome cylindrical, mostly tortuous, branched, 5-28 cm long, 2-11 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown or reddish-brown, nodes swollen, sometimes with slender and curved rootlets, stem remnants at the upper parts of nodes. Texture hard and fragile. Fracture uneven, bark bluish-grey, wood yellowish-white to yellow, pith bluish-grey. Odour slight; taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists several layers of cells, containing brown contents. Cortex broad, consisting of several layers of sclerenchymatous cells at outer side; Stone cells present. Endodermis distinct. Phloem relatively narrow, with numerous phloem fibres. Xylem vessels singly scattered or several in groups, arranged radially; xylem rays relatively broad. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells; some cells contain cystoliths (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-green or brown. Cystoliths yellow, pale yellow or colourless, elongated-elliptical or elliptical, 21-225 $ \\mu $m long (occasionally up to 295 $ \\mu $m), 14-84 $ \\mu $m in diameter; yellowish-white, white or polychromatic under the polarized light microscope. Phloem fibres elongated-fusiform, 6-27 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls thick, lumen linear; bright yellow or white under the polarized light microscope. Xylem fibres 10-44 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall relatively thick and lumen large, pits and pit canals distinct; yellowish-white or white under the polarized light microscope. Stone cells subrectangular, subsquare, subrounded or irregular in shape, 29-271 $ \\mu $m long and 14-123 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall thick and lumen large, pits and pit canals distinct; bright yellow or white under the polarized light microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 11-69 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Cork cells subpolygonal or elongated-polygonal in surface view. Simple starch granules rounded, subrounded or ovoid, 2-23 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum slit-shaped, dotted or V-shaped, striations indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-9 units (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 5, 1909, 262])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[253, 481, 1719, 2099])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cystoliths in parenchymatous cells D. Phloem fibres\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell 3. Cystolith 4. Cortex 5. Endodermis\n\n6. Phloem 7. Phloem fibre 8. Xylem 9. Xylem ray 10. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[291, 451, 1713, 2055])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cystolith 2. Phloem fibre 3. Xylem fibre 4. Stone cells 5. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n6. Cork cells 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[37, 13, 1901, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Indigo standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of indigo CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of methanol and dichloromethane (1:9, v/v).\n\nIndirubin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of indirubin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of methanol and dichloromethane (1:9, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and acetone (97:3, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of a mixture of methanol and dichloromethane (1:9, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately indigo standard solution (60 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), indirubin standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of indigo and indirubin.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[68, 15, 1958, 259])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 366, 821, 568])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[385, 635, 775, 866])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) indigo and (ii) indirubin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Indigo standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (2 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of indigo CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of N,N-dimethylformamide.\n\nIndirubin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (0.6 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.15 mg of indirubin CRS and dissolve in 250 mL of N,N-dimethylformamide."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 8 mL of N,N-dimethylformamide. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with N,N-dimethylformamide. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[37, 13, 1897, 263])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>70→45</td><td>30→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>45→40</td><td>55→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>40→10</td><td>60→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>10→0</td><td>90→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of indigo Std-FP and indirubin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of indigo and indirubin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of indigo and indirubin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from indigo and indirubin peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject indigo Std-FP, indirubin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of indigo and indirubin peaks in the chromatograms of indigo Std-FP, indirubin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify indigo and indirubin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of indigo Std-FP and indirubin Std-FP. The retention times of indigo and indirubin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 12, 1960, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, indigo)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(indirubin)</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[310, 840, 1780, 1514])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[36, 100, 209, 198])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[909, 51, 1071, 114])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XV.\n\nAnthrone sulphuric acid solution\n\nWeigh accurately 0.1 g of anthrone and dissolve in 100 mL of sulphuric acid (80%, v/v)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/baphicacanthis-cusiae-rhizoma-et-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix","la":"Baphicacanthis Cusiae Rhizoma et Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bistortae-rhizoma-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bistortae Rhizoma v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[607, 852, 1458, 1887])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[607, 852, 1458, 1887])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Bistortae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 拳参\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Quanshen"},{"name":"12","text":"Bistortae Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Polygonum bistorta L. (Polygonaceae). The rhizome is collected in early spring when bud or in autumn just before withering, soil removed, dried under the sun, then rootlets removed to obtain Histortae Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Compressed-cylindrical, usually curved into a shrimp-like shape, both ends obtuse or slightly narrowed, 2-11 cm long, 8-25 mm in diameter. Externally purple brown to dark brown, rough, one side protuberant and the other side flat or slightly furrowed, with thick annulated striations and remnants of rootlets or root scars. Texture hard. Fracture roundish or nearly reniform, pale brown to brown, dotted vascular bundles yellowish-white, arranged interruptedly in a ring. Odour slightly aromatic; taste bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of brown cells, containing brown masses. Cortex occupies about 1/4 of rhizome. Vascular bundles collateral, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem narrow. Xylem consists of vessels and xylem fibres. Pith broad, parenchymatous cells contain clusters of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[64, 7, 1961, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale brownish-red. Starch granules fairly abundant, broadly ovoid, long ellipsoid or subspheroid, 2-13 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum pointed, V-shaped or slit-shaped, striations distinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules rare, composed of 2-3 units. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, scattered singly, 19-65 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, spiral vessels occasionally visible, 10-55 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fibres scattered or in bundles, 6-30 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls relatively thick, pit canals distinct. Cork cells subsquare, containing brownishred masses (Fig. 3).\n\nDryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\n綿馬貫眾\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\n芫花\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 417, 1750, 2114])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Bistortae Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate D. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope) 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Pith\n\nRosae Laevigatae Fructus\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\n秦艽\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\n骨碎補\n\n覆盆子\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus\n\n沙苑子 Astragali Complanati Semen\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川楝子\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nBistortae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[252, 523, 1689, 2126])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Bistortae Rhizoma\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate 3. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n4. Spiral vessel 5. Fibres 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, water and formic acid (20:3:1.5:1.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Ammonia."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (30%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with ammonia vapor chamber for about 1 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[552, 97, 717, 156])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 158, 1237, 212])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 370, 922, 707])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of chlorogenic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 867, 830, 1596])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Bistortae Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (254 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chlorogenic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of chlorogenic acid (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[35, 10, 1895, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (70 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.7 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (30%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (30%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>96.5</td><td>3.5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>96.5→93</td><td>3.5→7</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>93→92</td><td>7→8</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>92</td><td>8</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of chlorogenic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of chlorogenic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from chlorogenic acid peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[551, 99, 717, 155])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of chlorogenic acid peak in the chromatogram of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of chlorogenic acid Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Bistortae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Bistortae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(gallic acid)</td><td>0.23</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.28</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(3-O-methylgallic acid)</td><td>0.72</td><td>$\\pm$0.05</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, chlorogenic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[358, 1604, 1809, 2285])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Bistortae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 15, 1926, 254])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 29.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bistortae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bistortae Rhizoma","la":"Bistortae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bletillae-rhizoma-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bletillae Rhizoma v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[397, 839, 1586, 1778])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bletillae Rhizoma A. Bletillae Rhizoma B. Cut surface of tuber"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[397, 839, 1586, 1778])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bletillae Rhizoma A. Bletillae Rhizoma B. Cut surface of tuber"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Bletillae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 白及\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baiji"},{"name":"12","text":"Bletillae Rhizoma is the dried tuber of Bletilla striata (Thunb.) Reichb. f. (Orchidaceae). The tuber is harvested in summer and autumn, rootlets removed, washed clean, boiled or steamed until no dry core visible, dried to semi-dryness under the sun, peeled to remove the outer tissue (phelloderm), then dried again under the sun to obtain Bletillae Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregularly oblate, mostly with 2-3 claw-like branches, 2.3-6.7 cm long, 0.6-2.5 cm thick. Externally yellowish-white, yellowish-brown to blackish-brown, with thin wrinkles, possessing several concentric rings and brown dotted rootlet scars, with raised stem scars at the upper part and a trace of joint to another tuber at the lower part. Texture hard, uneasily broken, cut surface whitish and horny. Odour slight; taste bitter and viscous on chewing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subrounded or subrectangular cells, covered with cuticle. Parenchyma consists of irregular cells, scattered with numerous mucilage cells and gelatinous starch masses. Mucilage cells large, sometimes containing raphides of calcium oxalate. Stele scattered with numerous vascular bundles in closed collateral type, mainly single, occasionally linked or appeared side by side; fibres in bundles, hat-shaped, arranged at the outer side of vascular bundles, some with small cells containing silica bodies outside the fibre bundles (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[30, 18, 1956, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellowish-white to pale yellowish-brown. Fibres in bundles, 20-51 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls lignified, with elliptical pits; bright white under the polarized microscope; sometimes with small cells contain silica bodies arranged on the outer side. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered or present in mucilage cells, needle crystals slender, 21-78 $ \\mu m $ long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Mucilage cells large, subrounded or elliptical, 98-325 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Epidermal cells irregular in shape, with walls sinuous, slightly thickened and lignified, with distinct pit canals. Gelatinous starch masses colourless, fairly abundant. Vessels mostly scalariform, 15-49 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; spiral vessels rare (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王 Wikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[105, 188, 204, 239])\n\n天葵子\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黃\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 443, 1739, 2074])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Bletillae Rhizoma\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphides of calcium oxalate in mucilage cell\n\nD. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Epidermis 3. Parenchyma 4. Gelatinous starch masses 5. Mucilage cell\n\n6. Raphides of calcium oxalate 7. Xylem 8. Phloem 9. Fibres 10. Silica body\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[197, 146, 289, 188])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nBletillae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[283, 678, 660, 1013])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[675, 676, 1061, 1014])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1095, 677, 1382, 1007])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1395, 675, 1709, 1018])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[283, 1052, 735, 1392])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[764, 1049, 1222, 1393])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1254, 1053, 1704, 1386])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[282, 1430, 732, 1767])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[763, 1429, 1219, 1764])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1254, 1425, 1475, 1767])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1487, 1426, 1707, 1765])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Bletillae Rhizoma\n\n1. Fibre bundles with cells containing silica bodies on the side (silica bodies $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n2. Raphides of calcium oxalate (2-1 scattered throughout the powder, 2-2 present in mucilage cell)\n\n3. Mucilage cell 4. Epidermal cells 5. Gelatinous starch masses\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 scalariform vessels, 6-2 spiral vessels)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Militarin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of militarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, ethyl acetate and water (4:1:5, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately militarin standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 8 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[925, 87, 1028, 138])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[370, 370, 1432, 658])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of militarin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 769, 802, 1595])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Bletillae Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Militarin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of militarin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Militarin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (32 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.32 mg of militarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (223 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>90</td><td>10</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-20</td><td>90→75</td><td>10→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>75→60</td><td>25→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>60→45</td><td>40→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of militarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of militarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of militarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from militarin peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject militarin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of militarin peak in the chromatogram of militarin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify militarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of militarin Std-FP. The retention times of militarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[48, 41, 1936, 252])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Bletillae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Bletillae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,militarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[345, 949, 1805, 1405])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Bletillae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[359, 159, 501, 207])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bletillae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bletillae Rhizoma","la":"Bletillae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bolbostemmatis-rhizoma-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[657, 921, 844, 1125])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[844, 927, 1014, 1105])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1137, 915, 1294, 1120])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[644, 1218, 1368, 1851])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma A. Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma B. Magnified image of tuber C. Magnified image of fracture of tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 22, 1872, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[657, 921, 844, 1125])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[844, 927, 1014, 1105])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1137, 915, 1294, 1120])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[644, 1218, 1368, 1851])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma A. Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma B. Magnified image of tuber C. Magnified image of fracture of tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 22, 1872, 263])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 土貝母\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tubeimu"},{"name":"10","text":"Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma is the dried tuber of Bolbostemma paniculatum (Maxim.) Franquet (Cucurbitaceae). The tuber is collected in autumn, washed clean, broken off, boiled until the tuber is devoid of the white core, taken out, then dried under the sun to obtain Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma."},{"name":"10","text":"Irregular masses, varying in size. Externally pale brown, pale reddish-brown to dark brown, bumpy. Texture hard and uneasily broken. Fracture corneous. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of small rectangular cells, arranged orderly, usually suberized. Several layers of parenchymatous cells located beneath epidermis often suberized. Vascular bundles small, scattered irregularly in a haphazard manner in the stele parenchyma. Parenchymatous cells of the stele contain gelatinous starch masses (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Gelatinous starch masses fairly abundant. Vessels sometimes visible, spiral, annular or reticulate, 5-30 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Epidermal cells rectangular, neatly arranged in lateral view; subpolygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls beaded or verrucose thickened, some cell walls suberized (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 4, 1947, 255])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[251, 493, 1732, 1969])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stele parenchymatous cells containing gelatinous starch masses\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Suberized cells 3. Vascular bundles\n\n4. Stele parenchyma containing gelatinous starch masses\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 17, 1898, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 424, 1670, 2069])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma (under the light microscope) 1. Gelatinous starch masses 2. Vessels 3. Epidermal cells (3-1 in lateral view 3-2 in surface view)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 14, 1946, 256])"},{"name":"10","text":"Tubeimoside I standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of tubeimoside I CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, dichloromethane, methanol and water (4:3:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately tubeimoside I standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nBolbostemmatis Rhizoma\n\n土貝母\n\n厚朴花\n\n月季花\n\nRosae Chinensis Flos\n\nBolbostemmatis Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Japonicae Flos\n\n金銀花\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[376, 370, 1421, 1061])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of tubeimoside I\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[383, 1217, 961, 1996])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Tubeimoside I standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of tubeimoside I (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 13, 1944, 257])"},{"name":"10","text":"Tubeimoside I standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of tubeimoside I CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Pipette 1 mL of the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (214 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>10→40</td><td>90→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>40→70</td><td>60→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of tubeimoside I Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of tubeimoside I should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of tubeimoside I peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from tubeimoside I peak should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject tubeimoside I Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of tubeimoside I peak in the chromatogram of tubeimoside I Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify tubeimoside I peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of tubeimoside I Std-FP. The retention times of tubeimoside I peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, tubeimoside I)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[382, 1539, 1798, 2105])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 22, 1944, 257])"},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"10","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 49.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 28.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bolbostemmatis-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma","la":"Bolbostemmatis Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bruceae-fructus-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bruceae Fructus v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[440, 1048, 1060, 1672])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1147, 1064, 1376, 1229])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1177, 1307, 1375, 1477])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1195, 1562, 1373, 1664])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 2mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bruceae Fructus\n\nA. Bruceae Fructus B. Magnified image of fruit\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of fruit\n\nD. Magnified image of seed"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[440, 1048, 1060, 1672])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1147, 1064, 1376, 1229])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1177, 1307, 1375, 1477])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1195, 1562, 1373, 1664])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 2mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bruceae Fructus\n\nA. Bruceae Fructus B. Magnified image of fruit\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of fruit\n\nD. Magnified image of seed"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Bruceae Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 鸦膽子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yadanzi"},{"name":"12","text":"Bruceae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Brucea javanica (L.) Merr. (Simaroubaceae). The fruit is collected in autumn when ripe, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Bruceae Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Ovoid to ellipsoid, slightly flat, 3-10 mm long, 2-8 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-green, yellowish-brown, dark green to dark brown, with raised polygonal reticulate wrinkles. Apex acuminate, base with a dented fruit stalk scar, two sides with obvious ridges. Shell hard but fragile, seed 1, ovoid, externally whitish to yellowish-white, endosperm yellowish-white, cotyledon green, oily. Odour slight but characteristic; taste extremely bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Outermost layer of exocarp consists of 1 layer of epidermal cells, covered with cuticle, with 1-2 layers of subsquare cells beneath the epidermal cells. Mesocarp consists of subrounded parenchymatous cells, with scattered clusters of calcium oxalate, and collateral vascular bundles bordering the endocarp. Outer side of endocarp wavy with protruding peaks, consisting of 2 layers of stone cells, forming annular bands, and 1 layer of crystal-containing cells. Outer band of stone cells consist of 1 to several layers of stone cells with distinct lumen; middle band is a crystal cells layer, consisting of 1 to several layers of cells with slightly thickened wall, usually containing prisms of calcium oxalate; inner band of stone cells relatively broad, made up of stone cells with indistinct boundaries. Testa consists of 1 layer of cells, boundaries indistinct. Endosperm cells polygonal, filled with aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells relatively small, filled with aleurone grains (Fig. 2).\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to dark brown. Stone cells of inner endocarp mostly in clumps, pale yellow to bright yellow, irregular in shape, walls thick, boundaries indistinct; bright yellow under the polarized microscope. Stone cells of outer endocarp scattered or in groups, colourless or pale yellow, subrounded, subpolygonal or irregular in shape, 15-119 $ \\mu $m long, 14-84 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with distinct pits and pit canals; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Crystal cells in groups, yellow to yellowish-brown, subpolygonal or subrounded, walls slightly beaded-thickened, usually containing prisms of calcium oxalate; crystal cells dark yellowish-brown, prisms of calcium oxalate polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered or present in crystal cells, 4-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered or present in parenchymatous cells, 6-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of exocarp colourless or pale yellowish-brown, subpolygonal in surface view, sometimes with slightly beaded-thickened walls, stomata rare, anomocytic. Epidermal cells of testa with granular surface, boundaries indistinct. Oil droplets numerous, colourless or pale yellow (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1267, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[337, 580, 977, 1141])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[345, 1312, 996, 1952])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1073, 495, 1673, 1548])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1070, 1641, 1686, 1954])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Bruceae Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of exocarp D. Magnified image of endocarp\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Vascular bundle 4. Stone cells band of outer endocarp\n\n5. Crystal cells of endocarp 6. Stone cells band of inner endocarp 7. Testa 8. Endosperm\n\n9. Cotyledon 10. Cuticle 11. Epidermis 12. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n13. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\nSaussureae Involucratae Herba\n\n天山雪莲\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n白花丹\n\nlumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nBruceae Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[327, 471, 1626, 1972])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Stone cells of inner endocarp 2. Stone cell of outer endocarp 3. Crystal cells\n\n4. Prisms of calcium oxalate 5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Epidermal cells of exocarp ( stoma)\n\n7. Epidermal cells of testa 8. Oil droplets\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Brucein D standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of brucein D CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol, formic acid and water (15:1:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 4 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately brucein D standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 10 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[375, 1909, 896, 2285])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of brucein D\n\n</div>\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 378, 758, 1189])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Bruceae Fructus extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Brucein D standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of brucein D (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Brucein D standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of brucein D CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with 10 mL of methanol (50%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[61, 34, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 100 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size, 16% carbon loading and $ 3 2 5 \\mathrm{~ m}^{2} / \\mathrm{g} $ surface area). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-6</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>6-30</td><td>95→90</td><td>5→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-35</td><td>90</td><td>10</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-40</td><td>90→82</td><td>10→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of brucein D Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of brucein D should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of brucein D peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from brucein D peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject brucein D Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of brucein D peak in the chromatogram of brucein D Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify brucein D peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of brucein D Std-FP. The retention times of brucein D peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Bruceae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[29, 35, 1940, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Bruceae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.42</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.52</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.83</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, brucein D)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[342, 821, 1802, 1434])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Bruceae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[61, 28, 1927, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bruceae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bruceae Fructus","la":"Bruceae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/buddlejae-flos-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Buddlejae Flos v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[984, 865, 1266, 1244])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[620, 1289, 1370, 1826])"},"sections":[{"name":"9","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[984, 865, 1266, 1244])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[620, 1289, 1370, 1826])"},{"name":"9","text":"Official Name: Buddlejae Flos\n\nChinese Name: 密蒙花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Mimenghua"},{"name":"9","text":"Buddleja Flos is the dried flower bud and inflorescence of Buddleja officinalis Maxim. (Loganiaceae). The flower bud and inflorescence are collected in spring before flowering, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Buddleja Flos."},{"name":"9","text":"Small, terminal branches of the irregularly conical inflorescence, bearing densely clustered flower buds, 1.0-4.5 cm long. Externally greyish-yellow to brownish-yellow, densely pubescent. Flower buds subglobular or short clavate-shaped, those on the upper part slightly larger, 0.5-1.2 cm long, 1.5-2.5 mm in diameter; calyx campanulate, apex 4-dentate; corolla tubular, of the same length as calyx or slightly longer, apex 4-lobed, lobes ovate; stamens 4, attached at the middle of corolla tube. Texture soft. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"9","text":"Colour brown. Non-glandular hairs numerous, usually 4-celled, 2 cells uniseriate arranged at the base, another 2 cells biseriate arranged at the apex; each cell bifurcated, each furcate 34-568 $ \\mu $m long, 7-28 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls extremely thick, lumens linear; another type of non-glandular hairs few, unicellular, walls with numerous spiny protuberance. Pollen grains globose, 11-21 $ \\mu $m in diameter, exine smooth, with 3 germinal pores. Glandular hairs consist of 1- to 2-celled head and 1- to 2-celled stalk, 2-celled head biseriate arranged in dumbbell-shaped or butterfly-shaped in surface view. Anther cells annular thickened. Epidermal cells of corolla tip villiform. Epidermal cells of corolla lobe subrounded to polygonal (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[551, 98, 717, 154])\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1071, 158, 1235, 212])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1427, 102, 1593, 162])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nBuddlejae Flos\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[237, 427, 1742, 2144])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Buddlejae Flos (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1-3. Non-glandular hairs 4. Unicellular non-glandular hairs 5. Pollen grains\n\n6-7. Glandular hair (in surface view) 8-9. Glandular hair (in lateral view)\n\n10. Anther cells 11. Epidermal cells of corolla tip 12. Epidermal cells of corolla lobe"},{"name":"9","text":"Linarin (buddleoside) standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of linarin CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 4 mL of methanol."},{"name":"9","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, water and formic acid (7:3:1.2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of ferric trichloride and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linarin standard solution (12 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[377, 1681, 1051, 2153])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structure of linarin (buddleoside)\n\n</div>\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n鬆金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCyathulae Radix\n\n川牛膝\n\nBuddlejae Flos\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[382, 373, 865, 1185])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Buddlejae Flos extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Linarin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of linarin (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"9","text":"Linarin (buddleoside) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of linarin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"9","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (326 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[26, 7, 1902, 256])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>1%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>65→35</td><td>35→65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"9","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of linarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of linarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from linarin peak should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"9","text":"Separately inject linarin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of linarin peak in the chromatogram of linarin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify linarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of linarin Std-FP. The retention times of linarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Buddlejae Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Buddlejae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, linarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4 (apigenin)</td><td>1.27</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\n覆盆子\n\n鬰金 Curcumae Radix\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nRubi Fructus\n\n川楝子\n\n豬牙皂\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nBuddlejae Flos\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[361, 379, 1743, 889])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Buddlejae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"9","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"9","text":"Linarin (buddleoside) standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 0.5 mg of linarin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nLinarin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the linarin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 1,10,20,30,40 mg/L for linarin."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh accurately 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 15 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/buddlejae-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Buddlejae Flos","la":"Buddlejae Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bufonis-venenum-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bufonis Venenum v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[835, 729, 944, 880])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB 5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1063, 729, 1131, 877])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC 5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[733, 983, 1242, 1287])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried secretion of Bufo bufo gargarizans Cantor A. Lumps of dried secretion B. Magnified image of fracture of lump C. Magnified image of fracture of lump (after water has been dripped on it)\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[824, 1764, 900, 1934])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB 5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1054, 1756, 1135, 1933])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[748, 2036, 1227, 2362])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried secretion of Bufo melanostictus Schneider A. Lumps of dried secretion B. Magnified image of fracture of lump C. Magnified image of fracture of lump (after water has been dripped on it)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[835, 729, 944, 880])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB 5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1063, 729, 1131, 877])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC 5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[733, 983, 1242, 1287])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried secretion of Bufo bufo gargarizans Cantor A. Lumps of dried secretion B. Magnified image of fracture of lump C. Magnified image of fracture of lump (after water has been dripped on it)\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[824, 1764, 900, 1934])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB 5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1054, 1756, 1135, 1933])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[748, 2036, 1227, 2362])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried secretion of Bufo melanostictus Schneider A. Lumps of dried secretion B. Magnified image of fracture of lump C. Magnified image of fracture of lump (after water has been dripped on it)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Bufonis Venenum\n\nChinese Name: 蟾酥\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chansu"},{"name":"10","text":"Bufonis Venenum is the dried secretion of Bufo bufo gargarizans Cantor or Bufo melanostictus Schneider (Bufonidae). Usually the toad is collected in summer and autumn, washed clean. The white serous fluid of the parotid glands and skin glands is squeezed out, filtered, then dried to obtain Bufonis Venenum."},{"name":"10","text":"Bufo bufo gargarizans Cantor: Flattened and rounded lumps, brown or reddish-brown, 65-117 mm in diameter, 2.5-20 mm thick. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Fracture brown, corneous, slightly lustrous. Odour slightly stinky, smelling the powder causes sneezing. A creamy white bump is produced on the fracture when water is dripped on it [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nBufo melanostictus Schneider: 65-112 mm in diameter, 3-16 mm thick [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 14, 1946, 255])"},{"name":"10","text":"Cinobufagin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of cinobufagin CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nResibufogenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of resibufogenin CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, acetone and ethyl acetate (4:3:2, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 25 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately cinobufagin standard solution, resibufogenin standard solution and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 11, 1895, 259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[450, 365, 877, 781])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[451, 900, 945, 1316])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[453, 1388, 878, 1799])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Chemical structures of (i) bufalin (ii) cinobufagin and (iii) resibufogenin\n\n</div>\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nBufonis Venenum\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[385, 378, 1275, 1053])\n\n1 2 3 (i) 3 (ii)\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Bufonis Venenum extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cinobufagin standard solution 2. Resibufogenin standard solution\n\n3. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried secretion of Bufo bufo gargarizans Cantor\n\n(ii) dried secretion of Bufo melanostictus Schneider\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of cinobufagin and resibufogenin (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"Bufalin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of bufalin CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nCinobufagin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of cinobufagin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nResibufogenin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of resibufogenin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[42, 18, 1869, 264])\n\nvolumetric flask. Wash the residue with methanol. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (296 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 120 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 18% carbon loading). The flow rate is about 0.7 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>70→55</td><td>30→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-40</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bufalin Std-FP, cinobufagin Std-FP and resibufogenin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 4 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject bufalin Std-FP, cinobufagin Std-FP, resibufogenin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin peaks in the chromatograms of bufalin Std-FP, cinobufagin Std-FP, resibufogenin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks [Fig. 4 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of bufalin Std-FP, cinobufagin Std-FP and resibufogenin Std-FP. The retention times of bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 6, 1948, 258])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Bufonis Venenum extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Bufonis Venenum extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(arenobufagin)</td><td>0.36</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(telocinobufagin)</td><td>0.52</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(bufotalin)</td><td>0.56</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(bufalin)</td><td>0.75</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,cinobufagin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6(resibufogenin)</td><td>1.05</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 1104, 1821, 1689])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried secretion of Bufo bufo gargarizans Cantor extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 1865, 1821, 2464])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried secretion of Bufo melanostictus Schneider extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 20, 1874, 264])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 4 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements."},{"name":"10","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 28.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 31.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"10","text":"Mixed bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 12.5 mg of bufalin CRS, 12.5 mg of cinobufagin CRS and 12.5 mg of resibufogenin CRS, and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed bufalin, cinobufagin and resibufogenin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 6, 40, 80, 100, 200 mg/L for bufalin, 6, 100, 200, 300, 500 mg/L for cinobufagin and 6, 40, 80, 100, 200 mg/L for resibufogenin.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 9, 1947, 258])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bufonis-venenum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bufonis Venenum","la":"Bufonis Venenum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bulbus-fritillariae-thunbergii-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[494, 1114, 781, 1600])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[781, 1174, 1151, 1573])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1177, 1158, 1472, 1590])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii 1. Dabei 2. Zhubei\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 2, 1943, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[494, 1114, 781, 1600])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[781, 1174, 1151, 1573])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1177, 1158, 1472, 1590])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii 1. Dabei 2. Zhubei\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 2, 1943, 253])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nChinese Name: 浙貝母\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhebeimu"},{"name":"11","text":"Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii is the dried bulb of Fritillaria thunbergii Miq. (Liliaceae). The bulb is collected in early summer when the plant withers, washed clean, sorted according to size, the central bud is removed from the larger bulbs (commonly known as \"Dabei\") but not from the smaller ones (commonly known as \"Zhubei\") , and the outer scale leaf removed by crushing. Afterwards, the crushed bulb mixed with calcinated shells powder (to absorb the juice that dashes out), and then dried to obtain Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii."},{"name":"11","text":"Dabei: Bulb enclosed by an outer single scale leaf, slightly crescent-shaped, 1.1-2.1 cm high, 10-45 mm in diameter. The outer surface whitish to pale yellow, the inner surface white or pale brown, covered with white powder. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken; fracture white to yellowish-white, highly starchy. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1).\n\nZhubei: Whole bulb oblate, 0.9-2.1 cm high, 11-35 mm in diameter. Externally whitish, the 2 outer scale leaves plump and fleshy, slightly reniform in shape, held to each other, enclosing 2-3 small scale leaves and the remains of the dried shrunken stem (Fig.1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Upper epidermis of scale leaves consists of 3-7 rows of cells, lower epidermis of 2-4 rows of cells, their outer wall thickened with cuticle. Occasional crystals of calcium oxalate are visible in the epidermal cells. Vessels small, scattered in the parenchyma tissue. Parenchyma cells replete with starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[32, 0, 1968, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-white. Starch granules numerous. Simple granules ovoid to broadly ovoid or ellipsoid, 5-52 $ \\mu $m in diameter; hilum pointed, cleft-like or V-shaped in the narrowed end, striations visible. Compound granules rare. Crystals of calcium oxalate rare, minute, mostly granular, some have fusiform, square or thin bacilliform shape. Vessels mostly spiral, 3-16 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Epidermal cells subpolygonal or rectangular, anticlinal wall slightly crooked and beaded-thickened; stomata less visible, subrounded or depressed-rounded, 50-58 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with 4-5 subsidiary cells (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Peimine standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of peimine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nPeiminine standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of peiminine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide (25%, v/v), petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and acetone (0.2:10:10, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth subnitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent 1\n\nTransfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent.\n\nSpray reagent 2\n\nWeigh 5 g of sodium nitrite and dissolve in 100 mL of water.\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[860, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\n女貞子\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1871, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 239])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草 Herba Leonuri 平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis 吴茱萸\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[235, 424, 1744, 1873])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vessels and parenchyma cells\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Crystals of calcium oxalate 3. Vessels\n\n4. Parenchyma cell 5. Starch granule 6. Lower epidermis 7. Scale leaf\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[335, 449, 971, 912])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1019, 447, 1650, 912])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[340, 982, 977, 1451])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1016, 980, 1648, 1453])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[334, 1522, 975, 2004])\n\n10 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1014, 1525, 1647, 1995])\n\n10 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Vessels 3. Epidermal cells and a stoma\n\n4. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 0, 1945, 252])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 2 mL of ammonium hydroxide (25%, v/v) and 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately peimine standard solution, peiminine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent 1 and the spray reagent 2. Dry the plate in air until the spots or bands become visible (about 1-3 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of peimine and peiminine.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[587, 1657, 1206, 2021])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[646, 2059, 1263, 2470])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) peimine and (ii) peiminine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[26, 0, 1962, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Peimine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of peimine CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Macerate the mixture for 2 h. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 1 6^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ) flow: 3.2 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>100→90</td><td>0→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>90→20</td><td>10→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of peimine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of peimine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of peimine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from peimine peak should not be less than 400000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject peimine Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of peimine peak in the chromatogram of peimine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify peimine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of peimine Std-FP. The retention times of peimine\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[43, 0, 1938, 254])\n\npeaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, peimine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.03</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 1161, 1736, 1851])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[28, 3, 1956, 244])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 18.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bulbus-fritillariae-thunbergii","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii","la":"Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bulbus-fritillariae-ussuriensis-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[523, 1025, 812, 1324])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[840, 1017, 1450, 1318])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[455, 1339, 755, 1625])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[816, 1346, 1147, 1608])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1161, 1350, 1502, 1659])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1942, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[523, 1025, 812, 1324])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[840, 1017, 1450, 1318])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[455, 1339, 755, 1625])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[816, 1346, 1147, 1608])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1161, 1350, 1502, 1659])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1942, 253])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nChinese Name: 平貝母\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Pingbeimu"},{"name":"11","text":"Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis is the dried bulb of Fritillaria ussuriensis Maxim. (Liliaceae). The bulb is collected in the spring, fibrous roots and soil removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis."},{"name":"11","text":"Oblate in shape, 0.5-1 cm high, 6-20 mm in diameter. Externally milky white to pale yellowish-white. Outer scale leaves 2, plump, uniform in size or the large scale leaf embracing the smaller one, apex slightly flat or dented, frequently slightly split, the scale leaf in the centre smaller, its bottom flat. Texture hard and fragile, fracture starchy. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"The upper epidermis of scale leaves consists of 4-8 layers of cells, the lower one of 2-7 layers, their cell wall slightly thickened, some with sinuate protuberance. Crystals of calcium oxalate are visible in epidermal cells. Vascular bundles small, vessels scattered in the parenchyma tissue. Parenchyma cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour whitish. Starch granules simple, ovoid to triangular-ovoid or short-claviform, 5-52 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum cleft-like, pointed or V-shaped at the narrow end, striations dense and distinct. Compound and simple starch granules with 2-4 hila are rare. Stomata depressed-rounded, 40-50 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with 4-6 subsidiary cells. Epidermal cells subrectangular, sub-square or strip-shaped in surface view, 80-195 $ \\mu m $ long and 21-47 $ \\mu m $ wide, anticlinal wall slightly crooked. Crystals of calcium oxalate minute, polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[25, 0, 1960, 246])"},{"name":"11","text":"Peimisine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of peimisine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nPingbeimine A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of pingbeimine A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide (25%, v/v), methanol and ethyl acetate (0.5:1:6, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of vanillin and dissolve in 10 mL of sulphuric acid."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ammonium hydroxide (25%, v/v) and 30 mL of a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (4:1, v/v). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately peimisine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), pingbeimine A standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for about 1 min. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of peimisine and pingbeimine A.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[859, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1132, 53, 1246, 106])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1063, 239])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 446, 1743, 2019])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Crystals of calcium oxalate D. Vessels\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Lower epidermis 3. Scale leaf 4. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[58, 170, 262, 237])\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[320, 464, 966, 946])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1013, 467, 1658, 947])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[318, 989, 969, 1458])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1010, 989, 1661, 1468])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[323, 1507, 967, 1984])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1013, 1504, 1662, 1984])\n\n50μm"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Epidermal cells and a stoma 3. Epidermal cells\n\n4. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[37, 0, 1945, 250])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[654, 395, 1341, 683])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[694, 932, 1323, 1415])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) peimisine (ii) pingbeimine A\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Peimisine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of peimisine CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 200-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of ammonium hydroxide (25%, v/v). Allow to stand for 1 h. Add 100 mL of dichloromethane. Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 200-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[29, 0, 1961, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ）flow: 2.8 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1%Triethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>70→65</td><td>30→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-32</td><td>65→56</td><td>35→44</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>32-60</td><td>56→45</td><td>44→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of peimisine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of peimisine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of peimisine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from peimisine peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject peimisine Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of peimisine peak in the chromatogram of peimisine Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify peimisine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of peimisine Std-FP. The retention times of peimisine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[39, 1, 1937, 255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.68(vs peak4)</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.76(vs peak4)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.90(vs peak4)</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,peimisine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.22(vs peak4)</td><td>±0.08</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.29(vs peak5)</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[389, 1028, 1751, 1485])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[27, 2, 1956, 246])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 17.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/bulbus-fritillariae-ussuriensis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis","la":"Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/calomelas-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calomelas v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"6","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},{"name":"6","text":"Official Name: Calomelas\n\nChinese Name: 輕粉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qingfen"},{"name":"6","text":"Calomelas is a synthetic product of mercurous chloride $ \\left(\\mathrm{H g}_{2} \\mathrm{C l}_{2}\\right) $ . The mineral should be stored in a container protected from light."},{"name":"6","text":"Scaly or snow-flake like crystals, or crystalline powder. White in colour, occur as lustrous. Light in weight; fragile; and translucent. Colour gradually turns dull upon exposure to light. Odourless (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"6","text":"Powder\n\nColour white to pale yellowish-white. Crystals colourless, irregular in shape with distinct corners, or occasionally with smooth horizontal edge. Striations visible. Bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"6","text":"Chemical test of mercurous chloride\n\nProcedure\n\nWeigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 5 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3% , w/v). Black powder can be observed."},{"name":"6","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI.\n\nOrpiment雌黄\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\n雄黄\n\nRealgar\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1892, 46, 1984, 89])\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba 半枝蓮\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n板蓝根\n\n1a\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[286, 592, 624, 804])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[655, 513, 1058, 894])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1149, 528, 1368, 848])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1395, 511, 1696, 890])\n\n3a\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[332, 982, 560, 1114])\n\n4a\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[287, 1185, 577, 1414])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[727, 1016, 1071, 1339])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1170, 946, 1697, 1423])\n\n5a\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[326, 1545, 574, 1779])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[643, 1484, 974, 1840])\n\n6a\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1155, 1570, 1328, 1770])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1378, 1505, 1660, 1844])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"6","text":"1,2,3. Crystal with smooth horizontal edge 4,5,6. Crystal with irregular shape\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 4, 1930, 256])"},{"name":"6","text":"Finely powdered mercurous chloride (0.5 g)."},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"6","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 2 $ \\theta $) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 2 $ \\theta $ values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a 2 $ \\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0. 0 5^{o} $ when compared to the corresponding 2 $ \\theta $ values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database."},{"name":"6","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard material and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20 values of the diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the characteristic diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample as listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Calomelas\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>21.508</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>28.264</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>40.331</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>43.893</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>58.333</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[382, 2017, 1614, 2525])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference XRPD pattern of Calomelas\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 6, 1984, 262])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic diffraction peaks (Fig. 3) each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta 2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0. 2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the values stated in Table 1."},{"name":"6","text":"Sodium chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.165 g of sodium chloride and place it in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Make up to the mark with water. Pipette 10 mL of the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Transfer 7 mL of the sodium chloride standard solution to a 50-mL test tube. Add 10 mL of nitric acid (15%, w/v) and 23 mL of water. Mix well."},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of diethyl ether. Shake for 5 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness on a water bath. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of nitric acid (15%, w/v), then add 30 mL of water. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL test tube."},{"name":"6","text":"Add 1 mL of silver nitrate solution (1.7% , w/v) and 9 mL of water to the test solution and sodium chloride standard solution. Allow to stand in the dark for 5 min. The turbidity of the test solution should be less intense when compared with the sodium chloride standard solution."},{"name":"6","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XV."},{"name":"6","text":"Iodine solution\n\nWeigh 12.69 g of iodine and 36.0 g of potassium iodide, place in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Add 3 drops of hydrochloric acid and make up to the mark with water. Filter the solution."},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh 24.81 g of sodium thiosulfate and 0.2 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate, place in a 1000-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with water.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 5, 1928, 255])"},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of starch and dissolve in 5 mL of water. Add the mixture slowly to 100 mL of boiling water with shaking and boil for about 2 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL brown conical flask. Freshly prepare the indicator."},{"name":"6","text":"Weigh accurately 0.15 g of potassium dichromate and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of water. Add 2.0 g of potassium iodide and mix well. Add 40 mL of sulphuric acid (10%, w/v) and allow to stand in the dark for 10 min. Add 250 mL of water. Titrate the solution with the sodium thiosulfate titrant. Towards the end of titration, add 3 mL of starch indicator and continue the titration until brilliant green colouration can be observed. Calculate the concentration of the sodium thiosulfate titrant according to the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {C} _ {\\mathrm {S} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3} ^ {2 -}} = \\frac {\\mathrm {W} _ {\\mathrm {C r} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {7} ^ {2 -}} \\times \\mathrm {P} _ {\\mathrm {C r} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {7} ^ {2 -}} \\times 6 \\times 1 0 0 0}{\\mathrm {V} _ {\\mathrm {S} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3} ^ {2 -}} \\times \\mathrm {M w} _ {\\mathrm {C r} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {7} ^ {2 -}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $ \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}^{2-}} $ = Molarity of sodium thiosulfate titrant (mol/L)\n\n$ \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}^{2-} $ = Volume of sodium thiosulfate titrant used (mL)\n\n$ \\mathrm{Mw}_{\\mathrm{C r}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}^{2-}= $ Molecular weight of potassium dichromate (294.18 g)\n\n$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{C r}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}^{2-}= $ Weight of potassium dichromate used (g)\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {P} _ {\\mathrm {C r} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {7}} ^ {2 -} = \\text {Purity of potassium dichromate} (\\%)\n$$"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/calomelas","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calomelas","la":"Calomelas"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/campsis-flos-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Campsis Flos v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[368, 836, 1543, 1883])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Campsis Flos\n\n</div>\n\nA. Campsis Flos B. Magnified image of inner part of flower C. Magnified image of calyx\n\n1. Stamen 2. Anther 3. Style\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[43, 22, 1918, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[368, 836, 1543, 1883])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Campsis Flos\n\n</div>\n\nA. Campsis Flos B. Magnified image of inner part of flower C. Magnified image of calyx\n\n1. Stamen 2. Anther 3. Style\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[43, 22, 1918, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official name: Campsis Flos\n\nChinese name: 凌霄花\n\nChinese phonetic name: Lingxiaohua"},{"name":"11","text":"Campsis Flos is the dried flower of Campsis radicans (L.) Bureau (Bignoniaceae). The flower is collected in summer and autumn at flowering period, dried under the sun to obtain Campsis Flos."},{"name":"11","text":"Intact flowers 5.2-7.5 cm in length, yellowish-brown to brown, often wrinkled. Calyx tube campanulate, 1.1-1.8 cm in length, coriaceous, 5-toothed at the apex; the teeth short triangular, about 1/3 in the length of calyx tube, longitudinal lines on the outer surface of calyx tube indistinct. Corolla 5-lobed at the apex, with fine striations on the surface, dark brown striations on the inner surface of corolla tube are distinct. Stamens 4, didynamous, inserted at base of corolla tube, anthers divergent. Style 1, flat and short triangular. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly bitter and sour (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Pollen grains subrounded, 22-39 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with 3 furrows, surface bearing extremely fine reticulate striations. Glandular hairs of corolla pale yellowish or yellowishbrown, multicellular, subrounded, oval or oblong in apical view, 73-108 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, cells palisade-like arranged in lateral view. Glandular hairs of calyx pale yellowish or yellowishbrown, multicellular, subrounded or oval in apical view, 56-93 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, cells palisade-like arranged in lateral view. Non-glandular hairs consist of 2-7 celled, apex obtusely rounded, with linear cuticular striations on surface. Upper epidermal cells of corolla subpolygonal, with straight anticlinal walls and smooth cuticle, some containing reddish-brown substance; the lower epidermal cells of corolla colourless, with finely striated cuticle. Sclenchymatous layer of anther consists of the inner wall cells and the outer epidermal cells; the inner wall cells subpolygonal, reticulated striations visible on surface, and the outer epidermal cells wall sinuous. Spiral vessels visible, 5-14 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2).\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Fl\n\n苦杏仁 Campsis Flos 菊花\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[246, 422, 1747, 2134])"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Pollen grain (fine reticulate striations $ \\rightarrow $ ） 2. Glandular hair of corolla (2-1 apical view, 2-2 lateral view)\n\n3. Glandular hair of calyx (3-1 apical view, 3-2 lateral view)\n\n4. Non-glandular hair (linear cuticular striations $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n5. Epidermal cells of corolla (5-1 upper epidermal cells, 5-2 lower epidermal cells)\n\n6. Sclenchymatous cells of anther (6-1 inner view, 6-2 outer view) 7. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[45, 34, 1941, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Acteoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of acteoside CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nCornoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of cornoside CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, formic acid and water (32:3:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 20 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the supernatant to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol (70%). Transfer the extract to a 5-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 3 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately acteoside standard solution, cornoside standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[376, 436, 1026, 1052])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[383, 1185, 721, 1637])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) acteoside and (ii) cornoside\n\n</div>\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum苦杏仁\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[381, 378, 961, 1221])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Campsis Flos extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cornoside standard solution 2. Acteoside standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of acteoside and cornoside (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"11","text":"Acteoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of acteoside CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nCornoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of cornoside CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (70%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 40 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 19, 1951, 256])"},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (229 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-6</td><td>98.5</td><td>1.5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>6-12</td><td>98.5→85</td><td>1.5→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>12-25</td><td>85→75</td><td>15→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of acteoside Std-FP and cornoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of acteoside and cornoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of acteoside and cornoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from acteoside and cornoside peaks should not be less than 100000 and 3000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject acteoside Std-FP, cornoside Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of acteoside and cornoside peaks in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP, cornoside Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify acteoside and cornoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP and cornoside Std-FP. The retention times of acteoside and cornoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Campsis Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 12, 1951, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Campsis Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(cornoside)</td><td>0.23</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.29</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.76</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,acteoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[330, 825, 1823, 1373])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Campsis Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 36, 1940, 252])"},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.2%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.57%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 39%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 37%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/campsis-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Campsis Flos","la":"Campsis Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/carthami-flos-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carthami Flos v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[645, 1235, 1343, 1916])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Carthami Flos A. Carthami Flos B. Magnified flower C. Magnified stamens and pistil"},"sections":[{"name":"9","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[645, 1235, 1343, 1916])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Carthami Flos A. Carthami Flos B. Magnified flower C. Magnified stamens and pistil"},{"name":"9","text":"Official Name: Carthami Flos\n\nChinese Name: 红花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Honghua"},{"name":"9","text":"Carthami Flos is the dried flower of Carthamus tinctorius L. (Asteraceae). The flower is collected in summer when it turns from yellow to red, then dried in a shaded area or under the sun to obtain Carthami Flos."},{"name":"9","text":"Tubular flowers without ovaries, 6-23 mm long. Corolla tube orange-red to red, slender, 5-lobed at the apex, lobes linear. Stamens 5, anthers yellow, fused into a tube. Pistil 1, style slender, extending beyond anther tube. Texture soft. Odour slightly fragrant, taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"9","text":"Colour orange-red. Pollen grains elliptical or spherical, 27-73 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (occasionally up to 86 $ \\mu $ m), with 3 germinal pores, exine dentate-spinose. Secretory ducts long tubular, 4-49 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, filling with yellowish-brown or reddish-brown secretions, generally accompanied by spiral vessels. Epidermal cells of stigma differentiated into conical unicellular hairs, straight or slightly curved. Epidermal cells of corolla tip with slightly thickened walls, outer walls with papillary protrusions. Epidermal cells of corolla subrectangular or long strip-shaped in surface view, anticlinal walls wavy. Cells of endothecium subrectangular in surface view, cross wall beaded-thickened. Cells of anther base subsquare or subrectangular, walls thickened. Prisms of calcium oxalate mainly present in parenchymatous cells, square, rectangular or cylindrical; white to yellowish-white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2).\n\nRosae Laevigatae Fructus\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos\n\n雞冠花\n\n沙苑子 Astragali Complanati Semen\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nCarthami Flos\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[288, 565, 1670, 2114])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Carthami Flos\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. Secretory ducts 3. Fragment of stigma\n\n4. Epidermal cells of corolla tip 5. Epidermal cells of corolla 6. Fragment of endothecium\n\n7. Fragment of anther base 8. Prisms of calcium oxalate (->)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[22, 13, 1925, 249])"},{"name":"9","text":"Hydroxysafflor yellow A standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of hydroxysafflor yellow A CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 5-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Freshly prepare the standard solution."},{"name":"9","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, water, formic acid and methanol (7:3:2:0.4, v/v)."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 2-mL centrifuge tube, then add 1 mL of acetone (80%). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 1 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter. Transfer 300 $ \\mu $ L of the filtrate to a 2-mL centrifuge tube and add 300 $ \\mu $ L of acetone. Centrifuge at about $ 2 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 1 min. Collect the supernatant."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{G H L F}_{2 5 4} $ plate (150 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ thickness), a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hydroxysafflor yellow A standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[378, 1926, 1025, 2450])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structure of hydroxysafflor yellow A\n\n</div>\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCarthami Flos\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[383, 377, 856, 1027])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Carthami Flos extract observed under visible light 1. Hydroxysafflor yellow A standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of hydroxysafflor yellow A (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"9","text":"Hydroxysafflor yellow A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (12 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of hydroxysafflor yellow A CRS and place it in a 25-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (25%). Freshly prepare the standard solution."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol (25%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (25%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"9","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (403 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 5, 1905, 257])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>70→45</td><td>30→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"9","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hydroxysafflor yellow A Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of hydroxysafflor yellow A should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of hydroxysafflor yellow A peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from hydroxysafflor yellow A peak should not be less than 8000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"9","text":"Separately inject hydroxysafflor yellow A Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of hydroxysafflor yellow A peak in the chromatogram of hydroxysafflor yellow A Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hydroxysafflor yellow A peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of hydroxysafflor yellow A Std-FP. The retention times of hydroxysafflor yellow A peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Carthami Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Carthami Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,hydroxysafflor yellow A)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.34</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.35</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.91</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 369, 1836, 1004])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Carthami Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"9","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 7, 1938, 252])"},{"name":"9","text":"Red pigment: Weigh accurately 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 200-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of acetone (80%). Heat the mixture on a water bath at $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1.5 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter through a sintered-glass filter (16-40 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ pore size) and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with acetone (80%). Combine the solution and make up to the mark with acetone (80%). Measure the absorbance at 518 nm. The absorbance should not be less than 0.20.\n\nYellow pigment: Weigh accurately 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 200-mL conical flask, then add 150 mL of water. Stir for 1 h. Filter through a sintered-glass filter (16-40 $ \\mu $ m pore size) and transfer the filtrate to a 500-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with water. Combine the solution and make up to the mark with water. Measure the absorbance at 401 nm. The absorbance should not be less than 0.40."},{"name":"9","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 32.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 26.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"9","text":"Hydroxysafflor yellow A standard stock solution, Std-Stock (600 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 6.0 mg of hydroxysafflor yellow A CRS and place it in a 10-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (25%). Freshly prepare the standard solution.\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the hydroxysafflor yellow A Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (25%) to produce a series of solutions of 3,6,12,30,60 mg/L for hydroxysafflor yellow A."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/carthami-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carthami Flos","la":"Carthami Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/caryophylli-flos-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caryophylli Flos v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[596, 901, 1038, 1558])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1109, 1006, 1293, 1430])\n\nCorolla\n\nGamosepal\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[686, 1601, 868, 1771])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[935, 1584, 1138, 1796])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1163, 1587, 1363, 1797])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[596, 901, 1038, 1558])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1109, 1006, 1293, 1430])\n\nCorolla\n\nGamosepal\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[686, 1601, 868, 1771])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[935, 1584, 1138, 1796])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1163, 1587, 1363, 1797])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Caryophylli Flos\n\nB. Magnified image of Caryophylli Flos\n\nC. Magnified image of Caryophylli Flos in top view\n\nD. Magnified image of Caryophylli Flos in longitudinal section (stamen $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nE. Magnified image of Caryophylli Flos in longitudinal section (style $ \\rightarrow $ )"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Caryophylli Flos\n\nChinese name: 丁香\n\nChinese phonetic name: Dingxiang"},{"name":"14","text":"Caryophylli Flos is the dried flower buds of Syzygium aromaticum (L.) Merr. & L.M.Perry (syn. Eugenia caryophyllata Thunb. (Myrtaceae). The flower buds are collected when the green flower buds turn red, then dried under the sun to obtain Caryophylli Flos."},{"name":"14","text":"The flower buds brownish-yellow to brown, pestle shaped, 1.1-1.9 cm in length. Corolla globular, 2-5 mm in diameter. Petal 4, tegular, brown, enclosing stamens and a style. Gamosepal cylindrical, slight flattened, 2-6 mm in diameter. The upper part with four triangle sepals, separating in cruciform. Texture hard. Odour strongly aromatic; taste pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Epidermis contains thickened cuticle, 1 layer of cells. Oil glands contain yellowish oily contents before staining procedure, the boundary of secretory cells indistinct. Cortex consists of irregularly arranged 2-3 layers of elliptical oil glands, radially elongated scattered towards the outside. Bicollateral vascular bundles arranged in an interrupted ring. Vascular bundles surrounded by few fibres outside; fibres with thickened and lignified walls. Aerenchyma consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, with large intercellular spaces. Stele parenchyma relatively small, containing subrounded parenchymatous cells. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, scattered in the parenchymatous cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour brown to dark reddish-brown. Pollen grain numerous, 12-22 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, triangular in polar view and two convex lense shape in equatorial view, with 3 furrows. Vessel mainly spiral, small. Epidermal cells of filament subrectangular, arranged orderly, cell wall slightly sinuous. Oil glands 34-114 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, frequently broken, boundary of cells indistinct, containing yellow oil. Endothelium cells of pollen sac pale yellow, sub-polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls strip thickened. Epidermal cells of petal sub-polygonal in surface view, cell wall beaded thickened. Epidermal cells of receptacle pale yellow, sub-polygonal, stomata occasionally found. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous scattered in rosette aggregate, 3-22 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, occurring in relatively small parenchymatous cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres spindle-shaped, apex obtuse and walls extremely thickened, 10-50 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; bright yellow under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸隶仁\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 424, 1744, 2130])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of middle part of gamosepal of Caryophylli Flos\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Magnified bicollateral vascular bundle and pericyclic fibre\n\nD. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Oil gland 3. Cortex 4. Pericyclic fibre 5. Bicollateral vascular bundle\n\n6. Aerenchyma 7. Stele parenchyma 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n4 ___\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇菜\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[268, 688, 1702, 2127])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of the powder of Caryophylli Flos\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. Spiral vessel 3. Epidermal cells of filament\n\n4. Oil gland 5. Endothelium cells of pollen sac 6. Epidermal cells of petal\n\n7. Epidermal cells of receptacle 8. Clusters of calcium oxalate 9. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"14","text":"Acetyleugenol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of acetyleugenol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\n$ \\beta $ -Caryophyllene standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nEugenol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of eugenol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and acetone (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 80 mL of sulphuric acid to 20 mL of ethanol and dissolve 0.5 g of vanillin. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately acetyleugenol standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), eugenol standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 7 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 425, 929, 710])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 1041, 797, 1343])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 1674, 873, 1892])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) acetyleugenol (ii) $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and (iii) eugenol\n\n</div>\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\nPoha\n\n沒藥\n\n茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 372, 887, 1213])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Caryophylli Flos extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Acetyleugenol standard solution 2. $ \\beta $ -Caryophyllene standard solution\n\n3. Eugenol standard solution 4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of acetyleugenol, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and eugenol (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[17, 23, 1943, 254])"},{"name":"14","text":"Acetyleugenol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of acetyleugenol CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\n$ \\beta $ - Caryophyllene standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nEugenol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (320 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 16.0 mg of eugenol CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-INNOWax, $ 0. 3 2 \\mathrm{~ m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{~ m} $ ) of which the internal wall is covered with polyethylene glycol in a layer about $ 0. 5 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. Helium is used as the carrier gas at 1.0 mL/min. The injection temperature is at $ 2 7 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 7 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 5:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>60</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5-11</td><td>60→150</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>11-18</td><td>150</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>18-23</td><td>150→195</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>23-38</td><td>195</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>38-40</td><td>195→225</td><td>15</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of acetyleugenol Std-FP, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene Std-FP and eugenol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of acetyleugenol, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and eugenol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of acetyleugenol, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and eugenol peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from acetyleugenol, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and eugenol peaks should not be less than 330000, 250000 and 380000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject acetyleugenol Std-FP, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene Std-FP, eugenol Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of acetyleugenol, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and eugenol peaks in the chromatograms of acetyleugenol Std-FP, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene Std-FP, eugenol Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify acetyleugenol, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and eugenol peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of acetyleugenol Std-FP, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene Std-FP and eugenol Std-FP. The retention times of acetyleugenol, $ \\beta $ -caryophyllene and eugenol peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Caryophylli Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Caryophylli Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(β-caryophyllene)</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,eugenol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(acetyleugenol)</td><td>1.10</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥 茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁 C\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[339, 384, 1823, 886])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Caryophylli Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.4% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 10%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 19%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/caryophylli-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caryophylli Flos","la":"Caryophylli Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cassiae-occidentalis-semen-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cassiae Occidentalis Semen v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[473, 1166, 968, 1528])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1105, 1128, 1364, 1247])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1016, 1352, 1512, 1684])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen A. Cassiae Occidentalis Semen B. Magnified seed (lateral view) C. Magnified seeds (surface view)"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[473, 1166, 968, 1528])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1105, 1128, 1364, 1247])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1016, 1352, 1512, 1684])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen A. Cassiae Occidentalis Semen B. Magnified seed (lateral view) C. Magnified seeds (surface view)"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cassiae Occidentalis Semen\n\nChinese Name: 望江南\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Wangjiangnan"},{"name":"12","text":"Cassiae Occidentalis Semen is the dried seed of Cassia occidentalis L. (Fabaceae). The ripe legume is collected in autumn and dried under the sun, the seed is tapped out, gathered and then dried again under the sun to obtain Cassiae Occidentalis Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Flattened-ovoid, 2-5.5 mm long, 2-5 mm wide, 1.0-1.5 mm thick. Externally greyish-green to greyish-brown, with elliptical and dented striations. Hilum located at the apex. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of extremely narrow-rectangular palisade cells, covered with smooth and transparent cuticle, cuticle gradually thickened; 1/3 part of the layer on the lower part comprises a light line. Brace cells lined up in 1 layer, the cells dumbbell-shaped, wall thick. Nutritive layer consists of 3-7 layers of parenchymatous cells, the innermost layer degenerated and obliterated; vascular bundles visible in nutritive layer at both sides of the outer edge of the seed. Endosperm consists of several layers of irregular cells, containing aleurone grains. Cotyledons 2, consisting of oval and elongated cells, primary vascular bundles scattered among (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 20, 1939, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Palisade cells colourless, narrow-rectangular in lateral view, polygonal in surface view, 31-78 $ \\mu $m long, walls thickened, lumens small, with a light line. Cuticle transparent. Spiral vessels sometimes visible. Brace cells dumbbell-shaped, suborbicular in surface view, 2 concentric circles visible, 6-34 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Endosperm cells irregular in shape, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets. Cotyledon cells oval or elongated, containing oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1268, 194, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[490, 535, 960, 1182])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[346, 1202, 959, 1497])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[993, 568, 1639, 952])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[348, 1531, 960, 1927])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[992, 966, 1641, 1417])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[996, 1432, 1640, 1880])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis of testa 2. Brace cells 3. Nutritive layer 4. Endosperm\n\n5. Cotyledon 6. Primary vascular bundles in cotyledon 7. Cuticle\n\n8. Vascular bundle in nutritive layer\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1047, 40, 1181, 87])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1295, 69, 1443, 119])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1750, 73, 1846, 119])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[340, 488, 972, 970])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1000, 486, 1639, 976])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[339, 985, 640, 1499])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[661, 988, 974, 1496])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1004, 998, 1638, 1493])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[340, 1517, 972, 2017])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1004, 1516, 1636, 2017])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Palisade cells (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in surface view) 2. Cuticle 3. Spiral vessels\n\n4. Brace cells (4-1 in lateral view, 4-2 in surface view) 5. Endosperm cells\n\n6. Cotyledon cells (oil droplets $ \\rightarrow $)"},{"name":"12","text":"Physcion standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of physcion CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (15:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (95%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately physcion standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[376, 1845, 840, 2121])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of physcion\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[30, 37, 1941, 261])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 373, 772, 1189])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Physcion standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of physcion (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Physcion standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (2 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of physcion CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol (75%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Wash the residue with ethanol (75%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol (75%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (285 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.5% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>47</td><td>53</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-25</td><td>47→75</td><td>53→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-50</td><td>75→95</td><td>25→5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of physcion Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of physcion should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of physcion peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from physcion peak should not be less than 60000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject physcion Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of physcion peak in the chromatogram of physcion Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify physcion peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of physcion Std-FP. The retention times of physcion peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 36, 1938, 266])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.34</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.98</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,physcion)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.26</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[336, 881, 1800, 1274])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cassiae Occidentalis Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[68, 33, 1899, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 21.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Physcion standard stock solution, Std-Stock (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 0.3 mg of physcion CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nPhyscion standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the physcion Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (95%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 4 mg/L for physcion."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cassiae-occidentalis-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cassiae Occidentalis Semen","la":"Cassiae Occidentalis Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/catharanthi-rosei-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Catharanthi Rosei Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/catharanthi-rosei-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Catharanthi Rosei Herba","la":"Catharanthi Rosei Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/caulis-clematidis-armandii-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caulis Clematidis Armandii v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[410, 787, 1562, 1947])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Caulis Clematidis Armandii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1967, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[410, 787, 1562, 1947])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Caulis Clematidis Armandii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1967, 255])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Caulis Clematidis Armandii\n\nChinese Name: 川木通\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chuanmutong"},{"name":"10","text":"Caulis Clematidis Armandii is the dried stem of the liana Clematis armandii Franch. (Ranunculaceae). The stem is collected in the spring and autumn and, after removal of the coarse bark, the stripped stem is dried whole under the sun or, while fresh, cut into thin slices, then the slices dried under the sun to obtain Caulis Clematidis Armandii."},{"name":"10","text":"The stem is long and cylindrical, slightly twisted, 1-5 cm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown or dull yellowish-brown, marked with longitudinal grooves and ridges. Nodes usually swollen, with leaf scars and branch scars. Any bark that remains is easily torn off. The texture is hard, not easily broken, the slices 2-5 mm thick, uneven along the margin, the bark that remains yellowish-brown, the xylem pale yellowish-brown or pale yellow, with yellowish-white radial striations and cracks, vessels scattered. Pith relatively small, whitish or yellowish-brown, occasionally hollowed. Odourless with a faint taste (Fig.1)."},{"name":"10","text":"The cork layer and cortex are usually rubbed off, and the stele surrounded by curved fibre bundles. 1-2 rows of lignified fibre bundles in the phloem, and stone cells, are visible, but some groups of sieve elements are obliterated and compressed. The fascicular cambium is obvious. The xylem bundles consists of vessel elements, tracheids, xylem fibres and xylem parenchyma cells, all having\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 249])\n\ncell wall lignified; the diameter of the vessels vary in size. Medullary rays 21-26 cells high, 6-8 cells wide, thin-walled, lignified, usually with small pits; secondary rays smaller. Parenchyma cells in the pith somewhat rounded, with a slightly lignified wall and having small pits (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Phloem fibres elongated, fusiform, with two ends relatively sharp, 287 882 $ \\mu $ m long or even longer occasionally, 21-28 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with thick lignified wall and small lumina, few phloem fibres have larger lumina and possess septa or simple pits. Stone cells subrectangular or fusiform, walls thick, pit canals and pits obvious, 38-119 $ \\mu $ m long, 28-39 $ \\mu $ m wide. Most bordered pitted vessels 28-152 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Bordered pitted tracheids also present, 17-25 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, the wall lignified. The xylem fibres, with thickened wall, lignified, 267 493 $ \\mu $ m long, 24-29 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, possessing simple pits, bordered pits, and aggregated reticulated pits. Xylem parenchyma cells rectangular, with the slightly slender end connected with fibres, 89 191 $ \\mu $ m long, 23-37 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, the wall thick and lignified, with simple pits (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 5 mL of dichloromethane Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer 1 mL of filtrate to another test tube Cautiously add about 1 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for 30 min. A red or reddish-brown ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"10","text":"Oleanolic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of oleanolic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and acetone (4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 0, 1983, 255])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 418, 1737, 2006])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Caulis Clematidis Armandii\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration (the part of pericycle fibres has rubbed off)\n\nC(i) and (ii). Phloem fibre bundles D. Ray cells\n\n1. Pericycle fibres 2. Phloem 3. Phloem fibres 4. Cambium 5. Xylem 6. Ray 7. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[17, 12, 1961, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[426, 435, 947, 799])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[997, 447, 1548, 797])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[429, 814, 766, 1192])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[790, 828, 1141, 1192])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1182, 818, 1542, 1183])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[430, 1214, 951, 1589])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1047, 1219, 1543, 1583])\n\n100 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Caulis Clematidis Armandii (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Phloem fibre 2. A group of stone cells 3. Subrectangular stone cells 4. Tracheid\n\n5. Xylem parenchyma cells 6. Xylem fibre 7. Bordered pitted vessels\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 254])"},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 150-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 5 mL of 12 M hydrochloric acid. Reflux the mixture for 3 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1.5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately oleanolic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 70^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of oleanolic acid.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[782, 1524, 1370, 1880])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[723, 1943, 1426, 2454])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) oleanolic acid and (ii) hesperidin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Hesperidin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (80 mg/L) [Hesperidin is added as a marker (Internal standard)].\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of hesperidin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL conical flask, then add 2.5 mL of hesperidin Std-FP and 15 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction twice. Combine the filtrate. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (205 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>97</td><td>3</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-20</td><td>97→82</td><td>3→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>82→65</td><td>18→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hesperidin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of hesperidin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of hesperidin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from hesperidin peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 253])"},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject hesperidin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of hesperidin peak in the chromatogram of the hesperidin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify hesperidin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of hesperidin Std-FP. The retention times of hesperidin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Caulis Clematidis Armandii extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Caulis Clematidis Armandii extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,hesperidin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[403, 1695, 1755, 2231])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Caulis Clematidis Armandii extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%.\n\n5. 8 Detection of Aristolochic Acid I (Appendix XIII): meet the requirements."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[585, 377, 1515, 1040])\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Lane</td><td>Sample</td><td>Results</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Blank(Methanol)</td><td>Negative</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Sample(Caulis Clematidis Armandii)</td><td>Oleanolic acid positive</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sample duplicate(Caulis Clematidis Armandii)</td><td>Oleanolic acid positive</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Standard(Oleanolic acid)</td><td>Oleanolic acid positive</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Spiked sample(Sample plus oleanolic acid)</td><td>Oleanolic acid positive</td></tr></table>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 TLC results of Caulis Clematidis Armandii extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/caulis-clematidis-armandii","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caulis Clematidis Armandii","la":"Caulis Clematidis Armandii"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/celosiae-cristatae-flos-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Celosiae Cristatae Flos v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[662, 874, 898, 1141])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[987, 945, 1155, 1127])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1170, 922, 1358, 1123])\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[588, 1237, 1338, 1901])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Celosiae Cristatae Flos A. Celosiae Cristatae Flos B. Magnified single flower C. Magnified seeds"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[662, 874, 898, 1141])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[987, 945, 1155, 1127])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1170, 922, 1358, 1123])\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[588, 1237, 1338, 1901])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Celosiae Cristatae Flos A. Celosiae Cristatae Flos B. Magnified single flower C. Magnified seeds"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Celosiae Cristatae Flos\n\nChinese Name: 雞冠花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jiguanhua"},{"name":"12","text":"Celosiae Cristatae Flos is the dried capitulum of Celosia cristata L. (Amaranthaceae). The capitulum is collected in autumn, then dried under the sun to obtain Celosiae Cristatae Flos."},{"name":"12","text":"Terminal inflorescence, mostly flatten and fleshy, cockscomb-shaped, 8-25 cm long, 5-22 cm wide. The upper margin broad, with wrinkles and densely linear scales, narrowed towards the base and usually with remnants of flat stem. Externally red, purplish-red or yellowish-white, with numerous florets aggregated below the middle part, persistent bracts and perianth of the florets membranous. Fruit an utricle, seeds black, oblate and reniform, petty, about 1 mm in diameter, lustrous. Texture pliable and light in weight. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Peduncle: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Cortex narrow, with parenchymatous cells occasionally contain microcrystals of calcium oxalate. Phloem relatively narrow. Cambium distinct. Xylem well developed, composed of vessels, xylem fibres and xylem parenchymatous cells. Vessels scattered singly or in groups. Pith broad, amphivasal vascular bundles scattered in the outer side of the pith area, microcrystals of calcium oxalate occasionally visible in the parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[43, 8, 1964, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-brown to brown. Pollen grains subglobular, 13-32 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, exine with small protrusions. Epidermal cells of peduncle subsquare or subrectangular, walls thin. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate present in parenchymatous cells or scattered, slightly arrowhead-like in shape, extremely minute; bright white to polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of testa polygonal, pits thickened. Anther cells annular thickened. Vessels mainly scalariform, 6-28 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Fibres mostly in bundles, 5-22 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 410, 1740, 2116])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of peduncle of Celosiae Cristatae Flos A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Cambium 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Pith 7. Amphivasal vascular bundle 8. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[176, 23, 341, 79])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[552, 99, 716, 154])\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 159, 1235, 211])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 102, 1590, 162])\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[236, 475, 1673, 2123])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Celosiae Cristatae Flos\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. Epidermal cells of peduncle\n\n3. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate in parenchymatous cells\n\n4. Epidermal cells of testa 5. Anther cells 6. Vessels 7. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Kaempferol standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of kaempferol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate, formic acid and glacial acetic acid (10:6:0.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of a mixture of ethanol, water and hydrochloric acid (50:20:8, v/v). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer 10 mL of supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of methanol and then filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately kaempferol standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 1 min). Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[552, 97, 717, 155])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 158, 1237, 212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[388, 368, 906, 695])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[436, 755, 907, 1019])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) isorhamnetin and (ii) kaempferol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 1173, 860, 1982])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Celosiae Cristatae Flos extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Kaempferol standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of kaempferol (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Kaempferol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (16 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.16 mg of kaempferol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of a mixture of ethanol, water and hydrochloric acid (50:20:8, v/v). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (365 nm) and a column $ (4.6\\times 250\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 27^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>60→40</td><td>40→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>40</td><td>60</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>40→25</td><td>60→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of kaempferol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of kaempferol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of kaempferol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from kaempferol peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[551, 99, 717, 154])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1072, 157, 1236, 212])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject kaempferol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of kaempferol peak in the chromatogram of kaempferol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify kaempferol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of kaempferol Std-FP. The retention times of kaempferol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Celosiae Cristatae Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Celosiae Cristatae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,kaempferol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(isorhamnetin)</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.64</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[356, 1597, 1798, 2300])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Celosiae Cristatae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 13, 1926, 254])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 11.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 21.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 16.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/celosiae-cristatae-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Celosiae Cristatae Flos","la":"Celosiae Cristatae Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/celosiae-semen-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Celosiae Semen v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[398, 848, 1576, 1972])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Celosiae Semen\n\nA. Celosiae Semen B. Magnified image of ripe seeds"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[398, 848, 1576, 1972])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Celosiae Semen\n\nA. Celosiae Semen B. Magnified image of ripe seeds"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Celosiae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 青葙子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qingxiangzi"},{"name":"12","text":"Celosiae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Celosia argentea L. (Amaranthaceae). The plant is cut up or the infructescence is collected in autumn when the fruit is ripe, dried under the sun, and the seeds gathered, foreign matter removed to obtain Celosiae Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Oblate, seldom rounded-reniform, 0.8-1.2 mm in diameter. Externally black or reddish-black, lustrous, slightly protuberant at the centre, with an oblique and small acute hilum on the slightly dented lateral side. Testa thin and fragile, polygonal reticulate striations visible when magnified. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Outer epidermal cell walls of testa dark reddish-brown, prominently thickened. Inner layer cells of testa flat, colourless or pale yellow. Endosperm cells filled with starch granules, aleurone grains and prisms of calcium oxalate. Cotyledons and radicle consist of parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-black. Outer epidermal cells of testa dark reddish-brown, polygonal to elongated-polygonal in surface view, with polygonal, grid-shaped and thickened striations. Inner layer cells of testa colourless or pale yellow, polygonal in surface view, with dense, fine and straight striations. Prisms of calcium oxalate present in endosperm cells, diamond-shaped, square or rectangular. Endosperm cells filled with starch granules, aleurone grains and oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\nSaussureae Involucratae Herba\n\n天山雪莲\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根\n\nMenispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nBruceae Fructus 鸦膽子\n\nCelosiae Semen\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[258, 481, 869, 1243])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[328, 1274, 870, 1978])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1140, 542, 1703, 1913])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Celosiae Semen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Cotyledon 2. Outer epidermis of testa 3. Inner layer cells of testa\n\n4. Endosperm 5. Radicle\n\nTinosporae Radix\n\n金果欖\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[362, 431, 992, 931])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1048, 431, 1623, 928])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[363, 954, 992, 1415])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1049, 953, 1622, 1412])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[365, 1445, 993, 1971])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1044, 1447, 1618, 1974])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Celosiae Semen (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Outer epidermal cells of testa 2. Inner layer cells of testa 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate (→)\n\n4. Aleurone grains 5. Oil droplets 6. Starch granules"},{"name":"12","text":"Celosin H standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of celosin H CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 50% ).\n\nCelosin I standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of celosin I CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol, water and formic acid (13:7:2:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 2 8 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 150-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of water. Load the sample solution to the solid-phase extraction column containing ODS packing (12 mL, 2 g) pre-conditioned with 24 mL of methanol and 24 mL of water. Add 24 mL of water to the column. Discard the eluant. Add 24 mL of a mixture of ethanol and water (6:4, v/v) to the column. Collect the eluant and transfer to a 150-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately celosin H standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), celosin I standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[384, 368, 1503, 794])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[438, 875, 1578, 1393])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) celosin H and (ii) celosin I\n\n</div>\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba杠板歸\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 379, 931, 1133])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Celosiae Semen extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Celosin H standard solution 2. Celosin I standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of celosin H and celosin I (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Celosin H standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of celosin H CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nCelosin I standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (120 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of celosin I CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 2 8 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction with 30 mL of ethanol (50%). Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in ethanol (50%). Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.20- $ \\mu $ m polyester filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[61, 33, 1927, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right) $ flow rate: 2.5 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times1 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.6 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.1% formic acid and acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid (70:30, v/v). The elution time is about 45 min."},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of celosin H Std-FP and celosin I Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of celosin H and celosin I should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of celosin H and celosin I peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from celosin H and celosin I peaks should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject celosin H Std-FP, celosin I Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of celosin H and celosin I peaks in the chromatograms of celosin H Std-FP, celosin I Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify celosin H and celosin I peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of celosin H Std-FP and celosin I Std-FP. The retention times of celosin H and celosin I peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Celosiae Semen extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Celosiae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,celosinH)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.24</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(celosinI)</td><td>1.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[324, 364, 1809, 926])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Celosiae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed celosin H and celosin I standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L for celosin H and 1200 mg/L for celosin I)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of celosin H CRS and 12.0 mg of celosin I CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/celosiae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Celosiae Semen","la":"Celosiae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/centellae-herba-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Centellae Herba v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[588, 956, 817, 1177])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[874, 1005, 1132, 1155])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1211, 976, 1416, 1163])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[586, 1282, 1431, 1827])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Centellae Herba\n\nA. Centellae Herba B. Magnified image of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of fruits D. Magnified image of flowers\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1872, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[588, 956, 817, 1177])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[874, 1005, 1132, 1155])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1211, 976, 1416, 1163])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[586, 1282, 1431, 1827])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Centellae Herba\n\nA. Centellae Herba B. Magnified image of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of fruits D. Magnified image of flowers\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1872, 260])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Centellae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 積雪草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jixuecao"},{"name":"15","text":"Centellae Herba is the dried whole plant of Centella asiatica (L.) Urb. (Apiaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer and autumn, soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Centellae Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Usually crumpled into masses. Roots cylindrical, 2-4 cm long, 1-1.5 mm in diameter, externally pale yellow or greyish-brown. Stems slender and curved, yellowish-brown, with fine longitudinal wrinkles, and usually with fibrous roots on the nodes. Leaves mostly crumpled and broken, when intact flattened out, subrounded or reniform, 10-50 mm in diameter, greyish-green, margins roughly crenate; petioles 3-6 cm long, twisted. Umbels axillary, small. Cremocarps oblate, with distinctly protuberant longitudinal ridges and fine reticulate striations; fruit stalk extremely short. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig.1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Root: Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex narrow, consists of 3-4 layers of parenchymatous cells. Phloem broad, cells mostly crumpled. Cambium consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged in a ring. Xylem fibres visible; xylem ray consists 1-3 rows of cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells subrounded or subsquare, with 2-4 layers of collenchymatous cells beneath. Cortex consists of 7-9 layers of parenchymatous cells, several outer layer cells with unevenly thickened walls. Collateral vascular bundles 6-8; slightly lignified fibre groups occurring at the outside of phloem; fascicular cambium distinct, consists of 2-3 layers of small cells; xylem vessels arranged radically. Pith large, consists of\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 4, 1944, 259])\n\nlarge parenchymatous cells. Secretory canal located in cortex and rays, 23-34 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, composed of 5-7 secretory cells [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells polygonal, relatively large. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cells. Spongy tissue arranged loosely. Collenchyma located at the upper side of midrib. Vascular bundles of midrib collateral. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells polygonal, relatively large [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour brownish-yellow. Stomata anomocytic or anisocytic. Prisms of calcium oxalate abundant, 3-21 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains spherical, 11-43 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, sculpture indistinct, with 3 germinal pores. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found. Vessels mainly spiral, 4-71 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Secretory canals contain numerous yellow substances. Non-glandular hairs multicellular, mostly broken, intact cells fusiform, relatively straight (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 22, 1898, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 421, 1747, 2113])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Centellae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 8, 1947, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 441, 1748, 2088])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Centellae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchymatous cell 3. Cortex 4. Fibres 5. Phloem 6. Fascicular cambium\n\n7. Xylem 8. Pith 9. Secretory canal\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[242, 391, 1736, 2133])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Centellae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Collenchyma 5. Xylem 6. Phloem\n\n7. Lower epidermis\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\nFritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nCentellae Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[285, 504, 1705, 2050])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Centellae Herba\n\n1. Stomata (1-1 anomocytic, 1-2 anisocytic) 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Pollen grains\n\n4. Cluster of calcium oxalate 5. Spiral vessel 6. Secretory canals 7. Non-glandular hair\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 25, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"15","text":"Asiaticoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of asiaticoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nMadecassoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of madecassoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, glacial acetic acid and water (8:2.6:2.5, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately asiaticoside standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), madecassoside standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n穿山龍 Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香 Inulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nCentellae Herba\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[442, 369, 1208, 1086])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[449, 1163, 1200, 1857])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) asiaticoside and (ii) madecassoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[31, 18, 1884, 259])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[385, 372, 925, 1134])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Centellae Herba extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Madecassoside standard solution 2. Asiaticoside standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of asiaticoside and madecassoside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Asiaticoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (250 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of asiaticoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (80%).\n\nMadecassoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (250 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of madecassoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (80%)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of methanol (80%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[41, 8, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (205 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.15% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>21</td><td>79</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-32</td><td>21→36</td><td>79→64</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>32-50</td><td>36→40</td><td>64→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>40→80</td><td>60→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of asiaticoside Std-FP and madecassoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of asiaticoside and madecassoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of asiaticoside and madecassoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from asiaticoside and madecassoside peaks should not be less than 100000 and 200000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject asiaticoside Std-FP, madecassoside Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of asiaticoside and madecassoside peaks in the chromatograms of asiaticoside Std-FP, madecassoside Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify asiaticoside and madecassoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of asiaticoside Std-FP and madecassoside Std-FP. The retention times of asiaticoside and madecassoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[31, 7, 1883, 264])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Centellae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Centellae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.98</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,madecassoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(asiaticoside)</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.22</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(madecassic acid)</td><td>1.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7(asiatic acid)</td><td>2.37</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[390, 1226, 1822, 1897])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Centellae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements (except cadmium should not be more than 5.5 mg/kg).\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[40, 19, 1946, 257])\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Total ash: not more than 12.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 26.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 24.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/centellae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Centellae Herba","la":"Centellae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/centipedae-herba-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Centipedae Herba v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[712, 935, 925, 1093])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1107, 866, 1254, 1089])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[566, 1172, 1323, 1922])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Centipedae Herba A. Centipedae Herba B. Magnified image of capitulum with numerous flowers C. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[712, 935, 925, 1093])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1107, 866, 1254, 1089])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[566, 1172, 1323, 1922])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Centipedae Herba A. Centipedae Herba B. Magnified image of capitulum with numerous flowers C. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Centipedae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 鵝不食草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Ebushicao"},{"name":"13","text":"Centipedae Herba is the dried whole plant of Centipeda minima (L.) A. Br. et Aschers. (Asteraceae). The whole plant containing rootlets is collected in summer and autumn at flowering, soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Centipedae Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Tangled masses of stems and leaves. Rootlets fine, pale yellow. Stems slender, frequently branched; texture fragile, easily broken, fracture yellowish-white. Leaves small, nearly sessile; lamina mostly crimped and broken, spatulate-shaped when intact flattened out, externally greyish-green or brown, margin 3-5-dentate. Capitulum yellow to yellowish-brown. Odour slightly fragrant, irritating with prolonged smelling; taste bitter and slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Epidermal cells subrounded, elongated tangentially, the walls finely thickened. Cortex consists of 5-8 layers of cells, the cells subrounded, relative large, with numerous clefts. Endodermis distinct. Phloem narrow, cells elongated tangentially. Xylem broad, vessels arranged radially [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, the cells subrounded or elongated tangentially. Cortex consists of 5-8 layers of cells, clefts large. Fibres 4-15 in bundles, located on outer side of the phloem. Phloem narrow, the cells elongated tangentially. Xylem broad, vessels arranged radially. Pith distinct [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 4, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green to greyish-brown. Glandular hairs paramecium-like in surface view, cells arranged in pairs, containing yellow contents. Non-glandular hairs biseriate, one unicellular, slightly short, the other bicellular, basal cell relatively short, apical cell hooked or rolled, with fine cuticular striations on the surface of 2/3 of upper part. Pollen grains pale yellow, subglobular, 11-23 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with 3 germinal furrows, exine spiny. Epidermal cells of corolla yellow, rectangular to subpolygonal in surface view, with cuticular striations, cells extended outwards to form tomentose protuberance. Epidermal cells of stem rectangular or subpolygonal, walls slightly thickened, with indistinct cuticular striations, stomata visible. Epidermal cells of leaf subpolygonal, anticlinal walls thin and sinuous in surface view, stomata anomocytic, subsidiary cells 4-6 (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 436, 951, 1061])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1033, 453, 1706, 1211])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[282, 1574, 904, 2005])\n\n25 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1031, 1240, 1703, 2001])\n\nB\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Centipedae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Endodermis\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Endodermis 5. Phloem 6. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 7, 1947, 256])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[234, 444, 1732, 2125])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Centipedae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibres\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Fibres 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[283, 449, 945, 938])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[972, 449, 1634, 940])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[284, 973, 942, 1467])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[972, 971, 1636, 1468])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[284, 1490, 943, 1987])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[971, 1504, 1637, 1987])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Centipedae Herba (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Glandular hairs 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Pollen grains 4. Epidermal cells of corolla\n\n5. Epidermal cells of stem with stomata 6. Epidermal cells of leaf with stomata\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 10, 1944, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"3,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (5:5:2:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 14, 1884, 262])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[376, 362, 926, 770])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[381, 929, 800, 1801])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Centipedae Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. 3,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[41, 12, 1945, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"3,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (326 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 190 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 12% carbon loading). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.08% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>12→22</td><td>88→78</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>22→35</td><td>78→65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peak should not be less than 55000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peak in the chromatogram of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP. The retention times of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Centipedae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Centipedae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.17</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.34</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.73</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[386, 1726, 1591, 2354])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Centipedae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 12, 1946, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 22.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 11.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/centipedae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Centipedae Herba","la":"Centipedae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cervi-cornu-pantotrichum-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cervi Cornu Pantotrichum v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[836, 707, 1316, 1336])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of young unossified hairy antler of male Cervus nippon Temminck A. A young unossified hairy male antler (Ergang) B. Slice\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[637, 1702, 1365, 2415])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"17","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[836, 707, 1316, 1336])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of young unossified hairy antler of male Cervus nippon Temminck A. A young unossified hairy male antler (Ergang) B. Slice\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[637, 1702, 1365, 2415])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"17","text":"Official Name: Cervi Cornu Pantotrichum\n\nChinese Name: 鹿茸\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Lurong"},{"name":"17","text":"Cervi Cornu Pantotrichum is the young unossified hairy antler of male Cervus nippon Temminck or Cervus elaphus Linnaeus (Cervidae). The former is known as \"Hualurong\" (sika deer pilose antler), the later known as \"Malurong\" (red deer pilose antler). The young unossified hairy antler is collected when the deer is aged 3, \"Ergang\" collected twice a year. The antler collected 45-50 days after Ching Ming festival is known as \"Tou cha rong\", the one collected after the beginning of Autumn is known as \"Er cha rong\". \"San Cha\" collect once a year, in late July. After the antler collected, then process: blood and dirt removed, washed clean, dried in the shaded area or baked to dryness below $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to obtain Cervi Cornu Pantotrichum."},{"name":"17","text":"Cervus nippon Temminck: Branched, cylindrical, commonly known as \"Ergang\" with one branch; the main branch as \"Dating\", 16-20 cm long, slightly triquetrous, apex obtuse and rounded. The side branch arising at about 2 cm from the cut face or at the cut face, known as \"Menzhuang\", 8-12 cm long, slightly smaller than the main branch in diameter. The out skin reddish-brown or brown, usually lustrous, densely covered with reddish-yellow or brownish-yellow soft hairs, relatively dense at the upper end, and sparse at the lower end, a greyish-black vein at the base between the main and side branches, the skin and hairs nestled each other. The cut surface yellowish-white, without bony element in the outer part, small pores densely distributed in the central part. Texture light in weight. Odour slightly stinky; taste slightly salty [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nCervus elaphus Linnaeus: Larger and thicker than Hualurong, with relatively more branches, commonly known as \"Sancha\" with three branches. The main branch (Dating) thick and strong, 25-100 cm long, 4-5 cm in diameter. The two branches arising from the base of antler, closed each other, the second branch farther away from the third one. Apex obtuse and rounded. The out skin greyish-brown, soft hairs thick and strong, dense, greyish-yellow to greyish-brown. The cut face greyish-black, small pores densely distributed in the central part. Texture tender or bony. Odour stinky; taste salty [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 8, 1944, 256])"},{"name":"17","text":"Cervus nippon Temminck: The outer surface of skin sinuous, soft hairs numerous and dense, or relatively sparse, easily fallen off during transverse section processing. Transverse section consists of layers of skin, mesenchyme and bone. Layers of skin consist of epidermis and dermis. The outermost layer of epidermis composed of the stratum corneum, arranged in scale-shape; the inner consists of several layers of stratum germinativum, with papilla protruding from the dermis. Dermis relatively thick, mainly consists of dense connective tissues, scattered with hair follicles, sebaceous glands and blood vessels. Sebaceous glands scattered singly or several in groups; blood vessels varied in size, with thickened wall and indistinct lumen, concentric and ring-shape striations distinct, surrounded by relatively dense or sparse tissues. Mesenchyme consists of sparse connective tissues. Bone mainly consists of bone trabecula. Bone trabecula spongy, covered with numerous bone lacunae and bone tubes on surface [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nCervus elaphus Linnaeus: Soft hairs relatively numerous, easily fallen off during transverse section processing. Papillary layer flattened, without obviously denticulate protruding. The inner part of dermis scattered with large long-oblate and elliptical blood vessels, elongated tangentially, lumen closed, surrounded by small blood vessels. Bone trabecula near mesenchyme relatively thin and sparse, and those in the central part relatively thick, sparsely reticulated, bone lacunae relatively less [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Cervus nippon Temminck: Colour pale yellow. Stratum corneum pale yellow, externally granular, rough and uneven, alveoli rounded and hole-shaped after soft hairs fall off, edge even. Fragments of bone nearly colourless or pale yellowish-brown, irregular pieces, with fine and dense longitudinal striations and dotted pores externally; bone lacunae relatively numerous, rounded, subtriangular or subfusiform, varing in size, 8-22 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, arranged irregularly, edge tubes visible faintly, showing radial furrows. Hairs mostly broken, the middle part of hair shaft 11-56 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, externally covered with many scale-shaped and transparent hair cuticle cells, imbricate, the free edge faced the hair point, spiny, with longitudinal and straight striations, medulla interrupted or absent, greyish-black or greyish-brown; bright white under the polarized microscope. Hair base often connected with hair follicle, base expanded, elliptical or subrounded [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 13, 1892, 261])\n\nCervus elaphus Linnaeus: Colour greyish-yellow. Stratum corneum pale yellow or brown, with relatively more alveoli. Bone lacunae of fragments of bone relatively large, arranged sparsely, 11-26 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with indistinct fine and dense longitudinal striations externally. The middle part of hair shaft 15-70 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, scale-shaped cells arranged parallel externally, medulla beaded or reticulate; bright white under the polarized microscope. Hair base often connected with hair follicle, base expanded, elliptical or subrounded [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[41, 2, 1946, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 431, 1746, 2078])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"17","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Sebaceous glands D. Blood vessel\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hair follicle 3. Sebaceous gland 4. Dermis 5. Blood vessel 6. Mesenchyme\n\n7. Bone 8. Bone trabecula 9. Stratum corneum 10. Stratum germinativum 11. Papillary layer\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 16, 1892, 258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[224, 440, 1749, 2073])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"17","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Sebaceous glands D. Blood vessel\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hair follicle 3. Sebaceous gland 4. Dermis 5. Blood vessel 6. Mesenchyme\n\n7. Bone 8. Bone trabecula 9. Stratum corneum 10. Stratum germinativum 11. Papillary layer\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 5, 1953, 255])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[256, 650, 1707, 2126])"},{"name":"17","text":"1. Stratum corneum (alveolus $ \\longrightarrow $ ） 2. Fragments of bone contain bone lacunae 3. Hair shaft\n\n4. Hair base with hair follicle\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[33, 21, 1898, 255])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[251, 592, 1712, 2130])"},{"name":"17","text":"1. Stratum corneum (alveolus\n\n4. Hair base with hair follicle\n\n2. Fragments of bone containing bone lacunae\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 14, 1945, 256])"},{"name":"17","text":"Cholesterol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of cholesterol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (40-60 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), diethyl ether and glacial acetic acid (5:5:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately cholesterol standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the TLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the TLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[451, 369, 879, 711])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[454, 764, 868, 1085])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[454, 1145, 655, 1355])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n( v )\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[455, 1432, 761, 1741])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[454, 1811, 638, 1992])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vi)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[454, 2072, 756, 2419])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) cholest-4-en-3-one (ii) cholesterol (iii) hypoxanthine (iv) inosine (v) uracil and (vi) uridine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 11, 1948, 255])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[385, 375, 1010, 1216])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference TLC chromatogram of Cervi Cornu Pantotrichum extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cholesterol standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) young unossified hairy antler of male Cervus nippon Temminck\n\n(ii) young unossified hairy antler of male Cervus elaphus Linnaeus\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of cholesterol (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"17","text":"5 mM Ammonium acetate solution\n\nWeigh 0.385 g of ammonium acetate and dissolve in 1000 mL of water."},{"name":"17","text":"Hypoxanthine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hypoxanthine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (10%).\n\nInosine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of inosine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (10%).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[33, 23, 1889, 260])\n\nUracil standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of uracil CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (10%).\n\nUridine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of uridine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (10%)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol (10%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 45 min. Centrifuge at about 3000 x g for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"17","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (260 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with alkyl reversed-phase bonded silica gel with diisopropyl side chain (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.4-1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>5mM Ammoniumacetate solution(%，v/v)</td><td>Flow rate(mL/min)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-18</td><td>1</td><td>99</td><td>0.4-1.0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>18-20</td><td>1-4</td><td>99-96</td><td>1.0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-25</td><td>4-5</td><td>96-95</td><td>1.0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-40</td><td>5-20</td><td>95-80</td><td>1.0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hypoxanthine Std-FP, inosine Std-FP, uracil Std-FP and uridine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of hypoxanthine, inosine, uracil and uridine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of hypoxanthine, inosine, uracil and uridine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from hypoxanthine, inosine, uracil and uridine peaks should not be less than 16000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[41, 6, 1946, 257])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cervi-cornu-pantotrichum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cervi Cornu Pantotrichum","la":"Cervi Cornu Pantotrichum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/changii-radix-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Changii Radix v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[470, 835, 1375, 1529])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[633, 1566, 962, 1772])\n\nB 5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1073, 1556, 1347, 1778])\n\nC 2mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Changii Radix\n\nA. Changii Radix\n\nB. Magnified image of reddish-brown mottle ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[470, 835, 1375, 1529])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[633, 1566, 962, 1772])\n\nB 5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1073, 1556, 1347, 1778])\n\nC 2mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Changii Radix\n\nA. Changii Radix\n\nB. Magnified image of reddish-brown mottle ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},{"name":"11","text":"Official name: Changii Radix\n\nChinese name: 明黛參\n\nChinese phonetic name: Mingdangshen"},{"name":"11","text":"Changii Radix is the dried root of Changium smyrnioides H. Wolff (Apiaceae). The root is collected in April and May, rootlets removed, washed, then boiled thoroughly until no white core is found. The boiled root is peeled, rewashed and dried under the sun to obtain Changii Radix."},{"name":"11","text":"Slender cylindrical, long fusiform or irregularly stripe-shaped, 5-22 cm in length, 3-30 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to pale brown when peeled; smooth, with longitudinally furrows and rootlet scars, sometimes the surface marked with reddish-brown mottles or with remnants of brownish cork. Texture hard and fragile, fracture surface horny. Bark relatively thin, yellowish-white, sometimes easily separated from whitish wood. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork mostly removed, while the remaining cork consists of several layers of flat cells. Phelloderm narrow, with small amount of secretory ducts scattered. Phloem broad, containing numerous secretory ducts surrounded by 5-7 secretory cells with yellow secretion inside. Cambium arranged in a ring. Xylem arranged radially, vessels single or 2-5 in groups. Masses of gelatinized starch granules found in parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Gelatinized starch granules fairly abundant, mostly found in parenchymatous cells, irregular pieces, colourless to light yellow. Fragments of secretory duct containing yellowish-brown secretion. Annular vessels 10-95 $ \\mu $m in diameter, lignified. Reticulate vessels 24-99 $ \\mu $m in diameter, lignified (Fig. 3).\n\n明黛參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸隶仁\n\nChangii Radix\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[332, 428, 993, 1159])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1022, 472, 1685, 972])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[353, 1235, 986, 1652])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1024, 1046, 1684, 1470])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[354, 1669, 989, 2059])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1018, 1555, 1687, 2018])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Changii Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory duct D. Gelatinized starch granule\n\n1. Cork 2. Phelloderm 3. Secretory duct 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n7. Gelatinized starch granule\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[305, 505, 980, 997])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1005, 507, 1677, 1005])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[309, 1028, 985, 1500])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1002, 1026, 1678, 1501])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[303, 1525, 979, 2011])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1004, 1525, 1680, 2014])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Mass of gelatinized starch granule 2. Fragment of secretory duct 3. Annular vessel\n\n4. Reticulate vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 14, 1973, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone and formic acid (6:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel 60 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ). Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[383, 371, 896, 666])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[376, 960, 861, 1800])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Changii Radix extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L) Weigh 2.0 mg of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (215 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.4 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-2</td><td>98</td><td>2</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>2-13</td><td>98→85</td><td>2→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>13-20</td><td>85→45</td><td>15→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>45</td><td>55</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu $ L each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak in the chromatograms of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside Std-FP. The retention times of vanillic acid 4-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Changii Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Changii Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, vanillic acid4-O-β-D-glucopyranoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.28</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>3.63</td><td>±0.09</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[330, 1627, 1820, 2269])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Changii Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.6%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/changii-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Changii Radix","la":"Changii Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/chebulae-fructus-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chebulae Fructus v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[651, 713, 1325, 1294])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[629, 1659, 1317, 2333])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Fruits B. Kernels C. Magnified seed"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[651, 713, 1325, 1294])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[629, 1659, 1317, 2333])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Fruits B. Kernels C. Magnified seed"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Chebulae Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 訶子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hezi"},{"name":"13","text":"Chebulae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula Retz. or Terminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt. (Combretaceae). The fruit is collected in autumn and winter when ripe, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Chebulae Fructus."},{"name":"13","text":"Terminalia chebula Retz.: Ellipsoid to ovoid, 2.2-3.8 cm long and 14-23 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to brown, slightly lustrous, marked with 5-6 longitudinal ribs and irregular wrinkles, the base with a rounded fruit stalk scar. Texture hard and compact. Sarcocarp yellowish-brown to dark yellowish-brown; kernels 1.4-2.5 cm long, 9-16 mm in diameter, pale yellow, rough and hard. Seeds narrowly fusiform, testa yellowish-brown. Odour slight; taste sour, astringent, and followed by sweet [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nTerminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.: Ellipsoid to ovoid, 2.3-3.5 cm long and 13-19 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to dark brown, with extremely shrunk wrinkles. Sarcocarp yellowish-brown to brown, kernels 1.6-3.2 cm long, 11-15 mm in diameter [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 8, 1970, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Pericarp:\n\nTerminalia chebula Retz.: Exocarp consists of 2-8 layers of irregular square collenchymatous cells, arranged closely, covered with cuticle. Mesocarp consists of several layers of big parenchymatous cells, arranged loosely with slightly thickened wall, 10-40 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ long radically, 50-140 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ long tangentially, containing brown resin mass. Sclerenchyma consists of 3-5 layers of rope-shaped sclerenchymatous cells, tangentially telescoped, with numerous radically ramifications. Mesocarp parenchyma consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, with vessels and small vascular bundles among. Vessels arranged in group closely, with ramification. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in sclerenchymatous cells and parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nTerminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.: Exocarp consists of 2-5 layers of collenchymatous cells, relatively flat rectangular, arranged closely and covered with cuticle. Rope-shaped sclerenchymatous cells 3-9 layers [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Terminalia chebula Retz.: Colour yellowish-brown. Exocarp epidermal cells colourless to pale yellow, polygonal in top view, walls slightly thickened, some contain brown masses. Sclerenchymatous cells colourless to yellow, fibre-like or varying in shape, 50-250 $ \\mu $m long, some contain brown masses and a few contain clusters of calcium oxalate. Stone cells pale yellow or bright yellow, some branched, walls 2-6 $ \\mu $m thick. Vessels mainly reticulate, spiral vessels sometimes visible, in bundles, 10-30 $ \\mu $m in diameter; bright white under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered or arranged as strip-shaped in cells, 10-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nTerminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.: Sclerenchymatous cells arrange in crisscross pattern or in other shapes, 80-230 $ \\mu $m long. Stone cells 3-8 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Spiral vessels 10-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate 10-50 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\n芫花\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[330, 438, 894, 1038])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[337, 1115, 891, 1597])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[986, 457, 1647, 1595])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[337, 1706, 893, 2009])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[988, 1712, 1480, 2011])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried pericarp of Terminalia chebula Retz.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericarp magnified\n\nD. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\nE. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Sclerenchyma 4. Vessels\n\n5. Mesocarp parenchyma 6. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n彗金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[243, 453, 1731, 2108])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried pericarp of Terminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericarp magnified D. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\nE. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Sclerenchyma 4. Vessels\n\n5. Mesocarp parenchyma 6. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\n芫花\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[349, 450, 716, 889])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[732, 452, 1366, 888])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1377, 451, 1636, 889])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[347, 1050, 984, 1508])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1001, 1049, 1638, 1505])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[349, 1525, 982, 1977])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1005, 1523, 1636, 1983])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried pericarp of Terminalia chebula Retz.\n\n1. Exocarp epidermal cells 2. Sclerenchymatous cells 3. Stone cells\n\n4. Vessels 5. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 159, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1427, 102, 1591, 162])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nChebulae Fructus\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[360, 449, 711, 887])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[726, 453, 1110, 893])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1125, 453, 1624, 895])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[356, 1055, 985, 1506])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1001, 1059, 1625, 1500])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[357, 1518, 989, 1971])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1003, 1519, 1624, 1970])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried pericarp of Terminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Exocarp epidermal cells 2. Sclerenchymatous cells 3. Stone cells\n\n4. Vessels 5. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"13","text":"Gallic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of gallic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-hexane and glacial acetic acid (4:3:3, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of ferric trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Kernels of Chebulae Fructus are removed before grinding and powdering. Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately gallic acid standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[381, 1735, 787, 2036])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of gallic acid\n\n</div>\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n金 Curcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nChebulae Fructus\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[388, 378, 874, 1189])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Chebulae Fructus extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Gallic acid standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula Retz.\n\n(ii) dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of gallic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Gallic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of gallic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Kernels of Chebulae Fructus are removed before grinding and powdering. Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (275 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The internal diameter of inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>100</td><td>0</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-70</td><td>100→30</td><td>0→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of gallic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of gallic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of gallic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from gallic acid peak should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject gallic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of gallic acid peak in the chromatogram of gallic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify gallic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of gallic acid Std-FP. The retention times of gallic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Chebulae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[551, 98, 716, 156])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1072, 157, 1235, 212])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1426, 102, 1590, 161])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Chebulae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, gallic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2.19</td><td>$\\pm$0.09</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.56</td><td>$\\pm$0.09</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.61</td><td>$\\pm$0.10</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[361, 863, 1818, 1374])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula Retz. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[356, 1558, 1820, 2088])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe fruit of Terminalia chebula Retz. var. tomentella Kurt. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[1454, 137, 1616, 193])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 44.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 46.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B).\n\nGallic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of gallic acid CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nGallic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the gallic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 5,10,30,50,100 mg/L for gallic acid."},{"name":"13","text":"Kernels of Chebulae Fructus are removed before grinding and powdering. Weigh accurately 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[40, 9, 1968, 260])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/chebulae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chebulae Fructus","la":"Chebulae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/chrysanthemi-flos-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chrysanthemi Flos v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[528, 948, 981, 1330])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1067, 1032, 1212, 1192])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\ncm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1254, 1030, 1430, 1197])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[579, 1451, 1372, 1827])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[528, 948, 981, 1330])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1067, 1032, 1212, 1192])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\ncm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1254, 1030, 1430, 1197])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[579, 1451, 1372, 1827])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>"},{"name":"24","text":"A. Chrysanthemi Flos (Boju) B. Magnified image of dried capitulum (top view)\n\nC. Magnified image of dried capitulum (bottom view)\n\nD. Bracteole E. Magnified image of bracteole\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nChrysanthemi Flos\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[379, 550, 1643, 1788])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of Chrysanthemi Flos (Chuju)\n\nA. Chrysanthemi Flos (Chuju) B. Magnified image of dried capitulum (top view)\n\nC. Magnified image of dried capitulum (bottom view)\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\nChrysanthemi Flos菊花\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[375, 462, 1649, 1793])\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of Chrysanthemi Flos (Gongju)\n\nA. Chrysanthemi Flos (Gongju) B. Magnified image of dried capitulum (top view)\n\nC. Magnified image of dried capitulum (bottom view)\n\nD. Bracteole E. Magnified image of bracteole\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nChrysanthemi Flos\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[380, 539, 1644, 1790])\n\nFigure 1 (iv) A photograph of Chrysanthemi Flos (Hangju)\n\nA. Chrysanthemi Flos (Hangju) B. Hangju connected forming plates\n\nC. Magnified image of dried capitulum (top view)\n\nD. Magnified image of dried capitulum (bottom view)\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1401, 202, 1758, 259])\n\nChrysanthemi Flos菊花\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[374, 526, 1643, 1786])"},{"name":"24","text":"A. Chrysanthemi Flos (Huaiju with whitish ligulate florets)\n\nB. Chrysanthemi Flos (Huaiju with yellow ligulate florets)\n\nC. Magnified image of dried capitulum (Huaiju with whitish ligulate florets)\n\nD. Magnified image of dried capitulum (Huaiju with yellow ligulate florets)\n\nE. Bracteole F. Magnified image of bracteole\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[50, 21, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"24","text":"Official name: Chrysanthemi Flos\n\nChinese name: 菊花\n\nChinese phonetic name: Juhua"},{"name":"24","text":"Chrysanthemi Flos is the dried capitulum of Chrysanthemum $ \\times $ morifolium (Ramat.) Hemsl. (Asteraceae). The capitulum is collected in autumn on flowering period. The capitulum is steamed and dried in the shade or heated in an oven at $ 60-70^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ or dried under the sun to obtain Chrysanthemi Flos. It is classified into \"Boju (亳菊)”, \"Chuju (滁菊)”, \"Gongju (貢菊)”, \"Hangju (杭菊)\" and \"Huaiju (懷菊)\" according to different localities of production and variations in processing methods."},{"name":"24","text":"Boju: Obconical or cylindrical, sometimes slightly compressed to fan-shaped, 15-27 mm in diameter. Involucre round dish-shaped; bracts 3-4 layers, ovate or eliptical, herbaceous, yellowish-green to brownish-green, the outer surface covered with white hairs, margin membranous. Receptacle hemispherical, without chaffs or chaffy hairs. Ligulate florets several layered, female, at the margin, whitish to pale yellowish-white, straight and upward, longitudinally folded and wrinkled. Tubular florets few, bisexual, occurring in the centre, mostly concealed by the ligulate florets, yellow, apex 5 toothed fissure. More bracteoles are seen in the inflorescence. Achenes undeveloped, pappus absent. Light in weight, texture soft, lax and brittle when dried. Odour fragrant; taste sweet and slightly bitter [Figure 1 (i)].\n\nChuju: Irregularly spherical or oblate, 15-25 mm in diameter. Ligulate florets whitish to pale yellowish-white, irregularly twisted, curved inward, margin wrinkled. Tubular florets abundant, mostly exposed. Bracteoles usually not found in the inflorescence [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\nGongju: Oblate or irregularly spherical, 15-25 mm in diameter. Ligulate florets white or whitish, obliquely upward, the upper part folded outward, margin slightly curved inward and wrinkled. Tubular florets fewer, mostly exposed. Bracteoles occasionally seen in inflorescences [Fig. 1 (iii)].\n\nHangju: Dried under the sun after being steamed, processed with dish-shaped or oblate, 25-40 mm in diameter, often several connected forming plates in commercial specification. Ligulate florets whitish to yellow, spread flatly or folded slightly, agglutinated to each other. Tubular florets abundant, exposed. Bracteoles usually not found in the inflorescence [Fig. 1 (iv)].\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[23, 15, 1955, 259])\n\nHuaiju: Irregular spherical or oblate, 19-25 mm in diameter. Mostly ligulate florets, whitish or yellow, irregular twisted, curved inward, margin wrinkled. Tubular florets few, mostly concealed. More bracteoles are seen in the inflorescence [Fig.1 (v)]."},{"name":"24","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Pollens grains subspherical, 23-37 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with reticulated porous striations and short spines on surface, with 3 germinal pores. T-shaped non-glandular hairs numerous, mostly broken, top cells long and large, with two subequal arms, stalks 2-4 celled. Glandular hairs few, with sole-shaped heads, 6-8 cells arranged opposite each other. Petal epidermal cells slightly elongated in surface view, anticlinal walls sinuous, fine and dense cuticular striations, radial. Epidermal cells of bract polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls straight or sinuous, with longitudinal cuticular striations; stomata anomocytic, oblong, with 3-6 accessory guard cells. Stigma fragments papillary, marginal cells villous. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, numerous and minute; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Wall of sclerenchymatous cells of anther thickened in a strip or network shape [Fig. 2 (i)-Fig. 2 (v)].\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nChrysanthemi Flos\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[247, 412, 1745, 2134])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of powder of Boju\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. T-shaped non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Petal epidermal cells 5. Bract epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Fragments of stigma showing papillae 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate\n\n8. Sclerenchymatous cells of anther\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[232, 399, 1753, 2131])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of Chuju\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. T-shaped non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Petal epidermal cells 5. Bract epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Fragments of stigma showing papillae 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate\n\n8. Sclerenchymatous cells of anther\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nChrysanthemi Flos\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[448, 450, 922, 751])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[969, 451, 1528, 739])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[450, 772, 952, 1147])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1051, 764, 1528, 1157])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[449, 1192, 973, 1558])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1009, 1198, 1527, 1561])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[382, 1606, 816, 1989])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[828, 1606, 1273, 1991])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1295, 1581, 1666, 2044])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of powder of Gongju\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. T-shaped non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Petal epidermal cells 5. Bract epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Fragments of stigma showing papillae 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate\n\n8. Sclerenchymatous cells of anther\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[239, 412, 1749, 2100])\n\nFigure 2 (iv) Microscopic features of powder of Hangju\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. T-shaped non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Petal epidermal cells 5. Bract epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Fragments of stigma showing papillae 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate\n\n8. Sclerenchymatous cells of anther\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nChrysanthemi Flos\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[233, 404, 1754, 2113])\n\nFigure 2 (v) Microscopic features of powder of Huaiju\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. T-shaped non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Petal epidermal cells 5. Bract epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Fragments of stigma showing papillae 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate\n\n8. Sclerenchymatous cells of anther\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[48, 31, 1937, 256])"},{"name":"24","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.2 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 25-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Freshly prepare the standard solution.\n\n3,5-O-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.2 mg of 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 25-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Freshly prepare the standard solution."},{"name":"24","text":"Weigh 2.2 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 25-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Freshly prepare the standard solution."},{"name":"24","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butyl acetate, formic acid and water (15:8:8, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"24","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"24","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter. Freshly prepare the test solution."},{"name":"24","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[375, 433, 999, 800])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[379, 923, 1103, 1353])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[377, 1502, 1068, 1832])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid (ii) 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and (iii) luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[379, 381, 1295, 1080])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3 4(i) 4(ii) 4(iii) 4(iv) 4(v)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Chrysanthemi Flos extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\n2. Luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution\n\n3. 3,5-O-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution\n\n4. Test solution of\n\n(i) Boju\n\n(ii) Chuju\n\n(iii) Gongju\n\n(iv) Hangju\n\n(v) Huaiju\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[41, 26, 1939, 257])"},{"name":"24","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and place it in a 50-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Freshly prepare the standard solution.\n\n3,5-O-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS and place it in a 50-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Freshly prepare the standard solution.\n\nLuteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS and place it in a 50-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Freshly prepare the standard solution."},{"name":"24","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 30 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Wash the residue with methanol ( 70% ). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m nylon filter. Freshly prepare the test solution."},{"name":"24","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (348 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>90→82</td><td>10→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-12</td><td>82</td><td>18</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>12-15</td><td>82→70</td><td>18→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-18</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[49, 11, 1937, 257])"},{"name":"24","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks should not be less than 3500, 10000 and 30000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5 (i), (ii), (iii), (iv) or (v)]."},{"name":"24","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu $ L each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i), (ii), (iii), (iv) or (v)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Chrysanthemi Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Chrysanthemi Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.31</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(luteolin 7-O-β-D-glucoside)</td><td>0.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,3,5-O-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.25</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum苦杏仁\n\n![](page=17,bbox=[328, 365, 1824, 926])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Boju extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=17,bbox=[328, 1076, 1824, 1638])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Chuju extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=17,bbox=[330, 1790, 1822, 2345])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Gongju extract\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=18,bbox=[329, 373, 1823, 924])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (iv) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hangju extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=18,bbox=[330, 1075, 1821, 1635])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (v) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Huaiju extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i), (ii), (iii), (iv) or (v)].\n\n![](page=19,bbox=[42, 33, 1937, 256])"},{"name":"24","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.6%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"24","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/chrysanthemi-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chrysanthemi Flos","la":"Chrysanthemi Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/chrysanthemi-indici-flos-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chrysanthemi Indici Flos v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Chrysanthemi Indici Flos\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[384, 828, 1530, 1868])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Chrysanthemi Indici Flos\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[384, 828, 1530, 1868])"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Chrysanthemi Indici Flos\n\nB. Magnified image of capitulum (top view)\n\nC. Magnified image of capitulum (lateral view)"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Chrysanthemi Indici Flos\n\nChinese Name: 野菊花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yejuhua"},{"name":"14","text":"Chrysanthemi Indici Flos is the dried capitulum of Chrysanthemum indicum L. (Asteraceae). The capitulum is collected at the early stage of flowering in autumn and winter, then dried under the sun or dried after steaming to obtain Chrysanthemi Indici Flos."},{"name":"14","text":"Subspherical, 3-6 mm in diameter, brownish-yellow. Involucre consists of 3-5 layers of bracts, the outer bracts ovate or strip-shaped, the middle part of the outer surface greyish-green to pale brown, often covered with white hairs, margins membranaceous, 2-5 mm long; the middle bracts triangular ovate, 4-7 mm long; the inner bracts elongated-elliptic, membranaceous, 4-8 mm long, hairs absent on the outer surface. The base of involucre sometimes with remnant of pedicel. Ligulate florets arranged in 1 whorl, yellow, crumpled and rolled; tubular florets numerous, deep yellow. Texture fluffy and soft, light in weight. Odour aromatic; taste bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour brownish-yellow. Non-glandular hairs arms slightly circuitous or T-shaped, apical cell unequally furcated, walls slightly thickened, sometimes thickened only on one side, 1-5 cells uniseriate arranged at the base, 20-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Glandular hairs consist of a 4- to 6-celled head, without stalk, cells biseriate and overlapping, oval in surface view and obtuse triangular in lateral view, 40-60 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Pollen grains numerous, yellow, subglobular to ellipsoid, 18-35 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with 3 germinal pores; outer layer of exine thicker than the inner layer, with columns visible inside the outer layer, and inverse reticulate and spiny protuberance on the surface, spines 3-10 $ \\mu m $ long. Epidermal cells of ovary wall elongated, composed of two types\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[30, 15, 1940, 262])\n\nof cells, first type arranged horizontally forming a broad line, second type located vertically on the side. Fragments of stigma showing papillae, with the cells elongated at the apex, border cells villiform. Epidermal cells of filament rectangular to subrectangular, 10-18 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Epidermal cells of bract polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls straight or sinuous, with longitudinal cuticular striations, stomata infinitive, rounded or oval, 18-25 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, subsidiary cells 3-6. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally visible, small; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Spiral vessels occasionally visible (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 75, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[735, 185, 853, 229])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1267, 194, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[301, 450, 1668, 2076])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Non-glandular hair 2. Glandular hairs 3. Pollen grains 4. Epidermal cells of ovary wall\n\n5. Fragments of stigma showing papillae 6. Epidermal cells of filament\n\n7. Epidermal cells of bract (7-1 epidermal cells of bract, 7-2 epidermal cells of bract with stoma $ \\longrightarrow $)\n\n8. Crystals of calcium oxalate 9. Spirel vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 41, 1939, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 5-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.0 mg of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 5-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.0 mg of 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 5-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.0 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 10-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate to dissolve the standard."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butyl acetate, formic acid and water (28:13:10, v/v). Shake well and use the upper layer."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[457, 371, 1009, 711])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[420, 776, 1307, 1122])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[457, 1192, 1026, 1758])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[460, 1821, 1131, 2134])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid (ii) 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid (iii) 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and (iv) luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside\n\n</div>\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 376, 1059, 1213])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3 4 5\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Chrysanthemi Indici Flos extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\n2. Luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution\n\n3. 4,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution\n\n4. 3,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution\n\n5. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"14","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and place it in a 100-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ).\n\n3,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS and place it in a 100-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ).\n\n4,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS and place it in a 100-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ).\n\nLuteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS and place it in a 100-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate to dissolve the standard."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 25 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (334 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-20</td><td>75→56</td><td>25→44</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>56→55</td><td>44→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-45</td><td>55→45</td><td>45→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>45</td><td>55</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks should not be less than 4000, 45000, 25000 and 15000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid, 3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid, 4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Chrysanthemi Indici Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[59, 31, 1928, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Chrysanthemi Indici Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.59</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2(3,4-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>0.95</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,3,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(luteolin7-O-β-D-glucoside)</td><td>1.08</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5(4,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>1.19</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>6(linarin)</td><td>1.81</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>7(luteolin)</td><td>1.89</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[338, 996, 1797, 1537])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Chrysanthemi Indici Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/chrysanthemi-indici-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chrysanthemi Indici Flos","la":"Chrysanthemi Indici Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cibotii-rhizoma-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cibotii Rhizoma v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[527, 1074, 753, 1291])\n\n2cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[825, 1014, 1086, 1353])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1134, 992, 1476, 1358])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n2cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cibotii Rhizoma A. Cibotii Rhizoma B. Transverse section C. Raw slices of Cibotii Rhizoma (\"Shenggoujipian\") D. Steamed slices of Cibotii Rhizoma (\"Shugoujipian\")\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1900, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[527, 1074, 753, 1291])\n\n2cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[825, 1014, 1086, 1353])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1134, 992, 1476, 1358])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n2cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cibotii Rhizoma A. Cibotii Rhizoma B. Transverse section C. Raw slices of Cibotii Rhizoma (\"Shenggoujipian\") D. Steamed slices of Cibotii Rhizoma (\"Shugoujipian\")\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1900, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Cibotii Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 狗脊\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gouji"},{"name":"11","text":"Cibotii Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Cibotium barometz (L.) J. Sm. (Dicksoniaceae). The rhizome is collected in autumn and winter, soil removed and dried; or hard roots, petioles and hairs removed, then cut into thick slices and dried under the sun to obtain \"Shenggoujipian\" (Raw slice of Cibotii Rhizoma); or after steaming the rhizome, dried under the sun until almost dry, then cut into thick slices and dried thoroughly to obtain \"Shugoujipian\" (Steamed slice of Cibotii Rhizoma)."},{"name":"11","text":"Irregular long lump-shaped, up to 56 cm long, 15-120 mm in diameter. Externally dark brown, covered with remnants of golden hairs; upper part with several reddish-brown remnants base of woody petiole; lower part showing remnants of black fibrous roots. Texture hard. Odourless; taste weak and slightly astringent. \"Shenggoujipian\" (Raw slice of Cibotii Rhizoma) irregular long slat-shaped or rounded, 4-19 cm long, 15-80 mm in diameter, 1.5-5 mm thick, cut surface pale brown, relatively smooth, with a protuberant brownish-yellow xylem annulation or stria at a distance of 1-7 mm from the edges; edges uneven, occasionally with remnants of golden hairs; texture fragile; easily broken, starchy. \"Shugoujipian\" (Steamed slice of Cibotii Rhizoma) blackish-brown; texture hard (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Non-glandular hairs present outside epidermis, mostly fragmented. Epidermis consists of 1-3 layer(s) of cells. Sclerenchyma locate beneath epidermis, consists of 10 or more layers of cells. Xylem consists of tracheids, with the presence of phloem and endodermis at both inner and outer parts of the xylem. Cortex and pith composed of parenchymatous cells, filled with starch granules in raw sample or with starch gelatinous masses in steamed rhizome, some filled with yellowish-brown contents (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Non-glandular hairs golden or yellowish-brown, mostly fragmented, 10-128 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall of middle cell thin, apical cell long and pointed, wall slightly thickened. Starch granules numerous in raw rhizome, simple starch granules ovoid to ellipsoid, 5-49 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum pointed, cleft-like, V-shaped or Y-shaped, striations visible in the large ones; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules few, composed of 2-3 units. Starch gelatinous masses colourless, present in steamed rhizome. Tracheids reticulate, commonly found, 22-73 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Sclerenchymatous cells yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, subrectangular or spindle-shaped, wall thickened, pits distinct. Endodermal cells yellow to yellowish-brown, subsquare, subrectangular or polygonal, wall slightly thickened and slightly sinuous (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[8, 4, 1969, 270])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[233, 448, 1705, 2138])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cibotii Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Sclerenchyma 4. Cortex\n\n5. Endodermis 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n餘甘子 Phyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nCibotii Rhizoma Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 546, 1710, 2180])"},{"name":"11","text":"1,2. Non-glandular hairs 3. Starch granules\n\n4. Starch gelatinous mass (in steamed rhizome) 5. Reticulate tracheids\n\n6. Sclerenchymatous cells 7. Endodermal cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 6, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"11","text":"Protocatechuic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of protocatechuic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nProtocatechuic aldehyde standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of protocatechuic aldehyde CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nPrepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and glacial acetic acid (5:5:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of ferric trichloride and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 30 mL of water and 5 mL of hydrochloric acid (22.4%, w/v). Transfer the solution to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 20 mL of diethyl ether. Combine the diethyl ether extracts and transfer to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate (2-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ ) and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately protocatechuic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), protocatechuic aldehyde standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[69, 8, 1966, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[431, 609, 885, 963])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[432, 1057, 852, 1401])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) protocatechuic acid and (ii) protocatechuic aldehyde\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Protocatechuic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of protocatechuic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 1% acetic acid.\n\nProtocatechuic aldehyde standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of protocatechuic aldehyde CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 1% acetic acid."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 30 mL of water and 1 mL of hydrochloric acid. Transfer the solution to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 50 mL of diethyl ether. Combine the diethyl ether extracts and transfer to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue for three times each with\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[30, 7, 1906, 263])\n\n1 mL of 1% acetic acid. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with 1% acetic acid. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (4.6\\times 250\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 4% acetic acid and acetonitrile (95:5, v/v). The elution time is about 30 min."},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of protocatechuic acid Std-FP and protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks should not be less than 8000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 and 1.0 respectively (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject protocatechuic acid Std-FP, protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks in the chromatograms of protocatechuic acid Std-FP, protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of protocatechuic acid Std-FP and protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP. The retention times of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cibotii Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 8, 1962, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cibotii Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(protocatechuic acid)</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,protocatechuic aldehyde)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[386, 768, 1751, 1226])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cibotii Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[12, 7, 1919, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 32.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 20.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"![](page=10,bbox=[301, 374, 1194, 1177])\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Lane</td><td>Sample</td><td>Results</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Blank(Methanol)</td><td>Negative</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Standard\n(Protocatechuic acid)</td><td>Protocatechuic acid positive</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Standard\n(Protocatechuic aldehyde)</td><td>Protocatechuic aldehyde positive</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sample\n(Cibotii Rhizoma)</td><td>Protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde positive</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sample duplicate\n(Cibotii Rhizoma)</td><td>Protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde positive</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Spiked sample\n(Sample plus protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde)</td><td>Protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde positive</td></tr></table>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 TLC results of Cibotii Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cibotii-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cibotii Rhizoma","la":"Cibotii Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cinnabaris-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnabaris v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"8","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},{"name":"8","text":"Official Name: Cinnabaris\n\nChinese Name: 朱砂\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhusha"},{"name":"8","text":"Cinnabaris is a mineral of the cinnabar group, containing mainly mercuric sulphide (HgS). The ore is collected, foreign matter removed to obtain Cinnabaris."},{"name":"8","text":"Masses, plates or powder aggregate, some in granules, powder or plates. Streaks red to brownishred; bright red or dull red in colour and occasionally leaden on the surface, with lustre. The colour will not stain on hand when touched. Heavy in weight. Opaque or semi-opaque. Cinnabaris in powder aggregate form is fragile, easily broken; those in masses are relatively hard. Odourless (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"8","text":"Colour bright to dull red. Crystals irregular, varying in size, dark red to bright red, edge uneven and black, some with longitudinal striations. Dark red, orange red or occasionally polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 8, 1984, 262])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[234, 402, 1738, 2157])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Cinnabaris\n\n</div>\n\n1-4. Cinnabaris with irregular shape\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 7, 1925, 256])"},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in an evaporating dish, then add 10 mL of hydrochloric acid and nitric acid (3:1, v/v). Evaporate to dryness on a water bath. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of water. Filter the extract."},{"name":"8","text":"(a) Transfer 2 mL of the test solution to a test tube and add 2 drops of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3% , w/v). Yellow precipitate can be observed.\n\n(b) Transfer 2 mL of neutral test solution to a test tube and add 2 drops of potassium iodide solution (16.5% , w/v) until scarlet precipitate can be observed. Add excess of potassium iodide solution (16.5% , w/v) until scarlet precipitate dissolves. Add 2 drops of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3% , w/v) and 0.2 g of ammonium chloride, reddish-brown precipitate can be observed."},{"name":"8","text":"Lead (II) acetate solution\n\nWeigh 10.0 g of lead (II) acetate and place it in a 100-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Add acetic acid until the solution becomes clear and make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"8","text":"(a) Transfer 2 mL of the test solution to a test tube and add 2 drops of barium chloride solution (5%, w/v). White precipitate can be observed. The precipitate is insoluble in hydrochloric acid.\n\n(b) Transfer 2 mL of the test solution to a test tube and add 2 drops of lead (II) acetate solution. White precipitate can be observed. Then add sodium hydroxide solution (4.3% , w/v), yellow precipitate can be observed.\n\n(c) Transfer 2 mL of the test solution to a test tube and add 2 drops of hydrochloric acid. No white precipitate can be observed.\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 14, 1976, 263])"},{"name":"8","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI."},{"name":"8","text":"Finely powdered mercuric sulphide (0.5 g)."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"8","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 20) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 20 values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a $ 2\\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0.05^{\\circ} $ when compared to the corresponding 20 values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database."},{"name":"8","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard material and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20 values of the diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the characteristic diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample as listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The 20 values of the six characteristic diffraction peaks of Cinnabaris\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>26.592</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>28.259</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>31.285</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>43.695</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>45.872</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>51.851</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 4, 1928, 257])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[381, 457, 1810, 992])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference XRPD pattern of Cinnabaris\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic diffraction peaks (Fig. 3) each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0. 2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the values stated in Table 1.\n\n</div>"},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.863 g of ferric ammonium sulphate and place it in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Add 2.5 mL of sulphuric acid and make up to the mark with water. Pipette 10 mL of the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water (each mL is equivalent to 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{g} $ of iron). Freshly prepare the solution."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of hydrochloric acid (28%, w/v). Reflux the solution for 10 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL volumetric flask. Neutralize the filtrate with 10 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3%, w/v). Make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"8","text":"(a) Pipette 10 mL of the test solution to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Add 25 mL of water, 4 mL of hydrochloric acid (28%, w/v) and 0.05 g of ammonium persulfate. Add 3 mL of ammonium thiocyanate solution (30%, w/v). Make up to the mark with water. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL test tube.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 13, 1984, 264])\n\n(b) Pipette 4 mL of the iron standard solution to a 50-mL volumetric flask, carry out the procedure as stated in (a). The colour of the test solution should be less intense when compared with the iron standard solution."},{"name":"8","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XV."},{"name":"8","text":"Ammonium thiocyanate titrant\n\nWeigh 3.806 g of ammonium thiocyanate and place it in a 500-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with water.\n\nFluorescein solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 g of fluorescein and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol.."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 1.699 g of silver nitrate and place it in a 100-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with water."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cinnabaris","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnabaris","la":"Cinnabaris"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cinnamomi-cortex-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnamomi Cortex v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Official Name: Cinnamomi Cortex\n\nChinese Name: 肉桂\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Rougui"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cinnamomi Cortex\n\nChinese Name: 肉桂\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Rougui"},{"name":"12","text":"Cinnamomi Cortex is the dried stem bark of Cinnamomum cassia Presl (Lauraceae). The bark is frequently collected in autumn, and dried in a shaded area to obtain Cinnamomi Cortex."},{"name":"12","text":"Channelled or quilled, 3.5-50 cm long, 0.5-6 cm wide when fully extended, 1-6 mm thick. Externally greyish-brown, slightly rough, with irregular fine wrinkles and transverse protuberant lenticels, sometimes showing greyish-white streaks; the inner surface reddish-brown, slightly even, with fine longitudinal striations and exhibiting oily trace on scratching. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Fracture uneven, the outer layer brown and relatively rough, the inner layer reddish-brown and oily, a yellowish-brown line visible between the two layers. Odour strongly aromatic; taste sweet and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells, the innermost layer of cells with thickened and lignified outer walls. Cortex scattered with stone cells and oil cells. Pericycle stone cells in groups, arranged in an interrupted ring; accompanied by fibre bundles outside, the outer walls of stone cells usually thinner. Phloem rays 1-2 rows of cells wide; containing fine raphides of calcium oxalate. Fibres usually 2-3 in bundles. Oil cells scattered throughout (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[46, 7, 1957, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour reddish-brown. Fibres mostly scattered singly, long fusiform, 69-618 $ \\mu $m long, 15-49 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls thick and lignified, pits indistinct; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells subrectangular or subrounded, 22-136 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls thickened and some with one thin side. Oil cells occasionally found, broken. Cork cells polygonal, containing reddish-brown masses. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered, relatively fine, varying in length; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 448, 1746, 2097])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cinnamomi Cortex\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. The innermost layer of cork cells\n\nD. Groups of stone cell at pericycle E. Ray cells containing raphides of calcium oxalate F. Fibres\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle 4. Phloem 5. Raphides of calcium oxalate 6. Fibre 7. Oil cell\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n梦金 Curcumae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Cortex\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[256, 589, 1631, 2072])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cinnamomi Cortex\n\n1. Fibre 2. Stone cells 3. Oil cells 4. Cork cells 5. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Cinnamaldehyde standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of cinnamaldehyde CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nCinnamic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of cinnamic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (4:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 45 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ F_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately cinnamaldehyde standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), cinnamic acid standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[445, 1876, 722, 1992])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[445, 2070, 746, 2241])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) cinnamaldehyde and (ii) cinnamic acid\n\n</div>\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\n鬆金 Curcumae Radix\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n豬牙皂\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCinnamomi Cortex\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 377, 898, 1136])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cinnamomi Cortex extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cinnamic acid standard solution 2. Cinnamaldehyde standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cinnamaldehyde standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (80 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.8 mg of cinnamaldehyde CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nCinnamic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (1 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of cinnamic acid CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (290 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.5% Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>28</td><td>72</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>28→32</td><td>72→68</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>32→40</td><td>68→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>40→60</td><td>60→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of cinnamaldehyde Std-FP and cinnamic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks should not be less than 25000 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject cinnamaldehyde Std-FP, cinnamic acid Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks in the chromatograms of cinnamaldehyde Std-FP, cinnamic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of cinnamaldehyde Std-FP and cinnamic acid Std-FP. The retention times of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cinnamomi Cortex extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[42, 8, 1966, 263])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cinnamomi Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,cinnamic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(cinnamaldehyde)</td><td>1.31</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.64</td><td>$\\pm$0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[387, 836, 1806, 1441])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cinnamomi Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[22, 13, 1893, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 8.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L for cinnamaldehyde and 10 mg/L for cinnamic acid)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of cinnamaldehyde CRS and 0.1 mg of cinnamic acid CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nMixed cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid Std-Stock dilute with ethanol ( 70% ) to produce a series of solutions of 10, 20, 30, 40, 100 mg/L for cinnamaldehyde and 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2 mg/L for cinnamic acid."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh accurately 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cinnamomi-cortex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnamomi Cortex","la":"Cinnamomi Cortex"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cinnamomi-ramulus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnamomi Ramulus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[409, 899, 1580, 1823])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cinnamomi Ramulus A. Cinnamomi Ramulus B. Slices\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1902, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[409, 899, 1580, 1823])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cinnamomi Ramulus A. Cinnamomi Ramulus B. Slices\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1902, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cinnamomi Ramulus\n\nChinese Name: 桂枝\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Guizhi"},{"name":"12","text":"Cinnamomi Ramulus is the dried young branch of Cinnamomum cassia Presl (Lauraceae). The young branch is collected in spring and summer, leaves removed, then dried under the sun; or dried after sliced to obtain Cinnamomi Ramulus."},{"name":"12","text":"Long, mostly branched and cylindrical, 20.8-55.8 cm long, thick end 2-18 mm in diameter. Externally reddish-brown to brown, with longitudinal ridges, fine wrinkles, dotted lenticels, pimple-like leaf scars, branch scars and bud scars. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Slices 1-3 mm thick, bark of cut surface reddish-brown, wood yellowish-white to pale yellowish-brown; pith subsquare. Odour characteristic and aromatic; taste sweet and slightly pungent, relatively strong for bark (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, with unicellular non-glandular hairs occasionally. Cork consists of 3-5 layers of cells, cells of the innermost layer with thickened outer wall. Cortex scattered with oil cells and stone cells. Groups of stone cells in pericycle arranged in a interrupted ring, accompanied by fibre bundles. Phloem scattered with secretory cells and fibres. Cambium distinct. Xylem vessels scattered singly or 2-4 in groups, arranged radially; xylem rays 1-2 cells wide, containing brown contents. Pith with slightly thickened and lignified cell wall (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour reddish-brown. Stone cells abundant, scattered singly or in groups, colourless, pale yellow or brown, subsquare, rectangular, subrounded or short fusiform, 20-70 $ \\mu m $ in diameter and 26-154 $ \\mu m $ long, margins slightly uneven, wall thick, some with very thin wall on one side, striations rare, pit canals obvious. Phloem fibres scattered singly, colourless or brown, fusiform, slightly curved, with sharp, mucronate or obtuse-rounded endings, 9-39 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, margins occasionally with toothlike projections, primary wall obvious, quite thick and lignified, pit canals indistinct. Cells of xylem ray subsquare or rectangular, wall thickened and beaded. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 5-59 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; spiral vessels few. Cork cells yellowish-brown, subsquare to roundish-rectangular or polygonal in surface view, containing reddish-brown contents. Oil cells often broken, intact cells subrounded or elliptic, 41-104 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, some containing pale brown oil droplets. Xylem fibres mainly in bundles, colourless to yellowish-brown (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[38, 7, 1912, 265])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 450, 1740, 1929])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cinnamomi Ramulus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cork 3. Oil cell 4. Fibre bundles 5. Groups of stone cells\n\n6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Xylem ray 10. Pith\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Cinnamomi Ramulus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[245, 593, 1719, 1956])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cinnamomi Ramulus (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Phloem fibre 3. Cells of xylem ray 4-1. Bordered-pitted vessel 4-2. Spiral vessel 5. Cork cells 6. Oil cell 7. Xylem fibres\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 7, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Cinnamaldehyde standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of cinnamaldehyde CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nCinnamic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of cinnamic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60- $ 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (8:2:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 45 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately cinnamaldehyde standard solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), cinnamic acid standard solution (0.6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[80, 12, 1969, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[431, 371, 714, 491])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[432, 575, 739, 743])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) cinnamaldehyde and (ii) cinnamic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Cinnamaldehyde standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of cinnamaldehyde CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nCinnamic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of cinnamic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.5% Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>28</td><td>72</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>28→32</td><td>72→68</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>32→40</td><td>68→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>40→60</td><td>60→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[35, 5, 1907, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of cinnamaldehyde Std-FP and cinnamic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks should not be less than 25000 and 13000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject cinnamaldehyde Std-FP, cinnamic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks in the chromatograms of cinnamaldehyde Std-FP, cinnamic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of cinnamaldehyde Std-FP and cinnamic acid Std-FP. The retention times of cinnamaldehyde and cinnamic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cinnamomi Ramulus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 8, 1968, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cinnamomi Ramulus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(coumarin)</td><td>0.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.87</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,cinnamic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(cinnamaldehyde)</td><td>1.29</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[299, 969, 1828, 1589])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cinnamomi Ramulus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[57, 14, 1901, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cinnamomi-ramulus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnamomi Ramulus","la":"Cinnamomi Ramulus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cirsii-herba-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cirsii Herba v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[556, 992, 829, 1161])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[869, 974, 1172, 1197])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1212, 960, 1416, 1159])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[515, 1234, 1547, 1803])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cirsii Herba A. Cirsii Herba B. Leaves C. Capitulum D. Magnified capitulum with flower"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[556, 992, 829, 1161])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[869, 974, 1172, 1197])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1212, 960, 1416, 1159])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[515, 1234, 1547, 1803])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cirsii Herba A. Cirsii Herba B. Leaves C. Capitulum D. Magnified capitulum with flower"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cirsii Herba\n\nChinese Name: 小薊\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xiaoji"},{"name":"12","text":"Cirsii Herba is the dried aerial part of Cirsium setosum (Willd.) MB. (Asteraceae). The aerial part is collected at flowering in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Cirsii Herba."},{"name":"12","text":"Stems cylindrical, sometimes branched on the upper part, 5-30 cm long, 2-5 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-green to purple, with longitudinal ridges and white pubescence; texture fragile, easily broken; fracture hollow. Leaves simple, alternate, sessile or short-petioled; lamina crumpled or broken, when intact flattened out, elongated-elliptic to oblong-lanceolate, 3-12 cm long, 5-30 mm wide; margins entire or serrulate to pinnatipartite, the tips of serrations spinescent; the upper surface greenish-brown and the lower surface greyish-green, covered with white pubescences on both surfaces. Capitulum solitary or several disposed on a loose terminal; involucres campanulate, bracts 5-8 layers, yellowish-green; flowers purplish-red. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Hypodermal collenchyma present at angular regions. Cortex consists of several to more than 10 layers of cells. Pericyclic fibre in bundles, slightly lignified. Phloem consists of several layers of small square parenchymatous cells. Cambium indistinct. Xylem vessels located in middle and lower parts of xylem. Pith usually hollow [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 7, 1974, 261])\n\nLeaf: Both upper and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of cells, non-glandular hairs easily fallen off during transverse section processing. Mesophyll mainly consists of spongy cells. Xylem fibres in bundles, located in upper side of vessels. Vascular bundle collateral. Phloem consists of several layers of small parenchymatous cells. Phloem fibres in bundles. Crystals of calcium oxalate scattered in leaf [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brownish-green to dark green. Epidermal cells polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls straight, stomata and non-glandular hairs occur on both surfaces, stomata anomocytic or anisocytic. Non-glandular hairs consist of 3-10 cells or more, apical cells slender whip-shaped, shrunken and twisted. Crystals of calcium oxalate scattered in mesophyll cells, 1-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter; blue under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains subglobular, 30-50 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with spiny exine (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 414, 1753, 2125])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Cirsii Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermal collenchyma 3. Cortex 4. Pericyclic fibre\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nCirsii Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[236, 473, 1701, 2099])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Cirsii Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Crystals of calcium oxalate 3. Mesophyll\n\n4. Xylem fibres 5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Phloem fibres 8. Lower epidermis\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus 水紅花子\n\n胡黃連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[401, 472, 1582, 2051])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cirsii Herba\n\n1. Epidermal cells with stomata 2. Epidermal cells with non-glandular hair\n\n3. Non-glandular hairs 4. Crystals of calcium oxalate 5. Pollen grains\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 10, 1967, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Linarin (buddleoside) standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of linarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol. Place it in a water bath at about $ 9 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 min."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (8:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol and then filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linarin standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n益智 Alpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黄連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[376, 372, 1053, 851])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of linarin (buddleoside)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 1005, 892, 1776])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cirsii Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Linarin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of linarin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[42, 10, 1965, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Linarin (buddleoside) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (160 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.6 mg of linarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Place it in a water bath at about $ 9 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 min."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 2 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.05% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>13→24</td><td>87→76</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-38</td><td>24</td><td>76</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>38-50</td><td>24→75</td><td>76→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of linarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of linarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from linarin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject linarin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of linarin peak in the chromatogram of linarin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify linarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of linarin Std-FP. The retention times of linarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Cirsii Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Cirsii Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.24</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, linarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.45</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[400, 1545, 1797, 2150])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cirsii Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 17, 1959, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 19.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cirsii-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cirsii Herba","la":"Cirsii Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cirsii-japonici-herba-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cirsii Japonici Herba v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[616, 972, 864, 1111])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[939, 983, 1112, 1110])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1130, 918, 1356, 1103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[444, 1188, 1399, 1825])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cirsii Japonici Herba\n\nA. Cirsii Japonici Herba B. Magnified image of leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of stem D. Capitulum\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[616, 972, 864, 1111])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[939, 983, 1112, 1110])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1130, 918, 1356, 1103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[444, 1188, 1399, 1825])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cirsii Japonici Herba\n\nA. Cirsii Japonici Herba B. Magnified image of leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of stem D. Capitulum\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cirsii Japonici Herba\n\nChinese Name: 大蓟\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Daji"},{"name":"12","text":"Cirsii Japonici Herba is the dried aerial part of Cirsium japonicum Fisch. ex DC. (Asteraceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn at flowering, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Cirsii Japonici Herba."},{"name":"12","text":"Stems cylindrical, base up to 12 mm in diameter; externally greyish-green to greenish-brown, with several longitudinal ridges and filamentous hairs; fracture greyish-white, pith lax or hollowed. Leaves crumpled, mostly broken, when intact flattened out, oblanceolate or obovate-elliptical, pinnatipartite, margin with unequal spines; the upper surface greyish-green or yellowish-brown and lower surface lighter in colour, with greyish-white filamentous hairs on both surfaces. Capitulum disposed on terminal, globose or elliptical, involucre campaniform, bract imbricate; feathery pappus greyish-white. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, usually shrunken. Non-glandular hairs easily fallen off during transverse section processing. Cortical parenchymatous cells prolonged tangentially. Hypodermal collenchyma consists of 3-11 layers of cells, relatively distinct at prominent regions. Cambium indistinct. Vascular bundle collateral. Phloem fibres, vessels and xylem fibers slightly lignified. Pith usually hollow in the centre [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nLeaf: Blade cells usually shrunken. Both upper and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of cells, non-glandular hairs easily fallen off during transverse section processing. Lower part of leave\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 5, 1945, 258])\n\ndeep wave-shaped, especially protrude near midrib. Hypodermal collenchyma consists of 2-9 layers of cells, relatively distinct at prominent regions. Vascular bundle collateral. Phloem fibres and xylem fibres in bundles, slightly lignified. Crystals of calcium oxalate aggregated in fan-shaped, rounded or irregular, scattered in leaf [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brownish-green to dark green. Non-glandular hairs consist of 4-18 cells, mostly broken, with a slender whip-shaped apical cell, bent or twisted, 3-7 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Lower epidermal cells subrectangular in surface view, with sinuous anticlinal walls. Stomata anomocytic or anisocytic, subsidiary cells 3-5. Crystals of calcium oxalate aggregated in fan-shaped, rounded or irregular in shape; bluish-white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 21, 1897, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[408, 415, 1695, 2026])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Cirsii Japonici Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Cortex 4. Hypodermal collenchyma 5. Phloem 6. Cambium\n\n7. Xylem 8. Phloem fibre 9. Xylem fibre 10. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 2, 1953, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[236, 415, 1737, 2102])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Cirsii Japonici Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration of leaf (midrib with blade) C. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Blade 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Upper epidermis 4. Hypodermal collenchyma 5. Xylem fibres\n\n6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Lower epidermis 9. Phloem fibres\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 21, 1890, 258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[240, 407, 1735, 2067])"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Non-glandular hair (1-1 non-glandular hair, 1-2 base, 1-3 apex)\n\n2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata 3. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 13, 1944, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Linarin (buddleoside) standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of linarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol. Place it in a water bath at about $ 9 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 min."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (8:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.5 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linarin standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 12, 1893, 262])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 373, 1047, 845])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of linarin (buddleoside)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 1006, 792, 1722])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cirsii Japonici Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Linarin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of linarin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Linarin (buddleoside) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (160 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.6 mg of linarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Place it in a water bath at about $ 9 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 min.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 10, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 2 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.05% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>13→24</td><td>87→76</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-38</td><td>24</td><td>76</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>38-50</td><td>24→75</td><td>76→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of linarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of linarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from linarin peak should not be less than 18000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject linarin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of linarin peak in the chromatogram of linarin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify linarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of linarin Std-FP. The retention times of linarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[31, 9, 1882, 265])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cirsii Japonici Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cirsii Japonici Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.24</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, linarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.48</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[380, 978, 1820, 1635])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cirsii Japonici Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 15, 1947, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 17.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cirsii-japonici-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cirsii Japonici Herba","la":"Cirsii Japonici Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cistanches-herba-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cistanches Herba v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1927, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"17","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1927, 258])"},{"name":"17","text":"Official Name: Cistanches Herba\n\nChinese Name: 肉蓰蓉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Roucongrong"},{"name":"17","text":"Cistanches Herba is the dried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche deserticola Y. C. Ma or Cistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight (Orobanchaceae). The herb is collected in spring before or at the time the stem emerges from the ground; the inflorescences and foreign matter removed, then cut into sections and dried under the sun to obtain Cistanches Herba."},{"name":"17","text":"Cistanche deserticola Y. C. Ma: Flattened-cylindrical, slightly curved, 3-33 cm long, 20-100 mm in diameter. Externally brown or greyish-brown, densely covered with imbricated scales, the apex of scales usually broken. Texture heavy, hard and slightly pliable, uneasily broken; fracture brown, dotted by pale brownish vascular bundles, arranged in sinuous rings. Odour slight; taste sweet and slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nCistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight: Subfusiform, flattened-fusiform and flattened-cylindrical, 4-26 cm long, 15-90 mm in diameter. Externally dark brown. Fracture granular, greyish-brown, dotted with scattered vascular bundles [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Stem and scale leaf of Cistanche deserticola Y. C. Ma: Stem epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells and scattered with leaf trace vascular bundles. Vascular bundles collateral, arranged in sinuous interrupted rings, and obliterated into a sharp, spine-like tail at the distal end of phloem. Pith is distinct. A scale leaf can be seen in the transverse section; it consists of upper epidermis, parenchyma, 3-7 vascular bundles, and lower epidermis [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 8, 1984, 263])\n\nStem of Cistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, but sometimes fallen off. Metaderm consists of several layers of suberized cells. The cortex is narrow. Collateral vascular bundles ovate, arranged irregularly, with 3-6 bundles grouped in a ring formation, with phloem on the inner and xylem on the outer side. Pith is indistinct [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Cistanche deserticola Y. C. Ma: Colour dark brown. Starch granules simple, subglobular, rectangular-ovoid to ellipsoid, 16-54 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum dotted or V-shaped, striations distinct; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Reticulate and spiral vessels mainly in group; reticulate vessels 25-65 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, spiral vessels 11-32 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Epidermal cells pale yellow, sub-rectangular in shape, wall thickened, pit canals oblique and clear [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nCistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight: Colour dark brown. Starch granules simple or compound compound granules composed of 2 units, subglobular, ovoid to ellipsoid, 5-50 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum V-shaped, cleft-like or star-shaped, striations indistinct; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Reticulate vessels 21-55 $ \\mu $m in diameter; spiral vessels 11-22 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Epidermal cells pale yellow, polygonal in shape [Fig. 3 (ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Acteoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of acteoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol.\n\nEchinacoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of echinacoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, methanol and formic acid (8:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[597, 15, 695, 69])\n\nArctii Fructus牛蒡子\n\n湖北貝母\n\nAurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\n延胡索 Corydalis Rhizoma\n\n砒霜 Arsenicum\n\n大青葉\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nCurcumae Longae Rhizoma\n\n蒼朮 薑黃\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 388, 1743, 2043])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche deserticola Y. C. Ma\n\nA. Sketch of stem B1. Section illustration of stem B2. Section illustration of scale leaf\n\nC. Stem cross section showing vascular bundles\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Leaf trace vascular bundle 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[21, 15, 1984, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[341, 629, 948, 1086])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[360, 1202, 937, 1783])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[997, 638, 1712, 1668])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n500 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried fleshy stem of Cistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundles\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Metaderm 3. Leaf trace vascular bundle 4. Xylem 5. Phloem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[170, 14, 327, 71])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[597, 15, 696, 69])\n\nArctii Fructus牛蒡子\n\n湖北貝母\n\nAurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\n延胡索\n\nCorydalis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1693, 26, 1901, 108])\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[364, 446, 968, 927])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1014, 444, 1630, 925])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[365, 986, 969, 1437])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1014, 987, 1631, 1441])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[364, 1500, 967, 1970])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche deserticola Y. C. Ma 1. Starch granules 2. Reticulate vessels 3. Spiral vessels 4. Epidermal cells a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\nOrpiment雌黄\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\n雄黄\n\nRealgar\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1892, 44, 1984, 89])\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[346, 448, 959, 921])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1018, 444, 1634, 922])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[348, 981, 959, 1453])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1018, 981, 1633, 1447])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[347, 1510, 958, 1987])\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight 1. Starch granules 2. Reticulate vessels 3. Spiral vessels 4. Epidermal cells a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 3, 1926, 259])"},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC polyamide plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately acteoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), echinacoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of acteoside and echinacoside.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 1023, 1271, 2414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) acteoside (ii) echinacoside and (iii) galactitol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 15, 1974, 264])"},{"name":"17","text":"Acteoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of acteoside CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (90%).\n\nEchinacoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of echinacoside CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (90%)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (90%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (90%). Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (90%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"17","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>86</td><td>14</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-21</td><td>86→83</td><td>14→17</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>21-30</td><td>83</td><td>17</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-31</td><td>83→80</td><td>17→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>31-60</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of acteoside Std-FP and echinacoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of acteoside and echinacoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of acteoside and echinacoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from acteoside and echinacoside peaks should not be less than 25000 and 7000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 2, 1929, 259])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Separately inject acteoside Std-FP, echinacoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of acteoside and echinacoside peaks in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP, echinacoside Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify acteoside and echinacoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP and echinacoside Std-FP. The retention times of acteoside and echinacoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cistanches Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cistanches Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(echinacoside)</td><td>0.45</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, acteoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.45</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[390, 1704, 1587, 2351])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche deserticola Y.C. Ma extract\n\n</div>\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[380, 392, 1535, 1007])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 10.0%\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[27, 5, 1929, 257])"},{"name":"17","text":"Dried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche deserticola Y.C. Ma\n\nWater-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 28.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\nDried fleshy stem, with scales, of Cistanche tubulosa (Schrenk) Wight Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"17","text":"Mixed acteoside and echinacoside standard stock solution, Std-Stock (400 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 20.0 mg of acteoside CRS and 20.0 mg of echinacoside CRS, and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (90%).\n\nMixed acteoside and echinacoside standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed acteoside and echinacoside Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (90%) to produce a series of solutions of 6, 30, 100, 200, 400 mg/L for both acteoside and echinacoside."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh accurately 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (90%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (90%). Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (90%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cistanches-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cistanches Herba","la":"Cistanches Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/citri-exocarpium-rubrum-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citri Exocarpium Rubrum v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[762, 866, 1401, 1203])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[679, 1260, 1261, 1871])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Citri Exocarpium Rubrum A. Outer surface of exocarps B. Inner surface of exocarps\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 16, 1872, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[762, 866, 1401, 1203])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[679, 1260, 1261, 1871])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Citri Exocarpium Rubrum A. Outer surface of exocarps B. Inner surface of exocarps\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 16, 1872, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Citri Exocarpium Rubrum\n\nChinese Name: 橘紅\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Juhong"},{"name":"13","text":"Citri Exocarpium Rubrum is the dried exocarp of Citrus reticulata Blanco (Rutaceae) and its cultivated varieties. The ripe fruit is collected in late autumn and early winter, the pericarp is split, exocarp collected after endocarp scratched off, then dried under the sun or in a shaded area to obtain Citri Exocarpium Rubrum."},{"name":"13","text":"Thin slices of exocarp, long stripes, elliptic to ovate or irregular in shape, the margin shrunken and curved inward. The outer surface yellowish-brown to orange-red, becoming dark brown on storage, with numerous yellowish-white protuberant or sunken oil cavities. The inner surface yellowish-white, with numerous sunken and transparent small spots. Texture fragile, easily broken and some slightly pliable. Odour fragrant; taste slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"The outermost layer of exocarp consists of 1 layer of small subsquare epidermal cells, covered with cuticle. Parenchymatous cells below the epidermis tangentially elongated, rectangular or irregular in shape. Fragments of oil cavities numerous, scattered in the exocarp. Prisms of calcium oxalate located inside the parenchymatous cells, polygonal or rhombic in shape. Fanshaped cluster crystals inside parenchymatous cells sometimes visible, usually aggregated into spheroid or amorphous masses (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 4, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Epidermal cells of exocarp polygonal, subsquare or rectangular in surface view, anticlinal walls thickened, stomata subrounded, subsidiary cells indistinct. The walls of parenchymatous cells of fragments of oil cavities slightly thickened. Prisms of calcium oxalate abundant, scattered in parenchymatous cells, rhombic to double-conical to irregularly polygonal, 4-31 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fan-shaped cluster crystals yellow, sometimes visible, usually aggregated into spheroid or amorphous masses; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 24, 1889, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[280, 755, 856, 982])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[934, 743, 1700, 1232])\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n\n5\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[280, 1426, 509, 1643])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[544, 1428, 771, 1641])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[934, 1427, 1164, 1669])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1199, 1428, 1426, 1670])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Citri Exocarpium Rubrum\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fan-shaped cluster crystals D. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Fan-shaped cluster crystals 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Parenchymatous cells\n\n5. Fragments of oil cavity\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 3, 1953, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[363, 448, 977, 938])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1012, 450, 1621, 933])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[363, 971, 975, 1458])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1011, 973, 1622, 1460])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[362, 1497, 974, 1985])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1012, 1497, 1622, 1988])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Epidermal cells of exocarp (stoma $ \\longrightarrow $ ） 2. Fragment of oil cavity $ \\longrightarrow $ ）\n\n3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Fan-shaped cluster crystals\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 25, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Hesperidin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hesperidin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nNobiletin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of nobiletin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (13:7:2, v/v). Use the lower layer."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hesperidin standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), nobiletin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 4, 1949, 256])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[373, 372, 1246, 1767])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hesperidin (ii) nobiletin and (iii) tangeretin\n\n</div>\n\n厚朴花\n\n金銀花\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 376, 879, 1195])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Citri Exocarpium Rubrum extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Hesperidin standard solution 2. Nobiletin standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of hesperidin and nobiletin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Hesperidin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (125 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.25 mg of hesperidin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nNobiletin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (3 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.15 mg of nobiletin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nTangeretin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (1 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of tangeretin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[41, 12, 1946, 256])\n\ntime. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>85→80</td><td>15→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-25</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-35</td><td>80→60</td><td>20→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-55</td><td>60→45</td><td>40→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hesperidin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP and tangeretin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of hesperidin, nobiletin and tangeretin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of hesperidin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from hesperidin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks should not be less than 10000, 100000 and 100000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject hesperidin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP, tangeretin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of hesperidin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks in the chromatograms of hesperidin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP, tangeretin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hesperidin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of hesperidin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP and tangeretin Std-FP. The retention times of hesperidin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 8, 1890, 265])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Citri Exocarpium Rubrum extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Citri Exocarpium Rubrum extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(hesperidin)</td><td>0.44</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,nobiletin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(tangeretin)</td><td>1.10</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[380, 981, 1814, 1510])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Citri Exocarpium Rubrum extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 19, 1945, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 33.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 31.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/citri-exocarpium-rubrum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citri Exocarpium Rubrum","la":"Citri Exocarpium Rubrum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/citri-reticulatae-pericarpium-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[715, 477, 1261, 872])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[728, 909, 909, 1100])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[911, 915, 1090, 1088])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1111, 980, 1248, 1041])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 5mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Chenpi\n\nA. Chenpi B. Magnified image of outer surface\n\nC. Magnified image of inner surface D. Lateral view of Chenpi\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[755, 1663, 959, 1904])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[938, 1658, 1239, 2013])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[729, 2056, 909, 2239])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[919, 2059, 1096, 2230])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1121, 2137, 1271, 2182])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Guang Chenpi A. Guang Chenpi B. Magnified image of outer surface C. Magnified image of inner surface D. Lateral view of Guang Chenpi"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[715, 477, 1261, 872])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[728, 909, 909, 1100])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[911, 915, 1090, 1088])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1111, 980, 1248, 1041])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 5mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Chenpi\n\nA. Chenpi B. Magnified image of outer surface\n\nC. Magnified image of inner surface D. Lateral view of Chenpi\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[755, 1663, 959, 1904])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[938, 1658, 1239, 2013])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[729, 2056, 909, 2239])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[919, 2059, 1096, 2230])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1121, 2137, 1271, 2182])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Guang Chenpi A. Guang Chenpi B. Magnified image of outer surface C. Magnified image of inner surface D. Lateral view of Guang Chenpi"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium\n\nChinese Name: 陳皮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chenpi"},{"name":"16","text":"Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium is the dried pericarp of the ripe fruit of Citrus reticulata Blanco and its cultivars (Rutaceae). The fruit is collected when it is ripe, the pericarp is peeled off and dried under the sun or at low temperature (below $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). The pericarp is subdivided into two types, known as \"Chenpi\" and \"Guang Chenpi\". Guang Chenpi is mainly grown in Guangdong while Chenpi is grown in the other parts of China."},{"name":"16","text":"Chenpi: Often peeled into several lobes connected at the base, or in singular irregular peel, 1-4 mm thick. Outer surface orange-red to reddish-brown, with fine wrinkles and dotted oil cavity, inner surface pale yellowish-white, rough, with yellowish-white or yellowish-brown vein-like vascular bundles. Texture slightly hard and fragile. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and bitter [Fig.1(i)].\n\nGuang Chenpi: Often peeled into three lobes connected at the base, regular in shape, about 1 mm thick. The dotted oil cavity relatively obverse, transparent when observed against light. Texture slightly soft and fragile. Odour highly aromatic; taste slightly pungent and slightly bitter [Fig.1(ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[31, 21, 1937, 261])"},{"name":"16","text":"Chenpi: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of small and subsquare cells, covered with cuticle Mesocarp relatively thick, consisting of parenchymatous cells, with oil cavities, vascular bundles, prisms of calcium oxalate and crystals of hesperidin scattered throughout. Parenchymatous cells of mesocarp rectangular to subrounded, loosely arranged, with unevenly thickened cell wall, sometimes beaded or thickened at corners. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, scattered in mesocarp, polyhedral, rhombic or biconical. Oil cavities large, ovoid to ellipsoid, arranged irregularly, consisting 1-2 layers of cells. Vascular bundles small, collateral, randomly scattered. Crystals of hesperidin numerous, mostly presenting in parenchymatous cells, fan-shaped, rounded or as amorphous masses, some crystals with radial striations [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nGuang Chenpi: Mesocarp relative thin. Crystals of hesperidin less abundant [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Chenpi: Colour yellowish-white to yellowish-brown. Epidermal cells of pericarp polygonal, subsquare or rectangular, cell walls slightly thickened, stomata actinocytic, subrounded, 18-29 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, subsidiary cells 6-8, indistinct. Parenchymatous cells of mesocarp numerous, cells irregular in shape, with unevenly thickened walls, sometimes beaded or thickened at corners. Crystals of hesperidin numerous, colourless or yellow, fan-shaped, rounded or as amorphous masses, some crystals with radial striations. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, polyhedral, rhombic or biconical, 3-32 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, 7-41 $ \\mu $ m long; sometimes two parallel polyhedral crystals or 3-5 prisms occur together in one cell. Spiral, pitted and reticulate vessels and tracheids small [Fig. 3(i)].\n\nGuang Chenpi: Stomata actinocytic, subrounded, 18-28 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, subsidiary cells 6-8, indistinct. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, polyhedral, rhombic or biconical, 4-33 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, 6-38 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long; sometimes two parallel polyhedral crystals or 3-5 prisms occur together in one cell [Fig. 3(ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 891, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1267, 194, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[257, 438, 1088, 1004])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[352, 1096, 881, 1556])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1122, 444, 1704, 1556])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[350, 1634, 888, 2050])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1121, 1633, 1677, 2047])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Chenpi A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle D. Crystals of hesperidin E. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Prism of calcium oxalate 3. Mesocarp 4. Oil cavity\n\n5. Vascular bundle 6. Crystal of hesperidin\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1047, 40, 1180, 87])\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1296, 69, 1443, 118])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1750, 73, 1846, 120])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[268, 500, 929, 781])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[300, 915, 832, 1400])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1003, 477, 1710, 1420])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[304, 1498, 931, 1973])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1007, 1513, 1703, 1940])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Guang Chenpi A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle D. Crystals of hesperidin E. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Prism of calcium oxalate 3. Mesocarp 4. Oil cavity\n\n5. Vascular bundle 6. Crystal of hesperidin\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[62, 40, 1939, 257])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[269, 673, 1704, 1828])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Chenpi\n\n1. Epidermal cells of pericarp (1-1 epidermal cells of pericarp, 1-2 epidermal cells of pericarp with stoma $ \\longrightarrow $)\n\n2. Parenchymatous cells of mesocarp\n\n3. Crystals of hesperidin (3-1 rounded, 3-2 fan-shaped)\n\n4. Prisms of calcium oxalate (4-1 two parallel polyhedral crystals in a cell, 4-2 rhombic crystals)\n\n5. Reticulate vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1295, 69, 1444, 119])\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[265, 674, 672, 1084])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[690, 677, 1252, 1083])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1267, 678, 1716, 1080])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[270, 1103, 636, 1444])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[668, 1103, 1181, 1446])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1212, 1102, 1718, 1446])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[270, 1464, 720, 1782])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[739, 1463, 1144, 1781])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1162, 1464, 1717, 1780])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Guang Chenpi\n\n1. Epidermal cells of pericarp with stoma (->) 2. Parenchymatous cells of mesocarp\n\n3. Crystals of hesperidin (3-1 amorphous, 3-2 rounded)\n\n4. Prisms of calcium oxalate (4-1 rhombic crystals, 4-2 two rhombic crystals in a cell)\n\n5. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"16","text":"Hesperidin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of hesperidin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (13:7:2, v/v). Shake well and use the lower layer."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hesperidin standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[418, 363, 1104, 918])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[418, 984, 1106, 1491])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[427, 1551, 879, 1862])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[428, 1927, 907, 2201])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hesperidin (ii) narirutin (iii) nobiletin and (iv) tangeretin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1266, 193, 1388, 240])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[379, 374, 807, 1073])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2(i) 2(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Hesperidin standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) Chenpi\n\n(ii) Guang Chenpi\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of hesperidin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"Hesperidin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hesperidin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nNarirutin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of narirutin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nNobiletin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of nobiletin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nTangeretin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of tangeretin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 90 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue for three times each with 3 mL of methanol. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (283 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>79</td><td>21</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>79→55</td><td>21→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hesperidin Std-FP, narirutin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP and tangeretin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of hesperidin, narirutin, nobiletin and tangeretin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of hesperidin, narirutin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from hesperidin, narirutin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks should not be less than 4000, 4000, 50000 and 35000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject hesperidin Std-FP, narirutin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP, tangeretin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of hesperidin, narirutin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks in the chromatograms of hesperidin Std-FP, narirutin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP, tangeretin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify\n\nhesperidin, narirutin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of hesperidin Std-FP, narirutin Std-FP, nobiletin Std-FP and tangeretin Std-FP. The retention times of hesperidin, narirutin, nobiletin and tangeretin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(narirutin)</td><td>0.30</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(hesperidin)</td><td>0.39</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.65</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,nobiletin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(tangeretin)</td><td>1.19</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[341, 1440, 1802, 1984])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Chenpi extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[339, 394, 1802, 977])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium - Guang Chenpi extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 39.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 29.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/citri-reticulatae-pericarpium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium","la":"Citri Reticulatae Pericarpium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/clematidis-radix-et-rhizoma-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clematidis Radix et Rhizoma v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[646, 700, 1346, 1297])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of the dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck A. Root and rhizome B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[726, 1658, 1256, 2344])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried root and rhizome of Clematis manshurica Rupr. A. Root and rhizome B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"23","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[646, 700, 1346, 1297])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of the dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck A. Root and rhizome B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[726, 1658, 1256, 2344])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried root and rhizome of Clematis manshurica Rupr. A. Root and rhizome B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n</div>"},{"name":"23","text":"Official Name: Clematidis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 威靈仙\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Weilingxian"},{"name":"23","text":"Clematidis Radix et Rhizoma is the dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck or Clematis manshurica Rupr. (Ranunculaceae). The root and rhizome are collected in autumn, soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Clematidis Radix et Rhizoma."},{"name":"23","text":"Roots slender-cylindrical, slightly curved, 5-30 cm long, 1-5 mm in diameter. Externally greyishbrown or brown, with longitudinal fine wrinkles, sometimes showing yellowish-white wood when bark exfoliated. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken; fracture showing a relatively thick bark layer, wood slightly squared and yellowish-white, usually with cleft between bark and wood. Rhizomes unevenly cylindrical, 1.5-3.5 cm long (occasionally up to 10 cm), 2.5-25 mm in diameter. Externally pale brownish-grey, crowned by remnants of stem, with numerous rootlets at the lower part. Texture relatively tough; fracture fibrous. Odour slight; taste bland [Fig.1 (i)]."},{"name":"23","text":"Root: The outer walls of epidermal cells thickened, brownish-black. Cortex broad, consisting of parenchytmatous cells, the cells contain with starch granules. Exodermis and endodermis distinct. Phloem fibre in bundles, or group with stone cells mainly present on the outer side of phloem. Cambium distinct. Xylem appearing diarch, completely lignified, subsquare (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"23","text":"Colour greyish-brown or brown. Starch granules numerous, single starch granules subrounded, 4-19 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum pointed; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules numerous, composed of 2-6 units. Epidermal cells subrectangular in surface view, 36-154 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 20-48 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide, outer periclinal walls dark brown. Vessels bordered-pitted, 11-60 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, bordered pits dense or sparse. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles, long fusiform, tapering towards the end or tail-like, 11-45 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls thickened and lignified, pit canals relatively dense (Fig. 3).\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 427, 1727, 2036])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Clematis chinensis Osbeck\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Phloem fibre bundles and stone cells\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Exodermis 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Phloem 6. Cambium\n\n7. Xylem 8. Stone cells 9. Phloem fibre\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[552, 98, 717, 154])\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n鬱金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1072, 158, 1235, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1428, 102, 1589, 162])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[278, 593, 1699, 1966])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck 1. Starch granules 2. Epidermal cells 3. Bordered-pitted vessels 4. Fibre a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"23","text":"Hederagenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of hederagenin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nOleanolic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of oleanolic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"23","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (5:3:0.1, v/v).\n\nAdd slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"23","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a cellulose extraction thimble. Place the cellulose extraction thimble in soxhlet extractor. Add 100 mL of ethyl acetate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Perform the soxhlet extraction for 2 h. Collect and dry the residue in air. Transfer the dried residue to a 50-mL conical flask and add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the sonication for two more times. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 30 mL of hydrochloric acid (7.3%, w/v). Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 30 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts and transfer to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 9, 1959, 262])"},{"name":"23","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hederagenin standard solution, oleanolic acid standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[437, 1136, 986, 1568])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[437, 1638, 1024, 2020])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hederagenin and (ii) oleanolic acid\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[386, 372, 822, 1092])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Oleanolic acid standard solution 2. Hederagenin standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of hederagenin and oleanolic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"23","text":"Hederagenin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (220 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.2 mg of hederagenin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nOleanolic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (240 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of oleanolic acid CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"23","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (75%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 30 mL of hydrochloric acid (7.3%, w/v). Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 30 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts and transfer to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 9, 1964, 261])\n\nin a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"23","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (205 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-45</td><td>60→10</td><td>40→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>10</td><td>90</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"23","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hederagenin Std-FP and oleanolic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of hederagenin and oleanolic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of hederagenin and oleanolic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from hederagenin and oleanolic acid peaks should not be less than 200000 and 150000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"23","text":"Separately inject hederagenin Std-FP, oleanolic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of hederagenin and oleanolic acid peaks in the chromatograms of hederagenin Std-FP, oleanolic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hederagenin and oleanolic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of hederagenin Std-FP and oleanolic acid Std-FP. The retention times of hederagenin and oleanolic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 5, 1898, 253])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, hederagenin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(oleanolic acid)</td><td>1.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[372, 1040, 1721, 1790])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Clematis chinensis Osbeck extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"23","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 15, 1957, 261])\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%."},{"name":"23","text":"Total ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"23","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%.\n\nWater-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 19.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"23","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"23","text":"Mixed hederagenin and oleanolic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (2000 mg/L for hederagenin and 2340 mg/L for oleanolic acid)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of hederagenin CRS and 11.7 mg of oleanolic acid CRS, and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed hederagenin and oleanolic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed hederagenin and oleanolic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 200, 500, 1000, 1500, 2000 mg/L for hederagenin and 117, 234, 585, 1170, 1755 mg/L for oleanolic acid."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/clematidis-radix-et-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clematidis Radix et Rhizoma","la":"Clematidis Radix et Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cnidii-fructus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cnidii Fructus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[583, 872, 1378, 1862])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[583, 872, 1378, 1862])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cnidii Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 蛇床子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shechuangzi"},{"name":"12","text":"Cnidii Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Cnidium monnieri (L.) Cuss. (Apiaceae). The ripe fruit is collected during summer or autumn, followed by removal of the foreign matter, then dried under the sun to obtain Cnidii Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Cremocarp ellipsoid, 1.5-4 mm long, 1-3 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-yellow to greyish-brown or yellowish-brown; stylopoida 2, curved outward at the top; occasionally with a small fruit stalk at the base. Dorsal side of the mericarps consists of 5 thin and prominent longitudinal ridges; commissures flat, with 2 brown and slightly raised longitudinal ribs, each in a furrow. Pericarp brittle, easily fallen off when rubbed. Seeds small, greyish-brown, oleaginous. Odour fragrant; taste pungent, cool and tongue-numbing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of flat cells, in a degenerative state, covered with cuticles. Mesocarp relatively thick, ridges prominent, consisting of vittae, reticulated cells, and vascular bundles. Vascular bundles are located in the middle of the ridges, surrounded by reticulated cells. There are 6 vittae, including 1 vitta between each 2 ridges and 2 vittae at commissural surface; vittae relatively large, oblong in shape. Endocarp consists of 1-2 layers of flat inlaid cells, rectangle in shape. Testa consists of 1 layer of cells, in a degenerative state and a few rows of cells in commissure with a small vascular bundle in the raphe. Endosperm contains numerous aleurone granules and fine cluster crystals or subprisms of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[23, 15, 1972, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Inlaid (endocarp) cells yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, subsquare to polygonal, cell walls bead-like thickened. Endosperm cells polygonal to ovate in shape, containing aleurone granules and small clusters of calcium oxalate, 3-6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Reticulated cells colourless to pale yellow, subsquare to subrounded, 10-34 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with reticulated striations; walls slightly thickened, non-lignified to slightly lignified. Vessel elements are mainly spiral, 4-19 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Vittae mostly dismembered, fragments of vittae yellowish-brown to reddish-brown in colour; septa can be observed occasionally, cellular trace indistinct on the surface, with subglobular oil droplets inside (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Bergapten standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of bergapten CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nImperatorin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of imperatorin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nOsthole standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of osthole CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nXanthotoxin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of xanthotoxin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of methanol, water and glacial acetic acid (70:30:5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC RP-18 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ (2-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ ) plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately bergapten standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), imperatorin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), osthole standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), xanthotoxin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[53, 1, 1929, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[232, 458, 1665, 2029])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cnidii Fructus\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle D. Endosperm\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Vascular bundles 4. Vitta 5. Endocarp 6. Testa\n\n7. Endosperm 8. Embryo 9. Raphe vascular bundle\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[22, 16, 1977, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[381, 669, 965, 1215])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1022, 669, 1649, 1246])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[368, 1254, 981, 1574])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1008, 1266, 1321, 1573])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1341, 1264, 1649, 1577])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[344, 1602, 800, 1911])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[824, 1616, 1277, 1905])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1319, 1609, 1647, 1904])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cnidii Fructus\n\n1. Inlaid (endocarp) cells 2. Endosperm cells 3. Reticulated cells\n\n4. Endosperm cells (containing aleurone granules and clusters of calcium oxalate)\n\n5. 6. Spiral vessels 7. Vitta\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[54, 2, 1925, 264])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[452, 624, 791, 835])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[451, 967, 800, 1320])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[452, 1435, 834, 1724])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[453, 1861, 791, 2054])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) bergapten (ii) imperatorin (iii) osthole and (iv) xanthotoxin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 16, 1969, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Bergapten standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of bergapten CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nImperatorin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of imperatorin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nOsthole standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of osthole CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nXanthotoxin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of xanthotoxin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (310 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>40→60</td><td>60→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>60→80</td><td>40→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bergapten Std-FP, imperatorin Std-FP, osthole Std-FP and xanthotoxin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin peaks should not be less than 30000, 95000, 150000 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[27, 3, 1930, 258])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5. The R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject bergapten Std-FP, imperatorin Std-FP, osthole Std-FP, xanthotoxin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin peaks in the chromatograms of bergapten Std-FP, imperatorin Std-FP, osthole Std-FP, xanthotoxin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of bergapten Std-FP, imperatorin Std-FP, osthole Std-FP and xanthotoxin Std-FP. The retention times of bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cnidii Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cnidii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(xanthotoxin)</td><td>0.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(bergapten)</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, imperatorin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(osthole)</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1892, 45, 1984, 85])\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba 半枝蓮\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[381, 393, 1719, 865])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cnidii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 13.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 4, 1929, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (5 mg/L for bergapten, 25 mg/L for imperatorin, 50 mg/L for osthole and 5 mg/L for xanthotoxin) Weigh accurately 0.25 mg of bergapten CRS, 1.25 mg of imperatorin CRS, 2.5 mg of osthole CRS and 0.25 mg of xanthotoxin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed bergapten, imperatorin, osthole and xanthotoxin StdStock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.5, 5.0 mg/L for bergapten, 1.25, 2.5, 5.0, 12.5, 25 mg/L for imperatorin, 2.5, 5.0, 10, 25, 50 mg/L for osthole and 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.5, 5.0 mg/L for xanthotoxin."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh accurately 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for four more times. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cnidii-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cnidii Fructus","la":"Cnidii Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/commelinae-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Commelinae Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/commelinae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Commelinae Herba","la":"Commelinae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corni-fructus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corni Fructus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[616, 1138, 1390, 1674])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1928, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[616, 1138, 1390, 1674])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1928, 255])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Corni Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 山茱萸\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shanzhuyu"},{"name":"12","text":"Corni Fructus is the dried ripe sarcocarp of Cornus officinalis Sieb. et Zucc. (Cornaceae). The fruit is collected in late autumn and early winter when the pericarp turns red. It is dipped in boiling water for several minutes, then the core is removed, and the seedless fruit is dried to obtain Corni Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregular pieces or sacciform, 0.9-2 cm long and 0.5-1.2 cm wide. Externally dark red, cracked and shrivelled. Sometimes with a rounded scar of persistent calyx at the apex and a fruit stalk scar at the base. Texture soft. Odour slight; taste sour, astringent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of slightly flat cells, covered by relatively thick cuticle. Mesocarp broad, consisting of numerous rows of parenchymatous cells varying in size, some cells contain dark brown pigment masses. Vascular bundles 8, arranged in an interrupted ring on the inner side. Fibres occasionally present. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour red to pale reddish-brown. Exocarp cells colourless to pale orange-yellow, polygonal or subrectangular in surface view, 7-34 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, anticlinal walls slightly bead-like thickened, outer periclinal walls granularly cutinized and thickened; rectangular in lateral view, with relatively thick cuticle. Mesocarp cells pale orange-brown, mostly shrunken, with indistinct boundaries. Inulin masses with fan-shaped striations, 23-105 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; orange to polychromatic under the\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 7, 1984, 263])\n\npolarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4-38 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells single or in groups; single cells ovoid, subsquare, rectangular or irregular in shape, 18-106 $ \\mu $m long, sometimes up to 176 $ \\mu $m, 11-63 $ \\mu $m in diameter, usually with distinct pits and a large lumen, sometimes with distinct striations and a small lumen. Fibres 9-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Mainly spiral vessels, 5-28 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Loganin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of loganin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nMorroniside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of morroniside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, water and formic acid (6:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 1 mL of sulphuric acid to 50 mL of glacial acetic acid and then add 0.5 mL of 4-methoxybenzaldehyde. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 5 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter the supernatant."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately loganin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) morroniside standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (0.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 9 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-7 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of loganin and morroniside.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[170, 14, 327, 72])\n\nDictamni Cortex\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[597, 15, 695, 68])\n\nArctii Fructus牛蒡子\n\n湖北貝母\n\nAurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[949, 60, 1016, 99])\n\n延胡索 Corydalis Rhizoma\n\nFritillariae Hupchoniae Bulhua Corni Fructus\n\n砒霜 Arsenicum\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1693, 26, 1901, 107])\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nCurcumae Longae Rhizoma\n\n蒼朮 薑黄\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 447, 932, 1001])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[943, 468, 1686, 973])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[260, 1129, 924, 1513])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[940, 1133, 1691, 1510])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Vascular bundles, fibres and clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchyma\n\nD. Exocarp covered by cuticle\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Vascular bundle 4. Hollow cavity 5. Pigment masses 6. Fibre\n\n7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Cuticle\n\nOrpiment\n\nCurculiginis Rhizoma\n\n雄黄\n\nRealgar\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 46, 1984, 86])\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n五味子\n\nSchisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[301, 457, 1686, 1968])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Exocarp cells (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view)\n\n2. Mesocarp cells 3. Inulin masses 4. Clusters of calcium oxalate 5. Stone cells 6. Fibre 7. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 3, 1934, 258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[401, 367, 926, 847])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[447, 941, 927, 1415])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) loganin and (ii) morroniside\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Loganin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (80 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of loganin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (50%). Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 6, 1978, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (240 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>98→92</td><td>2→8</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-30</td><td>92→90</td><td>8→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>90→88</td><td>10→12</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of loganin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of loganin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of loganin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from loganin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject loganin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of loganin peak in the chromatogram of loganin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify loganin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of loganin Std-FP. The retention times of loganin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Corni Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 4, 1935, 258])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Corni Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.27</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(morroniside)</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,loganin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[387, 849, 1893, 1559])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Corni Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[28, 21, 1976, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corni-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corni Fructus","la":"Corni Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-eucommiae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Eucommiae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[778, 867, 1224, 1202])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[736, 1217, 1229, 1480])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[753, 1511, 1215, 1764])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cortex Eucommiae\n\nA. Outer surface\n\nB. Outer surface with fracture showing resinous threads\n\nC. Inner surface with fracture showing resinous threads\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[48, 1, 1950, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[778, 867, 1224, 1202])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[736, 1217, 1229, 1480])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[753, 1511, 1215, 1764])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cortex Eucommiae\n\nA. Outer surface\n\nB. Outer surface with fracture showing resinous threads\n\nC. Inner surface with fracture showing resinous threads\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[48, 1, 1950, 249])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Cortex Eucommiae\n\nChinese Name: 杜仲\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Duzhong"},{"name":"10","text":"Cortex Eucommiae is the dried stem bark of Eucommia ulmoides Oliv. (Eucommiaceae). The bark is collected from April to June, the coarse outer portion removed, piled up for 5-7 days until the inner surface becomes purplish-brown, then dried under the sun to obtain Cortex Eucommiae."},{"name":"10","text":"Flat pieces, or the two edges somewhat curved inward, varying in size, 2-7 mm thick. Outer surface pale brown or greyish-brown, markedly wrinkled or fissured and channelled longitudinally; some pieces relatively thin, showing distinct lenticels when the coarse bark is unscraped. Inner surface dark purple, smooth. Texture fragile, easily broken; fracture linked by fine, dense, silvery, elastic and resinous threads. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"The transverse section sometimes shows evidence of rhytidome outside the cork cells. Cork consists of several rows of cells. Phloem consists of 5-7 bands of lignified stone cells, each band contains 2-6 rows of stone cells, with fibres nearby. Phloem rays 2-3 cells wide. Resinous masses scattered (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour brown. Resinous threads stripe-shaped or twisted into masses, the surface granular. Stone cells numerous, mostly in groups, subrectangular to subrounded, elongated-rectangular or irregular, 15-80 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, up to 180 $ \\mu $ m long, thick-walled, some containing resinous masses. Cork cells polygonal in surface view, 12-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with unevenly thickened, lignified and finely pitted walls; rectangular in lateral view, wall thickened on three sides and relatively thin on one side, pit canals distinct (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 0, 1965, 248])"},{"name":"10","text":"Pinoresinol diglucoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of pinoresinol diglucoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and formic acid (3:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add slowly 20 mL of sulphuric acid to 80 mL of water."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of water. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separatory funnel. Extract the solution with 50 mL of dichloromethane and discard the dichloromethane layer. Extract the aqueous layer with 50 mL of 1-butanol. Evaporate the 1-butanol extract to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately pinoresinol diglucoside standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of pinoresinol diglucoside.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 1, 1947, 251])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[246, 454, 1692, 2236])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cortex Eucommiae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cork cells D. Resinous masses E. Groups of stone cells\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2.Cortex 3. Resinous masses 4. Phloem 5. Phloem ray\n\n6. Groups of stone cells\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n連翹\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1044, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\nCortex Eucommiae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[243, 424, 1680, 1722])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cortex Eucommiae\n\n</div>\n\n1. Resinous threads 2. Elongated-rectangular stone cells 3. Subrectangular stone cells\n\n4. Irregular stone cell 5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 11, 255, 94])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 23, 1089, 118])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[947, 177, 1067, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[504, 449, 1545, 800])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of pinoresinol diglucoside\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Pinoresinol diglucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of pinoresinol diglucoside CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.4 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>90→80</td><td>10→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>80→60</td><td>20→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>60-70</td><td>60→0</td><td>40→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of pinoresinol diglucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of pinoresinol diglucoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of pinoresinol diglucoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from pinoresinol diglucoside peak should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 2, 1966, 251])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject pinoresinol diglucoside Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of pinoresinol diglucoside peak in the chromatogram of pinoresinol diglucoside Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify pinoresinol diglucoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of pinoresinol diglucoside Std-FP. The retention times of pinoresinol diglucoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Cortex Eucommiae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Cortex Eucommiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,pinoresinol diglucoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.59</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.23</td><td>±0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[391, 1672, 1750, 2455])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cortex Eucommiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[47, 0, 1948, 255])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 13.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"10","text":"Pinoresinol diglucoside standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of pinoresinol diglucoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nPinoresinol diglucoside standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the pinoresinol diglucoside Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 1, 10, 50, 100, 200 mg/L for pinoresinol diglucoside."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-eucommiae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Eucommiae","la":"Cortex Eucommiae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-magnoliae-officinalis-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[567, 592, 1423, 1350])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[546, 1573, 1440, 2416])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 251])"},"sections":[{"name":"18","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[567, 592, 1423, 1350])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[546, 1573, 1440, 2416])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 251])"},{"name":"18","text":"Official Name: Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis\n\nChinese Name: 厚樸\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Houpo"},{"name":"18","text":"Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis is the dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. or Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils. (Magnoliaceae). The stem bark is collected in the spring and summer, and then boiled in water until soft, then piled in a wet place until its inner surface and the surface of the transverse section purplish-brown or brown in colour and shiny or showing luster, then further softened by steaming, rolled and dried to obtain Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis."},{"name":"18","text":"Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. : The stem bark quilled singly or double-quilled, 25-75 cm long and 1-4 mm thick (known as Tongpo); the stem bark near the root has one end expanding like the mouth of a bell, 13-25 cm long and 3-8 mm thick (known as Xuetongpo). The outer surface greyish-yellow or greyish-brown, rough, sometimes scaly, easily exfoliated, with distinct elliptical lenticels and longitudinal wrinkles. The bark with the cork scraped off exhibits a yellowish-brown colour, the inner surface purplishbrown or dark brown, smooth, with fine and dense longitudinal striations and exhibits an oily trace on scratching. Texture hard, not easily broken, fracture granular, outer layer greyish-brown, inner layer purplish-brown or brown, oily, sometimes with numerous small bright spots visible. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and slightly bitter [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nCompared with Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils., Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. has a coarser outer surface, a less fibrous fracture, and a more oily and aromatic odour.\n\nMagnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils.: The processing procedure of stem bark is the same as that of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils., and stem bark is often classified to \"Tongpo\" and \"Xuetongpo\". The outer surface greyish-brown, with distinct elliptical lenticels and longitudinal wrinkles. The inner surface purplish-brown or dark brown, relatively smooth, with fine and dense\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 3, 1964, 250])\n\nlongitudinal striations; arid, less oily, with the fracture much more fibrous. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly pungent and slightly bitter [Fig. 1(ii)].\n\nCompared with Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils., Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils. has a finer outer surface, with the fracture being much more fibrous, dry and less oily."},{"name":"18","text":"Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. : The cork consists of more than 10 layers of cells, cells rectangular, with suberized and slightly lignified wall, rhytidome tissue visible. The outer side of cortex showing a ring of stone cells consisting of several layers of tangentially elongated stone cells, cells rectangular or elongated-rounded, 7-65 $ \\mu $m in diameter; the inner side scattered with single or groups of stone cells, some cells polygonal or irregularly branched, large in size, nearly up to 300 $ \\mu $m long, with distinct pit canals and striations; oil cells numerous, scattered, rounded or elliptical; fibre bundles occasionally observed. Phloem rays narrow; fibres mostly in several bundles, alternately arranged with the parenchyma cells of the sieve vessels, slightly tangentially arranged intermittently in layers; oil cells numerous, scattered. Parenchyma cells contain prismatic or polyhedral crystals of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nMagnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils. : The transverse section shows cork, cortex, and phloem from the outer to the inner side. The outer side of cortex shows a ring of stone cells consisting of 3-7 layers of tangentially elongated stone cells, cells rectangular or elongated-rounded, 9-50 $ \\mu $m in diameter; the inner side of cortex scattered with numerous irregular stone cells, nearly up to 220 $ \\mu $m long [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils.: Colour brown. Stone cells numerous, some of them subsquare, elongated-rounded or ovate, 7-65 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, and some of the cells irregularly branched, nearly up to 300 $ \\mu $ m long, sometimes pit canals and striations visible. Fibres numerous, rather long, mostly broken, with strongly thickened wall, lignified; bright polychrome when observed under the polarized microscope. Oil cells ellipsoid or subround, containing yellowish-brown oily contents. Cork cells yellowish-brown, polygonal in surface view. Prismatic or polyhedral crystals of calcium oxalate rare [Fig. 3(i)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 250])\n\nMagnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils. : Colour brown. Stone cells numerous, some of them sub-square, elongated-rounded or ovate, 9-50 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, some others irregularly branched, nearly up to 220 $ \\mu m $ long, sometimes pit canals and striations visible. Fibres mostly broken, with strongly thickened wall, lignified; bright polychrome when observed under the polarized microscope. Oil cells few, ellipsoid or subround, containing yellowish-brown oily contents. Cork cells yellowish-brown, polygonal in surface view. Prismatic or polyhedral crystals of calcium oxalate rare [Fig. 3(ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 4 mL of ethanol. Heat the mixture in a water bath (80-90 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) for 5 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a test tube. Add two drops of aqueous iron (III) chloride solution (9% , w/v). A dark green solution is observed."},{"name":"18","text":"Magnolol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of magnolol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nHonokiol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of honokiol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, toluene and ethyl acetate (5:4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"18","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[22, 7, 1962, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[425, 443, 891, 929])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[420, 965, 849, 1333])\n\n10μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n50μm\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[415, 1360, 950, 1681])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[428, 1747, 800, 2105])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[989, 437, 1610, 1228])\n\n10μm\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[988, 1239, 1609, 2069])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil cell D. Stone cells E. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. The ring of stone cells 4. Oil cells 5. Stone cells 6. Fibre bundles 7. Phloem\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 0, 1983, 254])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[248, 427, 1718, 2135])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil cell D. Stone cells E. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. The ring of stone cells 4. Oil cells 5. Stone cells 6. Fibre bundles 7. Phloem\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[13, 8, 1962, 254])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 446, 1624, 1754])\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils.\n\n1. Group of large irregular stone cells 2. Stone cells 3. Fibre bundles alternated with parenchyma ray cells\n\n4. Fibre bundles 5. Oil cell 6. Cork cells 7. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[410, 440, 742, 769])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[771, 451, 1170, 769])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1196, 450, 1594, 772])\n\n50μm\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[461, 832, 1124, 1361])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1224, 834, 1517, 1347])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[417, 1413, 813, 1847])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[848, 1411, 1243, 1841])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1290, 1411, 1570, 1832])\n\n50μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Group of large irregular stone cells 2. Stone cells 3. Fibre bundles alternated with parenchyma ray cells\n\n4. Fibre bundles 5. Oil cells 6. Cork cells 7. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[20, 0, 1943, 248])"},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to another tube. Evaporate the solvent to dryness with a gentle stream of nitrogen. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately magnolol standard solution, honokiol standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (0.5-1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm) and visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of magnolol and honokiol.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[762, 1498, 1311, 2409])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) magnolol and (ii) honokiol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[45, 0, 1961, 245])"},{"name":"18","text":"Magnolol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of magnolol CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"18","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (320 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-55</td><td>50→0</td><td>50→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-60</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"18","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of magnolol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of magnolol should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of magnolol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from magnolol peak should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Separately inject magnolol Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of magnolol peak in the chromatogram of magnolol Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify magnolol peak in\n\nthe chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of magnolol Std-FP. The retention times of magnolol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.24</td><td>±0.02</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.31</td><td>±0.02</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.40</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(honokiol)</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(marker,magnolol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[404, 1486, 1749, 2099])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[405, 385, 1753, 917])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried stem bark of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. var. biloba Rehd. et Wils. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[31, 4, 1919, 245])"},{"name":"18","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"18","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-magnoliae-officinalis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis","la":"Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-mori-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Mori v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[34, 0, 1944, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[34, 0, 1944, 252])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Cortex Mori\n\nChinese Name: 桑白皮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Sangbaipi"},{"name":"11","text":"Cortex Mori is the dried root bark of Morus alba L. (Moraceae). The root is collected in late autumn, when the leaves fall off, and in early spring, before the start of new growth, cut longitudinally, the root bark is stripped off, then dried under the sun to obtain Cortex Mori."},{"name":"11","text":"Quilled, channelled, flattened or segmented, varying in length and width, 1-4 mm thick. Outer surface mostly with orangish-yellow or yellowish-brown scales bark; inner surface yellowish-white to brownish-yellow, with fine longitudinal striations. Texture light and tenacious, strongly fibrous, uneasily broken, but easily stripped longitudinally, dusting on stripping. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork cells tangentially elongated and orderly arranged, slightly or fully lignified. Phloem rays 2-6 cells wide. Laticiferous tubes scattered. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles, unlignified or slightly lignified. Parenchyma cells contain starch grains, some contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Groups of sclerenchyma cells mixed with stone cells are scattered in the older root bark, most cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale greyish-yellow to pale brownish-yellow. Fibres numerous, 10-26 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, wall thickened, showing a polychrome when examined under a polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate 7-35 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, showing a polychrome when examined under a polarized microscope. Cork cells yellow, golden-yellow or yellowish-brown, polygonal or subsquare in surface view. Crystal-containing sclerenchyma cells suborbicular, subsquare or irregular in shape,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 2, 1966, 250])\n\nsometimes annular striations observed. Stone cells suborbicular, subsquare, rectangular or irregular in shape, 17-56 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, wall relatively thickened or extremely thickened, with distinct pits and pit-canals, some contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Simple starch granules subglobose to ovoid, 2-16 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with dotted, cleft or asteroidal hilum in larger granules; compound starch grains mostly composed of 2-5 units, showing a black, cross shape when examined under a polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Morusin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of morusin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and ethanol (10:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Collect the supernatant."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately morusin standard solution and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of morusin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 1, 1948, 250])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 456, 1751, 2057])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cortex Mori\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. A group of crystal-containing sclerenchyma cells mixed with stone cells\n\nD. Magnified image of laticifer, prisms of calcium oxalate and fibres\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex\n\n3. A group of crystal-containing sclerenchyma cells mixed with stone cells\n\n4. Starch grains 5. Phloem 6. Laticiferous tubes\n\n7. Prisms of calcium oxalate 8. Phloem ray 9. Phloem fibres\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n燈心草\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nCortex Mori\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[393, 448, 1589, 2116])\n\n25μm"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Phloem fibres 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Cork cells\n\n4. Sclerenchyma cells with prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Stone cell with prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Stone cell 7. Starch grains\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 0, 1947, 247])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[721, 460, 1273, 818])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of morusin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Morusin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of morusin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nMorusin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (8 mg/L)\n\nPipette 5 mL of morusin Std-Stock into a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter. Make appropriate dilution where necessary."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times1 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>57</td><td>43</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-38</td><td>57→46</td><td>43→54</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>38-65</td><td>46</td><td>54</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 0, 1967, 251])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of morusin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of morusin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of morusin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from morusin peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject morusin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of morusin peak in the chromatogram of morusin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify morusin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of morusin Std-FP. The retention times of morusin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Cortex Mori extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Cortex Mori extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,morusin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n葛根 Radix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[857, 24, 1090, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[947, 180, 1063, 241])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[391, 536, 1750, 1010])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cortex Mori extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 17.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[24, 0, 1956, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Morusin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of morusin CRS and dissolve in 250 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nMorusin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the morusin Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (95%) to produce a series of solutions of 1,4,8,12,16 mg/L for morusin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-mori","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Mori","la":"Cortex Mori"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-moutan-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Moutan v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-moutan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Moutan","la":"Cortex Moutan"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-phellodendri-amurensis-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Phellodendri Amurensis v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-phellodendri-amurensis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Phellodendri Amurensis","la":"Cortex Phellodendri Amurensis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-phellodendri-chinensis-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Phellodendri Chinensis v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cortex-phellodendri-chinensis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortex Phellodendri Chinensis","la":"Cortex Phellodendri Chinensis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corydalis-bungeanae-herba-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corydalis Bungeanae Herba v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[551, 975, 1158, 1405])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1175, 991, 1428, 1396])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[592, 1450, 1113, 1841])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1245, 1443, 1390, 1582])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1213, 1632, 1405, 1819])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nA. Corydalis Bungeanae Herba B. Magnified image of aerial part\n\nC. Magnified image of root and fracture of root\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of leaves and fruits (fruits $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nF. Magnified image of flowers G. Magnified image of seeds (caruncle $ \\rightarrow $ )"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[551, 975, 1158, 1405])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1175, 991, 1428, 1396])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[592, 1450, 1113, 1841])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1245, 1443, 1390, 1582])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1213, 1632, 1405, 1819])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nA. Corydalis Bungeanae Herba B. Magnified image of aerial part\n\nC. Magnified image of root and fracture of root\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of leaves and fruits (fruits $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nF. Magnified image of flowers G. Magnified image of seeds (caruncle $ \\rightarrow $ )"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 苦地丁\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Kudiding"},{"name":"16","text":"Corydalis Bungeanae Herba is the dried whole plant of Corydalis bungeana Turcz. (Papaveraceae). The whole plant is collected in summer when fruiting begins, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Corydalis Bungeanae Herba."},{"name":"16","text":"Crumpled into masses, length varies up to about 30 cm. Small root pieces occasionally found externally brown. Stems slender, frequently branched, externally yellowish-green to greyish-green, with 5 longitudinal ridges. Texture soft, fracture hollowed. Leaves mostly crumpled and crumbled, greyish-green to dark green; when whole, lamina bi- or tripinnatisect. Flowers rarely found, usually fallen off; if found the corolla bilabiate, lilac. Capsules flat, oblong to long-elliptic, legume-like; seeds flat-cordate, black, lustrous, often with pale yellowish-white membranous caruncle. Odour slight, taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"16","text":"Root: Cork consists of 1-5 layers of cells, mostly wrinkled or fallen off. Cortex parenchymatous cells tangentially elongated. Phloem parenchymatous cells tangentially elongated, mostly wrinkled. Xylem vessels numerous, mostly several in groups, xylem ray distinct [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells tangentially elongated. Cortex parenchymatous cells irregular in shape. Collenchyma consists of 7-11 layers of cells, present in the ridges. Pericycle fibres arranged in a ring. Fibres in the ridges arranged in crescent. Vascular bundles collateral, found in the ridges; phloem narrow; xylem composed of vessels, fibres and parenchymatous cells. Pith relatively large, hollow in the centre [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 34, 1953, 255])\n\nLeaf: Leaf base consists of 4-7 collateral vascular bundles. Upper and lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Cortex cells irregular in shape. Vascular bundle collateral; phloem narrow, cells mostly wrinkled; vessels lignified [Fig. 2 (iii)].\n\nPericarp: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of epidermal cells, cells mostly shrunken, cell walls slightly thickened. Sclerenchymatous cells located on the inner sides of exocarp and endocarp, walls slightly thickened. Mesocarp consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, cells subrounded or irregular in shape. Vascular bundles collateral; phloem cells small; vessels lignified. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of subrectangular cells, cell walls thickened, non-lignified or slightly lignified [Fig. 2 (iv)].\n\nSeed: Outermost layer of testa blackish-brown, palisade in shape, radially elongated, cell walls thickened, with fine and dense pits; beneath it are several layers of stone cells, cells oblong to subrounded, with thickened walls and distinct pits and pit canals. Decadent cells of testa dark brown. Inner layer of cells of testa oblong, tangentially elongated. Endosperm cells irregular in shape, containing oil droplets and aleurone grains [Fig. 2 (v)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Colour brownish-green to dark green. Lower epidermal cells with sinuous walls, with more stomata in lower epidermis; stomata anomocytic, subsidiary cells 3-6. Endocarp cell wall sinuous or beaded-thickened. Stone cells of inner layer of testa brown, oblong, polygonal to subrounded in surface view, with distinct pits and pit canals. Vessels mainly spiral. Endosperm cells irregular in shape, containing fatty oil droplets and aleurone grains. Outermost layer of testa reddish-brown to blackish-brown, cells palisade-like in lateral view, walls with reticulate pattern. Pericycle fibres mostly in bundles, walls thickened. Seed caruncle cells elongated-fusiform, walls thin (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[235, 391, 1733, 2106])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Xylem ray 5. Xylem\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膪草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[233, 435, 1727, 2111])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Collenchyma 4. Pericycle fibre 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n三棱\n\n天葵子\n\nSemiaquilogiae Radix\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[235, 393, 1744, 2128])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration of the leaf base\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Xylem 4. Phloem 5. Lower epidermis\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n天冬\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膪草\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[234, 402, 1745, 2115])\n\nFigure 2 (iv) Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Exocarp 2. Sclerenchyma 3. Mesocarp 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Endocarp\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[236, 384, 1749, 2130])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (v) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Testa (1a outermost layer, 1b inner layer with several layers of stone cells, 1c decadent cell layer) 2. Inner epidermis of testa 3. Endosperm\n\n</div>\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n天冬\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[925, 86, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1756, 51, 1912, 110])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[233, 417, 1749, 2118])"},{"name":"16","text":"1. Lower epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ ） 2. Endocarp cells in surface view\n\n3. Stone cells of inner layer of testa 4. Spiral vessels 5. Endosperm cells\n\n6. Outermost layer of testa in lateral view 7. Pericycle fibres\n\n8. Seed caruncle cells elongated fusiform"},{"name":"16","text":"Corynoline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of corynoline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and methanol (7:2.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth oxynitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water."},{"name":"16","text":"Transfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid into a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately corynoline standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[925, 86, 1028, 138])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[374, 369, 800, 625])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of corynoline\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[375, 789, 785, 1443])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Corynoline standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of corynoline (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"Corynoline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of corynoline CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (288 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Triethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-50</td><td>60→10</td><td>40→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of corynoline Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of corynoline should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of corynoline peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from corynoline peak should not be less than 60000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject corynoline Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of corynoline peak in the chromatogram of corynoline Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify corynoline peak in the chromatogram of the test solution\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[38, 37, 1949, 252])\n\nby comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of corynoline Std-FP. The retention times of corynoline peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,corynoline)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.16</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.25</td><td>$\\pm0.05$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[339, 1140, 1803, 1562])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Corydalis Bungeanae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 18.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 6.5%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corydalis-bungeanae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corydalis Bungeanae Herba","la":"Corydalis Bungeanae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corydalis-decumbentis-rhizoma-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1034, 891, 1277, 1138])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[419, 1182, 1555, 1741])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma A. Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 22, 1873, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1034, 891, 1277, 1138])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[419, 1182, 1555, 1741])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma A. Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 22, 1873, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 夏天無\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xiatianwu"},{"name":"11","text":"Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma is the dried tuber of Corydalis decumbens (Thunb.) Pers. (Papaveraceae). The tuber is collected in spring or early summer after the seedlings have emerged, foreign matter removed, washed clean, and dried to obtain Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma."},{"name":"11","text":"Subspheroidal, long-rounded or irregular, 0.4-3 cm long, 4-25 mm in diameter. Externally greyish yellow, dark green or dark brown, with tubercular and indistinct fine wrinkles. Apex obtuse and rounded, stem scars visible, surrounded by numerous pale yellow dotted leaf scars and rootlet scars. Texture hard. Fracture yellowish-white or yellow, granular or corneous, some tubers slightly starchy. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of tangentially elongated cells. Cortex consists of several layers of flattened cells, the cells pale yellow, with pits. Vascular bundles collateral, 4-7, arranged radially. Phloem narrow; xylem ribbon-like, vessel minute. Pith situated in the centre (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Hypodermal cells in pieces, pale yellowish-brown, subrectangular or irregular in shape, walls slightly thickened in interrupted beaded shape, pits obvious. Starch granules faintly visible, single starch granules subrounded or oblong, hilum pointed or flyer-shaped; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-6 units. Vessels mainly reticulate, 9-43 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1063, 159, 1223, 211])\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[239, 441, 1748, 2073])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vessel 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Xylem 5. Pith\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[33, 21, 1890, 260])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 654, 1723, 2142])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma 1. Hypodermal cells 2. Starch granules and gelatinized starch granules 3. Vessels a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[41, 14, 1944, 255])"},{"name":"11","text":"Palmatine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of palmatine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nProtopine standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of protopine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, water and formic acid (7:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 45 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately palmatine chloride standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), protopine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapor chamber until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 23, 1890, 261])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[379, 367, 887, 664])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[443, 738, 856, 1041])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) palmatine chloride and (ii) protopine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[383, 1204, 965, 2065])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Protopine standard solution 2. Palmatine chloride standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of palmatine and protopine (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[42, 13, 1944, 257])"},{"name":"11","text":"Palmatine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of palmatine chloride CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol.\n\nProtopine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of protopine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.4 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol ( 50% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (290 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 190 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 12% carbon loading). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.06% Trifluoroacetic acidand0.1% triethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>7→17</td><td>93→83</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>17→25</td><td>83→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of palmatine chloride Std-FP and protopine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of palmatine and protopine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of palmatine and protopine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from palmatine and protopine peaks should not be less than 70000 and 50000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject palmatine chloride Std-FP, protopine Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of palmatine and protopine peaks in the chromatograms of palmatine chloride Std-FP, protopine Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify palmatine and protopine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of palmatine chloride Std-FP and protopine Std-FP. The retention times of palmatine and protopine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,protopine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(palmatine)</td><td>1.46</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[388, 1710, 1808, 2347])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 13, 1944, 257])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 28.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corydalis-decumbentis-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma","la":"Corydalis Decumbentis Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corydalis-rhizoma-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corydalis Rhizoma v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[516, 975, 1511, 1734])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 4, 1928, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[516, 975, 1511, 1734])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 4, 1928, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Corydalis Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 延胡索\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yanhusuo"},{"name":"12","text":"Corydalis Rhizoma is the dried tuber of Corydalis yanhusuo W. T. Wang (Papaveraceae). The tuber is collected in early summer when the plant withered, removed the fibrous root, washed clean, boiled in water until no dry core visible, then dried under the sun to obtain Corydalis Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregularly flattened spherical, 5-27 mm in diameter. Externally yellow to yellowish-brown, with irregular reticulate wrinkles. Top end with slightly sunken stem scars, base usually with warty protuberances, or a slight dent like a navel. Texture hard and fragile, fractured surface yellow to yellowish-brown, cuticular, with waxy lustre. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis occasionally remains as remnants. Cortex broad, consisting 2-3 layers of sclerenchymatous cells at outer side, and with several layers of flattened cells. Laticifer intermittently arranged in several ring. Phloem narrow. Cambium indistinct. Vessels elements small, 2-7 in a bundle, and arranged in a interrupted ring. Pith visible in the centre. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules and starch gelatinous masses (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greenish-yellow. Starch gelatinous masses nearly colourless to pale yellow. Simple starch granules subglobular, hilum distinct; compound granules composed of 2-5 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Sclerenchymatous cells at outer side of cortex greenish-yellow, polygonal, subsquare or subrounded, 7-129 $ \\mu $m wide, or elongated, 42-283 $ \\mu $m\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[23, 15, 1975, 264])\n\nlong; cell walls slightly curved, lignified, some beaded, finely pitted, 2-17 $ \\mu m $ thick. Stone cells yellowish-green, subsquare, subrounded to subpolygonal, 17-87 $ \\mu m $ wide, 28-157 $ \\mu m $ long; wall 2-18 $ \\mu m $ thick. Vessels spiral, 5-52 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Corydaline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of corydaline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nTetrahydropalmatine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of tetrahydropalmatine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether $ ( 6 0-8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}) $ , ethyl acetate and isopropanol (8:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Iodine"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately corydaline standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), tetrahydropalmatine standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapor chamber for about 3 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of corydaline and tetrahydropalmatine.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[597, 15, 696, 69])\n\nArctii Fructus牛蒡子\n\n湖北貝母\n\nAurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\n延胡索 Corydalis Rhizoma\n\n砒霜 Arsenicum\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nCurcumae Longae Rhizoma\n\n蒼朮 薑黄\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[344, 644, 970, 1073])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[372, 1249, 969, 1689])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1014, 550, 1657, 1688])\n\n50μm\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Corydalis Rhizoma\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Laticifer\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Flattened cells 3. Laticifer 4. Cortex 5. Phloem 6. Cambium\n\n7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[13, 6, 1984, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[259, 424, 1690, 2015])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Corydalis Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch gelatinous mass 2. Starch granules 3. Sclerenchymatous cells 4. Stone cell 5. Spiral vessel a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 4, 1931, 258])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[438, 368, 1037, 711])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[446, 878, 1036, 1220])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) corydaline and (ii) tetrahydropalmatine\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Corydaline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of corydaline CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nTetrahydropalmatine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of tetrahydropalmatine CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min.\n\nProgramme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1)\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 3, 1979, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid and0.22% Triethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-27</td><td>20-32</td><td>80-68</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>27-29</td><td>32-42</td><td>68-58</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>29-36</td><td>42-47</td><td>58-53</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>36-40</td><td>47-65</td><td>53-35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-49</td><td>65-75</td><td>35-25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>49-60</td><td>75-100</td><td>25-0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of corydaline Std-FP and tetrahydropalmatine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of corydaline and tetrahydropalmatine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of corydaline and tetrahydropalmatine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from corydaline and tetrahydropalmatine peaks should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject corydaline Std-FP, tetrahydropalmatine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of corydaline and tetrahydropalmatine peaks in the chromatograms of corydaline Std-FP, tetrahydropalmatine Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify corydaline and tetrahydropalmatine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of corydaline Std-FP and tetrahydropalmatine Std-FP. The retention times of corydaline and tetrahydropalmatine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Corydalis Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[27, 3, 1930, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Corydalis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(dehydrocorydaline)</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,tetrahydropalmatine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(corydaline)</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.18</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 971, 1810, 1540])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Corydalis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[31, 22, 1976, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 24.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/corydalis-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corydalis Rhizoma","la":"Corydalis Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/croci-stigma-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Croci Stigma v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[478, 879, 1486, 1904])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1905, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[478, 879, 1486, 1904])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1905, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Croci Stigma\n\nChinese Name: 西紅花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xihonghua"},{"name":"11","text":"Croci Stigma is the dried stigma of Crocus sativus L. (Iridaceae). The flower is collected in the morning at flowering stage, the stigma removed and gathered, then dried under the sun or in shaded area; or dried at ambient temperature to obtain Croci Stigma."},{"name":"11","text":"Linear, 3-branched, 2-3.5 cm long, dark red or reddish-brown. The upper part relatively broader and slightly flattened, the margins of apex irregularly dentate, with a short slit at the inner side, sometimes a small piece of yellow style remained at the lower end. Texture lax, soft and light in weight, without oily lustre; fragile and easily broken after dried. Odour characteristic and slightly irritative; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour orangish-red. Epidermal cells long stripe-shaped in surface view, subsquare, subrounded or subrectangular in section view, wall thin, slightly sinuous, sometimes outer wall protruding or showing papillae. Terminal epidermal cells of stigma tubular protrusions, 18-56 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fragments of style consist of rectangular parenchymatous cells, arranged tightly, lumen containing many fine crystals of calcium oxalate. Vessels mostly annular, relative small, presented in fragments of style or stigma, spiral vessels sometimes visible. Pollen grains subspheroidal, 35-145 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, outer wall almost smooth or with fine spiny exine, containing many minute granular inclusions (Fig. 2).\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nCroci Stigma Rhizoma\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[280, 423, 1706, 2168])"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Epidermal cells (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view, 1-3 in section view)\n\n2. Tubular epidermal cell 3. Parenchymatous cells of style 4. Vessels 5. Pollen grains\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[29, 6, 1904, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Crocin I standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of crocin I CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 1-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%).\n\nCrocin II standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of crocin II CRS (Fig. 3) and place it in a 1-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, isopropanol, water and formic acid (65:35:20:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately crocin I standard solution, crocin II standard solution and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of crocin I and crocin II.\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\nLophatheri Herba淡竹葉\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\n紫苑\n\nCroci Stigma Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[411, 371, 1632, 1113])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[423, 1187, 1593, 1728])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) crocin I and (ii) crocin II\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Crocin I standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (70 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.4 mg of crocin I CRS and place it in a 20-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%).\n\nCrocin II standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of crocin II CRS and place it in a 50-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 5, 1905, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.04 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 40 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>90→50</td><td>10→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>50→40</td><td>50→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of crocin I Std-FP and crocin II Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of crocin I and crocin II should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of crocin I and crocin II peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from crocin I and crocin II peaks should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject crocin I Std-FP, crocin II Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of crocin I and crocin II peaks in the chromatograms of crocin I Std-FP, crocin II Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify crocin I and crocin II peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of crocin I Std-FP and crocin II Std-FP. The retention times of crocin I and crocin II peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[67, 7, 1967, 261])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Croci Stigma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Croci Stigma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(picrocrocin)</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3(crocin I)</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,crocin II)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[290, 984, 1868, 1677])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Croci Stigma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[38, 15, 1904, 265])"},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.03 g of the powdered sample and place it in a cellulose extraction thimble. Place the cellulose extraction thimble in soxhlet extractor. Add 70 mL of methanol to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Perform the soxhlet extraction for about 5 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the extract to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Pipette 5 mL of the solution into a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Measure the absorbance of the solution at 432 nm. The absorbance should not be less than 0.50."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 46.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 50.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 12, 1963, 262])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/croci-stigma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Croci Stigma","la":"Croci Stigma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/crotonis-fructus-unprocessed-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Crotonis Fructus unprocessed v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[689, 914, 1262, 1439])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[585, 1504, 802, 1712])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[884, 1505, 1093, 1710])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1222, 1442, 1394, 1714])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nA. Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of fruit\n\nC. Transverse section of fruit showing seeds in 3 loculi\n\nD. Magnified image of kernel without testa\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1964, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[689, 914, 1262, 1439])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[585, 1504, 802, 1712])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[884, 1505, 1093, 1710])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1222, 1442, 1394, 1714])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nA. Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of fruit\n\nC. Transverse section of fruit showing seeds in 3 loculi\n\nD. Magnified image of kernel without testa\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1964, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nChinese name: 巴豆（生）\n\nChinese phonetic name: Badou (Sheng)"},{"name":"13","text":"Crotonis fructus (unprocessed) is the unprocessed dried ripe fruit of Croton tiglium L. (Euphorbiaceae). The fruit is collected in autumn when ripe, spread out, then dried under the sun to obtain Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed)."},{"name":"13","text":"Ellipsoidal, oblong-ovoid, oval or sub-globose, usually 3-ribbed, 1.5-3 cm long, 2 cm wide. Externally greyish to brownish, rough, with 6 longitudinal lines, radially aligned with the loculi. Apex truncate, base with fruit stalk scar. Texture hard, on opening shell, 3 loculi in shell, each containing 1 seed. Seed slightly elliptic to oblong ovate shape, 5-7 mm in diameter; externally yellowish-brown to brown. Testa thin and brittle, endotesta white, membranous; kernels yellowishwhite, oily. Odour slight (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Pericarp: Exocarp consists of 1 layer cells, flattened to irregular in shape, multi-stellate hairs occasionally found. Mesocarp consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, scattered with stone cells, clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregates and vascular bundle occasionally found; the middle part consisting of cycle band of stone cells, about 4 layers; several layers of parenchymatous cells in the inner part. Endocarp consists of 3-5 layers sclerendrymatous cells [Fig. 2(i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 38, 1953, 253])\n\nSeed: Testa cells flattened, thin, boundaries indistinct, degenerated. Endotesta membranous consisting of 1 layer of cells. Endosperm surrounds the cotyledon, cells oblong or spherical. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, scattered [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour brownish-yellow. Mesocarp cells yellowish-brown, boundaries indistinct, shrunken. Exocarp cell reddish-brown, flattened, walls relatively thickened. Stone cells oblong numerous, 150-232 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Endocarp cell walls flattened, striated and thickened, containing beaded holes. Clusters of calcium oxalate 50-175 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, in rosette aggregate; polychromatic under polarized microscope. Fibres yellowish-brown, 25-90 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Multi-stellate hair occasionally found, star-shaped. Testa cells in groups, reddish-brown to yellowish-brown, subsquare to polygonal. Endosperm cells containing aleurone grains and oil droplets, intercellular layer distinct (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 44, 1967, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 402, 1737, 2121])"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Mesocarp containing cluster of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Mesocarp containing cluster of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Multi-stellate hair 2. Exocarp 3. Stone cell 4. Cluster of calcium oxalate 5. Mesocarp\n\n6. Vascular bundle 7. Band of stone cells 8. Parenchyma 9. Endocarp\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 46, 1961, 250])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[239, 390, 1748, 2127])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Testa 2. Endotesta 3. Cluster of calcium oxalate 4. Endosperm 5. Cotyledon\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[632, 42, 772, 114])\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1722, 48, 1852, 97])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n<table><tr><td>1a</td><td>2a</td><td>3a</td></tr><tr><td>4a</td><td>5a</td><td>5b</td></tr><tr><td>6a</td><td>6b</td><td>7a</td></tr><tr><td>8a</td><td>9a</td><td>10a</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Mesocarp cells 2. Exocarp cells 3. Stone cell 4. Endocarp cells\n\n5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Fibre 7. Multi-stellate hair\n\n8. Testa cells 9. Endosperm cells\n\n10. Endosperm cells containing aleurone grains ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) and oil droplets ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 39, 1945, 245])"},{"name":"13","text":"Crotonoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of crotonoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 30% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25% , v/v), water, methanol and n-butanol (0.5:1:4:4, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"13","text":"Remove the seed coat and pulverise the kernels to powder. Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered kernel sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (30%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately crotonoside standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Expose the plate to iodine vapour in a chamber for about 5-10 min. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n苦木 Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[371, 368, 801, 753])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of crotonoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 882, 795, 1721])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of kernel of Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed) extract observed under UV light (254 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Crotonoside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of crotonoside (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 39, 1944, 246])"},{"name":"13","text":"Crotonoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of crotonoside CRS and dissolve in 100 mL water."},{"name":"13","text":"Remove the seed coat and pulverise the kernels to powder. Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered kernel sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 25 mL of water. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the extract to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with water. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (215 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with alkyl reversed-phase bonded silica gel with diisopropyl side chain (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>100→95</td><td>0→5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-30</td><td>95→65</td><td>5→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of crotonoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of crotonoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of crotonoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from crotonoside peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject crotonoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of crotonoside peak in the chromatogram of crotonoside Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify crotonoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of crotonoside Std-FP. The retention times of crotonoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,crotonoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(magnoflorine)</td><td>2.77</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.92</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[329, 1569, 1818, 2199])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of kernel of Crotonis Fructus (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/crotonis-fructus-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Crotonis Fructus unprocessed","la":"Crotonis Fructus unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/curculiginis-rhizoma-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Curculiginis Rhizoma v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[27, 5, 1928, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[27, 5, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Curculiginis Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 仙茅\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xianmao"},{"name":"11","text":"Curculiginis Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Curculigo orchioides Gaertn. (Amaryllidaceae). The rhizome is collected in autumn or winter, removed the caudex and rootlets, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Curculiginis Rhizoma."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical, slightly curved, 1.7-9.7 cm long, 2-12 mm in diameter. Externally brown to blackish brown, coarse, with pitted scars of rootlets and transversal wrinkles. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken; fracture uneven, greyish-white to brown and blackish-brown in the centre. Odour slight and aromatic; taste bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex broad, with root-trace vascular bundles occasionally scattered. Mucilage cells numerous, scattered in parenchyma, subrounded, containing raphides of calcium oxalate. Endodermis distinct. Stele vascular bundles amphivasal or collateral, mostly scattered, near pericycle, gradually decreased inwards (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Raphides of calcium oxalate, numerous, scattered or in bundles, 43-188 $ \\mu $m long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Simple starch granule subrounded, 1-90 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum indistinct, compound granules composed of 2-5 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Mucilage cells numerous, subrounded or elliptic, 56-399 $ \\mu $m in diameter, filled with raphides of calcium oxalate; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells subpolygonal, cell wall thickened (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[23, 15, 1975, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Curculigoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of curculigoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and formic acid (8:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.2 g of potassium ferricyanide and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSolution B\n\nWeigh 0.2 g of ferric chloride and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent\n\nMix 5 mL of Solution A and 5 mL of Solution B to a 50-mL conical flask."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately curculigoside standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of curculigoside.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 1, 1930, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 402, 1741, 2039])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Curculiginis Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Mucilage cell containing raphides of calcium oxalate D. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Mucilage cell 4. Root-trace vascular bundle 5. Endodermis 6. Vascular bundle\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1891, 45, 1984, 89])\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 438, 1713, 2013])"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Scattered raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate in bundles 3. Starch granules\n\n4. Mucilage cell 5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 3, 1929, 260])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[381, 368, 973, 809])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of curculigoside\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Curculigoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of curculigoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 40 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue for two times each with 20 mL of methanol. Combine the solutions and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of water. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min.\n\nProgramme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 4, 1978, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.05% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>4-13</td><td>96-87</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>13-17</td><td>87-83</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>17-20</td><td>83-80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>20-30</td><td>80-70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of curculigoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of curculigoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of curculigoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from curculigoside peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject curculigoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of curculigoside peak in the chromatogram of curculigoside Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify curculigoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of curculigoside Std-FP. The retention times of curculigoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Curculiginis Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Curculiginis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.31</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,curculigoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 364, 1788, 1004])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Curculiginis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 14, 1976, 265])"},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 22.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/curculiginis-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Curculiginis Rhizoma","la":"Curculiginis Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/curcumae-longae-rhizoma-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Curcumae Longae Rhizoma v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Curcumae Longae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 薑黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jianghuang"},{"name":"13","text":"Curcumae Longae Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Curcuma longa L. (Zingiberaceae). The rhizome is collected in winter when the aerial part withered, removed the rootlets, washed clean, boiled or steamed thoroughly, then dried under the sun to obtain Curcumae Longae Rhizoma."},{"name":"13","text":"Irregularly ovoid, cylindrical or fusiform, curved, sometimes slightly branched into a Y-shape, 1.1- 10.3 cm long, 5-30 mm in diameter. Externally dark yellow to yellowish-brown, rough, with wrinkled striations, distinct cyclic nodes, and rounded scars of root branches and rootlets. Texture hard, fractured brownish-yellow to golden yellow, cuticular, with waxy lustre, endodermis ring distinct, scattered with dotted vascular bundles. Odour characteristic and aromatic; taste slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Epidermal cells flattened and thin-walled. Cork composed of 6-8 layers of cells, flattened and thin-walled, regularly arranged. A few leaf-trace vascular bundles scattered in cortex broad. Endodermis distinct. Stele broad, collateral vascular bundles mostly scattered near pericycle, gradually decreased inwards (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Yellowish-brown or reddish-brown. Starch gelatinous masses numerous, greyish-white, subround, polygonal or irregular in shape. Simple starch granules long elliptical or long ovate with angustate end or prominent beak, 5-65 $ \\mu $m long, hilum usually eccentric at the angustate\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 7, 1984, 262])\n\nend; black, off-centre cruciate shape under the polarized microscope. Oil cells elliptical or rounded-ovate, relatively large, 15-131.5 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, full of yellow or orange oily masses. Vessels mainly spiral and scalariform, 2.2-77.7 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Prisms of calcium oxalate in a small number, subsquare or rod-shaped; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Nonglandular hairs yellow to dark yellow, unicellular; usually broken, with pointed tip. Cork cells pale yellow, wall thin, frequently overlapping (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"13","text":"Bisdesmethoxycurcumin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of bisdesmethoxycurcumin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nCurcumin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of curcumin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nDesmethoxycurcumin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of desmethoxycurcumin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethanol and formic acid (15:0.4:0.3, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately bisdesmethoxycurcumin standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), curcumin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), desmethoxycurcumin standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[22, 3, 1928, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 423, 1742, 1974])"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Remnants of epidermis 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Leaf-trace bundle 5. Endodermis 6. Stele\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 14, 1984, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[394, 435, 1760, 2110])"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Starch gelatinous masses 2. Starch granules 3. Oil cell 4. Spiral vessel 5. Scalariform vessel\n\n6. Prisms of calcium oxalate 7. Non-glandular hair 8. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 3, 1926, 262])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of bisdesmethoxycurcumin, curcumin and desmethoxycurcumin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[457, 583, 1192, 792])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[461, 885, 1190, 1152])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[459, 1232, 1190, 1495])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) bisdesmethoxycurcumin (ii) curcumin and (iii) desmethoxycurcumin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Bisdesmethoxycurcumin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (52 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.3 mg of bisdesmethoxycurcumin CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nCurcumin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of curcumin CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nDesmethoxycurcumin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (52 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.3 mg of desmethoxycurcumin CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[13, 6, 1977, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>44</td><td>56</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-40</td><td>44→60</td><td>56→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-50</td><td>60→80</td><td>40→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bisdesmethoxycurcumin Std-FP, curcumin Std-FP and desmethoxycurcumin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of bisdesmethoxycurcumin, curcumin and desmethoxycurcumin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of bisdesmethoxycurcumin, curcumin and desmethoxycurcumin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from bisdesmethoxycurcumin, curcumin and desmethoxycurcumin peaks should not be less than 11000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject bisdesmethoxycurcumin Std-FP, curcumin Std-FP, desmethoxycurcumin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of bisdesmethoxycurcumin, curcumin and desmethoxycurcumin peaks in the chromatograms of bisdesmethoxycurcumin Std-FP, curcumin Std-FP, desmethoxycurcumin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify bisdesmethoxycurcumin, curcumin and desmethoxycurcumin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of bisdesmethoxycurcumin Std-FP, curcumin Std-FP and desmethoxycurcumin Std-FP. The retention times of bisdesmethoxycurcumin, curcumin and desmethoxycurcumin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 4, 1928, 260])\n\nthan 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Curcumae Longae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Curcumae Longae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(bisdesmethoxycurcumin)</td><td>0.83</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(desmethoxycurcumin)</td><td>0.91</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,curcumin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.43</td><td>$\\pm$0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1145, 1791, 1780])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Curcumae Longae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[29, 14, 1979, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 16.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/curcumae-longae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Curcumae Longae Rhizoma","la":"Curcumae Longae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/curcumae-radix-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Curcumae Radix v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[15, 658, 915, 1336])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling A. Root tubers B. Magnified transverse section of root tubers\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1280, 678, 1868, 1334])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[174, 1724, 935, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang A. Root tubers B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1031, 1703, 1935, 2403])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iv) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma longa L.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[22, 8, 1901, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"25","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[15, 658, 915, 1336])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling A. Root tubers B. Magnified transverse section of root tubers\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1280, 678, 1868, 1334])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[174, 1724, 935, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang A. Root tubers B. Magnified transverse section of root\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1031, 1703, 1935, 2403])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iv) A photograph of the dried root tubers of Curcuma longa L.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[22, 8, 1901, 253])"},{"name":"25","text":"Official Name: Curcumae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 鬱金\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yujin"},{"name":"25","text":"Curcumae Radix is the dried root tuber of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling, Curcuma longa L., Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang, or Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. (Zingiberaceae). The root tuber derived from the former two are known as \"Wenyujin\" and \"Huangsiyujin\", respectively, and the root tuber derived from the others are known as \"Guiyujin\" or \"Lusiyujin\", respectively, according to differences in the appearance. The root tuber is collected in winter when stem and leaves wither, soil and rootlets removed, boiled thoroughly, then dried under the sun or baked at $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to obtain Curcumae Radix."},{"name":"25","text":"Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling: Root tuber oblong or ovoid, slightly compressed or curved, both ends taper, 2.1-8.3 cm long, 6-19 mm in diameter. Externally pale brown or greyishbrown, with irregular longitudinal wrinkles, pale colour at the protuberant parts. Texture hard and compact; fracture greyish-brown and corneous; endodermis ring distinct. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nCurcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang: Root tuber long conical or oblong, 2.1-7.6 cm long, 7-23 mm in diameter. Externally pale brown or reddish-brown, with sparse and shallow wrinkles or relatively tough reticulate wrinkles. Texture hard and compact; fracture greyish-brown or brown and corneous; endodermis ring distinct. Odour slight; taste slightly pungent and bitter [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\nCurcuma phaeocaulis Val.: Root tuber long ellipsoid, relatively stout, 1.1-3.8 cm long, 7-15 mm in diameter. Externally grey or greyish-black, with wrinkles. Texture hard and compact; fracture brown or greyish-black, semi-corneous; endodermis ring distinct. Odour slight; taste bland [Fig. 1 (iii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 11, 1969, 259])"},{"name":"25","text":"Root tuber:\n\nCurcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling: Epidermal cells sometimes remain, the outer walls slightly thickened. Velamen narrow, consisting of 4-9 layers of cells with slightly sinuous and thin walls, arranged regularly. Cortex occupies about 1/2 of root. Oil cells rarely visible. Endodermis distinct. In stele, phloem bundles and xylem bundles each 35-52, arranged alternatively; each phloem bundle with 2-4 vessels, xylem fibres slightly lignified; vessels polygonal, with thin wall. Gelatinized starch granules in parenchymatous cells visible [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nCurcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang: Velamen consists of 3-6 layers of cells, cell walls occasionally thickened, the inner side of velamen showing 1-2 layers of sclerenchymatous cells arranged in a ring, with distinct striation. In stele, phloem bundles and xylem bundles each 37-58, arranged alternatively; each phloem bundle with 2-3 subrounded vessels [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nCurcuma phaeocaulis Val.: Velamen consists of 3-6 layers of cells with thin walls. In stele, phloem bundles (shriveled) and xylem bundles each 42-62, arranged alternatively; each phloem bundle with 1 or 2-3 flattened vessels [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"25","text":"Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling: Colour grey. Gelatinized starch granules scattered singly or in clumps, greyish-white, rounded, oblong or irregular polyhedral. Oil cells rare, subrounded or oblong, containing pale yellow or brownish-yellow oil droplets or secretions. Velamen cells greyish-brown, walls lignified and thickened. Vessels mainly spiral, scalariform or reticulate, 25-100 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nCurcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang: Colour greyish-brown. Gelatinized starch granules scattered singly or in clumps, greyish-white, rounded-ovate, oblong or subrounded polyhedral. Oil cells rare, subrounded or oblong, containing brownish-red oil droplets or secretions. Velamen cells greyish-brown, consisting of several layers of cells, frequently overlapping. Vessels mainly spiral, scalariform or reticulate, up to 170 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\nCurcuma phaeocaulis Val.: Colour brown. Gelatinized starch granules scattered singly or in clumps, greyish-white, rounded-ovate, oblong or subrounded-polyhedral. Oil cells rare, elongated-subrounded, subrounded or oblong, containing brownish-red oil droplets or secretions. Velamen cells greyish-brown, consisting of several layers of cells, frequently overlapping. Vessels mainly spiral, scalariform or reticulate, 25-150 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 3 (iii)].\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[235, 451, 1717, 2082])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root tuber of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Velamen 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Xylem 6. Phloem\n\n7. Pith 8. Gelatinized starch granules\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 459, 1681, 2065])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root tuber of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Velamen 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Xylem 6. Phloem\n\n7. Pith 8. Gelatinized starch granules\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[235, 459, 1702, 2069])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root tuber of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Velamen 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Pith\n\n8. Gelatinized starch granules\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n韂金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1427, 101, 1592, 162])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[236, 441, 1749, 2119])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root tuber of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Parenchymatous cells contain gelatinized starch granules 2. Oil cell\n\n3. Velamen cells 4. Vessels (4-1 scalariform vessel, 4-2 spiral vessels)\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus 水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 467, 943, 901])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1042, 466, 1604, 903])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[382, 1022, 947, 1467])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1039, 1025, 1603, 1463])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 1526, 943, 1970])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1044, 1527, 1603, 1970])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root tuber of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Parenchymatous cells contain gelatinized starch granules 2. Oil cell\n\n3. Velamen cells 4. Vessels (4-1 scalariform vessel, 4-2 spiral vessel)\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[243, 657, 1671, 2133])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (iii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root tuber of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Parenchymatous cells contain gelatinized starch granules 2. Oil cell\n\n3. Velamen cells 4. Vessels\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 12, 1922, 247])"},{"name":"25","text":"Germacrone standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of germacrone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"25","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (18:2, v/v)."},{"name":"25","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 150-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (95%). Filter the solution."},{"name":"25","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately germacrone standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5-8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[380, 1876, 712, 2105])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of germacrone\n\n</div>\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus\n\n鬆金 Curcumae Radix\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[383, 378, 1057, 1128])\n\n1 2(i) 2(ii) 2(iii)\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Curcumae Radix extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Germacrone standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried root tuber of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling\n\n(ii) dried root tuber of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang\n\n(iii) dried root tuber of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of germacrone (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"25","text":"Germacrone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of germacrone CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"25","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times each with 15 mL of methanol (50%). Wash the residue with methanol (50%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"25","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>51</td><td>49</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>51→20</td><td>49→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"25","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of germacrone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of germacrone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of germacrone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from germacrone peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"25","text":"Separately inject germacrone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of germacrone peak in the chromatogram of germacrone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify germacrone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of germacrone Std-FP. The retention times of germacrone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried root tuber of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling extract, Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang extract and Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[552, 97, 715, 156])\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[1073, 157, 1234, 212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried root tuber of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling extract, Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang extract and Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.62</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.76</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, germacrone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[379, 933, 1722, 1392])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root tuber of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[381, 1573, 1711, 2058])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root tuber of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang extract\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡寅連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[378, 371, 1733, 830])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root tuber of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"25","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"25","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"25","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[38, 14, 1962, 262])"},{"name":"25","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"25","text":"Germacrone standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of germacrone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGermacrone standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the germacrone Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 1,2,5,10,20 mg/L for germacrone."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/curcumae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Curcumae Radix","la":"Curcumae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cuscutae-semen-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cuscutae Semen v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1(ii) A photograph of dried ripe seeds of Cuscuta chinensis Lam.\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified seeds"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"Figure 1(ii) A photograph of dried ripe seeds of Cuscuta chinensis Lam.\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified seeds"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Cuscutae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 菟絲子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tusizi"},{"name":"15","text":"Cuscutae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Cuscuta australis R. Br. or Cuscuta chinensis Lam. (Convolvulaceae). The plant is collected in autumn when the fruit is ripe, dried under the sun, then threshed to gather the seeds and foreign matter removed to obtain Cuscutae Semen."},{"name":"15","text":"Subspherical, 0.9-1.5 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown, yellowish-brown or reddish-brown rough, with numerous fine protuberant dots; the hilum linear or oblate. Texture hard and compact. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, subsquare to subrectangular, side wall thickened. Palisade tissue consists of 2 layers elongated cells; outer palisade cells shorter than the inner ones; light line locates at the upper half of the inner palisade cells. A narrow parenchyma, made up of mostly shrunken cells, lies underneath the inner layer of palisade cells. Endosperm cells polygonal to subrounded, with thickened cell wall. Cotyledons twisted, several fragments of cotyledon can be seen. Cells of cotyledon subsquare to subrounded, containing aleurone grains [Fig. 2 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[54, 7, 1959, 263])"},{"name":"15","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to dark brown. Testa consists of epidermal cells and palisade cells. Epidermal cells of testa polygonal in surface view, walls thickened at corner; subsquare to subrectangular in lateral view, 15-55 $ \\mu $m wide, 26-71 $ \\mu $m high, side walls thickened. Palisade cells of testa 2 layers in lateral view, outer palisade cells shorter than inner ones; a light line presents at upper part of inner palisade cells; cells polygonal in surface view, walls thickened, shrunken. Endosperm cells polygonal or subrounded, walls thickened, containing aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells subrounded to subsquare, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets [Fig. 3 (i) and (ii)].\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[265, 471, 971, 1167])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[322, 1200, 967, 1665])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[322, 1665, 968, 1985])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1012, 503, 1693, 1963])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Cuscuta australis R. Br.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Outer palisade cells 3. Light line 4. Inner palisade cells\n\n5. Parenchyma 6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1235, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1426, 102, 1593, 161])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 442, 1742, 2080])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Cuscuta chinensis Lam.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Outer palisade cells 3. Light line 4. Inner palisade cells\n\n5. Parenchyma 6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\n芫花\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus 水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[329, 460, 1011, 988])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1046, 462, 1655, 987])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[329, 1028, 1010, 1493])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1046, 1027, 1654, 1493])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[329, 1526, 1011, 1987])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1051, 1524, 1655, 1988])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Cuscuta australis R. Br. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Testa consists of epidermal cell and palisade cells (in lateral view)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa (2-1 in surface view, 2-2 in lateral view)\n\n3. Palisade cells of testa (in surface view) 4. Endosperm cells contain aleurone grains\n\n5. Cotyledon cells contain aleurone grains and oil droplets\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCuscutae Semen\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[305, 465, 988, 997])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1020, 469, 1681, 999])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[305, 1028, 996, 1450])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1024, 1026, 1680, 1447])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[307, 1479, 997, 1988])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1018, 1475, 1682, 1991])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Cuscuta chinensis Lam. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Testa consists of epidermal cell and palisade cells (in lateral view)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of testa (2-1 in surface view, 2-2 in lateral view)\n\n3. Palisade cells of testa (in surface view) 4. Endosperm cells contain aleurone grains\n\n5. Cotyledon cells contain aleurone grains and oil droplets"},{"name":"15","text":"Hyperin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hyperin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (25:2:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 40 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hyperin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[552, 97, 717, 156])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nCuscutae Semen\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[434, 369, 930, 887])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[433, 960, 973, 1259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hyperin and (ii) kaempferol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[384, 1374, 853, 2224])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 (i) 2 (ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cuscutae Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Hyperin standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried ripe seed of Cuscuta australis R. Br.\n\n(ii) dried ripe seed of Cuscuta chinensis Lam.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[24, 9, 1925, 249])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of hyperin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Hyperin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of hyperin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (360 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>90→81</td><td>10→19</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>81</td><td>19</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>81→45</td><td>19→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hyperin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of hyperin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of hyperin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from hyperin peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[552, 99, 717, 155])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1072, 157, 1236, 213])"},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject hyperin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of hyperin peak in the chromatogram of hyperin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hyperin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of hyperin Std-FP. The retention times of hyperin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cuscutae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cuscutae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, hyperin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.43</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3(kaempferol)</td><td>2.02</td><td>±0.09</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[380, 1487, 1767, 1991])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Cuscuta australis R. Br. extract\n\n</div>\n\nGenkwa Flos芫花\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nCuscutae Semen\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡寅連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[384, 369, 1763, 864])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Cuscuta chinensis Lam. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 8.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[39, 13, 1962, 262])"},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cuscutae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cuscutae Semen","la":"Cuscutae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cyathulae-radix-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyathulae Radix v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Official Name: Cyathulae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 川牛膝\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chuanniuxi"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Cyathulae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 川牛膝\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chuanniuxi"},{"name":"11","text":"Cyathulae Radix is the dried root of Cyathula officinalis Kuan (Amaranthaceae). The root is collected in winter, root stock, rootlets and soil removed, baked or dried under the sun to half-dryness, piled up, then baked or dried under the sun thoroughly to obtain Cyathulae Radix."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical, straight or slightly curved, the upper part broad, tapering downward towards the distal end, occasionally branched, 14-54 cm long, 5-15 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown, with slightly twisted and fine longitudinal wrinkles, transverse lenticel-like protuberances and sparsely distributed rootlet scars. Texture tenacious, uneasily broken; fracture pale yellow to brownish-yellow; vascular bundles many scattered and spotted, arranged in several whorls, yellowish-white; those on the outer part small, the one in the centre relatively large. Odour slight; taste sweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex relatively narrow, parenchymatous cells elongated and arranged in rows tangentially. Vascular bundles arranged in several interrupted whorls, relatively small in the outermost whorl, sometimes having only 1 to several vessels. Cambium nearly in a ring in the outermost whorl. Xylem mainly consists of vessels and small xylem fibres; central xylem aggregated into 2 or more groups. Parenchymatous cells contain microcrystals of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[51, 8, 1960, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-brown to brown. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells, triangular, arrow-pointed, subsquare or irregular, 1-12 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres mostly in bundles, strip-shaped, with curved and tapered end, 6-49 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, walls slightly thickened, pits oblique or V-shaped, bordered pits observed, pit canals distinct with different width; pale blue or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 12-73 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Cork cells subsquare or subrectangular (Fig. 3).\n\nAllii Tuberosi Semen\n\n韮莱子\n\nGenkwa Flos\n\n芫花\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[247, 430, 1740, 2036])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cyathulae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cambium 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCyathulae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[352, 557, 1625, 2016])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cyathulae Radix\n\n1. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 2. Fibres 3. Bordered-pitted vessels 4. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"11","text":"Cyasterone standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.05 mg of cyasterone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, cyclohexane, methanol and glacial acetic acid (4:3:1.5:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately cyasterone standard solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 4 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nBuddlejae Flos密蒙花\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n豬牙皂\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nCyathulae Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[374, 369, 865, 896])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of cyasterone\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 1059, 894, 1900])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cyathulae Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cyasterone standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of cyasterone (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[23, 10, 1926, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Cyasterone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of cyasterone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (80%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (246 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times1 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (2.6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 2 7^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.5 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>90</td><td>10</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-15</td><td>90→66</td><td>10→34</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-48</td><td>66</td><td>34</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>48-60</td><td>66→55</td><td>34→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of cyasterone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of cyasterone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of cyasterone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from cyasterone peak should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject cyasterone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of cyasterone peak in the chromatogram of cyasterone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify cyasterone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of cyasterone Std-FP. The retention times of cyasterone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cyathulae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cyathulae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.97</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,cyasterone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.03</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.24</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[361, 1613, 1724, 2259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cyathulae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 15, 1926, 254])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 65.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 54.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cyathulae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyathulae Radix","la":"Cyathulae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cynanchi-paniculati-radix-et-rhizoma-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[517, 851, 1570, 1936])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma A. Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified transverse section of rhizome C. Magnified transverse section of root"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[517, 851, 1570, 1936])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma A. Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified transverse section of rhizome C. Magnified transverse section of root"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 徐長卿\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xuchangqing"},{"name":"12","text":"Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma is the dried root and rhizome of Cynanchum paniculatum (Bge.) Kitag. (Asclepiadaceae). The root and rhizome is collected in autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried in a shaded area to obtain Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Roots slender-cylindrical, curved, 4-26 cm long, 0.5-2 mm in diameter. Externally pale yellowish-white to pale brownish-yellow or brown; with fine longitudinal wrinkles and fibrous rootlets. Rhizomes irregularly cylindrical, knotty, 0.5-3 cm long, 0.5-1.5 mm in diameter. Some of the rhizomes with remnants of stem at the apex, stem slender-cylindrical, fracture hollow; nodes of rhizomes surrounded by numerous roots. Texture fragile, easily broken. Fracture starchy, bark whitish to yellowish-white, cambium ring pale brown, wood small. Odour aromatic; taste slightly pungent and cool (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Root: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, outer cell walls thickened. Cortex broad, consisting of parenchymatous cells, comprising about 2/3 of the root, containing clusters of calcium oxalate and numerous of starch granules. Endodermis scatterd with distinct casparian dots. Phloem narrow. Cambium indistinct. Xylem broad, cells lignified [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nRhizome: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle. Cortex broad, consisting of parenchymatous cells, containing clusters of calcium oxalate and numerous of starch granules. Vascular bundles amphiphloic siphonostele; phloem narrow, cells always compressed; cambium consists of 1-2 layers of cells; xylem broad, cells lignified. Pith always hollow [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[63, 8, 1962, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale greyish-brown to pale brown. Epidermal cells polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls slightly sinuous, lignified subsquare cells distribute among epidermal cells; subrectangular in lateral view, some with thickened fine stripes in radial walls. Parenchymatous cells mostly fragmented, subrounded to polygonal, containing numerous starch granules; pale white under the polarized microscope. Fibres colourless, slender. Vessels bordered-pitted, mostly in bundles. Clusters of calcium oxalate 10-55 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Starch granules numerous, simple starch granules subrounded or subrectangular, 3-11 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum dotted or indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-6 units (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 414, 1748, 2103])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n6. Clusters of calcium oxalate 7. Starch granules\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[552, 98, 716, 154])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1233, 211])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 101, 1590, 163])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[245, 423, 948, 1063])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[347, 1159, 935, 1525])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[347, 1630, 935, 1984])\n\n20 μm\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[990, 441, 1702, 2062])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem\n\n6. Pith 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate 8. Starch granules\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黄連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[314, 469, 939, 966])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[979, 473, 1658, 955])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[316, 994, 977, 1501])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1001, 991, 1659, 1500])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[319, 1522, 979, 1768])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[999, 1520, 1658, 1760])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[316, 1788, 1658, 2012])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Epidermal cells (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view)\n\n2. Parenchymatous cells with starch granules 3. Fibres 4. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n5. Clusters of calcium oxalate 6. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[552, 97, 714, 155])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1426, 101, 1595, 165])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1073, 157, 1232, 210])"},{"name":"12","text":"Paeonol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of paeonol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of ferric trichloride and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately paeonol standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 1677, 725, 2003])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of paeonol\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 372, 872, 1179])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Paeonol standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of paeonol (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Paeonol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of paeonol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[551, 98, 716, 156])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1427, 101, 1590, 162])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>85</td><td>15</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>85→75</td><td>15→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>75→30</td><td>25→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>60-90</td><td>30→0</td><td>70→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of paeonol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of paeonol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of paeonol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from paeonol peak should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject paeonol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of paeonol peak in the chromatogram of paeonol Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify paeonol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of paeonol Std-FP. The retention times of paeonol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 12, 1897, 255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,paeonol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[382, 948, 1838, 1422])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 15, 1963, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 16.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 16.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cynanchi-paniculati-radix-et-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma","la":"Cynanchi Paniculati Radix et Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cynomorii-herba-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cynomorii Herba v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[579, 854, 1529, 1868])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[579, 854, 1529, 1868])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Cynomorii Herba\n\nChinese Name: 鎖陽\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Suoyang"},{"name":"11","text":"Cynomorii Herba is the dried fleshy stem of Cynomorium songaricum Rupr. (Cynomoriaceae). The fleshy stem is collected in spring, inflorescence removed, cut into sections, then dried under the sun to obtain Cynomorii Herba."},{"name":"11","text":"Compressed-cylindrical, slightly curved, varying in length, up to 17 cm long, 15-50 mm in diameter. Externally brown, rough, with distinct longitudinal furrows and irregular pits, sometimes with remnants of triangular dark brown scales. Texture hard and heavy in weight, uneasily broken. Fracture pale brown to brown, with triangular yellow vascular bundles. Odour slight; taste sweet and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork cells mostly decadent and deciduous, residual of cork cells occasionally found. Cortex consists of several layers of cells. Vascular bundles abundant, collateral, fan-shaped or semirounded, scattered in stele irregularly, small in the outermost layer, and relatively large inward (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[60, 7, 1969, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour brown. Starch granules fairly abundant, mainly simple starch granules, subrounded, hemispherical or polygonal, 3-30 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum pointed, cleft-like, stellate or V-shaped; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-3 units. Vessels mainly reticulate and sometimes spiral, 7-35 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Cork cells brown, subsquare, rectangular or polygonal, walls slightly thickened (Fig. 3).\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[246, 509, 1732, 2052])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cynomorii Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Xylem 4. Phloem\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nCynomorii Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[399, 653, 992, 1263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1007, 656, 1583, 1260])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[399, 1279, 989, 1851])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1009, 1278, 1581, 1848])\n\n50μm\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cynomorii Herba\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Vessels 3. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"11","text":"L-Proline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of L-proline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of water."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-propanol, water, glacial acetic acid and ethanol (4:2:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1 g of ninhydrin and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of water. Allow to stand for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately L-proline standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[552, 97, 717, 156])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 157, 1237, 212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[385, 371, 913, 686])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 756, 661, 893])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) (+)-catechin and (ii) L-proline\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 1055, 936, 1701])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Cynomorii Herba extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. L-Proline standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of L-proline (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 11, 1927, 251])"},{"name":"11","text":"(+)-Catechin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of (+)-catechin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (60%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (60%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (205 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.4% phosphoric acid and acetonitrile (90:10, v/v). The elution time is about 50 min."},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of (+)-catechin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of (+)-catechin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of (+)-catechin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from (+)-catechin peak should not be less than 4000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject (+)-catechin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of (+)-catechin peak in the chromatogram of (+)-catechin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify (+)-catechin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of (+)-catechin Std-FP. The retention times of (+)-catechin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[39, 7, 1960, 263])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cynomorii Herba extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Cynomorii Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.76</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3[marker，(+)-catechin]</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[383, 972, 1724, 1638])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Cynomorii Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1454, 138, 1617, 193])"},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 26.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 27.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cynomorii-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cynomorii Herba","la":"Cynomorii Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cyperi-rhizoma-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyperi Rhizoma v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[769, 856, 1226, 1218])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[591, 1237, 1509, 1880])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cyperi Rhizoma A. Cyperi Rhizoma B. Magnified rhizome C. Fracture of unsteamed rhizome D. Fracture of steamed rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1899, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[769, 856, 1226, 1218])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[591, 1237, 1509, 1880])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Cyperi Rhizoma A. Cyperi Rhizoma B. Magnified rhizome C. Fracture of unsteamed rhizome D. Fracture of steamed rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1899, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Cyperi Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 香附\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xiangfu"},{"name":"12","text":"Cyperi Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Cyperus rotundus L. (Cyperaceae). The rhizome is collected in autumn and fibrous roots are burnt off, dried under the sun; or boiled briefly or steamed thoroughly, then dried under the sun to obtain Cyperi Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Fusiform, some slightly curved, 1.1-3.7 cm long, 4-11 mm in diameter. Externally dark brown to blackish-brown, with longitudinal wrinkles and 4-13 slightly prominent annular nodes containing brown fibrous roots and remnants of root-scars. For those rhizomes with completely removed fibrous roots, annular nodes indistinct, outer surface relatively smoother. Texture hard. Fracture of steamed rhizomes yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, horny; fracture of unsteamed rhizomes white and starchy, with obvious endodermis ring, stele dark, with scattered dotted vascular bundles. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermal cells pale yellow to brownish-yellow, always accompanied by hypodermal fibre bundles. Hypodermis underneath epidermis, consisting of 2-3 layers of sclerenchymatous cells. Cortex consists of parenchymatous cells, numerous secretory cells and a few collateral vascular bundles. Collateral vascular bundles surrounded by endodermis. Endodermal cells subsquare to rectangular, arranged in a ring. Stele consists of numerous amphivasal vascular bundles, secretory cells and parenchymatous cells. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules in unsteamed rhizome or starch gelatinous masses in steamed rhizome (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale brown to brown. Hypodermal fibres in bundles, reddish-brown to dark brown, 5-25 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, wall thickened. Epidermal cells pale yellow to brownish-yellow, polygonal, 12-34 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Secretory cells subrounded, 20-72 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, containing pale yellowish-brown to reddish-brown secretion, with 4-10 adjacent cells radially arranged in a circle, mostly broken. Parenchymatous cells numerous, subrounded, containing starch granules in unsteamed rhizome or starch gelatinous masses in steamed rhizome. Hypodermal cells (sclerenchymatous cells) pale yellow, subsquare, subrounded or irregular, walls slightly thickened. Starch granules numerous, subrounded or ovoid to ellipsoid, 4-29 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Stone cells rare, reddish-brown, subsquare, subrounded or subpolygonal, wall thickened (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[335, 445, 952, 927])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[342, 962, 953, 1200])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[341, 1258, 751, 1604])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[342, 1661, 686, 1983])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[730, 1663, 988, 1983])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1068, 475, 1662, 1982])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Cyperi Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermal layer D. Secretory cell E. Vascular bundle (collateral type) F. Vascular bundle (amphivasal type)\n\n1. Hypodermal fibre bundles 2. Epidermis 3. Hypodermis 4. Cortex\n\n5. Secretory cell 6. Vascular bundle (collateral type) 7. Endodermis\n\n8. Vascular bundle (amphivasal type) 9. Stele\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\nCyperi Rhizoma Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[310, 453, 1169, 853])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1199, 456, 1670, 849])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[313, 877, 979, 1482])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1002, 875, 1670, 1483])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[318, 1515, 702, 1972])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[734, 1511, 1105, 1966])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1128, 1517, 1672, 1970])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Cyperi Rhizoma\n\n1. Hypodermal fibre bundle 2. Epidermal cells 3. Secretory cell\n\n4. Parenchymatous cells containing starch gelatinous masses\n\n5. Hypodermal cells (sclerenchymatous cells) 6. Starch granules 7. Stone cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 7, 1902, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\alpha $ -Cyperone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and glacial acetic acid (9:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.5 g of 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine and dissolve in 20 mL of sulphuric acid ( 50% , v/v ). Transfer the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Filter the solution."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately $ \\alpha $ -cyperone standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible (about 30 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone.\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\nCyperi Rhizoma Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 371, 841, 639])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\alpha $ -Cyperone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of n-hexane."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of n-hexane. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with n-hexane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5, 0.32 mm $ \\times $ 30 m) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about 0.25 $ \\mu $ m thick. The injection temperature is at $ 250^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 250^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 10:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>120→160</td><td>1</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[41, 4, 1910, 265])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from $ \\alpha $ -cyperone peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject $ \\alpha $ -cyperone Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone peak in the chromatogram of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify $ \\alpha $ -cyperone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone Std-FP. The retention times of $ \\alpha $ -cyperone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cyperi Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Cyperi Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(cyperene)</td><td>0.36</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(selinene)</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.95</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,α-cyperone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n續斷\n\nCyperi Rhizoma Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[312, 369, 1891, 989])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Cyperi Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 7, 1902, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 13.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/cyperi-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyperi Rhizoma","la":"Cyperi Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/daturae-flos-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Daturae Flos v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1, 644, 1984, 2062])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Daturae Flos\n\n</div>\n\nA. Daturae Flos B. Magnified image of dried flower C. Magnified image of inner view of dissected flower"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1, 644, 1984, 2062])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Daturae Flos\n\n</div>\n\nA. Daturae Flos B. Magnified image of dried flower C. Magnified image of inner view of dissected flower"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Daturae Flos\n\nChinese Name: 洋金花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yangjinhua"},{"name":"13","text":"Daturae Flos is the dried flower of Datura metel L. (Solanaceae). The flower is collected at the beginning of flowering period in March to November, then dried under the sun or at an ambient temperature to obtain Daturae Flos."},{"name":"13","text":"Usually crumpled and strip-shaped, 8-16 cm long. Calyx tubular, 2/5 in length of corolla, greyishgreen to greyish-yellow, apex 5-lobed, with 5 longitudinal veins at the base, surface slightly pubescent; corolla trumpet-shaped, pale yellow or yellowish-brown, apex slightly 5-lobed, lobes short acuminate, with 3 distinct longitudinal veins below the acumen, slightly sunken between two lobes; stamens 5, filaments adnate to the corolla tube, 3/4 in length of corolla; pistil 1, stigma stick-shaped. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale yellow. Pollen grains spherical to subspherical, 42-65 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with stripe-shaped sculptures on the exine, 3 germinal pores occasionally visible. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered singly in parenchymatous cells, 7-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Spiral vessels 14-35 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Non-glandular hairs commonly visible on the surface of corolla and calyx, composed of 1-5 cells, one cell may be collapsed, forming a dumbbell shape. Glandular hairs not commonly seen (Fig. 2).\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\nDaturae Flos\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[247, 607, 1726, 2119])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Daturae Flos\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate 3. Spiral vessels\n\n4. Non-glandular hair 5. Glandular hairs\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[62, 33, 1924, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"L-Hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nScopolamine hydrobromide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of scopolamine hydrobromide CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), methanol and ethyl acetate (0.5:1:8.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth oxynitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Transfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid into a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 1 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 25 mL of a mixture of ethyl acetate and methanol (4:1, v/v). Sonicate (500 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution, scopolamine hydrobromide standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[434, 1106, 1073, 1784])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[433, 1844, 958, 2214])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate and (ii) scopolamine hydrobromide\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1267, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 375, 832, 1125])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Daturae Flos extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. L-Hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution\n\n2. Scopolamine hydrobromide standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"13","text":"0. 005 M Sodium 1-heptanesulphonate solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 g of sodium 1-heptanesulphonate and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\n0. 1 M Potassium dihydrogen phosphate solution\n\nWeigh 6.8 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\nSodium 1-heptanesulphonate - potassium dihydrogen phosphate buffer solution (pH 5)\n\nTransfer 500 mL of 0.005 M sodium 1-heptanesulphonate solution and 500 mL of 0.1 M potassium dihydrogen phosphate solution to a 1500-mL conical flask. Adjust the pH to 5 with 0.04 M potassium hydroxide solution."},{"name":"13","text":"L-Hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nScopolamine hydrobromide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of scopolamine hydrobromide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 2 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 20 mL of a mixture of ethyl acetate and methanol (4:1, v/v). Sonicate (500 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 150-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator in a water bath at about $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (216 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Sodium1-heptanesulphonate-potassium dihydrogenphosphate buffer solution(pH5)(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile:Methanol(10:1,v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>90→80</td><td>10→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-40</td><td>80→70</td><td>20→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP and scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks should not be less than 60000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[62, 27, 1925, 261])\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP, scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks in the chromatograms of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP, scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP and scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP. The retention times of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Daturae Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Daturae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, scopolamine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(L-hyoscyamine)</td><td>1.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.69</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[343, 379, 1803, 1013])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Daturae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 15.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 9.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[62, 33, 1924, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 26.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 15.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/daturae-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Daturae Flos","la":"Daturae Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/deinagkistrodon-agkistrodon-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Deinagkistrodon Agkistrodon v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[510, 984, 958, 1423])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[990, 996, 1469, 1422])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[667, 1538, 853, 1665])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[947, 1525, 1019, 1685])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1112, 1539, 1309, 1679])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[916, 1809, 1070, 1858])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[510, 984, 958, 1423])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[990, 996, 1469, 1422])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[667, 1538, 853, 1665])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[947, 1525, 1019, 1685])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1112, 1539, 1309, 1679])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[916, 1809, 1070, 1858])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>"},{"name":"7","text":"A. External surface of Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon)\n\nB. Internal surface of Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon)\n\nC. Magnified image of head with upturned snout\n\nD. Magnified image of fang\n\nE. Magnified image of dorsal scale\n\nF. Magnified image of tail with horny scale"},{"name":"7","text":"Official name: Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon)\n\nChinese name: 蕲蛇\n\nChinese phonetic name: Qishe"},{"name":"7","text":"Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon) is the dried body of Deinagkistrodon acutus (Güenther) [Agkistrodon acutus (Güenther)] (Viperidae). It is collected in summer or autumn. Cut open the abdomen, the viscera removed, washed clean, held open with bamboo pieces, coiled up in disc-shape with head at the centre, dried over a stove or under the sun, then removed the bamboo pieces to obtain Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon)."},{"name":"7","text":"It coiled up in a disc shape, 200-420 mm in diameter; body 5-14 cm wide, 0.7-1.3 m long, up to 2 m long. Head locates at the centre of the disc, slightly raised upward, triangular and flattened, with a prominent upturned snout, commonly known as \"Qiaobitou (upward nose)\". Each side of the upper jaw possesses 1 long tubular fang, sharp and hollow, often with 1-3 spare fangs behind. Dorsal scales possess strong keels and small tubercular warts. Back greyish-brown to dark brown, or brownish-black, back of head darker. Each side of the dorsum bears 16-22 V-shaped stripe patterns alternated blackish-brown with greyish-brown, both upper ends of the \"V\" stripe patterns usually unite at the mid-vertebral line, forming large squarish blotches, commonly known as \"Fangshengwen\", some of the \"V\" stripe patterns do not exactly contact with those on the other side but arranged alternatively. Abdomen opened, greyish-white or yellowish-white, scales relatively large, a row of large black subrounded spots extends along the latero-ventral junction at each side, commonly known as \"Lianzhuban (a patch like a string of beads)\". Inner wall of abdomen yellowish-white or greyish-yellow; spinous process of vertebrae relatively high, distinct; numerous ribs on both sides, mostly falcate, incline posteriorly. Tail tapers gradually, with a dark grey triangular horny scale at the end, commonly known as \"Fuzhijia (Buddha's nail)\". Odour stinky; taste slightly salty (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 33, 1950, 254])"},{"name":"7","text":"Colour pale yellow to yellowish-white. Keratinized scales nearly colourless or pale yellow, showing semi-circular or papillary protrusions laterally, subrounded, oval or polygonal in surface view, imbricate, 9-53 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, distributed with pale grey or pale brown fine granular matters. Epidermal cells nearly colourless or pale yellow, cell boundaries indistinct, dark brown pigment granules densely distributed, mostly aggregated irregularly, reticulated or branched. Striated muscle fibres relatively abundant, colourless, often broken, mostly flaky, edges relatively straight, intact fibres 15-289 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; with fine and dense stripes, straight or slightly wavy, some not clear. Bone fragments relatively abundant, nearly colourless or pale grey, in irregular pieces; bone lacunae subrounded or fusiform, mostly arranged in same direction, few irregularly arranged; bone canaliculi relatively small (Fig. 2).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[304, 685, 975, 1236])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1005, 683, 1681, 1238])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[303, 1274, 975, 1825])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1008, 1272, 1681, 1827])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon) (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Keratinized scale 2. Epidermal cells 3. Striated muscle fibres 4. Bone fragment\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 39, 1946, 255])"},{"name":"7","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XIV."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 10.0 mg of the powdered sample, sample duplicate and extraction positive control(s) into separate microtubes. Prepare blank tube(s) as extraction negative control(s). Perform DNA extraction using animal genomic DNA purification kit according to the manufacturer's manual.\n\nPrepare a polymerase chain reaction (PCR) reagent mixture including reaction buffer containing 1.5 mM $ \\mathrm{M g C l_{2}} $ (Taq buffer), 0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{M} $ of primer Deinagkistrodon-S (5'-GGC AAT TCA CTA CAC AGC CAA CAT CAA CT-3') and Deinagkistrodon-AS (5'-CCA TAG TCA GGT GGT TAG TGA TAC-3'), 0.2 mM deoxyribonucleotides mix (dNTP), and 0.5 U DNA polymerase. For sample, sample duplicate and extraction positive control(s), add 200 ng of template DNA into the PCR reagent. For extraction negative control(s) and PCR negative control(s), add the eluted solution from DNA extraction process and sterile water, respectively, to the PCR reagent. The PCR condition is shown in Table 1. Identify the PCR products by electrophoresis analysis shown in Table 2. Measure the size of DNA fragments by referring to the DNA size marker.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable1 Recommended PCR cycling conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>PCR system</td><td colspan=\"3\">PCR machine</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature ramp rate</td><td colspan=\"3\">2.2℃/second</td></tr><tr><td>Initial denaturation</td><td colspan=\"3\">95℃，5min</td></tr><tr><td>Number of cycles</td><td colspan=\"3\">30</td></tr><tr><td rowspan=\"4\">PCR profile of each cycle</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Time(min)</td><td>Remarks</td></tr><tr><td>95</td><td>0.5</td><td>Denaturation</td></tr><tr><td>63</td><td>0.5</td><td>Annealing</td></tr><tr><td>72</td><td>0.5</td><td>Extension</td></tr><tr><td>Final extension</td><td colspan=\"3\">72℃，5min</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 43, 1963, 257])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 Recommended electrophoresis and detection conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Separation unit</td><td>1.5% agarose gel with 0.001% SYBR dyes DNA gel stain</td></tr><tr><td>Loading volume</td><td>5μL</td></tr><tr><td>DNA size marker</td><td>100bp DNA ladder</td></tr><tr><td>Detector</td><td>UV transilluminator</td></tr><tr><td>Running voltage</td><td>80V</td></tr><tr><td>Running time</td><td>30min</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[388, 955, 1479, 1800])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Reference gel electropherogram of Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon) under the recommended PCR conditions and electrophoresis analysis conditions\n\n</div>\n\n1. 100 bp DNA ladder\n\n2. Extraction negative control\n\n3. Sample\n\n4. Sample duplicate\n\n5. Extraction positive control\n\n6. PCR negative control\n\nFor positive identification, the PCR products of sample, sample duplicate and extraction positive control(s) shall give a DNA band in between 300 and 400 bp under the recommended electrophoresis detection (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 40, 1950, 254])"},{"name":"7","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): The CMM shall meet the requirements for arsenic, cadmium and lead as specified in Appendix V. For mercury, Deinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon) should meet the specified limit of not more than 1.0 mg/kg, when the CMM will be processed as a decoction in the final consumption form; otherwise, the limit for mercury specified in Appendix V shall be applied.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 32.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"7","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 8.0%."},{"name":"7","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"7","text":"This CMM should be used after proper processing (such as decoction)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/deinagkistrodon-agkistrodon","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Deinagkistrodon Agkistrodon","la":"Deinagkistrodon Agkistrodon"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dendrobii-caulis-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dendrobii Caulis v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[366, 714, 1582, 2002])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dendrobii Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nA. Dendrobii Caulis B. Magnified image of node C. Magnified image of fracture\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1964, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[366, 714, 1582, 2002])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dendrobii Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nA. Dendrobii Caulis B. Magnified image of node C. Magnified image of fracture\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1964, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Dendrobii Caulis\n\nChinese name: 石斛\n\nChinese phonetic name: Shihu"},{"name":"12","text":"Dendrobii Caulis is the dried stem of cultivated Dendrobium nobile Lindl. (Orchidaceae). The stem is collected all year round, foreign matter removed, softened by dipping into boiling water or baking, then dried and rubbed by baking or under the sun until all leaf sheaths are removed and dried to obtain Dendrobii Caulis."},{"name":"12","text":"Flat-cylindrical or cylindrical, slightly zigzag bended, 2-10 mm in diameter, internode 1.8-7.5 cm long; surface golden yellow to dark yellow, lustrous, with deep longitudinal furrows and wrinkles; nodes distinct, brown, some with residual grey leaf sheaths. Light in weight and brittle, easily broken, fracture yellowish-white to greyish-yellow, loose, flat or slightly fibrous. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of flat cells, covered with thick cuticle, 2-3 layers of cells with slightly thickened wall located underneath the epidermis. Parenchyma scattered with numerous collateral vascular bundles, some cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate. Bundle sheath fibres several layers, located at the outer side of the vascular bundles, wall thick, lumen relatively small, with parenchymatous cells containing subrounded silica bodies outside the fibre bundles; always with several layers of fibres with relatively large lumen located on the inner side of vascular bundles (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 36, 1955, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brownish-yellow. Bundle sheath fibres mostly in bundles, wall thick, lumen relatively narrow, with sparse pits and pit canals, 10-65 $ \\mu $m in diameter, some surrounded by cells containing subrounded silica bodies of 4-21 $ \\mu $m in diameter; bright white or bright yellow under the polarized microscope. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered or present in parenchymatous cells, 12-122 $ \\mu $m long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells subpolygonal or long-polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls beaded, always accompanied by bright yellow cuticle. Fibres mostly in bundles, wall slightly thickened, lumen relatively large, with numerous pits and pit canals, pits dotted or slit-shaped, 9-42 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Vessels mainly scalariform and spiral vessels, 9-58 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 44, 1967, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 405, 1737, 2096])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Dendrobii Caulis A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Bundle sheath fibres and silica bodies D. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Parenchyma 3. Vascular bundle 4. Silica body\n\n5. Bundle sheath fibre 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Fibre 9. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n野老鸛草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[751, 145, 906, 186])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1691, 55, 1938, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[307, 534, 1684, 2007])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Bundle sheath fibres (silica body $ \\rightarrow $ ） 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n3. Epidermal cells 4. Fibres 5. Vessels (5-1 scalariform vessel, 5-2 spiral vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 45, 1966, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Dendrobine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dendrobine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), isopropanol and ethyl acetate (0.4:2:15, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the supernatant to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 4 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dendrobine standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Expose the plate to iodine vapour in a chamber for about 20 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 40, 1950, 249])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 369, 801, 925])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of dendrobine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 1058, 819, 1894])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Dendrobii Caulis extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Dendrobine standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of dendrobine (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[30, 44, 1966, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Dendrobine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of dendrobine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue with 10 mL of methanol. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the extract to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (DB-5 MS, $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{m} $) of which the internal wall is covered with phenyl arylene polymer in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. Helium is used as the carrier gas at $ 1. 3 \\mathrm{m L / m i n} $ . The injection temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 5:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-16</td><td>130→210</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>16-21</td><td>210</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>21-30</td><td>210→280</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>280</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dendrobine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dendrobine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dendrobine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dendrobine peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject dendrobine Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dendrobine peak in the chromatogram of dendrobine Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dendrobine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dendrobine Std-FP. The retention times of dendrobine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Dendrobii Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Dendrobii Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.97</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,dendrobine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.01</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[300, 1559, 1814, 2189])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Dendrobii Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dendrobii-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dendrobii Caulis","la":"Dendrobii Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dendrobii-officinalis-caulis-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[570, 982, 717, 1199])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[712, 966, 865, 1193])\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[901, 908, 1252, 1167])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1277, 1103, 1526, 1258])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[687, 1316, 1213, 1833])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1321, 1350, 1470, 1501])\n\nE 5mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis\n\nA. Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis (Tiepifengdou)\n\nB. Magnified image of stem (Tiepifengdou)\n\nC. Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis (Tiepishihu)\n\nD. Magnified image of node (Tiepishihu)\n\nE. Magnified image of fracture\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 15, 1873, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[570, 982, 717, 1199])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[712, 966, 865, 1193])\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[901, 908, 1252, 1167])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1277, 1103, 1526, 1258])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[687, 1316, 1213, 1833])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1321, 1350, 1470, 1501])\n\nE 5mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis\n\nA. Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis (Tiepifengdou)\n\nB. Magnified image of stem (Tiepifengdou)\n\nC. Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis (Tiepishihu)\n\nD. Magnified image of node (Tiepishihu)\n\nE. Magnified image of fracture\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 15, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 鐵皮石斛\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tiepishihu"},{"name":"11","text":"Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis is the dried stem of Dendrobium officinale Kimura et Migo $ ^{1} $ (Orchidaceae). The stem is collected from November to the following March, fibrous roots and foreign matter removed, the stem is twisted into a spiral while heating, then baked to dryness (known as Tiepifengdou); or cut into sections, then dried or baked at below $ 60^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (known as Tiepishihu)."},{"name":"11","text":"'Tiepifengdou' spiral or spring-like, usually with 2-6 spires, 5-13 mm in diameter. 'Tiepishihu' cylindrical, slightly bended, 1-4.5 mm in diameter; externally yellowish-green to dark yellow, with fine longitudinal wrinkles; nodes distinct, sometimes with greyish-white residual leaf sheaths. Texture compact and hard. Fracture even, greyish-white, slightly corneous. Odour slight; taste bitter and viscous when chewed (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, flattened, outer walls and lateral walls slightly thickened, covered with bright yellow cuticle. Parenchymatous cells polygonal, similar in size, scattered with numerous vascular bundles, the parenchymatous cells surrounding vascular bundles smaller in size. Mucilage cells scattered, containing raphides of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundles oblong, somewhat arranged in 4-5 rings. Bundle sheath fibres hat-shaped, consisting of 1-5 thick-walled fibre cells, arranged at the outer side of vascular bundles, some with parenchymatous cells containing subrounded silica bodies outside the fibre bundles (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 3, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-green. Bundle sheath fibres mostly arranged in bundles, 9-23 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls thick, lumens relatively narrow, some surrounded by cells contain subrounded silica bodies of 6-23 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; bright white or bright yellow under the polarized microscope. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered or present in mucilage cells or parenchymatous cells, 25-120 $ \\mu m $ long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells subpolygonal to elongated-polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls beaded. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, scalariform and spiral, 8-36 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 14, 1899, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 451, 1731, 2075])"},{"name":"11","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Parenchyma 3. Mucilage cells 4. Raphides of calcium oxalate 5. Vascular bundle\n\n6. Bundle sheath fibres 7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Silica body\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[41, 4, 1953, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[273, 459, 1715, 2063])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis\n\n1. Bundle sheath fibres (silica body $ \\longrightarrow $ ） 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate 3. Epidermal cells\n\n4. Vessels (4-1 bordered-pitted vessel, 4-2 scalariform vessel, 4-3 spiral vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 25, 1880, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"Naringenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of naringenin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (5:4:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately naringenin standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 8 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 8, 1948, 256])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 366, 1000, 713])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of naringenin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 875, 842, 1755])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Naringenin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of naringenin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Naringenin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (7.5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.75 mg of naringenin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[35, 6, 1904, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (226 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-40</td><td>75→55</td><td>25→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-50</td><td>55→5</td><td>45→95</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of naringenin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of naringenin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of naringenin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from naringenin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject naringenin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of naringenin peak in the chromatogram of naringenin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify naringenin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of naringenin Std-FP. The retention times of naringenin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 11, 1945, 259])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, naringenin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.37</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.46</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[383, 1042, 1840, 1655])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[40, 27, 1872, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dendrobii-officinalis-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis","la":"Dendrobii Officinalis Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/descurainiae-semen-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Descurainiae Semen v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Official Name: Descurainiae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 南葶藶子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Nantinglizi"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Descurainiae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 南葶藶子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Nantinglizi"},{"name":"12","text":"Descurainiae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Descurainia sophia (L.) Webb. ex Prantl. (Brassicaceae). The fruit is collected in summer when ripe, dried under the sun, then the seeds gathered and foreign matter removed to obtain Descurainiae Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Oblong-ellipsoid and slightly flattened, about 1 mm long, 0.5 mm wide. Externally yellowish-brown, marked with petty and dense reticulate texture, and 2 longitudinal shallow furrows. One end obtuse, the other slightly concave in the centre with a hilum or relatively truncate. Odour slight; taste slightly pungent and bitter, slightly viscous (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Outer epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of mucilage cells, with thickened inner walls, extending outwards to cellulose columns, oblique, lengthened or truncate at apex; cellulose columns truncate at apex, relatively wide, with mucilage striations in periphery. Inner epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of subsquare cells, with inner and lateral wall thickened and strongly lignified. Endosperm cells 1 layer, flat square, containing aleurone granules. Cotyledon composed of 2 slices, together with radicle occupies the major portion of the seed (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 7, 1960, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Outer epidermal cells of testa consist of 1 layer of mucilage cells, colourless to pale yellow, rectangular in lateral view, inner walls thickened, extending outwards to form cellulose column, cellulose column 8-17 $ \\mu $m long, apex truncate, mucilage striations visible in periphery; polygonal or subsquare in surface view, 16-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter, cellulose column visible at centre, mucilage striations visible in periphery. Inner epidermal cells of testa 1 layer in lateral view, pale yellow to reddish-brown, lateral walls unevenly thickened; polygonal, subsquare, sometimes long-polygonal in surface view, 12-43 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls 2-3 $ \\mu $m thick, lumens subrounded to polygonal. Endosperm cells colourless, flat square in section view, polygonal in surface view, walls slightly thickened, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets. Cotyledon cells irregular polygonal (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 416, 1708, 2123])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Descurainiae Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Testa magnified\n\n1. Outer epidermis of testa 2. Cellulose column\n\n3. Inner epidermis of testa 4. Endosperm 5. Cotyledons 6. Radicle\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n川棟子\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nDescurainiae Semen\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[383, 490, 990, 946])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1006, 491, 1601, 942])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[384, 984, 991, 1429])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1006, 983, 1599, 1436])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[383, 1470, 991, 1924])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1005, 1475, 1600, 1925])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Outer epidermal cells of testa (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in surface view)\n\n2. Inner epidermal cells of testa (2-1 in lateral view, 2-2 in surface view)\n\n3. Endosperm cells 4. Cotyledon cells"},{"name":"12","text":"Quercetin-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranosyl-7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-gentiobioside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of quercetin-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranosyl-7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-gentiobioside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, water and formic acid (6:3:2, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercetin-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranosyl-7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-gentiobioside standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[552, 96, 716, 156])\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 157, 1237, 212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[439, 373, 1117, 847])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[398, 920, 817, 1162])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) quercetin-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranosyl-7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-gentiobioside and (ii) sinapic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 1373, 881, 2114])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Descurainiae Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Quercetin-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranosyl-7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-gentiobioside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercetin-3-O- $ \\beta $ D-glucopyranosyl-7-O- $ \\beta $ D-gentiobioside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Sinapic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of sinapic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>65→50</td><td>35→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of sinapic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of sinapic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of sinapic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from sinapic acid peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[39, 12, 1959, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject sinapic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of sinapic acid peak in the chromatogram of sinapic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify sinapic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of sinapic acid Std-FP. The retention times of sinapic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Descurainiae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Descurainiae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,sinapic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.82</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.93</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.29</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.53</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 1593, 1914, 2127])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Descurainiae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 5.0%.\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/descurainiae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Descurainiae Semen","la":"Descurainiae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dictamni-cortex-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dictamni Cortex v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[628, 872, 1371, 1667])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1927, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[628, 872, 1371, 1667])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 5, 1927, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Dictamni Cortex\n\nChinese Name: 白鮮皮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baixianpi"},{"name":"12","text":"Dictamni Cortex is the dried root bark of Dictamnus dasycarpus Turcz. (Rutaceae). The root is collected in spring and autumn, the soil and fibrous roots removed, washed clean, then incised vertically. Afterward, the root bark is stripped off, then dried immediately to obtain Dictamni Cortex."},{"name":"12","text":"Quilled or semi-quilled shape, both edges of the cortex usually curved inwards, 3.1-17.5 cm long, 4-20 mm in diameter, 1-5 mm thick. Outer surface greyish-white or pale yellow, marked with fine longitudinal wrinkles, transverse lenticels and rootlet scars, frequently with small protruding granular dots; inner surface almost pale yellow, with fine longitudinal wrinkles. Texture fragile, easily broken, dusting on breaking, fracture uneven and somewhat lamellar. After cork is removed, showing numerous glittering small spots on exposuring to light. Odour muttony; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork contains 3-10 layers of cells, lengthened tangential, thin and flat. Cortex narrow, parenchymatous cells elongated round, tangentially arranged, with large clefts scattered. Phloem broad, parenchymatous cells arranged disparely, with clefts. Phloem ray consists of 1-3 rows of parenchymatous cells wide. Fibres mostly scattered singly in the cortex and phloem, subsquare or elongated-polygonal, walls extremely thickened, obviously striated and lignified. Parenchymatous cells contain abundant clusters of calcium oxalate and starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-white. Starch granules mostly simple, subrounded, 3-11 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with dotted or cleft-shaped hilum in large one and striation indistinct, compound granules composed\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 8, 1984, 263])\n\n2 units or more; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalates abundant, 6-33 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, frequently arranged as a strip; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres are more frequently observed, usually singly scattered, mostly fusiform, or slender fusiform with narrow lumen, 81-775 $ \\mu $ m long, 10-128 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, walls extremely thickened, lignified, striations meticulous and distinct; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cell polygonal or square in surface view (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Dictamnine standard solution\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of dictamnine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nFraxinellone standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of fraxinellone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nObсунопе standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of obacunone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Pretain a mixture of toluene, methanol and glacial acetic acid (10:0.5:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Car border=\"1\"><tr><td>Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ F_{254} $ plate,a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dictamnine standard solution(2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ),fraxinellone standard solution(5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ),obacunone standard solution(10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ )and the test solution(8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ )to the plate.Before the development,add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough.Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 5 min.Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development.Develop over a path of about 8.5cm.After the development,remove the plate from the chamber,mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible(about 3 min).Examine the plate under UV light(366 nm).Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[598, 15, 695, 69])\n\n延胡索 Corydalis Rhizoma\n\n砒霜 Arsenicum\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 438, 1737, 2004])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Dictamni Cortex\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate D. Fibre\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Fibres 5. Phloem ray 6. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[29, 14, 1984, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[299, 551, 727, 1203])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[734, 554, 1174, 1203])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1203, 557, 1696, 1201])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[294, 1224, 1173, 1867])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1202, 1223, 1698, 1869])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Dictamni Cortex\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate 3. Fibre 4. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[27, 4, 1924, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of dictamnine, fraxinellone and obacunone.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[469, 599, 749, 804])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[463, 921, 705, 1224])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[468, 1315, 950, 1706])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) dictamnine (ii) fraxinellone and (iii) obacunone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Dictamnine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (16 mg/L)\n\nWe 0.16 mg of dictamnine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (70%).\n\nFraxinellone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (64 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.64 mg of fraxinellone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nObacunone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (400 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of obacunone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (70%).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[13, 7, 1978, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table can see that the time (min) is the x-axis and the 0.1% Phosphoric acid (% , v/v) is the y-axis. The Acetonitrile (% , v/v) is the third axis. The Elution is the fourth axis.\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>85→47</td><td>15→53</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-35</td><td>47→37</td><td>53→63</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>37→10</td><td>63→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dictamnine Std-FP, fraxinellone Std-FP and obacunone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of dictamnine, fraxinellone and obacunone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of dictamnine, fraxinellone and obacunone peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from dictamnine, fraxinellone and obacunone peaks should not be less than 60000, 200000 and 200000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject dictamnine Std-FP, fraxinellone Std-FP, obacunone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of dictamnine, fraxinellone and obacunone peaks in the chromatograms of dictamnine Std-FP, fraxinellone Std-FP, obacunone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dictamnine, fraxinellone and obacunone peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of dictamnine Std-FP, fraxinellone Std-FP and obacunone Std-FP. The\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 1, 1930, 258])\n\nretention times of dictamnine, fraxinellone and obacunone peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Dictamni Cortex extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Dictamni Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(dictamnine)</td><td>0.80</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.85</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,obacunone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(fraxinellone)</td><td>1.05</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[389, 1205, 1731, 1851])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Dictamni Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 17, 1976, 265])\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 13.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 17.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dictamni-cortex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dictamni Cortex","la":"Dictamni Cortex"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dioscoreae-bulbiferae-rhizoma-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[365, 828, 1579, 1940])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma\n\nB. Magnified image of cut surface (showing granular fracture)\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface (showing orange-yellow spots)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1964, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[365, 828, 1579, 1940])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma\n\nB. Magnified image of cut surface (showing granular fracture)\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface (showing orange-yellow spots)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1964, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma\n\nChinese name: 黄蘩子\n\nChinese phonetic name: Huangyaozi"},{"name":"12","text":"Dioscoreae Bulbifera Rhizoma is the dried tuber of Dioscorea bulbifera L. (Dioscoreaceae). The tuber is collected in autumn, rootlets, residual stems and leaves removed, washed clean, cut into slices when fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Dioscoreae Bulbifera Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Mostly transversely cut thick slices, rounded or subrounded, 14-143 mm in diameter, 0.1-1.9 cm in thickness. Cork easily exfoliated, externally brownish-black, shrunken, white punctiform protuberances scars of rootlets numerous, occasionally with remains of curved rootlets. Transversely cut surface yellowish-white to yellowish-brown, flat or bumpy. Texture hard and fragile. Fracture granular, scattered with orange-yellow spots. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells, arranged densely, with relatively thick wall, slightly lignified. Stone cells arranged in an interrupted ring. Secretory canal located at the outer part of the basal tissue. Parenchymatous cells in basal tissue loosely arranged, containing abundant starch granules. Mucilage cells numerous, containing raphides of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundles few, collateral and scattered (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 36, 1955, 248])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brownish-yellow or greyish-yellow. Starch granules numerous, singly scattered or in group, mainly simple starch granules, oblong, ovoid or irregular-triangular, 6-82 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum pointed or stellate, located at the smaller end, usually not obvious; black and cruciated-shaped under the polarized microscope. Mucilage cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate, 14-122 $ \\mu m $ long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells pale yellow to brownish-yellow, mostly singly scattered or 2-5 in groups, long elliptical, subrounded, fusiform or irregular in shape, with striations, distinct pits and pit canals; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate and spiral, 8-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Cork cells subpolygonal or subrectangular, walls slightly thick, occasionally found. Secretory canals mostly broken, containing reddish-brown secretions (Fig. 3).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[643, 72, 764, 115])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 48, 1852, 97])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 446, 1734, 2073])"},{"name":"12","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells\n\nD. Vascular bundle\n\nE. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nF. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell 3. Secretory canal 4. Basal tissue 5. Mucilage cells\n\n6. Raphides of calcium oxalate 7. Vascular bundle\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[21, 43, 1954, 244])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[303, 560, 1684, 2022])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Starch granules (1-1 singly scattered, 1-2 in group)\n\n2. Mucilage cell with raphides of calcium oxalate 3. Stone cells\n\n4. Vessels (4-1 reticulate vessels, 4-2 spiral vessel) 5. Cork cells 6. Secretory canal\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Diosbulbin B standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of diosbulbin B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate and methanol (10:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol and dissolve 10 g of vanillin."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately diosbulbin B standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 42, 1950, 239])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[373, 371, 714, 736])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of diosbulbin B\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 866, 755, 1707])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Diosbulbin B standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of diosbulbin B (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 44, 1963, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Diosbulbin B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of diosbulbin B CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates. Wash the residue with methanol. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the extract to a 20-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>95→80</td><td>5→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-40</td><td>80→50</td><td>20→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-50</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of diosbulbin B Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of diosbulbin B should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of diosbulbin B peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from diosbulbin B peak should not be less than 380000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject diosbulbin B Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of diosbulbin B peak in the chromatogram of diosbulbin B Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify diosbulbin B peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of diosbulbin B Std-FP. The retention times of diosbulbin B peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,diosbulbin B)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[331, 1510, 1813, 2143])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dioscoreae-bulbiferae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma","la":"Dioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dioscoreae-nipponicae-rhizoma-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 875, 1266, 1096])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[678, 1147, 1340, 1872])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma A. Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 22, 1872, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 875, 1266, 1096])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[678, 1147, 1340, 1872])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma A. Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 22, 1872, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 穿山龍"},{"name":"11","text":"Chinese Phonetic Name: Chuanshanlong\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Dioscorea nipponica Makino (Dioscoreaceae). The rhizome is collected in spring and autumn. Rootlets and foreign matter removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical, slightly curved, 3-30 cm long, 4-18 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to brown, with irregular longitudinal furrows, dotted root scars and protuberant stem scars on one side. Residual rootlets occasionally present. Texture hard. Fracture even, white to yellowish-white, with small pale yellowish-brown vascular bundles. Odour slight; taste bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells, sometimes fallen off, the cells rectangular, tangentially extended and closely arranged. Cortex relatively narrow, mucous cells subrounded, containing raphides of calcium oxalate. Stele broad, scattered with collateral vascular bundles (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale yellow. Starch granules fairly abundant, broadly ovoid, elongated-ellipsoid or irregular in shape, 3-18 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum slit-shaped, striations distinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules rare, composed of 2-3 units. Raphides of calcium\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 4, 1945, 258])\n\noxalate scattered or in bundles, 31-101 $ \\mu $ m long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 8-61 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Cork cells brown, polygonal to subsquare. Lignified parenchymatous cells pale yellow or yellow, elongated elliptical, rectangular or rhombic, with small and sparse pits. Stone cells usually scattered singly, 42-276 $ \\mu $ m long, 30-80 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, walls 9-38 $ \\mu $ m thick, with several striated layers, distinct pits and pit canals (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[249, 524, 1731, 1944])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphides of calcium oxalate 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Raphides of calcium oxalate 4. mucous cells 5. Stele 6. Vascular bundles\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[41, 9, 1946, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[252, 419, 1748, 2123])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate 3. Bordered-pitted vessels 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Lignified parenchymatous cell 6. Stone cell\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 26, 1873, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Dioscin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.8 mg of dioscin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, n-butanol and ethyl acetate (5:4:1, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (65%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dioscin standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 373, 1273, 931])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of dioscin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 1096, 893, 1982])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Dioscin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of dioscin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[33, 23, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Dioscin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of dioscin CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (65%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (65%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-50</td><td>50→0</td><td>50→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dioscin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dioscin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dioscin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dioscin peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject dioscin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dioscin peak in the chromatogram of dioscin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dioscin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dioscin Std-FP. The\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 10, 1946, 258])\n\nretention times of dioscin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, dioscin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2.87</td><td>±0.08</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3.19</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[391, 1186, 1774, 1980])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 25, 1873, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 32.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dioscoreae-nipponicae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma","la":"Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dipsaci-radix-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dipsaci Radix v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[595, 866, 1370, 1907])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[595, 866, 1370, 1907])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Dipsaci Radix\n\nChinese Name: 續斷\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xuduan"},{"name":"12","text":"Dipsaci Radix is the dried root of Dipsacus asper Wall. ex Henry (Dipsacaceae). The root is collected in autumn, root stock and rootlets removed, piled up at room temperature until a green colour is developed inside, and then baked at a temperature about $ 4 5-6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until dryness to obtain Dipsaci Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical, somewhat flattened, slightly curved, 5-20 cm long, 4-20 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown or yellowish-brown, with slightly twisted or obviously twisted longitudinal wrinkles and furrows, showing transversal lenticels-like cicatrices and sparse rootlet scars. Texture soft but hardened after a long period of storage, easily broken. Fracture uneven, bark dark green to brown, the outer part pale brown to brown, wood yellowish-brown to greenish-brown, vessel bundles arranged radially. Odour slightly aromatic; taste bitter, with slight sweetish and followed by astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Phelloderm narrow, consists of 6-8 layers of cells. Groups of sieve tubes sparsely scattered in the phloem. Parenchymatous cells contain clusters of calcium oxalate. Cambium distinct or less distinct. Xylem ray broad; vessels dense near the cambium and lessened inward. Pith small, and most absent in small roots (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[60, 8, 1969, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Clusters of calcium oxalate fairly abundant, scattered or embedded in shrunken parenchymatous cells, 15-50 $ \\mu $m in diameter, sometimes arranged as a strip; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Some parenchymatous cells with fine, oblique crisscross striations, wall slightly thickened. Vessels bordered-pitted and reticulate, varying in diameter (occasionally up to 70 $ \\mu $m). Cork cells pale brown, subrectangular, subsquare, polygonal or elongated-polygonal in surface view, wall thin (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[30, 4, 1909, 266])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 435, 1731, 2120])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Dipsaci Radix\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate D. Sieve tubes\n\n1. Cork 2. Phelloderm 3. Phloem 4. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Sieve tubes 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Pith\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nDipsaci Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 751, 1702, 2176])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Dipsaci Radix\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Parenchymatous cells with oblique crisscross striations\n\n3. Bordered-pitted vessel 4. Reticulate vessels 5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 7, 1910, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Asperosaponin VI standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of asperosaponin VI CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, n-butanol and acetic acid (5:4:1, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 8 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately asperosaponin VI standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of asperosaponin VI.\n\nma 餘甘子 Phyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nArecae Pericarpium erba 大腹皮\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nLophatheri Herba淡竹葉\n\n車前子\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma\n\n香附\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繽斷\n\nDipsaci Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 373, 1502, 987])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of asperosaponin VI\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Asperosaponin VI standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of asperosaponin VI CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ) flow: 2.0 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>90→55</td><td>10→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>55→5</td><td>45→95</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[38, 4, 1909, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of asperosaponin VI Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of asperosaponin VI should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of asperosaponin VI peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from asperosaponin VI peak should not be less than 250000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject asperosaponin VI Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of asperosaponin VI peak in the chromatogram of asperosaponin VI Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify asperosaponin VI peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of asperosaponin VI Std-FP. The retention times of asperosaponin VI peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Dipsaci Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Dipsaci Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.69</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,asperosaponinVI)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nDipsaci Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[294, 384, 1810, 1050])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Dipsaci Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 12.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 9, 1903, 266])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 41.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 36.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dipsaci-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dipsaci Radix","la":"Dipsaci Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/draconis-sanguis-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Draconis Sanguis v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[897, 861, 1315, 1177])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[515, 1239, 970, 1708])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1009, 1260, 1434, 1714])"},"sections":[{"name":"8","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[897, 861, 1315, 1177])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[515, 1239, 970, 1708])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1009, 1260, 1434, 1714])"},{"name":"8","text":"Official Name: Draconis Sanguis\n\nChinese Name: 血竭\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xuejie"},{"name":"8","text":"Draconis Sanguis is the prepared resin of the fruit of Daemonorops draco Bl. (Arecaceae). The fruit is collected when ripe with densely hard squamae, red resin leaking from squamae; then dried under the sun, foreign matter removed, softened in hot water, and then taken out in a shaded area to obtain raw resin. The raw resin is then mixed with an excipient, dammar gum (a resin from trees of the Dipterocarpaceae family), to obtain Draconis Sanguis."},{"name":"8","text":"Subglobular to cuboidal, 60-80 mm in diameter, 7-8 cm high. Externally dark red, lustrous, attached with friction-caused red powder. Texture hard and fragile, broken surface red, ground powder brick red. Odour slight; taste bland. Insoluble in water, softened in hot water (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"8","text":"Dracorhodin perchlorate standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of dracorhodin perchlorate CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of ethyl acetate and ethanol (20:1, v/v)."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[72, 7, 1958, 262])"},{"name":"8","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dracorhodin perchlorate standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[446, 1024, 795, 1383])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Chemical structure of dracorhodin perchlorate\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[384, 375, 889, 1188])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Draconis Sanguis extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Dracorhodin perchlorate standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of dracorhodin (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"8","text":"Dracorhodin perchlorate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of dracorhodin perchlorate CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"8","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (440 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[38, 12, 1959, 263])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>15→80</td><td>85→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"8","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dracorhodin perchlorate Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dracorhodin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dracorhodin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dracorhodin peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"8","text":"Separately inject dracorhodin perchlorate Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dracorhodin peak in the chromatogram of dracorhodin perchlorate Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dracorhodin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dracorhodin perchlorate Std-FP. The retention times of dracorhodin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Draconis Sanguis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Draconis Sanguis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker,dracorhodin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2.05</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.22</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡寅連\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[381, 366, 1735, 933])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Draconis Sanguis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"8","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"8","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 7.0%."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a weighted cellulose extraction thimble. Place the cellulose extraction thimble in soxhlet extractor. Add 200 mL of ethanol to a 500-mL round-bottomed flask. Perform the soxhlet extraction until the mixture become colourless. Cool down to room temperature. Collect the thimble and dry it at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 4 h. Weigh accurately and calculate the percentage of ethanol-insoluble matter with reference to the dried substance. The amount of ethanol-insoluble matter in the sample should not be more than 25.0%.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 10, 1973, 260])"},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a test tube, then add 10 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Shake well for 10 min . Filter and transfer 5 mL of the filtrate to another test tube. Add 5 mL of freshly prepared 0.5% (w/v) copper acetate solution and allow to stand for 10 min. No green colour should be observed in the upper layer of the sample solution."},{"name":"8","text":"Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 39.0%."},{"name":"8","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"8","text":"Dracorhodin perchlorate standard stock solution, Std-Stock (400 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of dracorhodin perchlorate CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol.\n\nDracorhodin perchlorate standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the dracorhodin perchlorate Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 10, 40, 100, 200, 400 mg/L for dracorhodin perchlorate."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh accurately 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with ethanol. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/draconis-sanguis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Draconis Sanguis","la":"Draconis Sanguis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/drynariae-rhizoma-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Drynariae Rhizoma v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[499, 1171, 1510, 1824])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Drynariae Rhizoma A. Drynariae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 18, 1873, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[499, 1171, 1510, 1824])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Drynariae Rhizoma A. Drynariae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 18, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Drynariae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 骨碎補\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gusuibu"},{"name":"11","text":"Drynariae Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Drynaria fortunei (Kunze) J. Sm. (Polypodiaceae). The rhizome is collected all year round, soils removed, then dried under the sun or scorched off the hairs (ramenta) to obtain Drynariae Rhizoma."},{"name":"11","text":"Flattened long slat-shaped or lacerated strip, mostly curved, branched, 2-19.5 cm long, 0.5-2.5 cm wide, 2-8 mm thick. Externally densely covered with deep brown to dark brown hairy ramenta, and changing to brown or dark brown when burnt; the upper surface and both sides marked by protuberant or dented circular frond scars, rarely by frond-bases and remnants of fibrous roots. Texture fragile and light in weight, easily broken. Fracture reddish-brown, vascular bundles yellow dotted and arranged in a ring. Odour slight; taste bland and slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, subrounded or oblong, with relatively thickened outer wall. Basal part of scale leaves located at dented region of epidermis, consisting of 3-4 layers of cells, with thicken wall, containing reddish-brown pigments. Parenchymatous cells subrounded or irregularly undulated curved. Endodermis cells tangentially elongated; casparian dots indistinct. Vascular bundles amphicribral, 11-28 bundles arranged in a flattened annular ring, surrounded by endodermis. Tracheids of xylem mainly reticulate, 7-82 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 4, 1948, 257])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour brown. Fragments of scale leaf yellowish-brown or reddish-brown, cells of scales elongated or irregular in shape, some contain reddish-brown contents. Cortex cells subrectangular or subpolygonal, slightly lignified or unlignified, pit canals obvious; cells near to epidermis relatively small, walls slightly undulate, pit canals sparse; cells near to endodermis with thick walls, pit canals obvious. Tracheids mainly reticulate, colourless, yellow or yellowish-brown, 7-82 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fibres usually in bundles, orange-yellow or reddish-brown, fusiform, tapering at both ends, walls thick, pit canals mostly unobvious (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 14, 1892, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[244, 408, 1740, 2132])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Drynariae Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Endodermis and vascular bundles 1. Epidermis 2. Basal part of scale leaves 3. Endodermis 4. Parenchyma 5. Xylem 6. Phloem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 8, 1947, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[283, 678, 974, 1201])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1007, 682, 1702, 1203])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[283, 1226, 973, 1737])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1007, 1226, 1701, 1741])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Drynariae Rhizoma (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Fragments of scale leaf 2. Cortex cells 3. Tracheid 4. Fibres\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 26, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Naringin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of naringin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (8:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately naringin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 5, 1951, 255])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 376, 1091, 797])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of naringin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 971, 867, 1743])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Drynariae Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Naringin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of naringin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Naringin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of naringin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[34, 14, 1897, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (283 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.5% Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>5</td><td>95</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-15</td><td>5→10</td><td>95→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-50</td><td>10→25</td><td>90→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>25</td><td>75</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of naringin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of naringin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of naringin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from naringin peak should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject naringin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of naringin peak in the chromatogram of naringin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify naringin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of naringin Std-FP. The retention times of naringin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 8, 1946, 259])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Drynariae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Drynariae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.87</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, naringin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[386, 977, 1776, 1585])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Drynariae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements (except cadmium should not be more than 2.5 mg/kg).\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[31, 24, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/drynariae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Drynariae Rhizoma","la":"Drynariae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dryopteridis-crassirhizomatis-rhizoma-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[846, 864, 1212, 1176])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[649, 1252, 966, 1823])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1060, 1302, 1341, 1775])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\nA. Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\nB. Transverse section (1. rhizome 2. frond bases)"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[846, 864, 1212, 1176])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[649, 1252, 966, 1823])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1060, 1302, 1341, 1775])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\nA. Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\nB. Transverse section (1. rhizome 2. frond bases)"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 綿馬賁眾\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Mianmaguanzhong"},{"name":"13","text":"Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma is the dried rhizome and frond bases of Dryopteris crassirhizoma Nakai (Dryopteridaceae). The rhizome and frond bases are collected in autumn, fronds and fibrous roots removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma."},{"name":"13","text":"Elongated obovoid, slightly curved, 3-17.8 cm long, 22-95 mm in diameter. Externally yellowishbrown to dark brown, densely covered with regularly arranged frond bases and ramenta, with curved rootlets, and ramenta easily fallen off. Texture hard, fracture slightly even, yellowish-green to yellowish-brown, with 5-13 yellowish-white meristeles prominently visible, arranged in a ring, and with numerous leaf trace vascular bundles scattered on the outer part. The frond bases oblate, 2-9 mm in diameter. Texture hard and fragile, fracture slightly even, yellowish-green to yellowish-brown, with 5-13 yellowish-white meristeles arranged in a ring. Odour characteristic; taste bland and astringent, followed by bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Rhizome: Sclerenchyma consists of several layers of sclerenchymatous cells, brown to dark brown. Parenchymatous cells in basal tissue loosely arranged, containing yellowish-brown contents and starch guanules. Leaf trace vascular bundle scattered at the outer part of the basal tissue. Meristele amphicribral, 5-13 arranged in a ring; each surrounded by a layer of endodermis cells, casparian dots distinct. Xylem consists of polygonal tracheids. Intercellular glandular hairs present in the intercellar space, mostly broken [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 4, 1967, 263])\n\nFrond base: Sclerenchyma consists of several layers of sclerenchymatous cells, brown to dark brown. Parenchymatous cells in basal tissue loosely arranged, containing yellowish-brown contents and starch guanules. Meristele amphicribral, 5-13 arranged in a ring; each surrounded by a layer of endodermis cells, casparian dots distinct. Xylem consists of polygonal tracheids. Intercellular glandular hairs present in the intercellar space, mostly broken [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-brown to yellowish-brown. Starch granules abundant, simple starch granules subrounded, elliptical or ovate, 2-14 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum and striations indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Sclerenchymatous cells scattered singly or in bundles, yellowish-brown or brown, fibre-like, 6-42 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; pale yellowish-brown under the polarized microscope. Intercellular glandular hairs unicellular, mostly broken, occasionally intact, elliptical or long-ovate, base elongated, some contain yellowish-brown secretions. Tracheids mainly scalariform, few reticulate tracheids also found, 7-43 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 406, 1746, 2085])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Intercellular glandular hair 1. Sclerenchyma 2. Basal tissue 3. Leaf trace vascular bundle 4. Endodermis\n\n5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Intercellular glandular hair\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n彯金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 101, 1591, 164])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 586, 1717, 2053])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of frond base of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Intercellular glandular hair 1. Sclerenchyma 2. Basal tissue 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n6. Intercellular glandular hair\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[338, 564, 1647, 2109])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Sclerenchymatous cells 3. Intercellular glandular hair\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 14, 1959, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Dryocrassin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dryocrassin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and glacial acetic acid (20:5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dryocrassin standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 379, 1312, 1181])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of dryocrassin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 1347, 865, 1980])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Dryocrassin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of dryocrassin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[552, 97, 714, 155])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1427, 101, 1595, 164])"},{"name":"13","text":"0. 1 M Citric acid solution\n\nWeigh 9.6 g of citric acid and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\n0. 1 M Disodium hydrogen phosphate solution\n\nWeigh 17.8 g of disodium hydrogen phosphate and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\nDisodium hydrogen phosphate-citric acid buffer solution (pH 5.0)\n\nPipette 50 mL of 0.1 M disodium hydrogen phosphate solution, 50 mL of 0.1 M citric acid solution and 900 mL of water to a 1500-mL beaker. Adjust the pH value to 5.0 with 0.1 M citric acid solution."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of L-ascorbic acid and dissolve it in a mixture of 100 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide and 400 mL of methanol. Freshly prepare the solution."},{"name":"13","text":"Dryocrassin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of dryocrassin CRS and place it in a 5-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in 1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Make up to the mark with extraction solution. Keep at about $ - 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of extraction solution. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 20 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with extraction solution. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with extraction solution. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter. Freshly prepare the test solution."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (302 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Disodium hydrogen phosphate-citric acid buffer solution(pH5.0)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>70→100</td><td>30→0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dryocrassin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dryocrassin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dryocrassin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dryocrassin peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject dryocrassin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dryocrassin peak in the chromatogram of dryocrassin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dryocrassin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dryocrassin Std-FP. The retention times of dryocrassin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,dryocrassin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1427, 103, 1591, 163])\n\n豬牙皂\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[376, 361, 1859, 984])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[766, 118, 870, 173])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/dryopteridis-crassirhizomatis-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma","la":"Dryopteridis Crassirhizomatis Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ecliptae-herba-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ecliptae Herba v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[548, 855, 1512, 1810])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[548, 855, 1512, 1810])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Ecliptae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 墨旱蓮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Mohanlian"},{"name":"11","text":"Ecliptae Herba is the dried aerial part of Eclipta prostrata L. (Asteraceae). The aerial part of herb is collected at flowering stage, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Ecliptae Herba."},{"name":"11","text":"White-pubescent throughout. Stem cylindrical, with longitudinal ridges, 1-5 mm in diameter; externally greenish-brown to dark green. Leaves simple, opposite, almost sessile; blade crumpled and rolled or broken; when intact and flattened in whole, dark green, long-lanceolate, apex acute, base narrowly cuneate, margins entire or shallowly dentate. Capitula 2-8 mm in diameter. Achenes elliptic and flattened, 2-3 mm long, pale brown to brown. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and salty (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells with thick wall and non-glandular hairs. Sclerenchymatous cells in 2-4 layers. Cortex parenchymatous cells arranged loose, containing pigment masses. Fibre bundles are located on the outside of vascular bundles. Collateral vascular bundles 12-20, phloem narrow, cambium forms an interrupted ring. Pith broad (Fig. 2).\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of cells with stomata, non-glandular hairs occasionally with glandular hairs. Sclerenchymatous cells of 1-3 layers are located on the inside of the upper and lower epidermis. Palisade tissue and spongy tissue consist of 1 layer of palisade-shaped parenchymatous cells and 4-6 layers of subrounded parenchymatous cells, respectively. Collateral vascular bundles, 3-5, line the middle vein (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 13, 1983, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greenish-brown. Non-glandular hairs, frequently 3-celled, with walls thickened with distinct prominent warts, 260-800 $ \\mu $m long. Pollen grains spherical, 15-20 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with warts. Pigment masses are scattered; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly spiral and reticulate, 13-100 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Epidermal cells with wavy and sinuous anticlinal walls; anomocytic stomata are surrounded by 3-4 subsidiary cells (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Wedelolactone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of wedelolactone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (10:7:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately wedelolactone standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of wedelolactone.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[41, 2, 1921, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 519, 1736, 2034])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Ecliptae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch (A1: Stem, A2: Leaf) B. Section illustration (B1: Stem, B2: Leaf) C. Lamina\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Sclerenchyma 3. Cortex 4. Non-glandular hair 5. Fibre bundle 6. Phloem\n\n7. Interfascicular cambium 8. Xylem 9. Pith 10. Upper epidermis 11. Palisade tissue\n\n12. Spongy tissue 13. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[28, 15, 1976, 265])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[355, 411, 968, 886])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1013, 413, 1622, 878])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[355, 971, 971, 1423])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1010, 965, 1627, 1424])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[356, 1512, 969, 1966])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1017, 1514, 1620, 1960])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Non-glandular hairs 2. Pollen grains 3. Pigment masses 4. Spiral and reticulate vessels\n\n5. Anomocytic stomata\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 2, 1930, 256])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 379, 902, 704])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of wedelolactone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Wedelolactone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (16 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of wedelolactone CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.5%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>85→80</td><td>15→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-40</td><td>80→70</td><td>20→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>70→60</td><td>30→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of wedelolactone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of wedelolactone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of wedelolactone\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 5, 1984, 263])\n\npeak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from wedelolactone peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject wedelolactone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of wedelolactone peak in the chromatogram of wedelolactone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify wedelolactone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of wedelolactone Std-FP. The retention times of wedelolactone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ecliptae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ecliptae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.45</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.52</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,wedelolactone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 1850, 1830, 2373])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ecliptae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 7, 1927, 258])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 18.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 7.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 13, 1977, 265])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ecliptae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ecliptae Herba","la":"Ecliptae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/elephantopi-herba-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Elephantopi Herba v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 890, 1363, 1338])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[647, 1366, 980, 1699])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[993, 1365, 1211, 1705])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1239, 1366, 1342, 1711])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 890, 1363, 1338])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[647, 1366, 980, 1699])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[993, 1365, 1211, 1705])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1239, 1366, 1342, 1711])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Elephantopi Herba\n\nB. Upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of the fracture of rhizome"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Elephantopi Herba\n\nChinese Name: 地膽草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Didancao"},{"name":"13","text":"Elephantopi Herba is the dried whole plant of Elephantopus scaber L. (Asteraceae). The whole plant is collected during summer and autumn before flowering, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Elephantopi Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Whole herb 9-37 cm long. Roots fibrous, numerous, fascicled, with irregular longitudinal wrinkles. Stems erect, usually short, slightly scabrid. Rhizomes 0.5-5.5 cm long, 2-10 mm in diameter; densely pale appressed-hirsute; texture hard, uneasily broken, fracture surface pale yellow. Leaves basal, crumpled; when intact spatulate to oblanceolate, 4-24 cm long, 0.5-4.5 cm wide; externally yellowish-green to greyish-green, densely appressed with pale hirsute and glandular dots especially on the upper surface; apex blunt or shortly acute, base gradually narrow, margin crenate; veins on the lower surface more distinct. Petiole broad and short, slightly sheath-like. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Rhizome: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Cortex broad. Endodermis distinct, consisting of 1 layer of cells, cells sometimes contain oil droplets. Phloem narrow. Fibres occasionally scattered in the phloem, singly or several in groups. Vessels small, radially and interruptedly arranged. Pith large. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, scattered in the parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 29, 1952, 260])\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare to rectangular cells; nonglandular hairs usually fallen after staining. Several layers of collenchymatous cells arranged on the inner side of upper and lower epidermis of midrib. Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells, irregularly arranged. Cells of spongy tissue loosely arranged. Vascular bundles 3-5, bicollateral, arranged in an arch shape; several layers of sclerenchymatous cells surround the phloem, those near the lower epidermis in crescent-shaped; vessels several, arranged in rows [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green. Non-glandular hairs numerous, long, easily broken, consisting of 1-3 cells; apex cell long, base cells short, 10-52 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; cell wall relatively thick, with longitudinal fine lines inside. Lower epidermal cells elongated, polygonal or irregular in shape; stomata anisocytic. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, 8-20 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Vessels spiral or reticulate, relatively small, 7-35 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fibres slender, 10-28 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; bright bluish-white under the polarized microscope. Stone cells subsquare or irregularly rhombic, 18-100 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 241])\n\n天葵子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 446, 1728, 2116])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Elephantopi Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light micoscope) E. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized micoscope)\n\n1. Epidemis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Fibre 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n8. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma 白及\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba Hoveniae Semen\n\n連錢草 枳椇子\n\nElephantopi Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[236, 394, 1740, 2123])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Elephantopi Herba\n\nA.Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Sclerenchyma 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Lower epidermis\n\n了哥王 Wikstroemiae Radix\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nElephantopi Herba\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藑黄\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[239, 513, 1717, 2061])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Elephantopi Herba\n\n1. Non-glandular hairs 2. Lower epidermal cells with stoma ( $ \\rightarrow $ ） 3. Epidermal cells of rhizome\n\n4. Clusters of calcium oxalate 5. Fibres 6. Spiral vessel 7. Reticulate vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 37, 1950, 253])"},{"name":"13","text":"Deoxyelephantopin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.8 mg of deoxyelephantopin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, acetone and ethyl acetate (8:3:2, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Sulphuric acid (95% - 98%, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately deoxyelephantopin standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[372, 1916, 728, 2270])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of deoxyelephantopin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[672, 43, 817, 118])\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum片蘴黄\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 375, 811, 1198])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Elephantopi Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Deoxyelephantopin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of deoxyelephantopin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Deoxyelephantopin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of deoxyelephantopin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 35, 1951, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (208 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.03% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Phosphoric acid:Acetonitrile(0.03:99.97,v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>75→70</td><td>25→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>70→65</td><td>30→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>65</td><td>35</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>65→60</td><td>35→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of deoxyelephantopin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of deoxyelephantopin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of deoxyelephantopin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from deoxyelephantopin peak should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject deoxyelephantopin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of deoxyelephantopin peak in the chromatogram of deoxyelephantopin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify deoxyelephantopin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of deoxyelephantopin Std-FP. The retention times of deoxyelephantopin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Elephantopi Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[36, 36, 1959, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Elephantopi Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, deoxyelephantopin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.09</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.54</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[343, 776, 1810, 1420])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Elephantopi Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[925, 87, 1029, 138])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 14.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 7.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 14.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/elephantopi-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Elephantopi Herba","la":"Elephantopi Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/epimedii-herba-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epimedii Herba v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[386, 813, 1447, 1891])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing biternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\nChrysanthemi Indici Flos竹節参野菊花Panacis Japonici Rhizoma\n\nSmilacis Chinae Rhizoma 豆蔻\n\n菝葜 Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\n委陵菜 石蒜\n\nPotentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n洋金花 Daturae Flos\n\nTinosporae Radix\n\n金果欖\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[368, 540, 1651, 1792])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing ternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nSaussureae Involucratae Herba\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n白花丹 Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\nBruceae Fructus 鸦膽子\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[351, 538, 1608, 1788])\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium pubescens Maxim.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing ternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\nChrysanthemi Indici Flos竹節參\n\n野菊花 Panacis Japonici Rhizoma\n\nSmilacis Chinae Rhizoma 豆蔻\n\n菝葜 Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n洋金花 Daturae Flos\n\nTinosporae Radix\n\n金果欖\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[377, 542, 1643, 1788])\n\nFigure 1 (iv) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium koreanum Nakai\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing biternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 38, 1938, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"24","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[386, 813, 1447, 1891])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing biternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\nChrysanthemi Indici Flos竹節参野菊花Panacis Japonici Rhizoma\n\nSmilacis Chinae Rhizoma 豆蔻\n\n菝葜 Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\n委陵菜 石蒜\n\nPotentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n洋金花 Daturae Flos\n\nTinosporae Radix\n\n金果欖\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[368, 540, 1651, 1792])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing ternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nSaussureae Involucratae Herba\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n白花丹 Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\nBruceae Fructus 鸦膽子\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[351, 538, 1608, 1788])\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium pubescens Maxim.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing ternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\nChrysanthemi Indici Flos竹節參\n\n野菊花 Panacis Japonici Rhizoma\n\nSmilacis Chinae Rhizoma 豆蔻\n\n菝葜 Amomi Fructus Rotundus\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n洋金花 Daturae Flos\n\nTinosporae Radix\n\n金果欖\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[377, 542, 1643, 1788])\n\nFigure 1 (iv) A photograph of dried aerial part of Epimedium koreanum Nakai\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Intact flattened leaves showing biternately compound leaves after soaking into water\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of spinose serrulations on margin of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of base of leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of petiolule\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 38, 1938, 262])"},{"name":"24","text":"Official Name: Epimedii Herba\n\nChinese Name: 淫羊藿\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yinyanghuo"},{"name":"24","text":"Epimedii Herba is the dried aerial part of Epimedium brevicornu Maxim., Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim., Epimedium pubescens Maxim., or Epimedium koreanum Nakai (Berberidaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn when foliage branch growing luxuriantly, thick stalks and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun or in a shaded area to obtain Epimedii Herba."},{"name":"24","text":"Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.: Stems slender-cylindrical, 5-28 cm long, mostly hollow. Externally yellowish-green or pale green, lustrous. Stem leaves mostly biternately compound. Leaflets ovate, 2-10 cm long, 2-8 cm wide; slightly acute at the apex, terminal leaflets cordate at the base, lateral leaflets oblique-cordate at the base, relatively large on one side, auriculate, margin with yellow spinose serrulations; the upper surface yellowish-green, the lower surface greyish-green, main vein 7-9, sparsely covered with slender hairs at the base, veinlets protuberant on both surfaces, reticulate veins distinct; petiolules 1-7 cm long. Lamina papyraceous or near leathery. Texture fragile, easily broken. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nEpimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.: Stems 15-55 cm long. Stem leaves ternately compound. Leaflets long-ovate to ovate-lanceolate, 4-15 cm long, 2-8 cm wide; acuminate at the apex; bilateral leaflets distinctly oblique at the base, arrow-shaped on one side; the lower surface sparsely strigillose, or sparsely covered with slender hairs, sometimes nearly glabrous; petiolules 3-7 cm long. Lamina leathery [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\nEpimedium pubescens Maxim.: Stems 7-60 cm long. Stem leaves ternately compounded. Leaflets long-ovate to ovate-lanceolate, 3-20 cm long, 2-8 cm wide; the lower surface densely covered with white long pubescence; petiolules 1-7 cm long, pilose. Lamina thin and leathery [Fig. 1 (iii)].\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[61, 24, 1938, 262])\n\nEpimedium koreanum Nakai: Stems 10-25 cm long. Stem leaves biternately compound. Leaflets ovate, relatively large, 3-10 cm long, 2.5-8 cm wide; long-acuminate at the apex; the upper surface dark green, the lower surface pale green, the lower surface sparsely covered with slender hairs or nearly glabrous; petiolules 1-7 cm long. Lamina thin membranous or papyraceous [Fig. 1 (iv)]."},{"name":"24","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells mostly flattened, arranged tightly, covered with cuticle. Cortex relatively narrow, cells subrounded. Fibres consist of 3-10 layers of cells arranged in a ring, beneath cortex. Vascular bundles 8-27, collateral, scattered in parenchymatous cells, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem cells arranged tightly. Xylem vessels subrounded to subelliptic. Pith broad, sometimes hollow in the centre, parenchymatous cells relatively large [Fig. 2 (i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)].\n\n(No significant differences in the transverse section of the stem for 4 species)"},{"name":"24","text":"Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.: Upper and lower epidermis each consists of 1 layer of cells, cells rectangular or subsquare, covered with cuticle. Multicellular non-glandular hairs and remnant occasionally present on lower epidermis. Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells, arranged irregularly, containing brown content. Spongy tissue arranged loosely. Vascular bundles of midrib 3, collateral. Vessels and fibres present in xylem. Several layers of sclerenchymatous cells present in the inner side of epidermis of the midrib [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nEpimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.: Remnant of multicellular non-glandular hairs occasionally found on the lower surface of epidermis. Vascular bundles of midrib 5-7 [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nEpimedium pubescens Maxim.: Remnant of multicellular non-glandular hairs numerous, present on the lower epidermis. Vascular bundles of midrib 5 [Fig. 2 (iii)].\n\nEpimedium koreanum Nakai: Remnant of multicellular non-glandular hairs present on the lower epidermis. Vascular bundles of midrib 3-5 [Fig. 2 (iv)].\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花"},{"name":"24","text":"Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.: Colour yellowish-green. Non-glandular hairs multicellular, usually broken, basal cells short, gradually longer towards the apex, apical cell with the longest length, straight, curved or slightly twisted, some cells contain yellowish-brown contents. Anticlinal walls of upper and lower epidermal cells sinuately curved in surface view, unevenly thickened, lower epidermis with numerous anomocytic stomata, guard cells nearly semicircular, subsidiary cells 3-5. Columnar crystals of calcium oxalate frequently visible, mostly scattered among the sclerenchymatous cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres with visible wall pits [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nEpimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.: Non-glandular hairs with short basal cells, apical cells long fusiform, forming obtuse angle with basal cells. Anticlinal walls of upper and lower epidermal cells sinuously curved in surface view, outer periclinal walls of lower epidermal cells mostly with papillae protuberance, double circle-shaped in surface view [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\nEpimedium pubescens Maxim.: Non-glandular hairs numerous, basal cells usually shorter, apical cells very long, mostly twisted or straight. Anticlinal walls of upper and lower epidermal cells sinuately curved in surface view [Fig. 3 (iii)].\n\nEpimedium koreanum Nakai: Non-glandular hairs of 2 types, first type slender, apical cell with the longest length; second type the length of each cell nearly equal, usually with one cell collapsed; both types of non-glandular hairs contain yellowish-brown contents. Anticlinal walls of upper and lower epidermal cells sinuously curved in surface view [Fig. 3 (iv)].\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[61, 39, 1940, 258])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[236, 404, 1748, 2108])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried aerial part of Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch of leaf B. Section illustration of leaf C. Section magnified of mesophyll D. Sketch of stem E. Section illustration of stem\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Lower epidermis\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Sclerenchyma 8. Epidermis 9. Cortex 10. Fibres\n\n11. Phloem 12. Xylem 13. Pith\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[234, 422, 1734, 2117])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried aerial part of Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.\n\nA. Sketch of leaf B. Section illustration of leaf C. Section magnified of mesophyll\n\nD. Sketch of stem E. Section illustration of stem\n\n</div>\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Lower epidermis 5. Xylem\n\n6. Phloem 7. Sclerenchyma 8. Remnant of non-glandular hair 9. Epidermis\n\n10. Cortex 11. Fibres 12. Phloem 13. Xylem 14. Pith\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[241, 481, 872, 968])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[300, 1071, 890, 1370])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[932, 421, 1688, 1030])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[298, 1461, 909, 1999])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[956, 1081, 1696, 1341])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[958, 1352, 1711, 2041])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried aerial part of Epimedium pubescens Maxim.\n\nA. Sketch of leaf B. Section illustration of leaf C. Section magnified of mesophyll\n\nD. Sketch of stem E. Section illustration of stem\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Lower epidermis 5. Xylem\n\n6. Remnant of non-glandular hair 7. Phloem 8. Sclerenchyma 9. Epidermis\n\n10. Cortex 11. Fibres 12. Phloem 13. Xylem 14. Pith\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1047, 40, 1181, 87])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[237, 427, 1748, 2131])\n\nFigure 2 (iv) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried aerial part of Epimedium koreanum Nakai A. Sketch of leaf B. Section illustration of leaf C. Section magnified of mesophyll D. Sketch of stem E. Section illustration of stem\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Lower epidermis 5. Xylem\n\n6. Phloem 7. Remnant of non-glandular hair 8. Sclerenchyma 9. Epidermis\n\n10. Cortex 11. Fibres 12. Phloem 13. Xylem 14. Pith\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[60, 32, 1940, 260])\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[245, 509, 1723, 2103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried aerial part of Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Non-glandular hairs 2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata 3. Upper epidermal cells\n\n4. Columnar crystals of calcium oxalate (in sclerenchymatous cells) 5. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根\n\nMenispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[238, 415, 1714, 2148])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried aerial part of Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.\n\n</div>\n\n4. Columnar crystals of calcium oxalate (in sclerenchymatous cells) 5. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=13"},{"name":"24","text":"Icariin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of icariin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"24","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (7:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"24","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"24","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"24","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately icariin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 10 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air (about 20 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nSaussureae Involucratae Herba 天山雪莲\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\n白花丹 Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n山銀花\n\nEpimedii Herba\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[370, 366, 1000, 893])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of icariin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=16,bbox=[377, 1055, 1245, 1766])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2(i) 2(ii) 2(iii) 2(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Epimedii Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Icariin standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried aerial part of Epimedium brevicornu Maxim.\n\n(ii) dried aerial part of Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim.\n\n(iii) dried aerial part of Epimedium pubescens Maxim.\n\n(iv) dried aerial part of Epimedium koreanum Nakai\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of icariin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"24","text":"Icariin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of icariin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"24","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times each with 7 mL of ethanol (50%). Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"24","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>78→76</td><td>22→24</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-45</td><td>76→55</td><td>24→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>55→30</td><td>45→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"24","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of icariin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of icariin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of icariin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from icariin peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)]."},{"name":"24","text":"Separately inject icariin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of icariin peak in the chromatogram of icariin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify icariin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by\n\ncomparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of icariin Std-FP. The retention times of icariin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Epimedii Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Epimedii Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(epimedin A)</td><td>0.77</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(epimedin B)</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(epimedin C)</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,icariin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=18,bbox=[359, 1154, 1819, 1738])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried aerial part of Epimedium brevicornu Maxim. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=18,bbox=[359, 1904, 1819, 2499])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried aerial part of Epimedium sagittatum (Sieb. et Zucc.) Maxim. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=19,bbox=[351, 376, 1814, 961])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried aerial part of Epimedium pubescens Maxim. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=19,bbox=[349, 1160, 1813, 1771])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (iv) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried aerial part of Epimedium koreanum Nakai extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)]."},{"name":"24","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=20,bbox=[38, 41, 1936, 260])"},{"name":"24","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"24","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/epimedii-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epimedii Herba","la":"Epimedii Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/equiseti-hiemalis-herba-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Equiseti Hiemalis Herba v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 972, 1056, 1156])\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1103, 952, 1389, 1191])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[569, 1242, 1371, 1855])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba A. Equiseti Hiemalis Herba B. Magnified stem surface C. Magnified transverse section of stem"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 972, 1056, 1156])\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1103, 952, 1389, 1191])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[569, 1242, 1371, 1855])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba A. Equiseti Hiemalis Herba B. Magnified stem surface C. Magnified transverse section of stem"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Equiseti Hiemalis Herba\n\nChinese Name: 木賊\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Muzei"},{"name":"11","text":"Equiseti Hiemalis Herba is the dried aerial part of Equisetum hyemale L. (Equisetaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun or in a shaded area to obtain Equiseti Hiemalis Herba."},{"name":"11","text":"Long, tubular, unbranched stem, mostly cut into segments, varying in length, up to 60 cm long, 2-7 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-green to yellowish-green, with 18-30 longitudinal ridges, numerous fine and shiny protuberance on the ridges; nodes distinct, internodes 2.5-9 cm long, with scale leaves on the nodes, the base and teeth of the leaves dark brown, the middle part pale brownishyellow, the teeth linear-triangular, the bases fused to form a sheath around the stem, in most cases, the leaf sheath easily broken off and only remnants of leaf sheath remains on the nodes. Texture fragile and light in weight, easily broken. Fracture hollow, with numerous and small rounded cavities around the edge. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet and slightly astringent, sandy-like when chewed (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[30, 9, 1970, 260])"},{"name":"11","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle, surface with grooves and ridges. 2 transparent silica protuberances located on the each ridges; 2 sunken stomata located in each groove. Sclerenchyma occurring inside ridges and stretching into cortex parenchyma, in cuneate-shape. Sclerenchymatous cells located in grooves, 1-3 layers. Cortex consists of parenchymatous cells, cells subrounded or long cylindrical. An empty lumen located in each groove. Endodermis consists of 2 layers of cells, outer layer in a sinuous ring, and inner layer in a rounded ring, casparian dots distinct in both layers. Vascular bundles collateral, located between 2 layers of endodermis, and beneath the ridges. A centre lumen located in inner part of each vascular bundle. Pith parenchymatous cells located inside endodermis, flattened and shrunken (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour green to greenish-brown. Epidermal cells elongated rectangular, walls thickened, stomata subrounded or elliptical, 70-85 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with many thickened strips in guard cells. Endodermal cells elongated rectangular, walls slightly thickened. Tracheids mainly spiral and scalariform. Epidermal cells of leaf sheath brown, elongated rectangular, walls thickened (Fig. 3).\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 397, 1744, 2119])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section illustration of the part with sunken stomata D. Sunken stoma magnified\n\n1. Silica protuberances 2. Epidermis 3. Sclerenchyma 4. Cortex 5. Lumen\n\n6. Endodermis 7. Vascular bundles 8. Centre lumen in inner part of vascular bundle\n\n9. Pith parenchymatous cells\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1427, 102, 1591, 163])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nEquiseti Hiemalis Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[250, 620, 1733, 2124])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stem epidermal cells (in surface view) 2. Stem epidermal cells with sunken stomata\n\n3. Endodermal cells 4. Tracheids 5. Leaf sheath epidermal cells"},{"name":"11","text":"Kaempferol 3-$\\beta$-sophoroside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, glacial acetic acid and water (14:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 1634, 1077, 2177])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside\n\n</div>\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n鬆金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 158, 1237, 212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 375, 930, 1103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of water. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with water. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The internal diameter of inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 8, 1903, 255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>90→70</td><td>10→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of kaempferol 3-$ \\beta $-sophoroside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of kaempferol 3-$ \\beta $-sophoroside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of kaempferol 3-$ \\beta $-sophoroside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from kaempferol 3-$ \\beta $-sophoroside peak should not be less than 70000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside peak in the chromatogram of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside Std-FP. The retention times of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,kaempferol3-β-sophoroside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n豬牙皂\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[383, 367, 1785, 1083])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Equiseti Hiemalis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol.\n\nKaempferol 3-$\\beta$-sophoroside standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 1, 2, 5, 10, 20 mg/L for kaempferol 3- $ \\beta $ -sophoroside."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of water. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with water. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/equiseti-hiemalis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Equiseti Hiemalis Herba","la":"Equiseti Hiemalis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/eupatorii-chinensis-radix-et-rhizoma-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[390, 826, 1578, 1866])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nB. Cut surface of rhizome\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[390, 826, 1578, 1866])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nB. Cut surface of rhizome\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nChinese name: 廣東土牛膝\n\nChinese phonetic name: Guangdongtuniuxi"},{"name":"13","text":"Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma is the dried root and rhizome of Eupatorium chinense L. (Asteraceae). The root and rhizome are collected in autumn. Soil removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma."},{"name":"13","text":"Rhizome stout, knotty-lumpy, 9-72 mm in diameter, upper part showing several remnants of stem bases; texture hard, not easily broken. Roots numerous; originating from stout rhizome; slender and cylindrical, some slightly curved, 1.4-54 cm long and 1-5 mm in diameter; externally greyishbrown, yellowish-brown or greyish-yellow, showing fine longitudinal wrinkles and sparse rootlet scars, occasionally with transverse crack mark; fracture fibrous-like; bark relatively thin, easily separated from wood, greyish-brown, yellowish-brown or greyish-yellow; wood broad, yellowishwhite; small round pith visible at the centre. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Odour aromatic; taste slightly pungent and bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells suberized, densely arranged, subsquare rectangular or irregular in shape, some containing brown masses. Cortex broad, parenchymatous cells subrounded, elliptic or polygonal. Stone cells scattered singly or occasionally 2 in groups, subrounded, subsquare, elongated-elliptical or polygonal, wall obviously thickened, lignified. In the inner side of cortex, near the endodermis, secretory canals scattered singly or 2-3 in groups, opposite the vascular bundle, arranged in an interrupted ring, 30-91 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Endodermis distinct, consisting of 1 layer of cells, forming a wavy ring. Vascular bundles collateral, fibre bundles located on the outer side of phloem; phloem narrow; xylem lignified, vessels arranged closely and irregularly. Pith relatively small, parenchymatous cells subrounded or polygonal [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 40, 1948, 244])\n\nRhizome: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells suberized, densely arranged, subsquare, rectangular or irregular in shape, some containing brown masses. Root-trace vascular bundles sometimes visible. Cortex extremely narrow, parenchymatous cells subrounded, elliptic or polygonal. In the inner side of cortex, stone cells scattered singly or 2-8 in groups, subrounded, subsquare, elongated-elliptical or polygonal, wall obviously thickened, lignified. Fibres in bundles, arranged in an interrupted ring, mostly lignified. Phloem easily broken during preparation of slides, empty spaces appear between cortex and cambium. Cambium distinct. Xylem broad, consisting of vessels and xylem parenchymatous cells, lignified; xylem vessels radially arranged; xylem rays 1-7 rows of cells wide, mostly radially elongated. Pith large, parenchymatous cells subrounded or polygonal, often broken or hollow in the centre [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-yellow, yellowish-brown or dark brown. Stone cells abundant, singly scattered or in groups, pale yellowish-green, subrounded, subsquare, long stripe or irregular in shape, 36-525 $ \\mu $ m long, 21-149 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, wall heavily thickened, lignified, lumens relatively small, walls 7-28 $ \\mu $ m thick, with striations, pit canals and pits distinct; dark brown to black masses sometimes visible around and inside; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Fibres elongated, fusiform, singly scattered or in bundles, wall slightly thickened, lignified, with few simple pits, pit canals distinct, 11-65 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; white or bluish-white under the polarized microscope. Inulin occasionally found, fan-shaped or irregular, sometimes visible in parenchymatous cells, with weak radial striations; pale bluish-white under the polarized microscope. Fragments of secretory canals contain yellowish-brown secretions, with brown masses scattered around. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 12-150 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[631, 42, 772, 113])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 48, 1853, 97])\n\n苦木\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[254, 447, 1720, 2083])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cell D. Secretory canal\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Stone cell 4. Secretory canal 5. Endodermis 6. Fibre bundle\n\n7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 45, 1970, 247])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[245, 664, 1711, 2094])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Root-trace vascular bundle 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Stone cell\n\n5. Fibre bundle 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[632, 42, 772, 114])\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1722, 48, 1852, 97])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[303, 609, 734, 1005])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[773, 609, 1208, 1008])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1244, 610, 1679, 1007])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[306, 1060, 736, 1456])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[773, 1059, 1207, 1457])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1247, 1060, 1682, 1454])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[307, 1506, 738, 1903])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[773, 1512, 1207, 1903])\n\n50μm"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Stone cell 2. Fibre 3. Inulin 4. Fragments of secretory canals\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 42, 1945, 240])"},{"name":"13","text":"Euparin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of euparin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether $ ( 6 0-8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}) $ and ethyl acetate (9.4:0.6, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Collect the supernatant."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately euparin standard solution and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 1799, 706, 1977])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of euparin\n\n</div>\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 372, 878, 1209])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Euparin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of euparin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Euparin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of euparin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[23, 41, 1950, 238])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (240 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 90 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 14.8% carbon loading). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>90→80</td><td>10→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>80→60</td><td>20→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-25</td><td>60→45</td><td>40→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-60</td><td>45→20</td><td>55→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of euparin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of euparin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of euparin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from euparin peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject euparin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of euparin peak in the chromatogram of euparin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify euparin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of euparin Std-FP. The retention times of euparin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 43, 1957, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,euparin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[330, 807, 1815, 1434])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 9.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/eupatorii-chinensis-radix-et-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma","la":"Eupatorii Chinensis Radix et Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/eupatorii-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eupatorii Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[598, 934, 1360, 1257])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[512, 1299, 1464, 1846])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[598, 934, 1360, 1257])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[512, 1299, 1464, 1846])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Eupatorii Herba\n\nChinese Name: 佩蘭\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Peilan"},{"name":"14","text":"Eupatorii Herba is the dried aerial part of Eupatorium fortunei Turcz. (Asteraceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Eupatorii Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem cylindrical, up to 100 cm long, 2-6 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown or yellowish green, sometimes purple, with distinct nodes and longitudinal ridges; texture fragile; fracture white or hollow in pith. Leaves opposite, with petiole, lamina mostly crumpled and broken, green to brownish green. Lamina trifid or non-divided when intact and flattened; trifid lamina middle lobe larger, lobes lanceolate or oblong-lanceolate, base narrow, margin serrate; non-divided lamina ovate, ovate lanceolate or elliptical. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of rectangular-shaped cells, arranged orderly. Collenchyma underneath the epidermis, consisting of 2-3 layers of cells. Cortex consists of 3-11 layers of parenchymatous cells. Collateral vascular bundles 20-30, arranged in ring. Phloem fibres located outside the phloem arranged in a half-moon shape, lignified. Phloem narrow. Cambium consists of 2-3 layers of cells, arranged in a ring. Xylem broad. Pith large, occupied about 2/3 of stem [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[67, 9, 1966, 260])\n\nPetiole: Half-moon shaped. Vascular bundles 10-20, varied in size, arranged in a V-shaped. Non-glandular hair 3- to 10-celled, presents occasionally. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subrectangular cells, arranged orderly. Collenchyma underneath epidermis, consisting of 2-4 layers of cells. Ground parenchyma consists of parenchymatous cells. Xylem broad. Phloem narrow [Fig 2 (ii)].\n\nLeaf: Thin, tissue weakly differentiated. Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of subrectangular cells, arranged orderly. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of short cylindrical cells. Xylem broad. Phloem narrow. Spongy tissue loosely arranged. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer subrectangular cells, arranged orderly [Fig 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour yellowish-green to greenish-brown. Upper epidermal cells slightly sinuous in surface view. Lower epidermal cells sinuous in surface view, accompany with stomata, stomata anomocytic, 21-42 $ \\mu $m long and 15-29 $ \\mu $m wide. Non-glandular hairs 3- to 10-celled, occasionally found, mostly fragmented. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles, 9-26 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stem vessels spiral, scalariform, reticulate and bordered-pitted (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[39, 100, 203, 156])\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 416, 1732, 1864])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Eupatorii Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Cortex 4. Phloem fibres\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nEupatorii Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[244, 440, 1715, 1822])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of petiole of Eupatorii Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Collenchyma 4. Ground parenchyma\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[41, 9, 1909, 264])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[236, 405, 1723, 2169])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Eupatorii Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Xylem 4. Spongy tissue 5. Phloem 6. Lower epidermis\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\nEupatorii Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[291, 450, 1679, 2138])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Upper epidermal cells of leaf 2. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with stomata\n\n3. Non-glandular hair 4. Fibre 5. Spiral vessel of stem 6. Scalariform vessel of stem\n\n7. Reticulate vessel of stem 8. Bordered-pitted vessels of stem\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 8, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"14","text":"Coumarin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of coumarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (6:3:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL separating funnel. Extract for two times each with 20 mL of n-hexane and discard the n-hexane layer. Combine the methanol extracts."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately coumarin standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of coumarin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1778, 804, 1998])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of coumarin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[69, 11, 1969, 260])"},{"name":"14","text":"Coumarin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of coumarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (275 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.5% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-50</td><td>80→10</td><td>20→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>10</td><td>90</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of coumarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of coumarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of coumarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from coumarin peak should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[22, 5, 1903, 264])"},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject coumarin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of coumarin peak in the chromatogram of coumarin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify coumarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of coumarin Std-FP. The retention times of coumarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Eupatorii Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Eupatorii Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,coumarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.06</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[292, 1475, 1845, 1965])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Eupatorii Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[67, 14, 1964, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 22.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/eupatorii-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eupatorii Herba","la":"Eupatorii Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/euphorbiae-hirtae-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[379, 861, 1469, 1829])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba\n\nA. Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba (root $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nB. Upper surface of leaves and flowers on an intact stem\n\nC. Lower surface of leaves and flowers on an intact stem\n\nD. Magnified image of capitulum\n\nE. Magnified image of seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[379, 861, 1469, 1829])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba\n\nA. Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba (root $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nB. Upper surface of leaves and flowers on an intact stem\n\nC. Lower surface of leaves and flowers on an intact stem\n\nD. Magnified image of capitulum\n\nE. Magnified image of seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 259])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official name: Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba\n\nChinese name: 飛揚草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Feiyangcao"},{"name":"15","text":"Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba is the dried whole plant of Euphorbia hirta L. (Euphorbiaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer and autumn, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Main root slightly curved, bearing numerous rootlets. Stem subcylindrical, 15-55 cm long, 1-3 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish to brown, texture fragile, easily broken, hollow in the centre; aerial part covered by long hairs. Leaves opposite, crumpled, elliptic ovate to sub-rhombic when flattened out, 1-4 cm long, 0.5-1.3 cm wide; the upper surface greenish-brown, the lower surface pale greenish-brown, apex acute, base oblique, margin serrulate, 3 veins relatively distinct. Cymes numerous, head-like, axillary. Seeds ovate-triangle. Odour slight; taste slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Root: Cork consists of 2-8 layers of cells, containing reddish-brown contents and microcrystals of calcium oxalate. Cortex relatively narrow, few cells containing microcrystals of calcium oxalate. Phloem consists of several layers of cells. Laticiferous tubes scattered around the phloem. Cambium indistinct. Xylem occuping the majority of root, completely lignified, rays indistinct [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged in a ring, with multicellular nonglandular hairs on the surface. Cortex wide, scattered with laticiferous tubes. Laticiferous tubes scattered or aggregated, mainly observed beside phloem, sometimes scattered in cortex. Phloem narrow. Phloem fibres and laticiferous tubes found on the lateral part of phloem, arranged in an interrupt ring. Cambium indistinct. Xylem vessels lignified, linked up in a ring. Pith visible, parenchymatous cells subrounded and large [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 37, 1953, 251])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, rectangular to polygonal, containing pale brown to brown pigment granules (before staining). Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells. Spongy tissue consists of 2-3 layers of cells. Vascular bundle of midrib has large bundles sheaths, collateral. Laticiferous tubes scattered around the vascular bundle and phloem. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells. Multicellular non-glandular hairs raised from the upper and lower epidermis. Lower epidermal cells relatively small [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour pale yellow. Multicellular non-glandular hair consists of 2-6 cells, apical 2 cells relatively long with protuberance on the surface, 2-30 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Pollen grains pale yellow, subrounded, 5-26 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, smooth surface. Vessels 2-76 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, mainly spiral vessels; reticulate and scalariform vessels also visible. Epidermal cells of stem polygonal, some with yellow and brown contents. Phloem fibres mostly singly scattered, long fusiform, 2-27 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with thick and lignified walls, pits indistinct; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells colourless to pale brown, subpolygonal to subsquare on surface view, cork cells wall 1-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in thickness. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate scattered, 2-31 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Palisade cells of testa in groups, colourless in the lateral view; 1-2 rows, slender cylindrical, arranged densely. Lower epidermal cells of leaf wavy and curved around the anticlinal wall, stomata mostly anisocytic (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[23, 43, 1968, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 417, 1728, 2117])\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Microcrystal of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Microcrystal of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope) E. Laticiferous tube\n\n1. Cork 2. Microcrystal of calcium oxalate 3. Cortex 4. Laticiferous tube\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1939, 138])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[234, 415, 1724, 2120])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Phloem fibres D. Laticiferous tube\n\n1. Multicellular non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Phloem fibre\n\n5. Phloem 6. Laticiferous tube 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 45, 1966, 256])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[234, 421, 1741, 2110])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Laticiferous tubes\n\nD. Microcrystal of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Microcrystal of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Vascular bundle sheath\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Laticiferous tube 8. Microcrystal of calcium oxalate\n\n9. Multicellular non-glandular hair 10. Lower epidermis\n\n4 ___\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 44, 1962, 246])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[276, 514, 1685, 2033])"},{"name":"15","text":"1. Multicellular non-glandular hair 2. Pollen grains\n\n3. Vessels (3-1 spiral vessel, 3-2 reticulate vessel, 3-3 scalariform vessel)\n\n4. Epidermal cells of stem 5. Phloem fibre\n\n6. Cork cells 7. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 8. Palisade cells of testa\n\n9. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with stoma ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 45, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"15","text":"$ \\beta $ -Amyrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of $ \\beta $ - amyrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nLupeol standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of lupeol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and formic acid (6:3:3, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Add slowly 5 mL of sulphuric acid to 95 mL of ethanol and dissolve 5 g of vanillin."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC RP-18 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4 \\mathrm{S}} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately $ \\beta $ - amyrin standard solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) , lupeol standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 10 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots and bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[19, 38, 1951, 252])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[377, 432, 864, 838])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 960, 806, 1325])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) $ \\beta $ -amyrin and (ii) lupeol\n\n</div>\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[378, 374, 801, 1210])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. $ \\beta $ -Amyrin standard solution 2. Lupeol standard solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of $ \\beta $ - amyrin and lupeol (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[21, 40, 1946, 241])"},{"name":"15","text":"$ \\beta $ -Amyrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of $ \\beta $ -amyrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol.\n\nLupeol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of lupeol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 45 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction two more times each with 7 mL of ethanol. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (202 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (3.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 130 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and $ 1 8 5 \\mathrm{m}^{2} / \\mathrm{g} $ surface area). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.7 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.01% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>71→76</td><td>29→24</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-60</td><td>76→62</td><td>24→38</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of $ \\beta $ - amyrin Std-FP and lupeol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of $ \\beta $ - amyrin and lupeol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of $ \\beta $ - amyrin and lupeol peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from $ \\beta $ - amyrin and lupeol peaks should not be less than 4000 and 9000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject $ \\beta $ -amyrin Std-FP,lupeol Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of $ \\beta $ -amyrin and lupeol peaks in the chromatograms of $ \\beta $ -amyrin Std-FP,lupeol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify $ \\beta $ -amyrin and lupeol peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of $ \\beta $ -amyrin Std-FP and lupeol Std-FP. The retention times of $ \\beta $ -amyrin and lupeol peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(lupeol)</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,β-amyrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[337, 1647, 1813, 2261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[1690, 56, 1940, 138])"},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/euphorbiae-hirtae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba","la":"Euphorbiae Hirtae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/euphorbiae-pekinensis-radix-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[374, 832, 1565, 1948])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n</div>\n\nA. Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\nB. Magnified image of fracture surface of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[374, 832, 1565, 1948])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n</div>\n\nA. Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\nB. Magnified image of fracture surface of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\nChinese name: 京大戟\n\nChinese phonetic name: Jingdaji"},{"name":"12","text":"Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix is the dried root of Euphorbia pekinensis Rupr. (Euphorbiaceae). The root is collected during autumn and winter, washed clean, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregularly long-conical, slightly curved, some with branches, 4.5-25.6 cm long, 2-25 mm in diameter; the apex usually enlarged, with stem scars and remnants of stem base. Externally greyish-yellow to greyish-brown, rough, loose with longitudinal wrinkles and lateral lenticels; branch root few and twisted. Texture hard, uneasily broken, fracture greyish-white to yellowish-brown, fibrous. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of rectangular flat cells, outer layers usually broken. Cortex narrow. Laticiferous tubes abundant in phloem. Cambium distinct, arranged in a ring. Xylem relatively broad; vessels singly scattered or several in bundles, radially arranged, surrounded by fibres. Rays broad. Clusters of calcium oxalates in rosette aggregates and scattered in parenchyma (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 34, 1954, 247])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Single starch granules subspheroid or ovoid, 3-22 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum pointed or slit-shaped; compound starch granules composed of 2-4 units; distinct black cruciate shape under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4-28 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized light microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, 5-45 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, in rosette aggregates; polychromatic under the polarized light microscope. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles, walls thick but non-lignified, 6-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Bordered-pitted and reticulate vessels, usually broken, 6-92 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Non-articulate laticiferous tubes usually broken, containing yellowish and minute-granular inclusions. Cork cells polygonal (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[633, 42, 771, 113])\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 49, 1852, 97])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 449, 1731, 2018])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) E. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Laticiferous tube 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n7. Ray 8. Clusters of calcium oxalate 9. Fibre\n\n野老鸛草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[751, 145, 907, 186])\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1938, 138])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[255, 470, 1723, 2094])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Fibres 5. Vessels (5-1 bordered-pitted vessels, 5-2 reticulate vessels)\n\n6. Laticiferous tube 7. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Euphol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of euphol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (10:1.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately euphol standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 41, 1951, 242])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[371, 368, 932, 677])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of euphol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 839, 757, 1672])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Euphol standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of euphol (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 43, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Euphol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (90 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.5 mg of euphol CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times1 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with OS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>60→70</td><td>40→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>70→85</td><td>30→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>85→95</td><td>15→5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>95→99</td><td>5→1</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of euphol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of euphol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of euphol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from euphol peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject euphol Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of euphol peak in the chromatogram of euphol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify euphol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of euphol Std-FP. The retention times of euphol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.31</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.98</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,euphol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[332, 1578, 1816, 2209])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/euphorbiae-pekinensis-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix","la":"Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/euphorbiae-semen-unprocessed-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Euphorbiae Semen unprocessed v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[732, 890, 1012, 1093])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1152, 919, 1304, 1082])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[634, 1228, 1366, 1822])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed)\n\nA. Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of seeds\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of seed\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 23, 1879, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[732, 890, 1012, 1093])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1152, 919, 1304, 1082])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[634, 1228, 1366, 1822])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed)\n\nA. Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of seeds\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of seed\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 23, 1879, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed)\n\nChinese Name: 千金子（生）\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qianjinzi (Sheng)"},{"name":"12","text":"Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) is the unprocessed dried ripe seed of Euphorbia lathyris L. (Euphorbiaceae). The seed is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried to obtain Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed)."},{"name":"12","text":"Ellipsoidal or obovoid, 4.3-5.7 mm long, 3.3-4.7 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown, with irregular reticulate wrinkles, reticulate pits greyish-black, forming fine spots. A longitudinal furrowed raphe located on one side, a protuberant chalaza at the apex, a linear hilum at the lower end, with a whitish protuberant caruncle or its scar at the base. Testa thin and fragile, kernel white to yellowishwhite, oily. Odour slight (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermal cells of testa undulated, with relatively thickened outer wall, containing brown contents. Hypodermis consists of 1-3 layers of parenchymatous cells. Inner epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquared palisade cells, the inner part of lateral walls and inner walls prominently thickened. Endotesta consists 1 layer of brown palisade cells, the cells slender cylindrical, with thickened and lignified wall, pits occasionally visible. Perisperm consists of several layers of subsquare parenchymatous cells. Endosperm cells subrounded. Cotyledons cells squared or rectangular. Endosperm and cotyledons cells contain aleurone grains (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 2, 1947, 255])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour dark brown. Endotesta cells brown or dark brown, cells slender and columnar in lateral view, arranged densely, slightly curved, lower parts gradually smaller, endings truncate or obtuse, 72-281 $ \\mu $ m long, 9-22 $ \\mu $ m wide, walls 3-9 $ \\mu $ m thick, pit canals slender and sparse, lumens relatively wide, containing reddish-brown or dark brown contents; cells subrounded in surface view. Inner epidermal cells rectangular or subsquare in lateral view, 9-20 $ \\mu $ m wide, arranged in short palisade-like pattern, outer radial walls thin and relatively curved, inwards and inner walls thickened, walls 1-2 $ \\mu $ m thick; cells polygonal in surface view, arranged densely, walls relatively thickened, without cell interspace. Epidermal cells of testa elliptic or semicircular, slightly papillae or tomentose protuberance in shape, outer walls relatively thickened, lumens usually containing yellowish-brown or reddish-brown contents, some without pigment. Endosperm cells subrounded, 36-63 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, walls thin, lumens containing rounded or minute granular aleurone grains, also contain fatty oil droplets. Perisperm cells extremely rare, subpolygonal, walls relatively thick. Cotyledon cells pale yellowish-green, containing granular aleurone grains and fatty oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[34, 22, 1888, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 449, 1743, 2071])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis of testa 2. Hypodermis 3. Inner epidermis 4. Endotesta 5. Perisperm\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledons 8. Aleurone grains\n\nNelumbinis Receptaculum\n\n蓮房\n\nCirsii Japonici Herba\n\n大薊\n\nFritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederle Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed)\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[498, 435, 971, 856])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1004, 442, 1475, 854])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[499, 897, 972, 1240])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1004, 897, 1481, 1238])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[498, 1277, 971, 1648])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1003, 1280, 1484, 1649])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[497, 1679, 976, 1975])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1004, 1680, 1485, 1975])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Endotesta cells (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 in surface view) 2. Inner epidermal cells (in surface view)\n\n3. Epidermal cells of testa 4. Endosperm cells 5. Cotyledon cells 6. Aleurone grains 7. Fatty oil droplets\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 25, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Euphorbia factor $ L_{3} $ standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Equal amount of kieselguhr are added to the sample before powdering. Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[379, 1586, 840, 1956])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 10, 1948, 255])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 375, 855, 1248])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Euphorbia factor $ L_{3} $ standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $"},{"name":"12","text":"Equal amount of kieselguhr are added to the sample before powdering. Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (500 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with 5 mL of methanol. Filter and combine the filtrates. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[34, 13, 1889, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-25</td><td>70→74</td><td>30→26</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-31</td><td>74→90</td><td>26→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ peak should not be less than 9000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ peak in the chromatogram of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ Std-FP. The retention times of euphorbia factor $ \\mathrm{L}_{3} $ peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 9, 1946, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.58</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,euphorbia factorL3)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[384, 1067, 1770, 1652])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Euphorbiae Semen (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[40, 26, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 6.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/euphorbiae-semen-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Euphorbiae Semen unprocessed","la":"Euphorbiae Semen unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/farfarae-flos-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Farfarae Flos v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[481, 897, 1477, 1823])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Farfarae Flos\n\nA. Whole pieces of Farfarae Flos B. Transverse section\n\nC. After ripping of the bracts, the inner surface shows a dense cover of white flocky hairs\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[35, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[481, 897, 1477, 1823])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Farfarae Flos\n\nA. Whole pieces of Farfarae Flos B. Transverse section\n\nC. After ripping of the bracts, the inner surface shows a dense cover of white flocky hairs\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[35, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Farfarae Flos\n\nChinese Name: 款冬花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Kuandonghua"},{"name":"13","text":"Farfarae Flos is the dried flower bud of Tussilago farfara L. (Asteraceae). The flower bud is collected in December or before the ground is frozen while the flower is still under the ground, pedicels and soil removed, then dried in the shade to obtain Farfarae Flos."},{"name":"13","text":"Elongated-clavate. Solitary or 2-3 accreted at the base, 1-2.5 cm long, 5-10 mm in diameter. The upper part broader; the lower part gradually slender toward the base or with short pedicel, enclosed by numerous scaly bracts. Bracts externally purplish-red or pale red and the inner surface densely covered with white flocky hairs. Bud light in weight, showing white tomentose when ripped. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Rachis: Epidermis of rachis consists of 1 layer of subrounded or subsquare cells, covered with unevenly thickened cuticle. Cortex consists of 15-30 layers of subrounded parenchymatous cells. Endodermis sometimes visible. Vascular bundles arranged in a ring, frequently with secretory duct located closely to the top of each bundle, while sclerenchymatous cells usually scattered between the secretory duct and the phloem. Pith mainly consists of parenchymatous cells.\n\nBract: The upper epidermal cells of the bract subrounded, covered with cuticle; the lower ones subrectangular. A secretory duct located closely to the top of vascular bundle (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale red. Pollen grains yellow, subspherical, 25-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, some with fragments of anther. Inulin fairly abundant, with radial striations. Non-glandular hairs consist of 1-4 cells, abundant, stripe-shaped or twisted into masses, varying in length (occasionally up to 510 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ ). Epidermal cells of bract subrectangular or polygonal in surface view, with intermittent fine stripes, stomata anomocytic, subsidiary cells 4-7. Glandular hairs pestle-like in shape, varying in length (occasionally up to 216 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ ), 16-52 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; head 4- to 6-celled, expanded, ellipsoidal; base consists of 2 columns side by side, several to more than 10 cells (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 4, 1908, 266])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 438, 1737, 2062])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Farfarae Flos A. Sketch of rachis B. Section illustration of rachis C. Vascular bundles and secretory ducts D. Section illustration of bract\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Secretory duct 4. Bract trace vascular bundle\n\n5. Endodermis 6. Sclerenchymatous cells 7. Phloem 8. Xylem\n\n9. Pith 10. Cuticle 11. Upper epidermis of bract\n\n12. Lower epidermis of bract\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nFarfarae Flos Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[247, 666, 1718, 2084])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Farfarae Flos (under the light microscope)\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. Pollen grains and fragments of anther 3. Inulins\n\n4. Non-glandular hairs 5. Epidermal cells of bract with stomata\n\n6. Glandular hairs\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 6, 1903, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Tussilagone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of tussilagone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately tussilagone standard solution ( $ 2\\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution ( $ 8\\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of tussilagone.\n\nma 餘甘子 Phyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繽斷\n\nFarfarae Flos Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 367, 1025, 821])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of tussilagone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Tussilagone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of tussilagone CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The internal diameter of the inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 4, 1904, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>90-64</td><td>10-36</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>64-10</td><td>36-90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>10-5</td><td>90-95</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of tussilagone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of tussilagone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of tussilagone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from tussilagone peak should not be less than 250000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject tussilagone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of tussilagone peak in the chromatogram of tussilagone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify tussilagone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of tussilagone Std-FP. The retention times of tussilagone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Farfarae Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 7, 1967, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Farfarae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.52</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,tussilagone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.08</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[298, 885, 1833, 1617])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Farfarae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[51, 13, 1901, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 11.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 47.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 29.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/farfarae-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Farfarae Flos","la":"Farfarae Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fibraureae-caulis-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fibraureae Caulis v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1 A photograph of Fibraureae Caulis A. Fibraureae Caulis B. Oblique slices C. Magnified image of transverse section of stem"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"Figure 1 A photograph of Fibraureae Caulis A. Fibraureae Caulis B. Oblique slices C. Magnified image of transverse section of stem"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Fibraureae Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 黄藤\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Huangteng"},{"name":"14","text":"Fibraureae Caulis is the dried lianoid stem of Fibraurea recisa Pierre (Menispermaceae). The lianoid stem is collected in autumn and winter, cut into section when fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Fibraureae Caulis."},{"name":"14","text":"Long cylindrical, slightly twisted, 6-30 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown to yellowish-brown rough, with longitudinal furrows and transverse cracks, outer bark of the old stem easily fallen off. Texture hard, uneasily broken, dusting on breaking, fracture uneven, yellow, fibrous, showing radially arranged striations alternated with brownish-yellow and yellowish-brown colour, pores of vessels fine, xylem sometimes with cracks. Centre yellowish-brown or hollowed. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Cork consists of numerous layers of cells containing brown contents, sometimes the walls lignified and thickened. Cortex consists of several layers of flattened cells scattered with stone cell groups. Vascular bundles collateral. Fibres in bundle, scattered on the outside of phloem and inside of xylem; the ones outside form a sharply undulated ring circling the stem. Vessels large, up to about 30 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Xylem rays broad. Pith broad. Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered in the stone cells or in the neighboring cells of stone cells and fibre bundles. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花"},{"name":"14","text":"Colour pale yellow. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups, subsquare or polygonal, 40-120 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells yellowish-brown, subpolygonal in surface view, some with lignified and thickened walls. Woody fibres mostly in bundles, with thick walls. Vessels mostly bordered-pitted, usually broken, intact vessels up to 150 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Medullary ray cells rectangular. Prisms of calcium oxalate 20-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Starch granules subrounded or oblong, hilum pointed, usually compound starch granules; black and cruciated-shaped under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1267, 193, 1388, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[254, 527, 917, 1283])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[309, 1313, 917, 1594])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[310, 1644, 915, 1924])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[973, 558, 1658, 1925])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Fibraureae Caulis A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) D. Prisms of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Fibre 4. Phloem 5. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n6. Stone cell 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Pith\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\nFibraureae Caulis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[413, 449, 975, 819])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[992, 444, 1562, 814])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[410, 834, 976, 1196])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[991, 829, 1561, 1204])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[418, 1216, 982, 1593])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[996, 1219, 1563, 1581])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[414, 1607, 977, 1974])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[992, 1600, 1285, 1974])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1299, 1607, 1567, 1972])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Fibraureae Caulis\n\n1. Stone cells (1-1 with prisms of calcium oxalate 1-2 in group)\n\n2. Cork cells 3. Fibres 4. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n5. Medullary ray cells 6. Prisms of calcium oxalate 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[60, 34, 1899, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Jatrorrhizine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of jatrorrhizine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nPalmatine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of palmatine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, water and glacial acetic acid (7:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 20 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately jatrorrhizine chloride standard solution, palmatine chloride standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[372, 383, 960, 731])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 802, 972, 1149])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) jatrorrhizine chloride and (ii) palmatine chloride\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 1355, 896, 2082])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Fibraureae Caulis extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Jatrorrhizine chloride standard solution 2. Palmatine chloride standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of jatrorrhizine and palmatine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Jatrorrhizine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of jatrorrhizine chloride CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of a mixture of methanol and hydrochloric acid (99:1, v/v).\n\nPalmatine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of palmatine chloride CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of a mixture of methanol and hydrochloric acid (99:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of a mixture of methanol and hydrochloric acid (99:1, v/v). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (225 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP and palmatine chloride Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of jatrorrhizine and palmatine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks should not be less than 8000 and 3000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 35, 1936, 263])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP, palmatine chloride Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks in the chromatograms of jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP, palmatine chloride Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP and palmatine chloride Std-FP. The retention times of jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fibraureae Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fibraureae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(jatrorrhizine)</td><td>0.76</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, palmatine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.20</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.49</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[329, 372, 1794, 903])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Fibraureae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[39, 38, 1939, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"14","text":"Mixed jatrorrhizine chloride and palmatine chloride standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L for jatrorrhizine chloride and 800 mg/L for palmatine chloride)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of jatrorrhizine chloride CRS and 4.0 mg of palmatine chloride CRS, and dissolve in 5 mL of a mixture of methanol and hydrochloric acid (99:1, v/v).\n\nMixed jatrorrhizine chloride and palmatine chloride standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed jatrorrhizine chloride and palmatine chloride Std-Stock, dilute with a mixture of methanol and hydrochloric acid (99:1, v/v) to produce a series of solutions of 10, 30, 40, 60, 100 mg/L for jatrorrhizine chloride and 120, 160, 200, 240, 400 mg/L for palmatine chloride."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fibraureae-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fibraureae Caulis","la":"Fibraureae Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fici-pumilae-receptaculum-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fici Pumilae Receptaculum v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[369, 820, 1588, 1916])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum\n\n</div>\n\nA. Longitudinal section of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum (elongated-elliptical trough shape)\n\nB. Longitudinal section of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum (obovoid-conic gourd shape)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[29, 45, 1964, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[369, 820, 1588, 1916])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum\n\n</div>\n\nA. Longitudinal section of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum (elongated-elliptical trough shape)\n\nB. Longitudinal section of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum (obovoid-conic gourd shape)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[29, 45, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Fici Pumilae Receptaculum\n\nChinese name: 廣東王不留行\n\nChinese phonetic name: Guangdongwangbuliuxing"},{"name":"13","text":"Fici Pumilae Receptaculum is the dried receptacle of inflorescence of Ficus pumila L. (Moraceae). The receptacle of inflorescence is collected in autumn before it turns dark brown and splits open, peduncle cut off, cut into 2-4 longitudinal sections, achenes removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Fici Pumilae Receptaculum."},{"name":"13","text":"Obovoid-conic or elongated-elliptical, gourd-shaped or trough-shaped, 1.9-6.8 cm long, 1.3-4.5 cm wide, 1-14 mm thick. Externally greyish-yellow, yellowish-brown, yellowish-green or dark brown, shrunken. Internally greyish-yellow, yellowish-brown, reddish-brown or dark brown, remnants of uncleaned withered flower or elongated-spherical fruits often found. Apex truncated, with a small hole protrudes at the centre, inside filled with membranous bracteoles, outside often densely covered with fine yellowish-brown hairs. Base slightly small or handle-like, often with a short peduncle or peduncle scar. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Odour slight; taste bland, slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"1 layer of outer epidermal cells subsquare, subrectangular or subrounded, arranged orderly, walls thin, outer walls slightly thickened and cutinized, wavelike. Underneath the outer epidermis, 1-3 layers of collenchymatous cells arranged orderly, subsquare, some containing prisms of calcium oxalate or white masses. Collenchyma densely arranged, walls of cells slightly thickened at angles. Secretory cells scattered, containing reddish-brown masses. Collateral vascular bundles scattered, phloem parenchymatous cells densely arranged; xylem vessels radially arranged. Central spongy tissue broad, loosely arranged, walls slightly thickened. Inner epidermal cells rectangular, reddish-brown, non-glandular hairs present. Remnants of fruits often found (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 36, 1955, 246])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Collenchymatous cells subrounded to subsquare in shape, with thick walls; thickened angles bright white under the polarized microscope. Secretory cells abundant, subrounded or elongated-rounded, filled with yellowish-brown secretions. Prisms of calcium oxalate subsquare, present in collenchymatous cells, 2-20 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels small, mostly in bundles, mainly spiral type, scalariform and reticulate vessels also present, 4-32 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Spongy cells irregular in shape, often broken, wall slightly thickened. Non-glandular hairs unicellular or multicellular, base wide, apex acute, 72-1014 $ \\mu m $ long, 7-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[632, 42, 771, 113])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[325, 428, 942, 877])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[302, 959, 630, 1211])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[651, 962, 938, 1212])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[396, 1251, 951, 1648])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[342, 1677, 843, 2077])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[960, 434, 1746, 2076])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prism of calcium oxalate D. Non-glandular hair E. Vascular bundle F. Spongy tissue\n\n1. Outer epidermis 2. Prism of calcium oxalate 3. Collenchyma\n\n4. Secretory cell 5. Vascular bundle 6. Spongy tissue 7. Inner epidermis\n\n8. Non-glandular hair 9. Phloem 10. Xylem\n\n野老鸛草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1142, 134])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[750, 145, 906, 187])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1938, 137])\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[304, 636, 738, 1023])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[775, 634, 1211, 1027])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1248, 637, 1681, 1024])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[303, 1054, 741, 1445])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[774, 1055, 1212, 1447])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1246, 1060, 1680, 1441])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[302, 1484, 739, 1875])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[775, 1490, 1211, 1869])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1246, 1483, 1682, 1875])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Collenchymatous cells 2. Secretory cell 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Spiral vessels 5. Spongy cells 6. Unicellular non-glandular hair\n\n7. Multicellular non-glandular hair\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 44, 1964, 255])"},{"name":"13","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nRutin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rutin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, water and formic acid (20:3:3:1.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the supernatant to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (50%). Transfer the extract to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), rutin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[19, 39, 1950, 251])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 426, 1001, 796])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 1016, 1026, 1612])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid and (ii) rutin\n\n</div>\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n京大戦\n\nae Pekinensis Radix\n\n苦木 Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 371, 939, 1205])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chlorogenic acid standard solution 2. Rutin standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chlorogenic acid and rutin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nRutin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of rutin CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 38, 1951, 241])"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the supernatant to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the extract to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 120 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 11% carbon loading). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>98→95</td><td>2→5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>95→90</td><td>5→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>90→87</td><td>10→13</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-55</td><td>87→80</td><td>13→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-60</td><td>80→70</td><td>20→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and rutin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid and rutin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid and rutin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid and rutin peaks should not be less than 30000 and 140000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, rutin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid and rutin peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, rutin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid and rutin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and rutin Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid and rutin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,chlorogenic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(rutin)</td><td>2.10</td><td>±0.06</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.14</td><td>±0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[334, 1701, 1811, 2320])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Fici Pumilae Receptaculum extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fici-pumilae-receptaculum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fici Pumilae Receptaculum","la":"Fici Pumilae Receptaculum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/flos-magnoliae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flos Magnoliae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[478, 887, 1556, 1810])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Flos Magnoliae\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[478, 887, 1556, 1810])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Flos Magnoliae\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Flos Magnoliae\n\nChinese Name: 辛夷\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xinyi"},{"name":"11","text":"Flos Magnoliae is the dried flower bud of Magnolia biondii Pamp. (Magnoliaceae). The flower bud is collected in late winter to early spring, before the flower opens by removing it from the branchlet, then dried in a shaded area to obtain Flos Magnoliae."},{"name":"11","text":"The flower bud is silky-haired, elongated-ovoid, resembling the tip of a writing brush, 1.2-3.0 cm long, 8-16 mm in diameter. Usually the base with short pedicel, which exhibits whitish dotted lenticels. Bracts 2-3 layers, each layer consists of 2 segments, subtending small scaly buds between the 2 layers of bracts: outer surface of bracts densely covered with greyish-white, greyish-yellow or greyish-green pubescence, inner surface brownish, glabrous. Perianth consists of 9 brownish tepals; 3 tepals in the outermost whorl, linear, sepaloid, about 1/4 of the length of the inner 2 whorls; each of the 2 inner whorls consist of 3 tepals. Stamens and pistils numerous, spirally arranged. Texture light and fragile. Odour aromatic; taste pungent, cool and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"One layer of the epidermal cells, which resemble stone cells, are differentiated to form non-glandular hairs. The non-glandular hairs consist of 1-3 cells. A few groups of oil cells and stone cells are found in the cortex. Stone cells subround, fusiform or irregular, 34-206 $ \\mu $ m long, 16-99 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, mostly with striations. Vascular bundles arranged in a ring. A few groups of oil cells and stone cells are also found in the pith (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[25, 0, 1953, 250])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-green or pale yellowish-green. Non-glandular hairs numerous, scattered and frequently broken, consisting of 1-3 cells when whole, unicellular hairs are also found, cell wall 3-12 $ \\mu m $ thick, the basal cells short, thick and inflated, with extremely thickened wall, like that of stone cells; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Stone cells in groups, elliptical, irregular or branched, with a brownish-yellow secretion inside. Epidermal cells of the tepals flatsquared, anticlinal wall beaded; stoma present. Oil cells numerous, subround, oil droplets easily observed (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{1} $\n\nDissolve 0.85 g of bismuth subnitrate in 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water, then add 20 mL of aqueous potassium iodide solution (40%, w/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Transfer the solution to a test tube. Add 2 drops of potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{1}. $ An orange or orangish-brown precipitate is observed."},{"name":"11","text":"Fargesin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of fargesin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nMagnolin standard solution"},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and diethyl ether (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[405, 455, 943, 974])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[426, 1002, 936, 1429])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[985, 457, 1651, 1555])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[423, 1450, 940, 1883])\n\n100 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[985, 1563, 1640, 1885])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of pedicel of Flos Magnoliae\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Trichome (Non-glandular hairs) D. A group of stone cells\n\n1. Trichome (Non-glandular hairs) 2. Epidermis 3. Stone cells 4. Oil cells 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[13, 6, 1973, 252])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[378, 426, 992, 1005])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1024, 429, 1618, 995])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[376, 1030, 1108, 1538])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1129, 1032, 1617, 1528])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[377, 1557, 1005, 2045])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1027, 1560, 1619, 2052])\n\n100 μm"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Non-glandular hairs showing the base cells 2. Several group of stone cells 3. Stone cells\n\n4. Epidermal cells of tepals with stomata 5. Oil cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 0, 1966, 250])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min and then filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately fargesin standard solution, magnolin standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 70^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of fargesin and magnolin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[689, 1440, 1451, 1862])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[647, 1967, 1490, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) fargesin and (ii) magnolin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Magnolin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of magnolin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (280 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>60→45</td><td>40→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of magnolin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of magnolin should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of magnolin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from magnolin peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject magnolin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of magnolin peak in the chromatogram of the magnolin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify magnolin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of magnolin Std-FP. The retention times of magnolin peaks from the two chromatograms should\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1974, 251])\n\nnot differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Flos Magnoliae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Flos Magnoliae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, magnolin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.30</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>6 (fargesin)</td><td>1.50</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[397, 1301, 1759, 1787])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Flos Magnoliae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[25, 7, 1917, 246])\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/flos-magnoliae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flos Magnoliae","la":"Flos Magnoliae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/folium-ginkgo-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Folium Ginkgo v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[499, 854, 1526, 1792])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[499, 854, 1526, 1792])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Folium Ginkgo\n\nChinese Name: 銀杏葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yinxingye"},{"name":"16","text":"Folium Ginkgo is the dried leaf of Ginkgo biloba L. (Ginkgoaceae). The leaves are collected in autumn when they are still green, then dried immediately to obtain Folium Ginkgo."},{"name":"16","text":"The leaves are yellowish-green to pale brownish-yellow, fan-shaped, 3.5-12 cm long, 3-15 cm wide, 2-lobed, with fine, dichotomous-parallel veins radiating out from the base, often crumpled or broken. Margins at the apical side broadly rounded, irregularly undulate, those at the leaf base side entire. Petioles 2-8 cm long. Texture light. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"16","text":"Transverse section\n\nPetiole: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged orderly, covered with cuticle, sunken stomata occasionally visible. Collenchyma tissue present beneath the epidermis, mostly at the edges and corners of the petiole. Secretory canals are distributed in the peripheral region of the petiole. Cluster of calcium oxalate are present in the phloem. Vascular bundles collateral, in pairs (Fig. 2).\n\nLeaf Blade: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells covered with cuticle. A secretory canal always present and is located in-between two vascular bundles. The parenchyma cells contain chloroplasts, some with cluster of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundles distributed evenly in the leaf blade, surrounded by a few fibres. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, with sunken stomata visible (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1966, 250])"},{"name":"16","text":"Colour yellowish-green. Cluster of calcium oxalates scattered, 23-55 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, polychrome under the polarized microscope. Tracheids are bordered-pitted, occurring singly or bundled, 8-14 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Fibres dispersed singly or in bundles, 10-20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; Upper epidermal cells subrectangular with sinuate margin, up to 108 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length and 56 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in width. Sunken stomata appear beneath epidermis (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"16","text":"Bilobalide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of bilobalide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide B standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide C standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide C CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide J standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide J CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, acetone, methanol (10:8:8:0.6, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Acetic anhydride"},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (10%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the solution to a 50-mL conical flask. Rinse the residue with 5 mL of methanol (2%). Filter and transfer the washings to the same 50-mL conical flask. Load the solution to a solid-phase extraction column containing ODS packing (6 mL, 1000 mg) pre-conditioned with 10 mL of methanol and 10 mL of methanol (2%). Collect the eluant in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Add 10 mL of methanol (50%) to the extraction column and collect the eluant in the same 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 5 min . Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter.\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[860, 21, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 12, 1872, 83])\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 239])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[362, 437, 908, 807])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1066, 435, 1669, 829])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[290, 916, 927, 1132])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[959, 876, 1687, 1118])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[327, 1282, 828, 1778])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[943, 1303, 1650, 1775])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Folium Ginkgo\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch of petiole B. Section illustration of petiole C. Sketch of leaf blade D. Section illustration of leaf blade E. Vascular bundle in petiole F. Stomata on the lower epidermis\n\nF. Stomata on the lower epidermis\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Upper epidermis 3. Collenchyma tissue 4. Secretory canal\n\n5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Stoma 7. Xylem 8. Phloem\n\n9. Lower epidermis\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\n銀杏葉\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n燈心草\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 89])\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[956, 151, 1048, 202])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\n夏枯草\n\nSpica Prunellae\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Folium Ginkgo\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[318, 442, 965, 931])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1015, 439, 1671, 941])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[319, 993, 967, 1468])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1018, 991, 1670, 1480])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[315, 1545, 969, 2102])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1015, 1543, 1671, 2096])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Folium Ginkgo\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Tracheids 3. Fibres\n\n4. Upper epidermal cells 5. Stomata embedded in lower epidermis\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 0, 1944, 252])"},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel plate [immerse in sodium acetate solution (5% w/v) for 20 s. Dry the plate at $ 7 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Cool in a desiccator.], a twin trough chamber and freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately bilobalide standard solution, ginkgolide A standard solution, ginkgolide B standard solution, ginkgolide C standard solution and ginkgolide J standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with acetic anhydride vapour for about 15 min and heat at about $ 1 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for about 30 min. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[474, 1510, 844, 1810])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1162, 1542, 1607, 1779])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[479, 2028, 924, 2269])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1163, 2027, 1607, 2291])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) bilobalide (ii) ginkgolide A (iii) ginkgolide B (iv) ginkgolide C\n\n</div>\n\n北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1508, 7, 1924, 141])\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n( v )\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vi)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[438, 475, 891, 746])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1117, 475, 1642, 765])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(viii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[411, 975, 936, 1265])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1122, 926, 1644, 1264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (v) ginkgolide J (vi) quercetin (vii) kaempferol and (viii) isorhamnetin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"16","text":"Bilobalide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (540 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.4 mg of bilobalide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of ginkgolide A CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (240 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.4 mg of ginkgolide B CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide C standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (240 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.4 mg of ginkgolide C CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide J standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (180 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.8 mg of ginkgolide J CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"16","text":"Phosphate buffer solution\n\nWeigh 1.19 g of dibasic sodium phosphate and 8.25 g of monobasic potassium phosphate and dissolve in 1000 mL of water. Adjust the pH to 5.8 with phosphoric acid.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 0, 1944, 251])"},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol (90%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 500-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of phosphate buffer solution. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 5 min. Transfer the extract to a clean-up column filled with chromatographic siliceous earth capable of holding 20 mL of aqueous phase. Rinse the round-bottomed flask with two 5-mL portion of phosphate buffer solution. Transfer the solution to the clean-up column. After 15 min, elute with 100 mL of ethyl acetate. Collect the eluant in a 200-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol and transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Rinse the flask with 5 mL of water and transfer the solution to the same volumetric flask. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 15 min and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 0 2^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ) flow: 2.8 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>75→52</td><td>25→48</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bilobalide Std-FP, ginkgolide A Std-FP, ginkgolide B Std-FP, ginkgolide C Std-FP and ginkgolide J Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J peaks should not be less than 15000, 35000, 40000, 20000 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R values between bilobalide peak, ginkgolide A peak, ginkgolide B peak, ginkgolide C peak, ginkgolide J peak and their corresponding closest peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject bilobalide Std-FP, ginkgolide A Std-FP, ginkgolide B Std-FP, ginkgolide C Std-FP, ginkgolide J Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J peaks in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify the peaks of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing their retention times with those in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP. The retention times of the peaks of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[391, 1250, 1749, 1850])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Folium Ginkgo extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks (peak no.1: bilobalide; peak no.2: ginkgolide J; peak no.3: ginkgolide C; peak no.4: ginkgolide A; peak no.5: ginkgolide B) with retention times of the corresponding peaks in the Std-FP chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[57, 7, 1946, 250])"},{"name":"16","text":"Total ash: not more than 12.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 24.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/folium-ginkgo-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Folium Ginkgo v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[26, 5, 1927, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"22","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[26, 5, 1927, 256])"},{"name":"22","text":"Official Name: Folium Ginkgo\n\nChinese Name: 銀杏葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yinxingye"},{"name":"22","text":"Folium Ginkgo is the dried leaf of Ginkgo biloba L. (Ginkgoaceae). The leaves are collected in autumn when they are still green, then dried immediately to obtain Folium Ginkgo."},{"name":"22","text":"The leaves are yellowish-green to pale brownish-yellow, fan-shaped, 3.5-12 cm long, 3-15 cm wide, 2-lobed, with fine, dichotomous-parallel veins radiating out from the base, often crumpled or broken. Margins at the apical side broadly rounded, irregularly undulate, those at the leaf base side entire. Petioles 2-8 cm long. Texture light. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"22","text":"Petiole: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged orderly, covered with cuticle, sunken stomata occasionally visible. Collenchyma tissue present beneath the epidermis, mostly at the edges and corners of the petiole. Secretory canals are distributed in the peripheral region of the petiole. Cluster of calcium oxalate are present in the phloem. Vascular bundles collateral, in pairs (Fig. 2).\n\nLeaf Blade: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells covered with cuticle. A secretory canal always present and is located in-between two vascular bundles. The parenchyma cells contain chloroplasts, some with cluster of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundles distributed evenly in the leaf blade, surrounded by a few fibres. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, with sunken stomata visible (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[14, 15, 1976, 263])"},{"name":"22","text":"Colour yellowish-green. Cluster of calcium oxalates scattered, 23-55 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, polychrome under the polarized microscope. Tracheids are bordered-pitted, occurring singly or bundled, 8-14 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Fibres dispersed singly or in bundles, 10-20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; Upper epidermal cells subrectangular with sinuate margin, up to 108 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length and 56 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in width. Sunken stomata appear beneath epidermis (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"22","text":"Bilobalide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of bilobalide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide B standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide C standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide C CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinkgolide J standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginkgolide J CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, acetone, methanol (10:8:8:0.6, v/v).\n\nStaining reagent\n\nAcetic anhydride"},{"name":"22","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (10%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the solution to a 50-mL conical flask. Rinse the residue with 5 mL of methanol (2%). Filter and transfer the washings to the same 50-mL conical flask. Load the solution to a solid-phase extraction column containing ODS packing (6 mL, 1000 mg) pre-conditioned with 10 mL of methanol and 10 mL of methanol (2%). Collect the eluant in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Add 10 mL of methanol (50%) to the extraction column and collect the eluant in the same 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[170, 14, 327, 71])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[597, 15, 695, 68])\n\n延胡索 Corydalis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1692, 26, 1901, 108])\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 393, 1735, 1867])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Folium Ginkgo\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch of petiole B. Section illustration of petiole C. Sketch of leaf blade D. Section illustration of leaf blade E. Vascular bundle in petiole F. Stomata on the lower epidermis\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Upper epidermis 3. Collenchyma tissue 4. Secretory canal\n\n5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Stoma 7. Xylem 8. Phloem 9. Lower epidermis\n\nOrpiment\n\nCistanches Herba\n\nCurculiginis Rhizoma\n\n雄黄\n\nRealgar\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 47, 1984, 86])\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n五味子\n\nSchisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\n板蓝根\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[318, 445, 964, 933])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1018, 442, 1670, 942])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[319, 997, 965, 1473])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1019, 997, 1669, 1485])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[317, 1546, 970, 2103])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1017, 1544, 1670, 2098])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"22","text":"1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Tracheids 3. Fibres 4. Upper epidermal cells\n\n5. Stomata embedded in lower epidermis\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 0, 1936, 258])"},{"name":"22","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel plate [immerse in sodium acetate solution (5% , w/v) for 20 s. Dry the plate at $ 7 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Cool in a desiccator.], a twin trough chamber and freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately bilobalide standard solution, ginkgolide A standard solution, ginkgolide B standard solution, ginkgolide C standard solution and ginkgolide J standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with acetic anhydride vapour for about 15 min and heat at about $ 1 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for about 30 min. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[417, 1512, 788, 1813])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1104, 1543, 1551, 1786])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[423, 2033, 868, 2273])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1107, 2032, 1551, 2297])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) bilobalide (ii) ginkgolide A (iii) ginkgolide B (iv) ginkgolide C\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[25, 16, 1975, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n( v )\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[458, 469, 909, 738])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vi)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1097, 470, 1660, 759])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(viii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[430, 944, 954, 1232])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1139, 895, 1662, 1230])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (v) ginkgolide J (vi) quercetin (vii) kaempferol and (viii) isorhamnetin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"22","text":"Bilobalide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (540 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.4 mg of bilobalide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of ginkgolide A CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (240 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.4 mg of ginkgolide B CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide C standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (240 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.4 mg of ginkgolide C CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nGinkgolide J standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (180 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.8 mg of ginkgolide J CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"22","text":"Phosphate buffer solution\n\nWeigh 1.19 g of dibasic sodium phosphate and 8.25 g of monobasic potassium phosphate and dissolve in 1000 mL of water. Adjust the pH to 5.8 with phosphoric acid.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[41, 0, 1940, 258])"},{"name":"22","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol (90%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 500-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of phosphate buffer solution. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 5 min. Transfer the extract to a clean-up column filled with chromatographic siliceous earth capable of holding 20 mL of aqueous phase. Rinse the round-bottomed flask with two 5-mL portion of phosphate buffer solution. Transfer the solution to the clean-up column. After 15 min, elute with 100 mL of ethyl acetate. Collect the eluant in a 200-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol and transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Rinse the flask with 5 mL of water and transfer the solution to the same volumetric flask. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 15 min and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"22","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 0 2^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ) flow: 2.8 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>75→52</td><td>25→48</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"22","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bilobalide Std-FP, ginkgolide A Std-FP, ginkgolide B Std-FP, ginkgolide C Std-FP and ginkgolide J Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J peaks should not be less than 15000, 35000, 40000, 20000 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[11, 5, 1984, 262])\n\nThe R values between bilobalide peak, ginkgolide A peak, ginkgolide B peak, ginkgolide C peak, ginkgolide J peak and their corresponding closest peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"22","text":"Separately inject bilobalide Std-FP, ginkgolide A Std-FP, ginkgolide B Std-FP, ginkgolide C Std-FP, ginkgolide J Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J peaks in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify the peaks of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing their retention times with those in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP. The retention times of the peaks of bilobalide, ginkgolide A, ginkgolide B, ginkgolide C and ginkgolide J in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[387, 1355, 1747, 1970])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Folium Ginkgo extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks (peak no.1: bilobalide; peak no.2: ginkgolide J; peak no.3: ginkgolide C; peak no.4: ginkgolide A; peak no.5: ginkgolide B) with retention times of the corresponding peaks in the Std-FP chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 6, 1929, 255])"},{"name":"22","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 12.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"22","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 24.0%."},{"name":"22","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"22","text":"Standard solution\n\nMixed quercetin, kaempferol and isorhamnetin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L for both quercetin and kaempferol and 40 mg/L for isorhamnetin)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of quercetin CRS (Fig. 4), 5.0 mg of kaempferol CRS (Fig. 4), 2.0 mg of isorhamnetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed quercetin, kaempferol and isorhamnetin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed quercetin, kaempferol and isorhamnetin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 2.5, 10, 20, 30, 40 mg/L for both quercetin and kaempferol, and 1, 4, 8, 12, 16 mg/L for isorhamnetin.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[12, 12, 1984, 265])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/folium-ginkgo","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Folium Ginkgo","la":"Folium Ginkgo"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fraxini-cortex-v6-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fraxini Cortex v6 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[662, 695, 1375, 1327])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried branch bark and stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb.\n\nA. Branch bark (upper) and stem bark (lower)\n\nB. Magnified branch bark (left) and stem bark (right)\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[217, 1721, 915, 2408])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried branch bark and stem bark of Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh. A. Branch bark (upper) and stem bark (lower) B. Magnified branch bark (left) and stem bark (right)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1130, 1680, 1890, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried branch bark and stem bark of Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.\n\nA. Branch bark (upper) and stem bark (lower)\n\nB. Magnified branch bark (left) and stem bark (right)\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"17","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[662, 695, 1375, 1327])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried branch bark and stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb.\n\nA. Branch bark (upper) and stem bark (lower)\n\nB. Magnified branch bark (left) and stem bark (right)\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[217, 1721, 915, 2408])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried branch bark and stem bark of Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh. A. Branch bark (upper) and stem bark (lower) B. Magnified branch bark (left) and stem bark (right)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1130, 1680, 1890, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried branch bark and stem bark of Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.\n\nA. Branch bark (upper) and stem bark (lower)\n\nB. Magnified branch bark (left) and stem bark (right)\n\n</div>"},{"name":"17","text":"Official Name: Fraxini Cortex\n\nChinese Name: 秦皮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qinpi"},{"name":"17","text":"Fraxini Cortex is the dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb., Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh. or Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh. (Oleanaceae). The branch bark or stem bark is collected in spring and autumn, then dried under the sun to obtain Fraxini Cortex."},{"name":"17","text":"Fraxinus chinensis Roxb.: Quilled or channeled, 10-60 cm long, 1-3 mm thick. Externally greenishbrown to blackish-brown, not showing greyish-white patches of lichens. Purplish-red in the centre of the lenticels. Crescent leaf-scars visible. The inner surface yellowish-white to brown, smooth. Texture hard and fragile; fracture fibrous, yellowish-white. Odour slight; taste bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nFraxinus szaboana Lingelsh.: Quilled or channeled, 17-49 cm long, 1-3 mm thick. Externally greyish-white to greyish-brown, with distinct fine longitudinal wrinkles, showing greyish-white patches of lichens. Branch-scars opposite. Crescent leaf-scars visible. Odour slight; taste bitter [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\nFraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.: Quilled or channeled, 15-40 cm long, 1-2.9 mm thick. Externally greenish-brown to blackish-brown, occasionally showing greyish-white patches of lichens. Odour slight; taste relatively bitter [Fig. 1 (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Fraxinus chinensis Roxb.: Slat-shaped pieces or channeled, 2-6 mm thick. Externally shrunken obviously, the thick stem bark with numerous ramous cracks. Lenticels dotted or ovate, reddish-brown. Texture hard; fracture relatively fibrous [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 11, 1972, 259])\n\nFraxinus szaboana Lingelsh.: Slat-shaped pieces, 2-6 mm thick. Externally greyish-brown, with rimose furrows. Lenticels dotted or ovate, greyish-white in surrounding and reddish-brown in centre [Fig.1 (ii)].\n\nFraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.: Slat-shaped pieces, 2-6 mm thick. Externally greyish-brown, with rimose furrows and reddish-brown, rounded or transversely slender lenticels [Fig. 1 (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Transverse section\n\nBranch bark or stem bark:\n\nFraxinus chinensis Roxb.: Cork consists of 5-10 layers of cells, cells rectangular or squared. Phelloderm consists of several layers of polygonal collenchymatous cells. Cortex relatively broad, fibres and stone cells scattered singly or in groups. Phloem relatively broad, ring of sclerenchymatous cells composed of stone cells and fibre bundles located on the outside, occasionally interrupted; stone cells in the band relatively large, with distinct pit canals. Some bands composed of fibres and stone cells, located in parenchymatous cells of phloem and sieve tubes; two bands arranged alternately, interrupted by phloem rays. Phloem rays 1-4 rows of cells wide. Parenchymatous cells contain crystals of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nFraxinus szaboana Lingelsh.: Cortex relatively narrow. Groups of stone cells in the ring of sclerenchymatous cells located in the outside of phloem relatively well developed. Parenchymatous cells of phloem and sieve tubes relatively broad [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nFraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.: Groups of stone cells in the ring of sclerenchymatous cells located in the outside of phloem relatively well developed [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Colour greenish-brown or yellowish-white. Stone cells relatively abundant, scattered singly or in groups, subrounded, subsquare, subrectangular, elliptical, subfusiform or shortly and irregularly branched, 22-79 $ \\mu $m in diameter, 125-190 $ \\mu $m long, walls extremely thickened, pit canals distinct. Cork cells polygonal, walls slightly thickened and lignified, pits relatively sparse. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles, extremely long, mostly broken, straight or slightly curved, 10-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter, margin undulate or lumpy, walls extremely thickened and lignified, pits indistinct, lumens linear; bright brownish-white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Phloem ray 1-4 cells wide, lumens filled with microcrystals of calcium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[0, 3, 1981, 261])\n\noxalate; bright white or yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Crystals of calcium oxalate occur in parenchymatous cells of cork, phloem and ray cells, slightly fine fusiform or granular, up to 3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bright white under the polarized microscope [Fig. 3 (i), (ii) and (iii)].\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n夢金 Curcumae Radix\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nFraxini Cortex\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[340, 622, 904, 895])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[345, 1020, 906, 1395])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[343, 1554, 903, 1905])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[968, 576, 1693, 1937])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Phloem D. Ring of sclerenchymatous cells\n\n1. Cork 2. Phelloderm 3. Cortex 4. Ring of sclerenchymatous cells\n\n5. Phloem 6. Fibres 7. Sieve tubes and parenchymatous cells 8. Phloem rays\n\n9. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[244, 435, 1723, 2096])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Phloem D. Ring of sclerenchymatous cells\n\n1. Cork 2. Phelloderm 3. Cortex 4. Ring of sclerenchymatous cells\n\n5. Phloem 6. Fibres 7. Sieve tubes and parenchymatous cells 8. Phloem rays\n\n9. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\nRubi Fructus皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n彯金 Curcumae Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1071, 158, 1236, 212])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nFraxini Cortex\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[251, 392, 1741, 2041])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Phloem D. Ring of sclerenchymatous cells\n\n1. Cork 2. Phelloderm 3. Cortex 4. Ring of sclerenchymatous cells\n\n5. Phloem 6. Fibres 7. Sieve tubes and parenchymatous cells 8. Phloem rays\n\n9. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n胡黃連\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[255, 489, 1749, 2124])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Cork cells 3. Fibres 4. Phloem ray cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nGentianae Macrophyllae Radix\n\nRubi Fructus\n\nCelosiae Cristatae Flos雞冠花\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nCurcumae Radix\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1427, 102, 1591, 162])\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nCyathulae Radix川牛膝\n\nFraxini Cortex\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[448, 520, 1536, 2042])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh.\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Cork cells 3. Fibres 4. Phloem ray cells 5. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n水紅花子\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[265, 442, 1750, 2105])\n\nFigure 3 (iii) Microscopic features of powder of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Cork cells 3. Fibres 4. Phloem ray cells 5. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nFootnote: Microscopic features of powder have no significant differences between the dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb., Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh. and Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[553, 97, 715, 155])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1073, 157, 1235, 212])"},{"name":"17","text":"Esculetin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of esculetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nEsculin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of esculin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and formic acid (8:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately esculetin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), esculin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[437, 1892, 803, 2051])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[438, 2121, 818, 2433])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) esculetin and (ii) esculin\n\n</div>\n\nAcanthopanacis Cortex五加皮\n\nAlpiniae Oxyphyllae Fructus\n\nPolygoni Orientalis Fructus\n\n胡黃連\n\n水紅花子\n\nPicrorhizae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[379, 378, 1286, 1096])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3 (i) 3 (ii) 3 (iii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Fraxini Cortex extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Esculin standard solution\n\n2. Esculetin standard solution\n\n3. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb.\n\n(ii) dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh.\n\n(iii) dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of esculetin and esculin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"17","text":"Esculetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of esculetin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nEsculin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (105 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.1 mg of esculin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol (50%).\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[41, 8, 1976, 261])"},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"17","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (334 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 2 8^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-55</td><td>5→25</td><td>95→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-60</td><td>25→55</td><td>75→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of esculetin Std-FP and esculin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of esculetin and esculin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of esculetin and esculin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from esculetin and esculin peaks should not be less than 40000 and 30000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Separately inject esculetin Std-FP, esculin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of esculetin and esculin peaks in the chromatograms of esculetin Std-FP, esculin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify esculetin and esculin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of esculetin Std-FP and esculin Std-FP. The retention times\n\nof esculetin and esculin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fraxini Cortex extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fraxini Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(esculin)</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, esculetin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.03</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[379, 1200, 1807, 1764])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus chinensis Roxb. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[378, 1933, 1822, 2487])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus szaboana Lingelsh. extract\n\n</div>\n\n覆盆子\n\nRubi Fructus\n\n懜金 Curcumae Radix\n\nSennae Folium\n\n番瀉葉\n\n豬牙皂\n\n皂角刺 Gleditsiae Spina\n\nToosendan Fructus\n\nGleditsiae Fructus Abnormalis\n\nFraxini Cortex\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[384, 380, 1836, 915])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried branch bark or stem bark of Fraxinus stylosa Lingelsh. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 7.0%."},{"name":"17","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0% Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"17","text":"Mixed esculetin and esculin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (600 mg/L for esculetin and 2000 mg/L for esculin)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.2 mg of esculetin CRS and 4.0 mg of esculin CRS, and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nMixed esculetin and esculin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed esculetin and esculin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 1.5, 15, 30, 60, 150 mg/L for esculetin and 5, 50, 100, 200, 500 mg/L for esculin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fraxini-cortex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fraxini Cortex","la":"Fraxini Cortex"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fritillariae-cirrhosae-bulbus-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[650, 695, 1349, 1348])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried bulb of Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don A. Bulbs B. Magnified image of bulbs\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[647, 1717, 1379, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried bulb of Fritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia\n\n</div>\n\nA. Bulbs B. Magnified image of bulbs\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 17, 1878, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[650, 695, 1349, 1348])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried bulb of Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don A. Bulbs B. Magnified image of bulbs\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[647, 1717, 1379, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried bulb of Fritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia\n\n</div>\n\nA. Bulbs B. Magnified image of bulbs\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 17, 1878, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus\n\nChinese Name: 川貝母\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chuanbeimu"},{"name":"14","text":"Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus is the dried bulb of Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don, Fritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia (Liliaceae). The bulb is collected in summer and autumn, fibrous root, outer coarse bark and soil removed, then dried under the sun or at a lower temperature $ ( 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}-5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} ) $ to obtain Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus."},{"name":"14","text":"Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don: Subconical or subspherical, some nearly oblate, 0.4-1.4 cm high, 4-16 mm in diameter. Externally whitish. Outer scale leaves 2, varying considerably in size, with the large scale leaf closely embracing the small one, the uncovered part appearing crescent-shaped, commonly known as \"Huaizhong Baoyue\" (holding the moon in the arms). Apex closed, with subcylindrical and slightly tapered buds and 1-2 small scale leaves inside; the apex of the buds obtuse to slightly acute, the base even and slightly concave, with a greyish-brown disk at the central part, with remnants of fibrous roots occasionally attached. Texture hard and fragile. Fracture white, starchy. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nFritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia: Smaller in size, 0.3-0.8 cm high, 3-9 mm in diameter [Fig.1 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"The outer epidermis and inner epidermis of scale leaves consist of 1 layer of cells, the cells subsquared or subrectangular. Parenchymatous cells subrounded, filled with starch granules. Vessels small, scattered in the parenchyma [Fig. 2 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 4, 1946, 257])"},{"name":"14","text":"Colour whitish to pale yellow. Starch granules fairly abundant, broadly ovoid, long spheroidal or irregularly spheroidal, some with uneven or slightly branch-like edges, 5-64 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum short slit-shaped, pointed, V-shaped or U-shaped, with faint striations visible; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells subrectangular, anticlinal walls sinuous, anomocytic stomata occasionally found, rounded to oblate. Spiral vessels 5-46 $ \\mu $ m in diameter [Fig. 3 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 15, 1902, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[250, 449, 1745, 2087])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried bulb of Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration of scale leaf C. Starch granules (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Starch granules (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Outer epidermis 2. Parenchyma 3. Vessels 4. Inner epidermis 5. Big scale leaf 6. Small scale leaf\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 3, 1946, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[246, 425, 1746, 2111])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried bulb of Fritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration of scale leaf C. Starch granules (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Starch granules (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Outer epidermis 2. Parenchyma 3. Vessels 4. Inner epidermis 5. Big scale leaf 6. Small scale leaf\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 20, 1891, 257])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[249, 415, 1714, 2119])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried bulb of Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don 1. Starch granules 2. Epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\longrightarrow $ ） 3. Spiral vessel a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 10, 1953, 256])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[284, 727, 989, 1259])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[998, 728, 1704, 1257])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[280, 1281, 983, 1804])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1001, 1286, 1701, 1795])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried bulb of Fritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia 1. Starch granules 2. Epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\longrightarrow $ ） 3. Spiral vessels a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 26, 1874, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Peimisine standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of peimisine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol, ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and water (18:2:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1 g of vanillin and dissolve in 100 mL of sulphuric acid."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 30 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately peimisine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (12 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the TLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the TLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[382, 372, 1055, 690])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of peimisine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[384, 878, 1015, 1665])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 (i) 2 (ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference TLC chromatogram of Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Peimisine standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried bulb of Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don\n\n(ii) dried bulb of Fritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of peimisine (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[33, 22, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XV.\n\nPrimer sequences for PCR-RFLP\n\nITS-P1: 5'-CGT AAC AAG GTT TCC GTA GGT GAA-3'\n\nITS-P3: 5'-GCT ACG TTC TTC ATC GAT-3'"},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 20 mg of the powdered sample and place it in a 1.5-mL microcentrifuge tube. Extract DNA from plant tissue according to the manufacturer's manual of DNeasy Plant Mini Kit. Briefly, after re-suspension, add the lysis buffer with detergent or surfactant remove lipid on cell surface and to lyse the cells; RNAse A is then added to remove RNA; Afterwards, add ethanol to precipitate DNA; the isolated DNA is ready to use after re-dissolved in TE buffer or UltraPure water."},{"name":"14","text":"Perform PCR for the method blank, samples (extracted DNA) and sample duplicate. Identify the PCR products by electrophoresis analysis. Measure the fragment length of the DNA fragments by referring to the DNA size marker. Carry out restriction analysis with the PCR products using Sma I restriction enzyme (total reaction volume 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ). Perform electrophoresis and detection for the method blank and the RFLP products. Measure the fragment length of all polymorphic DNA fragments by referring to the DNA size marker.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[375, 1670, 1448, 2443])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 PCR products generated by primers (ITS-P1 and ITS-P3) flanking the nrDNA ITS1 region using template DNA from 4 batches of Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus (Lane 1-4) and blank sample (B)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 14, 1946, 256])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[374, 393, 1447, 1121])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 7 A reference PCR-RFLP patterns of ITS1 region digested with Sma I. M: 1 kb DNA ladder; B: Blank; 1-2: Fritillaria cirrhosa D. Don; 3-4: Fritillaria unibracteata Hsiao et K. C. Hsia\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the PCR products of sample and sample duplicate must give a band at 0.3 kb in the electrophoresis detection (Fig. 6). The correct PCR products of sample and sample duplicate are then proceeding to restriction analysis. The RFLP products of sample and sample duplicate must give two bands at $ \\sim $0.1 and 0.2 kb in the electrophoresis detection (Fig. 7)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[32, 23, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 8.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XIV."},{"name":"14","text":"0. 2 M Sodium hydroxide solution\n\nWeigh 0.4 g of sodium hydroxide and dissolve in 50 mL of water.\n\nBromocresol green solution\n\nWeigh 0.05 g of bromocresol green and dissolve in 6 mL of 0.2 M sodium hydroxide solution. Transfer the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Add 1 g of monobasic potassium phosphate and make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"14","text":"Peimisine standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of peimisine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of dichloromethane.\n\nPeimisine standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the peimisine Std-Stock. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Pipette 10 mL of dichloromethane to dissolve the residue and produce a series of solutions of 2.5, 5, 12.5, 25, 37.5 mg/L for peimisine.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[40, 11, 1946, 258])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fritillariae-cirrhosae-bulbus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus","la":"Fritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fritillariae-hupehensis-bulbus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[619, 1060, 975, 1431])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1010, 1017, 1336, 1358])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[669, 1517, 972, 1756])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1076, 1459, 1339, 1718])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1928, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[619, 1060, 975, 1431])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1010, 1017, 1336, 1358])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[669, 1517, 972, 1756])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1076, 1459, 1339, 1718])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus\n\nChinese Name: 湖北貝母\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hubeibeimu"},{"name":"12","text":"Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus is the dried bulb of Fritillaria hupehensis Hsiao et K. C. Hsia (Liliaceae). The bulb is collected in early summer when the plant withers. After removed of soil, the bulb is soaked in saturated calcium hydroxide (0.15%) or washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus."},{"name":"12","text":"Oblate, 8-37 mm in diameter. The outer surface off-white to pale brown, the outer scale leaves 2, fleshy, slightly reniform in shape, or varying considerably in size; the large scale closely embracing the small one, and enclosing 2-6 small scales and the remains of dried shrunken stem. The inner surface white to pale yellow. Base depressed, with remains of epidermis and a few fibrous roots. The single scale leaf reniform-shoe-shaped, 0.7-3.5 cm high, 4-18 mm in diameter. Texture fragile, easily broken, fracture whitish, starchy. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Outer epidermis of scale leaves consists of 3-5 layers of cells, inner epidermis consists of 2-4 layers of cells, covered with cuticle. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules. Crystals of calcium oxalate occasionally visible in epidermal cells. Vessels small, scattered in parenchyma (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour off-white to pale brown. Starch granules fairly abundant, broadly ovoid, long ellipsoid or subspheroid, 11-70 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum pointed, V-shaped or slit-shaped, striations distinct;\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 6, 1984, 263])\n\ncompound granules rare, composed of 2-3 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Crystals of calcium oxalate subsquare, rhombic, granular or clustered, 20-117 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Spiral vessels, 6-32 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Epidermal cells square or polygonal, anticlinal walls irregularly beaded, 25-75 $ \\mu $m in diameter; stoma occasionally visible, oblate, with 4-5 subsidiary cells (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Hupehenine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hupehenine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 4 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, acetone and diethylamine (8:2:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 2 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hupehenine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of hupehenine.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[22, 1, 1929, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[255, 419, 1728, 1962])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration of scale leaf C. Vessels D. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Outer epidermis 2. Parenchyma 3. Vessels 4. Starch granules\n\n5. Inner epidermis 6. Scale leaf 7. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[22, 7, 1984, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[314, 453, 1653, 1996])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Crystal of calcium oxalate 3. Vessels 4. Epidermal cells 5. Stoma\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 3, 1930, 259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[455, 366, 933, 895])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[457, 1053, 962, 1575])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hupehenine and (ii) peiminine\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Peiminine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (480 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.96 mg of peiminine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 4 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v). Allow to stand for 1 h. Add 20 mL of a mixture of dichloromethane and acetone (1:2, v/v). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 4, 1984, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 8 8^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ）flow: 0.8 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.02% Triethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>40→5</td><td>60→95</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of peiminine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of peiminine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of peiminine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from peiminine peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject peiminine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of peiminine peak in the chromatogram of peiminine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify peiminine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of peiminine Std-FP. The retention times of peiminine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 4, 1930, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, peiminine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4 (hupehenine)</td><td>1.56</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[381, 893, 1776, 1581])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[33, 20, 1976, 265])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fritillariae-hupehensis-bulbus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus","la":"Fritillariae Hupehensis Bulbus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-evodiae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Evodiae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[100, 711, 911, 1451])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried fruits of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1037, 712, 1836, 1448])\n\nA photograph of dried fruits of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. officinalis (Dode) Huang\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[562, 1727, 1381, 2469])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of dried fruits of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. bodinieri (Dode) Huang\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"17","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[100, 711, 911, 1451])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried fruits of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1037, 712, 1836, 1448])\n\nA photograph of dried fruits of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. officinalis (Dode) Huang\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[562, 1727, 1381, 2469])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of dried fruits of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. bodinieri (Dode) Huang\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},{"name":"17","text":"Official Name: Fructus Evodiae\n\nChinese Name: 吴茱萸\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Wuzhuyu"},{"name":"17","text":"Fructus Evodiae is the dried and nearly ripe fruit of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth., Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. officinalis (Dode) Huang or Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. bodinieri (Dode) Huang (Rutaceae). The fruit spur is cut off from August to November when the fruit indehiscent, then dried under the sun or at a temperature not more than $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ , and foreign matters including shoot, leaf and fruit stalk removed to obtain Fructus Evodiae."},{"name":"17","text":"Spheroidal or slightly flattened to pentagonal-spheroidal, 2-7 mm in diameter. Externally dark yellowish-green to brown, rough, with numerous punctiform protuberances or foveola. A pentagonal-stellate cleft is present at the apex, while remnant of a yellow-tomentose fruit stalk at the base. Texture hard and fragile. Transverse section shows 5-locular ovary, with each locule possessing 1 yellowish seed. Odour strongly aromatic; taste pungent and bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"17","text":"Epidermis of exocarp consists of 1 row of cells. Oil-secretory cavity large, 45-460 $ \\mu $m in diameter, distributed in the mesocarp parenchyma cells. Vascular bundle is distributed just outside of the endocarp. Clusters of calcium oxalate are distributed in the mesocarp parenchyma cells, but denser just outside of the endocarp. Prisms of calcium oxalate are occasionally found among the cluster of calcium oxalate. Endocarp consists of 4-5 rows of subrectangular parenchyma cells. Stone cells are sometimes found (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"17","text":"Colour brown. Non-glandular hairs 1-8 celled, 90-440 $ \\mu $m long, warts obvious, some lumina contain brownish-yellow to brownish-red contents. Glandular hairs with a 7-18 celled head, ellipsoid, usually containing yellowish-brown contents, and with a 1-5 celled stalk. Cluster\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 1, 1967, 249])\n\nof calcium oxalate frequently found, 6-15 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; prisms of calcium oxalate are also found occasionally. Stone cells suborbicular, rectangular or irregular in shape. Pale yellow fragments of oil-secretory cavities are sometimes visible (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"17","text":"Evodiamine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of evodiamine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nRutaecarpine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rutaecarpine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, ethanol and triethylamine (19:5:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min and then filter."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately evodiamine standard solution and rutaecarpine standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (1-6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to those of evodiamine and rutaecarpine.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1871, 83])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1064, 239])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nFructus Ev\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 420, 1743, 2152])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of the pericarp of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate D. Stone cells\n\n</div>\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 411, 1742, 2157])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of the pericarp of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. officinalis (Dode) Huang\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Oil-secretory cavity 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Prisms of calcium oxalate 7. Endocarp\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 22, 1088, 119])\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1670, 11, 1871, 83])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[947, 177, 1065, 239])\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nFructus Ev\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[239, 398, 1739, 2119])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of the pericarp of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. bodinieri (Dode) Huang\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Oil-secretory cavity 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Prisms of calcium oxalate 7. Endocarp\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參 Radix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[440, 437, 1545, 1929])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried and nearly ripe fruit of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Multicellular non-glandular hair with brownish-red content\n\n2. Multicellular non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Clusters of calcium oxalate 5. Prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Stone cell\n\n7. Fragment of oil-secretory cavity\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nHerba Taxilli 桑寄生\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n桑白皮\n\nCortex Mori\n\n女貞子\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1669, 11, 1871, 83])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[947, 178, 1065, 240])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[432, 440, 1558, 1916])\n\n25μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried and nearly ripe fruit of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. officinalis (Dode) Huang\n\n</div>\n\n1. Multicellular non-glandular hair with brownish-yellow content\n\n2. Multicellular non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Clusters of calcium oxalate 5. Prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Stone cell\n\n7. Fragment of oil-secretory cavity\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1613, 149, 1693, 199])\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[433, 439, 1541, 1946])\n\n25 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(iii) Microscopic features of powder of dried and nearly ripe fruit of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. bodinieri (Dode) Huang\n\n</div>\n\n1. Multicellular non-glandular hair with brownish-red content\n\n2. Multicellular non-glandular hair 3. Glandular hair\n\n4. Clusters of calcium oxalate 5. Prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Stone"},{"name":"17","text":"Evodiamine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of evodiamine CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter. Make appropriate dilution where necessary."},{"name":"17","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>90→70</td><td>10→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>70→0</td><td>30→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[21, 0, 1967, 249])"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of evodiamine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of evodiamine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of evodiamine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from evodiamine peak should not be less than 500000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Separately inject evodiamine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of evodiamine peak in the chromatogram of evodiamine Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify evodiamine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of evodiamine Std-FP. The retention times of evodiamine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Fructus Evodiae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Fructus Evodiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, evodiamine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3 (Rutaecarpine)</td><td>1.02</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n葛根 Radix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[389, 418, 1752, 1193])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried and nearly ripe fruit of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[391, 1375, 1753, 2147])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried and nearly ripe fruit of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. officinalis (Dode) Huang extract\n\n</div>\n\n北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nSemen Cassiae決明子\n\n遠志\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[388, 416, 1747, 1183])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried and nearly ripe fruit of Euodia rutaecarpa (Juss.) Benth. var. bodinieri (Dode) Huang extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 7.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 14.0%.\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[41, 0, 1946, 251])"},{"name":"17","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 27.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 30.0%."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"17","text":"Mixed evodiamine and rutaecarpine standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of evodiamine CRS and 10.0 mg of rutaecarpine CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol.\n\nMixed evodiamine and rutaecarpine standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed evodiamine and rutaecarpine Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 0.5, 5, 50, 100, 150 mg/L for both evodiamine and rutaecarpine."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh accurately 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Macerate for 60 min. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter. Transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times without macerating. Combine the filtrate and make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-evodiae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Evodiae","la":"Fructus Evodiae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-forsythiae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Forsythiae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[425, 966, 1533, 1649])\n\n1 cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Fructus Forsythiae (A: Qingqiao B: Laoqiao)\n\n1. Fruit 2. Fruit splits open, showing pericarp and seeds 3. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1943, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[425, 966, 1533, 1649])\n\n1 cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Fructus Forsythiae (A: Qingqiao B: Laoqiao)\n\n1. Fruit 2. Fruit splits open, showing pericarp and seeds 3. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1943, 253])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Fructus Forsythiae\n\nChinese Name: 連翹\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Lianqiao"},{"name":"10","text":"Fructus Forsythiae is the dried fruit of Forsythia suspensa (Thunb.) Vahl (Oleaceae). The fruit is collected in autumn when nearly ripe, but still greenish, foreign matter removed, steamed thoroughly and dried under the sun (known as \"Qingqiao\"), or the fruit is collected when fully ripe, foreign matter removed, dried under the sun (known as \"Laoqiao\") to obtain Fructus Forsythiae."},{"name":"10","text":"Long-ovoid to ovoid, slightly compressed, 1.5-2.5 cm long, 5-10.3 mm in diameter. Externally with irregular longitudinal wrinkles, numerous small protuberant maculae, and a distinct longitudinal furrow on each of the two surfaces. Apex acute, bearing a small fruit stalk or its scar at the base. \"Qingqiao\" is mostly indehiscent, externally greenish-brown to brown, texture hard; seeds numerous, yellowish-green to brown, slender, winged on one side. \"Laoqiao\" is dehiscent starting from apex or into two segments, the outer surface yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, the inner surface mostly pale yellowish-brown, smooth, with a longitudinal septum, its texture brittle; seeds brown, mostly fallen off. Odour slightly aromatic; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 row of elongated tangentially oriented epidermal cells, and covered by a cuticle. Mesocarp parenchyma irregularly arranged, vascular bundles are scattered in the parenchyma on the outer portion, stone cells or fibres are sometimes also visible while many layers of stone cells with inlaid fibres are found on the inner portion, their shape oblong to subrounded, wall thickness variable, mostly tangentially parqueted. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of flat and tiny parenchyma cells (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 0, 1965, 250])"},{"name":"10","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to brown. Fibres mostly in bundles, the wall thickened. Stone cells oblong to suborbicular. Mesocarp parenchyma cells suborbicular, subsquare, polygonal or irregular in shape, some part of the walls slightly beaded-thickened. Vessels mostly spiral (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"Phillyrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of phillyrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethanol and glacial acetic acid (8:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add slowly 20 mL of sulphuric acid to 80 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of water. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separatory funnel. Extract for three times each with 10 mL of hexane-ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v) and discard the upper layer. Extract the aqueous layer for three times each with 15 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately phillyrin standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of phillyrin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 2, 1947, 251])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[230, 445, 1728, 1925])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Fructus Forsythiae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells in xylem D. Stone cells with inlaid fibres in the innermost layers of mesocarp\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Exocarp 3. Vascular bundle 4. Stone cells\n\n5a. Outer layers of mesocarp 5b. Inner layers of mesocarp with many layers of stone cells and inlaid fibres\n\n6. Endocarp\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\n燈心草\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1044, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n決明子\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Fructus Forsythiae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[440, 466, 1562, 1800])\n\n50μm"},{"name":"10","text":"1. Fibres 2. Stone cells oblong 3. Stone cells suborbicular\n\n4. Mesocarp parenchyma cells 5. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 11, 255, 95])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 23, 1089, 119])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[947, 177, 1067, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[566, 446, 1473, 800])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of phillyrin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Phillyrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of phillyrin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (229 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-35</td><td>80→55</td><td>20→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-50</td><td>55→0</td><td>45→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of phillyrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of phillyrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of phillyrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from phillyrin peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 1, 1966, 249])\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject phillyrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of phillyrin peak in the chromatogram of phillyrin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify phillyrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of phillyrin Std-FP. The retention times of phillyrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fructus Forsythiae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Fructus Forsythiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.40</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, phillyrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.80</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[392, 1742, 1754, 2449])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Fructus Forsythiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[50, 0, 1949, 254])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 17.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-forsythiae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Forsythiae","la":"Fructus Forsythiae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-ligustri-lucidi-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Ligustri Lucidi v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[461, 815, 1434, 1838])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[42, 1, 1947, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[461, 815, 1434, 1838])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[42, 1, 1947, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Fructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\nChinese Name: 女貞子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Nüzhenzi"},{"name":"11","text":"Fructus Ligustri Lucidi is the dried ripe fruit of Ligustrum lucidum Ait. (Oleaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in winter, by removing from leafy branches, steamed or treated with boiling water for a few minutes, then dried; or dried directly without treatment to obtain Fructus Ligustri Lucidi."},{"name":"11","text":"Reniform, sometimes ovoid or ellipsoid, 5-8.5 mm long, 3-6.5 mm in diameter. Externally blackishpurple or greyish-black, shrunken and uneven, with a fruit stalk scar or persistent calyx and a short fruit stalk at the base. Texture light. Pericarp thin, mesocarp relatively lax and soft, easily stripped off, endocarp woody, yellowish-brown, with longitudinal ridges. Seed 1, reniform, purplish-black, oily. Odour slight; taste sweet, with slight bitterness and somewhat astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of subpolygonal epidermis cells, containing oil droplets, the cuticle covering the outer and lateral walls thickened. Mesocarp consists of 10-20 layers of parenchyma cells, scattered with vascular bundles near the bulge of the endocarp. Endocarp consists of 4-8 layers of lignified fibres, its surface rough and uneven. Epidermal cells of testa composed of elongated round or oblong parenchyma cells, often with secretory cells on the ridges. Parenchyma of testa consisting of several rows of parenchyma cells, mostly shrunken. Endosperm and cotyledons consist of parenchyma cells. When fruit is ripening, only one seed is developed from 2 celled ovary (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-brown or blackish-grey. Fibres of endocarp in bundles or single, colourless or pale yellow, cross-overlapping in the bundles; the single fibre has a band or a strip-shape, mostly curved, straight or twisted, tapering, blunt or branched at one end, some fibres are\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1966, 249])\n\nenlarged, boot-shaped, 9-54 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Epidermal cells of the pericarp sub-oblate in surface view, with the cuticle of the outer wall thickened, divided into 4-10 irregular small lumina by several arris, 36-80 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, containing yellowish-brown or purplishbrown masses. Epidermal cells of testa scattered with secretory cells, pale-brown or brown in colour. Secretory cells rounded or oblong, 45-96 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, containing yellowishbrown secretion and oil droplet (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Salidroside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of salidroside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of water, methanol and acetic acid (90:10:2, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 500-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 200 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture. Discard the filtrate. Repeat the above procedure for one more time. Add 200 mL of methanol to the residue. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol. Transfer 1 mL of solution to a centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer 200 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of supernatant to the Diol clean-up column (4 mL, 500 mg) pre-conditioned with 2 mL of ethyl acetate. Add 1.5 mL of ethyl acetate to the clean-up column and discard this portion of eluant. Add 2.5 mL of ethyl acetate to the clean-up column and collect the eluant in a 10-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the elution for one more time with 2.5 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the eluants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 200 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately salidroside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[102, 6, 206, 60])\n\n補骨脂 Fructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[861, 22, 1087, 117])\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n浙貝母\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1671, 12, 1872, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1063, 239])\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 425, 1699, 1751])\n\n25μm\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Fructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Exocarp\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Vascular bundles 4. Endocarp 5. Epidermal cells of testa\n\n6. Parenchyma of testa 7. Endosperm 8. Cotyledon 9. Aborted seed\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n決明子\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 431, 1730, 1959])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Fructus Ligustri Lucidi (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Fibres of endocarp 2. Pericarp epidermis 3. Epidermal cells of testa\n\n4. A single fibre cell from the endocarp\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 1, 1942, 255])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of salidroside.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[732, 583, 1323, 869])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of salidroside\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Salidroside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of salidroside and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol ( 70% ). Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (230 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>4</td><td>96</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-35</td><td>4→6</td><td>96→94</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>6→15</td><td>94→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of salidroside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of salidroside should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of salidroside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from salidroside peak should not be less than 14000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 and the R value between peak 3 and peak 4 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject salidroside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of salidroside peak in the chromatogram of salidroside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify salidroside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of salidroside Std-FP. The retention times of salidroside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Fructus Ligustri Lucidi extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Fructus Ligustri Lucidi extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.84(vs peak3)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(tyrosol)</td><td>0.93(vs peak3)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, salidroside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.11(vs peak3)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.64(vs peak4)</td><td>±0.09</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[857, 24, 1089, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[947, 180, 1062, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[392, 411, 1752, 1090])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Fructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 27.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 27.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 0, 1958, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Salidroside standard stock solution, Std-Stock (400 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 4.0 mg of salidroside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nSalidroside standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the salidroside Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (70%) to produce a series of solutions of 5,10,20,40,80 mg/L for salidroside."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the filtrate. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (70%). Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-ligustri-lucidi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Ligustri Lucidi","la":"Fructus Ligustri Lucidi"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-psoraleae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Psoraleae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[453, 826, 1451, 1723])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1939, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[453, 826, 1451, 1723])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1939, 252])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Fructus Psoraleae\n\nChinese Name: 補骨脂\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Buguzhi"},{"name":"11","text":"Fructus Psoraleae is the dried ripe fruit of Psoralea corylifolia L. (Fabaceae). The fruit is collected in autumn when it is ripe, dried under the sun, and foreign matter removed to obtain Fructus Psoraleae."},{"name":"11","text":"Ovoid to ellipsoid or reniform, slightly flattened, 3-5 mm long, 2-4 mm wide, about 1-1.5 mm thick. Externally blackish-brown to greyish-brown, with finely reticulate wrinkles. Apex obtuse-rounded, slightly prominent, the depressed end showing a scar of fruit stalk. Texture hard. Pericarp thin, not easily stripped from the seed; seed 1, cotyledons 2, yellowish-white, oily. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of epidermal cells. Intramural glands arranged under the epidermal cells of the exocarp and often broken off. Mesocarp consists of small vascular bundles and mesocarp parenchyma cells; some mesocarp cells contain minute crystals of calcium oxalate. Epidermal cells of testa consist of 1 layer of 35-63 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ long cylindrical palisade cells and 1 layer of sunken support cells, and below it, several layers of shrunken or compressed parenchyma cells make up the hypodermal tissue. Next to the hypodermis is the endodermal tissue of the testa, consisting of 1 layer of elongated-oblong to suborbicular pigmented parenchyma cells. The cotyledons consist of parenchyma cells containing small aleurone and fatty oil, while the endosperm and radicle consist of simple parenchyma cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-yellow. Cylindrical palisade cells of testa yellowish-brown, 35-63 $ \\mu m $ long, cell wall thickened, appearing as a \"V\" shape in lateral view, subpolygonal in surface view. Crystals of calcium oxalate minute, scattered in the mesocarp parenchyma cells, polychromatic\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 1, 1966, 249])\n\nunder a polarized microscope. Unicellular non-glandular hair present, 150-568 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in length, with densely dotted protuberances on the surface. Support cells of testa sunken in lateral view, thicker cell wall in the middle. Glandular hairs capped by a multicellular or unicellular head with a short stalk. Intramural glands round when completely intact, 160-400 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, but more often broken into pieces; the cells in the middle are smaller, polygonal in shape, and surrounded by radially oriented elongated cells (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Isopsoralen standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isopsoralen CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nPsoralen standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of psoralen CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and acetic acid (3:1:0.05, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of water. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Concentrate the solution to about 10 mL at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Transfer the solution into a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Mix the solution with 10 mL of ethyl acetate for about 1 min. Allow to stand for about 5 min until the two layers are separated. Transfer the ethyl acetate extract to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately isopsoralen standard solution, psoralen standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[859, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 239])\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草 Herba Leonuri 平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis Fructus Ev 吴茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 424, 1704, 1690])\n\n25μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Fructus Psoraleae\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Intramural gland 3. Vascular bundle 4. Crystal of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Cylindrical palisade cells of testa 6. Support cells of testa 7. Hypodermal cells of testa\n\n8. Endospermal cells of testa 9. Cotyledon 10. Radicle 11. Endosperm\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1044, 24, 1175, 89])\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1363, 64, 1446, 104])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1614, 150, 1693, 199])\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Fructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[274, 432, 1649, 2087])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Fructus Psoraleae\n\n1. Cylindrical palisade cells of testa (Lateral view)\n\n2. Cylindrical palisade cells of testa (Surface view)\n\n3. Unicellular non-glandular hairs 4. Support cells of testa\n\n5. Crystals of calcium oxalate 6. Glandular hair 7. Intramural glands\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[41, 0, 1946, 254])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of isopsoralen and psoralen.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[812, 575, 1134, 760])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[798, 891, 1152, 1033])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) isopsoralen and (ii) psoralen\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Isopsoralen standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isopsoralen CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (308 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>50→30</td><td>50→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>30→15</td><td>70→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-50</td><td>15→10</td><td>85→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>10</td><td>90</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[23, 0, 1966, 250])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of isopsoralen Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of isopsoralen should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of isopsoralen peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from isopsoralen peak should not be less than 6000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject isopsoralen Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of isopsoralen peak in the chromatogram of isopsoralen Std-FP and the retention times of the eight characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify isopsoralen peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of isopsoralen Std-FP. The retention times of isopsoralen peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eight characteristic peaks of Fructus Psoraleae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eight characteristic peaks of Fructus Psoraleae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(psoralen)</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,isopsoralen)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.01</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>3.06</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>3.30</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>3.73</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[857, 24, 1090, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[947, 180, 1063, 241])\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 458, 1750, 1003])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Fructus Psoraleae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above eight characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 23.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 0, 1958, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Mixed psoralen and isopsoralen standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of psoralen CRS and 2.0 mg of isopsoralen CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed psoralen and isopsoralen standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed psoralen and isopsoralen Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 10,40,60,100,150 mg/L for both psoralen and isopsoralen."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatant. Make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/fructus-psoraleae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructus Psoraleae","la":"Fructus Psoraleae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/galangae-fructus-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Galangae Fructus v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[388, 839, 1565, 1835])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Galangae Fructus"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[388, 839, 1565, 1835])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Galangae Fructus"},{"name":"15","text":"B. Magnified image of seeds (B-1 after pericarp removed, covered with a yellowish-white membranous aril; B-2 seeds covered with a yellowish-white membranous aril; B-3 seeds membranous aril removed)\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of seeds (showing endosperm)"},{"name":"15","text":"Official name: Galangae Fructus\n\nChinese name: 紅豆蔻\n\nChinese phonetic name: Hongdoukou"},{"name":"15","text":"Galangae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Alpinia galanga (L.) Willd. (Zingiberaceae). The fruit is collected in autumn when it turns red, foreign matter removed, then dried in the shade to obtain Galangae Fructus."},{"name":"15","text":"Long spheroidal, slightly narrow in the middle, 7-14 mm in length, 5-9 mm in diameter. Externally reddish-brown to dark red, somewhat shrunken, apex with yellowish-white tubular persistent calyx, base with a fruit stalk scar. Pericarp thin, easily broken. Seeds 6, oblate to triangular-polyhedral, reddish-brown to blackish-brown, covered with a yellowish-white membranous aril, endosperm greyish-white. Odour aromatic; taste pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Pericarp: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of flat cells. Mesocarp parenchymatous cells irregular in shape. The outer part of mesocarp consists of 1 layer, rarely 2 layers of stone cells, arranged in a ring. Stone cells subrounded, elliptical or irregular in shape, pits distinct, 12-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Vascular bundles sparsely scattered in parenchymatous cells. Oil cells 1 layer, subrounded, subeliptical, 40-125 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, next to endocarp. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of flat parenchymatous cells. Prisms of calcium oxalate 2-12 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, scattered in parenchymatous cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope [Fig. 2(i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 14, 1944, 260])\n\nSeed: Aril consists of 4-7 layers of cells, rounded or tangentially elongated, with slightly thickened walls. The outer part of testa consists of 1-7 layers of thick-walled fibres, fibres rounded or polygonal in shape, 6-45 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. 1 layer of flattened, yellowish-brown or dark brown pigment cells beneath the fibre layers. Oil cells 1 layer, square or rectangular, 14-54 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Pigment layer consists of 3-5 layers of cells containing brown contents. Endotesta consists of 1 layer of thick-walled palisade cells, 60-70 $ \\mu $ m in length, 20-30 $ \\mu $ m in width, yellowish-brown or reddish-brown, the inner and lateral walls of inner part strongly thickened; lumen found in the outer part, containing silica nodules. Perisperm cells filled with masses of starch granules and sometimes prisms of calcium oxalate. Endosperm cells contain aleurone grains and oil droplets (before stained). Parenchymatous cells of embryo small [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour brown. The stone cells of pericarp subrounded, elliptical or irregular in shape, 12-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, pits distinct. Fibres usually in bundles, long fusiform in shape, walls thick, lumen linear. The palisade cells of endotesta in groups, reddish-brown or yellowish-brown; polygonal or subsquare in surface view, with lumen containing silica nodules; cells arranged in the form of palisade in lateral view, lumen found at one end, containing silica nodules. Oil cells rectangular or square in lateral view. Pigment cells flattened, yellowish-brown to brown. Prisms of calcium oxalate 2-12 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, scattered in parenchymatous cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. The cells of perisperm filled with masses of starch granules and sometimes prisms of calcium oxalate. The cells of endosperm contain aleurone grains and oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 393, 1746, 2129])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Galangae Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified D. Prisms of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) E. Prisms of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Stone cell 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle 5. Oil cell\n\n6. Endocarp 7. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[245, 424, 1739, 2071])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Galangae Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n5. Pigment layer 6. Endotesta 7. Perisperm\n\n1. Aril 2. Outer part of testa 3. Pigment cells 4. Oil cells layer\n\n8. Endosperm 9. Embryo 10. Silica nodules\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[302, 483, 1677, 2054])"},{"name":"15","text":"1. Pericarp with exocarp, stone cells ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) and mesocarp 2. Fibres\n\n3. Endotesta palisade cells with silica nodules ( $ \\rightarrow $ ）(3-1 in surface view, 3-2 in lateral view show thick walled palisade cells $ \\rightarrow $ ）\n\n4. Oil cells ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) and pigment cells 5. Prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Perisperm cells\n\n7. Endosperm cells with aleurone grains ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) and oil droplets ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[29, 30, 1943, 254])"},{"name":"15","text":"4-Hydroxybenzaldehyde standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\n4-Hydroxybenzoic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.1 mg of 4-hydroxybenzoic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, ethanol and acetic acid (17:4:2:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), 4-hydroxybenzoic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 435, 818, 731])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 957, 852, 1243])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and (ii) 4-hydroxybenzoic acid\n\n</div>\n\n明黛参 Changii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 374, 875, 1211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Galangae Fructus extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. 4-Hydroxybenzoic acid standard solution\n\n2. 4-Hydroxybenzaldehyde standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"4-Hydroxybenzaldehyde standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\n4-Hydroxybenzoic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of 4-hydroxybenzoic acid CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 12 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>94</td><td>6</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-20</td><td>94→45</td><td>6→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde Std-FP and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid peaks should not be less than 10000 and 5000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde Std-FP, 4-hydroxybenzoic acid Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid peaks in the chromatograms of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde Std-FP, 4-hydroxybenzoic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the eight characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde Std-FP and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid Std-FP. The retention times of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and 4-hydroxybenzoic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[27, 20, 1943, 260])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eight characteristic peaks of Galangae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eight characteristic peaks of Galangae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,4-hydroxybenzoic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(4-hydroxybenzaldehyde)</td><td>1.51</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.74</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.96</td><td>$\\pm$0.07</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.08</td><td>$\\pm$0.08</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2.43</td><td>$\\pm$0.11</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>2.66</td><td>$\\pm$0.12</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>2.83</td><td>$\\pm$0.14</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[329, 1170, 1822, 1783])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Galangae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above eight characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.9%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.8%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.9%."},{"name":"15","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 7.5%.\n\nWater-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.5%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.3%.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[17, 18, 1951, 257])"},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/galangae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Galangae Fructus","la":"Galangae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ganoderma-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ganoderma v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[515, 1091, 812, 1365])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[831, 1068, 1153, 1348])\n\n2 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1161, 1089, 1482, 1376])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[529, 1451, 1086, 1626])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1115, 1416, 1462, 1653])\n\nC 0.5mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst.\n\nA. Sporocarps B. Longitudinal section of pileus\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of pileus\n\n1. Context 2. Spore-producing tube layer 3. Pores of tubes\n\nPotentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n翻白草\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nLini Semen\n\n亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n白花蛇舌草\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n三棱\n\n天葵子\n\nSemiaquilegiae Radix\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nGanoderma\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\n天花粉\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[378, 530, 1648, 1793])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma sinense Zhao, Xu et Zhang A. Sporocarps B. Longitudinal section of pileus C. Magnified image of lower surface of pileus\n\n1. Context 2. Spore-producing tube layer 3. Pores of tubes\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[48, 42, 235, 121])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[925, 87, 1028, 138])"},"sections":[{"name":"25","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[515, 1091, 812, 1365])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[831, 1068, 1153, 1348])\n\n2 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1161, 1089, 1482, 1376])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[529, 1451, 1086, 1626])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1115, 1416, 1462, 1653])\n\nC 0.5mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst.\n\nA. Sporocarps B. Longitudinal section of pileus\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of pileus\n\n1. Context 2. Spore-producing tube layer 3. Pores of tubes\n\nPotentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n翻白草\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nLini Semen\n\n亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n白花蛇舌草\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n三棱\n\n天葵子\n\nSemiaquilegiae Radix\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nGanoderma\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\n天花粉\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[378, 530, 1648, 1793])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma sinense Zhao, Xu et Zhang A. Sporocarps B. Longitudinal section of pileus C. Magnified image of lower surface of pileus\n\n1. Context 2. Spore-producing tube layer 3. Pores of tubes\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[48, 42, 235, 121])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[925, 87, 1028, 138])"},{"name":"25","text":"Official Name: Ganoderma\n\nChinese Name: 靈芝\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Lingzhi"},{"name":"25","text":"Ganoderma is the dried sporocarp of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst. or Ganoderma sinense Zhao, Xu et Zhang (Polyporaceae). The sporocarp is collected all year round, foreign matter removed, cut off the lower end of the stipe with culture medium, then dried in the shade, baked at $ 4 0-5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ or dried under the sun to obtain Ganoderma."},{"name":"25","text":"Cultivated species umbrella-shaped, consisting of pileus and stipe. Pileus subreniform, semicircular or suborbicular, 40-250 mm in diameter, 0.4-2.5 cm thick; upper surface yellowish-brown, brown to reddish-brown, lustrous, with concentric annular grooves and bands, also with distinct or indistinct radial wrinkles, margin thin and truncate, flat or incurved; context whitish to pale brown, thicker near the stipe, 1.6 cm at the thickest part, become thinner toward the margin; the spore-producing tube layer pale brown to brown, monolayer, the length of tube varies from short to long, 1.28 cm at the longest part; fine and dense pores of the tubes found on the lower surface, yellowish-brown, 4-7 pores per millimeter. Spores found on the raw ones, fine, brown. Stipe cylindrical, flat-cylindrical to subquadrangular, attachment of the stipe to the pileus varies from lateral to nearly central, occasionally eccentric, 2.5-20.2 cm long, 9-72 mm in diameter, same colour as pileus or purplish-brown, with lacquer-like lustre. Texture hard; odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly bitter and astringent [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[36, 14, 1955, 262])"},{"name":"25","text":"Pileipellis consists of densely arranged palisade hyphae, hyphae clavate, pale yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Context underneath pileipellis, consisting of interwoven, branched and non-septate hyphae, with 1-2 interrupted layers of pigmented hyphae. Pore-like clefts located near the middle of context. The spore-producing tube layer monolayer, tubes longitudinally arranged, 2.5-13.0 mm long, walls covered with the dark brown spores (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"25","text":"Pores of tubes subrounded or polygonal, 130-280 $ \\mu $m in diameter, dissepiment 25-275 $ \\mu $m wide. Spores mainly scattered near the tube walls or in the pores (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"25","text":"Colour pale brown to brown. Spores brown, ovoid, apex truncate, with colourless sporophores, nearly truncate at the end, 8-12 $ \\mu $m long, 5-8 $ \\mu $m wide, double-walled, episporium smooth, colourless, endosporium pale brown, with warty protuberance, sometimes with oil droplets at the centre. Hyphae scattered or aggregated to form clumps, colourless or pale brown, slender, slightly curved, branched, 2-8 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fragments of pileipellis pale yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, palisade-like hyphae densely arranged, clavate, apex enlarged, unbranched, 5-13 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Hyphae of pigment layer dark brown, consisting of hyphae and pigment (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[927, 87, 1024, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 52, 1911, 108])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 399, 1744, 2111])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of longitudinal section of pileus of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of pileipellis D. Magnified image of context\n\nE. Magnified image of tubes\n\n1. Pileipellis 2. Context 3. Cleft 4. Pigment layer 5. Dissepiment 6. Tube 7. Hyphae\n\n8. Spore\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[235, 399, 1759, 2139])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of transverse section of tube layer of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst. A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of tubes\n\n</div>\n\n1. Tube 2. Dissepiment 3. Spore\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[198, 146, 288, 188])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[700, 141, 827, 190])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[307, 500, 930, 971])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1032, 503, 1676, 969])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[308, 1021, 937, 1487])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1032, 1020, 1676, 1490])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[308, 1539, 933, 2013])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1033, 1540, 1675, 2013])\n\n25 μm"},{"name":"25","text":"1. Spores (1-1 spores, 1-2 spore magnified under oil immersion lens; sporophores $ \\longrightarrow $ )\n\n2. Hyphae (2-1 hyphae, 2-2 clump of hyphae)\n\n3. Fragment of pileipellis (3-1 in top view, 3-2 in lateral view)\n\n4. Hyphae of pigment layer"},{"name":"25","text":"Ergosta-4,6,8(14),22-tetraen-3-one standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ergosta-4,6,8(14),22-tetraen-3-one CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 100 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"25","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether $ ( 6 0-8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}) $ , ethyl acetate and formic acid (15:5:1, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"25","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"25","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethyl acetate. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"25","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ergosta-4,6,8(14), 22-tetraen-3-one standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[452, 367, 960, 757])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[465, 825, 1022, 1269])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 Chemical structures of (i) ergosta-4,6,8(14),22-tetraen-3-one and (ii) ganoderic acid A\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[376, 1448, 834, 2132])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst. extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Ergosta-4,6,8(14),22-tetraen-3-one standard solution\n\n2. Test solution\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[36, 36, 1564, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of ergosta 4,6,8(14),22-tetraen-3-one (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"25","text":"Ganoderic acid A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of ganoderic acid A CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"25","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"25","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.04% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-50</td><td>70→62</td><td>30→38</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"25","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ganoderic acid A Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ganoderic acid A should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ganoderic acid A peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ganoderic acid A peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 7).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])"},{"name":"25","text":"Separately inject ganoderic acid A Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of ganoderic acid A peak in the chromatogram of ganoderic acid A Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 7) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ganoderic acid A peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of ganoderic acid A Std-FP. The retention times of ganoderic acid A peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst. extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.77</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, ganoderic acid A)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[342, 1563, 1819, 2374])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 7 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried sporocarp of Ganoderma lucidum (Leyss. ex Fr.) Karst. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[43, 31, 1960, 265])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 7)."},{"name":"25","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"25","text":"Total ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ganoderma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ganoderma","la":"Ganoderma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/garciniae-resina-unprocessed-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Garciniae Resina unprocessed v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1 A photograph of Garciniae Resina (unprocessed)\n\nA. Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) (cylindrical)\n\nB. Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) (irregular mass)\n\nC. Fracture"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"Figure 1 A photograph of Garciniae Resina (unprocessed)\n\nA. Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) (cylindrical)\n\nB. Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) (irregular mass)\n\nC. Fracture"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Garciniae Resina (unprocessed)\n\nChinese Name: 藤黄（生）\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tenghuang (sheng)"},{"name":"11","text":"Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) is the unprocessed gum-like resin of Garcinia hanburyi Hook. f. (Clusiaceae). The resin is collected in August to September before flowering. The stem 3 m above the ground is cut spirally, in order to make emulsions leak from the wound, emulsions collected, solidified, and dried under the sun; melted and molded into a cylindrical object or other forms, then dried under the sun to obtain Garciniae Resina (unprocessed)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical or irregular masses, up to 34 cm long, 3-16 cm in diameter. Externally reddish-yellow to orange-yellow, sometimes covered with yellowish-green powder, slightly sticky when held. Texture fragile. Fracture smooth, reddish-yellow to orange-yellow, with wax-like lustre, some have cavities at the centre. Odour slightly aromatic (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"No significant identification feature was found."},{"name":"11","text":"Standard solutions\n\nGambogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of gambogenic acid CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nGambogic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of gambogic acid CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nRemarks: Gambogic acid exists as a mixture of R- and S-form.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 35, 1954, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, methanol and diethylamine (5:3:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 150-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Stand for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate [Immerse in methanol and dry in air. Heat the plate at $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min.], a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately gambogenic acid standard solution, gambogic acid standard solution and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 1 h. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 15 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[454, 368, 1200, 700])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[491, 763, 1201, 1099])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[496, 1174, 1201, 1507])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Chemical structures of (i) gambogenic acid (ii) R-gambogic acid and (iii) S-gambogic acid (epigambogic acid)\n\n</div>\n\nRemarks: Gambogic acid exists as a mixture of R- and S-form.\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[924, 86, 1028, 139])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1704, 149, 1942, 217])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[378, 367, 917, 1469])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Gambogenic acid standard solution\n\n2. Gambogic acid standard solution (lower band: R-gambogic acid; upper band: S-gambogic acid)\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of gambogenic acid and gambogic acid (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Gambogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (75 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of gambogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of acetonitrile.\n\nGambogic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of gambogic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of acetonitrile.\n\nRemarks: Gambogic acid exists as a mixture of R- and S-form."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.15 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of acetonitrile. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue for four times each with 5 mL of acetonitrile. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with acetonitrile. Pipette 1 mL of the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with acetonitrile. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (360 nm) and a column $ (4.6\\times150\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 28^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.1% formic acid and acetonitrile (27.5:72.5, v/v). The elution time is about 45 min."},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of gambogenic acid Std-FP and gambogic acid Std-FP. Gambogic acid elutes as R-gambogic acid and S-gambogic acid isomeric peaks in the chromatogram of gambogic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of gambogenic acid, R-gambogic acid and S-gambogic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of gambogenic acid, R-gambogic acid and S-gambogic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from gambogenic acid, R-gambogic acid and S-gambogic acid peaks should not be less than 6000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])"},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject gambogenic acid Std-FP, gambogic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of gambogenic acid, R-gambogic acid and S-gambogic acid peaks in the chromatograms of gambogenic acid Std-FP, gambogic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify gambogenic acid, R-gambogic acid and S-gambogic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of gambogenic acid Std-FP and gambogic acid Std-FP. The retention times of gambogenic acid, R-gambogic acid and S-gambogic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,gambogenic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(R-gambogenic acid)</td><td>1.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(S-gambogenic acid)</td><td>1.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[342, 1672, 1808, 2231])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Garciniae Resina (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements."},{"name":"11","text":"Note: No foreign matter requirement is proposed due to it is resin."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 7.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 37.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 69.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[925, 86, 1028, 138])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/garciniae-resina-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Garciniae Resina unprocessed","la":"Garciniae Resina unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/geranii-caroliniani-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Geranii Caroliniani Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[397, 823, 1586, 1962])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[397, 823, 1586, 1962])"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Geranii Caroliniani Herba B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf D. Magnified image of branch\n\nE. Magnified image of fruit and fruit persistent style\n\nF. Magnified image of fruit and its segment G. Magnified image of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Geranii Caroliniani Herba\n\nChinese name: 野老鸛草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Yelaoguancao"},{"name":"13","text":"Geranii Caroliniani Herba is the dried aerial part of Geranium carolinianum L. (Geraniaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn when the fruit is beginning to ripe, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Geranii Caroliniani Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Stems varying in length and up to 50 cm long, 1-7 mm in diameter, frequently branched, nodes swollen, externally greyish-yellow to purplish, longitudinally furrowed and sparsely pubescent, texture fragile, fracture yellowish-white, sometimes hollow. Leaves opposite, slenderly petioled, lamina palmatisect, 5-7 parted, lobes stripe-shaped and pinnatipartite about 3-5 at upper part. Persistent calyx visible. Capsule spherical, 0.3-0.5 cm long, covered with scabrous pubescence, persistent style 1-1.5 cm long, beak-shaped, sometimes 5-lobed, curled upward. Odour slight, taste bland (Fig.1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Non-glandular hairs found on the surface of epidermis, abundant, mostly unicellular. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with a thin layer of cuticle. Cortex parenchymatous cells irregular arranged. Pericycle fibres arranged in a ring, slightly lignified. Vascular bundles collateral, arranged in an interrupted ring beneath pericycle fibres. Phloem broad. Xylem composed of vessels, few fibres and parenchymatous cells. Pith relatively large, mainly consisting of parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 37, 1947, 249])\n\nLeaf: Petiole base consists of 5-7 collateral vascular bundles. Upper and lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of epidermal cell. Palisade tissue consists of 2 layers of cylindrical cells. Vascular bundles surrounded by sclerenchyma. 1-7 layers of sclerenchymatous cells present on the inner side of upper and lower epidermis of vein. The spongy tissue consists of irregular shaped parenchymatous cells. Vascular bundles collateral, xylem consists of lignified vessels, phloem crescent-shaped. Glandular and non-glandular hairs visible on the upper and lower epidermis, mostly fallen off after staining process; the head of glandular hairs elliptical, consisting of 1-2 cells, stalk mostly 1-celled, occasionally 2 cells visible; non-glandular hairs mostly unicellular. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally visible [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green. Upper epidermal cells subpolygonal, with straight or slightly sinuous anticlinal walls. Lower epidermal cells irregular in shape, with sinuous anticlinal wall. Stomata anomocytic, subsidiary cells 4-5. Head of glandular hairs elliptical, consisting of 1-2 cells, stalk mostly 1-celled, occasionally 2 cells visible. Non-glandular hairs mostly unicellular, bent or straight, surface with fine warty protrusions. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, 3-13 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, occasionally visible in leaf, but densely distributed in persistent calyx fragment; bright white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 48, 1852, 97])\n\n京大戟\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 471, 1729, 2121])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Geranii Caroliniani Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Cuticle 3. Epidermis 4. Cortex 5. Pericycle fibre\n\n6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1693, 55, 1938, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 449, 1744, 2115])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Geranii Caroliniani Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration (petiole base)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Sclerenchyma 4. Spongy tissue 5. Xylem\n\n6. Phloem 7. Glandular hair 8. Lower epidermis 9. Non-glandular hair\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[248, 402, 1702, 2123])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Geranii Caroliniani Herba\n\n1. Upper epidermal cells with anomocytic stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n2. Lower epidermal cells with anomocytic stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) 3. Glandular hairs\n\n4. Non-glandular hairs 5. Clusters of calcium oxalate in calyx cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 40, 1946, 242])"},{"name":"13","text":"Corilagin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of corilagin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, formic acid and methanol (6:1.5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of water. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately corilagin standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 1558, 997, 1956])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of corilagin\n\n</div>\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 375, 904, 1207])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Geranii Caroliniani Herba extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Corilagin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of corilagin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Corilagin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of corilagin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 100 mL of water. Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue with 25 mL of water. Combine the extract. Evaporate the combined extract to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in water. Transfer the extract to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[29, 40, 1951, 243])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (266 nm) and a column $ ( 2. 1 \\times1 0 0 \\mathrm{~ m m} ) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 130 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and $ 1 8 5 \\mathrm{~ m}^{2} / \\mathrm{g} $ surface area). The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>93</td><td>7</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>3-4</td><td>93→85</td><td>7→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>4-16</td><td>85→65</td><td>15→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of corilagin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of corilagin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of corilagin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from corilagin peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject corilagin Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of corilagin peak in the chromatogram of corilagin Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify corilagin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of corilagin Std-FP. The retention times of corilagin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Geranii Caroliniani Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 42, 1970, 259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Geranii Caroliniani Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.26</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,corilagin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.02</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.23</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.26</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.56</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[330, 1051, 1816, 1653])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Geranii Caroliniani Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1690, 56, 1940, 138])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 24.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/geranii-caroliniani-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Geranii Caroliniani Herba","la":"Geranii Caroliniani Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ginseng-folium-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginseng Folium v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[663, 874, 950, 1153])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1064, 869, 1353, 1175])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[798, 1204, 1252, 1911])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ginseng Folium A. Ginseng Folium B. Upper surface of palmately compound leaf C. Lower surface of palmately compound leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 19, 1872, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[663, 874, 950, 1153])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1064, 869, 1353, 1175])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[798, 1204, 1252, 1911])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ginseng Folium A. Ginseng Folium B. Upper surface of palmately compound leaf C. Lower surface of palmately compound leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 19, 1872, 260])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Ginseng Folium\n\nChinese Name: 人参枼\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Renshenye"},{"name":"14","text":"Ginseng Folium is the dried leaf of Panax ginseng C. A. Mey. (Araliaceae). The leaf is collected in autumn, then dried under the sun or dried in a shaded area to obtain Ginseng Folium."},{"name":"14","text":"Leaves palmately compound, dark green to yellowish-green, petiolate, 10-36 cm long. Leaflets usually 5, occasionally 7 or 9, ovate or obovate, leaflet 2-16 cm long, 1-7 cm wide; base cuneate, apex acuminate, margin serrulate with setae. Upper surface with some setae; lower surface with protuberant veins. Texture papery and fragile. Odour slightly fragrant; taste predominantly bitter with slightly sweet aftertaste (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare to rectangular cells. Palisade tissue absent, mesophyll cells rounded or subrounded, with clusters of calcium oxalate visible. Collenchyma consists of 1-3 layers of cells, underneath the upper and lower epidermis of the midrib. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found in the midrib parenchymatous cells. Vascular bundles collateral. Xylem vessels radially arranged. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of irregularly shaped cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-green. Anticlinal walls of lower epidermal cells undulated and curved; stomata anomocytic, with 4-5 subsidiary cells, 3 subsidiary cells occasionally visible, surface of guard cells contain chloroplasts. Anticlinal walls of upper epidermal cells slightly curved,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 6, 1944, 259])\n\nanomocytic stomata occasionally visible. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, 12-42 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Scalariform and spiral vessels visible, reticulate vessels occasionally visible, 6-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Mesophyll cells rounded to subrounded (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 13, 1899, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 396, 1742, 2138])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Ginseng Folium A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate 1. Upper epidermis 2. Mesophyll 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4. Collenchyma 5. Vascular bundles 6. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 4, 1953, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 461, 1689, 2122])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Ginseng Folium\n\n1. Lower epidermal cells with stomata 2. Upper epidermal cells 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Vessel (4-1 scalariform vessel, 4-2 spiral vessel) 5. Mesophyll cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 25, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Ginsenoside Re standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of ginsenoside Re CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinsenoside $ R g_{1} $ standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.4 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, n-butanol and ethyl acetate (5:4:1, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ginsenoside Re standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n穿山龍 Dioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nGinseng Folium\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[443, 365, 1267, 1269])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[451, 1346, 1156, 2094])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 9, 1892, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[445, 367, 1003, 1073])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ (ii) ginsenoside Re and (iii) ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 1240, 1039, 1993])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ginseng Folium extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Ginsenoside Re standard solution 2. Ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ standard solution 3. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[42, 13, 1945, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Ginsenoside Re standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (170 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.7 mg of ginsenoside Re CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nGinsenoside $ R g_{1} $ standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (70 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.7 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (70%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Discard the petroleum ether layer. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Extract the residue in 20 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>79</td><td>21</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>79→70</td><td>21→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>70→68</td><td>30→32</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ginsenoside Re Std-FP and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks should not be less than 15000 and 12000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject ginsenoside Re Std-FP, ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks in the chromatograms of ginsenoside Re Std-FP, ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-FP and the retention times of the nine characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of ginsenoside Re Std-FP and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-FP. The retention times of ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the nine characteristic peaks of Ginseng Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the nine characteristic peaks of Ginseng Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,ginsenosideRg1)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(ginsenosideRe)</td><td>1.08</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.31</td><td>$\\pm0.06$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.39</td><td>$\\pm0.06$</td></tr><tr><td>5(ginsenosideRb1)</td><td>2.57</td><td>$\\pm0.05$</td></tr><tr><td>6(ginsenosideRc)</td><td>2.75</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>2.86</td><td>$\\pm0.06$</td></tr><tr><td>8(ginsenosideRb2)</td><td>2.94</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr><tr><td>9(ginsenosideRd)</td><td>3.39</td><td>$\\pm0.04$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 6, 1947, 256])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[384, 370, 1809, 1138])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ginseng Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above nine characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[32, 23, 1873, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 33.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 28.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"14","text":"Mixed ginsenoside $ R b_{1} $ ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ R g_{1} $ standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L for ginsenoside $ R b_{1} $ 500 mg/L for ginsenoside Re and 200 mg/L for ginsenoside $ R g_{1} $ ）\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ CRS (Fig. 4), 5.0 mg of ginsenoside Re CRS and 2.0 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nMixed ginsenoside Rb $ _{j} $ , ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ Rg_{1} $ standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ , ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (70%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.5, 2.5, 5, 25, 100 mg/L for ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ , 12.5, 25, 90, 125, 250 mg/L for ginsenoside Re and 5, 10, 36, 50, 100 mg/L for ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ ."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ginseng-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginseng Folium","la":"Ginseng Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ginseng-radix-et-rhizoma-rubra-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[516, 1099, 964, 1667])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[987, 1233, 1230, 1528])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1295, 1219, 1492, 1566])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra\n\nA. Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra (flat square-columnar root) (rhizome $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nB. Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra (fusiform root) (rhizome $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra (cylindrical root) (rhizome $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface of root\n\nE. Magnified image of upper part of root"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[516, 1099, 964, 1667])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[987, 1233, 1230, 1528])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1295, 1219, 1492, 1566])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra\n\nA. Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra (flat square-columnar root) (rhizome $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nB. Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra (fusiform root) (rhizome $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra (cylindrical root) (rhizome $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface of root\n\nE. Magnified image of upper part of root"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra\n\nChinese Name: 红参\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hongshen"},{"name":"14","text":"Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra is the steamed dried root and rhizome of the cultivar of Panax ginseng C.A. Mey. (Araliaceae). The root and rhizome are harvested in autumn, washed clean, steamed at $ 9 8^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ , then air-dried to obtain Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra."},{"name":"14","text":"Main roots fusiform, cylindrical or flat square-columnar, 2-12 cm long, 10-20 mm in diameter. Externally reddish-brown to yellowish-brown, occasionally exhibiting a few dark yellowish-brown patches, with longitudinal fissures, wrinkles and rootlet scars; the upper part sometimes exhibiting interrupted indistinct annulations, the lower part sometimes bearing 2-3 twisted and intersected branch roots and curved rootlets or just showing remnants of rootlets. Rhizomes 1-2 cm long, showing several impressed-circular stem scars, some bearing 1-2 entire or broken adventitious roots. Texture hard and fragile, fracture flat, horny. Odour delicately aromatic; taste sweet at first, then slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Cork consists of several layers of flat cells. Cortex narrow. Phloem showing clefts in the outer part, parenchymatous cells in the inner part densely arranged; resin canals scattered. Cambium in a ring. Xylem rays broad. Vessels singly scattered or in groups, with interrupted radial arrangement. Parenchymatous cells contain clusters of calcium oxalate, the crystals in rosette aggregate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[30, 32, 1957, 250])"},{"name":"14","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Fragments of resin canals contain yellow or yellowish-brown secretions. Cluster of calcium oxalate numerous, in rosette aggregate, 14-69 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Reticulate vessels and scalariform vessels visible, 15-71 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Gelatinization Starch granules irregular; outline indistinct. Cork cells polygonal in surface view, narrow-rectangular in lateral view, walls thickened (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[104, 188, 202, 238])\n\n天葵子\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黃\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[244, 445, 915, 1277])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[351, 1302, 862, 1607])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[353, 1624, 863, 2009])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[975, 493, 1695, 942])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[973, 956, 1695, 1415])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[973, 1423, 1696, 1962])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate D. Resin canal\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Resin canal 5. Phloem 6. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n7. Cambium 8. Xylem\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[925, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連銭草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[279, 509, 702, 937])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[726, 506, 1208, 937])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1228, 505, 1705, 944])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[280, 969, 703, 1398])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[725, 967, 1211, 1401])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1226, 963, 1703, 1398])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[281, 1425, 989, 1894])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1008, 1429, 1704, 1886])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Resin canal 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n3. Vessels (3-1 reticulate vessel, 3-2 scalariform vessel) 4. Gelatinized starch granules\n\n5. Cork cells (5-1 surface view, 5-2 lateral view)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"14","text":"Ginsenoside $ R b_{l} $ standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rb}_{1} $ CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nGinsenoside Re standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginsenoside Re CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nGinsenoside Rf standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginsenoside Rf CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nGinsenoside $ R g_{1} $ standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, n-butanol, ethyl acetate and methanol (5:4:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), ginsenoside Re standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), ginsenoside Rf standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[449, 363, 1173, 1144])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[446, 1214, 1155, 1952])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[476, 1999, 1173, 2611])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[672, 42, 817, 118])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[481, 370, 1029, 1071])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ (ii) ginsenoside Re (iii) ginsenoside Rf and (iv) ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 1296, 1054, 2102])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {R b} _ {1}\n$$\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rb}_{1} $ ginsenoside Re, ginsenoside Rf and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rg}_{1} $ (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[48, 42, 235, 122])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[925, 85, 1028, 138])"},{"name":"14","text":"Ginsenoside $ R b_{1} $ standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (350 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.5 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rb}_{1} $ CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nGinsenoside Re standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (150 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of ginsenoside Re CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nGinsenoside $ R g_{1} $ standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (350 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.5 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (70%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>81</td><td>19</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>81→69</td><td>19→31</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>69</td><td>31</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>69→60</td><td>31→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rb}_{1} $ Std-FP, ginsenoside Re Std-FP and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rg}_{1} $ Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rb}_{1} $ , ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rg}_{1} $ should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rb}_{1} $ ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rg}_{1} $ peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rb}_{1} $ , ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{Rg}_{1} $ peaks should not be less than 8000, 15000 and 8000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ Std-FP, ginsenoside Re Std-FP, ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ , ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks in the chromatograms of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ Std-FP, ginsenoside Re Std-FP, ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ , ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ Std-FP, ginsenoside Re Std-FP and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ Std-FP. The retention times of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ , ginsenoside Re and ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(ginsenosideRg1)</td><td>0.62</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2(ginsenosideRe)</td><td>0.63</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3(ginsenosideRf)</td><td>0.78</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,ginsenosideRb1)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(ginsenosideRc)</td><td>1.05</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>6(ginsenosideRb2)</td><td>1.09</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>7(ginsenosideRd)</td><td>1.18</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[347, 375, 1810, 1249])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 34.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 28.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ginseng-radix-et-rhizoma-rubra","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra","la":"Ginseng Radix et Rhizoma Rubra"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/glechomae-herba-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glechomae Herba v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[459, 990, 1554, 1719])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[459, 990, 1554, 1719])"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Glechomae Herba B. A single herb of Glechomae Herba C. Magnified image of upper surface of reniform leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of lower surface of reniform leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of upper surface of subcordate leaf\n\nF. Magnified image of lower surface of subcordate leaf\n\nG. Magnified image of fragment of stem\n\nH. Magnified image of cut surface of stem\n\nI. Stem node with adventitious roots"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Glechomae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 連錢草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Lianqiancao"},{"name":"13","text":"Glechoma Herba is the dried aerial part of Glechoma longituba (Nakai) Kupr. (Lamiaceae). The aerial part is collected from spring to autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Glechoma Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Herb 4-60 cm long, sparsely pubescent. Stems square-columnar, slender and twisted, 0.9-2.3 mm in diameter; externally yellowish-green to greenish-brown, with adventitious roots at the nodes; texture fragile, easily broken, fracture usually hollow in the centre. Leaves simple, opposite, mostly crumpled, reniform to subcordate, when whole and flattened, 1.5-5.0 cm long, 1.0-3.5 cm wide, greenish-brown to yellowish-green, margin crenate, both upper and lower surfaces sparsely pubescent; petiole slender, 2.5-11 cm long. Odour aromatic on rubbing; taste slightly bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Non-glandular and glandular hairs found on the surface of epidermis; stomata, remnants of glandular and non-glandular hairs occasionally visible. Non-glandular hairs mostly broken, conical-shaped, cell wall relatively thick. Glandular hairs small, head unicellular, subrounded to oblong, with yellow content; stalk unicellular and short. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subrectangular to subsquare cells, outer walls serratedly thickened. Collenchyma well developed at four angles of stem. Cortex consists of 3-9 layers of parenchymatous cells, cells subrounded, unequal in size. Endodermis distinct, consisting of 1 layer of tangentially-elongated parenchymatous cells. Pericycle fibre crescent-shaped and in bundles, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem located inside the pericycle fibres, consisting of 1-2 layers of flattened parenchymatous cells. Cambium distinct, arranged in an\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 36, 1948, 248])\n\ninterrupted ring. Xylem consists of 3-7 vessels, radially arranged, broader along four angles; vessels and xylem fibres alternatively arranged. Pith broad and distinct, usually hollow in the centre [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nLeaf: Non-glandular hairs found on the surface of both the upper and lower epidermis, bicellular, conical-shaped, cell wall relatively thick. Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells subrectangular to subsquare, covered with cuticle; glandular hairs, glandular scales and stomata occasionally visible. Mesophyll consists of 2-3 layers of subrounded parenchymatous cells, palisade tissue sometimes obscurely visible. Collenchyma consists of several layers of cells located on the upper and lower parts of the midrib. Xylem occupies about 2/3 of the vascular bundle; vessels orderly arranged. Phloem hoof-shaped. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells subrectangular to subsquare, covered with cuticle; glandular hairs, glandular scales and stomata occasionally visible [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green. Non-glandular hairs 3 types: the first type multicellular, the cell wall slightly thickened, some with warty protuberances; the second type multicellular, with 1 to several cells frequently shrunken; the third type unicellular and long-conical, a few contains brownish-yellow contents in cell cavity. Head of glandular hair unicellular, subrounded to elliptic, 12-30 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; stalk unicellular. The anticlinal walls of the upper epidermal cells of leaf sinuous, with relatively fine and dense parallel cuticular striations; stomata sometimes observed. The cell walls of the lower epidermal cells of the leaf sinuous, stomata frequently observed, oblong to subrounded in shape, diacytic. Glandular scale subspherical or oblate, with a head of 8 cells, arranged radially; stalk very short, some contain brownish-yellow contents. Vessels mostly spiral and bordered-pitted, 10-39 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王 Wikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 237])\n\n天葵子\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 417, 1740, 2094])"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C-D. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Glandular hair 3. Epidermis 4. Collenchyma\n\n5. Cortex 6. Endodermis 7. Pericycle fibre 8. Phloem 9. Cambium\n\n10. Xylem 11. Pith\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[233, 402, 1748, 2113])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Glechomae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Mesophyll 4. Collenchyma\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Lower epidermis\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n天葵子\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[244, 425, 1719, 2117])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Glechomae Herba (under light microscope)\n\n1. Non-glandular hairs (1-1 multicellular, 1-2 with shrunken cells, 1-3 unicellular)\n\n2. Glandular hair 3. Upper epidermal cells of leaf with stomata (->)\n\n4. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with stomata (->) 5. Glandular scale\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 spiral vessels, 6-2 bordered-pitted vessel)\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[44, 38, 1950, 245])"},{"name":"13","text":"Caffeic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of caffeic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nRosmarinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-hexane and formic acid (10:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately caffeic acid standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), rosmarinic acid standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[672, 42, 817, 118])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 368, 831, 558])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[449, 656, 1037, 897])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) caffeic acid and (ii) rosmarinic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[375, 1058, 849, 1886])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Glechomae Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Caffeic acid standard solution 2. Rosmarinic acid standard solution 3. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of caffeic acid and rosmarinic acid (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[44, 39, 1951, 244])"},{"name":"13","text":"Caffeic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of caffeic acid CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nRosmarinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 80 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue for four times each with 5 mL of methanol (70%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-70</td><td>80→50</td><td>20→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of caffeic acid Std-FP and rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of caffeic acid and rosmarinic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of caffeic acid and rosmarinic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from caffeic acid and rosmarinic acid peaks should not be less than 17000 and 80000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject caffeic acid Std-FP, rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of caffeic acid and rosmarinic acid peaks in the chromatograms of caffeic acid Std-FP, rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify caffeic acid and rosmarinic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of caffeic acid Std-FP and rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The retention times of caffeic acid and rosmarinic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Glechomae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Glechomae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.31</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(caffeic acid)</td><td>0.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(marker,rosmarinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[337, 1663, 1806, 2106])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Glechomae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[49, 45, 234, 123])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[926, 89, 1025, 138])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 11.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/glechomae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glechomae Herba","la":"Glechomae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/gynostemmatis-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gynostemmatis Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[669, 822, 1383, 1414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Gynostemmatis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[634, 1755, 1379, 2416])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of leaves and fruits of Gynostemmatis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1900, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[669, 822, 1383, 1414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Gynostemmatis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[634, 1755, 1379, 2416])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of leaves and fruits of Gynostemmatis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1900, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Gynostematis Herba\n\nChinese Name: 絞股藍\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jiaogulan"},{"name":"14","text":"Gynostemmatis Herba is the dried aerial part of Gynostemma pentaphyllum (Thunb.) Makino (Cucurbitaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Gynostemmatis Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Stems slender, green, greyish-green or greyish-brown, 1-7 mm in diameter, externally with longitudinal grooves and covered by fine pubescence. Compound leaves greyish-brown, greyish-green or green, usually broken; leaflets usually 5-7, membranous; petioles 1-6 cm long; lateral leaflets ovate-oblong or oblong-lanceolate; central leaflet larger, 0.6-6 cm long, 0.5-5.5 cm wide; apex acuminate, base cuneate; pilose on both surfaces; margin serrate, with arista at the apex of teeth. Fruits occasionally present, spherical and green. Odour grassy; taste slightly sweet [Fig. 1 (i) or 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Leaf: Upper and lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, with non-glandular hairs at the vein; the upper epidermal cells tangentially elongated. Mesophyll consists of 1-2 layers of palisade tissue and 3-4 layers of spongy tissue. Vascular bundle of midrib collateral. Collenchyma consists 2-3 layers of cells, arranged in the inner sides of upper and lower epidermis of midrib (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[69, 9, 1972, 259])\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of flat cells, with thickened and cuticulate walls; sometimes with non-glandular hairs. Collenchyma consists of 4-6 layers of cells, located at the corners of the stem. Cortex relatively narrow. Pericycle fibre bundles crescent-shaped, arranged in a complete or interrupted ring. Bicollateral vascular bundles 9-10, varying in size, arranged radially. Stone cells in groups located between pericycle fibre bundles. Pith relatively large. Parenchymatous cells contain starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour green to greenish-brown. Glandular hairs pale yellow or colourless, consisting of 4-celled elliptical head and 1- to 2-celled stalk, 19-47 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Non-glandular hairs consist of 1-12 cells, 29-646 $ \\mu $ m long and 9-135 $ \\mu $ m in diameter at base, with linear striations on the surface, sometimes middle cells narrow. Stone cells pale yellow or colourless, subsquare, subrounded or irregular in shape, 27-219 $ \\mu $ m long and 15-94 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, pits and pit canals distinct. Upper epidermal cells of leaf subpolygonal or irregular in surface view, with shrivelled surface, anticlinal walls almost straight. Lower epidermal cells irregular in shape, stomata anomocytic, anticlinal walls slightly wavy, with glandular hairs. Epidermal cells of stem subrectangular in surface view, stomata visible. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles, 5-47 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with distinct pits and pit canals; white, yellow or polychromatic under the polarized light microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate and spiral, bordered-pitted vessels occasionally visible, 5-89 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Simple starch granules elongated-ellipsoid, ellipsoid or subrounded, 6-49 $ \\mu $ m long and 3-25 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum and striations indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized light microscope; compound starch granules rare (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[54, 8, 1906, 266])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 409, 1745, 2053])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf and stem of Gynostemmatis Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch of midvein B. Section illustration of midvein\n\nC. Sketch of stem D. Section illustration of stem E. Partial magnified of Photo D\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Collenchyma 4. Palisade tissue\n\n5. Spongy tissue 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Lower epidermis 9. Epidermis\n\n10. Cortex 11. Pericycle fibres 12. Stone cells 13. Cambium 14. Pith 15. Starch granules\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n繢斷 Gynostemmatis Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 455, 1716, 2006])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Gynostemmatis Herba\n\n1. Glandular hair 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Stone cells 4. Upper epidermal cells of leaf\n\n5. Lower epidermal cells of leaf 6. Epidermal cells of stem 7. Fibres 8. Reticulate vessels\n\n9. Spiral vessels 10. Bordered-pitted vessels 11. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 6, 1903, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Ombuoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of ombuoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Place it in a water bath at about $ 60^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to dissolve the standard.\n\nRutin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of rutin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid, water and methanol (8:1:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ombuoside standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), rutin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 8 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of ombuoside and rutin.\n\nma 餘甘子 Phyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Gynostemmatis Herba Rhizoma\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[449, 366, 955, 700])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[447, 773, 1140, 1374])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[445, 1441, 894, 1897])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) ombuin (ii) ombuoside and (iii) panaxadiol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 4, 1913, 263])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[371, 359, 1127, 1362])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (iv) quercetin and (v) rutin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"Ombuin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of ombuin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nOmbuoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (35 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.35 mg of ombuoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Place it in a water bath at about $ 60^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to dissolve the standard.\n\nQuercetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of quercetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nRutin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (120 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of rutin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 10, 1968, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (370 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>90→80</td><td>10→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-50</td><td>80→30</td><td>20→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>30→0</td><td>70→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ombuin Std-FP, ombuoside Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and rutin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of ombuin, ombuoside, quercetin and rutin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of ombuin, ombuoside, quercetin and rutin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from ombuin, ombuoside, quercetin and rutin peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[22, 4, 1903, 265])"},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject ombuin Std-FP, ombuoside Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP, rutin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of ombuin, ombuoside, quercetin and rutin peaks in the chromatograms of ombuin Std-FP, ombuoside Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP, rutin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ombuin, ombuoside, quercetin and rutin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of ombuin Std-FP, ombuoside Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and rutin Std-FP. The retention times of ombuin, ombuoside, quercetin and rutin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Gynostemmatis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Gynostemmatis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(rutin)</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(ombuoside)</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(quercetin)</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.87</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, ombuin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\nCyperi Rhizoma\n\n香附\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[301, 366, 1864, 1032])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Gynostemmatis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[28, 9, 1902, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"14","text":"Panaxadiol standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of panaxadiol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nPanaxadiol standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the panaxadiol Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 16, 40, 80, 200, 500 mg/L for panaxadiol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/gynostemmatis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gynostemmatis Herba","la":"Gynostemmatis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hedyotidis-diffusae-herba-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[517, 883, 1429, 1777])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\nA. Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba B. Magnified image of capsules\n\nC. A leaf branch with flower D. Root"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[517, 883, 1429, 1777])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\nA. Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba B. Magnified image of capsules\n\nC. A leaf branch with flower D. Root"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 白花蛇舌草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baihuasheshecao"},{"name":"14","text":"Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba is the dried whole plant of Hedyotis diffusa Willd. (Rubiaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Usually tangled into masses or cut into sections, varying in length, externally greyish-yellow to greyish-green. Main root slightly curved, bearing numerous rootlets. Stems slender, slightly compressed, many branched at the base. Leaves simple opposite, sessile, mostly crumpled and broken, linear to linear-lanceolate when intact flattened out; apex acute, margin slightly curved inwards. Flowers solitary or forming a paired inflorescences funnelform, frequently fallen off. Capsules solitary or opposite in leaf axis, compressed globose, 1-4 mm in diameter, loculicidal. Calyx persistent, 4-lobed at the upper part. Texture fragile, easily broken. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Root: Cork consists of several layers of subrounded to subrectangular cells, arranged densely Cortex scattered with numerous raphides of calcium oxalate which sometimes present in phloem. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad; vessels arranged radially [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[37, 34, 1949, 254])\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare to ovate cells. Cortex narrow, scattered with raphides and clusters of calcium oxalate. Endodermis consists of 1 layer of subrounded to irregular cells. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad, arranged in a ring. Pith broad, mostly occupied 1/2 portion of the diameter of stem, containing numerous starch granules [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of large subsquare cells. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of irregularly shaped cells, scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate. Spongy tissue consists of loosely arranged cells, scattered with raphides of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundle of midrib amphicribrals; xylem vessels arranged radially. Collenchyma located in the inner side of lower epidermis at the midrib. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of relatively small cells [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-yellow to greyish-green. Raphides of calcium oxalate abundant, slender, 21-189 $ \\mu m $ long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains subspherical, 19-35 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with distinct reticulate sculptures on the surface. Lower epidermal cells polygonal or irregular in shape; stomata paracytic, arranged densely. Fibres of pericarp long fusiform or strip-shaped, walls slightly thickened and lignified, always in group and arranged in a crisscross pattern; bright white under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate small, 4-16 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, usually in rosette aggregate; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mostly spiral, reticulate vessels sometimes visible. Starch granules abundant, mainly simple, broadly ovoid, long spheroidal or irregularly spheroidal, 4-16 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; striations mostly indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 202, 242])\n\n天葵子\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 388, 1744, 2135])\n\n1. Cork 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate 3. Cortex 4. Phloem 5. Cambium\n\n6. Xylem\n\nBletillae Rhizoma 白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[255, 379, 1711, 2147])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope) E. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nF. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Cluster of calcium oxalate 4. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Endodermis 6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Pith 10. Starch granules\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[103, 188, 202, 241])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[244, 421, 1751, 2138])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) D. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope) E. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) F. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Raphides of calcium oxalate 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Collenchyma 9. Lower epidermis\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[197, 146, 289, 188])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[700, 140, 829, 191])\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膪草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[303, 698, 617, 1025])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[652, 693, 969, 1027])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1007, 697, 1322, 1023])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1358, 697, 1681, 1019])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[302, 1064, 740, 1390])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[773, 1062, 1212, 1390])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1245, 1066, 1681, 1390])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[303, 1428, 518, 1755])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[558, 1429, 952, 1753])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[993, 1429, 1315, 1755])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1357, 1425, 1683, 1754])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Pollen grains\n\n3. Lower epidermal cells with paracytic stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n4. Fibres of pericarp 5. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 spiral vessel, 6-2 reticulate vessel) 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"14","text":"Asperuloside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of asperuloside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol and water (8:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately asperuloside standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 7 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 1943, 889, 2337])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of asperuloside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[924, 86, 1028, 139])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 373, 778, 1197])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Asperuloside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of asperuloside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Asperuloside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of asperuloside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (240 nm) and a column $ (4.6\\times 250\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 35^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>95→90</td><td>5→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>90→85</td><td>10→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>85→82</td><td>15→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of asperuloside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of asperuloside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of asperuloside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from asperuloside peak should not be less than 60000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject asperuloside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of asperuloside peak in the chromatogram of asperuloside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify asperuloside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of asperuloside Std-FP. The retention times of asperuloside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[37, 35, 1947, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.30</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.83</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,asperuloside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[340, 884, 1803, 1259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): The CMM shall meet the requirements for arsenic, lead and mercury as specified in Appendix V. For cadmium, Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba should meet the specified limit of not more than 5.0 mg/kg, when the CMM will be processed as a decoction in the final consumption form; otherwise, the limit for cadmium specified in Appendix V shall be applied.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Total ash: not more than 12.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hedyotidis-diffusae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba","la":"Hedyotidis Diffusae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hedysari-radix-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hedysari Radix v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1241, 1292, 1390, 1443])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1239, 1526, 1405, 1656])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hedysari Radix\n\nA. Hedysari Radix B. Magnified image of root\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\nD. Magnified image of fracture of root"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1241, 1292, 1390, 1443])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1239, 1526, 1405, 1656])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hedysari Radix\n\nA. Hedysari Radix B. Magnified image of root\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\nD. Magnified image of fracture of root"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Hedysari Radix\n\nChinese Name: 红芪\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hongqi"},{"name":"13","text":"Hedysari Radix is the dried root of Hedysarum polybotrys Hand.-Mazz. (Fabaceae). The root is collected in spring and autumn, rootlets and top part removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Hedysari Radix."},{"name":"13","text":"Long cylindrical, straight, rarely branched, 3-24 mm in diameter. Externally dull reddish-brown, with longitudinal wrinkles, scanty rootlet scars and transversely elongated lenticel-like protrusions. Outer bark easily exfoliated, exposing yellowish inner layer. Texture hard and tough, uneasily broken, fracture fibrous and starchy, bark yellowish-white, wood pale yellowish-brown, cambium ring brown, with radial striations. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet, with slight soybean-like flavour when chewed (Fig.1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Cork consists of several layers of flat cells, sometimes fallen off. Phelloderm narrow, some cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate. Phloem broad, fibre bundles abundant, surrounded by cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres, rays distinct, usually with clefts on the outer side. Cambium in a ring. Xylem consists of several vessels singly scattered or in groups, always accompanied by crystal fibres, rays distinct, sometimes scattered with clefts on the inner side. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Fibres numerous, mainly in bundles, 4-28 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, usually surrounded by cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres, walls of crystal cells unevenly thickened; bright polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalates abundant, scattered or present in crystal cells, double-conical, polygonal, subsquare or rectangular, 2-21 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, 4-37 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long; polychromatic or yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Starch granules numerous, simple starch granules subrounded or elliptical, 1-23 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum dotted, slit-shaped or stellate; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-9 units. Cork cells colourless to yellowish-brown, rectangular, subsquare or polygonal in surface view. Vessels mainly reticulate and bordered-pitted, 3-148 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[61, 36, 1939, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[383, 412, 855, 1132])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[296, 1195, 922, 1585])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[298, 1649, 913, 2011])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[999, 471, 1651, 2000])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Hedysari Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of phelloderm D. Magnified image of crystal fibre\n\n1. Cork 2. Phelloderm 3. Cleft 4. Phloem fibre 5. Phloem\n\n6. Phloem ray 7. Cambium 8. Xylem fibre 9. Xylem\n\n10. Xylem ray 11. Prism of calcium oxalate 12. Starch granule\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nHedysari Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[362, 455, 1623, 2119])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Hedysari Radix\n\n1. Crystal fibres (1-1 crystal fibres, 1-2 singly scattered fibre)\n\n2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Starch granules 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Vessels (5-1 reticulate vessel, 5-2 bordered-pitted vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"13","text":"Formonetin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of formononetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nOnonin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ononin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and methanol (4:4:1.3, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 3 mL of sulphuric acid to 97 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 4 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately formonetin standard solution (0.2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) , ononin standard solution (0.3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[374, 368, 901, 588])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[424, 660, 1070, 925])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) formonetin and (ii) ononin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 1083, 830, 1869])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Hedysari Radix extract observed under UV light (254 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Ononin standard solution 2. Formononetin standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of formonetin and ononin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Formonetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of formonetin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nOnonin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of ononin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture in a water bath at $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Wash the residue with methanol. Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (230 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>92→80</td><td>8→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-35</td><td>80→55</td><td>20→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-50</td><td>55→40</td><td>45→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of formonetin Std-FP and ononin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of formonetin and ononin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of formonetin and ononin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from formonetin and ononin peaks should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 36, 1940, 263])\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject formonetin Std-FP, ononin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of formonetin and ononin peaks in the chromatograms of formonetin Std-FP, ononin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify formonetin and ononin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of formonetin Std-FP and ononin Std-FP. The retention times of formonetin and ononin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Hedysari Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Hedysari Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.29</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.45</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,ononin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(formononetin)</td><td>1.31</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[339, 1762, 1807, 2297])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hedysari Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[61, 33, 1932, 261])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 32.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 25.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hedysari-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hedysari Radix","la":"Hedysari Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-andrographidis-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Andrographidis v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[428, 799, 1554, 1838])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[42, 1, 1945, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[428, 799, 1554, 1838])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[42, 1, 1945, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Herba Andrographidis\n\nChinese Name: 穿心蓮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chuanxinlian"},{"name":"12","text":"Herba Andrographidis is the dried aerial parts of Andrographis paniculata (Burm. f.) Nees (Acanthaceae). The herb is collected in early autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Herba Andrographidis."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem quadrangular and frequently branched, 2-50 cm long, 2-5 mm in diameter, nodes slightly swollen. Texture fragile, easily broken. Leaves simple, opposite, petiole short or nearly sessile. Lamina crumpled and easily broken, but when wholly intact, lanceolate to ovate-lanceolate, the apex acuminate, the base cuneate-decurrent, and the margins entire to undulate; the upper surface green, the lower surface greyishgreen, glabrous on both surfaces. Odour slight; taste extremely bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of a layer of cutinized cells with a few glandular hairs; some cells contain a crystal of calcium carbonate (cystolith). Collenchyma dense at the four corners of the stems. Parenchyma tissue in cortex contains chloroplastids. Endodermis is composed of a layer of thicker-walled cells. Phloem narrow. Xylem well developed, composed of vessels, xylem fibres and xylem ray cells. The pith is composed of large parenchyma cells, with small acicular crystals of calcium oxalate occurring throughout.\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of a layer of subsquare or rectangular cells, some containing a globular to elongated-ellipsoid to clavate cystolith crystal. Coniform, 1-4-celled non-glandular hairs of up to 195 $ \\mu $ m in length, and sometimes glandular scales are also present. Palisade tissue consisting of a row of columnar cells lines up the upper part of the midrib, with collenchyma tissue beneath the epidermis. The spongy tissue consists of 4-5 rows of loosely organized parenchyma\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1966, 249])\n\ncells, with large air spaces present. Xylem tissue is situated on the upper part, while the phloem on the lower part; they make up the collateral and grooved vascular bundles, with crystal-containing cells occurring above the xylem. Lower epidermis consists of a layer of subsquare cells, with cystoliths present (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour green. Coniform, 1-4-celled non-glandular hairs of up to 195 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long are present, their tip may be blunt or sharp, and with cuticular striations at base. Vessels are of spiral or reticulate type, 11-43 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Epidermis with glandular hairs are found. Ovoid, ellipsoid, subglobular cystolith-containing cells abundant, 36-148 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 12-45 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide. Most of the stomata are diacytic. 4-, 6- or 8-celled glandular scales are present, 22-37 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Andrographolide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of andrographolide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nDehydroandrographolide standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of dehydroandrographolide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and ethanol (4:2:1, v/v).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[102, 6, 206, 60])\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[861, 21, 1087, 116])\n\n浙貝母\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nHerba Andrographidis Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1064, 239])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 437, 1736, 2083])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Herba Andrographidis\n\nA. Sketch of stem B. Section illustration of stem\n\nC. Sketch of leaf D. Section illustration of leaf\n\nE. A cystolith crystal shown in leaf\n\n1. Epidermal cells 2. Collenchyma 3. Cystolith 4. Cortex 5. Endodermis 6. Phloem\n\n7. Xylem 8. Pith with acicular crystal of calcium oxalate 9. Upper epidermal cells 10. Palisade tissue\n\n11. Spongy tissue 12. Lower epidermal cells\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n北沙参\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1363, 63, 1446, 105])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[256, 459, 1673, 1841])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Herba Andrographidis (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Non-glandular hairs 2. Vessels 3. Epidermis with a glandular hair and cystolith\n\n4. Cystoliths 5. Epidermis with glandular scales and stoma (surface view)\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[44, 0, 1944, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately andrographolide standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and dehydroandrographolide standard solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[716, 1589, 1154, 2009])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[715, 2070, 1152, 2488])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) andrographolide and (ii) dehydroandrographolide\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[23, 1, 1961, 248])"},{"name":"12","text":"Andrographolide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of andrographolide CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nDehydroandrographolide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of dehydroandrographolide CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatant. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (215 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>78→75</td><td>22→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-30</td><td>75→70</td><td>25→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>70→55</td><td>30→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of andrographolide Std-FP and dehydroandrographolide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide peaks should not be less than 8000 and 40000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig.5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[30, 1, 1937, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject andrographolide Std-FP, dehydroandrographolide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide peaks in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing their retention times with those in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP. The retention times of andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Herba Andrographidis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Herba Andrographidis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker 1, andrographolide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.85 (vs peak 4)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.97 (vs peak 4)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4 (marker 2, dehydroandrographolide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[389, 1782, 1751, 2421])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Herba Andrographidis extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[34, 5, 1959, 252])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 13.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%.\n\n5. 8 Leaves: not less than 30%.\n\nNote: The leaves ratio refers to the weight of leaves against the total weight of the CMM sample."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 15.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L\n\nfor andrographolide and 100 mg/L for dehydroandrographolide)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of andrographolide CRS and 2.5 mg of dehydroandrographolide CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 3, 1942, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Measure accurately the volume of the mixed andrographolide and dehydroandrographolide Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 20, 40, 60, 100, 150 mg/L for andrographolide and 10, 20, 30, 50, 75 mg/L for dehydroandrographolide."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-andrographidis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Andrographidis","la":"Herba Andrographidis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-desmodii-styracifolii-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Desmodii Styracifolii v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[607, 627, 1413, 1348])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[598, 1656, 1418, 2414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii (The dried herbs were soaked in water to obtain specimen)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[41, 0, 1967, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[607, 627, 1413, 1348])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[598, 1656, 1418, 2414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii (The dried herbs were soaked in water to obtain specimen)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[41, 0, 1967, 252])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Herba Desmodii Styracifolii\n\nChinese Name: 廣金錢草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Guangjinqiancao"},{"name":"13","text":"Herba Desmodii Styracifolii is the dried aerial part of Desmodium styracifolium (Osb.) Merr. (Fabaceae/ Leguminosae). The aerial part is collected in the summer and autumn, after removal of foreign matter, it is dried under the sun to obtain Herba Desmodii Styracifolii."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem cylindrical, up to 1 m long, densely covered with yellow, spreading pubescence. Texture slightly fragile; fracture shows a pith in the center. Leaves alternate, leaflets 1-3, rounded or oblong, 15-52 mm in diameter; retuse at the apex, cordate or obtusely rounded at the base, margin entire. The upper surface yellowish-green or greyish-green, glabrous, the lower surface densely covered with white, silky tomentum, lateral veins pinnate. Petiole 1-3 cm long; stipules 2, lanceolate about 0.8 cm long. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly sweet [Fig. 1(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of a layer of cells, with hooked and straight non-glandular hair. Glandular hairs, which are enlarged or swollen on the lower part. Pericycle fibre bundles distinct, connected into rings. Parenchyma cells contain pigment masses or prisms of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis consist of one layer of cells, with non-glandular and glandular hairs on the lower surface. Palisade and spongy tissues consist of palisade-shaped parenchyma\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1954, 252])\n\ncells and irregular or subround parenchyma cells, respectively. Parenchyma cells contain pigment masses or prisms of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-green to pale green. Non-glandular hairs abundant; straight non-glandular hairs up to 1300 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length, the tip gradually sharpened; curved non-glandular hairs up to 260 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length, the tip bent into a hook. Glandular hairs club-like in shape, enlarged and expanded at base; the cells of the enlarged base vary in number, up to about 20 cells, the head consists of 1-2 cells. Epidermal cells of the stem subsquare, subrectangular or polygonal in shape, bearing non-glandular hairs, sometimes glandular hair and stoma observed on the surface. Fibres often in bundles, surrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres, showing a polychrome when examined under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, spiral and scalariform. The stoma mostly of the paracytic type. Pigment masses orange or orange-brown [Fig. 3(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 15 mL of water. Boil the mixture in a water bath for 10 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer 5 mL of the supernatant to a test tube. Evaporate the solution to about 0.5 mL on a water bath. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol. Transfer 1.5 mL of the solution to a test tube. Add approximately 25 mg of magnesium turnings to the mixture, then add 0.5 mL of hydrochloric acid. Allow to stand for 30 min. A reddish-brown solution is observed."},{"name":"13","text":"Isovitexin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isovitexin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of tetrahydrofuran, methanol, water and formic acid (4:6:14:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Dissolve 1.0 g of aluminium trichloride in 100 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 427, 1741, 2132])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii A. Stem sketch B. Stem C. Non-glandular hair D. Glandular hair E. Pigment masses F. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Glandular hair 3. Epidermis 4. Cortex 5. Pericycle fibres 6. Pigment masses\n\n7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Pith 10. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 391, 1737, 1532])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii\n\n</div>\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Fibre bundle 4. Spongy tissue 5. Xylem 6. Non-glandular hair\n\n7. Pigment masses 8. Phloem 9. Pericycle fibres 10. Lower epidermis 11. Glandular hair\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 259])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[390, 435, 1603, 1910])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Epidermal cells of the stem with non-glandular hairs and stomata 2. Glandular hair\n\n3. Hooked non-glandular hair 4. Straight non-glandular hair\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[25, 7, 1950, 258])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[349, 454, 995, 869])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1007, 454, 1284, 872])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[346, 881, 993, 1221])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1294, 455, 1620, 871])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1005, 886, 1619, 1218])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[345, 1230, 940, 1510])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[950, 1230, 1616, 1510])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii\n\n1. Spiral vessel 2. Scalariform vessel 3. Bordered-pitted vessel 4. Crystal fibres 5. Stomata\n\n6. Pigment masses\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1973, 252])"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 30 mL of water. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separatory funnel. Shake the solution with 100 mL of dichloromethane and discard the lower layer. Extract twice each with 100 mL of 1-butanol. Combine the 1-butanol extracts, wash with 100 mL of water and discard the lower layer. Evaporate the 1-butanol extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC RP-18 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ (40-63 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ ) plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately isovitexin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (365 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of isovitexin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[673, 1549, 1402, 1963])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of isovitexin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Isovitexin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isovitexin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m RC filter. Repeat the extraction twice. Combine the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m RC filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (340 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.7 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>85</td><td>15</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-60</td><td>85→78</td><td>15→22</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of isovitexin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of isovitexin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of isovitexin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from isovitexin peak should not be less than 26000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and peak 4 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject isovitexin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of isovitexin peak in the chromatogram of isovitexin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify isovitexin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of isovitexin Std-FP. The retention times of isovitexin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[55, 0, 1969, 251])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.79</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, isovitexin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[402, 946, 1753, 1455])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Herba Desmodii Styracifolii extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[30, 5, 1908, 245])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 18.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 12.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-desmodii-styracifolii","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Desmodii Styracifolii","la":"Herba Desmodii Styracifolii"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-ephedrae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Ephedrae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 738, 1966, 2008])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Herba Ephedrae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1965, 246])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 738, 1966, 2008])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Herba Ephedrae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1965, 246])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Herba Ephedrae\n\nChinese Name: 麻黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Mahuang"},{"name":"12","text":"Herba Ephedrae is the dried herbaceous stem of Ephedra sinica Stapf (Ephedraceae). The green herbaceous stem is collected in early autumn dried in shade, or dried to a half-dry condition in shade and then fully dried under the sun to obtain Herba Ephedrae."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem very slender, cylindrical, slightly flattened, with few branches, 1-2 mm in diameter. Some plants have brown, woody stems. Externally pale green to yellowish-green, with fine longitudinal ridges, slightly rough. Nodes distinct, internodes 2-6 cm long. Scaly leaves on the nodes membranous, 3-4 mm long, lower part 1/4-1/2 connate, tubular, reddish-brown in colour, the upper part with 2 lobes (rarely 3), lobes acutely triangular, apex greyish-white, reflexed. Texture of stem light, fragile, easily broken, fracture slightly fibrous with yellowish-green edge and dark reddish-brown suborbicular pith. Odour slightly fragrant; taste slightly bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Subround and slightly oblate. Epidermal cells covered with thick cuticle. Ridges 20-32, in an undulating formation, with sunken stomata located between two ridges. Hypodermal fibre bundles are located just below the epidermis of the ridges, with thickened and unlignified wall. Cortex relatively broad, with few fibre bundles. Pericycle fibre bundles crescent-shaped. Collateral vascular bundles about 10, interruptedly arranged in a ring, phloem narrow, cambium ring subround, the\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 0, 1954, 250])\n\nxylem triangular. The xylem of young stems consists of several separate triangular bundles, while that of old ones forming a ring. Pith broad, parenchyma cells containing brown masses, occasionally showing perimedullary fibres. The outer wall of epidermal cells, cortex fibres and cortex parenchyma cells are covered by numerous fine sandy crystals or prisms of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Pale brown in colour. Epidermis fragments commonly observed, transverse section view of cells subrectangular, covered with cuticle layer which up to 31 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ outer wall covered with numerous sandy crystals of calcium oxalate. Stomata peculiar, sunken, long-rounded, guard cells phone-shaped and with two rather thick ends in lateral view. Fibres slender, 3-26 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with thickened wall; sometimes the wall covered with sandy crystals of calcium oxalate to form crystalinlaid fibres. Polychrome when observed under the polarized microscope. Sandy crystals of calcium oxalate numerous, occurring on the outer wall of epidermal cells, cortex fibres and cortex parenchyma cells. Intensely white to polychrome under the polarized microscope. Spiral and bordered pitted vessels, fine, 3-42 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, perforation plates with numerous round perforations (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Ninhydrin solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 g of ninhydrin and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 4 mL of hydrochloric acid (6.2% , v/v). Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 15 min. Allow the solid residue to settle. Transfer the supernatant to another test tube, adjust the pH to about 12 with sodium hydroxide solution (20%, w/v), then add 4 mL of dichloromethane, vortex. Collect the lower layer and evaporate to dryness on a warm water bath (50-60 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol. Spot the solution onto a filter paper with a capillary. Spray the filter paper evenly with ninhydrin solution and heat at about $ 110^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). A purplish-blue spot is observed.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[12, 0, 1984, 254])\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n\n5\n\n6\n\n7\n\n8\n\n9\n\nA\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[462, 883, 878, 1106])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[462, 1126, 880, 1450])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[463, 1464, 872, 1799])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[898, 576, 1578, 1689])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 μm\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Herba Ephedrae\n\nA. Sketch (of the young stem) B. Section illustration C. Epidermis and sunken stoma\n\nD. Pericycle fibre bundles E. Sandy crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Stomata 3. Hypodermal fibre bundles 4. Cortex 5. Pericycle fibre bundles 6. Phloem\n\n7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[22, 3, 1962, 252])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[384, 456, 1598, 1595])"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Epidermis fragments with stoma 2. Epidermis fragments with cuticle 3. Surface view of stomata\n\n4. Lateral view of stomata 5. Phloem fibres 6. Sandy crystals of calcium oxalate 7. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1967, 243])"},{"name":"12","text":"Ephedrine hydrochloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of ephedrine hydrochloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and ammonium solution (20:5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of ninhydrin and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 0.5 mL of ammonium solution and 10 mL of dichloromethane. Reflux the mixture for 60 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifugal tube. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol and filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ephedrine hydrochloride standard solution and the test solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of ephedrine hydrochloride.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 0, 1956, 254])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[864, 403, 1259, 824])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[861, 896, 1254, 1309])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) ephedrine hydrochloride and (ii) pseudoephedrine hydrochloride\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Ephedrine hydrochloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (24 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.4 mg of ephedrine hydrochloride CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 50 mL of a mixture of methanol and 0.01 M potassium phosphate, monobasic solution (3:97, v/v). Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (210 nm) and a Ether-linked Phenyl bonded silica gel with Polar Endcapping column $ (4. 6 \\times1 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ $ (4 \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, pH:1.5-7.0). The flow rate is about 1.5 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of methanol and 0.1 M potassium phosphate, monobasic solution (3:97, v/v). The elution time is about 40 min.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[41, 0, 1967, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ephedrine hydrochloride Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ephedrine hydrochloride should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ephedrine hydrochloride peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ephedrine hydrochloride peak should not be less than 3000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject ephedrine hydrochloride Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of ephedrine hydrochloride peak in the chromatogram of the ephedrine hydrochloride Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify ephedrine hydrochloride peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of ephedrine hydrochloride Std-FP. The retention times of ephedrine hydrochloride peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Herba Ephedrae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Herba Ephedrae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.58</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, ephedrine hydrochloride)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(pseudoephedrine hydrochloride)</td><td>1.18</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 0, 1944, 252])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[401, 363, 1758, 958])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Herba Ephedrae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[40, 0, 1968, 248])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-ephedrae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Ephedrae","la":"Herba Ephedrae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-leonuri-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Leonuri v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 862, 921, 1137])\n\n0. 5cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1090, 930, 1318, 1099])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[578, 1163, 1444, 1736])\n\n1 cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Herba Leonuri A. Stem node B. Internode C. Aerial part\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1941, 254])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 862, 921, 1137])\n\n0. 5cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1090, 930, 1318, 1099])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[578, 1163, 1444, 1736])\n\n1 cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Herba Leonuri A. Stem node B. Internode C. Aerial part\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1941, 254])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Herba Leonuri\n\nChinese Name: 益母草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yimucao"},{"name":"13","text":"Herba Leonuri is the dried aerial parts of Leonurus japonicus Houtt. (Lamiaceae). The dried herb is collected in summer before flowering and dried under the sun to obtain Herba Leonuri."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem quadrangular, 2-10 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-green or yellowish-green, the upper part ramose, furrowed longitudinally on 4 sides, texture light and pliable, broken stem shows pith in the centre. Leaves opposite, blades greyish-green when dry, mostly shrivelled and in fragments, fallen off easily. Verticillaster axillary, flowers pale purple, calyx tubular, corolla bilabiate. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Epidermal cells are covered with cuticle and trichomes. Glandular scales with a 4-8-celled head and a unicellular stalk, non-glandular hairs 1-4-celled. Hypodermal collenchyma cells abundant in the angular region. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchyma cells, fine crystals of calcium oxalate can be observed occasionally. Endodermis distinct. Pericyclic fibre bundles slightly lignified. Phloem relatively narrow. Cambium indistinct. Xylem is well developed in the angular region, with medullary parenchyma cells relatively large, some cells containing raphides of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis consist of one layer of cells, with trichomes. Palisade and spongy tissues consist of palisade-shaped parenchyma cells and irregular or subround parenchyma cells, respectively [Fig.2(ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 1, 1965, 248])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green. Non-glandular hairs are easily visible, composed of 1-4-cells, slightly curved, with a thick wall and fine warty protuberance on the surface. Glandular scales consists of 4-8-celled head, subrounded or elliptical in top view, stalk very short. Diacytic and anomocytic stomata are clearly visible in pieces of scattered epidermal tissue of the disintegrated leaves, 12-30 $ \\mu $m long, 10-20 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Extremely fine crystals of calcium oxalate are found occasionally. Vessels are mainly bordered-pitted, scalariform or spiral vessels are also observed, 6-55 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stachydrine hydrochloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of stachydrine hydrochloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of n-butanol, hydrochloric acid and ethyl acetate (8:2:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Dissolve 0.85 g of bismuth subnitrate in 50 mL of acetic acid (20%, v/v) and then add 20 mL of potassium iodide solution (40%, w/v) to obtain potassium iodobismuthate solution. Transfer 1 mL of potassium iodobismuthate solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Add 2 mL of 0.6 M hydrochloric acid and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a cellulose extraction thimble. Place the cellulose extraction thimble in soxhlet extractor. Add 300 mL of ethyl acetate to a 500-mL round-bottomed flask. Perform the soxhlet extraction for about 12 h. Collect and air dry the residue. Transfer the residue to a 50-mL conical flask. Add 25 mL of absolute ethanol and 150 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.5 mL of absolute ethanol.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[859, 21, 1088, 117])\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 239])\n\nerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nFructus Evo\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 451, 1723, 1906])\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of the stem of Herba Leonu A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphides of calcium oxalate in the pith 1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Hypodermal collenchyma 4. Cortex 5. Endodermis 6. Pericyclic fibre bundles 7. Phloem 8. Cambium 9. Xylem 10. Pith 11. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n燈心草\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n太子参\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\n地黄\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 398, 1716, 1693])\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of the transverse section of the leaf of Herba Leonuri\n\nA. Section illustration of leaf blade B. Section illustration of leaf blade farther away from midvein\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Lower epidermis\n\n5. Non-glandular hair 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Hypodermal collenchyma\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[948, 177, 1063, 240])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[234, 434, 1688, 1817])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of the aerial parts of Herba Leonuri\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Glandular scale 3. Diacytic stomata\n\n4. Crystals of calcium oxalate 5. Bordered-pitted vessels 6. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 0, 1965, 248])"},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately stachydrine hydrochloride standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in an oven at $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 15 min). Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of stachydrine hydrochloride.\n\n(i)\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[841, 1072, 1148, 1237])\n\n.HCl\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[575, 1361, 1575, 1547])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) stachydrine hydrochloride and (ii) palmitic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Palmitic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (400 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of palmitic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL conical flask. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5, 0.32 mm $ \\times $ 30 m) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about 0.25 $ \\mu $m thick. The injection temperature is at $ 210^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 300^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 15:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[37, 0, 1932, 253])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-2</td><td>70</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2-27</td><td>70→220</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>27-37</td><td>220</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>37-40</td><td>220→250</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>250</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of palmitic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of palmitic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of palmitic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from palmitic acid peak should not be less than 500000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and peak 5 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject palmitic acid Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of palmitic acid peak in the chromatogram of palmitic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify palmitic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of palmitic acid Std-FP. The retention times of palmitic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Herba Leonuri extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[20, 1, 1966, 251])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Herba Leonuri extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.29</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.51</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, palmitic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[385, 894, 1754, 1595])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Herba Leonuri extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[45, 2, 1946, 247])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 13.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 19.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 16.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stachydrine hydrochloride standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of stachydrine hydrochloride CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of absolute ethanol.\n\nStachydrine hydrochloride standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the stachydrine hydrochloride Std-Stock, dilute with absolute ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 100,300,500,700,900 mg/L for stachydrine hydrochloride."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-leonuri","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Leonuri","la":"Herba Leonuri"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-taxilli-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Taxilli v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[435, 851, 1564, 1785])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1943, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[435, 851, 1564, 1785])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1943, 252])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Herba Taxilli\n\nChinese Name: 桑寄生\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Sangjisheng"},{"name":"12","text":"Herba Taxilli is the dried leaf-bearing twig of Taxillus chinensis (DC.) Danser (Loranthaceae). The herb is harvested in winter through the following spring. The leaf-bearing twigs are removed from the thick stems, cut into sections and dried, or steamed and dried to obtain Herba Taxilli. This plant mainly parasitizes on the host plant of Morus alba L."},{"name":"12","text":"Leaf-bearing twig cylindrical, 3-4 cm long, 2-15 mm in diameter, branched. Externally reddish-brown or greyish-brown, marked with fine longitudinal striae and numerous small, protruding brown lenticels; some young stems covered with reddish-brown pubescence. Texture hard. Fracture irregular; bark thin, reddish-brown; wood pale in colour. Leaves mostly curled and with short petioles; when smoothed out, blade ovate to elliptical, 3-8 cm long, 2-5 cm wide; externally dull yellowish-brown or dull green; young leaves covered with fine pubescence. Leaf apex obtuse, base orbicular to broadly cuneate; entire; texture coriaceous. Odour slight; taste mild, and slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem: Cork consists of 10 or more rows of cells, some containing brown contents. Cortex narrow, containing groups of stone cells in older stems, parenchyma cells with brown contents. Stone cells and fibre bundles are found in the pericycle, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem extremely narrow, rays scattered with stone cells. Fasicular cambium distinct. Xylem rays 1-4 rows of cells wide, stone cells also occurring near the pith; vessels scattered singly or in groups of 2-3. Pith contains groups of stone cells and parenchyma cells; the latter has brown contents. Some groups of stone cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate or brown contents (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 3, 1965, 249])\n\nLeaf: Both the upper and lower epidermis contain stomata, cells small, subrectangular. Palisade tissue in the upper part consists of 3-4 rows of relatively long cells, that in the lower part consists of 2-3 rows of relatively short cells, arranged orderly and tightly. Spongy tissues located in the middle, containing many scattered round clusters of calcium oxalate, arranged loosely. The midrib bulge is prominent on both upper and lower leaf surfaces. Vascular bundles collateral, with fibre bundles above and below it. Many stone cells in groups are present in the parenchyma cells, containing prisms of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Stone cells relatively numerous, singly scattered or several in groups, pale yellow to nearly colourless, subsquare or suborbicular, cells wall are mostly thickened on three sides, with distinct striation, some also with distinct pit canals; bright orange colour is observed under the polarized microscope (characteristics in the stem and leaf). Prisms of calcium oxalate are relatively numerous, scattered or mostly contained in the stone cells, 4-33 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic observed under the polarized microscope (characteristics in the stem and leaf). Cluster of calcium oxalate subrounded; 5-37 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with short and obtuse angles; scattered or mostly inside pale brown parenchyma cells; polychromatic observed under the polarized microscope (characteristics in the leaf, occasionally observed in the stems). Xylem fibres are mostly in bundles, relatively long, about 6-32 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, two ends tapering, walls relatively thin, pit canals scarce; bright yellowish-white colour is observed under the polarized microscope (characteristics in the stem). Pericyclic fibres are mostly in bundles, slender, with the top end sharp; 10-39 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with extremely thickened wall, lumen linear or of a ditch-shaped grain; polychromatic observed under the polarized microscope (characteristics in the stem). Bordered pitted, reticulate and spiral vessels are frequently visible (characteristics in the stem and leaf). Stellate hairs of pale yellow to yellowish-brown colour present; singly and scattered or overlapped, trifurcately or tetrafurcately branched, branches mostly curved, ends tapering, with relatively thickened wall; bright orange colour is observed under the polarized microscope (characteristics in the leaf and young stem) (Fig. 3).\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nHerba Taxilli\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1065, 239])\n\na Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[231, 423, 1735, 1941])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of stem and leaf of Herba Taxilli A. Sketch of stem B. Section illustration of stem C. Sketch of leaf D. Section illustration of leaf\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells 4. Pericyclic fibre bundles 5. Phloem 6. Cambium\n\n7. Xylem 8. Pith 9. Group of stone cells 10. Upper epidermal cells\n\n11. Palisade tissue 12. Spongy tissue 13. Lower epidermal cells\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n銀杏葉\n\n燈心草\n\n連翹\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n夏枯草\n\nSpica Prunellae\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n地黄\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\nHerba Taxilli\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 423, 1731, 2270])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Herba Taxilli\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Xylem fibres 5. Stellate hairs 6. Vessels 7. Pericyclic fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[54, 1, 1957, 250])"},{"name":"12","text":"Quercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of quercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, butan-2-one, formic acid and water (24:3.6:1.5:0.9, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Dissolve 2.5 g of aluminum chloride in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of methanol ( 50% ). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min and then filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercitrin standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercitrin.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[753, 1858, 1272, 2313])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of quercitrin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[19, 1, 1967, 250])"},{"name":"12","text":"Quercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of quercitrin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (256 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.001M Sodium dihydrogen phosphate(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>95→85</td><td>5→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-35</td><td>85→81</td><td>15→19</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>81</td><td>19</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of quercitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of quercitrin should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of quercitrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from quercitrin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[28, 0, 1926, 251])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject quercitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of quercitrin peak in the chromatogram of quercitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify quercitrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of quercitrin Std-FP. The retention times of quercitrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Herba Taxilli extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Herba Taxill extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.34</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.77</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, quercitrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[387, 1737, 1749, 2351])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Herba Taxilli extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[26, 6, 1953, 250])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%.\n\n5. 8 Cardiac glycoside\n\nReagent\n\nAlkaline 3,5-dinitrobenzoic acid solution\n\nDissolve 1.0 g of dinitrobenzoic acid in 100 mL of ethanol.\n\nDissolve 4.3 g of sodium hydroxide in 100 mL of water.\n\nMix 1 mL of each of the above solution."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 50 mL of ethanol (80%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of hot water and then filter. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separatory funnel. Extract for four times each with 15 mL of diethyl ether and discard the diethyl ether layer. Add appropriate amount of saturated lead acetate solution to the aqueous solution until the precipitation is completed and then filter. Transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL conical flask, add 10 mL of ethanol and appropriate amount of saturated sodium sulphate solution to remove the lead ion and then filter. Extract for three times each with 15 mL of dichloromethane. Combine the dichloromethane extracts. Evaporate the extracts to 1 mL at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Spot the concentrated solution onto a filter paper. Dry in air. Apply the alkaline 3,5-dinitrobenzoic acid solution to the dried spot. No purplish-red colour should be observed.\n\nNote: Herba Taxilli contains toxic cardiac glycoside when parasitizes on the oleander trees.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[38, 13, 1088, 117])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[947, 178, 1064, 240])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/herba-taxilli","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Herba Taxilli","la":"Herba Taxilli"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hippophae-fructus-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hippophae Fructus v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[389, 841, 1589, 1969])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Hippophae Fructus (dried under the sun)\n\nA. Hippophae Fructus\n\nB. Magnified image of fruits\n\nC. Cross (above) and longitudinal section (below) of fruits\n\nD. Magnified image of seeds\n\nGalangae Fructus\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n龍脷葉\n\nSauropi Folium\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n紅豆蔻\n\n酸棗仁\n\nHippophae Fructus\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[377, 520, 1640, 1782])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of Hippophae Fructus (steamed before dried)\n\nA. Hippophae Fructus\n\nB. Magnified image of fruits\n\nC. Cross (above) and longitudinal section (below) of fruits\n\nD. Magnified image of seeds"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[389, 841, 1589, 1969])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Hippophae Fructus (dried under the sun)\n\nA. Hippophae Fructus\n\nB. Magnified image of fruits\n\nC. Cross (above) and longitudinal section (below) of fruits\n\nD. Magnified image of seeds\n\nGalangae Fructus\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n龍脷葉\n\nSauropi Folium\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n紅豆蔻\n\n酸棗仁\n\nHippophae Fructus\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[377, 520, 1640, 1782])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of Hippophae Fructus (steamed before dried)\n\nA. Hippophae Fructus\n\nB. Magnified image of fruits\n\nC. Cross (above) and longitudinal section (below) of fruits\n\nD. Magnified image of seeds"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Hippophae Fructus\n\nChinese name: 沙棘\n\nChinese phonetic name: Shaji"},{"name":"14","text":"Hippophae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Hippophae rhamnoides L. (Elaeagnaceae). The fruit is collected in autumn and winter when the fruit is ripe, then dried under the sun or steamed for about 5 minutes followed by drying under the sun or in the oven to obtain Hippophae Fructus."},{"name":"14","text":"Elliptical shaped, 5-8 mm in length, 3-5 mm in width, reddish-orange to reddish-brown, wrinkles, texture soft (dried under the sun). Round to oval shaped, 4-7 mm in length, 3-6 mm in width, yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, wrinkles, texture hard (steamed before dried). Seed 1, elliptical shaped, 3-5 mm in length, 2-4 mm in width, externally brown to dark brown, lustrous, with a vertical groove; kernel white. Odour slight; taste sour and astringent (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 8, 1973, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Epidermis of testa contains thickened cuticle, 1 layer of cells. Seed coat of palisade cells consist of 1 layer of sclerenchymatous cell, compact arranged, column shape. 1 layer of brace cells connect with palisade cells, dumbbell shape. Endotesta consists of several layers of cells, with walls markedly thickened cell, irregularly arranged, elongated elliptical. Endosperm consists of 1 layer of flat parenchymatous cells. Cotyledon consists of irregularly polygonal parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour brownish-yellow or orange to reddish-brown. Peltate hair numerous, containing more than 100 unicellular hairs, separated in the terminal end, 2-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, 56-240 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length; bright white and bright yellow to brown under the polarized microscope. Scars of fallen hair consist of 7-8 rounded cells, slightly thickened walls. Epidermal cells of pericarp polygonal in surface view, anticlinal wall slightly thickened. Oil droplets numerous, bright yellow, 2-106 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Granular contents numerous, orange-red or orange-yellow, visible in parenchymatous cells of sarcocarp. Cotyledon cells contain oil droplets and aleurone grains. Aleurone grains irregular; outline indistinct. Epidermal cells of testa subrectangular, wall thickened, arranged closely, palisade. Pericarp cells subtriangular, irregular or sub-longrounded, vaguely visible horizontal striation (Fig. 3).\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\nHippophae Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[244, 454, 1726, 2062])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Hippophae Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (from palisade cells layer to endotesta) D. Section magnified (from endotesta to cotyledon)\n\n1. Epidermis of testa 2. Palisade cells 3. Brace cells 4. Endotesta\n\n5. Endosperm 6. Cotyledon\n\n明黛參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[307, 698, 749, 1029])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[773, 697, 1218, 1033])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1235, 694, 1436, 1032])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1474, 695, 1668, 1033])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[307, 1089, 751, 1421])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[774, 1098, 1219, 1414])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1235, 1090, 1681, 1411])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[308, 1484, 749, 1813])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[774, 1484, 1216, 1813])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1235, 1481, 1681, 1816])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of the powder of Hippophae Fructus\n\n1. Peltate hair 2. Scar of fallen hair ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) 3. Epidermal cells of pericarp\n\n4. Oil droplets 5. Granular content ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Cotyledon cells (aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ ） 7. Aleurone grains\n\n8. Epidermal cells of testa 9. Pericarp cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 17, 1943, 254])"},{"name":"14","text":"Narcissoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of narcissoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nRutin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of rutin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid, water and ethanol (16:2:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 10 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately narcissoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), rutin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 435, 1018, 1030])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1255, 1017, 1839])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) narcissoside and (ii) rutin\n\n</div>\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥 茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁 Hipp\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[377, 373, 871, 1200])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Hippophae Fructus extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"For positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of narcissoside and rutin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[28, 20, 1966, 255])"},{"name":"14","text":"Narcissoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (4 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of narcissoside CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nRutin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (1.5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of rutin CRS and dissolve in 200 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction two more times and combine the supernatants. Wash the residue with methanol. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times 75\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-2</td><td>90→88</td><td>10→12</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>2-4</td><td>88→86</td><td>12→14</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>4-7</td><td>86→84</td><td>14→16</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>7-9</td><td>84→82</td><td>16→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>9-11</td><td>82→80</td><td>18→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of narcissoside Std-FP and rutin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of narcissoside and rutin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of narcissoside and rutin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from narcissoside and rutin peaks should not be less than 60000 and 40000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject narcissoside Std-FP, rutin Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of narcissoside and rutin peaks in the chromatograms of narcissoside Std-FP, rutin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify narcissoside and rutin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of narcissoside Std-FP and rutin Std-FP. The retention times of narcissoside and rutin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Hippophae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Hippophae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(rutin)</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,narcissoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[331, 373, 1823, 713])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hippophae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.7%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.4%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 26%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 23%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hippophae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hippophae Fructus","la":"Hippophae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/homalomenae-rhizoma-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Homalomenae Rhizoma v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[393, 816, 1564, 1940])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Homalomenae Rhizoma A. Homalomenae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of fracture surface C. Magnified image of cut surface"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[393, 816, 1564, 1940])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Homalomenae Rhizoma A. Homalomenae Rhizoma B. Magnified image of fracture surface C. Magnified image of cut surface"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Homalomenae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 千年健\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qiannianjian"},{"name":"12","text":"Homalomenae Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Homalomena occulta (Lour.) Schott (Araceae). The rhizome is collected in spring and autumn, washed clean, outer bark and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Homalomenae Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical, sometimes slightly flattened, straight or curved, rarely branched, 4-21 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, coarse, with distinct longitudinal furrows, occasionally with rounded root scars and leaf bases remnants at the node. Texture hard, when broken, with yellow brush-like fibre bundles on broken side, holes on the other side; cut surface yellowish-white, reddish-brown or pinkish-purple, with sparse brown spots. Odour characteristic and aromatic; taste pungent, slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of flattened cells, sometimes removed. Ground tissue scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate, fibre bundles, secretory cavities, vascular bundles and mucilage cells containing raphides of calcium oxalate, sometimes also with pigment masses. Fibres with thickened wall, in bundles, lignified. Secretory cavities large, varied in size, surrounded by suberized cells. Vascular bundles collateral and amphivasal, usually accompanied by fibre bundles (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 20, 1957, 251])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, 17-68 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Raphides of calcium oxalate slender, needle-shaped, 20-131 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ long, usually in bundles, present in the mucilage cells or scattered; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres colourless or pale pinkish-purple, 8-83 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, usually broken, sometimes in bundles, walls slightly thick or thick, lignified, with distinct pits and pit canals; bright white under the polarized microscope. Cork cells pale brown, subrectangular, subsquare, polygonal or elongated-polygonal in surface view. Stone cells occasionally found, subsquare or rectangular, single or in groups; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Vessels usually in groups, 6-68 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, mainly spiral and scalariform (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 239])\n\n天葵子\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\nHomalomenae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 391, 1748, 2122])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Homalomenae Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C-E. Section magnified\n\n1. Cork 2. Cluster of calcium oxalate 3. Fibre bundle 4. Secretory cavity\n\n5. Collateral vascular bundle 6. Amphivasal vascular bundle\n\n7. Mucilage cell containing raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[197, 146, 289, 188])\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nHomalomenae Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[368, 402, 1610, 2052])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Homalomenae Rhizoma\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate (2-1 in mucilage cell, 2-2 scattered)\n\n3. Fibres (3-1 wall slightly thick, 3-2 wall thick) 4. Cork cells 5. Stone cell\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 scalariform vessels, 6-2 spiral vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Linalool standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of linalool CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and ethyl acetate (9:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 100 mL of ethanol and dissolve 6 g of vanillin. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of n-hexane. Cap the flask. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linalool standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[376, 1936, 869, 2078])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of linalool\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 375, 822, 1268])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Homalomenae Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Linalool standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of linalool (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Linalool standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of linalool CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of n-hexane."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of n-hexane. Cap the flask. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with n-hexane. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with n-hexane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (DB-5 MS, $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{m} $) of which the internal wall is covered with phenyl arylene polymer in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. The injection temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 4:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>70→100</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>15-43</td><td>100→240</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>43-45</td><td>240→280</td><td>20</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linalool Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of linalool should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of linalool peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from linalool peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject linalool Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of linalool peak in the chromatogram of linalool Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify linalool peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of linalool Std-FP. The retention times of linalool peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Homalomenae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[37, 36, 1951, 258])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Homalomenae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, linalool)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.58</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.63</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.70</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.79</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[322, 900, 1804, 1570])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Homalomenae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): The CMM shall meet the requirements for arsenic, lead and mercury as specified in Appendix V. For cadmium, Homalomenae Rhizoma should meet the specified limit of not more than 2.0 mg/kg, when the CMM will be processed as a decoction in the final consumption form; otherwise, the limit for cadmium specified in Appendix V shall be applied.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[380, 60, 626, 128])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1042, 149, 1133, 205])\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 8.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/homalomenae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Homalomenae Rhizoma","la":"Homalomenae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/houttuyniae-herba-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Houttuyniae Herba v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[647, 795, 1331, 1394])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Houttuyniae Herba\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[643, 1713, 1338, 2313])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1926, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[647, 795, 1331, 1394])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Houttuyniae Herba\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[643, 1713, 1338, 2313])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1926, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Houttuyniae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 魚腥草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yuxingcao"},{"name":"14","text":"Houttuyniae Herba is the dried aerial part of Houttuynia cordata Thunb. (Saururaceae). The aerial part of herb is collected in summer when leaves and spikes flourish, foreign matter removed, then the aerial part is dried under the sun to obtain Houttuyniae Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Stems flattish-cylindrical, somewhat twisted, 15-32 cm long, 1.5-4.5 mm in diameter; externally brownish-yellow to brown, with several longitudinal ridges; nodes prominent, with remnants of rootlets on nodes on the lower part; texture fragile. Leaves simple, alternate, blade curled and shrivelled, cordate when flattened, 2.5-8 cm long, 2-6.5 cm wide, apex acuminate, margins entire; the upper surface dark yellowish-green to dark brown, the lower surface greyish-green to greyish-brown; petioles 1-3.5 cm long, bearing stipule at the base, forming a sheath. Spikes terminal, yellowishbrown. Odour fishy when rubbed; taste slightly astringent [Fig. 1 (i)-(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Leaf: Upper and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of cells, with non-glandular hairs on the veins. The upper epidermal cells tangentially elongated, with scattered oil cells. Beneath the epidermis, 1 layer of subsquare, relatively large parenchymatous cells. Mesophyll consists of 1 layer of palisade tissue and several layers of spongy tissues, with small clusters of calcium oxalate and starch granules, usually scattered. Vascular bundle of midrib collateral. Collenchyma arranged on the inner side of lower epidermis of midrib (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 8, 1984, 263])\n\nStem: Epidermal cells subsquare, arranged in 1 layer, sometimes with oil cells scattered; beneath the epidermis, 1 layer of subsquare, relatively large parenchymatous cells. Pericycle fibres arranged in a complete or interrupted ring. Collateral vascular bundles arranged in a circle. Pith large, with scattered small clusters of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-green to brown. Oil cells subrounded or elliptical, 33-104 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, sometimes containing yellow oil droplets. Glandular hairs, each with a 2-5 celled head containing pale brown contents, devoid of stalk, 34-168 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ , sometimes up to 214 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 9-24 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; the top cell usually shrivelled. Non-glandular hairs, each consists of 1-11 cells, 48-318 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ , sometimes up to 476 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 19-94 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter at base, with linear striations on the surface. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, the larger ones up to 57 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; prisms of calcium oxalate occasionally present; orange-yellow or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of leaf polygonal, with wavy striations on the surface; stomata anomocytic, with 3-4 subsidiary cells. Fibres slender, 4-37 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Spiral, scalariform, reticulate, and bordered-pitted vessels frequently visible. Starch granules scattered, simple granules ovoid, ellipsoid or irregular in shape, 2-29 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ , sometimes up to 39 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; hilum pointed, cleft-like, V-shaped or stellate, striations sometimes distinct; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope; compound granules rare (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"14","text":"Quercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.9 mg of quercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, butan-2-one, formic acid and water (24:3.6:1.5:0.9, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter the supernatant.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[22, 1, 1928, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 430, 1741, 1984])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf and stem of Houttuyniae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch of leaf midvein B. Section illustration of leaf midvein\n\nC. Starch granules and clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in mesophyll\n\nD. Sketch of stem E. Section illustration of stem F. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in pith\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Oil cell 5. Non-glandular hair 6. Xylem\n\n7. Phloem 8. Collenchyma 9. Lower epidermis 10. Starch granules 11. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n12. Epidermis 13. Cortex 14. Pericycle fibres 15. Phloem 16. Xylem 17. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 9, 1984, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[312, 527, 1676, 1878])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Houttuyniae Herba\n\n1. Oil cell 2. Glandular hair 3. Non-glandular hair\n\n4. Clusters of calcium oxalate (4-1 small ones, 4-2 large ones) 5. Epidermal cells of leaf\n\n6. Fibres 7. Vessels 8. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 3, 1926, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercitrin standard solution (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 80^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercitrin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[445, 1049, 952, 1530])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[449, 1600, 925, 1710])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) quercitrin and (ii) methylnonylketone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"Methylnonylketone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (160 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.6 mg of methylnonylketone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare 20.0 g of test sample by cutting the sample into pieces of less than 3 mm in length and place it in a 1000-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 500 mL of water and a few glass\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 5, 1984, 264])\n\nbeads, shake and mix well. Connect the round-bottomed flask to a volatile oil determination tube and then connect the volatile oil determination tube to a reflux condenser. Add 3 mL of water and then 2 mL of ethyl acetate through the top of reflux condenser until the graduated tube of volatile oil determination tube is filled. Heat the flask by reflux for 3 h. Allow to stand for a while. Open the stopcork at the lower part of volatile oil determination tube and run off the water layer slowly until the ethyl acetate layer is 5 mm above the zero mark. Transfer the ethyl acetate layer to a 10-mL centrifuge tube, then add 0.5 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer 1 mL of the supernatant to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (DB-5, 0.25 mm $ \\times $ 30 m) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about 0.25 $ \\mu $m thick. The injection temperature is at $ 2 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 3 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ The splitless injection mode is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>60</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>60→140</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>30-50</td><td>140→200</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>50-57</td><td>200→270</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>57-60</td><td>270</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of methylnonylketone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of methylnonylketone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of methylnonylketone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from methylnonylketone peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 1, 1929, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject methylnonylketone Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of methylnonylketone peak in the chromatogram of methylnonylketone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify methylnonylketone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of methylnonylketone Std-FP. The retention times of methylnonylketone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Houttuyniae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Houttuyniae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.99</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,methylnonylketone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 1629, 1800, 2143])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Houttuyniae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[12, 15, 1975, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"Quercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of quercitrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 10 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Wash the residue with methanol ( 70% ). Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 3) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 3 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>95→80</td><td>5→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>80→65</td><td>20→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of quercitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of quercitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of quercitrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from quercitrin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 1, 1928, 258])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/houttuyniae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Houttuyniae Herba","la":"Houttuyniae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hoveniae-semen-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hoveniae Semen v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n(1) \n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[471, 1236, 984, 1556])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1203, 1175, 1385, 1255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(2) \n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1112, 1381, 1268, 1549])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1295, 1377, 1504, 1565])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hoveniae Semen A. Hoveniae Semen B. Magnified image of seeds (1 in lateral view, 2 in surface view)"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n(1) \n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[471, 1236, 984, 1556])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1203, 1175, 1385, 1255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(2) \n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1112, 1381, 1268, 1549])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1295, 1377, 1504, 1565])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n5 mm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hoveniae Semen A. Hoveniae Semen B. Magnified image of seeds (1 in lateral view, 2 in surface view)"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Hoveniae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 枳椇子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhijuzi"},{"name":"12","text":"Hoveniae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Hovenia acerba Lindl. (Rhamnaceae). The ripe fruit with fleshy stalk is collected in autumn, dried under the sun, pericarp removed; the seeds gathered and then dried again under the sun to obtain Hoveniae Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Subrounded and slightly flattened, 2.8-5.7 mm long, 2.2-5 mm wide, 0.9-2.6 mm thick. Externally reddish-brown, yellowish-brown or blackish-brown, lustrous, scattered with dented dots; dorsal side slightly protuberant, ventral side flattened, with a longitudinal convex raphe. One dented edge with hilum, apex with a slightly convex chalaza. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Odour slight; taste slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Outer epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of palisade cells, outer part of the layer bearing a light line, covered with cuticle, elongated strip-shaped. Pigment layer consists of several layers of cells, subovate or polygonal, containing reddish-brown contents; underneath the pigment layer is several layers of parenchymatous cells, without pigments. Inner epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of parenchymatous cells. Perisperm cells decadent, occasionally contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Endosperm consists of cells with relatively thickened walls, containing aleurone grains. Cotyledons consists of cells with thin walls, filled with aleurone grains; fine and minute spheroidal crystals arranged along the margins of the inner side of cotyledons; raphe vascular bundle visible (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 28, 1957, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Palisade cells of testa polygonal in surface view, walls thickened, lumen small; elongated strip-shaped in lateral view, 114-245 $ \\mu $m long, densely arranged, covered with cuticle, with a distinct light line on the outer side; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pigment cells subovate or polygonal, containing reddish-brown contents. Spheroidal crystals fine and minute, 2-8 $ \\mu $m in diameter; bright white under the polarized microscope. Cotyledon cells subrectangular or elongatedovate, walls thin, containing fine and minute spheroidal crystals, and oil droplets. Prisms of calcium oxalate 2-11 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[672, 42, 816, 119])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 202, 239])\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[249, 523, 1722, 1970])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Hoveniae Semen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphe vascular bundle D. Spheroidal crystals (under the light microscope) E. Spheroidal crystals (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Light line 3. Outer epidermis of testa (palisade tissue) 4. Pigment layer\n\n5. Parenchymatous cells 6. Inner epidermis of testa 7. Perisperm 8. Endosperm\n\n9. Spheroidal crystals 10. Cotyledon 11. Raphe vascular bundle\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[197, 146, 289, 188])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[700, 140, 829, 191])\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nHoveniae Semen\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[279, 638, 739, 1010])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[773, 642, 1221, 1004])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1248, 640, 1707, 1005])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[278, 1069, 736, 1423])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[764, 1072, 1223, 1425])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1246, 1071, 1704, 1433])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[280, 1474, 738, 1836])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[765, 1479, 1224, 1838])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1247, 1475, 1705, 1840])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Palisade cells of testa (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view (light line $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n2. Pigment cells 3. Spheroidal crystals 4. Cotyledon cells ( oil droplet $ \\rightarrow $)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[360, 160, 499, 207])"},{"name":"12","text":"Dihydromyricetin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dihydromyricetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (10:8:5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Heat the mixture in a water bath at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dihydromyricetin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 138])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[370, 369, 837, 680])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of dihydromyricetin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 854, 781, 1685])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Hoveniae Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Dihydromyricetin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of dihydromyricetin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Dihydromyricetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of dihydromyricetin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of a mixture of methanol (50%) and formic acid (99.9:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (290 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>88→80</td><td>12→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-45</td><td>80→35</td><td>20→65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-50</td><td>35→5</td><td>65→95</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dihydromyricetin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dihydromyricetin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dihydromyricetin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dihydromyricetin peak should not be less than 18000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject dihydromyricetin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dihydromyricetin peak in the chromatogram of dihydromyricetin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dihydromyricetin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dihydromyricetin Std-FP. The retention times of dihydromyricetin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Hoveniae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Hoveniae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, dihydromyricetin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.16</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.79</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[345, 1500, 1806, 1968])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hoveniae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hoveniae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hoveniae Semen","la":"Hoveniae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hydrargyri-oxydum-rubrum-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"0. 5cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[925, 1221, 1295, 1784])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[26, 5, 1929, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"7","text":"0. 5cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[925, 1221, 1295, 1784])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[26, 5, 1929, 261])"},{"name":"7","text":"Official Name: Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum\n\nChinese Name: 红粉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hongfen"},{"name":"7","text":"Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum is a synthetic product of red mercuric oxide (HgO). The mineral should be stored in a container protected from light."},{"name":"7","text":"Occurs as orange plates or crystalline powders. One side of the plate is smooth and slightly lustrous, while the other side is relatively coarse, lustreless, as if covered with a layer of powder. Texture hard and fragile. Colour gradually darkens upon exposure to light. Odourless (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"7","text":"Colour orange. Crystals are irregular in shape; dark orange, dark reddish-orange or dark yellowish orange in colour; translucent or opaque; striations visible. Crystals dark reddish-orange to bright orange, occasionally polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Polarizability weak (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"7","text":"Chemical test of mercuric salt"},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of water. Add 0.6 mL of hydrochloric acid to dissolve the mixture. Neutralize the solution with about 4 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3%, w/v). Yellow precipitate can be observed. Then, add 4 mL of potassium iodide solution (16.5%, w/v), scarlet precipitate can be observed. Add\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 7, 1977, 264])\n\npotassium iodide solution (16.5% , w/v) until scarlet precipitate dissolves. Add 13 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3% , w/v) and 0.2 g of ammonium chloride, reddish-brown precipitate can be observed."},{"name":"7","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI."},{"name":"7","text":"Finely powdered mercuric oxide (0.5 g)."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"7","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 2 $ \\theta $ ) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 2 $ \\theta $ values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a $ 2\\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0. 0 5^{\\circ} $ when compared to the corresponding 2 $ \\theta $ values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database."},{"name":"7","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard material and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20 values of the diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the characteristic diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample as listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>30.172</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>31.604</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>32.518</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>37.379</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>50.311</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 3, 1928, 261])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[233, 386, 1732, 2053])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum\n\n</div>\n\n1-4. Irregular crystal of Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[21, 6, 1984, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[380, 413, 1823, 1032])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference XRPD pattern of Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic diffraction peaks (Fig. 3) each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0.2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the values stated in Table 1."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 25 mL of hydrochloric acid (28%, w/v). A slight turbidity can be observed."},{"name":"7","text":"Sodium chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.165 g of sodium chloride and place it in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Make up to the mark with water. Pipette 10 mL of the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Transfer 3 mL of the sodium chloride standard solution to a 50-mL test tube. Add 10 mL of nitric acid (15%, w/v) and 27 mL of water. Mix well."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of water and 3 mL of nitric acid. Add 27 mL of water.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 5, 1928, 261])"},{"name":"7","text":"Add 1 mL of silver nitrate solution (1.7% , w/v) and 9 mL of water to the test solution and sodium chloride standard solution. Allow to stand in the dark for 5 min. The turbidity of the test solution should be less intense when compared with the sodium chloride standard solution."},{"name":"7","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XV."},{"name":"7","text":"Ammonium thiocyanate titrant\n\nWeigh 3.806 g of ammonium thiocyanate and place it in a 500-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with water.\n\nFluorescein solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 g of fluorescein and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol.\n\nSilver nitrate solution\n\nWeigh 1.699 g of silver nitrate and place it in a 100-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh accurately 0.2 g of sodium chloride and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of water. Add 5 mL of dextrin solution (2%, w/v), 0.1 g of calcium carbonate and 8 drops of fluorescein solution. Titrate the solution with the silver nitrate solution until the colour turns from yellowish-green to red. Calculate the concentration of the silver nitrate solution according to the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {C} _ {\\mathrm {A g N O} _ {3}} = \\frac {\\mathrm {W} _ {\\mathrm {N a C l}} \\times \\mathrm {P} _ {\\mathrm {N a C l}} \\times 1 0 0 0}{\\mathrm {V} _ {\\mathrm {A g N O} _ {3}} \\times \\mathrm {M w} _ {\\mathrm {N a C l}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $ \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{A g N O}_{3}} $ = Molarity of silver nitrate solution (mol/L)\n\n$ \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{A g N O}_{3}} $ = Volume of silver nitrate solution used (mL)\n\n$ \\mathrm{Mw}_{\\mathrm{NaCl}} $ = Molecular weight of sodium chloride (58.44 g)\n\n$ \\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{NaCl}} $ = Weight of sodium chloride used (g)\n\n$ \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{NaCl}} $ = Purity of sodium chloride (%)\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[13, 7, 1984, 262])"},{"name":"7","text":"Pipette 25 mL of silver nitrate solution to a 250-mL conical flask. Then add 50 mL of water, 2 mL of nitric acid and 2 mL of ferric ammonium sulphate solution (8%, w/v). Titrate the solution with the ammonium thiocyanate titrant until persistent brown colouration can be observed. Calculate the concentration of the ammonium thiocyanate titrant according to the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\mathrm {C} _ {\\mathrm {N H} _ {4} \\mathrm {S C N}} = \\frac {\\mathrm {C} _ {\\mathrm {A g N O} _ {3}} \\times \\mathrm {V} _ {\\mathrm {A g N O} _ {3}}}{\\mathrm {V} _ {\\mathrm {N H} _ {4} \\mathrm {S C N}}}\n$$\n\nwhere $ \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{NH},\\mathrm{SCN}}=\\mathrm{Molarity of ammonium thiocyanate titrant (mol/L)} $\n\n$ \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{N H}_{4} \\mathrm{SCN}}=\\mathrm{V o l u m e o f a m m o n i u m t h i o c y a n a t e t i t r a n t u s e d (m L)} $\n\n$ \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{A g N O}_{3}} $ = Molarity of silver nitrate solution (mol/L)\n\n$ \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{A g N O}_{3}} $ = Volume of silver nitrate solution used (mL)"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hydrargyri-oxydum-rubrum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum","la":"Hydrargyri Oxydum Rubrum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hydrargyrum-chloratum-compositum-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[665, 836, 1323, 1399])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum in plate-shaped\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[660, 1733, 1350, 2324])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 6, 1928, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"7","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[665, 836, 1323, 1399])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum in plate-shaped\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[660, 1733, 1350, 2324])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 6, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"7","text":"Official Name: Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum\n\nChinese Name: 白降丹\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baijiangdan"},{"name":"7","text":"Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum is a synthetic mixture of mercurous chloride $ \\mathrm{(H g_{2} C l_{2}} $ ）and mercuric chloride $ \\mathrm{(H g C l_{2}} $ ). The mineral should be stored in a container protected from light."},{"name":"7","text":"Plate-like aggregate. White to pale yellowish-white in colour. Thick in the middle and thin towards the edges; one side is smooth while the other side is relatively coarse; lateral side showing needle-shaped to columnar-shaped crystals, varying in length and unevenly arranged. Opaque, with pearl lustre. Heavy, texture soft and fragile. Broken powder in needle-shaped to columnae-shaped. Extremely toxic. Odourless (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"7","text":"Colour white to yellowish-white. Crystals in columnar, subsquare to irregular shape, with sharp corners; colourless and transparent, dark at the edge; sometimes striations can be observed. Bright white, polychromatic or weakly polarized under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"7","text":"(a) Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 20-mL test tube, then add 5 mL of water. Dissolve the sample in 1 drop of nitric acid (15%, w/v). Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 20-mL test tube. Add 3 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3%, w/v) and mix well. Yellow precipitate can be observed.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[878, 43, 984, 129])\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1892, 45, 1984, 88])\n\n前胡\n\nPeucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1622, 70, 1784, 124])\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba 半枝蓮\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[247, 494, 1716, 2002])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum\n\n</div>\n\n1,2. Subsquare crystals 4,6. Irregular shape crystals 3,5. Columnar crystals\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 5, 1933, 259])\n\n(b) Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 20-mL test tube, then add 5 mL of water. Dissolve the sample in 1 drop of nitric acid (15%, w/v). Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 20-mL test tube. Neutralize the filtrate with about 100 $ \\mu $ L of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3%, w/v). Then, add 0.15 mL of potassium iodide solution (16.5%, w/v), scarlet precipitate can be observed. Add potassium iodide solution (16.5%, w/v) until scarlet precipitate dissolves. Add 3 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (4.3%, w/v) followed by 0.05 g of ammonium chloride and mix well, reddish-brown precipitate can be observed.\n\nFor positive identification of the mercuric salt, the sample must show the above observations. For positive identification of the mercurous salt, the sample must show the clear solution."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 20-mL test tube, then add 5 mL of water. Dissolve the sample in 1 drop of nitric acid (15%, w/v). Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 20-mL test tube. Add 10 mL of nitric acid (15%, w/v) and mix well. Add silver nitrate solution (1.7%, w/v) until white precipitate can be observed. Collect and dissolve the precipitate in 2 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (36%, w/v). Add nitric acid (15%, w/v) until white precipitate is formed."},{"name":"7","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI."},{"name":"7","text":"Finely powdered mercurous chloride (0.5 g) and mercuric chloride (0.5 g)."},{"name":"7","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"7","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 20) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 20 values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a $ 2\\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0. 0 5^{\\circ} $ when compared to the corresponding 20 values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database.\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[13, 7, 1975, 263])"},{"name":"7","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard materials and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20 values of the diffraction peaks of the standard materials and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the characteristic diffraction peaks of (i) mercurous chloride standard material and test sample dominant in mercurous chloride, (ii) mercuric chloride standard material and test sample dominant in mercuric chloride or (iii) both standard materials and test sample that contain a mixture of mercurous and mercuric chloride.\n\nThe 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum dominant in mercurous chloride and dominant in mercuric chloride are listed in Table 1 (i) and (ii) respectively. The 20 values of the seven characteristic diffraction peaks of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum containing a mixture of mercurous and mercuric chloride are listed in Table 1 (iii).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1(i) The 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum dominant in mercurous chloride\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>21.574</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>28.187</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>32.974</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>40.274</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>43.901</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[371, 1828, 1793, 2250])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) A reference XRPD pattern of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum dominant in mercurous chloride\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[23, 1, 1931, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1(ii) The 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum dominant in mercuric chloride\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>20.446</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>21.609</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>33.051</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>37.284</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>43.902</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[380, 998, 1626, 1412])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) A reference XRPD pattern of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum dominant in mercuric chloride\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1(iii) The 20 values of the seven characteristic diffraction peaks of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum containing a mixture of mercurous and mercuric chloride\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>20.446</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>21.592</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>28.187</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>33.013</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>37.284</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>40.274</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>43.902</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 3, 1984, 265])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 372, 1769, 775])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(iii) A reference XRPD pattern of Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum containing a mixture of mercurous and mercuric chloride\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic diffraction peaks [Fig. 3 (i) or (ii)]; or seven characteristic diffraction peaks [Fig. 3 (iii)] each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0. 2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the corresponding values stated in Table 1 (i), (ii) or (iii)."},{"name":"7","text":"(2) For external use only.\n\n(1) Avoid prolonged use."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hydrargyrum-chloratum-compositum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum","la":"Hydrargyrum Chloratum Compositum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hyoscyami-semen-unprocessed-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyoscyami Semen unprocessed v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[450, 1142, 976, 1622])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 1154, 1510, 1526])\n\nC 0.5mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed)\n\nA. Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed)\n\nB. Magnified image of seeds\n\nC. Magnified image of longitudinal section of seeds"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[450, 1142, 976, 1622])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1042, 1154, 1510, 1526])\n\nC 0.5mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed)\n\nA. Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed)\n\nB. Magnified image of seeds\n\nC. Magnified image of longitudinal section of seeds"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed)\n\nChinese Name: 天仙子（生）\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tianxianzi (Sheng)"},{"name":"16","text":"Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed) is the unprocessed dried ripe seed of Hyoscyamus niger L. (Solanaceae). The fruit is collected in summer and autumn when the pericarp turns yellow, exposed to the sun, afterwards the seeds tapped out, pericarp and stalk removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed)."},{"name":"16","text":"Flattened-subreniform to flattened-ovoid, about 1 mm in diameter. Externally brownish-yellow or greyish-yellow, with fine and dense reticulate striations, and a pitted hilum located at the slightly acute end. Cut surface greyish-white, oily, containing endosperm, embryo curved. Odour slight (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"16","text":"Exotesta cells with irregularly undulant protuberances, apex of the protuberances tapering or blunt, cell walls with transparent striations. Endotesta consists of 1 layer of cells, cells mostly tangentially elongated, with thin and slightly wizened walls, containing brown contents. Endosperm cells with slightly thickened walls, containing aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells with thin walls, containing oil droplets (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 16, 1939, 263])"},{"name":"16","text":"Exotesta cells scattered or in groups, yellow or greyish-yellowish brown, polygonal or elongated polygonal in surface view, 75-311 $ \\mu m $ long, 25-123 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, anticlinal walls thickened, undulant, with distinct striations, containing yellowish-brown contents; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Endosperm cells polygonal or subrounded in surface view, cell walls slightly thickened, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets. Cotyledon cells colourless, subpolygonal, with thin walls, containing oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[385, 673, 897, 1132])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1046, 837, 1593, 1245])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[376, 1200, 897, 1737])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1046, 1270, 1592, 1677])\n\nC 25 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed)\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Exotesta 2. Endotesta 3. Endosperm 4. Cotyledon\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1046, 40, 1180, 87])\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[374, 694, 969, 1191])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1019, 695, 1613, 1183])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[375, 1240, 970, 1670])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1018, 1239, 1614, 1672])\n\n50μm"},{"name":"16","text":"1. Exotesta cells 2. Endosperm cells (oil droplets $ \\rightarrow $ , aleurone grains $ \\rightarrow $ ）\n\n3. Cotyledon cells (oil droplets $ \\rightarrow $)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"16","text":"L-Hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nScopolamine hydrobromide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of scopolamine hydrobromide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $"},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), methanol and ethyl acetate (0.5:1:8.5, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth oxynitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent 1\n\nTransfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid into a 100-mL\n\nvolumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent.\n\nSpray reagent 2\n\nWeigh 5 g of sodium nitrite and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol (60%)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 15 mL of petroleum ether (30-60 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and discard the filtrate. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 30 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue for three times each with 10 mL of ethanol. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), scopolamine hydrobromide standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent 1 and the spray reagent 2, then dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[443, 1195, 1035, 1823])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[445, 1895, 897, 2212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate and (ii) scopolamine hydrobromide\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1267, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 378, 898, 1143])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed) extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Scopolamine hydrobromide standard solution\n\n2. L-Hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"0. 005 M Sodium 1-heptanesulphonate solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 g of sodium 1-heptanesulphonate and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\n0. 01 M Potassium dihydrogen phosphate solution\n\nWeigh 0.68 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\nSodium 1-heptanesulphonate - potassium dihydrogen phosphate buffer solution (pH 5)\n\nTransfer 500 mL of 0.005 M sodium 1-heptanesulphonate solution and 500 mL of 0.01 M potassium dihydrogen phosphate solution to a 1500-mL conical flask. Adjust the pH to 5 with 1.6 M potassium hydroxide solution."},{"name":"16","text":"L-Hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nScopolamine hydrobromide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of scopolamine hydrobromide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a cellulose extraction thimble. Place the cellulose extraction thimble in soxhlet extractor. Add 200 mL of petroleum ether (30-60 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Perform the soxhlet extraction for 2 h. Discard the petroleum ether (30-60 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and dry the residue in air. Transfer the dried residue to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask and add 5 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 75 mL of ethanol. Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with ethanol. Combine the solution and make up to the mark with ethanol. Pipette 20 mL of the solution to a 50-mL conical flask. Adjust the pH to 5 with hydrochloric acid solution (10%, v/v). Pre-condition a solid-phase extraction column (strong cation exchange, 6 mL, 500 mg) with 3 mL of methanol, 3 mL of a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and ethanol (1:15, v/v), then followed by 3 mL of ethanol and 5 mL of ethanol [adjust the pH to 4 with hydrochloric acid solution (10%, v/v)]. Allow to stand for 10 min. Load the sample solution to the pre-conditioned SPE column. Add 3 mL of ethanol to the column and discard the eluant. Add 5 mL of a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and ethanol (1:15, v/v) to the column. Collect the eluant in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to near dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Sodium1-heptanesulphonate-potassiumdihydrogen phosphate buffer solution(pH5)(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>90→82</td><td>10→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>82→77</td><td>18→23</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-35</td><td>77</td><td>23</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-40</td><td>77→73</td><td>23→27</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP and scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP, scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks in the chromatograms of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP, scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of L-hyoscyamine sulphate dihydrate Std-FP and scopolamine hydrobromide Std-FP. The retention times of L-hyoscyamine and scopolamine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, scopolamine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(L-hyoscyamine)</td><td>1.27</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[334, 1008, 1801, 1812])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hyoscyami Semen (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hyoscyami-semen-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyoscyami Semen unprocessed","la":"Hyoscyami Semen unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hyperici-ascyri-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyperici Ascyri Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[380, 814, 1428, 1910])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hyperici Ascyri Herba\n\nA. Hyperici Ascyri Herba B. A branch with leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of upper part of stem\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of lower part of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of capsule and its transverse section F. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[380, 814, 1428, 1910])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hyperici Ascyri Herba\n\nA. Hyperici Ascyri Herba B. A branch with leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of upper part of stem\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of lower part of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of capsule and its transverse section F. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official name: Hyperici Ascyri Herba\n\nChinese name: 紅旱蓮\n\nChinese phonetic name: Honghanlian"},{"name":"15","text":"Hyperici Ascyri Herba is the dried aerial part of Hypericum ascyron L. (Hypericaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn when the fruit is ripe, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Hyperici Ascyri Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Stems quadrangular, gradually showing cylindrical near base, 1.1-9.0 mm in diameter; externally reddish-brown to brownish-green, nodes conspicuous; texture hard, fracture yellowish-white, with pith or hollow in the centre. Leaves simple, opposite; usually fallen off, crumpled and broken, ovate-lanceolate when intact and flattened out. Capsules conical, 0.6-2.6 cm long, 2.9-10.3 mm in diameter, reddish-brown, 5-valved at the apex; texture hard, with numerous seeds in axis. Seeds tiny, oblong, reddish-brown. Odour slightly aromatic; taste bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Upper Part of Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare or subrectangular cells, covered with cuticle. Collenchyma present in the angular region, with cells subrounded or irregular in shape. Cortex narrow. Secretory canals arranged in a ring at the outer side of phloem. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad. Pith broad, sometimes broken or hollow, mostly occupying about 1/2 of the stem, sometimes containing clusters of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2(i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 38, 1947, 250])\n\nLower Part of Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare or subrectangular cells, covered with cuticle. Collenchyma present in the angular region, obviously lignified. Cortex narrow. Secretory canals arranged in a ring at the outer side of phloem. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad. Pith broad, almost hollowd [Fig. 2(ii)].\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare cells. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of palisade cells. Spongy tissue with cells arranged loosely. Collenchyma located in the inner side of upper and lower epidermis at the midrib. Vascular bundles collateral, xylem vessels arranged radially. Secretory canals arranged at the outer side of phloem, forming a semicircle. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of irregular cells, relatively small [Fig. 2(iii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour reddish-brown to brownish-green. Secretory canals usually contain pale yellow to yellowish-brown secretion. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with walls sinuous; stomata anomocytic, arranged densely. Collenchymatous cells subrounded to subrectangular, with thickened walls. Epidermal cells of testa pale yellow to yellowish-brown, stellate in shape, outer wall striations thickened. Clusters of calcium oxalate sometimes visible; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mostly spiral and bordered-pitted vessels, respectively 10-48 $ \\mu $ m and 6-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Epidermal cells of stem in fragments, subsquare to subrectangular, pale yellow to pale yellowish-brown. Fibres usually in bundles, 3-15 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, walls relatively thickened; bright white to yellowish-brown under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[632, 41, 773, 114])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 50, 1853, 99])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[300, 475, 975, 946])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[312, 1006, 591, 1219])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[602, 1008, 884, 1218])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1024, 472, 1677, 868])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[308, 1297, 949, 1692])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1021, 875, 1674, 1279])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1019, 1286, 1677, 1696])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of upper part of stem of Hyperici Ascyri Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope) E. Secretory canal\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Cortex 4. Secretory canal 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n8. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1938, 138])\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[255, 440, 1723, 2055])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of lower part of stem of Hyperici Ascyri Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory canal 1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Cortex 4. Secretory canal 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 43, 1967, 260])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[232, 454, 1726, 2139])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Hyperici Ascyri Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Collenchyma 5. Xylem 6. Phloem\n\n野老鸛草\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1057, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[750, 145, 906, 187])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1692, 55, 1938, 138])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[266, 674, 1704, 2129])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of the powder of Hyperici Ascyri Herba\n\n1. Secretory canal ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) 2. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with anomocytic stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Collenchymatous cells 4. Epidermal cells of testa 5. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 bordered-pitted vessel, 6-2 spiral vessels) 7. Epidermal cells of stem\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 45, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"15","text":"Hyperoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of hyperoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nIsoquercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of isoquercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (12:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (95%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hyperoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), isoquercitrin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 1 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[24, 37, 1956, 251])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[376, 436, 850, 963])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[380, 1092, 850, 1604])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[381, 1727, 871, 2052])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hyperoside (ii) isoquercitrin and (iii) quercetin\n\n</div>\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n京大戟 Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n苦木\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[378, 375, 835, 1206])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Hyperici Ascyri Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Hyperoside standard solution 2. Isoquercitrin standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of hyperoside and isoquercitrin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Hyperoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.05 mg of hyperoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nIsoquercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.05 mg of isoquercitrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nQuercetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.05 mg of quercetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[23, 38, 1951, 243])"},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction two more times. Wash the residue with ethanol ( 70% ). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (355 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>90</td><td>10</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-15</td><td>90→85</td><td>10→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-45</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>70→40</td><td>30→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hyperoside Std-FP, isoquercitrin Std-FP and quercetin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of hyperoside, isoquercitrin and quercetin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of hyperoside, isoquercitrin and quercetin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from hyperoside, isoquercitrin and quercetin peaks should not be less than 100000, 100000 and 150000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject hyperoside Std-FP, isoquercitrin Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of hyperoside, isoquercitrin and quercetin peaks in the chromatograms of hyperoside Std-FP, isoquercitrin Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hyperoside, isoquercitrin and quercetin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of hyperoside Std-FP, isoquercitrin Std-FP and quercetin Std-FP. The retention times of hyperoside, isoquercitrin and quercetin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Hyperici Ascyri Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Hyperici Ascyri Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, hyperoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(isoquercitrin)</td><td>1.02</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(quercetin)</td><td>1.65</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[335, 1734, 1821, 2168])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hyperici Ascyri Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hyperici-ascyri-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyperici Ascyri Herba","la":"Hyperici Ascyri Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hyperici-japonici-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyperici Japonici Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[632, 1143, 941, 1382])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1023, 901, 1330, 1383])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hyperici Japonici Herba A. Hyperici Japonici Herba B. Magnified stem and leaf C. Magnified flowers\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1898, 264])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[632, 1143, 941, 1382])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1023, 901, 1330, 1383])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Hyperici Japonici Herba A. Hyperici Japonici Herba B. Magnified stem and leaf C. Magnified flowers\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1898, 264])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nChinese Name: 田基黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tianjihuang"},{"name":"15","text":"Hyperici Japonici Herba is the dried herb of Hypericum japonicum Thunb. ex Murray (Clusiaceae). The whole plant is collected at flowering stage in spring and summer, washed clean, dried under the sun to obtain Hyperici Japonici Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Herb 17-67 cm long. Root fibrous, yellowish-brown. Stems solitary or basally branched, glabrous, with 4 ribs, externally yellowish-green or yellowish-brown. Texture fragile, easily broken; fracture hollow at centre. Leaf simple, opposite, sessile; lamina ovate or rounded-ovate, margin entire, 3-5-basinerved, the translucent glandular dots small. Cymes terminal; flowers small, orangish-yellow. Odourless; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Root: Cork consists of 6-8 layers of cells. Cortex narrow, consisting of 3-4 layers of parenchymatous cells. Phloem narrow, cells mostly crumpled. Xylem occupies the major portion of the root, consisting of vessels and fibres; xylem rays consist 1 row of cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of epidermal cells. Cortex narrow, consisting of 3-4 layers of parenchymatous cells, arranged loosely; oil cavities scattered in the cortex, subrounded or elliptical. Endodermis distinct. Phloem narrow, cells mostly crumpled. Xylem broad, with vessels, fibres and rays; xylem rays consists 1 row of cells. Pith generally hollow [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 10, 1971, 259])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer relatively large cells, rectangular. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cells. Spongy tissue arranged loosely, containing numerous and rounded oil cavities. Vascular bundles of midrib collateral and wedge-shaped. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer rectangular, relatively large cells [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour greyish-green. Anticlinal walls of upper and lower epidermis of leaf sinuate to undulate, stomata anisocytic. Epidermal cells of young stem polygonal, anticlinal wall straight, stomata anomocytic. Pollen grains spherical, 14-42 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, exine smooth. Secretory cells often broken, intact cells subrounded or elliptical, 26-115 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, wall somewhat thick, pits present, lumen containing orangish-red or brownish-red resinous secretions. Some oil cavities broken, intact cavities 20-112 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, surrounded by 10-12 secretory cells, lumen usually containing grey secretions or oil droplets. Epidermal cells of testa finely linear-scalariform, anticlinal wall sinuate, lignified. Cork cells pale yellow, subpolygonal or subsquare in surface view, wall thick. Vessels mainly spiral, 3-23 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[36, 98, 208, 197])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 425, 1741, 1909])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Hyperici Japonici Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Xylem\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Hyperici Japonici Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 428, 1740, 1902])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Secretory cells 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Phloem\n\n6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 7, 1914, 261])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[239, 408, 1732, 1654])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Oil cavities 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Lower epidermis\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Hyperici Japonici Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[292, 453, 741, 800])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[775, 452, 1222, 786])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1250, 455, 1693, 790])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[291, 814, 736, 1151])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[775, 819, 1219, 1148])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1249, 821, 1693, 1150])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[302, 1185, 747, 1514])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[774, 1183, 1222, 1512])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1249, 1185, 1692, 1516])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Hyperici Japonici Herba (under the light microscope)\n\n1. Epidermal cells of leaf 2. Epidermal cells of young stem 3. Pollen grains 4. Secretory cell\n\n5. Oil cavities 6. Epidermal cells of testa (6-1 in lower surface view, 6-2 in upper surface view)\n\n7. Cork cells 8. Spiral vessels\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 7, 1903, 259])"},{"name":"15","text":"Isoquercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of isoquercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nQuercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of quercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (10:0.8:0.6, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately isoquercitrin standard solution (0.3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), quercitrin standard solution (0.3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent for about 30 s and heat at about 105 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of isoquercitrin and quercitrin.\n\nRubiae Radix et Rhizoma 餘甘子\n\n茜草 Phyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Hyperici Japonici Herba Rhizoma\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[438, 376, 912, 864])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[443, 943, 937, 1469])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) isoquercitrin and (ii) quercitrin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[31, 12, 1899, 263])"},{"name":"15","text":"Isoquercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of isoquercitrin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nQuercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of quercitrin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (350 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Acetic acid*(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>10→20</td><td>90→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-7</td><td>20</td><td>80</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>7-9</td><td>20→28</td><td>80→72</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>9-16</td><td>28</td><td>72</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>16-24</td><td>28→32</td><td>72→68</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>24-35</td><td>32</td><td>68</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n* Adjust the pH to 4 with triethylamine"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of isoquercitrin Std-FP and quercitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of isoquercitrin and quercitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of isoquercitrin and quercitrin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from isoquercitrin and quercitrin peaks should not be less than 80000 and 35000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[65, 14, 1961, 259])\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 and 1.0 respectively (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject isoquercitrin Std-FP, quercitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of isoquercitrin and quercitrin peaks in the chromatograms of isoquercitrin Std-FP, quercitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify isoquercitrin and quercitrin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of isoquercitrin Std-FP and quercitrin Std-FP. The retention times of isoquercitrin and quercitrin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Hyperici Japonici Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Hyperici Japonici Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(taxifolin-7-O-α-L-rhamnoside)</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,isoquercitrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(quercitrin)</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(quercetin)</td><td>2.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[32, 1, 1913, 266])\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[296, 361, 1841, 1126])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Hyperici Japonici Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[66, 13, 1960, 258])"},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/hyperici-japonici-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyperici Japonici Herba","la":"Hyperici Japonici Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ilicis-cornutae-folium-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ilicis Cornutae Folium v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1 A photograph of Ilicis Cornutae Folium\n\nA. Upper surface of leaves\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 20, 1878, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"Figure 1 A photograph of Ilicis Cornutae Folium\n\nA. Upper surface of leaves\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 20, 1878, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Ilicis Cornutae Folium\n\nChinese Name: 枸骨枼\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gouguye"},{"name":"14","text":"Ilicis Cornutae Folium is the dried leaf of Ilex cornuta Lindl. ex Paxt. (Aquifoliaceae). The leaf is collected in autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Ilicis Cornutae Folium."},{"name":"14","text":"Subrectangular, oblong-rectangular, occasionally oblong, 2.6-10.0 cm long, 1.1-4.1 cm wide, with short petiole. Apex with 3 relatively large and hard spines, the apical one usually recurved; base truncate or broadly cuneata, sometimes each of both sides with 1-3 spines, edges slightly recurved; the oblong blade usually free from spines or only with 1 spine at apex. Upper surface yellowish-green to greenish-brown, lustrous; lower surface greyish-yellow to greyish-green. Veins pinnate, middle vein concave above. Texture coriaceous, hard and thick. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Leaf: Upper epidermal cells subsquare, thick-walled, covered with a thick cuticle; unicellular non-glandular hairs rising from the midrib occasionally found. Palisade tissue consists of 2-4 layers of cells, located on the inner side of the upper epidermis; spongy tissue arranged loosely. One to several layers of collenchymatous cells located on the inner side of the upper and lower epidermis of the midrib. Vascular bundle of midrib collateral, with lignified fibre bundles located above and below the vascular bundle. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in the parenchyma, occasionally found near lower epidermis. Lower epidermal cells relatively small. Stone cells occasionally scattered in the collenchyma inside the lower epidermis of midrib and outside the lignified fibre bundles below the phloem [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 5, 1945, 258])\n\nLeaf margin: Collenchymatous cells, stone cells and lignified fibre bundles sequentially occur inside the epidermis of the leaf margin. Several layers of collenchymatous cells found at the margin near the petiole. Stone cells occasionally found in the collenchyma inside the epidermis of the leaf margin [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nPetiole: Epidermal cells subsquare, thick-walled, covered with a thick cuticle. Several layers of collenchymatous cells located inside the epidermis. Vascular bundles collateral, the one in the middle of the midrib relatively large, one small bundle located on either side of the middle bundle, sometimes with groups of fibres beneath the vascular bundle. Parenchyma scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-green to greenish-brown. Upper epidermal cells subsquare or polygonal, walls straight or slightly wavy, thick, pit canals distinct in surface view. Lower epidermal cells polygonal or irregular in shape, smaller than upper epidermis, walls slightly thin, stomata anomocytic, arranged densely. Non-glandular hairs occasionally found, unicellular. Collenchymatous cells subsquare, subrounded, polygonal or irregular in shape, with thick walls. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups, subrounded, polygonal, fusiform, ovate or irregular in shape, 6-27 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls slightly thin, striations sometimes present, pit canals distinct; bright white under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate 10-33 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, angles blunt; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles of two or more, 4-22 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; bright white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[40, 24, 1887, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 407, 1741, 2120])\n\nA. Sketch B. Magnified image of sketch C. Section illustration of leaf\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue 5. Xylem 6. Phloem\n\n7. Fibre bundles 8. Clusters of calcium oxalate 9. Collenchyma 10. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 10, 1946, 256])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 610, 1735, 2128])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of petiole of Ilicis Cornutae Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Fibre bundles 4. Xylem 5. Phloem 6. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n7. Parenchyma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 21, 1897, 256])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[236, 427, 1751, 2127])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Upper epidermal cells 2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata 3. Non-glandular hair\n\n4. Collenchymatous cells 5. Stone cells 6. Cluster of calcium oxalate 7. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 14, 1945, 255])"},{"name":"14","text":"Lupeol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of lupeol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nUrsolic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ursolic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, dichloromethane and methanol (5:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 80 mL of sulphuric acid to 20 mL of ethanol and dissolve 0.5 g of vanillin."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately lupeol standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), ursolic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 14, 1892, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[445, 367, 1077, 899])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[447, 971, 1167, 1462])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) lupeol and (ii) ursolic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[39, 10, 1948, 256])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[384, 373, 894, 1212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ilicis Cornutae Folium extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Ursolic acid standard solution 2. Lupeol standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of lupeol and ursolic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Lupeol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (70 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.7 mg of lupeol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nUrsolic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (120 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of ursolic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 25 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with 5 mL of methanol. Combine the solutions. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[45, 17, 1874, 265])\n\nsolution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (202 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (3.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.01%Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-27</td><td>71</td><td>29</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>27-31</td><td>71→80</td><td>29→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>31-36</td><td>80→88</td><td>20→12</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>36-60</td><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of lupeol Std-FP and ursolic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of lupeol and ursolic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of lupeol and ursolic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from lupeol and ursolic acid peaks should not be less than 90000 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject lupeol Std-FP, ursolic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of lupeol and ursolic acid peaks in the chromatograms of lupeol Std-FP, ursolic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify lupeol and ursolic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of lupeol Std-FP and ursolic acid Std-FP. The retention times of lupeol and ursolic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 9, 1945, 260])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ilicis Cornutae Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ilicis Cornutae Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,ursolic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(lupeol)</td><td>2.19</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[378, 1033, 1803, 1407])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ilicis Cornutae Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements (except cadmium should not be more than 6.0 mg/kg).\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[31, 24, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 8.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ilicis-cornutae-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ilicis Cornutae Folium","la":"Ilicis Cornutae Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ilicis-pubescentis-radix-et-caulis-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[535, 842, 1525, 1852])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis A. Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis B. Magnified image of stem in slice C. Magnified image of root in slice"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[535, 842, 1525, 1852])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis A. Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis B. Magnified image of stem in slice C. Magnified image of root in slice"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 毛冬青\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Maodongqing"},{"name":"13","text":"Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis is the dried root and stem of Ilex pubescens Hook. & Arn. (Aquifoliaceae). The root and stem are collected all year round, washed clean, cut into sections or slices, then dried under the sun to obtain Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis."},{"name":"13","text":"Cylindrical pieces, lumpy when cut, varying in size, 10-80 mm in diameter, 1-10 mm thick. Root externally greyish-brown to brown, slightly rough, with fine wrinkles and transverse lenticels; bark thin, sometimes sloughing off, slightly thickened in the old root; wood yellowish-white to pale yellowish-brown, striations fine and dense, fracture uneven with longitudinal striation; texture hard, uneasily broken; odour slight; taste bitter, astringent and followed by a sweet taste. Stem externally greyish-brown, slightly rough, with fine wrinkles and transverse lenticels; bark thin; wood broad, whitish to yellowish-white, xylem ray distinct, arranged radially; pith small, eccentric; texture hard, uneasily broken; odour slight; taste bitter, astringent and followed by a sweet taste (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Cork consists of 5-10 layers of cells. Cortex narrow. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups in the cortex. Phloem narrow. Cambium indistinct. Xylem broad, occupying the major portion of the root, vessels scattered singly or 2-4 in a group. Xylem rays consist of 1-8 rows of cells, gradually widening outward. Annual ring distinct [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 31, 1953, 257])\n\nStem: Cork consists of several layers of cells, elongated tangentially, thin and flat. Cortex narrow, consisting of 4-8 layers of cells, with several stone cells scattered. Phloem relatively narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad. Xylem rays distinct, arranged radially. Pith small, eccentric. Crystals of calcium oxalate scattered; polychromatic under the polarized microscope [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups, the cells subtriangular, subsquare, subrounded or irregular in shape, some lumens contain brown contents; bright yellow to brown under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres abundant, 10-20 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, sometimes with spiral striations; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Xylem ray cells show distinct pit canals. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted. Simple starch granules subrounded, 5-10 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with pointed, cleft-like or star-like hilum; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-3 units. Prisms of calcium oxalate subrectangular or irregularly squared, 15-50 $ \\mu $ m long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 238])\n\n天葵子\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 411, 1745, 2101])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Stone cells (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cell 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray 8. Annual ring\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1025, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[247, 416, 1748, 2095])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Crystals of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Crystals of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cell 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray\n\n8. Pith 9. Crystal of calcium oxalate\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba白花蛇舌草\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[102, 188, 203, 238])\n\n瓜子金\n\n天葵子\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[243, 407, 1758, 2087])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Xylem fibres 3. Xylem ray cells 4. Bordered-pitted vessels 5. Starch granules\n\n6. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[46, 38, 1954, 237])"},{"name":"13","text":"Ilexgenin A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ilexgenin A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate, methanol, water and formic acid (20:5:2:1:0.3, v/v). Shake well and use the lower layer."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (320 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Collect the supernatant."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ilexgenin A standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[673, 42, 817, 118])\n\n天葵子\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[369, 367, 873, 762])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of ilexgenin A\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[376, 968, 852, 1856])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Ilexgenin A standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of ilexgenin A (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[48, 37, 1954, 242])"},{"name":"13","text":"Ilexgenin A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of ilexgenin A CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (500 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-16</td><td>81→80</td><td>19→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>16-30</td><td>80→70</td><td>20→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>70→65</td><td>30→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-50</td><td>65→55</td><td>35→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>55→10</td><td>45→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>60-65</td><td>10</td><td>90</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ilexgenin A Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ilexgenin A should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ilexgenin A peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ilexgenin A peak should not be less than 1200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject ilexgenin A Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of ilexgenin A peak in the chromatogram of ilexgenin A Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ilexgenin A peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of ilexgenin A Std-FP. The retention times of ilexgenin A peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,ilexgeninA)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[340, 1528, 1825, 2071])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[49, 45, 234, 123])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[926, 89, 1025, 138])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 8.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ilicis-pubescentis-radix-et-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis","la":"Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ilicis-rotundae-cortex-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ilicis Rotundae Cortex v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[634, 979, 900, 1112])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1075, 945, 1330, 1109])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[587, 1291, 1375, 1606])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex A. Ilicis Rotundae Cortex B. Magnified image of outer surface of bark C. Magnified image of inner surface of bark\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 24, 1872, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[634, 979, 900, 1112])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1075, 945, 1330, 1109])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[587, 1291, 1375, 1606])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex A. Ilicis Rotundae Cortex B. Magnified image of outer surface of bark C. Magnified image of inner surface of bark\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 24, 1872, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Ilicis Rotundae Cortex\n\nChinese Name: 救必應\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jiubiying"},{"name":"12","text":"Ilicis Rotundae Cortex is the dried bark of Ilex rotunda Thunb. (Aquifoliaceae). The bark is collected in summer and autumn, then dried under the sun to obtain Ilicis Rotundae Cortex."},{"name":"12","text":"Quilled, semi-quilled or slightly curved flat pieces, varying in size, 1.5-25.7 cm long, 0.4-8.2 cm wide, the bark 1-25 mm thick. Outer surface greyish-white to greyish-brown, rough, usually wrinkled. Inner surface yellowish-brown to blackish-brown, with shallow longitudinal striations. Texture hard and brittle. Fracture slightly even. Odour slightly fragrant; taste bitter and slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of 5-12 layers of tangentially prolonged cells. Stone cells arranged singly or in groups, scattered in cortex, formed an interrupted ring. Phloem broad, scattered with stone cells and prisms of calcium oxalate. Phloem rays consist of 1-5 rows of radially prolonged cells. Prisms of calcium oxalate present in the parenchymatous cells of cortex and phloem (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale brown to brown. Stone cells numerous, scattered singly or in groups, pale yellowishgreen or pale yellow, subsquare, elliptical or irregular in shape, 13-138 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, pit canals obvious, some contain prisms of calcium oxalate; prisms of calcium oxalate polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, scattered in parenchymatous cells, polyhedral, square, fusiform or subbiconical, 9-58 $ \\mu $ m long, 6-43 $ \\mu $ m wide; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells scattered singly or in groups, colourless or pale\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 8, 1944, 258])\n\nbrown, polygonal, walls thick, small pits canals visible. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found, scattered singly. Fibres rare and broken (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 15, 1897, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[251, 535, 1725, 1948])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells 4. Phloem 5. Phloem ray 6. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\nFritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 660, 1729, 2132])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex\n\n1. Stone cells (1-1 scattered singly, 1-2 in group, 1-3 containing prisms of calcium oxalate)\n\n2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Cork cells 4. Cluster of calcium oxalate 5. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 25, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Pedunculoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of pedunculoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nSyringin (eleutheroside B) standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of syringin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol, formic acid and water (8.5:1:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately pedunculoside standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), syringin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 1-2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[448, 373, 1120, 772])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[449, 872, 1028, 1152])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) pedunculoside and (ii) syringin (eleutheroside B)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 1319, 939, 2106])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Syringin standard solution 2. Pedunculoside standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of pedunculoside and syringin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[33, 13, 1882, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Pedunculoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (340 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.4 mg of pedunculoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nSyringin (eleutheroside B) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of syringin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 40 mL of ethanol (50%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.05% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>5→10</td><td>95→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-20</td><td>10→20</td><td>90→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>20</td><td>80</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>20→40</td><td>80→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of pedunculoside Std-FP and syringin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of pedunculoside and syringin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of pedunculoside and syringin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from pedunculoside and syringin peaks should not be less than 400000 and 50000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 6, 1946, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject pedunculoside Std-FP, syringin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of pedunculoside and syringin peaks in the chromatograms of pedunculoside Std-FP, syringin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify pedunculoside and syringin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of pedunculoside Std-FP and syringin Std-FP. The retention times of pedunculoside and syringin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(syringin)</td><td>0.23</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.25</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, pedunculoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[388, 1717, 1802, 2302])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ilicis Rotundae Cortex extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 20, 1880, 265])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements (except cadmium should not be more than 5.5 mg/kg).\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 24.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 31.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ilicis-rotundae-cortex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ilicis Rotundae Cortex","la":"Ilicis Rotundae Cortex"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/impatientis-caulis-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Impatientis Caulis v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[367, 808, 1581, 2068])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Impatientis Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nA. Impatientis Caulis\n\nB. Magnified image of stem (showing longitudinal wrinkles and nodes)\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse sections of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[367, 808, 1581, 2068])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Impatientis Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nA. Impatientis Caulis\n\nB. Magnified image of stem (showing longitudinal wrinkles and nodes)\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse sections of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Impatientis Caulis\n\nChinese name: 鳯仙透骨草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Fengxiantougucao"},{"name":"12","text":"Impatientis Caulis is the dried stem of Impatiens balsamina L. (Balsaminaceae). The stem is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Impatientis Caulis."},{"name":"12","text":"Long, tubular stem, with few branches, varying in length, up to 60 cm long, 3-20 mm in diameter. Externally pale yellowish-white, yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, shriveled, longitudinal wrinkles distinct, node brown. Light in weight and texture fragile. Fracture mostly hollow in centre or white pith visible. Odour slight, taste slightly acidic (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with a thin layer of cuticle, mostly non-glandular hairs fallen off after staining process. Collenchyma consists of 4-5 layer of cells. Cortex parenchymatous cells irregular in shape. Phloem narrow, arranged in a ring. Mostly, xylem vessels single scattered. Pith relatively large, mostly hollow in the centre; sometimes raphides of calcium oxalate visible; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Raphides of calcium oxalate visible; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Non-glandular hair composed of 1-20 cells, some non-glandular hairs with short branch, 13-48 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Fibres pit distinct. Vessels mostly spiral type (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 44, 1954, 252])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[234, 382, 1739, 2121])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Impatientis Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Impatientis Caulis A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) D. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Epidermis 3. Collenchyma 4. Cortex 5. Phloem 6. Xylem\n\n7. Raphides of calcium oxalate 8. Pith\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 49, 1852, 97])\n\n鴨跖草\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[268, 462, 1694, 2069])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Impatientis Caulis\n\n1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Non-glandular hair\n\n3. Non-glandular hair with short branch 4. Fibres 5. Spiral vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[21, 39, 1944, 243])"},{"name":"12","text":"Scopoletin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of scopoletin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60- $ 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (3:2:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately scopoletin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[382, 433, 737, 747])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[382, 872, 779, 1099])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and (ii) scopoletin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[376, 1264, 875, 2064])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Impatientis Caulis extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Scopoletin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of scopoletin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 38, 1945, 245])"},{"name":"12","text":"2-Methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nScopoletin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (2 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of scopoletin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (344 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 130 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and $ 1 8 5 \\mathrm{~ m}^{2} / \\mathrm{g} $ surface area). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-50</td><td>90→50</td><td>10→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone Std-FP and scopoletin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and scopoletin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and scopoletin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and scopoletin peaks should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone Std-FP,scopoletin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and scopoletin peaks in the chromatograms of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone Std-FP,scopoletin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and scopoletin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone Std-FP and scopoletin Std-FP. The retention times of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone and scopoletin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Impatientis Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Impatientis Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, scopoletin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone)</td><td>1.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[335, 380, 1818, 1000])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Impatientis Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 17.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"2-Methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone standard stock solution, Std-Stock (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 0.5 mg of 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\n2-Methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.5,1,2,5,50 mg/L for 2-methoxy-1,4-naphthoquinone."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh accurately 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/impatientis-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Impatientis Caulis","la":"Impatientis Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/inulae-radix-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inulae Radix v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[7, 812, 1466, 1916])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Inulae Radix A. Inulae Radix B. Transverse section of root C. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[7, 812, 1466, 1916])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Inulae Radix A. Inulae Radix B. Transverse section of root C. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 260])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Inulae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 土木香\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tumuxiang"},{"name":"11","text":"Inulae Radix is the dried root of Inula helenium L. (Asteraceae). The root is collected in autumn, soil and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Inulae Radix."},{"name":"11","text":"Conical, slightly curved, 5-29 cm long. Externally yellowish-brown or dark brown, with longitudinal wrinkles and fibrous root scars. Root vertex large with sunken stem scars and remnants of leaf sheath, surrounded by cylindrical rootlets. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Fracture slightly even, yellowishwhite to pale greyish-yellow, with dented-dotted oil cavities. Odour slightly fragrant; taste bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex consists of around 10 layers of tangential elongated parenchymatous cells. Phloem broad. Cambium arranged in ring, indistinct. Xylem vessel rare, singly scattered or several in groups, arranged radially. Xylem fibres rare, in bundles, surrounding the vessels at the centre of xylem. Oil cavity 50-350 $ \\mu $m in diameter, scattered in the phloem and xylem (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown to brown. Inulin abundant, colourless, in irregular pieces; white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate, 10-83 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Cork cells yellowish-brown, polygonal. Oil cavities occasionally found, mostly fractured. Xylem fibres long-fusiform, with oblique ends and oblique pits (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 7, 1946, 256])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[250, 415, 1741, 2085])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n500 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Inulae Radix\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil Cavity\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Oil cavity 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Xylem fibres\n\n厚朴花\n\n月季花\n\nRosae Chinensis Flos\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[283, 667, 691, 1062])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[729, 665, 1136, 1067])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1172, 667, 1700, 1063])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[284, 1096, 810, 1803])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[828, 1094, 1214, 1800])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1240, 1094, 1702, 1798])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Inulae Radix\n\n1. Inulin 2. Reticulate vessel 3. Cork cells 4. Xylem fibres 5. Fragment of oil cavity\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[46, 13, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"11","text":"Alantolactone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of alantolactone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nIsoalantolactone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isoalantolactone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane and ethyl acetate (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Mix cautiously 25 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v) into 25 mL of ice-cold glacial acetic acid. Add 2.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde. Add further 50 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC plate of silica gel with 5% silver nitrate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately alantolactone standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) , isoalantolactone standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm and analyze within 30 min. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 13, 1892, 261])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[380, 365, 941, 673])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[384, 747, 963, 1065])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) alantolactone and (ii) isoalantolactone\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[385, 1238, 912, 2053])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference TLC chromatogram of Inulae Radix extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Alantolactone standard solution 2. Isoalantolactone standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of alantolactone and isoalantolactone (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 11, 1945, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Alantolactone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of alantolactone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nIsoalantolactone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of isoalantolactone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (194 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.05% phosphoric acid and acetonitrile (45:55, v/v). The elution time is about 40 min."},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of alantolactone Std-FP and isoalantolactone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of alantolactone and isoalantolactone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of alantolactone and isoalantolactone peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from alantolactone and isoalantolactone peaks should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject alantolactone Std-FP, isoalantolactone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of alantolactone and isoalantolactone peaks in the chromatograms of alantolactone Std-FP, isoalantolactone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify alantolactone and isoalantolactone peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of alantolactone Std-FP and isoalantolactone Std-FP. The retention times of alantolactone and isoalantolactone peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 11, 1889, 264])\n\nshould not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Inulae Radix extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Inulae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.87</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(isoalantolactone)</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, alantolactone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 1138, 1851, 1684])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Inulae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 14, 1946, 257])"},{"name":"11","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 14.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/inulae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inulae Radix","la":"Inulae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/isatidis-folium-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isatidis Folium v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[486, 1035, 1436, 1793])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 4, 1929, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[486, 1035, 1436, 1793])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 4, 1929, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Isatidis Folium\n\nChinese Name: 大青葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Daqingye"},{"name":"12","text":"Isatidis Folium is the dried leaf of Isatis indigotica Fort. (Brassicaceae). The leaf is collected 2-3 times in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Isatidis Folium."},{"name":"12","text":"Crumpled and mostly rolled, some are broken, the upper surface dark greyish-green, lower surface with a distinct prominent midvein. When wholly intact, oblong to oblanceolate, 5-20 cm long, 1.5-7 cm wide, base attenuate to the petiole with auricles, margin entire to slightly wavy, apex obtuse; petioles 2-11 cm long, pale brownish-yellow. Texture fragile. Odour slight; taste slightly sour, bitter, and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Upper and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of rectangular cells. Palisade tissue and spongy tissue are indistinctly differentiated. Vascular bundles collateral, 4-9, with a large one in the middle. Sclerenchyma located immediately outside of the xylem and the phloem. Undefined pigment bodies, bluish-black, scattered in the parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greenish-brown. Bluish-black pigment bodies are scattered in the mesophyll. Crystals with unknown identity, irregular in shape, 30-110 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, are also found in the mesophyll. Sclerenchymatous cells occur in bundles. Vessels are in groups, by spiral and reticulate types, 7-55 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Anticlinal walls of lower epidermal cells slightly sinuous and beaded. Stomata anomocytic, with 3-4 subsidiary cells (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 15, 1984, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Indigo standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of indigo CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of methanol and dichloromethane (1:9, v/v).\n\nIndirubin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of indirubin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of a mixture of methanol and dichloromethane (1:9, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and acetone (97:3, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture. Discard the filtrate. Add 20 mL of dichloromethane to the residue. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of a mixture of methanol and dichloromethane (1:9, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately indigo standard solution (60 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), indirubin standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of indigo and indirubin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[170, 14, 326, 72])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[598, 15, 694, 68])\n\n延胡索 Corydalis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1693, 25, 1901, 108])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 391, 1687, 1963])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Isatidis Folium\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Bluish-black pigment bodies D. Crystals with unknown identity\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Sclerenchyma 3. Xylem 4. Phloem\n\n5. Bluish-black pigment bodies 6. Lower epidermis 7. Crystal with unknown identity\n\nOrpiment雌黄\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\n雄黄\n\nRealgar\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1891, 45, 1984, 89])\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n五味子\n\nSchisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[342, 453, 960, 913])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1033, 455, 1646, 906])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[345, 978, 962, 1424])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1032, 978, 1647, 1425])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[390, 1487, 962, 1940])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1032, 1486, 1647, 1932])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Isatidis Folium (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Bluish-black pigment bodies 2. Crystals with unknown identity 3. Sclerenchymatous cells\n\n4. Spiral vessels 5. Lower epidermal cells 6. Anomocytic stomata\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 3, 1931, 259])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[376, 365, 872, 939])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) indigo and (ii) indirubin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Indigo standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (2 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of indigo CRS and place it in a 50-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in 5 mL of a mixture of methanol and dichloromethane (1:9, v/v). Make up to the mark with methanol. Pipette 1 mL of the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol.\n\nIndirubin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (6 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of indirubin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.6 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 25 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 4, 1978, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>80-78</td><td>20-22</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>78-45</td><td>22-55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>45-43</td><td>55-57</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>43-40</td><td>57-60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of indigo Std-FP and indirubin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of indigo and indirubin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of indigo and indirubin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from indigo and indirubin peaks should not be less than 60000 and 40000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject indigo Std-FP, indirubin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of indigo and indirubin peaks in the chromatograms of indigo Std-FP, indirubin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify indigo and indirubin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of indigo Std-FP and indirubin Std-FP. The retention times of indigo and indirubin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Isatidis Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Isatidis Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(indigo)</td><td>0.87</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,indirubin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n延胡索\n\nCorydalis Rhizoma\n\n大青葉\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[392, 369, 1715, 1032])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Isatidis Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 21.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 7.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 14, 1984, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 22.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Indirubin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.5 mg of indirubin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nIndirubin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the indirubin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 0.1, 1, 1.5, 3, 4 mg/L for indirubin."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh accurately 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/isatidis-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isatidis Folium","la":"Isatidis Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/isatidis-radix-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isatidis Radix v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[635, 907, 1302, 1891])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[635, 907, 1302, 1891])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 255])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Isatidis Radix\n\nChinese Name: 板蓝根\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Banlangen"},{"name":"11","text":"Isatidis Radix is the dried root of Isatis indigotica Fort. (Brassicaceae). The root is collected in autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Isatidis Radix."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical, slightly twisted, 3-20 cm long, 3-20 mm in diameter. Externally pale greyish-yellow to brownish-yellow, with longitudinal wrinkles, transverse lenticels and branch roots or branch root scars. The top part of the root slightly expanded, marked by dark green to dark brown remnants of petiole bases arranged in whorls, and by dense tubercles. Texture compact or slightly soft, fracture yellowishwhite to yellowish-brown in bark, and yellow in wood. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet, then bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex narrow. Phloem broad, rays distinct, usually with clefts at the outer part. Cambium in a ring. Vessels subrounded, usually 2-3 in a group or singly scattered, arranged radially; xylem fibres in bundles. Parenchyma contains abundant starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale brown to yellowish-brown. Starch granules numerous; simple granules subrounded, ovoid or subsquare, 2-21 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum distinct, dotted, slit-shaped or V-shaped, striations indistinct; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope; compound granules composed of 2-5 units. Stone cells colourless to pale yellow, subsquare, subrectangular\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 7, 1984, 263])\n\nor irregular in shape, sometimes slightly acute at one end, 24-185 $ \\mu $m long, 9-75 $ \\mu $m in diameter; pit canals and striations distinct; yellowish-white to greyish-white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate, 3-93 $ \\mu $m in diameter; spiral and bordered-pitted vessels occasionally visible. Xylem fibres mostly in bundles, 6-34 $ \\mu $m in diameter, sometimes with distinct pits and pit canals. Cork cells colourless to yellow, subpolygonal or elongated-polygonal in surface view (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Epigoitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of epigoitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), methanol and ethyl acetate (0.5:3:7, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of boiling water. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol and then filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately epigoitrin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of epigoitrin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[22, 2, 1930, 255])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 440, 1736, 1997])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Isatidis Radix\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cork and starch granules in parenchyma D. Phloem, cambium and xylem\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Starch granules\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 45, 1984, 86])\n\n墨旱莲\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[277, 448, 1690, 1966])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Stone cells 3. Reticulate vessels 4. Spiral vessels\n\n5. Bordered-pitted vessel 6. Xylem fibres 7. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 1, 1930, 259])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 369, 643, 666])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of epigoitrin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Epigoitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (7 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.7 mg of epigoitrin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of water."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of water. Immediately place the mixture in a boiling water bath for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with water. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with water. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (240 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size and 70 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.6 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>98</td><td>2</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-40</td><td>98→85</td><td>2→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>85</td><td>15</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of epigoitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of epigoitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of epigoitrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from epigoitrin peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 4, 1984, 263])\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject epigoitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of epigoitrin peak in chromatogram of epigoitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify epigoitrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of epigoitrin Std-FP. The retention times of epigoitrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Isatidis Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Isatidis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.25</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.29</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, epigoitrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 1795, 1881, 2410])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Isatidis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 6, 1927, 258])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 34.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 31.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/isatidis-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isatidis Radix","la":"Isatidis Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/isodonis-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isodonis Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[380, 809, 1440, 1907])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried aerial part of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Magnified image of leaves (Left: upper surface, Right: lower surface)\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf (brownish-red dots $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nCommelinae Herba\n\nCommelinae Herba\n\n鸭跖草 Isodonis Herba\n\n京大戟\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n苦木\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[367, 503, 1648, 1784])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Isodon serra (Maxim.) Kudo A. Aerial part of herb B. Magnified image of leaves (Left: upper surface, Right: lower surface) C. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},"sections":[{"name":"25","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[380, 809, 1440, 1907])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried aerial part of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara.\n\nA. Aerial part of herb\n\nB. Magnified image of leaves (Left: upper surface, Right: lower surface)\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf (brownish-red dots $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nCommelinae Herba\n\nCommelinae Herba\n\n鸭跖草 Isodonis Herba\n\n京大戟\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n苦木\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[367, 503, 1648, 1784])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried aerial part of Isodon serra (Maxim.) Kudo A. Aerial part of herb B. Magnified image of leaves (Left: upper surface, Right: lower surface) C. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},{"name":"25","text":"Official name: Isodonis Herba\n\nChinese name: 溪黄草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Xihuangcao"},{"name":"25","text":"Isodonis Herba is the dried aerial part of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara. or Isodon serra (Maxim.) Kudo (Lamiaceae). The aerial part is collected during summer and autumn before flowering, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Isodonis Herba."},{"name":"25","text":"Stem square-columnar, arris obtuse, longitudinal grooves distinct, frequently opposite-branched, 30-110 cm long, 2-5 mm in diameter; externally yellowish-green to brown, covered with pubescence and brownish-red glandular dots. Leaves opposite, petiolate, crumpled and easily broken; intact ones ovate to ovate-lanceolate or lanceolate, 4-10 cm long, 1.5-4.5 cm wide; leaf blade acuminate at apex, margin obtusely serrate; both surfaces greyish-green, covered with pubescence and brownish-red glandular dots. After soaked with water, then kneaded with fingers, yellowish-brown juice visible. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet, then bitter [Fig. 1 (i)]."},{"name":"25","text":"Stem: Remnants of non-glanlular hair frequently observed on the surface of epidermis. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare or rectangular cells. Collenchymatous cells 4-9 layers, subpolygonal or irregular polygonal, present at outer parts of cortex near ridges. Cortex narrow. Pericycle fibre bundles beneath the cortex, arranged in an interrupted ring, abundant near ridges. Phloem relatively narrow. Xylem radially arranged, relatively broad near ridges. Pith large, parenchymatous cells large, occasionally hollow in the centre [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 42, 1965, 259])\n\nLeaf: Remnants of non-glandular hair frequently observed on the surface of epidermis. Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of flat rectangular cells. Palisade tissue consists of 2-3 layers of cells. Spongy tissue consists of several layers of loosely arranged irregular cells. Collenchyma located under the epidermis of midrib. Vascular bundles of midrib collateral; vessels usually several in rows; phloem cells polygonal. Lower epidermis cells relatively small [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"25","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Non-glandular hairs composed of 1-3 cells, conical, base relatively broad, 11-92 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Glandular scales numerous; oblate-spherical, with yellowishbrown content, 4-8 cells, 14-49 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Glandular hairs small; head of 1-2 cells, subglobose, 10-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; stalk unicellular. Upper epidermal cells rectangular or polygonal, anticlinal walls slightly sinuate and thick, larger; compared with the lower epidermal, stomata occasionally observed, paracytic or diacytic. Lower epidermal cells irregular in shape, anticlinal walls usually sinuate, stomata diacytic or anomocytic, subrounded or oblong in shape. Vessels numerous, mainly spiral and reticulate vessels, 8-42 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Brownish-red dots numerous, scattered on the surface of epidermal cells (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1059, 91, 1142, 133])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[751, 145, 906, 186])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1937, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[312, 621, 938, 1306])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[313, 1477, 865, 1957])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[989, 549, 1709, 1987])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Collenchyma 4. Cortex\n\n5. Pericycle fibre bundle 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\nCommelinae Herba\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nSedi Herba垂盆草\n\n黑種草子\n\nNigellae Semen\n\n京大戟\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n鴨跖草\n\n石斛\n\nArtemisiae Anomalae Herba\n\n苦木 Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[235, 398, 1738, 2123])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Collenchyma 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Lower epidermis\n\n西河柳\n\n野老鸛草\n\n紅旱蓮\n\nHyperici Ascyri Herba\n\nPolygonati Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1058, 91, 1144, 134])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1691, 55, 1938, 138])\n\n黄精\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[306, 731, 739, 1055])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[779, 731, 1210, 1055])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1249, 732, 1680, 1052])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[307, 1105, 739, 1425])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[778, 1100, 1210, 1425])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1248, 1099, 1678, 1426])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[307, 1463, 738, 1786])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[776, 1464, 1209, 1784])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1249, 1466, 1682, 1784])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"25","text":"1. Non-glandular hair 2. Glandular scale 3. Glandular hairs\n\n4. Upper epidermal cells with stoma ( $ \\rightarrow $ ） 5. Reticulate vessel\n\n6. Lower epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n7. Spiral vessel 8. Lower epidermal cells with brownish-red dots ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"25","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"25","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, ethanol and formic acid (8:3:1.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"25","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (50%). Filter the extract. Make an appropriate dilution if necessary."},{"name":"25","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately rosmarinic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[393, 1927, 979, 2158])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of rosmarinic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[19, 40, 1948, 246])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[376, 371, 841, 1200])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of dried aerial part of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara. extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Rosmarinic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of rosmarinic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"25","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"25","text":"Weigh 0.7 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[22, 45, 1966, 253])"},{"name":"25","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (329 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>85→60</td><td>15→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"25","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of rosmarinic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of rosmarinic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from rosmarinic acid peak should not be less than 60000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"25","text":"Separately inject rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of rosmarinic acid peak in the chromatogram of rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify rosmarinic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The retention times of rosmarinic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of dried aerial part of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara. extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[20, 40, 1947, 255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of dried aerial part of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.77</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,rosmarinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[330, 804, 1815, 1441])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried aerial part of Isodon lophanthoides (Buch. -Ham. ex D. Don) H. Hara. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"25","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[21, 44, 1966, 256])"},{"name":"25","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"25","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"25","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/isodonis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isodonis Herba","la":"Isodonis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/kansui-radix-unprocessed-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kansui Radix unprocessed v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[581, 841, 1411, 1908])\n\nA. Kansui Radix (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of transverse section of root tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 21, 1873, 264])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[581, 841, 1411, 1908])\n\nA. Kansui Radix (unprocessed) B. Magnified image of transverse section of root tuber\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 21, 1873, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Kansui Radix (unprocessed)\n\nChinese Name: 甘遂（生）\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gansui (Sheng)"},{"name":"11","text":"Kansui Radix (unprocessed) is the unprocessed dried root tuber of Euphorbia kansui T. N. Liou ex T. P. Wang (Euphorbiaceae). The root tuber is collected in spring before flowering or in late autumn after the stem and leaves withered, soil removed, and the outer bark removed by dashing, then dried under the sun to obtain Kansui Radix (unprocessed)."},{"name":"11","text":"Ellipsoid, long cylindrical or moniliform, 1-6 cm long, 0.5-2.5 cm in diameter. Externally whitish or yellowish-white, with brown patches of cork remaining at pits. Texture fragile, easily broken. Fracture starchy and white, wood slightly radial-striated; long cylindrical root tubers appearing more fibrous. Odour slight (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Remaining cork consists of several layers of cork cells. Cortex narrow, scattered with oval, subtriangular, subsquare, rectangular or polygonal sclerenchymatous cells, dotted with laticiferous tubes. Phloem broad, dotted with laticiferous tubes. Cambium in a ring. Xylem vessels singly scattered, several to 10 or more in groups, arranged radially; xylem ray consists of 2-10 or more rows of cells. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour whitish. Starch granules numerous, single starch granules spherical or hemispherical, 5-34 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum dotted, slit-shaped or stellate; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-8 units. Non-articulated\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 6, 1944, 258])\n\nlaticiferous tubes contain yellow and minute granular inclusions. Sclerenchymatous cells rectangular, fusiform, subtriangular or polygonal, 15-60 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls slightly lignified or non-lignified. Bordered-pitted vessels frequently visible, usually accompanied by fibre bundles, 13-80 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[263, 516, 948, 921])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[282, 1324, 585, 1650])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1030, 490, 1703, 1178])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1029, 1208, 1639, 1548])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1030, 1573, 1704, 1962])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Kansui Radix (unprocessed)\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Laticiferous tube\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Laticiferous tube 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[40, 11, 1946, 257])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 529, 1720, 2125])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Kansui Radix (unprocessed)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Non-articulated laticiferous tube 3. Sclerenchymatous cells\n\n4. Bordered-pitted vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 23, 1881, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"Euphol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of euphol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (30-60 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and acetone (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately euphol standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[377, 1796, 947, 2098])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of euphol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 9, 1948, 257])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 373, 801, 1131])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Kansui Radix (unprocessed) extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Euphol standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of euphol (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Euphol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (150 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of euphol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 40 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue for three times each with 5 mL of ethyl acetate. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the solutions and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol and transfer the solution\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[39, 20, 1853, 262])\n\nto a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue for four times each with 5 mL of methanol. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times1 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with OS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>45→20</td><td>55→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-50</td><td>20→15</td><td>80→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>15→5</td><td>85→95</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of euphol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of euphol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of euphol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from euphol peak should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject euphol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of euphol peak in the chromatogram of euphol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify euphol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of euphol Std-FP. The retention times of euphol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Kansui Radix (unprocessed) extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 10, 1946, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Kansui Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.24</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.34</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.97</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,euphol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 883, 1750, 1483])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Kansui Radix (unprocessed) extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[39, 27, 1872, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 58.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 11.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/kansui-radix-unprocessed","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kansui Radix unprocessed","la":"Kansui Radix unprocessed"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/kochiae-fructus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kochiae Fructus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[489, 858, 1512, 1964])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Kochiae Fructus A. Kochiae Fructus B. Magnified fruits with persistent perianth C. Magnified fruits without persistent perianth D. Magnified seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 14, 1908, 264])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[489, 858, 1512, 1964])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Kochiae Fructus A. Kochiae Fructus B. Magnified fruits with persistent perianth C. Magnified fruits without persistent perianth D. Magnified seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 14, 1908, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Kochiae Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 地膚子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Difuzi"},{"name":"12","text":"Kochiae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Kochia scoparia (L.) Schrad. (Chenopodiaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in autumn, dried under the sun, then foreign matter removed to obtain Kochiae Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Flattened, spherical, and five-pointed star in shape, 2-4 mm in diameter, covered with persistent perianth. Externally pale brown to greyish-brown, with 5 membranous winglets; the centre of dorsal surface has a slightly prominent, dotted fruit stalk scar and 5-11 radial veins. After taking off the perianth, a membranous and translucent pericarp visible. Seeds flattened and ovoid, 1.5-2.5 mm long, brownish-black to black. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Persistent perianth consists of 1 layer of parenchymatous cells, stone cells present occasionally. Pericarp consists of 1 layer of U-shaped sclerenchymatous cells, containing numerous small prisms of calcium oxalate. Testa consists of 1 layer of cells, yellowish-brown. Perisperm consists of polygonal parenchymatous cells, filled with minute starch granules. Radicle relatively small, consisting of parenchymatous cells, filled with aleurone grains. Cotyledons relatively large, the cells filled with aleurone grains and oil droplets. Epicotyl located at the centre of the cotyledons (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 7, 1973, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to brown. Epidermal cells of persistent perianth polygonal, wall slightly thickened. Stomata of persistent perianth anomocytic, 19-35 $ \\mu $ m long, 15-30 $ \\mu $ m wide. Pollen grains from persistent perianth yellowish-green, spherical, 15-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Stone cells occasionally found, short fibre-like, wall thick and slightly lignified. Testa cells brown, rectangular to irregular. Perisperm cells consist of polygonal parenchymatous cells, filled with minute starch granules. Non-glandular hairs consist of 2-3 cells, 4-18 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate present in persistent perianth, 8-53 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pericarp cells rectangular to subsquare, wall thickened and sinuous, containing numerous prisms of calcium oxalate, 2-10 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[35, 99, 209, 198])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 454, 1742, 2118])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Kochiae Fructus\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Persistent perianth 2. Pericarp 3. Testa 4. Radicle\n\n5. Perisperm 6. Cotyledon 7. Epicotyl\n\nRubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nLophatheri Herba淡竹葉\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nKochiae Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[294, 594, 693, 858])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[708, 591, 1005, 866])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1015, 588, 1367, 861])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1381, 591, 1684, 858])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[296, 894, 679, 1379])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[715, 890, 1058, 1383])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1085, 890, 1378, 1374])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1411, 887, 1686, 1104])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1414, 1125, 1686, 1374])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[296, 1416, 972, 1845])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1011, 1415, 1694, 1855])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Epidermal cells of persistent perianth 2. Stoma from persistent perianth\n\n3. Pollen grains from persistent perianth 4. Stone cells 5. Testa cells 6. Perisperm cells\n\n7. Non-glandular hair 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Pericarp cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 12, 1906, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Momordin Ic standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of momordin Ic CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, butan-1-ol and glacial acetic acid (12:7:1, v/v). Shake well and use the upper layer."},{"name":"12","text":"Mix cautiously 25 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v) into 25 mL of ice-cold glacial acetic acid. Add 2.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde. Add further 50 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately momordin Ic standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 8 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of momordin Ic.\n\n餘甘子 Phyllanthi Fructus\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nKochiae Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 372, 1279, 826])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of momordin Ic\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Momordin Ic standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of momordin Ic CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (215 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of methanol, water and glacial acetic acid (85:15:0.2, v/v). The elution time is about 60 min."},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of momordin Ic Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of momordin Ic should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of momordin Ic peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from momordin Ic peak should not be less than 3000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[28, 6, 1908, 264])\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject momordin Ic Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of momordin Ic peak in the chromatogram of momordin Ic Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify momordin Ic peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of momordin Ic Std-FP. The retention times of momordin Ic peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Kochiae Fructus extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Kochiae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.85</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, momordin Ic)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.42</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>3.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nCyperi Rhizoma\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\n香附\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\nKochiae Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[301, 366, 1826, 922])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Kochiae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 10.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[28, 11, 1907, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/kochiae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kochiae Fructus","la":"Kochiae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/leonuri-fructus-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Leonuri Fructus v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[599, 867, 1379, 1501])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[687, 1565, 848, 1807])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[896, 1564, 1043, 1796])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1144, 1560, 1309, 1678])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1149, 1703, 1308, 1812])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[599, 867, 1379, 1501])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[687, 1565, 848, 1807])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[896, 1564, 1043, 1796])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1144, 1560, 1309, 1678])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1149, 1703, 1308, 1812])"},{"name":"12","text":"A. Leonuri Fructus\n\nB. Dorsal view\n\nC. Ventral view\n\nD. Top view\n\nE. Bottom view\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Leonuri Fructus\n\nChinese name: 茺蔚子\n\nChinese phonetic name: Chongweizi"},{"name":"12","text":"Leonuri Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Leonurus japonicus Houtt. (Lamiaceae). The plant is collected in autumn when the fruit is ripe, dried under the sun, then the fruit is tapped out and foreign matter removed to obtain Leonuri Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Oblong-triquetrous, 1.5-3.3 mm long, 0.8-2.0 mm in diameter. Apex relatively broad, truncate; base cuneate, with a dented fruit stalk scar. Surface grey, greyish-brown or brown, with protruded maculae, brown or dark brown, irregular in shape, varying in density. Pericarp thin, endosperm and cotyledon off-white, oily. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of rectangular cells, varying in height; some cells with thickened wall and reticulate pits. Mesocarp consists of 2-3 layers of cells. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of sclerenchymatous cells (beaker cells), with thickened inner and lateral walls, boundaries indistinct; lumen small, usually containing 1 prism of calcium oxalate. Testa consists of 1 layer of cells, boundaries indistinct. Endosperm cells subpolygonal, filled with aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells relatively small, filled with aleurone grains (Fig. 2)"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brown. Sclenchymatous cells (beaker cells) of endocarp pale yellow to brownishyellow, irregular in surface view, with thickened walls, anticlinal walls deeply sinuate, pits dotted; boundaries indistinct in lateral view, with lumen at one side; always with 1 large prism of calcium oxalate and numerous fine crystals of calcium oxalate in each cells; prism of calcium oxalate polychromatic, crystals of calcium oxalate polychromatic or white, and sclenchymatous cells white under the polarized microscope. Exocarp cells colourless to yellowish-brown, subpolygonal in surface view, scattered with wall thickened cells, yellowishbrown to reddish-brown; rectangular in lateral view, wall thickened cells with reticulate pits. Mesocarp cells yellowish-brown, polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls wavy. Prisms of calcium oxalate square, hexagonal or subspherical in shape, scattered or present in sclenchymatous cells of endocarp, 5-26 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of testa yellowish-brown, polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls sometimes beaded. Endosperm cells polygonal or subrounded, wall slightly thickened, containing oil droplets and aleurone granules. Cotyledon cells subsquare, subrounded or polygonal, containing oil droplets and aleurone granules (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 43, 1972, 255])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 411, 1738, 2036])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Leonuri Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of exocarp D. Magnified image of endocarp\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Endocarp 4. Testa 5. Endosperm\n\n6. Cotyledon 7. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n野老鸛草\n\nPolygonati Rhizoma\n\nDeinagkistrodon (Agkistrodon)\n\nHyperici Ascyri Herba\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 92, 1143, 134])\n\n黄精\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[751, 145, 907, 186])\n\nValerianae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1936, 135])\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\n纈草\n\nLeonuri Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[304, 479, 698, 868])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[713, 480, 1116, 864])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1130, 486, 1396, 862])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1408, 480, 1679, 866])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[307, 937, 758, 1285])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[769, 935, 1213, 1288])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1225, 940, 1676, 1274])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[307, 1308, 677, 1638])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[688, 1307, 1088, 1640])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1101, 1303, 1387, 1642])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1400, 1307, 1676, 1643])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[305, 1656, 704, 2030])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[716, 1663, 1164, 2026])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1176, 1658, 1677, 2034])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Sclenchymatous cells of endocarp (1-1 surface view, 1-2 lateral view)\n\n2. Exocarp cells (2-1, 2-2 surface view; 2-3, 2-4 lateral view) 3. Mesocarp cells\n\n4. Prisms of calcium oxalate 5. Epidermal cells of testa 6. Endosperm cells\n\n7. Cotyledon cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 45, 1966, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Stachydrine hydrochloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of stachydrine hydrochloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, formic acid and water (3:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 30 mL of methanol (50%). Reflux the mixture for 20 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately stachydrine hydrochloride standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Expose the plate to iodine vapour in a chamber for about 20-30 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[386, 1896, 740, 2166])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of stachydrine hydrochloride\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[19, 39, 1948, 248])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 371, 808, 1210])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Leonuri Fructus extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stachydrine hydrochloride standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of stachydrine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stachydrine hydrochloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of stachydrine hydrochloride CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 30 mL of methanol (50%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the combined filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (50%). Transfer the extract to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.22- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 44, 1967, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right) $ flow: 3.2 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with polyamine chemically bonded to cross-linked polyvinyl alcohol polymer (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>90→55</td><td>10→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of stachydrine hydrochloride Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of stachydrine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of stachydrine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from stachydrine peak should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject stachydrine hydrochloride Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of stachydrine peak in the chromatogram of stachydrine hydrochloride Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify stachydrine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of stachydrine hydrochloride Std-FP. The retention times of stachydrine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Leonuri Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Leonuri Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, stachydrine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.64</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[330, 373, 1816, 925])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Leonuri Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 7.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 45, 1966, 258])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/leonuri-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Leonuri Fructus","la":"Leonuri Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lini-semen-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lini Semen v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[466, 1058, 1162, 1658])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1233, 1037, 1388, 1284])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1287, 1380, 1439, 1669])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lini Semen A. Lini Semen B. Magnified image of seed C. Magnified image of hilum (1) and raphe (2)"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[466, 1058, 1162, 1658])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1233, 1037, 1388, 1284])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1287, 1380, 1439, 1669])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lini Semen A. Lini Semen B. Magnified image of seed C. Magnified image of hilum (1) and raphe (2)"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Lini Semen\n\nChinese Name: 亞麻子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yamazi"},{"name":"16","text":"Lini Semen is the dried ripe seed of Linum usitatissimum L. (Linaceae). The plant is collected in autumn when the seed is ripe, the harvested plant dried under the sun, the seeds tapped out, foreign matter removed, then the seeds are gathered and dried under the sun to obtain Lini Semen."},{"name":"16","text":"Ovoid, flat, 2.3-5.9 mm long, 1.6-2.9 mm wide, 0.7-1.3 mm thick. Externally brown to reddish-brown, smooth and lustrous. One end acuminate and slightly oblique, the other end obtuse, with raphe and dented hilum on the acuminate end. Testa and endosperm thin, cotyledons 2, yellowish-white, oily. Odour slight, slightly flavoured and viscous on chewing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"16","text":"Epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of rectangular cells with mucilaginous walls. Hypodermis consists of 1 layer of cells, walls slightly thickened. Sclerenchymatous cells layer consists of 1 layer of densely arranged cells, walls thickened. Parenchymatous cells layer consists of collapsed cells, boundaries indistinct. Pigment layer consists of 1 layer of flattened cells filled with pigment masses. Endosperm consists of several layers of polygonal cells filled with aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells relatively small, filled with aleurone grains (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 27, 1956, 257])"},{"name":"16","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to pale brown. Pigment cells pale yellow to yellow, square, rectangular or polygonal in surface view, 9-49 $ \\mu $m in diameter, anticlinal walls finely beaded, always containing yellowish-brown, brown or reddish-brown masses. Pigment masses yellowish-brown, brown or reddish-brown, square, rectangular or polygonal, edges usually finely serrated. Epidermal cells of testa large, polygonal in surface view, 16-69 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with mucilaginous walls. Sclerenchymatous cells pale yellow to yellow, strip-shaped, 4-23 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls thickened or slightly thickened, with fine and dense pits; yellow or white under the polarized microscope. Hypodermal cells colourless to pale yellow, subpolygonal to subrounded in surface view, walls slightly thickened. Endosperm cells polygonal to subpolygonal, wall slightly thickened, filled with aleurone grains and oil droplets. Cotyledon cells polygonal to subpolygonal, relatively small, filled with aleurone grains and oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[104, 188, 202, 240])\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 391, 1737, 2065])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Lini Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of testa 1. Epidermis of testa 2. Hypodermis 3. Sclerenchymatous cell layer 4. Parenchymatous cell layer 5. Pigment layer 6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[197, 146, 289, 188])\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma 白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 52, 1911, 108])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1025, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[700, 140, 828, 191])\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nLini Semen\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[294, 528, 673, 989])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[698, 528, 1107, 980])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1128, 527, 1690, 987])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[293, 1005, 675, 1421])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[688, 1002, 1047, 1424])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1060, 1003, 1341, 1425])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1359, 1003, 1690, 1424])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 1441, 802, 1910])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[828, 1445, 1246, 1906])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1273, 1442, 1690, 1906])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"16","text":"1. Pigment cells 2. Pigment masses 3. Epidermal mucilage cells of testa\n\n4. Sclerenchymatous cells (4-1 walls thickened, 4-2 walls slightly thickened)\n\n5. Hypodermal cells 6. Endosperm cells 7. Cotyledon cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"16","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of linoleic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\n$ \\alpha $ - Linolenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of acetone, glacial acetic acid and dichloromethane (5:4:2, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Add slowly 5 mL of sulphuric acid to 95 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of n-hexane. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture in a water bath at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of isopropanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC RP-18 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linoleic acid standard solution (3.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid standard solution (2.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[46, 41, 236, 122])\n\n毛冬青 Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1702, 148, 1942, 221])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[443, 365, 975, 771])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[459, 835, 980, 1173])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[477, 1231, 1074, 1731])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) linoleic acid (ii) $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid and (iii) secoisolariciresinol 1,4-diglucoside\n\n</div>\n\n了哥王 Wikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[672, 43, 819, 118])\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum片蘴黄\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 375, 878, 1347])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Lini Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. $ \\alpha $ - Linolenic acid standard solution 2. Linoleic acid standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of linoleic acid and $ \\alpha $ - linolenic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of linoleic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol.\n\n$ \\alpha $ - Linolenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of n-hexane. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture in a water bath at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 h. Centrifuge at\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 36, 1949, 255])\n\nabout $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in isopropanol. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL of volumetric flask and make up to the mark with isopropanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with OS bonded silica gel (3.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 40^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.6 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile:Isopropanol(9:1,v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>80→25</td><td>20→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>25</td><td>75</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-18</td><td>25→0</td><td>75→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>18-40</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linoleic acid Std-FP and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of linoleic acid and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of linoleic acid and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from linoleic acid and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid peaks should not be less than 32000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject linoleic acid Std-FP, $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of linoleic acid and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid peaks in the chromatograms of linoleic acid Std-FP, $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the\n\ntest solution. Identify linoleic acid and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of linoleic acid Std-FP and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid Std-FP. The retention times of linoleic acid and $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Lini Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Lini Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,α-linolenic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(linoleic acid)</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.77</td><td>±0.08</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.87</td><td>±0.08</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.99</td><td>±0.07</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>3.16</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[337, 1436, 1819, 1984])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lini Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[49, 45, 234, 123])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[926, 89, 1025, 138])"},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"16","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 8.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lini-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lini Semen","la":"Lini Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/litseae-fructus-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Litseae Fructus v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[655, 917, 963, 1103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1138, 924, 1322, 1100])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC 2mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[590, 1225, 1453, 1854])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Litseae Fructus A. Litseae Fructus B. Magnified image of ripe fruit C. Magnified image of seed with endocarp\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[655, 917, 963, 1103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1138, 924, 1322, 1100])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC 2mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[590, 1225, 1453, 1854])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Litseae Fructus A. Litseae Fructus B. Magnified image of ripe fruit C. Magnified image of seed with endocarp\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 18, 1873, 260])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Litseae Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 華澄茄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Bichengqie"},{"name":"11","text":"Litseae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Litsea cubeba (Lour.) Pers. (Lauraceae). The fruit is collected in autumn when the fruit is ripe, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Litseae Fructus."},{"name":"11","text":"Subspherical, 3-7 mm in diameter; externally brown to blackish-brown, with reticulated wrinkles. Occasionally the base bears a fine fruit stalk. A hard fragile kernel visible when the pericarp removed. Seed 1, cotyledons 2, yellowish-brown, oily. Odour fragrant; taste slightly pungent and bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with thick cuticle. Mesocarp broad, cells subelliptical. Numerous oil cells mainly scattered on the outer side of the mesocarp; stone cells scattered singly or in groups on the inner side of the mesocarp. Endocarp narrow, consists of 4-6 layers of fusiform stone cells, palisade-like; prisms of calcium oxalate scattered in endocarp. Testa consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells. Degenerated endosperm consists of 1 layer of cells. Cotyledon 2, occupying the major portion of the transverse section, composed of subrounded cells (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 5, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour brown. Stone cells of mesocarp scattered singly or in groups, rectangular to subrounded, 10-112 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls thickened, lumens small, pits and pit canals distinct. Oil cells numerous, elliptic or rounded, 20-122 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, containing yellowish-brown oil droplets. Stone cells of endocarp yellow, polygonal in top view, fusiform or arranged in palisade-like pattern in lateral view, lumens narrow and small. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, minute, 1-15 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 429, 1745, 2083])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Litseae Fructus\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells of mesocarp D. Stone cells of endocarp E. Endosperm\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Oil cells 3. Mesocarp 4. Stone cells of mesocarp 5. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n6. Endocarp 7. Testa 8. Endosperm 9. Cotyledon\n\nCirsii Japonici Herba\n\n大薊\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\nFritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix Polyporus 豬苓\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nLitseae Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[311, 447, 978, 937])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[996, 446, 1670, 933])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[312, 960, 981, 1453])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[995, 960, 1672, 1454])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[316, 1486, 977, 1982])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[995, 1484, 1673, 1982])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Stone cell of mesocarp 2. Oil cell\n\n3. Stone cells of endocarp (3-1 in top view, 3-2 in lateral view)\n\n4. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 25, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of linoleic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (7:2:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the solution. Make appropriate dilution where necessary."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linoleic acid standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 2-4 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[377, 2028, 945, 2457])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of linoleic acid\n\n</div>\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nLitseae Fructus\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[385, 376, 891, 1297])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Litseae Fructus extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Linoleic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of linoleic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of linoleic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 14, 1892, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (200 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.01% Acetic acid in acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.01% Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-42</td><td>30→90</td><td>70→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>42-60</td><td>90</td><td>10</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linoleic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of linoleic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of linoleic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from linoleic acid peak should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject linoleic acid Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of linoleic acid peak in the chromatogram of linoleic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify linoleic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of linoleic acid Std-FP. The retention times of linoleic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Litseae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 9, 1949, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Litseae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, linoleic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[383, 890, 1712, 1514])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Litseae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[31, 24, 1874, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 7.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/litseae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Litseae Fructus","la":"Litseae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lobeliae-chinensis-herba-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lobeliae Chinensis Herba v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[572, 924, 1082, 1784])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1124, 1021, 1412, 1698])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba A. Lobeliae Chinensis Herba B. Leaves and flower on an intact stem"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[572, 924, 1082, 1784])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1124, 1021, 1412, 1698])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba A. Lobeliae Chinensis Herba B. Leaves and flower on an intact stem"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Lobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\nChinese Name: 半邊蓮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Banbianlian"},{"name":"14","text":"Lobeliae Chinensis Herba is the dried herb of Lobelia chinensis Lour. (Campanulaceae). The herb is collected in the summer, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Lobeliae Chinensis Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Usually wound up into masses. Rhizomes short, cylindrical, 1-2 mm in diameter, externally pale brown or pale brownish-yellow, longitudinally fine. Roots slender, yellow, bearing lateral rootlets. Stem slender, branched, greyish-green, with obvious nodes, some bearing fibrous roots. Leaves alternate, greenish-brown, sessile, lamina mostly crumpled, when whole, narrowly lanceolate to long-ovate, 0.8-3.2 cm long, 0.2-0.5 cm wide. Pedicels slender, flower small, solitary and axillary, corolla tubular at the base, 5-lobed on the upper part, oblique on one side. Odour slight, taste slightly sweet and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Root: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle. Cortex broad, parenchymatous cells irregular. Endodermis distinct, cells rectangular or square, casparian strips visible. Vascular bundle small. Pericycle consists of 1 layer of parenchymatous cells. Laticiferous tubes scattered in the phloem. Phloem bundles arranged alternatively with xylem bundles. Xylem bundles 3-5 [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 33, 1955, 256])\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle, cells square or subsquare walls thickened. Cortex broad, parenchymatous cells polygonal and sparsely arranged. Crystals of calcium oxalate occasionally found in the cortex. Endodermis distinct, cells rectangular or square, casparian strips visible. Vascular bundle small. Phloem arranged in a ring, scattered with laticiferous tubes. Xylem radially arranged. Pith located in the central [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis each consists of 1 layer of subsquare cells, tangentially elongated, covered with cuticle. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cells, across the upper part of the midrib. Spongy tissue cells subrounded. Vascular bundle small, collateral, irregular in shape. Unicellular non-glandular hairs occasionally arise from the lower epidermis [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greenish-yellow to brownish-yellow. Epidermal cells of leaf with sinuous anticlinal walls. Epidermal cells of stem irregularly rectangular. Stomata found in the surface of leaf and stem, anomocytic, subsidiary cells 3-7. Laticiferous tubes occur alongside vessels, containing granular and oily contents. Parenchymatous cells contain inulin, inulin fan-shaped or irregular in shape; green under the polarized microscope. Crystals of calcium oxalate frequently occur beside vessels, sometimes arranged in a row; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Spiral vessels and reticulate vessels visible, 6-67 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Non-glandular hairs occasionally visible, occurring in two types: first type slender, consisting of 1-3 cells, walls slightly thickened; second type short conical, unicellular, with warty walls on the surface (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n三棱\n\n天葵子\n\nSemiaquilogiae Radix\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[221, 444, 1703, 2138])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of the transverse section of root of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Pericycle 5. Phloem\n\n6. Laticiferous tube 7. Xylem 8. Casparian strip\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1025, 137])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[236, 449, 1731, 2131])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of the transverse section of stem of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Laticiferous tube\n\n6. Xylem 7. Pith 8. Casparian strip\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[239, 452, 1671, 2129])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of the transverse section of leaf of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Unicellular non-glandular hair 5. Vascular bundle 6. Lower epidermis\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[925, 87, 1026, 137])\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膪草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連銭草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[250, 610, 1704, 2115])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Lobeilae Chinensis Herba\n\n1. Epidermal cells and stomata of leaf 2. Epidermal cells and stoma of stem\n\n3. Laticiferous tube ( $ \\rightarrow $ ） 4. Inulins 5. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 spiral vessel, 6-2 reticulate vessels)\n\n7. Non-glandular hairs (7-1 slender shape, 7-2 short conical)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"14","text":"Diosmin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of diosmin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 0.02 mL of the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol.\n\nLinarin (buddleoside) standard solution\n\nWeigh 8.0 mg of linarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 0.01 mL of the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (8:1:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately diosmin standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), linarin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[924, 86, 1028, 138])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[375, 365, 1295, 687])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[451, 749, 1302, 1058])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) diosmin and (ii) linarin (buddleoside)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[376, 1233, 903, 2042])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Diosmin standard solution 2. Linarin standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of diosmin and linarin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Diosmin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (80 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 8.0 mg of diosmin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 0.2 mL of the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol.\n\nLinarin (buddleoside) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (18 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.8 mg of linarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 0.2 mL of the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (267 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>83</td><td>17</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>83→70</td><td>17→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of diosmin Std-FP and linarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of diosmin and linarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of diosmin and linarin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from diosmin and linarin peaks should not be less than 10000 and 300000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])"},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject diosmin Std-FP, linarin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of diosmin and linarin peaks in the chromatograms of diosmin Std-FP, linarin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify diosmin and linarin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of diosmin Std-FP and linarin Std-FP. The retention times of diosmin and linarin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(diosmin)</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.07</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, linarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[339, 1607, 1819, 2135])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lobeliae Chinensis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[46, 31, 1960, 266])"},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): The CMM shall meet the requirements for arsenic and mercury as specified in Appendix V. For cadmium and lead, Lobeliae Chinensis Herba should meet the specified limits of not more than 3.0 mg/kg and 12.0 mg/kg, respectively, when the CMM will be processed as a decoction in the final consumption form; otherwise, the limits for cadmium and lead specified in Appendix V shall be applied.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 14.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 6.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 40.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lobeliae-chinensis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lobeliae Chinensis Herba","la":"Lobeliae Chinensis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lonicerae-flos-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lonicerae Flos v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[3, 764, 1967, 1991])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried flower bud and opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.\n\nA. Lonicerae Flos B. Flower buds C. Magnified image of dissected flower (spread after soaking)\n\nD. Magnified image of corolla tube (surface view) E. Magnified image of corolla tube (partial view)\n\nF. Magnified image of calyx (surface view) G. Magnified image of calyx tooth (partial view)\n\n洋金花 Daturae Flos\n\nTinosporae Radix\n\n金果欖\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[341, 539, 1620, 1788])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried flower bud and opening flower of Lonicera hypoglauca Miq.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Lonicerae Flos B. Flower buds C. Magnified image of dissected flower (spread after soaking)\n\nD. Magnified image of corolla tube (surface view) E. Magnified image of corolla tube (partial view)\n\nF. Magnified image of calyx (surface view) G. Magnified image of calyx tooth (partial view)\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 38, 1937, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"20","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[3, 764, 1967, 1991])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried flower bud and opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.\n\nA. Lonicerae Flos B. Flower buds C. Magnified image of dissected flower (spread after soaking)\n\nD. Magnified image of corolla tube (surface view) E. Magnified image of corolla tube (partial view)\n\nF. Magnified image of calyx (surface view) G. Magnified image of calyx tooth (partial view)\n\n洋金花 Daturae Flos\n\nTinosporae Radix\n\n金果欖\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[341, 539, 1620, 1788])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried flower bud and opening flower of Lonicera hypoglauca Miq.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Lonicerae Flos B. Flower buds C. Magnified image of dissected flower (spread after soaking)\n\nD. Magnified image of corolla tube (surface view) E. Magnified image of corolla tube (partial view)\n\nF. Magnified image of calyx (surface view) G. Magnified image of calyx tooth (partial view)\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 38, 1937, 262])"},{"name":"20","text":"Official Name: Lonicerae Flos\n\nChinese Name: 山銀花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shanyinhua"},{"name":"20","text":"Lonicerae Flos is the dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz. or Lonicera hypoglauca Miq. (Caprifoliaceae). The flower bud is collected in early summer before anthesis, then dried to obtain Lonicerae Flos."},{"name":"20","text":"Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.: Flower bud clavate, slightly curved, 1.5-5.4 cm long upper part 1.5-3 mm in diameter, lower part 0.5-1.5 mm in diameter. Externally greenish-brown to yellowish-white. Corolla surface densely covered with yellowish-white bristles, occasionally scattered with pale yellow to orange-yellow glandular dots; calyx tube surface almost glabrous, apex 5-lobed, lobes long-triangular, sparsely pubescent, ciliate at the edges. Corolla of opening flower slightly tubular, bilabiate at apex, upper lip 4-lobed, lower lip entire, the length of lips shorter than half length of the corolla. Stamens 5, pistil 1, style glabrous. Texture slightly hard and springy. Odour delicately aromatic; taste slightly bitter and sweet [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nLonicera hypoglauca Miq.: Flower bud 1.5-4.5 cm long, upper part 1.3-3 mm in diameter, lower part 0.5-1.5 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to dark brown. Corolla surface glabrous or sparsely pubescent, with scattered orange-yellow glandular spots; calyx tube and tooth surfaces almost glabrous, ciliate at the edges. Lips in opening flowers have the same length as or slightly longer than the corolla tube [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[60, 16, 1940, 265])"},{"name":"20","text":"Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.: Epidermis of corolla extremely thin, the cells subrectangular. Glandular hairs few, thick-walled non-glandular hairs relatively numerous on the epidermis. Head of glandular hairs mostly disk-shaped or inverted conical, top flat or slightly retuse, 5- to 16-celled in the lateral view, arranged in 1-3 layers, 37-125 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, cells contain pale brown to yellowish-brown contents; stalk of glandular hairs 3- to 5-celled, 2(3-) cells always parallel arranged at the junction with the head, 30-168 $ \\mu $ m long, 12-25 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Thick-walled non-glandular hairs unicellular, horn-shaped, 15-347 $ \\mu $ m long, with few warty protuberances on the surface, spiral striations occasionally visible, lumen of the cells of short horn-shaped hairs relatively narrower compared with those of the long hairs; base slightly enlarged, 8-35 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Pollen grains usually adhered to the surface, yellow, subrounded to rounded-triangular. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found, mainly scattered in lower layer of epidermal cells of corolla [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nLonicera hypoglauca Miq.: Epidermis of corolla with few glandular hairs, sparse thick-walled non-glandular hairs sparse, or nearly glabrous. Head of glandular hairs shield-like and large, mainly 13- to 20-celled in top view, 8- to 18-celled in lateral view, arranged in 1-2 layers, 71-150 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, containing reddish-brown, brown or pale brown contents; stalk of glandular hairs 1- to 3-celled, extremely short, 15-60 $ \\mu $ m long, 10-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Thick-walled non-glandular hair spouse of nearly glabrous, unicellular, 35-575 $ \\mu $ m long, with small and dense warty protuberance on the surface, base 15-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.: Colour yellowish-green to pale yellow. Pollen grains relatively numerous, yellow, rounded-triangular or subrounded, 50-87 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with fine granular sculptures visible on the surface of exine, with 3 furrows. Non-glandular hairs 2 types, first type thick walled non-glandular hairs on outer surface of corolla, unicellular, short horn-shaped, 8-35 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, usually broken, with few warty protuberances on the surface; second type thin-walled non-glandular hairs on inner surface of corolla, long and curved, 15-25 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, usually fragmented, with few fine warty protuberances on the surface. Clusters of calcium oxalate relatively abundant, scattered in parenchymatous cells, 10-30 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Spiral vessels frequently found, 5-10 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fibrous layer of anther reticulate, with thickened walls. Glandular hairs occasionally found, with disk-shaped or inverted conical heads [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[30, 13, 1943, 261])\n\nLonicera hypoglauca Miq.: Colour pale yellow. Pollen grains relatively numerous, rounded triangular or subrounded, 50-90 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Thick-walled non-glandular hairs on outer surface of corolla straight, 15-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter, usually broken, with small and dense warty protuberances on the surface; non-glandular hairs on inner surface of corolla 15-30 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate 12-30 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Spiral vessels 5-10 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Glandular hairs occasionally found, with shieldlike and large heads [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 449, 934, 987])\n\nA-1\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1015, 447, 1592, 978])\n\nA-2\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[401, 1119, 927, 1650])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1017, 1120, 1591, 1649])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[401, 1691, 722, 2013])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[807, 1691, 1137, 2010])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1226, 1691, 1597, 2015])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of corolla surface of dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.\n\n</div>\n\nA-1. Corolla surface A-2. Epidermal cells B. Glandular hairs C. Non-glandular hairs D. Pollen grain E. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Pollen grain E. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) F. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[310, 446, 939, 977])\n\nA-1\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1062, 452, 1675, 967])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nA-2\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[311, 1083, 794, 1538])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[865, 1084, 1679, 1542])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[306, 1605, 659, 1972])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[748, 1601, 1163, 1971])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1248, 1601, 1674, 1973])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of corolla surface of dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera hypoglauca Miq.\n\nA-1. Corolla surface A-2. Epidermal cells B. Glandular hair C. Non-glandular hair\n\nD. Pollen grain E. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nF. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[796, 74, 893, 118])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[735, 185, 854, 229])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[333, 447, 1000, 793])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1064, 446, 1640, 787])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[334, 866, 1003, 1194])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1062, 864, 1640, 1193])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[331, 1270, 534, 1612])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[573, 1269, 1020, 1613])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1061, 1267, 1641, 1614])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[333, 1682, 840, 2012])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[862, 1682, 1392, 2018])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Pollen grains 2. Non-glandular hairs (2-1 thick-walled, 2-2 thin-walled)\n\n3. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4. Vessels 5. Fibrous layer of anther\n\n6. Glandular hairs (6-1 in lateral view, 6-2 in top view)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nSaussureae Involucratae Herba\n\n天山雪莲\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\n白花丹\n\naginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\nPlumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[328, 447, 985, 772])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1059, 443, 1660, 767])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[328, 847, 985, 1158])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1058, 847, 16"},{"name":"20","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"20","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butyl acetate, formic acid and water (7:2.5:2.5, v/v). Shake well and use the upper layer."},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"20","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[445, 371, 1002, 715])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[394, 769, 1703, 2334])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid (ii) dipsacoside B and (iii) macranthoidin B\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[1267, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[379, 376, 981, 1133])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2(i) 2(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Lonicerae Flos extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.\n\n(ii) dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera hypoglauca Miq.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of chlorogenic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"20","text":"Dipsacoside B standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dipsacoside B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nMacranthoidin B standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of macranthoidin B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"20","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butyl acetate, methanol and formic acid (10:10:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"20","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[38, 37, 1939, 262])"},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"20","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dipsacoside B standard solution, macranthoidin B standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 110^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[378, 1337, 1320, 2132])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3(i) 3(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Lonicerae Flos extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Macranthoidin B standard solution\n\n2. Dipsacoside B standard solution\n\n3. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz.\n\n(ii) dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera hypoglauca Miq.\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[71, 30, 1906, 263])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of dipsacoside B and macranthoidin B (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"20","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nDipsacoside B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of dipsacoside B CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\nMacranthoidin B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of macranthoidin B CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 40 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with 7 mL of methanol (50%) and vortex the mixture for 1 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"20","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ）flow: 2.0 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>88.5→85</td><td>11.5→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-12</td><td>85→71</td><td>15→29</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>12-18</td><td>71→67</td><td>29→33</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>18-30</td><td>67→55</td><td>33→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"20","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, dipsacoside B Std-FP and macranthoidin B Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid, dipsacoside B and macranthoidin B should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid, dipsacoside B and macranthoidin B peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid, dipsacoside B and macranthoidin B peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 7 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, dipsacoside B Std-FP, macranthoidin B Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid, dipsacoside B and macranthoidin B peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, dipsacoside B Std-FP, macranthoidin B Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 7 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid, dipsacoside B and macranthoidin B peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, dipsacoside B Std-FP, macranthoidin B Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid, dipsacoside B and macranthoidin B peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lonicerae Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lonicerae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.56</td><td>$\\pm$0.05</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.89</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, macranthoidin B)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(dipsacoside B)</td><td>1.10</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[341, 773, 1795, 1200])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 7 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera macranthoides Hand.-Mazz. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[340, 1375, 1795, 1811])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 7 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried flower bud or with opening flower of Lonicera hypoglauca Miq. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 7 (i) or (ii)]."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lonicerae-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lonicerae Flos","la":"Lonicerae Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lonicerae-japonicae-caulis-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[442, 838, 1550, 1934])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis A. Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis B. Young branch with node and pubescence C. Transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 10, 1900, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[442, 838, 1550, 1934])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis A. Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis B. Young branch with node and pubescence C. Transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 10, 1900, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 忍冬藤\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Rendongteng"},{"name":"13","text":"Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis is the dried stem and branch of Lonicera japonica Thunb. (Caprifoliaceae). The stem and branch are collected in autumn and winter, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis."},{"name":"13","text":"Long-cylindrical, 1-7 mm in diameter. Externally brownish-red or pale grey, glabrous or pubescent; outer bark easily fallen off, appearing yellowish-white. Branches nodose, with leaf scars. Texture brittle. Fracture uneven, yellowish-white, hollow. Odour slight; taste of older branches slightly bitter, and the younger ones weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Young branch: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of flat cells, containing pigments. Cortex consists of several layers of compressed cells on the outer side, followed by 1-2 layers of cortex fibres on the inner side. Cork consists of several layers of subpolygonal cells. Phloem narrow, with some cells of the phloem rays contain clusters of calcium oxalate. Cambium in a ring. In xylem, the vessels usually scattered singly or in groups; xylem rays consist of 1-2 rows of cells. Pith with a hollow centre, the pith cells lignified (Fig. 2).\n\nOlder stem: Cork lines the outermost layer of the older stem. Phloem fibres often in bundles, with a fairly thick wall. Phloem rays distinct (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[69, 6, 1973, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale reddish-brown. Clusters of calcium oxalate usually found in a row in the parenchymatous cells, 5-28 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, 40-374 $ \\mu $m long (occasionally up to 907 $ \\mu $m), 6-32 $ \\mu $m in diameter (occasionally up to 46 $ \\mu $m), usually broken, wall thick, basal part slightly swollen; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells brownish-yellow, subpolygonal, often with scars of non-glandular hairs that resemble stone cells. Cork cells subrectangular or polygonal. Cortex fibres 14-98 $ \\mu $m in diameter (occasionally up to 122 $ \\mu $m), with straight wall and large lumen; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Phloem fibres 8-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall extremely thickened, pits and pit canals indistinct; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres 7-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter, pits distinct; bright white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly spiral and reticulate, 5-75 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[29, 5, 1898, 264])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 430, 1699, 2050])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis A. Sketch of young branch B. Section illustration of young branch C. Clusters of calcium oxalate in phloem D. Sketch of older stem E. Section illustration of older stem F. Clusters of calcium oxalate and phloem fibres\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Cortex fibre 5. Cork\n\n6. Phloem 7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Cambium 9. Xylem 10. Pith\n\n11. Xylem ray 12. Phloem fibre 13. Phloem ray\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizon\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nLonicerae Japonicae Caulis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[284, 449, 1703, 2039])"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Non-glandular hair\n\n3. Epidermal cells with scar of non-glandular hair 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Cortex fibre 6. Phloem fibre 7. Xylem fibre\n\n8. Vessels (8-1 spiral vessel, 8-2 reticulate vessels)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 6, 1903, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Loganin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of loganin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol and water (10:1.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately loganin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of loganin.\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\nLonicerae Japonicae Caulis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 367, 1007, 762])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[396, 832, 914, 1372])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid and (ii) loganin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nLoganin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of loganin CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 45 mL of methanol (50%). Reflux the mixture for 40 min. Cool down to room temperature. Centrifuge at about $ 3 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[36, 6, 1906, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (236 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-6</td><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>6-7</td><td>88→87</td><td>12→13</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>7-9</td><td>87→86</td><td>13→14</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>9-35</td><td>86</td><td>14</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and loganin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid and loganin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid and loganin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid and loganin peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, loganin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid and loganin peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, loganin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid and loganin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and loganin Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid and loganin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 12, 1960, 261])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.46</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,loganin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[293, 1159, 1841, 1778])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[37, 18, 1903, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lonicerae-japonicae-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis","la":"Lonicerae Japonicae Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lonicerae-japonicae-flos-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lonicerae Japonicae Flos v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[420, 839, 1495, 1962])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lonicerae Japonicae Flos A. Lonicerae Japonicae Flos B. Magnified image of a flower bud C. Magnified image of flower\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[420, 839, 1495, 1962])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lonicerae Japonicae Flos A. Lonicerae Japonicae Flos B. Magnified image of a flower bud C. Magnified image of flower\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Lonicerae Japonicae Flos\n\nChinese Name: 金銀花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jinyinhua"},{"name":"13","text":"Lonicerae Japonicae Flos is the dried flower bud of Lonicera japonica Thunb. (Caprifoliaceae). The flower bud is collected in early summer before flowering, then dried under the sun to obtain Lonicerae Japonicae Flos."},{"name":"13","text":"Clavate, stout in upper part and tapered downwards, slightly curved, 2-3 cm long, about 3 mm in diameter on upper part and 1.5 mm in diameter on lower part. Externally yellowish-white or greenishwhite, gradually darken on keeping, densely covered with pubescence. Foliaceous bracts occasionally visible. Calyx green, 5-lobed at the apex, lobes pubescent, about 2 mm long. Corolla tubular when open, apex 2-lipped; stamens 5, epipetalous, yellow; pistil 1, ovary glabrous. Odour delicately fragrant; taste bland, slightly bitter and sweet (Fig.1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale green to pale yellowish-green. Non-glandular hairs numerous, occurring in two types: one type thick-walled non-glandular hairs, extremely numerous, unicellular, bicellular rare, straight or slightly curved, 45-900 $ \\mu $m long, 14-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls 5-10 $ \\mu $m thick; another type thin-walled non-grandular hairs, unicellular, extremely long, bent or wrinkled, 11-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter, surface with fine warty protrusions. Glandular hairs numerous, occuring in two types: one type with an inverted conical head, apex flat, consisting of 10-33 cells, arranged in 2-4 layers in lateral view, 48-108 $ \\mu $m in diameter, some cells contain pale yellow\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 4, 1944, 258])\n\ncontents; stalk 1- to 5-celled, 70-250 $ \\mu $m long, 15-58 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with thin walls and indistinct cuticle striations; another type with subrounded or slightly oblate head, consisting of 6-20 cells, 20-80 $ \\mu $m in diameter; stalk 2- to 4-celled, 24-80 $ \\mu $m long, 13-32 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Pollen grains extremely numerous, yellow, subglobular or rounded-triangular, 60-92 $ \\mu $m in diameter, exine with fine and dense short spiny and granular sculptures, 3 germinal pores. Anther cells subpolygonal or irregular in shape, walls beaded, with slightly curved or straight striations. Vessels mostly in groups, mainly spiral, relatively small, 10-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered or occurred in parenchymatous cells, 6-45 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with sharp angles; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 19, 1898, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 407, 1745, 2127])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Lonicerae Japonicae Flos\n\n1. Non-glandular hairs (1-1 thick-walled non-glandular hairs, 1-2 thin-walled non-glandular hairs)\n\n2. Glandular hair (2-1 inverted conical head, 2-2 subrounded or slightly oblate head)\n\n3. Pollen grains 4. Anther cells 5. Vessels 6. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[41, 14, 1945, 256])"},{"name":"13","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nLuteolin-7-O-glucoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of luteolin-7-O-glucoside CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (20:3:1.5:1.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2 g of ferric trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution, luteolin-7-O-glucoside standard solution and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 11, 1891, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[444, 370, 969, 697])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[443, 767, 1065, 1053])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid and (ii) luteolin-7-O-glucoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[385, 1211, 904, 2041])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Lonicerae Japonicae Flos extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chlorogenic acid standard solution 2. Luteolin-7-O-glucoside standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chlorogenic acid and luteolin-7-O-glucoside (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 14, 1944, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nLuteolin-7-O-glucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of luteolin-7-O-glucoside CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about 3000 x g for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (350 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with phenyl modified ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>92</td><td>8</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-15</td><td>92→80</td><td>8→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-35</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-45</td><td>80→70</td><td>20→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>70→92</td><td>30→8</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and luteolin-7-O-glucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid and luteolin-7-O-glucoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid and luteolin-7-O-glucoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid and luteolin-7-O-glucoside peaks should not be less than 30000 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[42, 17, 1868, 265])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, luteolin-7-O-glucoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid and luteolin-7-O-glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, luteolin-7-O-glucoside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid and luteolin-7-O-glucoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and luteolin-7-O-glucoside Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid and luteolin-7-O-glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Lonicerae Japonicae Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Lonicerae Japonicae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(chlorogenic acid)</td><td>0.62</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,luteolin-7-O-glucoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.27</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.49</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 8, 1945, 257])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[385, 364, 1822, 1196])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lonicerae Japonicae Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[31, 21, 1874, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 37.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 32.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"13","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nChlorogenic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the chlorogenic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 20, 40, 60, 200, 400 mg/L for chlorogenic acid."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh accurately 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about 3500 x g for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Pipette 10 mL of the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lonicerae-japonicae-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lonicerae Japonicae Flos","la":"Lonicerae Japonicae Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lophatheri-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lophatheri Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[33, 8, 1901, 264])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[33, 8, 1901, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Lophatheri Herba\n\nChinese Name: 淡竹葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Danzhuye"},{"name":"13","text":"Lophatheri Herba is the dried stem and leaf of Lophatherum gracile Brongn. (Poaceae). The stem and leaf are collected before the rachis emerges in summer, dried under the sun, then tied up in small bundles to obtain Lophatheri Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Herb 13-68.9 cm long. Stems cylindrical, nodiferous, externally yellowish-green, fracture hollow. Leaf sheaths dehiscent. Lamina lanceolate, some crumpled and rolled, 5.6-25.5 cm long, 0.8-3.8 cm wide, externally pale green or yellowish-green; veins parallel, bearing lateral veinlets, forming rectangular reticulate venation, more distinct at the lower surfaces. Texture pliable and light in weight. Odour slight; taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of small, round-rectangular densely set cells, with outer thickened wall, laminated; small and short unicellular non-glandular cells, stomata and cuticles present on the surface. Vascular bundles collateral, surrounded by a sheath of fibres, arranged in an interrupted ring beneath epidermis. Further inside, larger vascular bundles embedded in the ground parenchyma arranged in a second interrupted ring. Pith always hollow and broken [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 9, 1974, 260])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermal cells vary in size; the cells located above the mesophyll tissue between veins relative large, the cells located on the vein or above mechanical tissue relative minute. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of short, cylindrical cells. Spongy tissue consists of 2-4 layers of cells. Vessels sparse, arranged in a V-shaped. Phloem located beneath xylem, with 2-3 layers of fibres between the phloem and xylem. Cells of lower epidermis rectangular, relatively small, arranged in order, covered with thick cuticle. Layer of fibre bundles present on the inner side of the upper and lower epidermis of the vein [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale greyish-green. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, long or short conical. Lower epidermal cells of sheath subrectangular in surface view, anticlinal walls undulate, beaded, lignified, long and short cells arranged alternately. Upper epidermal cells of leaf rectangular or subsquare, anticlinal wall thin, sinuous. Long and short cells of lower epidermis of leaf arranged alternately or several connected, long cell rectangular, anticlinal wall sinuous, short cell is silica cell, dumbbell-shaped arranged along the veins; stomata mainly presented in lower epidermis of leaf, numerous, guard cells dumbbell-shaped, subsidiary cells nearly round-triangular. Epidermal cells of stem long subrectangular, stomata occasionally found (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[40, 100, 202, 156])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[244, 421, 1745, 1794])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Lophatheri Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Vascular bundles 3. Fibre layer 4. Pith (hollow) 5. Cortex parenchyma\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Lophatheri Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 402, 1739, 1797])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Lophatheri Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Lower epidermis\n\n5. Xylem 6.Phloem 7. Layer of fibre bundle\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[244, 457, 1700, 1694])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Lophatheri Herba (under the light microscope)\n\n1. Non-glandular hairs (1-1 long unicellular, 1-2 short unicellular, 1-3 short unicellular in surface view,\n\n3. Upper epidermal cells of leaf 4. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with stomata\n\n1-4 short unicellular) 2. Lower epidermal cells of sheath\n\n5. Epidermal cells of stem with stomata\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[68, 13, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Isoorientin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.9 mg of isoorientin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (5:0.8:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 10 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of water. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol (70%). Make appropriate dilution where necessary."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately isoorientin standard solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 5 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent for 30 s and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[36, 5, 1902, 264])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of isoorientin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 524, 983, 890])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of isoorientin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Isoorientin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of isoorientin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (30%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of ethanol (30%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (360 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 5, 1964, 263])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>1% Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>5</td><td>95</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>5→13</td><td>95→87</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>13</td><td>87</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-37</td><td>13→16</td><td>87→84</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>37-60</td><td>16</td><td>84</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of isoorientin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of isoorientin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of isoorientin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from isoorientin peak should not be less than 14000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject isoorientin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of isoorientin peak in the chromatogram of isoorientin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify isoorientin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of isoorientin Std-FP. The retention times of isoorientin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lophatheri Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[28, 7, 1905, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lophatheri Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, isoorientin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.36</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[298, 885, 1820, 1468])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lophatheri Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[66, 15, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 11.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 8.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lophatheri-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lophatheri Herba","la":"Lophatheri Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lycii-fructus-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lycii Fructus v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 856, 1351, 1514])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[583, 1551, 828, 1733])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[876, 1560, 1029, 1718])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1043, 1559, 1193, 1721])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n2 mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1238, 1560, 1408, 1717])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 mm\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[638, 856, 1351, 1514])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[583, 1551, 828, 1733])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[876, 1560, 1029, 1718])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1043, 1559, 1193, 1721])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n2 mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1238, 1560, 1408, 1717])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 mm\n\n</div>"},{"name":"16","text":"A. Lycii Fructus\n\nB. Magnified image of cut surface\n\nC. Magnified image of base showing white fruit stalk scar\n\nD. Magnified image of apex showing protrudent style scar\n\nE. Magnified image of seeds"},{"name":"16","text":"Official name: Lycii Fructus\n\nChinese name: 枸杞子\n\nChinese phonetic name: Gouqizi"},{"name":"16","text":"Lycii Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Lycium barbarum L. (Solanaceae). The fruit is collected in summer and autumn when the fruit turns red, dried in hot and sunny days and separated from the fruit stalk; or dried in the shade until the exocarp shrinks, then dried under the sun until the exocarp hardens and then separated from fruit stalk to obtain Lycii Fructus."},{"name":"16","text":"Subfusiform or ellipsoid, slightly flat, 7.4-25.5 mm in length, 2.5-10.5 mm in diameter. Externally bright red to red, slightly lustrous with irregular wrinkles, apex slightly acute with a protrudent style scar, and a white or pale yellow fruit stalk scar at the base. Pericarp pliable and shrunken; mesocarp fleshy, soft, moist and viscous. Seeds 10-65, round or subreniform, flat and bent upwards, 1.1-2.7 mm in length, 0.8-2.1 mm in width, pale yellow to yellow on surface. Odour slight; taste sweet, slightly sour (Fig.1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 10, 1973, 256])"},{"name":"16","text":"Fruit: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of flat sub-rectangular or rectangular cells, anticlinal walls thickened, slightly lignified, outer periclinal wall covered with cuticle layer, with fine dentation on outer surface. Mesocarp usually broken, consists of several rows of irregularly shaped parenchymatous cells, loosely-packed, cells closer to exocarp are relatively smaller, containing orange pigment particles and vascular bundles. Vascular bundles bicollateral, abundant, dispersed in mesocarp. Endocarp usually broken, consisting of 1 layer of oblong cells, thinner when compared with exocarp, slightly lignified. Phloem cells subpolygonal. Xylem consists of vessels with small diameter, located at the middle of two phloem strands [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nSeed: Outer epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of U-shaped stone cell, lateral and inner wall thickened. Inner epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of subrectangular cells, with inner and lateral wall thinkened, strongly lignified. Cotyledons 2, cells subrounded, filled with aleurone grains. Perisperm consists of 1 layer of flat rectangular parenchymatous cells. Endosperm cells polygonal, filled with aleurone grains. Radicle consists of subrounded parenchymatous cells, filled with aleurone grains [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Colour yellowish-orange. Parenchymatous cells of mesocarp subpolygonal, walls thin and indistinct, lumen containing orange-red or reddish-brown spherical granules, often found together with epidermal cells of exocarp. Epidermal cells of exocarp polygonal or elongated polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls straight or wave-curved, the outer periclinal walls with parallel cuticle striations, with some orange-red or reddish-brown spherical granules. Stone cells of testa yellowish or colourless, irregular polygonal in surface view, walls thickened, wave-curved, striations distinct; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Endosperm cells polygonal or irregular in shape, filled with aleurone grains and accompanied by yellowish-orange oil droplets of varying size. Cotyledon cells subsquare or subpolygonal, with yellowish-orange oil droplets. Spiral vessels usually in bundle and broken, 4-14 $ \\mu m $ in diameter [Fig. 3].\n\n明黛參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥 茯苓 Myrrha\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡 Storax\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[298, 769, 922, 1128])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[304, 1157, 923, 1736])\n\n20μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[958, 804, 1690, 1711])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of fruit of Lycii Fructus\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of vascular bundle\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Vascular bundle 4. Endocarp\n\n5. Phloem 6. Xylem\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸隶仁\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[243, 442, 1745, 2069])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Lycii Fructus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified section D. Endosperm containing aleurone grains\n\n1. Outer epidermis of testa 2. Inner epidermis of testa 3. Cotyledon\n\n4. Perisperm 5. Endosperm 6. Radicle 7. Aleurone grains\n\n明黛參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[306, 477, 978, 953])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[305, 983, 978, 1457])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1010, 479, 1676, 831])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1008, 864, 1675, 1160])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1007, 1176, 1676, 1461])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[307, 1530, 980, 2040])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1006, 1531, 1680, 2044])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"16","text":"1. Parenchymatous cells of mesocarp 2. Epidermal cells of exocarp\n\n3. Stone cells of testa\n\n4. Endosperm cells with oil droplets ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) and aleurone grains ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n5. Cotyledon cells with oil droplets ( $ \\rightarrow $ ） 6. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"16","text":"Rutin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of rutin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nScopoletin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of scopoletin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid, methanol and water (6:1:0.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 2 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Place the sample in freezer $ (-20^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}) $ at least 16 h and powder the sample. Weigh 1.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Centrifuge the mixture at about $ 2000\\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Evaporate the filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.1% formic acid. Transfer the extract to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with 0.1% formic acid. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately rutin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), scopoletin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 432, 721, 637])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[381, 863, 782, 1098])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1317, 1018, 1909])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) betaine (ii) scopoletin and (iii) rutin\n\n</div>\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\nPoha\n\n沒藥\n\n茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[377, 378, 848, 1209])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Lycii Fructus extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Rutin standard solution 2. Scopoletin standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of rutin and scopoletin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 17, 1960, 257])"},{"name":"16","text":"Rutin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rutin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nScopoletin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (2.5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of scopoletin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Pipette 125 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"16","text":"Place the sample in freezer $ (-20^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}) $ at least 16 h and powder the sample. Weigh 1.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Centrifuge the mixture at about $ 2000\\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Evaporate the filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.1% formic acid. Transfer the extract to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with 0.1% formic acid. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (350 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times 50 \\mathrm{~mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-2</td><td>95→92</td><td>5→8</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>2-7</td><td>92</td><td>8</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>7-15</td><td>92→80</td><td>8→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of rutin Std-FP and scopoletin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of rutin and scopoletin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of rutin and scopoletin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from rutin and scopoletin peaks should not be less than 90000 and 10000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject rutin Std-FP, scopoletin Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of rutin and scopoletin peaks in the chromatograms of rutin Std-FP, scopoletin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify rutin and scopoletin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of rutin Std-FP and scopoletin Std-FP. The retention times of rutin and scopoletin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lycii Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lycii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(scopoletin)</td><td>0.79</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,rutin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥 茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[332, 376, 1823, 865])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lycii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.8%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method (at $ 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ): not more than 10%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 60%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 55%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lycii-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lycii Fructus","la":"Lycii Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lycoridis-radiatae-bulbus-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[393, 826, 1304, 1883])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus\n\nA. Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus B. Magnified image of bulb C. Magnified lateral view of bulb"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[393, 826, 1304, 1883])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus\n\nA. Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus B. Magnified image of bulb C. Magnified lateral view of bulb"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus\n\nChinese Name: 石蒜\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shisuan"},{"name":"15","text":"Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus is the dried bulb of Lycoris radiata (L'Hérit.) Herb. (Amaryllidaceae). The bulb is collected in autumn, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus."},{"name":"15","text":"Ovoid to spherical, 3.4-5 cm long, 25-40 mm in diameter. Externally covered with 2-3 layers of dark brown dry membranous scales, easily fallen off. 10-20 layers of fleshy scales inside growing on the shortened stem plate, with yellowish-white bud in the centre. Odour characteristic, slightly irritating; taste very bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Transverse section of scale leaf\n\nEpidermis consists of 1 layer of small parenchymatous cells. Mesophyll consists of parenchymatous cells, filled with starch granules. Mucilage cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundle small, collateral, scattered in the mesophyll (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour pale brown. Starch granules rounded or polygonal, 20-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum slit-shaped or stellate; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Mucilage cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate, crystals 100-150 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels spiral and reticulate, 12-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Fibres in bundle; yellowish-brown under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[310, 564, 907, 897])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[336, 1005, 923, 1425])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[335, 1509, 927, 1959])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[951, 539, 1667, 1936])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"15","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Prisms of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Outer epidermis 2. Mesophyll 3. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Vascular bundle 5. Inner epidermis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[61, 24, 1964, 262])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[302, 446, 981, 955])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1003, 447, 1681, 951])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[304, 972, 981, 1474])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1002, 968, 1683, 1480])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[304, 1498, 985, 2005])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1000, 1501, 1339, 2006])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1348, 1503, 1682, 2004])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate in mucilage cell 3. Vessels 4. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 41, 1937, 263])"},{"name":"15","text":"Galantamine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of galantamine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nLycorine hydrochloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of lycorine hydrochloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), methanol, n-hexane and dichloromethane (0.2:1:2:7, v/v). Use the lower layer."},{"name":"15","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 4.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 6 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 30 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately galantamine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), lycorine hydrochloride standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapour chamber for about 20 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[374, 370, 778, 694])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[436, 772, 858, 1145])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) galantamine and (ii) lycorine hydrochloride\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[380, 1304, 921, 2111])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Galantamine standard solution 2. Lycorine hydrochloride standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[38, 37, 1937, 263])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of galantamine and lycorine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"0. 1 M hydrochloric acid solution\n\nPipette 8.33 mL of hydrochloric acid to a 1000-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"15","text":"Galantamine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of galantamine CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nLycorine hydrochloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (600 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of lycorine hydrochloride CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 3 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v). Allow to stand for 30 min. Add 40 mL of a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (4:1, v/v). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 1.5 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue with a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (4:1, v/v). Combine the solutions. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 6 mL of formic acid (2%, v/v). Pre-condition a Mixed-Mode cation-exchange (MCX) solid-phase extraction column (3 mL, 60 mg) with 3 mL of methanol, allow to stand for 10 min, then followed by 3 mL of water. Load the sample solution to the SPE column. Add 2 mL of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid solution followed by 2 mL of methanol to the pre-conditioned column and discard the eluant. Add 3 mL of a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and methanol (1:4, v/v) to the column. Collect the eluant and evaporate the solvent to dryness under nitrogen. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 1-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (289 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05%Diethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>92</td><td>8</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-12</td><td>92→87</td><td>8→13</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>12-30</td><td>87</td><td>13</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-50</td><td>87→80</td><td>13→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>60-70</td><td>80→68</td><td>20→32</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of galantamine Std-FP and lycorine hydrochloride Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of galantamine and lycorine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of galantamine and lycorine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from galantamine and lycorine peaks should not be less than 60000 and 15000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject galantamine Std-FP, lycorine hydrochloride Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of galantamine and lycorine peaks in the chromatograms of galantamine Std-FP, lycorine hydrochloride Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify galantamine and lycorine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of galantamine Std-FP and lycorine hydrochloride Std-FP. The retention times of galantamine and lycorine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, lycorine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.20</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.42</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>5(galantamine)</td><td>1.77</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[341, 886, 1803, 1798])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lycoridis-radiatae-bulbus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus","la":"Lycoridis Radiatae Bulbus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lysimachiae-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lysimachiae Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[667, 1265, 1348, 1834])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lysimachiae Herba A. Lysimachiae Herba B. Leaves C. Rootlets D. Leaf after soaking in water\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 8, 1900, 265])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"5mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[667, 1265, 1348, 1834])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Lysimachiae Herba A. Lysimachiae Herba B. Leaves C. Rootlets D. Leaf after soaking in water\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 8, 1900, 265])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Lysimachiae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 金錢草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jinqiancao"},{"name":"15","text":"Lysimachiae Herba is the dried herb of Lysimachia christinae Hance (Primulaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Lysimachiae Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Herb usually twisted into masses, glabrous or sparsely pubescent. Stem twisted, externally brown to dark brownish-red, with longitudinal striations; stem nodes of the lower part sometimes with rootlets, fracture solid. Leaves opposite, mostly shriveled, when intact and flattened, broad ovate or cordate, 1-4 cm long, 1-4.5 cm wide, base slightly concave, margin entire; petioles 0.8-5 cm long; upper surface greyish-green to reddish-brown, lower surface relatively pale, midrib distinctly protuberant; after soaking in water, the black or brown stripes visible under the light. Odour slight; taste weak (Fig.1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Root: Epidermal cells subsquare to rectangular, slightly lignified. Cortex relatively broad consisting of approximately 10 layers of parenchymatous cells. Stele occupies about 1/2 of the root. Endodermis consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged in a ring, located in the outermost part of stele. Phloem narrow. Xylem broad [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 8, 1968, 261])\n\nStem: Glandular hairs present occasionally, each with an unicellular head and a 1- to 2-celled stalk. Epidermis covered with cuticle. Cortex broad, scattered with reddish-brown secretion. Secretory canals scattered, containing reddish-brown lumpy secretion. Endodermis distinct. Pericyclic fibres arranged in an interrupted ring, with slightly lignified wall. Phloem narrow. Xylem arranged in a ring. Pith usually hollowed, parenchymatous cells contain starch granules [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermal cells subsquare, covered with cuticle, without stoma. Palisade tissue normally 1 layer of cells, seldom 2 layers. Spongy tissue consists of 4-6 layers of cells. Secretory canal scattered, containing reddish-brown lumpy secretion. Midvein 1 distinctly protruding, consisting of xylem, phloem and collenchymatous cells. Lateral veins small, undeveloped. Collenchymatous cells located next to the phloem and near the lower epidermis. Lower epidermis similar to the upper epidermis, but with stomata. Glandular hairs present occasionally, each with an unicellular head and a 1- to 2-celled stalk [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour pale brown to brown. Upper epidermal cells with slightly sinuous wall. Lower epidermal cells with slightly sinuous wall, stomata anisocytic or anomocytic, 26-65 $ \\mu $m long and 24-48 $ \\mu $m wide, subsidiary cells 3-5. Glandular hairs reddish-brown, consisting of unicellular head and 1- to 2-celled stalk, head 18-41 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Glandular hairs always fallen off from epidermis, leaving a cicatrix surrounded by fine striations. Content of secretory canal numerous, brown to reddish-brown, subrounded or irregular. Fibres rectangular, lignified, 7-38 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Vessels mainly spiral, bordered-pitted or reticulate, 4-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Simple starch granules, subrounded, 4-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum cleft-like or pointed; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules mostly composed of 2-5 units (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[53, 9, 1915, 264])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 425, 1739, 2164])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Lysimachiae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizon香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Lysimachiae Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 434, 1735, 2102])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Lysimachiae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Secretory canal 5. Endodermis\n\n6. Pericyclic fibre 7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[37, 9, 1902, 264])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[244, 436, 1698, 2136])"},{"name":"15","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle D. Blade\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Secretory canal 4. Lateral vein\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Collenchyma 8. Glandular hair\n\n9. Spongy tissue 10. Lower epidermis\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Lysimachiae Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[255, 443, 1720, 1787])"},{"name":"15","text":"1. Upper epidermal cells 2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata\n\n3. Glandular hair 4. Cicatrix 5. Contents of secretory canal 6. Fibre\n\n7. Spiral vessel 8. Bordered-pitted vessel 9. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 7, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"15","text":"Kaempferol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of kaempferol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (10:6:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of ethanol (80%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately kaempferol standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of kaempferol.\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix紫苑\n\n績斷 Lysimachiae Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[378, 368, 841, 636])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[380, 704, 840, 1020])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) kaempferol and (ii) quercetin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"15","text":"Kaempferol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of kaempferol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nQuercetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of quercetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (80%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (364 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The internal diameter of inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 4, 1905, 266])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>70→55</td><td>30→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-35</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>55→30</td><td>45→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of kaempferol Std-FP and quercetin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of kaempferol and quercetin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of kaempferol and quercetin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from kaempferol and quercetin peaks should not be less than 100000 and 50000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject kaempferol Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of kaempferol and quercetin peaks in the chromatograms of kaempferol Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify kaempferol and quercetin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of kaempferol Std-FP and quercetin Std-FP. The retention times of kaempferol and quercetin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lysimachiae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[67, 8, 1967, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Lysimachiae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(quercetin)</td><td>0.85</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,kaempferol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[359, 880, 1770, 1355])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Lysimachiae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[60, 15, 1903, 266])"},{"name":"15","text":"Total ash: not more than 13.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/lysimachiae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lysimachiae Herba","la":"Lysimachiae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/magnoliae-officinalis-flos-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnoliae Officinalis Flos v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[785, 856, 943, 1166])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[567, 1231, 1363, 1941])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Magnoliae Officinalis Flos A. Magnoliae Officinalis Flos B. Magnified image of carpels, stamens and pedicel C. Magnified image of carpels and receptacle\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[785, 856, 943, 1166])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[567, 1231, 1363, 1941])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Magnoliae Officinalis Flos A. Magnoliae Officinalis Flos B. Magnified image of carpels, stamens and pedicel C. Magnified image of carpels and receptacle\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Magnoliae Officinalis Flos\n\nChinese Name: 厚朴花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Houpohua"},{"name":"12","text":"Magnoliae Officinalis Flos is the dried flower bud of Magnolia officinalis Rehd. et Wils. (Magnoliaceae). The flower bud is collected in spring before flowering, steamed briefly, then dried under the sun to obtain Magnoliae Officinalis Flos."},{"name":"12","text":"Flower buds long-conical or oblong, 3.0-6.8 cm long, 1.1-3.2 cm in diameter. Tepals fleshy, surface scabrous, outermost whorl of tepals relatively large and thin, reddish-brown to blackish-brown; inner whorls relatively small and thick, reddish-brown to dark brown. Stamens numerous, spirally arranged on elongated receptacles, anthers flat linear, filaments short and board; carpels numerous, separated, spirally arranged on elongated receptacles. Pedicels long, densely covered with yellowish-white or greyish-white villi. Texture fragile, easily broken. Odour fragrant; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Pedicel: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle, with non-glandular hairs. Cortex consists of parenchymatous cells, scattered with collateral or amphicribral vascular bundles, varying in size. Inner vascular bundles fusiform or oblong, collateral, arranged in an interrupted ring. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells. Oil cells and groups of stone cells scattered in parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 2, 1947, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brown. Oil cells numerous, subrounded to subelliptic, 24-96 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls slightly thickened, filling with yellowish-orange to brownish-yellow substances. Non-glandular hairs very long, 1- to 5-celled, 10-42 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, mostly broken, walls thick, basal cells relatively short; bright yellowish-white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells always in groups, colourless to pale yellow, irregularly branched, walls slightly thickened, lumens large, some with pits; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains ellipsoid to subglobular, 38-72 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, exine thin, nearly smooth or slightly scabrous, one anacolpus barely visible. Epidermal cells of tepals yellowish-brown, polygonal to subpolygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls slightly beaded-thickened or straight; walls of some cells relatively thin, colourless to pale yellow, surface with cuticular striations; stomata occasionally found. Cells of endothecium pale yellow, subpolygonal in surface view, walls beaded-thickened. Crystals of calcium oxalate present in parenchymatous cells, small clusters numerous, prisms few; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly spiral, 4-51 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[251, 468, 1742, 2077])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of pedicel of Magnoliae Officinalis Flos A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermis and non-glandular hairs D. Oil cell 1. Epidermis 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Cortex 4. Amphicribral vascular bundle 5. Collateral vascular bundle 6. Oil cell 7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Stone cells 10. Pith\n\n</div>\n\nNelumbinis Receptaculum莲房\n\n穿山龍\n\nCirsii Japonici Herba\n\n大薊\n\nFritillariae Cirrhosae Bulbus\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\n川貝母\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1727, 59, 1831, 116])\n\n石上柏\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba Magnoliae Officinalis Flos\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[280, 470, 551, 842])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[562, 472, 893, 839])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[906, 470, 1241, 840])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1257, 469, 1471, 834])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1480, 471, 1706, 839])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[284, 859, 631, 1231])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[639, 859, 989, 1227])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1007, 862, 1345, 1228])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1356, 860, 1703, 1227])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[285, 1253, 715, 1616])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[729, 1250, 1198, 1617])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1210, 1245, 1707, 1611])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[284, 1636, 628, 2000])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[639, 1641, 987, 2005])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[998, 1635, 1347, 2006])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1372, 1638, 1702, 2007])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Oil cells 2. Non-glandular hair (2-1 apex, 2-2 base) 3. Stone cells 4. Pollen grains\n\n5. Epidermal cells of tepals 6. Cells of endothecium 7. Crystals of calcium oxalate 8. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 23, 1874, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Honokiol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of honokiol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nMagnolol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of magnolol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (7:3, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol and dissolve 5 g of vanillin. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately honokiol standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), magnolol standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 6, 1948, 256])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[420, 368, 971, 717])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[454, 781, 970, 1104])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) honokiol and (ii) magnolol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[385, 1303, 937, 2115])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Magnoliae Officinalis Flos extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Magnolol standard solution 2. Honokiol standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of honokiol and magnolol (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[34, 12, 1881, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Honokiol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of honokiol CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nMagnolol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of magnolol CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (70%). Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (320 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-40</td><td>50→0</td><td>50→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-45</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of honokiol Std-FP and magnolol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of honokiol and magnolol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of honokiol and magnolol peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from honokiol and magnolol peaks should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 7, 1946, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject honokiol Std-FP, magnolol Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of honokiol and magnolol peaks in the chromatograms of honokiol Std-FP, magnolol Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify honokiol and magnolol peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of honokiol Std-FP and magnolol Std-FP. The retention times of honokiol and magnolol peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Magnoliae Officinalis Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Magnoliae Officinalis Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.40</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(honokiol)</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,magnolol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.03</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.12</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[380, 1848, 1900, 2459])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Magnoliae Officinalis Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 18, 1875, 265])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/magnoliae-officinalis-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnoliae Officinalis Flos","la":"Magnoliae Officinalis Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/mahoniae-caulis-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mahoniae Caulis v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 670, 1984, 2372])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried stem of Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde A. Stems B. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 19, 1872, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 670, 1984, 2372])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the dried stem of Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde A. Stems B. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 19, 1872, 261])"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Mahoniae Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 功勞木\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gonglaomu"},{"name":"16","text":"Mahoniae Caulis is the dried stem of Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr. or Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde (Berberidaceae). The stems are collected all year round, leaves and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Mahoniae Caulis."},{"name":"16","text":"Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr.: Cylindrical in shape, varying in length, 6-40 mm in diameter, externally greyish-brown or brown, with distinct longitudinal furrows and fine transverse fissures, sometimes outer bark relatively smooth. Texture hard. In transverse section, cortex narrow, easily stripped with the fibres, xylem yellow, several concentric rings and closely arranged radial striations. Deeper colour in pith, ray white and distinct. Odour slight; taste bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nMahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde: 3-12 mm in diameter, with fine longitudinal furrows [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr.: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle. Rhytidome scattered with numerous fibre bundles and 2 cork layers. Phloem narrow, with fibre bundles located at the outer side. Phloem ray cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad, vessels occurring singly or in groups, arranged radially. Xylem ray consists of 2-4 rows of cells. Pith composes of parenchymatous cells, prisms of calcium oxalate occasionally found [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nMahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde: Rhytidome narrow, with 1 cork layer inside. Phloem fibre bundles and prisms of calcium oxalate relatively few [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 4, 1947, 257])"},{"name":"16","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to brown. Phloem fibres scattered singly or in bundles, yellow, 9-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, pit canals distinct, walls relatively thickened and lignified; yellowishgreen under the polarized microscope. Stone cells fairly abundant, occasionally adhere with cork cells, polygonal, subrounded or subrectangular, 16-88 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls extremely thickened. Xylem fibres scattered or in bundles, 7-38 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls thickened; pale yellowish-green to greenish-white under the polarized microscope. Bordered-pitted vessels 7-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, 7-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells subpolygonal to subrectangular [Fig. 3 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[33, 20, 1890, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[250, 480, 1739, 2042])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried stem of Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Fibre bundles 3. Cork layer 4. Phloem 5. Prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Phloem ray\n\n7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Xylem ray 10. Pith 11. Rhytidome\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 1, 1948, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[246, 467, 1731, 2095])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried stem of Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate 1. Epidermis 2. Fibre bundles 3. Cork layer 4. Phloem 5. Prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Phloem ray 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Xylem ray 10. Pith 11. Rhytidome\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 20, 1891, 256])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[251, 475, 1711, 2111])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried stem of Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr.\n\n1. Phloem fibres 2. Stone cells 3. Xylem fibres 4. Vessel 5. Prisms of calcium oxalate 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 5, 1946, 256])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[256, 411, 1730, 2127])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried stem of Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde 1. Phloem fibre 2. Stone cells 3. Xylem fibres 4. Vessel 5. Prism of calcium oxalate 6. Cork cells a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\nFootnote: Microscopic features of powder have no significant differences between the dried stem of Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr. and Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 25, 1874, 262])"},{"name":"16","text":"Berberine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of berberine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (90%).\n\nJatrorrhizine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 10.0 mg of jatrorrhizine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (90%).\n\nPalmatine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of palmatine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (90%)."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol, ammonium hydroxide solution (25% , v/v) and water (10:3.5:3.5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of ethanol (90%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter. Transfer the filtrate to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (90%)."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately berberine chloride standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), jatrorrhizine chloride standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), palmatine chloride standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 30 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n穿山龍\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba\n\nMahoniae Caulis\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[445, 373, 890, 654])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[451, 722, 891, 1013])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[454, 1093, 890, 1380])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) berberine chloride (ii) jatrorrhizine chloride and (iii) palmatine chloride\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 25, 1876, 257])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[386, 376, 1094, 1006])\n\n1 2 3 4 (i) 4 (ii)\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Mahoniae Caulis extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Jatrorrhizine chloride standard solution 2. Palmatine chloride standard solution\n\n3. Berberine chloride standard solution\n\n4. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried stem of Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde\n\n(ii) dried stem of Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of berberine, jatrorrhizine and palmatine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"Berberine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of berberine chloride CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (80%).\n\nJatrorrhizine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of jatrorrhizine chloride CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (80%).\n\nPalmatine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of palmatine chloride CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (80%)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 9, 1947, 257])\n\n10 min. Pipette 1 mL of supernatant to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (80%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.5% Triethylamine* (%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile (%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>73→70</td><td>27→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n*Adjust the pH to 2.5 with phosphoric acid"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of berberine chloride Std-FP, jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP and palmatine chloride Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of berberine, jatrorrhizine and palmatine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of berberine, jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from berberine, jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks should not be less than 18000, 15000 and 18000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject berberine chloride Std-FP, jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP, palmatine chloride Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of berberine, jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks in the chromatograms of berberine chloride Std-FP, jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP and palmatine chloride Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify berberine, jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of berberine chloride Std-FP, jatrorrhizine chloride Std-FP and palmatine chloride Std-FP. The retention times of berberine, jatrorrhizine and palmatine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[34, 11, 1889, 263])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Mahoniae Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Mahoniae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(jatrorrhizine)</td><td>0.58</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(palmatine)</td><td>0.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,berberine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[387, 991, 1804, 1798])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried stem of Mahonia bealei (Fort.) Carr. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[41, 3, 1946, 256])\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[388, 366, 1804, 1182])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried stem of Mahonia fortunei (Lindl.) Fedde extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[32, 23, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"16","text":"Mixed berberine chloride, jatrorrhizine chloride and palmatine chloride standard stock solution, Std-Stock (300 mg/L for berberine chloride, 220 mg/L for jatrorrhizine chloride and 260 mg/L for palmatine chloride)\n\nWeigh accurately 3.0 mg of berberine chloride CRS, 2.2 mg of jatrorrhizine chloride CRS and 2.6 mg of palmatine chloride CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (80%).\n\nMixed berberine chloride, jatrorrhizine chloride and palmatine chloride standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed berberine chloride, jatrorrhizine chloride and palmatine chloride Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (80%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.6, 3, 9, 37.5, 75 mg/L for berberine chloride, 0.4, 2.2, 6.6, 28, 56 mg/L for jatrorrhizine chloride and 0.5, 2.6, 7.8, 32.5, 65 mg/L for palmatine chloride."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/mahoniae-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mahoniae Caulis","la":"Mahoniae Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/medulla-junci-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Medulla Junci v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1942, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1942, 252])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Medulla Junci\n\nChinese Name: 燈心草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Dengxincao"},{"name":"11","text":"Medulla Junci is the dried stem pith of Juncus effusus L.(Juncaceae). The stem is collected from late summer to autumn, dried under the sun, the outer part removed, the pith so obtained is then straightened out or ligated into a small bundle to obtain Medulla Junci."},{"name":"11","text":"Long-slender and cylindrical, up to 90 cm long, 1-3 mm in diameter. Externally white or pale yellowishwhite, with fine longitudinal striations. Texture light, soft, slightly elastic, when broken, the fracture is white. Odour slight; tasteless (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Medulla Junci is composed of ventilate parenchyma tissue. The parenchyma cells stellate in shape, branches 4-8, 5-51 $ \\mu $m long, 5-12 $ \\mu $m in diameter, each cell branch is connected with branches of other stellate cells to form air cavities, mostly in triangular shape, occasionally quadrilateral (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale yellow. The parenchyma cells stellate in shape, branches 4-8, 5-51 $ \\mu $ m long, 5-12 $ \\mu $ m wide, connected with branches of other stellate cells to form air cavities mostly in triangular shape; fine pits occasionally observed, intercellular layer thin, sometimes beaded (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[25, 0, 1965, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Dehydroeffusol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dehydroeffusol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nEffusol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of effusol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, cyclohexane and glacial acetic acid (30:70:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dehydroeffusol standard solution, effusol standard solution and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 15 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of dehydroeffusol and effusol.\n\nHerba Taxilli 桑寄生\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n桑白皮\n\nCortex Mori\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n女貞子\n\nRadix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1669, 11, 1871, 83])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[947, 178, 1065, 239])\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\nRadix Panacis Quinquefolii\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nFructus Ev\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[307, 429, 1690, 1742])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Medulla Junci\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stellate parenchyma cell 2. Quadrilateral cavity 3. Triangular cavity\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n燈心草\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n地黄\n\n王不留行\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[346, 422, 1661, 1709])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Medulla Junci (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stellate parenchyma cell 2. Triangular cavity\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[38, 10, 257, 95])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[857, 23, 1089, 119])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[947, 177, 1067, 240])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1613, 12, 1922, 128])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[762, 367, 1211, 699])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[774, 815, 1224, 1142])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) dehydroeffusol and (ii) effusol\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Dehydroeffusol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dehydroeffusol CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (282 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 0, 1968, 250])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>85→46</td><td>15→54</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>46</td><td>54</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dehydroeffusol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dehydroeffusol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dehydroeffusol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dehydroeffusol peak should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and peak 4 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject dehydroeffusol Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dehydroeffusol peak in the chromatogram of dehydroeffusol Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dehydroeffusol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dehydroeffusol Std-FP. The retention times of dehydroeffusol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Medulla Junci extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Medulla Junci extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(effusol)</td><td>0.97</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, dehydroeffusol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(juncusol)</td><td>1.12</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n葛根 Radix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[856, 26, 1091, 118])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[945, 180, 1061, 240])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[395, 481, 1752, 1189])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Medulla Junci extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 5.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 0, 1958, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Dehydroeffusol standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of dehydroeffusol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nEffusol standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of effusol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed dehydroeffusol and effusol standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the dehydroeffusol Std-Stock and effusol Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 4 mg/L for both dehydroeffusol and effusol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonciate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the filtrate. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/medulla-junci","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Medulla Junci","la":"Medulla Junci"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/melicopes-pteleifoliae-caulis-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[632, 836, 1361, 1916])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis A. Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis B. Magnified image of slice of stem"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[632, 836, 1361, 1916])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis A. Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis B. Magnified image of slice of stem"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 三叉苦\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Sanchaku"},{"name":"12","text":"Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis is the dried stem of Melicope pteleifolia (Champ. ex Benth.) T. G. Hartley (Rutaceae). The stem is collected throughout the year, cut into segments when fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical, 10-50 mm in diameter, mostly cut into segments or thick slices. Externally bark greyishbrown to brown with dense brown lenticels, easily rubbed off. Wood yellowish-white with concentric annulations, with the pith in central. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Fracture yellowish-white. Odour rancid; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of 5-10 or more layers of subsquare cells, slightly elongated tangentially, some containing brown pigment, easily fallen off during transverse section processing. In young stem, epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare cells. Cortex relative narrow, scattered with secretory cavities, fibre bundles and stone cells arranged in an interrupted ring in the innermost area of the cortex. Phloem relatively narrow. Xylem broad, ray consists of 1-2 rows of cells. Pith distinct, scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[30, 11, 1938, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellow. Cork cells subpolygonal, 12-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, some containing brown pigment. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 30-70 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, pit fine and arranged densely. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups, subsquare or irregular in shape, 25-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres in bundles, walls relatively thin; bright white under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered, 8-20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[62, 26, 1940, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[420, 414, 876, 1002])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[316, 1064, 896, 1476])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[314, 1585, 896, 2002])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[913, 442, 1702, 2040])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) D. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Secretory cavity 3. Cortex 4. Fibre bundle\n\n5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[348, 493, 994, 969])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1013, 489, 1665, 961])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[349, 995, 996, 1463])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1012, 990, 1665, 1472])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[348, 1492, 674, 1976])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[687, 1490, 996, 1975])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1016, 1493, 1664, 1978])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Cork cells 2. Bordered-pitted vessel 3. Stone cells 4. Xylem fibres\n\n5. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Evolitrine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of evolitrine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, ethanol and triethylamine (9:3:0.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately evolitrine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[375, 1874, 889, 2170])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of evolitrine\n\n</div>\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba杠板歸\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 374, 831, 1181])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Evolitrine standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of evolitrine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Evolitrine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of evolitrine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 45 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (243 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-15</td><td>70→65</td><td>30→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-25</td><td>65</td><td>35</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-30</td><td>65→60</td><td>35→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-35</td><td>60→55</td><td>40→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-50</td><td>55→40</td><td>45→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>40→30</td><td>60→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of evolitrine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of evolitrine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of evolitrine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from evolitrine peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject evolitrine Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of evolitrine peak in the chromatogram of evolitrine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify evolitrine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of evolitrine Std-FP. The retention times of evolitrine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[31, 35, 1939, 263])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.72</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,evolitrine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[343, 984, 1802, 1789])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/melicopes-pteleifoliae-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis","la":"Melicopes Pteleifoliae Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/menispermi-rhizoma-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Menispermi Rhizoma v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[390, 760, 1558, 2041])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nA. Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[390, 760, 1558, 2041])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nA. Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nB. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 北豆根\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Beidougen"},{"name":"14","text":"Menispermi Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Menispermum dauricum DC. (Menispermaceae). The rhizome is collected in spring and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Menispermi Rhizoma."},{"name":"14","text":"Slender cylindrical, tortuous and branched, 12-120 cm long, 1-12 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to dark brown, mostly bearing numerous curved rootlets, and exhibiting prominent rootlet scars and longitudinal wrinkles, the outer bark easily exfoliated. Texture tough, uneasily broken, fracture uneven and fibrous, wood yellowish, arranged radially, medullated. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, some cells suberized. Cortex relatively broad. Vascular bundles collateral. Pericyclic fibres cresent, located in the outer side of vascular bundles. Phloem relatively narrow. Cambium distinct. Xylem lignified. Pith relatively large, parenchymatous cells arranged loosely. Stone cell rare, singly scattered in cortex and pith (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Pericyclic fibres pale yellow, long-fusiform, 11-43 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, tapering, blunt at the end, sometimes with septa, pits dotted. Xylem fibres relatively long, 8-32 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls slightly thickened and lignified, with pits, pits obliquely slit-shaped or cruciform. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 18-99 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Cork cells colourless to yellowish-brown, rectangular, subsquare or polygonal in surface view. Stone cells scattered singly, pale yellow to yellow, rectangular or subrounded, 19-263 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[62, 26, 1949, 261])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 447, 1743, 2039])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Menispermi Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericyclic fibres 1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Pericyclic fibres 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Pith 8. Stone cell\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 770, 1711, 2140])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\n1. Pericylic fibre 2. Xylem fibres 3. Bordered-pitted vessel 4. Cork cells 5. Stone cell\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"14","text":"Dauricine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dauricine CRS (Fig. 4) and place it in a 1-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with ethanol.\n\nDaurisoline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of daurisoline CRS (Fig. 4) and place it in a 1-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), methanol and dichloromethane (0.1:1:8, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dauricine standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), daurisoline standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Fumigate the plate with iodine vapour chamber for about 10 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[367, 353, 1179, 1295])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) dauricine and (ii) daurisoline\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1267, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 376, 849, 1151])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Menispermi Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Daurisoline standard solution 2. Dauricine standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of dauricine and daurisoline (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Dauricine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of dauricine CRS and place it in a 10-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with ethanol (50%).\n\nDaurisoline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (90 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.9 mg of daurisoline CRS and place it in a 10-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with ethanol (50%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (282 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 190 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 12% carbon loading). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.07% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>95→83</td><td>5→17</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>83→75</td><td>17→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dauricine Std-FP and daurisoline Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of dauricine and daurisoline should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of dauricine and daurisoline peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from dauricine and daurisoline peaks should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject dauricine Std-FP, daurisoline Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of dauricine and daurisoline peaks in the chromatograms of dauricine Std-FP, daurisoline Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dauricine and daurisoline peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing\n\nits retention time with that in the chromatograms of dauricine Std-FP and daurisoline Std-FP. The retention times of dauricine and daurisoline peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Menispermi Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Menispermi Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, daurisoline)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(dauricine)</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[337, 1314, 1802, 1785])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Menispermi Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/menispermi-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Menispermi Rhizoma","la":"Menispermi Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/menthae-haplocalycis-herba-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Menthae Haplocalycis Herba v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[388, 918, 1016, 1832])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1051, 1035, 1237, 1207])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1248, 1046, 1406, 1197])\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1003, 1306, 1257, 1539])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1282, 1366, 1385, 1471])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1000, 1635, 1131, 1762])\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1141, 1635, 1262, 1761])\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1283, 1650, 1380, 1757])\n\n1 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\nA. Menthae Haplocalycis Herba B. A single herb of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\nC. Magnified image of inflorescence D. Magnified image of flower\n\nE. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf F. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nG. Magnified image of dotted glandular scales on leaf surface H. Magnified image of purplish-brown stem\n\nI. Magnified image of pale green stem J. Magnified image of transverse section of stem"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[388, 918, 1016, 1832])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1051, 1035, 1237, 1207])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1248, 1046, 1406, 1197])\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1003, 1306, 1257, 1539])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1282, 1366, 1385, 1471])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1000, 1635, 1131, 1762])\n\n1 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1141, 1635, 1262, 1761])\n\n2 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1283, 1650, 1380, 1757])\n\n1 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\nA. Menthae Haplocalycis Herba B. A single herb of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\nC. Magnified image of inflorescence D. Magnified image of flower\n\nE. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf F. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nG. Magnified image of dotted glandular scales on leaf surface H. Magnified image of purplish-brown stem\n\nI. Magnified image of pale green stem J. Magnified image of transverse section of stem"},{"name":"17","text":"Official name: Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\nChinese name: 薄荷\n\nChinese phonetic name: Bohe"},{"name":"17","text":"Mentha Haplocalycis Herba is the dried aerial part of Mentha canadensis L. (syn. Mentha haplocalyx Briq.) (Lamiaceae). The aerial part is collected on sunny days in summer and autumn when most of the flowers bloom, then dried under the sun or in the shade to obtain Menthae Haplocalycis Herba."},{"name":"17","text":"Stems square, with opposite branches, 44-102 cm in length, 0.3-5.5 mm in diameter; externally pale green, pale brown to purplish-brown, nearly glabrous to pubescent, internodes 1.7-11.0 cm long; texture fragile, fracture white, pith hollow. Leaves opposite, short petioled; lamina crumpled and rolled, broadly lanceolate, long-elliptical or ovate in shape when whole and flattened, 2.5-9.2 cm in length, 1.0-4.1 cm in width; the upper surface dark green and the lower surface greyish-green, sparsely pubescent, bearing dotted glandular scales, concave when observed under microscope. Verticillaster axillary, calyx campanulate, with 5 terminal teeth, corolla pale purple when fresh, yellowish-brown when dried. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and gives a cooling sensation in mouth (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"17","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, subsquare to subrectangular in shape, covered with cuticle, rarely bearing hairs or glandular scales. Collenchyma located at the four ridges, consisting of several to more than 10 layers of collenchymatous cells beneath epidermis. Crystals sometimes observed, dispersed throughout the stem, but more usually located underneath epidermis; polychromatic under polarized microscope. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, intercellular space relatively large, loosely packed. Endodermis distinct, consisting of 1 layer of cells. Vascular bundles more well developed at the four ridges, with several smaller vascular bundles located between two ridges. Phloem narrow, cells usually slightly shrunken. Cambium arranged in a ring. Xylem more well developed at the four ridges, vessels arranged radially. Pith composed of relatively large parenchymatous cells, usually hollow at the centre of stem [Fig. 2(i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[17, 23, 1931, 252])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermal cells subsquare to subrectangular in shape, hairs sometimes observed. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cells, rarely 2 layers. Spongy tissue consists of 4-7 layer of cells. Collenchyma consists of 1-2 layers of collenchymatous cells underneath the upper and lower epidermis at the main vein of leaf. Xylem 2-4 vessels arranged radially. Phloem cells relatively small, located underneath the xylem. Lower epidermal cells small and flat, subsquare in shape, hairs sometimes observed. Crystals mainly located at the palisade tissue, but could also be observed at epidermis, spongy tissue or vascular bundle; polychromatic under polarized microscope. Non-glandular hairs mainly located at midrib or veins, usually broken, slightly bent, fine warty protuberance occasionally observed. Glandular scales often sunken in depressions of epidermis; consist of an extremely short unicellular stalk and a glandular head of 8 radiating cells beneath a raised cuticle which is usually broken. Small glandular hairs usually broken, stalk unicellular with small elliptic, unicellular head [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Colour yellowish-green. Glandular scales top view circular in shape, lateral view oblate in shape, 8 cells in head, 49-107 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, usually shrunken, containing pale yellowish-brown secretions; stalk unicellular, extremely short. Small glandular hairs head elliptic, unicellular, 17-33 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, some contains pale yellowish-brown secretions; stalk unicellular. Non-glandular hairs usually broken, intact ones contain 1-8 cells, slightly bent, 12-82 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, wall thickness up to 10 $ \\mu $ m, fine warty protuberance occasionally observed. Crystals located inside epidermal cells or parenchymatous cells of stems and leaves, colourless to pale yellow in colour, slightly fan-shaped or irregular in shape, radial striations indistinct; polychromatic under the polarized microscope, radial striations sometimes observable. Upper epidermal cells of leaf irregular in shape in surface view, cell wall slightly sinuous anticlinal. Lower epidermal cells of leaf wall sinuous anticlinal, stomata diacytic. Vessels mostly spiral and reticulate vessels, 7-65 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥 茯苓 Myrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡 Storax\n\nStellariae Radix 蘇合香\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 420, 1712, 2113])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope) D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Crystal 4. Cortex 5. Endodermis\n\n6. Vascular bundle 7. Phloem 8. Cambium 9. Xylem 10. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[937, 72, 1032, 124])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[246, 409, 1750, 2123])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Glandular scale and crystals (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Crystals (under polarized microscope) E. Small glandular hair\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Collenchyma\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 6. Radicle 7. Lower epidermis 8. Crystal\n\n9. Non-glandular hair 10. Glandular scale 11. Small glandular hair\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紅豆蔻\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸棗仁\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[287, 492, 1684, 2065])"},{"name":"17","text":"1. Glandular scales (1-1 top view, 1-2 lateral view) 2. Small glandular hairs\n\n3. Non-glandular hair 4. Crystals 5. Upper epidermal cells of leaf\n\n6. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n7. Vessels (7-1 reticulate vessel, 7-2 spiral vessels)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"17","text":"Diosmin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of diosmin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the solution to a 1-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol.\n\nLinarin (buddleoside) standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of linarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the solution to a 1-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol.\n\nRosmarinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (9:1:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 40 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Wash the residue with methanol and combine the extracts. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately diosmin standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), linarin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), rosmarinic acid standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 5 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[368, 425, 1127, 2396])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) diosmin (ii) linarin (buddleoside) (iii) menthol and (iv) rosmarinic acid\n\n</div>\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 375, 904, 1210])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Diosmin standard solution\n\n2. Linarin standard solution\n\n3. Rosmarinic acid standard solution\n\n4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of diosmin, linarin and rosmarinic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"17","text":"Diosmin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of diosmin CRS and dissolve in 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol.\n\nLinarin (buddleoside) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of linarin CRS and dissolve in 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Pipette 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the solution to a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol.\n\nRosmarinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 40 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Wash the residue with methanol and combine the extracts. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"17","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times 50 \\mathrm{~mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>95→81</td><td>5→19</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-13</td><td>81</td><td>19</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>13-15</td><td>81→75</td><td>19→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-20</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of diosmin Std-FP, linarin Std-FP and rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of diosmin, linarin and rosmarinic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of diosmin, linarin and rosmarinic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from diosmin, linarin and rosmarinic acid peaks should not be less than 10000, 60000 and 9000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"17","text":"Separately inject diosmin Std-FP, linarin Std-FP, rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of diosmin, linarin and rosmarinic acid peaks in the chromatograms of diosmin Std-FP, linarin Std-FP, rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify diosmin, linarin and rosmarinic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of diosmin Std-FP, linarin Std-FP and rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The retention times of diosmin, linarin and rosmarinic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, diosmin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.12</td></tr><tr><td>4 (rosmarinic acid)</td><td>1.24</td><td>±0.12</td></tr><tr><td>5 (linarin)</td><td>1.87</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[327, 370, 1824, 864])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Menthae Haplocalycis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"17","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.7%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.85%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nToluene distillation method: not more than 12%."},{"name":"17","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12%."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/menthae-haplocalycis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Menthae Haplocalycis Herba","la":"Menthae Haplocalycis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/mori-ramulus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mori Ramulus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[756, 880, 1169, 1375])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Mori Ramulus\n\nA. Mori Ramulus B. Slices\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[35, 9, 1900, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[756, 880, 1169, 1375])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Mori Ramulus\n\nA. Mori Ramulus B. Slices\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[35, 9, 1900, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Mori Ramulus\n\nChinese Name: 桑枝\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Sangzhi"},{"name":"11","text":"Mori Ramulus is the dried slender branch (new sprout) of Morus alba L. (Moraceae). The branch is collected in late spring to early summer, the leaves removed, then dried under the sun; or the branch sliced while fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Mori Ramulus."},{"name":"11","text":"Slender and cylindrical, occasionally branched, 5-25 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-yellow to yellowish-brown, with numerous yellowish-brown dotted lenticels and fine longitudinal striations, nodes usually swollen, with leaf scars and axillary buds. Texture hard and tough, uneasily broken; fracture fibrous. Slices 1-4 mm thick, the bark relatively narrow; the xylem broad, with pale yellow to yellow radial striations and cracks; the pith white to pale yellow. Odour slight; taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells, the cells rectangular, arranged densely, with relatively thick wall, slightly lignified. Cortex relatively narrow, containing stone cells and prisms of calcium oxalate, arranged in an interrupted ring. Fibre bundles present in the outside of phloem. Phloem ray distinct, consisting 1-3 rows of cells. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad; xylem ray distinct, arranged radially; large xylem vessels scattered singly or 2-3 in groups. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-yellow. Starch granules abundant, simple starch granules subrounded or polygonal, 2-11 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum pointed; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-10 units. Stone cells subellipitical or triangular, 13-61 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with fine pit canals and thickened wall; blue-white under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered singly, occasionally found in stone cells, polyhedral, cubical, and rhombic, 7-36 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 13-90 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, pits arranged closely. Phloem fibres scattered singly, slender, 5-36 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, wall thick, pit indistinct, lumen narrow, slit-shaped; xylem fibres in bundles, numerous, fusiform, tapering slightly towards the end; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells brownish-yellow, subrectangular or irregular (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[12, 2, 1922, 275])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 438, 1745, 2114])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Mori Ramulus A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells D. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells 4. Phloem fibres 5. Phloem ray 6. Phloem\n\n7. Cambium 8. Xylem ray 9. Xylem 10. Pith 11. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\nRubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nMori Ramulus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[260, 529, 1712, 2142])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Mori Ramulus\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Stone cells 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Bordered-pitted vessels 5. Phloem fibre 6. Xylem fibres 7. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 7, 1910, 259])"},{"name":"11","text":"Mulberrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of mulberrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, acetone and glacial acetic acid (5:2:1:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately mulberrin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of mulberrin.\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nMori Ramulus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 370, 927, 755])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of mulberrin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Mulberrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of mulberrin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>80-58</td><td>20-42</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>58-35</td><td>42-65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>35-15</td><td>65-85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of mulberrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of mulberrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of mulberrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from mulberrin peak should not be less than 120000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[34, 5, 1905, 264])\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject mulberrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of mulberrin peak in the chromatogram of mulberrin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify mulberrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of mulberrin Std-FP. The retention times of mulberrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Mori Ramulus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Mori Ramulus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(oxyresveratrol)</td><td>0.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, mulberrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(morusin)</td><td>1.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 1708, 1743, 2407])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Mori Ramulus extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[73, 10, 1962, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/mori-ramulus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mori Ramulus","la":"Mori Ramulus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/morindae-officinalis-radix-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Morindae Officinalis Radix v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[449, 868, 1550, 1791])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Morindae Officinalis Radix A. Morindae Officinalis Radix B. Transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1901, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[449, 868, 1550, 1791])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Morindae Officinalis Radix A. Morindae Officinalis Radix B. Transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1901, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Morindae Officinalis Radix\n\nChinese Name: 巴戟天\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Bajitian"},{"name":"13","text":"Morindae Officinalis Radix is the dried root of Morinda officinalis F. C. How (Rubiaceae). The root is collected throughout the year, the rootlets removed, washed, then semi-dried, followed by gentle beating with a cudgel to compress the root tissues, then dried under the sun to obtain Morindae Officinalis Radix."},{"name":"13","text":"Compressed-cylindrical, slightly curved, varying in length, 4-20 mm in diameter. Externally greyish yellow to dark grey, with longitudinal wrinkles and transverse cracks, some bark transversely broken and wood exposed. Texture tough. Fracture with thicken bark, purple to dark brown, easily separated from wood; wood hard, 1-6 mm in diameter, yellowish-brown to yellowish-white. Odour slight; taste sweet and slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Cork consists of several layers of suberized cells. Cortex narrow; stone cells present singly or in groups in the outer part of cortex, arranged in an interrupted ring; parenchymatous cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate. Phloem relatively broad; parenchymatous cells in the inner part contains raphides of calcium oxalate. Cambium distinct. Xylem vessels scattered singly or 2-3 in groups, arranged radially; xylem rays 1-3 rows of cells wide (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-brown to dark brown. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered in the parenchymatous cells, mostly in bundles, 19-132 $ \\mu $m long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups, subrounded, subsquare, strip-shaped or irregular, 18-81 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall thick, pit distinct; bright orangish-red and polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres mostly in bundles, 6-72 $ \\mu $m in diameter, long-fusiform, with relatively large bordered pits and pit canals, pit apertures obliquely slit-shaped, V-shaped and cross-shaped; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells subsquare or subpolygonal. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 13-65 $ \\mu $m in diameter, pits arranged closely (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 4, 1908, 263])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[276, 452, 1695, 2109])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Morindae Officinalis Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells D. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cells 3. Cortex 4. Raphides of calcium oxalate 5. Phloem\n\n6. Cambium 7. Xylem ray 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[66, 8, 1961, 258])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[278, 443, 1613, 2168])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Morindae Officinalis Radix\n\n1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Stone cells 3. Fibres 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Bordered-pitted vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 6, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Nystose standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of nystose CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, water, formic acid and glacial acetic acid (6:3:2:2, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 11 mL of sulphuric acid to 89 mL of ethanol.Add 2.75 g of $ \\alpha $ -naphthol and 7 mL of water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately nystose standard solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of nystose.\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\nLophatheri Herba淡竹葉\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 367, 844, 1001])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of nystose\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Nystose standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of nystose CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol (60%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (60%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 9 2^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ flow: 1.2 L/min] and a Hydrophilic Interaction Chromatography (HILIC) column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ $ 5 \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size. The internal diameter of inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-18</td><td>15→28</td><td>85→72</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>18-40</td><td>28→40</td><td>72→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[35, 4, 1906, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of nystose Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of nystose should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of nystose peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from nystose peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject nystose Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of nystose peak in the chromatogram of nystose Std-FP and the retention times of the twelve characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify nystose peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of nystose Std-FP. The retention times of nystose peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the twelve characteristic peaks of Morindae Officinalis Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the twelve characteristic peaks of Morindae Officinalis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.67</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,nystose)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.26</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>1.59</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>1.67</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>1.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>1.83</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>1.89</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n香附\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[381, 375, 1702, 1080])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Morindae Officinalis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above twelve characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 11, 1905, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 50.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 60.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/morindae-officinalis-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Morindae Officinalis Radix","la":"Morindae Officinalis Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/myristicae-semen-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Myristicae Semen v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[522, 1052, 773, 1374])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[529, 1394, 815, 1646])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[791, 1194, 1003, 1501])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1050, 1053, 1379, 1383])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1061, 1403, 1369, 1703])\n\nC 5mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Myristicae Semen A. Myristicae Semen B. Magnified image of shrunken embryo ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) C. Magnified image of transverse section of kernel\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[1268, 194, 1388, 239])"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[522, 1052, 773, 1374])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[529, 1394, 815, 1646])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[791, 1194, 1003, 1501])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1050, 1053, 1379, 1383])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1061, 1403, 1369, 1703])\n\nC 5mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Myristicae Semen A. Myristicae Semen B. Magnified image of shrunken embryo ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) C. Magnified image of transverse section of kernel\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[1268, 194, 1388, 239])"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Myristicae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 肉豆蔻\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Roudoukou"},{"name":"16","text":"Myristica Semen is the dried kernel of Myristica fragrans Houtt. (Myristicaceae). The kernel is collected in summer and winter, foreign matter removed, washed clean, then dried at around $ 4 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to obtain Myristica Semen."},{"name":"16","text":"Ovoid-globose to ellipsoid, one end smoothly rounded, the other end vaguely truncate, 1.7-3.4 cm long, 13-23 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown to greyish-yellow, sometimes covered with white powder (lime powder). Kernel covered with pale longitudinal furrows and irregular reticulated wrinkles. A protuberant hilum is located at the truncated end, yellowish-white; chalaza dark and dented. Raphe longitudinally furrowed, connecting the two ends. Texture hard, fracture showing marble-like striations, brownish-yellow, interweaved. A dried and shrunken embryo visible at the truncated end, oily. Odour strongly fragrant; taste pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"16","text":"Outer layers of perisperm consist of about 10 layers of tangentially flattened and wrinkled cells, containing brown contents (before stained), scattered with small vascular bundles; the dark brown perisperm (before stained) penetrates into endosperm, forming marble-like striations; oil cells numerous, 25-125 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Endosperm cells subrounded, walls thin, filled with starch granules, oil droplets and aleurone grains, scattered with pale yellow cells (before stained) (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"16","text":"Colour reddish-brown to brown. Fatty oil abundant, becoming oil droplets when heated, turning into raphide-shaped cluster or feather-shaped crystals when cooled. Perisperm cells in pieces, polygonal, containing brownish-red, bright red or yellowish-brown pigments. Endosperm cells yellow, subpolygonal, filled with starch granules, oil droplets and aleurone grains. Starch granules mainly simple starch granules, subrounded, 10-25 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum dotted, slit-shaped or asteroidal; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules few, composed of 2-8 units, 15-35 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum distinct. Vessels mainly spiral, reticulate vessels also found, yellow, 10-25 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[62, 40, 1948, 258])\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[325, 1247, 693, 1564])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[783, 865, 1704, 1568])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Myristicae Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle 1. Perisperm 2. Vascular bundle 3. Endosperm 4. Oil cell\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[248, 389, 1752, 2129])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Myristicae Semen\n\n</div>\n\n1. Fatty oil 2. Perisperm cells 3. Endosperm cells 4. Starch granules 5. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[62, 32, 1926, 260])"},{"name":"16","text":"Myristicin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of myristicin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (19:1, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol and dissolve in 5 g of vanillin."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately myristicin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[448, 373, 612, 572])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[386, 638, 1077, 912])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[449, 979, 807, 1212])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 1289, 804, 1435])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) $ \\alpha $ -pinene (ii) dehydrodiisoeugenol (iii) myristicin and (iv) safrole\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1266, 193, 1388, 240])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 373, 849, 1211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Myristicae Semen extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Myristicin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of myristicin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"16","text":"Myristicin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of myristicin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $\n\n$ \\alpha $ -Pinene standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (130 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.3 mg of $ \\alpha $ -pinene CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\nSafrole standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of safrole CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $"},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of n-hexane Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with n-hexane. Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with n-hexane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5, $ 0. 3 2 \\mathrm{~ m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{~ m} $) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. The injection temperature is at $ 2 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 10:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1)\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-1</td><td>40</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>1-37</td><td>40→220</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>37-47</td><td>220</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of myristicin Std-FP, $ \\alpha $ -pinene Std-FP and safrole Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of myristicin, $ \\alpha $ -pinene and safrole should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of myristicin, $ \\alpha $ -pinene and safrole peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from myristicin, $ \\alpha $ -pinene and safrole peaks should not be less than 250000, 25000 and 100000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject myristicin Std-FP, $ \\alpha $ -pinene Std-FP, safrole Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu $ L each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of myristicin, $ \\alpha $ -pinene and safrole peaks in the chromatograms of myristicin Std-FP, $ \\alpha $ -pinene Std-FP, safrole Std-FP and\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[60, 31, 1925, 260])\n\nthe retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify myristicin, $ \\alpha $ -pinene and safrole peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of myristicin Std-FP, $ \\alpha $ -pinene Std-FP and safrole Std-FP. The retention times of myristicin, $ \\alpha $ -pinene and safrole peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Myristicae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Myristicae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(α-pinene)</td><td>0.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(γ-terpinene)</td><td>0.43</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(terpinen-4-ol)</td><td>0.59</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(safrole)</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, myristicin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[346, 1385, 1821, 2048])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Myristicae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"16","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 11.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/myristicae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Myristicae Semen","la":"Myristicae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/myrrha-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Myrrha v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[418, 1118, 1079, 1660])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1113, 1180, 1546, 1601])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Myrrha\n\n</div>\n\nA. Myrrha B. Fracture surface"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[418, 1118, 1079, 1660])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1113, 1180, 1546, 1601])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Myrrha\n\n</div>\n\nA. Myrrha B. Fracture surface"},{"name":"10","text":"Official name: Myrrha\n\nChinese name: 没蘌\n\nChinese phonetic name: Moyao"},{"name":"10","text":"Myrrha is the dried resin from the trunk bark of Commiphora myrrha (T.Nees) Engl. (syn. C. molmol Engl.) (Burseraceae). The bark should be cut in May to July and November to January, and a milky, liquid resin would exude from the incision. The solidified resin is collected from incised bark, freed from foreign matter, then dried in well-ventilated place to obtain Myrrha."},{"name":"10","text":"Irregular granular agglomerates, varied in size, 10-70 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, nearly translucent part in brownish-black colour, covered with yellow dust-like powder. Texture hard and fragile, fracture surface uneven, lusterless. Odour aromatic; taste bitter and slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Not applicable for Myrrha."},{"name":"10","text":"Standard solution\n\n(-)- $ \\beta $ -Elemene standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 25 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nn-Hexane.\n\nStaining reagent\n\nIodine.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 16, 1956, 255])"},{"name":"10","text":"Place the sample in freezer $ (-10^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}) $ for at least 12 h and powder the sample. Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of ethyl acetate. Homogenize the mixture for 10 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2000\\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel 60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Expose the plate to iodine vapour in a chamber for about 5 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[382, 1516, 587, 1800])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[386, 1927, 657, 2155])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Chemical structures of (i) (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and (ii) myrrhone\n\n</div>\n\n明黛参 Changii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥 茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[378, 373, 796, 1211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Myrrha extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. (-)- $ \\beta $ -Elemene standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"(-) - $ \\beta $ -Elemene standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 10.0 mg of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nMyrrhone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (80 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of myrrhone CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 50 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"10","text":"Place the sample in freezer $ (-10^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}) $ for at least 12 h and powder the sample. Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of ethyl acetate. Homogenize the mixture for 5 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2000\\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5, 0.32 mm $ \\times $ 30 m) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about 0.25 $ \\mu $ m thick. Nitrogen is used as carrier gas at 1.5 mL/min. The injection temperature is at $ 240^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 300^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 10:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>100</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3-13</td><td>100→110</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>13-26</td><td>110→162</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>26-30</td><td>162</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>162→222</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>45-46</td><td>222→282</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>46-48</td><td>282</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene Std-FP and myrrhone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone peaks should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 7 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject (-) $ - \\beta $ -elemene Std-FP, myrrhone Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of (-) $ - \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone peaks in the chromatograms of (-) $ - \\beta $ -elemene Std-FP, myrrhone Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify (-) $ - \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of (-) $ - \\beta $ -elemene Std-FP and myrrhone Std-FP. The retention times of (-) $ - \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Myrrha extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Myrrha extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker，(-)-β-elemene)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(epicurzerenone)</td><td>1.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.85</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2.23</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7(myrrhone)</td><td>2.43</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[298, 1778, 1820, 2317])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Myrrha extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 10%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 12%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 9.5%."},{"name":"10","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 8.5%.\n\nWater-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 24%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (C) ."},{"name":"10","text":"Mixed (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1200 mg/L for (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and 400 mg/L for myrrhone)\n\nWeigh accurately 12.0 mg of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene CRS and 4.0 mg of myrrhone CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nMixed (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone Std-Stock, dilute with ethyl acetate to produce a series of solutions of 3, 6, 15, 30, 60 mg/L for (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and 5, 10, 20, 40, 80 mg/L for myrrhone."},{"name":"10","text":"Place the sample in freezer $ (-10^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C}) $ for at least 12 h and powder the sample. Weigh accurately 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of ethyl acetate. Homogenize the mixture for 5 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2000\\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5, 0.32 mm $ \\times $ 30 m) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about 0.25 $ \\mu $ m thick. Nitrogen is used as carrier gas at 1.5 mL/min. The injection temperature is at $ 240^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 300^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 10:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 3) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 3 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>100</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3-13</td><td>100→110</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>13-26</td><td>110→162</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>26-30</td><td>162</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>162→222</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>45-46</td><td>222→282</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>46-48</td><td>282</td><td>-</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the mixed (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone Std-AS (15 mg/L for (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and 20 mg/L for myrrhone). The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from (-)- $ \\beta $ -elemene and myrrhone peaks should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between (-) $ - \\beta $ -elemene peak and the closest peak; and the R value between myrrhone peak and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/myrrha","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Myrrha","la":"Myrrha"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/nelumbinis-receptaculum-v7-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Nelumbinis Receptaculum v7 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[713, 853, 1235, 1137])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[595, 1237, 981, 1577])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[993, 1242, 1357, 1551])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[617, 1605, 985, 1829])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[994, 1604, 1376, 1823])\n\n1 cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Nelumbinis Receptaculum A. Nelumbinis Receptaculum B. Sliced pieces showing broken sponge-like fracture\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[713, 853, 1235, 1137])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[595, 1237, 981, 1577])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[993, 1242, 1357, 1551])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[617, 1605, 985, 1829])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[994, 1604, 1376, 1823])\n\n1 cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Nelumbinis Receptaculum A. Nelumbinis Receptaculum B. Sliced pieces showing broken sponge-like fracture\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 16, 1873, 262])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Nelumbinis Receptaculum\n\nChinese Name: 蓮房\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Lianfang"},{"name":"10","text":"Nelumbinis Receptaculum is the dried receptacle of Nelumbo nucifera Gaertn. (Nymphaeaceae). The receptacle is collected in autumn when the fruit is ripe, fruits removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Nelumbinis Receptaculum."},{"name":"10","text":"Obconical or funnel-shaped, frequently dilacerated, 53-140 mm in diameter, 3.8-7 cm high. Externally greyish-brown to purplish-brown, with fine longitudinal striations and wrinkles. The top with numerous rounded hollows and the base with the remnants of pedicel. Texture lax. Fracture sponge-like, brown. Odour slight; taste slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour purplish-brown to yellowish-brown. Epidermal cells polygonal in surface view, with double circle-shaped papillae. Brown cells subsquare to subrounded, walls slightly thickened, with reddish-brown contents in lumen. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregates numerous, 8-52 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres in bundles, pitted, 8-32 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Vessels mainly spiral, annular vessels occasionally found, 8-60 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 2).\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[247, 408, 1741, 2133])"},{"name":"10","text":"1. Epidermal cells (papillae---->) 2. Brown cells 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4. Fibres\n\n5. Vessels (5-1 spiral vessel, 5-2 annular vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[41, 24, 1879, 261])"},{"name":"10","text":"Hyperoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hyperoside CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (8:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (95%). Heat the mixture in a water bath at about $ 60^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (95%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hyperoside standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nNelumbinis Receptaculum\n\n蓮房\n\nCirsii Japonici Herba\n\n大薊\n\n穿山龍\n\nDioscoreae Nipponicae Rhizoma\n\n仙鶴草\n\nAgrimoniae Herba\n\nIlicis Rotundae Cortex救必應\n\n石上柏\n\nDrynariae Rhizoma骨碎補\n\n土木香\n\nInulae Radix\n\nSelaginellae Doederleinii Herba Nelumbinis Receptaculum\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[384, 379, 932, 956])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structure of hyperoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[384, 1132, 773, 1788])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Nelumbinis Receptaculum extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Hyperoside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of hyperoside (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 12, 1881, 263])"},{"name":"10","text":"Hyperoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of hyperoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 7 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min in a water bath at $ 50^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (360 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>15-17</td><td>85-83</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-35</td><td>17-18</td><td>83-82</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-42</td><td>18-28</td><td>82-72</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>42-50</td><td>28-60</td><td>72-40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-55</td><td>60-90</td><td>40-10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hyperoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of hyperoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of hyperoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from hyperoside peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 7, 1946, 257])"},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject hyperoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of hyperoside peak in the chromatogram of hyperoside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hyperoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of hyperoside Std-FP. The retention times of hyperoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Nelumbinis Receptaculum extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Nelumbinis Receptaculum extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, hyperoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(quercetin-3-O-β-D-glucuronide)</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.73</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[387, 1662, 1858, 2230])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Nelumbinis Receptaculum extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 20, 1882, 265])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"10","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 7.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/nelumbinis-receptaculum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Nelumbinis Receptaculum","la":"Nelumbinis Receptaculum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/nelumbinis-stamen-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Nelumbinis Stamen v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[575, 1139, 1472, 1672])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Nelumbinis Stamen\n\nA. Nelumbinis Stamen B. Magnified image of stamens"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[575, 1139, 1472, 1672])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Nelumbinis Stamen\n\nA. Nelumbinis Stamen B. Magnified image of stamens"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Nelumbinis Stamen\n\nChinese Name: 蓮鬚\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Lianxu"},{"name":"13","text":"Nelumbinis Stamen is the dried stamen of Nelumbo nucifera Gaertn. (Nymphaeaceae). The stamen is collected at flowering period on sunny days in summer, dried under the sun under paper cover, or in shaded area to obtain Nelumbinis Stamen."},{"name":"13","text":"Linear. Anthers twisted, longitudinally split, 1.2-1.8 cm long, about 1 mm in diameter, pale yellow, yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Filaments slender, slightly curved, 1.0-2.2 cm long, pale purple, yellowish-purple to reddish-purple. Texture lax. Odour slightly aromatic; taste astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Epidermal cells rectangular, polygonal or irregular in shape, covered with cuticle. Connective composed of 1 vascular bundle and parenchymatous cells. 1 locule divided into 2 pollen sacs, endothecium slightly thickened and beaded; each pollen sac is lined with residual tapetum; some pollen grains scattered in pollen sac, subspherical or oblong (Fig. 2).\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n山銀花"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Epidermal cells of connective subrounded to rounded in surface view, anticlinal walls sinuous; outer walls papillated in lateral view. Epidermal cells of anther rectangular, polygonal or irregular in shape in surface view; outer walls wavy in lateral view and with distinct cuticle striations. Endothecium cells strip-shaped, walls slightly thickened and beaded, lumens sometimes contain yellowish-brown or reddish-brown contents. Pollen grains subspherical to oblong, 45-81 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, composed of two outer walls, with tricolpate and granular reticulate striations on the surface. Parenchymatous cells of filaments and connective vary in shape, walls thin and sinuous, containing yellow, yellowish-brown or reddish-brown contents. Clusters of calcium oxalate sometimes visible and usually aggregated in rosette-shape; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly spiral, 4-19 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, double-spiral and annular vessels sometimes visible (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 400, 1743, 2108])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of anther of Nelumbinis Stamen A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Cuticle 2. Epidermis 3. Vascular bundle 4. Connective parenchymatous cells 5. Endothecium 6. Residual tapetum 7. Pollen grains\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\n天山雪莲\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1042, 151, 1164, 194])\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1294, 69, 1444, 119])\n\nMenispermi Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1750, 71, 1847, 121])\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[327, 657, 553, 998])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[568, 659, 879, 997])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[891, 659, 1315, 996])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1328, 654, 1661, 995])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[327, 1023, 753, 1357])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[779, 1021, 1207, 1359])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1234, 1021, 1659, 1358])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[325, 1388, 752, 1733])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[777, 1388, 1206, 1732])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1234, 1387, 1661, 1734])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Epidermal cells of connective (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view)\n\n2. Epidermal cells of anther (2-1 in surface view, 2-2 in lateral view)\n\n3. Endothecium cells (3-1 without contents, 3-2 with yellowish-brown contents)\n\n4. Pollen grains 5. Parenchymatous cells of filaments and connective\n\n6. Clusters of calcium oxalate 7. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"13","text":"Astragalin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of astragalin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nKaempferol standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of kaempferol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-hexane, formic acid and water (6.5:2.5:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately astragalin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), kaempferol standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[421, 365, 905, 841])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[424, 908, 905, 1169])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) astragalin and (ii) kaempferol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[374, 1314, 851, 2147])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Nelumbinis Stamen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Kaempferol standard solution 2. Astragalin standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of astragalin and kaempferol (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Astragalin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of astragalin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (75%).\n\nKaempferol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.05 mg of kaempferol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (75%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol (75%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Wash the residue with ethanol (75%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol (75%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (360 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (3.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>20</td><td>80</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-50</td><td>20→63</td><td>80→37</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of astragalin Std-FP and kaempferol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of astragalin and kaempferol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of astragalin and kaempferol peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from astragalin and kaempferol peaks should not be less than 50000 and 100000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 35, 1940, 262])\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject astragalin Std-FP, kaempferol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of astragalin and kaempferol peaks in the chromatograms of astragalin Std-FP, kaempferol Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify astragalin and kaempferol peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of astragalin Std-FP and kaempferol Std-FP. The retention times of astragalin and kaempferol peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Nelumbinis Stamen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Nelumbinis Stamen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, astragalin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(kaempferol)</td><td>1.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[335, 1823, 1795, 2262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Nelumbinis Stamen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 18.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 20.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/nelumbinis-stamen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Nelumbinis Stamen","la":"Nelumbinis Stamen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/nigellae-semen-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Nigellae Semen v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[591, 850, 1389, 1621])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[710, 1635, 827, 1824])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[853, 1647, 957, 1826])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1078, 1685, 1165, 1793])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1222, 1685, 1288, 1791])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Nigellae Semen\n\n</div>\n\nA. Nigella Semen B. Magnified image of seeds C. Magnified image of cut surface of seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[591, 850, 1389, 1621])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[710, 1635, 827, 1824])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[853, 1647, 957, 1826])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 mm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1078, 1685, 1165, 1793])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1222, 1685, 1288, 1791])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Nigellae Semen\n\n</div>\n\nA. Nigella Semen B. Magnified image of seeds C. Magnified image of cut surface of seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Nigellae Semen\n\nChinese name: 黑種草子\n\nChinese phonetic name: Heizhongcaozi"},{"name":"12","text":"Nigellae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Nigella glandulifera Freyn et Sint. (Ranunculaceae). The seed is collected in autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Nigellae Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Triangular-ovoid, 2.2-3.5 mm long, 1.2-2.2 mm wide. Externally black and rough, apex somewhat narrow and sharp; base relatively obtuse, with irregular protrudings. Texture hard; cut surface greyish-white, oily. Odour slightly aromatic; taste pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Outer epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of cells, varying in size, subrectangular or irregularly oblong, frequently elongated tangentially, outer walls mostly with papillate or non-glandular hair-like excrescences, walls slightly thickened, dark brown before stained colour; cuticle somewhat thin, finely and densely granulate striations indistinctly visible. Parenchyma of testa consists of 3-4 layers of cells, rectangular or irregular in shape, somewhat elongated tangentially. Inner epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of cells, flattened and brown. Perisperm consists of 1 layer of rectangular cells, elongated radially, sometimes dilapidated. Endosperm cells polygonal, filled with aleurone grains. Cotyledons 2, cells polygonal or subrounded, the outer layer of cells slightly elongated radially, filled with aleurone grains. Vascular bundle small, in the middle of outer layers of endosperm (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[18, 35, 1949, 246])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-black. Outer epidermal cells of testa dark brown, polygonal in surface view, varying in size, the outer walls papillate in lateral view. Inner epidermal cells of testa brown, rectangular, subsquare or subpolygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls beaded, periclinal walls finely and densely reticulate-striated. Endosperm cells polygonal, containing oil droplets and aleurone grains. Oil droplets numerous, rounded, colourless, light red after stained with Sudan III. Aleurone grains plenty, colourless, reddish-brown after stained with iodine. Cotyledon cells rectangular or subrectangular. Vessels rare, small, mainly spiral (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 43, 1967, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 401, 1738, 2096])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Nigellae Semen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C-D. Section magnified\n\n1. Outer epidermis of testa 2. Parenchyma of testa 3. Inner epidermis of testa\n\n4. Perisperm 5. Endosperm 6. Cotyledon 7. Vascular bundle\n\n西河柳\n\n野老鸛草\n\nPolygonati Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1144, 134])\n\n巴豆（生）\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1691, 55, 1939, 138])\n\nValerianae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n黄精\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nNigellae Semen\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[359, 448, 976, 851])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1002, 446, 1625, 856])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[356, 883, 978, 1292])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1000, 878, 1625, 1293])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[357, 1317, 660, 1617])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[678, 1319, 983, 1617])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1001, 1316, 1300, 1618])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1325, 1314, 1624, 1611])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[356, 1642, 979, 2050])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1001, 1641, 1625, 2049])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Outer epidermal cells of testa (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view)\n\n2. Inner epidermal cells of testa 3. Endosperm cells with oil droplets ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n4. Oil droplets (4-1 without staining, 4-2 stained with Sudan III)\n\n5. Aleurone grains (5-1 without staining, 5-2 stained with iodine)\n\n6. Cotyledon cells 7. Spiral vessels\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[27, 45, 1965, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Hederagenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hederagenin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-hexane and formic acid (5:3:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 25 mL of methanol and 2.5 mL of hydrochloric acid. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with methanol. Combine the extract and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately hederagenin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[19, 41, 1950, 246])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[370, 370, 887, 756])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of hederagenin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 934, 796, 1772])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Nigellae Semen extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Hederagenin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of hederagenin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 43, 1965, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Hederagenin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of hederagenin CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 25 mL of methanol and 2.5 mL of hydrochloric acid. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with methanol. Combine the extract and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>95→60</td><td>5→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>60→30</td><td>40→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>30→20</td><td>70→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hederagenin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of hederagenin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of hederagenin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from hederagenin peak should not be less than 160000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[20, 40, 1948, 245])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject hederagenin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of hederagenin peak in the chromatogram of hederagenin Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hederagenin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of hederagenin Std-FP. The retention times of hederagenin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Nigellae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Nigellae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.58</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(marker,hederagenin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[332, 1676, 1812, 2295])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Nigellae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 7.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 27.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/nigellae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Nigellae Semen","la":"Nigellae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/olibanum-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Olibanum v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[455, 1044, 1138, 1731])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1146, 1300, 1488, 1647])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Olibanum\n\nA. Olibanum B. Fracture surface\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 22, 1905, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[455, 1044, 1138, 1731])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1146, 1300, 1488, 1647])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Olibanum\n\nA. Olibanum B. Fracture surface\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 22, 1905, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Olibanum\n\nChinese name: 乳香\n\nChinese phonetic name: Ruxiang"},{"name":"12","text":"Olibanum is the dried resin from the bark of Boswellia sacra Flück. (syn. Boswellia carteri Birdw.) (Burseraceae). The resin is collected in spring and summer. Resin exuded from the bark wound is left for a few days to dry. The solidified resin lumps are collected by scraping them off the bark or picking up those that have fallen onto the ground, freed from the foreign matter to obtain Olibanum."},{"name":"12","text":"Often long ellipsoid, tear-like or circular-like lumps, 5-40 mm in diameter, sometimes stuck together in a cluster, yellowish-white, translucent, the colour deepens over time. Cover with yellowish-white powder. Texture firm and brittle. Softens when heated. Fracture surface waxy or with glass-like luster. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Not applicable for Olibanum.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[49, 30, 1938, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"3-O-Acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 7.0 mg of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\n11-Keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (6:4:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (330 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 3 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the supernatant to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 6 min). Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum苦杏仁\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[380, 435, 861, 833])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[381, 975, 813, 1367])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Chemical structures of (i) 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and (ii) 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[378, 374, 1010, 1207])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Olibanum extract observed under UV light (254 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. 11-Keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution\n\n2. 3-O-Acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"3-O-Acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (160 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.2 mg of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\n11-Keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[46, 16, 1938, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (330 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 20-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ ( 2. 1 \\times1 0 0 \\mathrm{~ m m} ) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Formic Acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-7</td><td>68</td><td>32</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>7-12</td><td>68→70</td><td>32→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>12-15</td><td>70→74</td><td>30→26</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-21</td><td>74→84</td><td>26→16</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>21-35</td><td>84</td><td>16</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid peaks should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 4).\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP, 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu $ L each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid peaks in the chromatograms of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP, 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-FP. The retention times of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Olibanum extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Olibanum extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(11-keto-β-boswellic acid)</td><td>0.44</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,3-O-acetyl-11-keto-β-boswellic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.63</td><td>$\\pm$0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[333, 1788, 1821, 2296])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Olibanum extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[43, 32, 1936, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.7%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.1%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 4.8%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.8%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[36, 25, 1929, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (285 mg/L for 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid, 95 mg/L for 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid) Weigh accurately 5.7 mg of 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid CRS and 1.9 mg of 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid CRS, and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 9,18,36,72,143 mg/L for 3-O-acetyl-11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid and 3,6,12,24,48 mg/L for 11-keto- $ \\beta $ -boswellic acid."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/olibanum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Olibanum","la":"Olibanum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/oroxyli-semen-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Oroxyli Semen v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[566, 843, 1443, 1961])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Oroxyli Semen A. Oroxyli Semen B. Cotyledons\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[566, 843, 1443, 1961])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Oroxyli Semen A. Oroxyli Semen B. Cotyledons\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Oroxyli Semen\n\nChinese Name: 木蝴蝶\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Muhudie"},{"name":"13","text":"Oroxyli Semen is the dried ripe seed of Oroxylum indicum (L.) Vent. (Bignoniaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in autumn and winter, dried under the sun until the fruit dehiscent, the seed gathered and dried under the sun to obtain Oroxyli Semen."},{"name":"13","text":"Butterfly-shaped thin slices, testa extending on three sides, forming wide wings, 4.3-10 cm long, 3-5 cm wide. Externally pale yellowish-white with silky lustre; the wing membranous and translucent, with radial striations, the margin mostly broken. Texture light in weight. After removal of testa, membranous endosperms tightly cover the cotyledons. Cotyledons 2, butterfly-shaped, pale yellow to yellowish-green; 8-22 mm in diameter. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Endosperm consists of 2-4 layers of cells. Upper epidermal cells of cotyledon square to rectangular. Lower epidermal cells relatively small. Palisade tissue cells rectangular, containing oil droplets and chloroplasts. Spongy tissue cells oval or irregular, containing oil droplets and starch granules. Primary vascular bundles scattered in the spongy tissue (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 8, 1973, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellow to greenish-yellow. Testa cells elongated, fibre-shaped, 15-49 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, wall sinuously thickened; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Endosperm cells polygonal to subsquare, wall beaded. Crystals of calcium oxalate abundant, present in testa and endosperm cells, 2-19 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[28, 4, 1898, 266])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 445, 662, 2107])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[901, 451, 1407, 1254])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[721, 1356, 1612, 2040])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of cotyledon of Oroxyli Semen\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Endosperm 2. Upper epidermis of cotyledon 3. Palisade tissue\n\n4. Spongy tissue 5. Primary vascular bundle 6. Lower epidermis of cotyledon\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繽斷\n\nOroxyli Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 412, 1716, 2174])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Oroxyli Semen 1,2. Testa cells 3. Endosperm cells 4. Crystals of calcium oxalate a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 6, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Baicalein standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of baicalein CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nChrysin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chrysin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and glacial acetic acid (5:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of ferric trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with methanol (80%). Combine the solution and make up to the mark with methanol (80%). Freshly prepare the test solution."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate (2-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ ) and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately baicalein standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), chrysin standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of baicalein and chrysin.\n\nma 餘甘子 Phyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nArecae Pericarpium\n\nLophatheri Herba 大腹皮\n\n淡竹葉 田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Hert\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n車前子\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\n紫苑\n\nOroxyli Semen Rhizoma\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[442, 370, 911, 675])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[444, 743, 1032, 1181])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[442, 1251, 897, 1545])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[442, 1614, 990, 2012])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) baicalein (ii) baicalin (iii) chrysin and (iv) oroxin A\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 7, 1906, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Baicalein standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of baicalein CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nBaicalin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of baicalin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nChrysin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of chrysin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (80%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter. Freshly prepare the test solution."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (275 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>55→0</td><td>45→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of baicalein Std-FP, baicalin Std-FP and chrysin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of baicalein, baicalin and chrysin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of baicalein, baicalin and chrysin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from baicalein, baicalin and chrysin peaks should not be less than 110000, 10000 and 200000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 9, 1966, 260])\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject baicalein Std-FP, baicalin Std-FP, chrysin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of baicalein, baicalin and chrysin peaks in the chromatograms of baicalein Std-FP, baicalin Std-FP, chrysin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify baicalein, baicalin and chrysin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of baicalein Std-FP, baicalin Std-FP and chrysin Std-FP. The retention times of baicalein, baicalin and chrysin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Oroxyli Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Oroxyli Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(oroxin B)</td><td>0.41</td><td>±0.06</td></tr><tr><td>2(oroxin A)</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3(baicalin)</td><td>0.77</td><td>±0.06</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,baicalein)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6(chrysin)</td><td>1.12</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[37, 99, 208, 191])\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[300, 384, 1833, 856])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Oroxyli Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[68, 8, 1965, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/oroxyli-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Oroxyli Semen","la":"Oroxyli Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/orpiment-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Orpiment v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[21, 6, 1926, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"8","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[21, 6, 1926, 256])"},{"name":"8","text":"Official Name: Orpiment\n\nChinese Name: 雌黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Cihuang"},{"name":"8","text":"Orpiment is a mineral of the sulphides of the orpiment group, containing mainly arsenic (III) sulphide $ \\left(\\mathrm{A s}_{2} \\mathrm{S}_{3}\\right) $ . The ore is collected, foreign matter removed to obtain Orpiment."},{"name":"8","text":"Aggregates of lumpy masses, irregular in shape. Yellow, sometimes with red plaques; surface with yellow striations and resin-like lustre, often covered with a layer of yellow powder, leaving a mark when scratched with a fingernail. Texture fragile, easily broken, fracture with resin-like lustre, thin pieces flexible. Odour garlic-like (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"8","text":"Powder\n\nColour yellow. Crystals dark yellow to dark yellowish-green, subsquare, subrectangular to irregular in shape, with striation, translucent. Polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"8","text":"(I) Chemical test of arsenic salt"},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 8 mL of sulphuric acid. Heat the mixture on a hot plate and boil for about 15 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer 1 mL of the mixture to a 250-mL conical flask. Add 20 mL of water and 2 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (40%, w/v). Add 2.0 g of sodium hydrogen\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1892, 43, 1984, 87])\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[249, 409, 1708, 1804])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Orpiment\n\n</div>\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[27, 5, 1928, 258])\n\ncarbonate and mix well. Transfer 1 mL of upper layer to a test tube. Add 1 drop of silver nitrate solution (1.7% ,w/v) and mix well. Yellow precipitate can be observed."},{"name":"8","text":"Potassium chlorate-nitric acid solution\n\nDissolve excess amount of potassium chlorate in nitric acid."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.01 g of the powdered sample and place it in a test tube, then add 1 drop of water. Dissolve the sample in 2 mL of potassium chlorate-nitric acid solution. Add 3 drops of barium chloride solution ( 5% , w/v ). Allow the white precipitate to settle. Discard the supernatant. Add 2 mL of water to the precipitate and mix well. White precipitate cannot be dissolved."},{"name":"8","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI."},{"name":"8","text":"Finely powdered arsenic (III) sulphide (0.5 g)."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"8","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 20) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 20 values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a $ 2\\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0. 0 5^{\\circ} $ when compared to the corresponding 20 values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database."},{"name":"8","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard material and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20 values of the diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the characteristic diffraction peaks of the standard material and test sample as listed in Table 1.\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 5, 1984, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The 20 values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of Orpiment\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>18.535</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>22.346</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>24.130</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>28.028</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>32.126</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[381, 990, 1582, 1518])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference XRPD pattern of Orpiment\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic diffraction peaks (Fig. 3) each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0.2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the values stated in Table 1."},{"name":"8","text":"Lead (II) acetate absorbent\n\nWeigh 5.0 g of lead (II) acetate and place it in a 100-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Add acetic acid until the solution becomes clear and make up to the mark with water. Soak thoroughly 1.0 g of cotton wool in 12 mL of lead (II) acetate solution. Extrude excess solution and dry at a temperature below $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 4, 1929, 257])\n\nMercuric bromide test paper\n\nWeigh 2.5 g of mercuric bromide and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol. Soak filter paper in mercuric bromide solution for 1 h. Dry the paper and store in the dark.\n\nAcidic tin (II) chloride solution\n\nWeigh 20.0 g of tin (II) chloride dihydrate and dissolve in 50 mL of hydrochloric acid."},{"name":"8","text":"A is a 100 mL conical flask with standard ground joint; B is a standard hollow ground glass stopper connected to glass conduit C (external diameter 8.0 mm, internal diameter 6.0 mm), the total length of B and C is about 180 mm, D is a plastic screw, the upper part of which has an aperture 6.0 mm in diameter and the lower part of which has an aperture 8.0 mm in diameter; E is a plastic screw cap which has an aperture 6.0 mm in diameter. A wad of lead acetate cotton wool weighing about 60 mg is packed into tube C to a depth of about 60-80 mm. A disc of mercuric bromide test paper is placed between the contacting surface of D and E.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[592, 1269, 937, 2069])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Apparatus for the test of arsenic (III) oxide in CMM samples\n\n</div>"},{"name":"8","text":"Arsenic (III) oxide standard stock solution, Std-Stock\n\nWeigh 0.132 g of arsenic (III) oxide and place it in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then add 5 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (20%, w/v). Neutralize the solution with sulphuric acid (10%, w/v) and further add 10 mL of sulphuric acid (10%, w/v). Make up to the mark with water.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 8, 1984, 263])"},{"name":"8","text":"Pipette 10 mL of the Std-Stock to a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then add 10 mL of sulphuric acid (10%, w/v). Make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the solution."},{"name":"8","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of hydrochloric acid (28%, w/v) and stir for 30 min. Filter the mixture. Wash the residue for two times, each with 10 mL of hydrochloric acid (28%, w/v) by stirring for 10 min. Combine the extracts to a 500-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Transfer 10 mL of the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"8","text":"(a) Transfer 2 mL of test solution to the flask (Fig. 4), add 5 mL of hydrochloric acid, 21 mL of water, 5 mL of potassium iodide solution (16.5% , w/v) and 5 drops of acidic tin (II) chloride solution. Allow the mixture to stand for about 10 min.Add 2.0 g of zinc granule and heat the mixture on a water bath at about $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 45 min. Collect and examine the mercuric bromide test paper.\n\n(b) Transfer 2 mL of standard solution to the flask (Fig. 4), carry out the procedure as stated in (a). Collect and examine the mercuric bromide test paper. Less intense orange spot should be observed in (a) when compared to (b)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/orpiment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Orpiment","la":"Orpiment"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/osmundae-rhizoma-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Osmundae Rhizoma v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[567, 917, 939, 1458])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[934, 978, 1216, 1439])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1225, 1148, 1412, 1464])\n\n5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[553, 1483, 878, 1738])\n\nB 2cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[898, 1559, 1197, 1707])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1230, 1577, 1414, 1687])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Osmundae Rhizoma\n\nA. Osmundae Rhizoma\n\nB. Cut surface of Osmundae Rhizoma (1 rhizome, 2 frond bases)\n\nC. Lateral view of frond bases D. Magnified image of cut surface of frond base\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[567, 917, 939, 1458])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[934, 978, 1216, 1439])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1225, 1148, 1412, 1464])\n\n5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[553, 1483, 878, 1738])\n\nB 2cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[898, 1559, 1197, 1707])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1230, 1577, 1414, 1687])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n2 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Osmundae Rhizoma\n\nA. Osmundae Rhizoma\n\nB. Cut surface of Osmundae Rhizoma (1 rhizome, 2 frond bases)\n\nC. Lateral view of frond bases D. Magnified image of cut surface of frond base\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Osmundae Rhizoma\n\nChinese name: 紫萁實罛\n\nChinese phonetic name: Ziqiguanzhong"},{"name":"14","text":"Osmundae Rhizoma is the dried rhizome and frond bases of Osmunda japonica Thunb. (Osmundaceae). The rhizome and frond bases are collected in spring and autumn, washed clean, rootlets removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Osmundae Rhizoma."},{"name":"14","text":"Conical, subfusiform or cylindrical, slightly curved, obtuse at apex, occasionally branched, relatively acute at bottom, 5-19 cm long, 25-93 mm in diameter. Rhizome diageotropic or obliquely grown, ramenta-free, sometimes bearing remnants of dark and hard rootlets at the lower side, densely covered remains of frond bases at the upper side. Fracture surface brown in colour, leaf-trace vascular bundles arranged in rings at the outer part, with a relatively broad and light-brown rhizome at the centre. Frond bases oblong, obliquely upward, 2.9-9.2 cm long, 2.1-9.3 mm in diameter, externally brown or brownish-black, slightly convex at dorsal surface, obtusely round at margins, auricled wings easily fallen off, usually absent or dilacerated. Cut surface oblong or crescent-shaped, almost hollow at centre, U-shaped meristele often detached from the outside tissues. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Odour slight; taste sweet and slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Frond base: Epidermis mostly broken and fallen off, consisting of 1 layer of cells, brownish yellow in colour; underneath 2-18 layers of subrounded parenchymatous cells. Hypodermis occupying about 3/7 of frond base, consisting of 8-23 layers of brown, subrounded or polygonal, lignified sclerenchymatous cells forming a ring around the frond base, cell walls highly thickened; some containing oily substances; sclerenchymatous cells at two sides with even thicker cell walls and more dark brown in colour. Parenchyma of cortex occupying about 3/7 of frond base; parenchymatous cells subrounded or polygonal, containing numerous starch granules. Endodermis narrow and distinct. Meristele occupying about 1/7 of frond base,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 38, 1947, 246])\n\namphicribral, U-shaped. Xylem arranged in U-shape, composed of 1-5 layers of tracheids, cells polygonal in shape, cell wall relatively thick. Phloem being a relative thin layer surrounding the xylem, scattered with yellowish-brown secretory cells. Secretory cells scattered within phloem, polygonal or subrounded in shape, yellowish-brown in colour before staining. Sclerenchyma consists of 1-10 layers of sclerenchymatous cells, subrounded or polygonal, forming a zone in the depressed side of the U-shaped meristele, cell walls usually thick and more dark brown in colour at two sides of meristele. Starch granules numerously found in the parenchymatous cells of hypodermis [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nRhizome: Sclerenchyma consists of 3-7 layers of sclerenchymatous cells, subrectangular or oblong in shape, tightly packed. Parenchyma of cortex underneath sclerenchyma consists of 1-2 layers of parenchymatous cells, polygonal or subrounded in shape; parenchyma locating at the centre of rhizome consisting of 3-12 layers of parenchymatous cells, polygonal and irregular in shape, usually broken at the centre. Meristele occupying 4/5 of rhizome, consisting of 7-13 subrounded or U-shaped meristeles arranged closely in a ring, amphicribal, surrounded by endodermis. Phloem inside the meristele, narrow, consisting of 1 layer of cells. Xylem occupying the whole meristele, well-developed, tightly packed and distinct, cells polygonal and with relative thick cell walls [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour dark brown. Sclerenchymatous cells numerous, orange-brown to dark brown, fibre-like, in groups or singly scattered; cells subrounded or polygonal in shape, extremely thickened cell wall, cell cavity rarely observed. Tracheids scalariform, colourless to pale yellowish, 13-51 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, 2-11 in groups. Parenchymatous cells abundant, two types: first type hexagonal in shape; second type subrectangular or subpolygonal in shape; cell wall colourless to yellowish, cell wall thin to slightly thickened; containing numerous starch granules; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Fibre orange-brown to brown in colour, 12-53 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; bright orange under the polarized microscope. Secretory cells occasionally observed, yellowish-brown, subrounded to rounded, containing brown secretion. Xylem cells yellow, hexagonal to polygonal in shape, cell wall relatively thick. Starch granules numerously found; cluster of starch granules composed of 10-230 units; single starch granules subrounded or sub-oblong in shape, 4-15 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[646, 75, 763, 113])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1723, 49, 1851, 97])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 413, 1745, 2138])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of frond base of Osmundae Rhizome\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\nD. Starch granules in parenchyma (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Starch granules in parenchyma (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermis 3. Parenchyma of cortex 4. Endodermis\n\n5. Meristele 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Secretory cells 9. Sclerenchyma\n\n10. Starch granules\n\n---\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1693, 54, 1938, 137])\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 397, 1733, 2124])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Osmundae Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Sclerenchyma 2. Parenchyma of cortex 3. Meristele\n\n4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[632, 41, 773, 115])\n\n京大戟\n\n鴨跖草\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\nOsmundae Rhizoma\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[285, 597, 1709, 1934])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Sclerenchymatous cells (1-1 fibre-like, 1-2 in groups, 1-3 singly scattered)\n\n2. Scalariform tracheids\n\n3. Parenchymatous cells containing starch granules (3-1 hexagonal, 3-2 subrectangular)\n\n4. Fibres 5. Secretory cells 6. Xylem cells\n\n7. Starch granules (7-1 cluster form, 7-2 single form)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 40, 1945, 242])"},{"name":"14","text":"Protocatechuic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of protocatechuic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nProtocatechuic aldehyde standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of protocatechuic aldehyde CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60- $ 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (5:5:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 20.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 150 mL of ethanol (60%) and 1.5 mL hydrochloric acid. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 30 mL of water. Transfer the extract to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract the aqueous layer for two times each with 30 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts. Adjust the pH to 7.0 with water. Transfer the extract to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the extract to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of ethyl acetate and add 0.5 g silica gel. Evaporate the extract to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of ethyl acetate. Load the sample extract to the solid phase extraction (SPE) column containing silica gel packing (50 $ \\mu $m particle size, 20 mL, 5 g). Add 20 mL of ethyl acetate to the SPE column. Collect the eluate and transfer the eluate to 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the eluate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in a mixture of 1 mL of methanol and 10 $ \\mu $L of glacial acetic acid."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately protocatechuic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), protocatechuic aldehyde standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 433, 760, 724])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 854, 707, 1133])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) protocatechuic acid and (ii) protocatechuic aldehyde\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 1280, 910, 2084])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Osmundae Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Protocatechuic acid standard solution 2. Protocatechuic aldehyde standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 40, 1946, 240])"},{"name":"14","text":"Protocatechuic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of protocatechuic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.1% formic acid.\n\nProtocatechuic aldehyde standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of protocatechuic aldehyde CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of 0.1% formic acid."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of water. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 3 3 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of 0.1% formic acid. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (290 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times 50 \\mathrm{~mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.4 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Formic acid:Acetonitrile(0.1:99.9,v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-8</td><td>100</td><td>0</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>8-10</td><td>100→98</td><td>0→2</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>98→90</td><td>2→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of protocatechuic acid Std-FP and protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks should not be less than 2000 and 3000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 258])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject protocatechuic acid Std-FP, protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks in the chromatograms of protocatechuic acid Std-FP, protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of protocatechuic acid Std-FP and protocatechuic aldehyde Std-FP. The retention times of protocatechuic acid and protocatechuic aldehyde peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Osmundae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Osmundae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(protocatechuic acid)</td><td>0.51</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,protocatechuic aldehyde)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.11</td><td>±0.08</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2.63</td><td>±0.10</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>3.23</td><td>±0.12</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[336, 402, 1815, 1002])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Osmundae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[22, 44, 1966, 257])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/osmundae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Osmundae Rhizoma","la":"Osmundae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/panacis-japonici-rhizoma-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Panacis Japonici Rhizoma v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[564, 1025, 757, 1733])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1063, 1048, 1309, 1335])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[787, 1143, 944, 1657])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1083, 1449, 1336, 1716])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Panacis Japonici Rhizoma A. Panacis Japonici Rhizoma B. Magnified image of dented stem scar C. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[564, 1025, 757, 1733])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1063, 1048, 1309, 1335])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[787, 1143, 944, 1657])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1083, 1449, 1336, 1716])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Panacis Japonici Rhizoma A. Panacis Japonici Rhizoma B. Magnified image of dented stem scar C. Magnified image of transverse section of rhizome"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Panacis Japonici Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 竹節參\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhujieshen"},{"name":"14","text":"Panacis Japonici Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Panax japonicus C. A. Mey. (Araliaceae). The rhizome is collected in autumn, main root and outer bark removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Panacis Japonici Rhizoma."},{"name":"14","text":"Slightly cylindrical, somewhat curved, some with fleshy lateral roots. 4-28 cm long, 4-31 mm in diameter. Externally brownish-yellow or dark brown, rough, with dense longitudinal wrinkles and root scars. Nodes obvious, internode 0.5-2.5 cm long, each showing a dented stem scar, with obvious yellowish-brown dots of vascular bundle, arranged in a ring. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Fracture yellowish-white to pale yellow, with small dots of yellow vascular bundles, arranged in a ring. Odour slight; taste bitter with a slightly sweet aftertaste (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Cork consists of 2-10 layers of cells. Cortex relatively broad, containing few secretory canals. Vascular bundles collateral. Phloem distinct, secretory canals occasionally found. Cambium in a ring. Xylem bundles made up of 2-4 strands arranged radially or in a single row. Xylem fibres obvious, usually in several bundles. Pith visible, parenchymatous cells subrounded, scattered with numerous clusters of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour yellowish-white to yellowish-brown. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, usually aggregated in rosette shape, 17-99 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres in bundles, walls slightly thickened, some crisscrossed to become V-shaped. Cork cells polygonal in surface view, narrow-rectangular in lateral view, walls thickened. Secretory ducts occasionally found, containing yellow masses. Vessels scalariform and reticulate, 10-86 $ \\mu $m in diameter; bordered-pitted vessels occasionally found. Starch granules numerous, mainly simple starch granules, subrounded, 4-15 $ \\mu $m in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[62, 42, 1939, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 576, 1686, 1937])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of the transverse section of Panacis Japonici Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate D. Secretory canal\n\n1. Cork 2. Cluster of calcium oxalate 3. Cortex 4. Secretory canal\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem fibre bundle 9. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1046, 40, 1181, 87])\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n白花丹\n\nPlumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1295, 69, 1443, 119])\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[245, 601, 1731, 2129])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Xylem fibres\n\n3. Cork cells (3-1 in surface view, 3-2 in lateral view) 4. Secretory canals (->)\n\n5. Vessels (5-1 scalariform vessel, 5-2 reticulate vessels) 6. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"14","text":"Chikusetsusaponin IV standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of chikusetsusaponin IV CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nChikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of chikusetsusaponin IVa CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nChikusetsusaponin V standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of chikusetsusaponin V CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-butanol, water, methanol and formic acid (10:5:3.5:2:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chikusetsusaponin IV standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), chikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), chikusetsusaponin V standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[442, 369, 1490, 788])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[440, 854, 1269, 1279])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[437, 1342, 1370, 1892])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) chikusetsusaponin IV (ii) chikusetsusaponin IVa and (iii) chikusetsusaponin V\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 381, 1155, 1216])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3 4\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Panacis Japonici Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chikusetsusaponin V standard solution 2. Chikusetsusaponin IV standard solution\n\n3. Chikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution 4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chikusetsusaponin IV, chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Chikusetsusaponin IV standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (180 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.8 mg of chikusetsusaponin IV CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nChikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of chikusetsusaponin IVa CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%).\n\nChikusetsusaponin V standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (350 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.5 mg of chikusetsusaponin V CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with alkyl reversed-phase bonded silica gel with diisopropyl side chain (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>70→66</td><td>30→34</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>66→55</td><td>34→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chikusetsusaponin IV Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP and chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chikusetsusaponin IV, chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chikusetsusaponin IV, chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chikusetsusaponin IV, chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks should not be less than 30000, 60000 and 6000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject chikusetsusaponin IV Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chikusetsusaponin IV, chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V\n\npeaks in the chromatograms of chikusetsusaponin IV Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chikusetsusaponin IV, chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chikusetsusaponin IV Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP and chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP. The retention times of chikusetsusaponin IV, chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Panacis Japonici Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Panacis Japonici Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,chikusetsusaponinV)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(chikusetsusaponinIV)</td><td>1.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(chikusetsusaponinIVa)</td><td>1.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[330, 1509, 1792, 2188])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Panacis Japonici Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 36, 1936, 264])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/panacis-japonici-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Panacis Japonici Rhizoma","la":"Panacis Japonici Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/panacis-majoris-rhizoma-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Panacis Majoris Rhizoma v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Panacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[607, 855, 1354, 1491])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[668, 1502, 961, 1796])\n\nB 1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1075, 1685, 1196, 1786])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1208, 1593, 1388, 1779])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Panacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[607, 855, 1354, 1491])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[668, 1502, 961, 1796])\n\nB 1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1075, 1685, 1196, 1786])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1208, 1593, 1388, 1779])"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Panacis Majoris Rhizom\n\nB. Magnified image of stem scars\n\nC. Magnified image of fracture surface of rhizomes dried under sun\n\nD. Magnified image of fracture surface of steamed (or boiled) rhizomes\n\n1. Stem scars 2. Internodes\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[51, 26, 1927, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Panacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\nChinese name: 珠子参\n\nChinese phonetic name: Zhuzishen"},{"name":"13","text":"Panacis Majoris Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Panax japonicus C. A. Mey. var. major (Burk. ) C. Y. Wu et K. M. Feng (Araliaceae). The rhizome is collected in autumn, freed from rough bark and rootlet, dried under the sun, or dried after steaming or boiling thoroughly to obtain Panacis Majoris Rhizoma."},{"name":"13","text":"Slightly spheroidal, conical or irregularly rhombic, occasionally beaded, 5-28 mm in diameter. Outer surface brownish-yellow to yellowish-brown, with distinct verrucous protrusions and wrinkles, occasionally with rounded sunken stem scars, sometimes small internodes remained on one or both sides. Texture hard, fracture surface uneven, pale yellowish-white, powderly. Odour slight, taste bitter and slightly sweet with irritating sensation. Steamed (or boiled) rhizomes fracture surface yellowish-white or yellowish-brown, slightly horny, taste slightly sweet and bitter, no irritation to the throat on chewing (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[42, 22, 1925, 256])"},{"name":"13","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex slightly narrow with rounded or oblong secretory canals, 32-500 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, surrounded by 5-18 secretory cells. Secretory canals in phloem relatively small. Cambium arranged in an interrupted ring. Rays broad. Xylem vessels subpolygonal, arranged radially or in V-shape. Pith in centre. Parenchymatous cells contain starch granules (before staining process) and clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, 20-96 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells polygonal in surface view. Starch granules abundant, simple starch granules ovoid or oblong, 5-25 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum pointed, cleft, striations indistinct; compound starch granules usually composed of 2-4 units, some up to 8 units; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Gelatinous starch masses observed (steamed or boiled samples). Secretory canals mostly broken into fragments, sometimes brownish-yellow secretion observed. Vessels mainly scalariform, spiral or reticulate, 35-76 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷\n\nAngelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 430, 1733, 1803])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Panacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory canals\n\nD. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cluster of calcium oxalate 4. Secretory canal\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Ray 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nUncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n鈎藤\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷\n\nAngelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n芍木仁 菊花\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[382, 444, 1602, 1772])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate 2. Cork cells\n\n3. Starch granules 4. Gelatinous starch masses (steamed or boiled samples)\n\n5. Secretory canals\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 scalariform vessels, 6-2 spiral vessels, 6-3 reticulate vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[46, 35, 1945, 256])"},{"name":"13","text":"Chikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of chikusetsusaponin IVa CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (80%).\n\nChikusetsusaponin V standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of chikusetsusaponin V CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (80%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-butanol, water, methanol and formic acid (10:5:3.5:0.5:0.3, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (80%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution, chikusetsusaponin V standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子參\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 435, 1228, 827])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 968, 1359, 1467])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) chikusetsusaponin IVa and (ii) chikusetsusaponin V\n\n</div>\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[381, 379, 1065, 1220])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Panacis Majoris Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution 2. Chikusetsusaponin V standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Chikusetsusaponin IVa standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of chikusetsusaponin IVa CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol (80%).\n\nChikusetsusaponin V standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chikusetsusaponin V CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol (80%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (80%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (80%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 21, 1935, 256])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ ( 2. 1 \\times 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m} ) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>85→65</td><td>15→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>65</td><td>35</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-15</td><td>65→10</td><td>35→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP and chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks should not be less than 40000 and 80000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks in the chromatograms of chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP, chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chikusetsusaponin IVa Std-FP and chikusetsusaponin V Std-FP. The retention times of chikusetsusaponin IVa and chikusetsusaponin V peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[50, 9, 1927, 259])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Panacis Majoris Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Panacis Majoris Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.45</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.51</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,chikusetsusaponinV)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(chikusetsusaponinIVa)</td><td>1.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[330, 1099, 1820, 1639])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Panacis Majoris Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.9%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.7%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/panacis-majoris-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Panacis Majoris Rhizoma","la":"Panacis Majoris Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/paridis-rhizoma-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Paridis Rhizoma v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[509, 1105, 714, 1665])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[772, 1122, 1055, 1655])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1084, 994, 1413, 1259])\n\nB 1cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1110, 1362, 1385, 1567])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1127, 1617, 1326, 1759])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz.\n\nA. Dorsal view B. Ventral view\n\nC. Enlarged ventral view D. Apex view\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section\n\n1. Sunken scar of stem 2. Warty scar of fibrous root 3. Internodes\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[609, 155, 753, 205])"},"sections":[{"name":"28","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[509, 1105, 714, 1665])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[772, 1122, 1055, 1655])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1084, 994, 1413, 1259])\n\nB 1cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1110, 1362, 1385, 1567])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1127, 1617, 1326, 1759])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz.\n\nA. Dorsal view B. Ventral view\n\nC. Enlarged ventral view D. Apex view\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section\n\n1. Sunken scar of stem 2. Warty scar of fibrous root 3. Internodes\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[609, 155, 753, 205])"},{"name":"28","text":"Official name: Paridis Rhizoma\n\nChinese name: 重楼\n\nChinese phonetic name: Chonglou"},{"name":"28","text":"Paridis Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz. or Paris polyphylla var. chinensis (Franch.) Hara (Liliaceae $ ^{*} $ ). The rhizome is collected in autumn, freed from fibrous root, washed clean, dried under the sun to obtain Paridis Rhizoma.\n\nBased on the chemical profile differences among the 2 species, they are separated into 2 parts under the collective name \"Paridis Rhizoma\".\n\nPart I Dried rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz."},{"name":"28","text":"Nodular cylindrical, somewhat curved, 4.5-12 cm in length, 12-40 mm in diameter. Outer surface yellowish-brown or rarely greyish-brown and relatively smooth. Internode length 0.5-6 mm; with densely lamellar and prominent annular striations. Distinct nodes with elliptic sunken scars on one side; sparsely rare fibrous roots or warty scars of fibrous roots on the other side. Scale leaves and remains of stems found at the apex. Texture hard, fracture surface smooth, white to pale brown, powderly or horny. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and numbing (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[33, 15, 1935, 257])"},{"name":"28","text":"Epidermis consists of 1-2 layers of cells, with lignified thick wall, arranged irregularly, slightly wavy, and mostly tangentially elongated. The epidermal cells of thicker rhizomes often fallen off; metaderm usually unseen due to fallen off. Cortex cells square, polygonal, and uniform in size. Endodermis cells unseen. Raphides of calcium oxalate, usually found in mucous cells which round-like and with same size comparing to the surrounding parenchymatous cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Leaf trace vascular bundles occasionally scattered in cortex. Few mucous cells found in vascular cylinder. The ratio of the width of cortex to the radius of vascular cylinder around 1.5:1. Ground tissue contains numerous starch granules. Amphivasal vascular bundles 20-25, xylem vessels polygonal, lignified, continuously or intermittently arranged in ring shape on the outside of the phloem. Phloem cells small and often squeezed (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"28","text":"Colour off white. Starch granules numerous, simple, subrounded, long elliptic, oval or reniform in shape, 3-18 $ \\mu $m in diameter; with dot-shaped, crack-like, herringbone and star-shaped hilums; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Raphides of calcium oxalate scattered or in bundles, 95-201 $ \\mu $m in length; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells yellowish-brown, subsquare, polygonal or sub-rectangular with thick wall of 15-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter, sometimes connected with parenchymatous cells. Vessels mostly scalariform and reticulate, light yellow or transparent, 10-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[45, 14, 1920, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 422, 1742, 1989])"},{"name":"28","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the polarized light)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Leaf trace vascular bundles 5. Mucous cell 6. Xylem 7. Phloem\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[330, 448, 973, 930])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[327, 958, 971, 1429])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1013, 448, 1656, 1436])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[327, 1476, 969, 1916])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1014, 1482, 1330, 1907])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1358, 1477, 1653, 1912])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n3. Epidermal cells 4. Vessels (4-1 scalariform vessels, 4-2 reticulate vessels)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[50, 38, 1944, 257])"},{"name":"28","text":"Chonglou saponin I (Diosgenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2)-[\\alpha $ -L-arabinofuranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow4)]-\\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/Polyphyllin D) standard solution Weigh 0.1 mg of chonglou saponin I CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol. Chonglou saponin II (Diosgenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2)-[\\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow4)-\\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow4)]-\\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/Asperin) standard solution Weigh 0.1 mg of chonglou saponin II CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol. Chonglou saponin VII (Pennogenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2)-[\\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow4)-\\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow4)]-\\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/Dioscinin) standard solution Weigh 0.1 mg of chonglou saponin VII CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol. Chonglouoside H (Pennogenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2)-[\\alpha $ -L-arabinofuranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow4)]-\\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside) standard solution Weigh 0.1 mg of chonglouoside H CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"28","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol, ethyl acetate and water (30:22:15:3, v/v)."},{"name":"28","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"28","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"28","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chonglou saponin I standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), chonglou saponin II standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), chonglou saponin VII standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), chonglouoside H standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 422, 1011, 1030])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 1152, 1010, 1867])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[45, 8, 1942, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 431, 1010, 1141])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1277, 1011, 1851])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of\n\n</div>\n\n(i) chonglou saponin I (diosgenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ - $ [\\alpha $ -L-arabinofuranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow 4) ] $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/polyphyllin D)\n\n(ii) chonglou saponin II (diosgenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ [\\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow4) $ - $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow4) $ ]- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/asperin)\n\n(iii) chonglou saponin VII (pennogenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow2) $ - $ [\\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow4) $ - $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow4) $ ]- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/dioscinin) and\n\n(iv) chonglouoside H (pennogenin 3-O- $ \\{\\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ - $ [\\alpha $ -Larabinofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 4) $ ]- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside} )\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[378, 378, 941, 1319])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of dried rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz. extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Chonglou saponin I standard solution 2. Chonglouoside H standard solution\n\n3. Chonglou saponin II standard solution 4. Chonglou saponin VII standard solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chonglou saponin I, chonglou saponin II, chonglou saponin VII and chonglouoside H (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[56, 32, 1941, 259])"},{"name":"28","text":"Chonglou saponin I (Diosgenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- (1 $ \\rightarrow $ 2)-[ $ \\alpha $ -L-arabinofuranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow 4) ] $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/Polyphyllin D) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of chonglou saponin I CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nChonglou saponin II (Diosgenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ - $ [ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl $ (1\\rightarrow 4) $ - $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 4) $ ]- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/Asperin) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of chonglou saponin II CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nChonglou saponin VII (Pennogenin 3-O- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- (1 $ \\rightarrow $ 2)-[ $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- (1 $ \\rightarrow $ 4)- $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- (1 $ \\rightarrow $ 4)]- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside/Dioscinin) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of chonglou saponin VII CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nChonglouoside H (Pennogenin 3-O-{ $ \\alpha $ -L-rhamnopyranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 2) $ -[ $ \\alpha $ -L-arabinofuranosyl- $ (1\\rightarrow 4) $ ]- $ \\beta $ -D-glucopyranoside} ) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of chonglouoside H CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"28","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue for two times each with 10 mL of methanol. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"28","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 40^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.55 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>64→55</td><td>36→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-12</td><td>55→48</td><td>45→52</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[19, 3, 1929, 260])"},{"name":"28","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chonglou saponin I Std-FP, chonglou saponin II Std-FP, chonglou saponin VII Std-FP and chonglouoside H Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chonglou saponin I, chonglou saponin II, chonglou saponin VII and chonglouoside H should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chonglou saponin I, chonglou saponin II, chonglou saponin VII and chonglouoside H peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chonglou saponin I, chonglou saponin II, chonglou saponin VII and chonglouoside H peaks should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"28","text":"Separately inject chonglou saponin I Std-FP, chonglou saponin II Std-FP, chonglou saponin VII Std-FP, chonglouoside H Std-FP and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chonglou saponin I, chonglou saponin II, chonglou saponin VII and chonglouoside H peaks in the chromatograms of chonglou saponin I Std-FP, chonglou saponin II Std-FP, chonglou saponin VII Std-FP, chonglouoside H Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chonglou saponin I, chonglou saponin II, chonglou saponin VII and chonglouoside H peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chonglou saponin I Std-FP, chonglou saponin II Std-FP, chonglou saponin VII Std-FP and chonglouoside H Std-FP. The retention times of chonglou saponin I, chonglou saponin II, chonglou saponin VII and chonglouoside H peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[48, 6, 1933, 261])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz. extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,chonglou saponin VII)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(chonglouoside H)</td><td>1.09</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(chonglou saponin II)</td><td>1.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(chonglou saponin I)</td><td>2.17</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[334, 1037, 1822, 1584])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Paris polyphylla var. yunnanensis (Franch.) Hand.-Mazz. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[42, 38, 1887, 255])"},{"name":"28","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.4%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"28","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12%."},{"name":"28","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14%.\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[46, 33, 1918, 261])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/paridis-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Paridis Rhizoma","la":"Paridis Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/perillae-caulis-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Perillae Caulis v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[616, 1205, 1426, 1896])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Perillae Caulis\n\nA. Perillae Caulis B. Slices\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[616, 1205, 1426, 1896])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Perillae Caulis\n\nA. Perillae Caulis B. Slices\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Perillae Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 紫蘇梗\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zisugeng"},{"name":"12","text":"Perillae Caulis is the dried stem of Perilla frutescens (L.) Britt. (Lamiaceae). The stem is collected after fruit has ripened in autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun or cut into slices while still fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Perillae Caulis."},{"name":"12","text":"Quadrangular in transverse section, with four obtuse angles, the length varies, 5-15 mm in diameter. Externally purplish-green to purplish-brown or brownish-yellow, with longitudinal furrows and fine longitudinal striations, nodes slightly swollen, bearing opposite branch scars and leaf scars. Texture light and hard, fracture lobed. Stem slices usually rhomboidal, 2-5 mm thick, wood yellowish-white, radiate striations fine and dense, the pith white, lax or falling off. Odour slightly aromatic; taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, tangentially elongated. Hypodermal collenchyma present at angular regions. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells. Pericyclic fibre bundles arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem narrow. Cambium indistinct. In the xylem, vessels are scattered singly or in groups, arranged radially; xylem rays consists of 1 to several rows of cells. Pith consists of large parenchymatous cells, with raphides of calcium oxalate occasionally present (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-white to greyish-green. Small raphides of calcium oxalate present mainly in parenchymatous cells, 2-16 $ \\mu m $ long (occasionally up to 28 $ \\mu m $); bright white under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres abundant, slender, 8-45 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with a large lumen; bright white under the polarized microscope. Pericyclic fibres fusiform, 10-46 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, sometimes with distinct pit canals; white to orange or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Parenchymatous cells of xylem rectangular, with distinct pits and pit canals, slightly lignified. Epidermal cells brownish-yellow, polygonal in surface view. Glandular hairs occasionally found, consisting of unicellular head and short stalk. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted and spiral, 8-81 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[34, 99, 210, 198])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[253, 432, 1736, 1938])"},{"name":"12","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Raphides of calcium oxalate in parenchyma\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermal collenchyma 3. Pericyclic fibre bundle 4. Cortex\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n茜草\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nPerillae Caulis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[264, 443, 1716, 1987])"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Xylem fibre 3. Pericyclic fibres\n\n4. Parenchymatous cells of xylem 5. Epidermal cells 6. Glandular hair\n\n7. Vessels (7-1 bordered-pitted vessel, 7-2 spiral vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 7, 1903, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and formic acid (5:4:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately rosmarinic acid standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of rosmarinic acid.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[76, 9, 1972, 256])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 373, 1074, 662])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of rosmarinic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (70%). Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>88→70</td><td>12→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>70→40</td><td>30→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[42, 5, 1908, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of rosmarinic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of rosmarinic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from rosmarinic acid peak should not be less than 90000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of rosmarinic acid peak in the chromatogram of rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify rosmarinic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The retention times of rosmarinic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Perillae Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Perillae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,rosmarinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[291, 377, 1858, 1026])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Perillae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 10.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 6, 1904, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/perillae-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Perillae Caulis","la":"Perillae Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/perillae-folium-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Perillae Folium v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[375, 824, 1546, 2012])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A Photograph of Perillae Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Perillae Folium B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf C. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[375, 824, 1546, 2012])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A Photograph of Perillae Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Perillae Folium B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf C. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Perillae Folium\n\nChinese name: 紫蘇葉\n\nChinese phonetic name: Zisuye"},{"name":"14","text":"Perillae Folium is the dried leaf of Perilla frutescens (L.) Britton (Lamiaceae). The leaf is collected in summer when foliage growing luxuriantly, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Perillae Folium."},{"name":"14","text":"Mostly crumpled, rolled and broken, oval or broadly ovate as whole, 2.9-14.2 cm in length, 1.9-12.7 cm in width. Apex acuminate or acute, sometimes short caudate, base rounded or broadly cuneate, margin crenate. Both surfaces purple or the upper green and the lower purple, scattered with greyish-white hairs, relatively denser along veins, and occurring numerous dented and dotted glandular scales on the lower surface. Petioles 0.5-8.0 cm in length, 1-4 mm in diameter, purple, purplish-red to purplish-green, covered with long hair. Texture fragile. Odour fragrant; taste slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of tiny and flattened cells, with walls slightly thickened. Non-glandular hair consists of 2-8 cells, mostly present in main vein. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of cylindrical cells, containing clusters of calcium oxalate. Spongy tissue consists of several layers of loosely arranged cells. Clusters of calcium oxalate extremely small, 2-10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, scattering in mesophyll cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Raphides of calcium oxalate extremely small, scattered in parenchyma. Vascular bundle of midrib crescent-shaped. Xylem vessels arranged radially. Phloem scattered with a few of fibres. Collenchyma located in the inner sides of upper and lower epidermis of midrib. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of relatively small and irregular cells with stomata and a few glandular hairs. Glandular scales and small glandular hairs occasionally found on the lower surface of epidermis (Fig. 2).\n\nPoria\n\n沒藥\n\n茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁"},{"name":"14","text":"Colour brownish-green to dark green. Small glandular hairs consist of 1-2 celled head, stalk unicellular, 22-32 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Glandular scales consist of 6-8 celled heads, flatten-rounded or subrounded, stalk unicellular and short, 54-83 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, containing yellow oil droplets, irregularly triangular in shape after broken. Non-glandular hairs consist of 2-8 celled heads, 27-52 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, sickle-shaped curved, apex acute or slightly obtuse, occasionally with shrunken middle cells, some cells containing red to purplish-red contents; bright yellow or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Raphides of calcium oxalate extremely small, scattered in parenchyma; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Upper epidermal cells irregular, with walls sinuous and moniliform thickened, cutinized striations distinct, stomata present on both upper and lower epidermis, most of the stomata on the lower epidermis. Lower epidermal cells with walls sinuous, stomata mostly diacytic. Clusters of calcium oxalate extremely small, 2-10 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, scattering in mesophyll cells; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mostly spiral, and scalariform, 10-41 $ \\mu $ m and 7-37 $ \\mu $ m in diameter respectively. Fibres single or in bundles, 15-34 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; bright yellow to polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n明黛参 Changii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 398, 1731, 1967])"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified non-glandular hair\n\nD. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Raphides of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\nF. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nH. Glandular scale I. Glandular hair\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Raphides of calcium oxalate 7. Xylem 8. Phloem\n\n9. Collenchyma 10. Glandular scale 11. Glandular hair 12. Lower epidermis\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\nLycii Fructus\n\n枸杞子\n\n紅豆蔻\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸隶仁\n\nPerillae Folium\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[300, 548, 1720, 2097])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Small glandular hair 2. Glandular scale 3. Non-glandular hair\n\n4. Raphides of calcium oxalate 5. Upper epidermal cells\n\n6. Lower epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n8. Vessels (8-1 spiral, 8-2 scalariform) 9. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[28, 17, 1958, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Eugenol standard solution\n\nPipette 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of eugenol CRS (Fig. 4) to 2 mL of n-hexane.\n\n(S)-Perillaldehyde standard solution\n\nPipette 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of (S)-perillaldehyde CRS (Fig. 4) to 1 mL of n-hexane."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (28% , v/v), acetone and petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} $ C) (0.1:2:10, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 80 mL of sulphuric acid to 20 mL of ethanol and dissolve 1 g of vanillin. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 125 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 2-L round-bottomed flask, then add 900 mL of water and a few glass beads, shake and mix well. Connect the round-bottomed flask to a volatile oil determination tube. Connect to the reflux condenser and add water through the top of reflux condenser until the graduated tube of volatile oil determination tube is filled and overflows to the round-bottomed flask. Heat the flask gently until boiling by using an electric heating jacket or other appropriate means. Continue the gentle boiling for about 5 h until the volume of oil does not increase. Stop heating, allow it to stand for a while. Open the stopcork at the lower part of volatile oil determination tube and run off the water layer slowly until the oily layer is 5 mm above the zero mark. Allow to stand for at least 1 h, open the stopcock again, run off the remaining water layer carefully until the oily layer is just on the zero mark. Collect the oily layer. Pipette 40 $ \\mu $L of the oily layer and dissolve in 1 mL of n-hexane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately eugenol standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), (S)-perillaldehyde standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 384, 706, 590])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[381, 833, 506, 1105])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1336, 959, 1592])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1834, 1031, 2098])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) eugenol (ii) (S)-perillaldehyde (iii) rosmarinic acid and (iv) scutellarin\n\n</div>\n\n明黛参 Changii Radix\n\nHippophae Fructus沙棘Lycii Fructus枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥\n\n茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[378, 371, 892, 1211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of the volatile oil obtained from Perillae Folium observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. (S)-Perillaldehyde standard solution 2. Eugenol standard solution 3. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of eugenol and (S)-perillaldehyde (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nScutellarin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of scutellarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction two more times and combine the supernatants. Wash the residue with ethanol ( 70% ). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 35^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.5 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>3-6</td><td>88→80</td><td>12→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>6-10</td><td>80→77</td><td>20→23</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of rosmarinic acid Std-FP and scutellarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of rosmarinic acid and scutellarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of rosmarinic acid and scutellarin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from rosmarinic acid and scutellarin peaks should not be less than 90000 and 15000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject rosmarinic acid Std-FP, scutellarin Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of rosmarinic acid and scutellarin peaks in the chromatograms of rosmarinic acid Std-FP, scutellarin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify rosmarinic acid and scutellarin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of rosmarinic acid Std-FP and scutellarin Std-FP. The retention times of rosmarinic acid and scutellarin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Perillae Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Perillae Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3(scutellarin)</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,rosmarinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[329, 1639, 1821, 2044])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Perillae Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.1%\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 13%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 11%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/perillae-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Perillae Folium","la":"Perillae Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/persicae-semen-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Persicae Semen v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[617, 806, 1344, 1453])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of the seeds of Prunus persica (L.) Batsch A. Seeds B. Lateral view of seed C. Cotyledon of seed\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[635, 1737, 1386, 2416])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the seeds of Prunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Seeds B. Lateral view of seed C. Cotyledon of seed\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1901, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[617, 806, 1344, 1453])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of the seeds of Prunus persica (L.) Batsch A. Seeds B. Lateral view of seed C. Cotyledon of seed\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[635, 1737, 1386, 2416])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of the seeds of Prunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Seeds B. Lateral view of seed C. Cotyledon of seed\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1901, 262])"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Persicae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 桃仁\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Taoren"},{"name":"16","text":"Persicae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Prunus persica (L.) Batsch or Prunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch. (Rosaceae). The ripe fruit is collected, sarcocarp and endocarp are removed, the seed gathered and then dried under the sun to obtain Persicae Semen."},{"name":"16","text":"Prunus persica (L.) Batsch: Prolate-ovate, 1.1-2.1 cm long, 0.6-1.4 cm wide, 2-4 mm thick. Externally yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, with numerous granular protuberance. One end acute, expanded in the middle, the other end obtuse-rounded and slightly oblique, with relatively thin edge. A short linear hilum occurring at the apex and a relatively indistinct and slightly dark chalaza at the round end, with many longitudinal vascular bundles radiated from the chalaza. Testa thin, cotyledons 2, white and oily. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nPrunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch.: Subovoid, relatively smaller but thicker, 0.8-1.7 cm long, 0.6-1.1 cm wide and 4-7 mm thick [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Testa consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, scattered with stone cells throughout. Vascular bundles pass through testa. Perisperm cells consist of 1 layer of cells, shrunken, underneath testa. Endosperm consists of 1 to several layers of rectangular to square cells, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets. Cotyledons consist of polygonal parenchymatous cells, containing aleurone grains, crystals of calcium oxalate and oil droplets. Primary vascular bundles scattered among cotyledons. Crystals of calcium oxalate, rosette-aggregated, scattered along testa and cotyledons [Fig. 2 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[69, 8, 1972, 259])"},{"name":"16","text":"Prunus persica (L.) Batsch: Colour yellowish-white. Stone cells pale yellow to brownish-yellow, conchoidal, helmet-shaped, bow-shaped or elliptic in lateral view, 49-218 $ \\mu $m high, 32-208 $ \\mu $m wide at the base, with one side wall thicker and more densely striated than the other side, the thicker side of the cell wall about 8-40 $ \\mu $m thick; cell subrounded, rounded, polygonal or subsquare in surface view, with large and dense pits at the bottom wall. Testa cells orangish-red, subrounded to polygonal. Endosperm cells contain oil droplets, walls slightly thickened. Cotyledon cells contain small crystals of calcium oxalate, aleurone grains and oil droplets [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nPrunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch.: Stone cells conchoidal, oblong, elliptic or stripe-shaped in lateral view, 43-233 $ \\mu $m high, 26-217 $ \\mu $m wide, with one side wall thicker and more densely striated than the other side, the thicker side of the wall 9-35 $ \\mu $m thick; cell subrounded, subhexagonal, prolately polygonal or subsquare in surface view, with smaller pits in the unevenly thickened bottom walls [Fig. 3 (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[280, 501, 1011, 853])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[357, 944, 1017, 1353])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1063, 556, 1686, 1337])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[357, 1445, 1008, 1841])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1065, 1340, 1685, 1843])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Prunus persica (L.) Batsch\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cotyledon\n\nD. Cotyledon (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Testa 3. Vascular bundles 4. Perisperm 5. Endosperm\n\n6. Cotyledon 7. Primary vascular bundle 8. Crystal of calcium oxalate\n\n桂枝 Cinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nPersicae Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[239, 430, 1747, 1995])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Prunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cotyledon D. Cotyledon (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Testa 3. Vascular bundles 4. Perisperm 5. Endosperm\n\n6. Cotyledon 7. Primary vascular bundle 8. Crystal of calcium oxalate\n\n西紅花 Croci Stigma\n\nPersicae Semen\n\nEupatorii Herba佩蘭\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\n桃仁 Persicae Semen\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\nSpatholobi Caulis\n\n羅布麻葉\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[399, 453, 777, 903])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[795, 456, 1252, 892])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1280, 454, 1588, 903])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[397, 929, 770, 1361])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[789, 934, 1253, 1362])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1274, 934, 1588, 1361])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[398, 1386, 1059, 1636])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1079, 1390, 1583, 1621])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[396, 1658, 981, 1984])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1006, 1652, 1587, 1990])"},{"name":"16","text":"1. Stone cell (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 with pits) 2. Stone cell (2-1 in lateral view, 2-2 with pits)\n\n3. Stone cell (3-1 in base view, 3-2 with pits) 4. Testa cells\n\n5. Endosperm cells containing oil droplets\n\n6. Cotyledon cells containing crystals of calcium oxalate and oil droplets\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nRubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nicarpium大腹皮\n\n桂枝\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nLophatheri Herba淡竹葉\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\n車前子\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\n紫苑\n\nPersicae Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[344, 452, 755, 806])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[776, 452, 1290, 809])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1313, 455, 1645, 806])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[346, 850, 753, 1159])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[774, 850, 1295, 1167])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1321, 850, 1645, 1163])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[346, 1187, 988, 1645])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1003, 1196, 1645, 1646])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[348, 1671, 571, 1982])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[587, 1667, 1111, 1983])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1128, 1667, 1645, 1986])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Prunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch.\n\n1. Stone cell (1-1 in lateral view, 1-2 with pits) 2. Stone cells (2-1 in lateral view, 2-2 with pits)\n\n3. Stone cells (3-1 in lateral view, 3-2 with pits) 4. Stone cells (in base view) 5. Testa cells\n\n6. Endosperm cells containing oil droplets\n\n7. Cotyledon cells containing crystals of calcium oxalate and oil droplets\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 7, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"16","text":"Amygdalin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of amygdalin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol and water (40:15:6, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Mix cautiously 25 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v) into 25 mL of ice-cold glacial acetic acid. Add 2.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde. Add further 50 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter the mixture and wash the residue with 10 mL of petroleum ether. Discard the filtrate. Dry the residue in air. Transfer the dried residue to a 25-mL conical flask and add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately amygdalin standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of amygdalin.\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\n紫苑\n\nPersicae Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[374, 368, 1164, 1212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) amygdalin (ii) methyl palmitate and (iii) palmitic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"16","text":"Amygdalin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of amygdalin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture and wash the residue with 5 mL of petroleum ether. Discard the filtrate. Dry the residue in air. Transfer the dried residue to a 25-mL conical flask and add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.5-1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[28, 3, 1905, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Flow rate(mL/min)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>0→50</td><td>100→50</td><td>0.5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-15</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>0.5→1.0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of amygdalin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of amygdalin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of amygdalin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from amygdalin peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject amygdalin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of amygdalin peak in the chromatogram of amygdalin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify amygdalin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of amygdalin Std-FP. The retention times of amygdalin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Persicae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[65, 11, 1965, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Persicae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.98</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, amygdalin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[289, 759, 1859, 1344])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Prunus persica (L.) Batsch extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[286, 1482, 1851, 2096])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Prunus davidiana (Carr.) Franch. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[35, 17, 1907, 263])"},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%.\n\n5. 8 Acid Value (Appendix XIV): not more than 10.0."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/persicae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Persicae Semen","la":"Persicae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/peucedani-decursivi-radix-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Peucedani Decursivi Radix v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Peucedani Decursivi Radix\n\nChinese Name: 紫花前胡\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zihuaqianhu"},{"name":"11","text":"Peucedani Decursivi Radix is the dried root of Peucedanum decursivum (Miq.) Maxim. (Apiaceae). The root is collected in autumn and winter when stem and leaves withered, freed of aerial part, soil and rootlet, washed clean, then dried under the sun or at ambient temperature to obtain Peucedani Decursivi Radix."},{"name":"11","text":"Irregular cylindrical, conical or fusiform, slightly twisted, branched at the lower part, 3-18 cm long, 5-22 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to dark brown, with longitudinally wrinkles, rootlet scars and transverse lenticel-like cicatrices. The top part is stubby, frequently with stem scars and fibrous leaf-bases, annular striation occasionally at the upper end. Texture hard, easily broken; fracture uneven, off-white or pale yellow, yellow oil spots scattered in cortex. Odour strongly aromatic; taste slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of 10 or more layers of flattened cells. Cortex narrow, with scattered oil cavities. Phloem broad, cells arranged somewhat densely, frequently with oil cavities, subrounded with 5-10 secretory cells, 42-166 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Phloem ray slightly curved. Cambium in a ring. Xylem shows vessels arranged radially, xylem rays relatively wide. Starch granules abundantly present in the parenchymatous cells (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 9, 1983, 262])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-yellow to yellowish-brown. Starch granules abundant, simple granules subrounded or polygonal, hilum dotted or slit-shaped visible in large granules, 5-20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, compound granules composed of 2-10 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Fibre in bundles or scattered singly, long-strip shaped, 8-35 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, pit canals distinctly visible; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate and scalariform in type, 14-80 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Oil cavities mostly fractured, containing yellowish-brown secretions. Cork cells subpolygonal or irregular (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Nodakenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of nodakenin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone and formic acid (6:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately nodakenin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of nodakenin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[29, 3, 1929, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 449, 1749, 1987])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Peucedani Decursivi Radix\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil cavity\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Oil cavity 4. Phloem ray 5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[31, 17, 1984, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[494, 501, 1495, 1949])\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Fibre 3. Reticulate vessels 4. Scalariform vessels\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Peucedani Decursivi Radix\n\n</div>\n\n5. Fragments of oil cavity 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[33, 2, 1936, 260])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 381, 1024, 663])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of nodakenin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Nodakenin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (14 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.14 mg of nodakenin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 125-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (325 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>60-50</td><td>40-50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-20</td><td>50-35</td><td>50-65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>35-10</td><td>65-90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of nodakenin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of nodakenin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of nodakenin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from nodakenin peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 6, 1978, 262])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject nodakenin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of nodakenin peak in the chromatogram of nodakenin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify nodakenin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of nodakenin Std-FP. The retention times of nodakenin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Peucedani Decursivi Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Peucedani Decursivi Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, nodakenin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2.61</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>4.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>4.23</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 1757, 1725, 2455])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Peucedani Decursivi Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 7, 1927, 260])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Nodakenin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (400 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 4.0 mg of nodakenin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nNodakenin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the nodakenin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 5.2, 10, 15.2, 20, 40 mg/L for nodakenin.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[12, 7, 1984, 263])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/peucedani-decursivi-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Peucedani Decursivi Radix","la":"Peucedani Decursivi Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/peucedani-radix-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Peucedani Radix v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1926, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1926, 256])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Peucedani Radix\n\nChinese Name: 前胡\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qianhu"},{"name":"11","text":"Peucedani Radix is the dried root of Peucedanum praeruptorum Dunn (Apiaceae). The root is collected in winter to next spring, when stem and leaves wither or before floral stem grows, freed of aerial part, soil and rootlet, washed clean, then dried under the sun or at ambient temperature to obtain Peucedani Radix."},{"name":"11","text":"Irregular cylindrical, conical or fusiform, slightly twisted, frequently branched at the lower part, 4-15 cm long, 3-18 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to dark brown, marked with longitudinal furrows or wrinkles and transverse lenticel-like cicatrices. The top part is stubby, frequently with stem scars and fibrous leaf-bases remains at the root stock, with numerous fine annular striations at the upper end. Texture relatively flexible, and hard when dried, easily broken; fracture uneven, pale yellow, numerous brownish-yellow oil spots scattered in cortex, cambium ring brown. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of 10 or more layers of flatted cells. Cortex narrow. Phloem broad, cells at the outside frequently with clefts, oil cavities frequently scattered, 40-330 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Phloem ray slightly curve. Cambium in a ring. Xylem vessels scattered singly or aggregated in groups, radially arranged, xylem rays distinct, with oil cavities scattered in the xylem. Parenchymatous cells contain abundant starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-yellow to yellowish-brown. Starch granules abundant, simple granules\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 5, 1984, 262])\n\nsubrounded or polygonal, 3-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, dotted or slit-shaped hilum visible in large granules; compound granules composed of 2-10 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Fibres in bundles or scattered singly, 86-300 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 11-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Pit sparse, pit canals occasionally visible; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Stone cells singly scattered, subrectangular or elongated ovate, 33-152 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 9-60 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, the wall thick, striation mostly distinct; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate, 12-79 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Oil cavities mostly fractured, containing yellowish-brown or yellowish-green secretions or oil droplets. Cork cells yellowish-brown, subpolygonal, rectangular or triangular (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Praeruptorin A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of praeruptorin A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nPraeruptorin B standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of praeruptorin B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and acetone (10:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[35, 1, 1927, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 416, 1751, 2133])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Peucedani Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil cavity 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Oil cavity 4. Cleft 5. Phloem ray 6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 9, 1984, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[257, 719, 1708, 2193])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Peucedani Radix\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Fibre 3. Stone cell 4. Vessels 5. Fragments of oil cavities 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[44, 3, 1938, 260])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[374, 359, 1953, 1232])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) praeruptorin A and (ii) praeruptorin B\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately praeruptorin A standard solution, praeruptorin B standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of praeruptorin A and praeruptorin B."},{"name":"11","text":"Praeruptorin A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of praeruptorin A CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 15, 1976, 263])\n\nPraeruptorin B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (6 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of praeruptorin B CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 125-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (325 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>25→5</td><td>75→95</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of praeruptorin A Std-FP and praeruptorin B Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of praeruptorin A and praeruptorin B should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of praeruptorin A and praeruptorin B peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from praeruptorin A and paeruptorin B peaks should not be less than 9000 and 11000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject praeruptorin A Std-FP, praeruptorin B Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of praeruptorin A and praeruptorin B peaks in the chromatograms of praeruptorin A Std-FP, praeruptorin B Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify praeruptorin A and praeruptorin B peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of praeruptorin A Std-FP and praeruptorin B Std-FP. The retention times of praeruptorin A and praeruptorin B peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[35, 2, 1932, 259])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Peucedani Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Peucedani Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(praeruptorin A)</td><td>0.67</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.74</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,praeruptorin B)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(praeruptorin E and qianhucoumarin H)</td><td>1.16</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[376, 970, 1741, 1678])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Peucedani Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 21, 1977, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 24.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 26.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/peucedani-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Peucedani Radix","la":"Peucedani Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/phyllanthi-fructus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Phyllanthi Fructus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[558, 987, 1430, 1937])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Phyllanthi Fructus\n\nA. Phyllanthi Fructus B. Longitudinal section C. Outer surface of endocarp D. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[558, 987, 1430, 1937])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Phyllanthi Fructus\n\nA. Phyllanthi Fructus B. Longitudinal section C. Outer surface of endocarp D. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Phyllanthi Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 餘甘子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yuganzi"},{"name":"13","text":"Phyllanthi Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Phyllanthus emblica L. (Euphorbiaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in October to December, foreign matter removed, dried under the sun to obtain Phyllanthi Fructus."},{"name":"13","text":"Spheroidal or flattish-spheroidal, some cracked, 12-20 mm in diameter. Externally brown to dark green, with pale yellow granular protuberance, wrinkles, and 6 indistinct ribs. Fruit stalk about 1 mm long. Exocarp fused with mesocarp, 1-4 mm thick, texture hard and fragile. Endocarp yellowish-white, hard kernel-like, showing 6 indistinct ribs on the surface and several veins striations (vascular bundles) at the upper part of dorsal suture, cracking to 6 valves after dried. Seeds 6, subtriquetrous, brown. Odour slight; taste sour, astringent and followed by sweet (Fig.1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Transverse section subrounded, externally with 6 raised ribs, and divided into 3 loculi by the septum which radiates from the centre. Exocarp consists of 1 layer subrounded epidermal cell, arranged regularly, covered with thick cuticle. Septum consists of several rows of flattened cells, cells relatively small, arranged densely. The 2-7 layers of sclerenchymatous cells located underneath the epidermis, subrounded, elongated-rounded or subpolygonal. Mesocarp relatively thick, consisting of parenchymatous cells, the cells often containing crystals of calcium oxalate, small vascular bundles scattered throughout the mesocarp. Endocarp consists\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[72, 8, 1964, 261])\n\nof sclerenchymatous cells; 4-6 layers of stone cells arranged in a dense layer on the outer side, the cells relatively small, with extremely thickened wall and indistinct lumen; next 5-7 layers of slender, slat-shaped lignified fibres arranged in the middle part, palisade-like; 4-6 layers of subpolygonal sclerenchymatous cells situated in the innermost side, with relatively thickened wall, and with a distinct lumen. Each locule contains 2 subtriangular seeds. Each seed bordered by a testa 2-3 layers of palisade-like cell located between testa and endotesta, arranged densely, with a relatively thickened wall and dense pit canals. Pigment layer very thin, located immediately inside the endotesta; the cells subpolygonal or subrounded, the lumen containing reddish-brown contents. Endosperm cells subpolygonal, containing numerous clusters of calcium oxalate and some starch granules. Cotyledon cells relatively small, subpolygonal, with distinct nuclei (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour brown. Epidermal cells of exocarp yellow to nearly colourless, subpolygonal or subsquare, 25-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, wall slightly thickened. Fibres of endocarp abundant, scattered singly or in groups, nearly colourless, slat-shaped, 10-30 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, wall with sparse pit canals. Pigment cells scattered singly or in groups, subpolygonal or subrounded, lumen containing reddish-brown contents. Sclerenchymatous cells scattered singly or in groups, pale yellow or nearly colourless, subpolygonal or subrounded, 100-163 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 50-88 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide, wall relatively thickened, 13-23 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ thick, pit canals distinct. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups, pale yellow to nearly colourless, subpolygonal or subrounded, 63-100 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 50-75 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide, wall extremely thickened, 25-38 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ thick, lumen indistinct, pit canals fine and dense. Palisade cells of testa pale yellow to nearly colourless, polygonal in surface view, cylindrical or subpolygonal in the section view, arranged densely, 125-175 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 50-100 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide, wall thickened, pit canals extremely fine and dense, lumen large. Crystals of calcium oxalate relatively abundant, polyhedral, rhombic, subsquare or irregular, some broken; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate found in endosperm cells, approximately equal in size, 5-9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[39, 100, 203, 161])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[399, 477, 979, 969])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[415, 1013, 971, 1934])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1062, 457, 1661, 1500])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1071, 1596, 1536, 1928])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibre of endocarp D. Palisade tissue of testa\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Sclerenchyma of exocarp 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Septum 6. Stone cells of endocarp 7. Fibres of endocarp\n\n8. Sclerenchyma of endocarp 9. Testa 10. Palisade tissue of testa\n\n11. Endotesta 12. Pigment layer 13. Endosperm 14. Cotyledon\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Phyllanthi Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[257, 473, 1693, 2018])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Phyllanthi Fructus\n\n1. Epidermal cells of exocarp 2. Fibres of endocarp 3. Pigment cells\n\n4. Sclerenchymatous cells 5. Stone cells 6. Palisade cells of testa (6-1 in surface view, 6-2 in section view)\n\n7. Crystals of calcium oxalate 8. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 7, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Gallic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of gallic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nEllagic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of ellagic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, formic acid, ethyl acetate and water (7:2.5:2:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately gallic acid standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), ellagic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5 \\mathrm{~} ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of gallic acid and ellagic acid.\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Phyllanthi Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 370, 832, 697])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[444, 779, 936, 1166])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) gallic acid and (ii) ellagic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Gallic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of gallic acid CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nEllagic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of ellagic acid CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[37, 4, 1905, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (273 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>5</td><td>95</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-35</td><td>5→50</td><td>95→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of gallic acid Std-FP and ellagic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of gallic acid and ellagic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of gallic acid and ellagic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from gallic acid and ellagic acid peaks should not be less than 4000 and 90000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject gallic acid Std-FP, ellagic acid Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of gallic acid and ellagic acid peaks in the chromatograms of gallic acid Std-FP, ellagic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify gallic acid and ellagic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of gallic acid Std-FP and ellagic acid Std-FP. The retention times of gallic acid and ellagic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 7, 1968, 260])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Phyllanthi Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Phyllanthi Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(gallic acid)</td><td>0.36</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.50</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.56</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,ellagic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[360, 1016, 1816, 1551])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Phyllanthi Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[33, 19, 1906, 261])\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/phyllanthi-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Phyllanthi Fructus","la":"Phyllanthi Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/phyllanthi-urinariae-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[381, 807, 1560, 1962])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba\n\nB. Magnified image of branch and capsules\n\nC. Magnified leaf (left: upper surface, right: lower surface)\n\nD. Magnified capsule (left: top view, right: bottom view)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[28, 44, 1957, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[381, 807, 1560, 1962])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba\n\nB. Magnified image of branch and capsules\n\nC. Magnified leaf (left: upper surface, right: lower surface)\n\nD. Magnified capsule (left: top view, right: bottom view)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[28, 44, 1957, 258])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official name: Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba\n\nChinese name: 葉下珠\n\nChinese phonetic name: Yexiazhu"},{"name":"15","text":"Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba is the dried whole plant of Phyllanthus urinaria L. (Euphorbiaceae $ ^{*} $ ). The plant is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Varying in length. Fibrous roots numerous. Stem yellowish-green to brownish-green, base brown, young one brownish-red, cylindrical, 0.4-3.3 mm in diameter, surface with fine longitudinal wrinkles, hollow, texture brittle. Leaf small, papyraceous, easily fallen, subsessile, oblong to obovate, 5-21 mm in long, 1.5-9 mm in width, apex mucronulate or obtuse, base oblique; upper surface green, lower surface greyish-green, main vein small. Capsule small, infra-axillary, triangular flat-spherical, green, brownish-yellow to reddish-brown, 1.2-2.8 mm in diameter, surface with small protuberance, with persistent sepal. Odour slightly fragrant, taste slightly bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Root: Cork consists of serval layers of cells. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymayous cells. Phloem narrow. Xylem occupies the majority of root, consisting of vessels and fibres. Xylem ray consists of 1-2 rows of cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Hypodermis consists of 1 layer of relatively large cells. Cortex narrow. Pericycle fibre in bundles, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem narrow. Xylem consists of vessels and fibres. Xylem ray consists of 1-3 rows of cells. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells, hollow in the centre [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 38, 1953, 251])\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis each consists of 1 layer of cells, some cells outer wall papillary protruded, non-glandular hair sometimes observed at the margin of the leaf. Mesophyll consists of 1 layer of palisade cells and several layers of spongy cells; palisade cells appear across the upper part of the midrib, some containing clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregates. Vascular bundle of midrib collateral, with fibre bundles on the lower side. Collenchyma consists of 1-2 layers of cells, present at the inner side of the lower epidermis of midrib [Fig. 2 (iii)].\n\nCapsule: With six locules, each containing 1 seed. Exocarp consists of 1 layer of cells. Mesocarp consists of serval layers of subrounded cells. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of palisade cells. Testa consists of 1 layer of wall thickened cells, inner side with 1 layer of pigment cells. Endosperm consists of parenchymayous cells [Fig. 2 (iv)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour brownish-green. Clusters of calcium oxalate present in mesophyll cells, parenchymatous cells, or scattered, 6-42 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Lower epidermal cells of leaf irregular in surface view, anticlinal walls deeply sinuate, stomata abundant, mainly paracytic, some cells outer wall papillary protuberance. Upper epidermal cells of leaf irregular in surface view, anticlinal walls deeply sinuate, relatively few cells with papillary protuberance on outer wall. Sclerenchymatous cells of testa linear, subpolygonal or subrectangular in surface view, with fine and dense pits and pit canals; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of stem rectangular or elongated-polygonal in surface view, stomata paracytic. Non-glandular hair consists of 1-5 cells, unicellular or multicellular 24-209 $ \\mu $ m in long, 18-67 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with cuticle striations on surface. Epidermal cells of persistent sepal subpolygonal or subrectangular in surface view, wall slightly thickened. Pericycle fibre 4-23 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, wall thickened, lumen narrow or linear; bright white or orange under the polarized microscope. Mesocarp cells subrounded or subpolygonal in surface view, walls thickened at the corners; often linked with vessels. Cork cells rectangular or polygonal in surface view, brown. Vessels mainly spiral type, 3-39 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 41, 1967, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 405, 1733, 2122])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Xylem 5. Xylem ray\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1059, 91, 1142, 133])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[751, 145, 906, 186])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1938, 138])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[245, 486, 1705, 1997])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericycle fibres\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermis 3. Cortex 4. Pericycle fibre 5. Phloem\n\n6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray 8. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[23, 44, 1968, 258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[237, 405, 1727, 2134])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section illustration of margin D. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Cluster of calcium oxalate 3. Palisade tissue 4. Vascular bundle of midrib\n\n5. Spongy tissue 6. Fibre bundle 7. Collenchyma 8. Lower epidermis 9. Non-glandular hair\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1692, 55, 1939, 138])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[294, 1004, 893, 1546])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[930, 829, 1684, 1675])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (iv) Microscopic features of transverse section of capsule of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Endocarp 4. Testa 5. Endosperm\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1722, 49, 1851, 96])\n\n鴨跖草\n\n京大戟\n\nNigellae Semen\n\n石斛\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[236, 433, 1752, 2125])"},{"name":"15","text":"1. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n2. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with paracytic stomata (papillary protuberance $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Upper epidermal cells of leaf 4. Sclerenchymatous cells of testa 5. Epidermal cells of stem\n\n6. Non-glandular hairs (6-1 unicellular, 6-2 multicellular) 7. Epidermal cells of persistent sepal\n\n8. Pericycle fibre 9. Mesocarp cells 10. Cork cells 11. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[20, 40, 1945, 243])"},{"name":"15","text":"Corilagin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of corilagin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 4 mL of water."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, formic acid and methanol (5:1.5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of water. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately corilagin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[383, 1558, 996, 1958])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of corilagin\n\n</div>\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[377, 372, 786, 1211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Corilagin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of corilagin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Corilagin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of corilagin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of methanol (50%). Reflux the mixture at about $ 1 0 0 ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the extract to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 3 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Wash the residue for two times each with 10 mL of methanol (50%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.22- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[22, 40, 1950, 241])"},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (266 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 40^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.5%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-4</td><td>95→91</td><td>5→9</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>4-9</td><td>91</td><td>9</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>9-14</td><td>91→86</td><td>9→14</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>14-20</td><td>86</td><td>14</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of corilagin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of corilagin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of corilagin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from corilagin peak should not be less than 19000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject corilagin Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of corilagin peak in the chromatogram of corilagin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify corilagin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of corilagin Std-FP. The retention times of corilagin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[23, 43, 1965, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,corilagin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[334, 868, 1823, 1326])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): The CMM shall meet the requirements for arsenic, lead and mercury as specified in Appendix V. For cadmium, Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba should meet the specified limit of not more than 3.3 mg/kg, when the CMM will be processed as a decoction in the final consumption form; otherwise, the limit for cadmium specified in Appendix V shall be applied.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Total ash: not more than 12.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/phyllanthi-urinariae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba","la":"Phyllanthi Urinariae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/picrasmae-ramulus-et-folium-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[378, 839, 1528, 2016])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nA. Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium B. Branch with leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of the upper surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of the lower surface of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of the cut surface of branch\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[378, 839, 1528, 2016])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nA. Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium B. Branch with leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of the upper surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of the lower surface of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of the cut surface of branch\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nChinese name: 苦木\n\nChinese phonetic name: Kumu"},{"name":"13","text":"Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium is the dried branch and leaf of Picrasma quassioides (D. Don) Benn. (Simaroubaceae). The branch and leaf are collected during summer to autumn, then dried under the sun to obtain Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium."},{"name":"13","text":"Branch cylindrical, varies in length, 1-30 mm in diameter; externally greyish-green or brownish green to dark green, with numerous dotted white lenticels; texture fragile, easily broken; fracture uneven, pale yellow, annual rings distinct; pith yellow, relatively large. Leaves alternate, oddpinnate, easily fallen off; texture fragile, usually broken; intact leaflets ovate-oblong or ovate-lanceolate, nearly sessile, 3-16 cm in length, 1-6 cm in width; apex acute and base oblique or slightly rounded, margin crenate; both surfaces green, lower surface sometimes pale purplish-red, slightly pubescent on midrib. Odour slight; taste extremely bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Cork consists of several to over 10 layers of cells, cork cells rectangular, arranged densely, with relatively thick wall, slightly lignified. Cortex relatively narrow, consisting of several layers of cells. Parenchymatous cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Fibre bundles scatter between cortex and phloem, relatively large. Cambium ring distinct. Xylem broad; xylem rays consist of 1-2 rows of cells; vessels singly scattered or 2-3 in groups. Pith distinct, cells relatively large, cell walls slightly thickened and lignified [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 38, 1954, 248])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare, thick-walled cells, covered with cuticle. Collenchyma consists of 3-4 layers of cells, located underneath the upper and lower epidermis of the midrib. Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of palisade cells. Spongy cells irregular or subrounded, loosely arranged. Vascular bundles of midrib 2, the principal one collateral, crescent; the small one bicollateral, located over the principal vascular bundles. Vessels several arranged in a row. Phloem cells relatively small, arranged densely and irregularly. Fibres lignified, singly scattered or in bundles, surrounding the vascular bundle. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, scattering in the parenchymatous cells. Lower epidermal cells 1 layer, subrounded or subsquare, relatively small, regularly and densely arranged [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green. Cork cells polygonal. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, in rosette aggregates, 5-41 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Xylem ray cells long, walls slightly thickened and pit canals distinct. Prisms of calcium oxalate numerous, 4-34 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, relatively large, usually broken, 4-78 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Fibres slender, in bundles, 6-32 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Upper epidermal cells polygonal or subsquare, walls straight or slightly wavy. Lower epidermal cells polygonal or irregular in shape, smaller than the upper epidermal cells, walls distinctly wavy; stomata anomocytic, densely arranged (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[631, 42, 772, 113])\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 404, 1736, 2117])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of branch of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium A. Sketch B. Section illustration C-D. Section magnified 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Prism of calcium oxalate 4. Fibre bundle 5. Phloem 6. Phloem ray 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Xylem ray 10. Pith\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1059, 91, 1142, 133])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[751, 145, 906, 186])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1693, 55, 1938, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[256, 416, 1743, 2113])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue 5. Xylem\n\n6. Phleom 7. Fibre 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Lower epidermis\n\nCommelinae Herba\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\nDioscoreae Bulbiferae Rhizoma\n\nDendrobii Caulis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1721, 48, 1852, 96])\n\n京大戟\n\n黑種草子\n\nNigellae Semen\n\n鴨跖草\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n石斛\n\n苦木 Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[345, 433, 745, 795])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[791, 430, 1190, 793])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1240, 427, 1643, 794])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[343, 850, 744, 1208])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[790, 853, 1191, 1206])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1241, 850, 1646, 1208])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[342, 1257, 743, 1609])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[788, 1253, 1195, 1612])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1240, 1253, 1647, 1608])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[500, 1669, 903, 2018])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1077, 1671, 1482, 2026])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Cork cells 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate 3. Xylem ray cells\n\n4. Prisms of calcium oxalate 5. Bordered-pitted vessels 6. Fibre bundle\n\n7. Upper epidermal cells (striation $ \\rightarrow $ ） 8. Lower epidermal cells with anomocytic stoma ( $ \\rightarrow $ ）\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 40, 1944, 242])"},{"name":"13","text":"4,5-Dimethoxycanthin-6-one standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and methanol (7:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[374, 1803, 714, 2029])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one\n\n</div>\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 373, 785, 1202])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. 4,5-Dimethoxycanthin-6-one standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"4,5-Dimethoxycanthin-6-one standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.6 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[28, 40, 1951, 241])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (355 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>90→82</td><td>10→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-25</td><td>82</td><td>18</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-60</td><td>82→50</td><td>18→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one peak should not be less than 180000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one peak in the chromatogram of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one Std-FP. The retention times of 4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 42, 1958, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.62</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,4,5-dimethoxycanthin-6-one)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[337, 894, 1816, 1488])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/picrasmae-ramulus-et-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium","la":"Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/piperis-fructus-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Piperis Fructus v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[611, 672, 1383, 1271])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Piperis Fructus - Black Pepper\n\nA. Black Pepper B. Magnified image of fruit C. Apex D.\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section of fruit\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[627, 1724, 1379, 2394])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of Piperis Fructus - White Pepper\n\nA. White Pepper B. Magnified image of fruit C. Apex D. Base"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[611, 672, 1383, 1271])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of Piperis Fructus - Black Pepper\n\nA. Black Pepper B. Magnified image of fruit C. Apex D.\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section of fruit\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[627, 1724, 1379, 2394])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of Piperis Fructus - White Pepper\n\nA. White Pepper B. Magnified image of fruit C. Apex D. Base"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Piperis Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 胡椒\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hujiao"},{"name":"14","text":"Piperis Fructus is the dried and almost ripe or ripe fruit of Piper nigrum L. (Piperaceae). The fruit is collected when it is dark green (almost ripe), then dried under the sun to obtain \"Black Pepper\". The fruit is also collected when it turns red (ripe), macerate in water for several days, followed by removal of sarcocarp, then dried under the sun to obtain \"White Pepper\"."},{"name":"14","text":"Black Pepper: Spheroidal, 3.5-5.5 mm in diameter. Externally blackish-brown, with raised reticulated wrinkles, a small remnant of style at the apex and a scar of fruit stalk at the base. Texture hard, exocarp may be stripped, endocarp greyish-white to pale yellow. Transverse section yellowish-white, starchy, with a small hollowed space at the centre. Odour aromatic; taste pungent [Fig.1(i)].\n\nWhite Pepper: 3-4.5 mm in diameter, externally greyish-white to pale yellowish-white, smooth, with numerous linear striations of light colour between apex and base [Fig.1(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Black Pepper: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of epidermal cells and 2-3 layers of stone cells. Mesocarp consists of parenchymatous cells, scattered with oil cells and vascular bundles. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of stone cells (beaker cells), inner tangential wall strongly thickened, sometimes containing crystals of calcium oxalate. Testa consists of 2-3 layers of brown to dark brown compressed cells on the outer side, hyaline layer consists of 1 layer of transparent cells on the inner side. Perisperm consists of thin-walled polygonal cells, filled with small starch granules [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nWhite Pepper: Exocarp absent. Mesocarp incomplete, consisting of parenchymatous cells, scattered with oil cells [Fig. 2(ii)].\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花"},{"name":"14","text":"Black Pepper: Colour dark grey. Stone cells of exocarp subsquare, rectangular or irregular in shape, 8-78 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls relatively thickened. Stone cells of endocarp (beaker cells) subpolygonal in surface view, 10-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter, square in lateral view, one side of cell wall thin, strongly thickened on the other side of the cell, sometimes embedded with crystals of calcium oxalate. Cells of testa brown to reddish-brown, polygonal. Cells of hyaline layer transparent, rectangular or subpolygonal. Perisperm cells polygonal, filled with small starch granules. Starch granules small, scattered singly or aggregated in mass, subrounded; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope [Fig. 3(i)].\n\nWhite Pepper: Colour yellowish-white. Cells of endocarp, testa, hyaline layer and perisperm can be observed in white pepper and showing the same features as described in black pepper [Fig. 3(ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 391, 1028, 1000])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[299, 1096, 1008, 1586])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1094, 432, 1673, 1615])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[302, 1650, 1018, 2062])\n\n20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1094, 1698, 1605, 2054])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of Piperis Fructus - Black Pepper A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundles D-E. Section magnified\n\n</div>\n\n1. Epidermis of exocarp 2. Stone cells 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundles\n\n5. Oil cell 6. Endocarp stone cells (beaker cells) 7. Crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n8. Testa 9. Hyaline layer 10. Perisperm\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[259, 588, 916, 1148])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[313, 1404, 908, 1933])\n\n20 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[959, 523, 1675, 1941])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Mesocarp 2. Oil cell 3. Endocarp stone cells (beaker cells)\n\n4. Crystals of calcium oxalate 5. Testa 6. Hyaline layer 7. Perisperm\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[312, 435, 923, 964])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[959, 431, 1665, 963])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[312, 998, 927, 1523])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[956, 996, 1675, 1522])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[316, 1550, 927, 2032])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[955, 1550, 1297, 2036])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1325, 1547, 1668, 1790])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1327, 1802, 1670, 2031])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Stone cells of exocarp 2. Stone cells of endocarp (beaker cells)\n\n3. Lateral view of endocarp, testa cells and hyaline layer 4. Cells of testa\n\n5. Cells of hyaline layer 6. Perisperm cell 7. Starch granule\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nPiperis Fructus\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[260, 519, 1679, 2121])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Stone cells of endocarp (beaker cells)\n\n2. Lateral view of endocarp, testa cells and hyaline layer\n\n3. Cells of testa 4. Cells of hyaline layer 5. Perisperm cell 6. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[61, 31, 1926, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Piperine standard solution\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of piperine CRS (Fig. 4) and place it in a 5-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with ethanol. Freshly prepare the standard solution."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and acetone (8:4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture. Freshly prepare the test solution."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately piperine standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[373, 1624, 1018, 1844])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of piperine\n\n</div>\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[378, 374, 819, 1195])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Piperis Fructus extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Piperine standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) White Pepper\n\n(ii) Black Pepper\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of piperine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Piperine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of piperine CRS and place it in a 10-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Freshly prepare the standard solution."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask wrapped in aluminium foil, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL amber glass volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter. Freshly prepare the test solution."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-30</td><td>50→55</td><td>50→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>55→80</td><td>45→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of piperine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of piperine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of piperine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from piperine peak should not be less than 9000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject piperine Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of piperine peak in the chromatogram of piperine Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify piperine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of piperine Std-FP. The retention times of piperine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Piperis Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Piperis Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, piperine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.80</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.57</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>3.45</td><td>±0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[343, 1051, 1807, 1587])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Piperis Fructus - Black Pepper extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[344, 1744, 1810, 2289])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Piperis Fructus - White Pepper extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[61, 31, 1933, 261])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/piperis-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Piperis Fructus","la":"Piperis Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/plantaginis-herba-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Plantaginis Herba v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[615, 671, 1378, 1271])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried herb of Plantago asiatica L.\n\nA. Whole plant B. Magnified image of capsules on spike\n\nC. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[615, 1704, 1363, 2340])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried herb of Plantago depressa Willd.\n\nA. Whole plant B. Magnified image of capsules on spike\n\nC. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[1268, 194, 1388, 239])"},"sections":[{"name":"21","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[615, 671, 1378, 1271])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried herb of Plantago asiatica L.\n\nA. Whole plant B. Magnified image of capsules on spike\n\nC. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[615, 1704, 1363, 2340])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried herb of Plantago depressa Willd.\n\nA. Whole plant B. Magnified image of capsules on spike\n\nC. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[1268, 194, 1388, 239])"},{"name":"21","text":"Official Name: Plantaginis Herba\n\nChinese Name: 車前草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Cheqiancao"},{"name":"21","text":"Plantaginis Herba is the dried herb of Plantago asiatica L. or Plantago depressa Willd. (Plantaginaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer, soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Plantaginis Herba."},{"name":"21","text":"Plantago asiatica L.: Roots fibrous, fascicled. Leaves basal, petiolate long; lamina crumpled, when whole, ovate-elliptic or broadly ovate, 3-13 cm long, 2.1-8 cm wide; externally greyish-green or blackish-green, with 5-7 distinct veins; obtuse or short acute at the apex, margin entire or irregularly sinuate-dentate. Spike several, continuous, with a long scape. Capsules ellipsoid, circumscissile near base, calyx persistent. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nPlantago depressa Willd.: Main roots straight and long. Lamina relatively narrow, long-elliptic or elliptic-lanceolate, 5-10 cm long, 2-3 cm wide [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"21","text":"Root\n\nPlantago asiatica L.: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subrounded cells, covered with cuticle. Cortex broad, cells subrounded to elongated. Endodermis distinct. Phloem narrow, surrounding the xylem. Xylem broad, vessels arranged radially [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 37, 1937, 263])\n\nPlantago depressa Willd.: Cork consists of several layers of cells, rectangular to elongated, tangentially extended, closely arranged. Cortex relatively narrow. Phloem broad, with clefts. Cambium distinct, undulated. Xylem broad, vessels arranged radially [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"21","text":"Plantago asiatica L.: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare to rectangular cells. Mesophyll cells rounded to subrounded. Vascular bundle collateral. Phloem cells subrounded, arranged loosely. Xylem vessels radially arranged. Collenchymatous cells subrounded, found beneath the lower epidermis and in the peripheric part of vascular bundle. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of square to subsquare cells, orderly arranged [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nPlantago depressa Willd.: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of rectangular to elongated cells [Fig. 3 (ii)]."},{"name":"21","text":"Plantago asiatica L.: Colour greyish-green or brownish-yellow. Upper epidermal cells subrectangular, with cuticle striations. Lower epidermal cells subrectangular, walls deeply wavy and undulated. Stomata found in both surfaces, anomocytic, with 3-4 subsidiary cells. Glandular hairs colourless to yellowish-brown, with a unicellular stalk and 2-celled head, subrounded, biseriate. Non-glandular hairs from petiole and bract abundant, usually broken, intact hair composed of dozens of cells, cells sub-cylindrical, slender, usually wall shrunken or fused. Non-glandular hairs of scape rare, 2-6 cells, walls relatively thickened. Pericarp cells irregular in shape in surface view, anticlinal walls deeply undulant, lignified, pits sparse. Anticlinal walls of epidermal cells of sepal deeply undulate, usually extended. Pollen grains colourless to pale yellow, subrounded, 17-33 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with warty sculptures on the surface. Fragment of endothecium polygonal, with dense cylindrical striations. Fibres slender, with relatively thickened walls, slightly lignified. Vessels mainly reticulate and spiral, 8-47 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 4 (i)].\n\nPlantago depressa Willd.: Upper epidermal cells subrectangular, walls wavy and undulated. Non-glandular hairs from the head of root abundantly, usually broken, intact hair composed of dozens of cells, cells sub-cylindrical, slender, usually shrunken. Non-glandular hairs from scape and surface of leaf rare, 4-8 cells, apical cell relatively small, trianglular, walls relatively thickened. Pollen grains 16-30 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fragment of endothecium rare. Vessels 6-55 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 4 (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[233, 411, 1739, 2135])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of the transverse section of dried root of Plantago asiatica L.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[239, 436, 1711, 2120])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of the transverse section of dried root of Plantago depressa Willd.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[236, 381, 1702, 2128])\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of the transverse section of dried leaf of Plantago asiatica L.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Mesophyll 3. Collenchyma 4. Xylem 5. Phloem 6. Lower epidermis\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n車前草\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\nPlantaginis Herba\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[235, 386, 1745, 2120])\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of the transverse section of dried leaf of Plantago depressa Willd.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Mesophyll 3. Collenchyma 4. Xylem 5. Phloem 6. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[61, 30, 1948, 260])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[279, 636, 1683, 1872])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried herb of Plantago asiatica L. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Epidermal cells and stomata of leaf (1-1 upper epidermal cells, 1-2 lower epidermal cells)\n\n2. Glandular hairs 3. Non-glandular hairs (3-1 petiole and bract, 3-2 scape) 4. Pericarp cells\n\n5. Epidermal cells of sepal 6. Pollen grains 7. Fragment of endothecium 8. Fibres\n\n9. Vessels (9-1 reticulate vessel, 9-2 spiral vessel)\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[255, 672, 1716, 2123])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried herb of Plantago depressa Willd. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Epidermal cells and stomata of leaf (1-1 upper epidermal cells, 1-2 lower epidermal cells)\n\n2. Glandular hairs 3. Non-glandular hair (3-1 head of root, 3-2 scape and surface of leaf)\n\n4. Pericarp cells 5. Epidermal cells of sepal 6. Pollen grains 7. Fibre\n\n8. Vessels (8-1 reticulate vessel, 8-2 spiral vessel)"},{"name":"21","text":"Acteoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of acteoside CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (60%).\n\nPlantamajoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of plantamajoside CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (60%) containing 0.05% phosphoric acid."},{"name":"21","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol, formic acid and water (18:3:1.5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"21","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"21","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately acteoside standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), plantamajoside standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3-5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba杠板歸\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[418, 366, 1069, 966])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[432, 1038, 1111, 1635])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 Chemical structures of (i) acteoside and (ii) plantamajoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[1266, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[380, 375, 1111, 1097])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3(i) 3(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Plantaginis Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Plantamajoside standard solution 2. Acteoside standard solution\n\n3. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried herb of Plantago asiatica L.\n\n(ii) dried herb of Plantago depressa Willd.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of acteoside and plantamajoside (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"21","text":"Acteoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (160 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.6 mg of acteoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (60%).\n\nPlantamajoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of plantamajoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (60%) containing 0.05% phosphoric acid."},{"name":"21","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"21","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>88→87</td><td>12→13</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-45</td><td>87→85</td><td>13→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>85→80</td><td>15→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"21","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of acteoside Std-FP and plantamajoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of acteoside and plantamajoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of acteoside and plantamajoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from acteoside and plantamajoside peaks should not be less than 30000 and 13000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 7)."},{"name":"21","text":"Separately inject acteoside Std-FP, plantamajoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of acteoside and plantamajoside peaks in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP, plantamajoside Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 7) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify acteoside and plantamajoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP and plantamajoside Std-FP. The retention times of acteoside and plantamajoside peaks in the\n\nchromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of dried herb of Plantago asiatica L. extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of dried herb of Plantago asiatica L. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.96</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,plantamajoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.36</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(acteoside)</td><td>1.46</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.51</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.83</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[356, 1311, 1812, 2160])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 7 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried herb of Plantago asiatica L. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 7)."},{"name":"21","text":"Acteoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (160 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.6 mg of acteoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (60%).\n\nPlantamajoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of plantamajoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (60%) containing 0.05% phosphoric acid."},{"name":"21","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"21","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 3) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 3 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>88→87</td><td>12→13</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-45</td><td>87→85</td><td>13→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>85→80</td><td>15→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"21","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of acteoside Std-FP and plantamajoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of acteoside and plantamajoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of acteoside and plantamajoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from acteoside and plantamajoside peaks should not be less than 30000 and 13000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[58, 35, 1898, 259])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 8)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/plantaginis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Plantaginis Herba","la":"Plantaginis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/plantaginis-semen-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Plantaginis Semen v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[759, 873, 1136, 1140])\n\nHilum\n\n5 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Plantaginis Semen A. Plantaginis Semen B. Magnified seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1902, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[759, 873, 1136, 1140])\n\nHilum\n\n5 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Plantaginis Semen A. Plantaginis Semen B. Magnified seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1902, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Plantaginis Semen\n\nChinese Name: 車前子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Cheqianzi"},{"name":"12","text":"Plantaginis Semen is the dried ripe seed of Plantago asiatica L. (Plantaginaceae). The fruit-spike is collected in summer and autumn when seed is ripe, and dried the fruit-spike under the sun. The seed gathered and foreign matter removed, then dried again under the sun to obtain Plantaginis Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Ovoid, irregularly oblong-ovoid or triangular-ovoid oblong, slightly flattened, about 2 mm long, 1 mm wide. Externally yellowish-brown to blackish-brown, with a pitted, greyish-white hilum on one side. Texture hard. Viscous and slippery when moistened. Odour slight; taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Testa consists of 2 layers of cells; outer layer is mucilaginous layer, cell wall thin, easily broken; inner layer is pigment layer, the cells contain yellowish-brown contents, subsquare or subrectangular, with slightly curved cell wall. Endosperm cells polygonal, subsquare or subrectangular, the lumen filled with aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells subrounded or subsquare, arranged orderly, containing small aleurone grains and oil droplets (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[60, 8, 1969, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour dark yellowish-brown. Inner epidermal cells of testa yellowish-brown, subrectangular, 28-85 $ \\mu $ m long, 7-29 $ \\mu $ m wide, wall thin, slightly curved. Outer epidermal cells of testa subsquare or slightly elongated tangentially, wall thin. Endosperm cells polygonal, wall thickened, lumen filled with aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells subrounded or subsquare, arranged orderly, composed of parenchymatous cells, containing small aleurone grains and oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[30, 7, 1915, 263])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 391, 1740, 1651])"},{"name":"12","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Mucilaginous layer (outer layer of testa) 2. Pigment layer (inner layer of testa)\n\n3. Endosperm 4. Cotyledon\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Plantaginis Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[259, 445, 1702, 1630])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Plantaginis Semen (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Inner epidermal cells of testa 2. Outer epidermal cells of testa 3. Endosperm cells\n\n4. Cotyledon cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 7, 1904, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Acteoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of acteoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGeniposidic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of geniposidic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, water, formic acid and glacial acetic acid (8:2:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 5 mL of sulphuric acid to 95 mL of ethanol and dissolve 1.0 g of vanillin. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately acteoside standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), geniposidic acid standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 4 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of acteoside and geniposidic acid.\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n桂枝 Kochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Plantaginis Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[422, 371, 1106, 1003])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[428, 1073, 912, 1506])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) acteoside and (ii) geniposidic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Acteoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (680 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 6.8 mg of acteoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nGeniposidic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (420 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.2 mg of geniposidic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[52, 5, 1907, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The internal diameter of inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.5%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>95→82</td><td>5→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>82</td><td>18</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>82→52</td><td>18→48</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of acteoside Std-FP and geniposidic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of acteoside and geniposidic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of acteoside and geniposidic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from acteoside and geniposidic acid peaks should not be less than 20000 and 10000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject acteoside Std-FP, geniposidic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of acteoside and geniposidic acid peaks in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP, geniposidic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify acteoside and geniposidic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of acteoside Std-FP and geniposidic acid Std-FP. The retention times of acteoside and geniposidic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Plantaginis Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 7, 1963, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Plantaginis Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(geniposidic acid)</td><td>0.32</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, acteoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.17</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.23</td><td>$\\pm$0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[378, 861, 1749, 1569])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Plantaginis Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[52, 13, 1899, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%.\n\nWater-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/plantaginis-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Plantaginis Semen","la":"Plantaginis Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/platycladi-cacumen-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Platycladi Cacumen v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[472, 848, 1583, 1917])\n\nA. Platycladi Cacumen B. Magnified Platycladi Cacumen\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1900, 264])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[472, 848, 1583, 1917])\n\nA. Platycladi Cacumen B. Magnified Platycladi Cacumen\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1900, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Platycladi Cacumen\n\nChinese Name: 側柏葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Cebaiye"},{"name":"13","text":"Platycladi Cacumen is the dried twig and leaf of Platycladus orientalis (L.) Franco (Cupressaceae). The twig and leaf are collected in summer and autumn, then dried in shaded area to obtain Platycladi Cacumen."},{"name":"13","text":"Frequently branched, twigs flattened. Leaves small, scaly, decussate, densely and closely attached to twigs, yellowish-green to dark green. Texture fragile, easily broken. Odour delicately aromatic; taste bitter, astringent and slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Oblong, subrounded twigs located at the centre with 2 subtriangular leaves. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of square to rectangular epidermal cells, covered with cuticle, containing numerous microcrystals of calcium oxalate. Hypodermal fibre 1-2 layers, located underneath the epidermis, arranged discontinuously, with thickened wall, slightly lignified to lignified. Cortex consists of subrounded parenchymatous cells, arranged loosely. Large resin canals located in cortex. Stomata sunken. Leaf vascular bundles collateral, sometimes accompanied by wing-like transfusion tissue. Twig vascular tissue large, collateral; phloem relatively narrow, xylem relatively broad, medullary rays and pith present. Parenchymatous cells contain microcrystals of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[69, 9, 1971, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-green. Upper epidermal cells of leaf narrowly rectangular, wall slightly thickened, containing numerous microcrystals of calcium oxalate. Lower epidermal cells subsquare, containing numerous sunken stomata, anomocytic or actinocytic type, guard cells dumbbell-shaped, 31-50 $ \\mu m $ long and 19-36 $ \\mu m $ wide. Tracheids bordered-pitted, sometimes present. Fibres slender, 9-24 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[39, 6, 1905, 266])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 407, 1731, 2124])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Platycladi Cacumen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Leave vascular bundle D. Stoma E. Epidermis F. Twig vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermal fibre 3. Cortex 4. Resin canal\n\n5. Transfusion tissue 6. Leave vascular bundle\n\n7. Stoma 8. Phloem 9. Xylem 10. Pith\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Platycladi Cacumen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[289, 749, 996, 1164])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1013, 747, 1694, 1161])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[291, 1198, 960, 1681])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[976, 1192, 1690, 1429])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[978, 1462, 1690, 1669])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Platycladi Cacumen\n\n1. Upper epidermal cells with microcrystals of calcium oxalate 2. Lower epidermal cells with sunken stomata\n\n3. Bordered-pitted tracheid 4. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 7, 1903, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Quercitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of quercitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (20:2:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercitrin standard solution and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercitrin.\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Platycladi Cacumen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[409, 364, 1069, 900])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[426, 1000, 899, 1493])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) amentoflavone and (ii) quercitrin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Amentoflavone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of amentoflavone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nQuercitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of quercitrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[61, 4, 1908, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (350 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-50</td><td>60→10</td><td>40→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of amentoflavone Std-FP and quercitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of amentoflavone and quercitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of amentoflavone and quercitrin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from amentoflavone and quercitrin peaks should not be less than 80000 and 13000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject amentoflavone Std-FP, quercitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of amentoflavone and quercitrin peaks in the chromatograms of amentoflavone Std-FP, quercitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify amentoflavone and quercitrin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of amentoflavone Std-FP and quercitrin Std-FP. The retention times of amentoflavone and quercitrin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 7, 1968, 261])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Platycladi Cacumen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Platycladi Cacumen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.36</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(quercitrin)</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.59</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,amentoflavone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.17</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[291, 1080, 1813, 1601])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Platycladi Cacumen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[81, 14, 1910, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/platycladi-cacumen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Platycladi Cacumen","la":"Platycladi Cacumen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/platycladi-semen-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Platycladi Semen v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1 A photograph of Platycladi Semen A. Platycladi Semen B. Magnified image of ripe kernel"},"sections":[{"name":"17","text":"Figure 1 A photograph of Platycladi Semen A. Platycladi Semen B. Magnified image of ripe kernel"},{"name":"17","text":"Official Name: Platycladi Semen\n\nChinese Name: 柏子仁\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baiziren"},{"name":"17","text":"Platycladi Semen is the dried ripe kernel of Platycladus orientalis (L.) Franco (Cupressaceae). The ripe seed is collected in autumn and winter, dried under the sun, then the testa removed to obtain Platycladi Semen."},{"name":"17","text":"Long ovoid to elongate-ellipsoid, 0.4-0.7 cm long, 1.5-3 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to pale yellowish-brown, covered with membranous tegmen, apex slightly acute, with a small dark brown dot, base obtusely rounded. Texture soft, oily. Odour slightly aromatic; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"17","text":"Apex end: Endotesta shrunken, cell borders mostly indistinct. Endosperm developed, epidermal cells tangentially elongated. Epidermal cells of embryo subsquare to subpolygonal. Vascular bundles located in the middle of the embryo, cells relatively small. Endosperm and embryo cells filled with aleurone grains [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nBase end: Transverse section shows two pieces of cotyledon [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[28, 34, 1939, 262])"},{"name":"17","text":"Mount in chloral hydrate: Endotesta cells brownish-yellow, elongated rectangular. Endosperm cells nearly colourless, pale yellow to yellowish-green, subpolygonal, subrectangular or subrounded, thin-walled. Embryo cells near colourless, subrectangular to strip-shaped in lateral view, relatively evenly arranged, thin-walled. Oil droplets red observed after stained with Sudan III TS, relatively numerous, rounded.\n\nMount in liquid paraffin: Aleurone grains yellowish-brown observed after stained with iodine TS, relatively numerous, elliptic to subrounded, 3-9 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[359, 680, 906, 1133])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[347, 1237, 905, 1480])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[346, 1580, 905, 1859])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1016, 669, 1650, 1795])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of apex end of Platycladi Semen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C-D. Section magnified\n\n1. Endotesta 2. Epidermis of endosperm 3. Endosperm 4. Epidermis of embryo\n\n5. Embryo 6. Vascular bundle 7. Aleurone grain\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[329, 708, 890, 1153])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[334, 1194, 885, 1499])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[331, 1554, 885, 1869])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[987, 725, 1676, 1832])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of base end of Platycladi Semen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C-D. Section magnified\n\n1. Endotesta 2. Epidermis of endosperm 3. Endosperm 4. Epidermis of embryo\n\n5. Embryo 6. Vascular bundle 7. Aleurone grain\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[60, 32, 1962, 259])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[233, 445, 1742, 2122])"},{"name":"17","text":"1. Endotesta cells\n\n2. Endosperm cells (2-1 epidermal cells of endosperm in lateral view, 2-2 core cells of endosperm)\n\n3. Embryo cells (3-1 epidermal cells of embryo in lateral view, 3-2 core cells of embryo in lateral view)\n\n4. Oil droplets (4-1 no stained, 4-2 stained with Sudan III TS)\n\n5. Aleurone grains (5-1 no stained, 5-2 stained with iodine TS)"},{"name":"17","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of linoleic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\n$ \\alpha $ - Linolenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nOleic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of oleic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate, glacial acetic acid and acetonitrile (6:2.5:0.1:0.25, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of 2',7'-dichlorofluorescein and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Kieselguhr is added to the sample before powdering in the ratio of 1:1. Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of n-hexane. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter and transfer 10 mL of the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of sodium hydroxide in ethanol (4%, w/v). Heat the mixture in a water bath at $ 7 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Add 1 mL of sulphuric acid in ethanol (50%, v/v). Transfer the solution to a separating funnel. Extract with 10 mL of n-hexane and 20 mL of boiled water at room temperature. Transfer the n-hexane layer to another separating funnel. Wash the n-hexane layer for three times each with 10 mL of boiled water at room temperature. Collect the n-hexane layer and add appropriate amount of anhydrous sodium sulphate. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC plate of silica gel H with 15% silver nitrate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linoleic acid standard solution, $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid standard solution, oleic acid standard solution and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n洋金花 Daturae Flos\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[451, 364, 971, 765])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[447, 830, 968, 1163])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[446, 1223, 1091, 1628])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[449, 1712, 806, 2041])\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n( v )\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[447, 364, 975, 765])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vi)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[449, 830, 979, 1165])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[452, 1223, 1099, 1621])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) linoleic acid (ii) $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid (iii) oleic acid (iv) methyl arachidonate (v) methyl linoleate (vi) methyl linolenate and (vii) methyl oleate\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[376, 381, 1213, 1066])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Platycladi Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Oleic acid standard solution 2. Linoleic acid standard solution\n\n3. $ \\alpha $ - Linolenic acid standard solution 4. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of linoleic acid, $ \\alpha $ -linolenic acid and oleic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"17","text":"Methyl arachidonate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (23 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.23 mg of methyl arachidonate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nMethyl linoleate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of methyl linoleate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nMethyl linolenate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (800 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 8.0 mg of methyl linolenate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate.\n\nMethyl oleate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of methyl oleate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"17","text":"Kieselguhr is added to the sample before powdering in the ratio of 1:1. Weigh 0.8 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 20 min. Centrifuge at about $ 6 9 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Collect the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Pipette 5 mL of the solution and transfer to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Add 3 mL of potassium hydroxide in methanol (2.8%, w/v) and heat at $ 4 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Add 0.5 mL of sulphuric acid in methanol (50%, v/v) and 1 mL of boron trifluoride in methanol (14%, w/v). Mix well and heat at $ 4 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 15 min. Then, add 10 mL of isooctane and 20 mL of saturated sodium chloride solution. Transfer the solution to a 100-mL separating funnel. Shake well and collect the isooctane extract. Extract the aqueous layer for two times each with 5 mL of isooctane. Combine the isooctane extracts and add appropriate amount of anhydrous sodium sulphate. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in isooctane. Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with isooctane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"17","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (VF-23 MS, $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{~ m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{~ m} $) of which the internal wall is covered with cyanopropyl polysiloxane in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{\\mu m} $ thick. The injection temperature is at $ 2 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 3 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 10:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-9</td><td>80→170</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>9-24</td><td>170→200</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>24-26</td><td>200→250</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>26-31</td><td>250</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[70, 37, 1921, 258])"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of methyl arachidonate Std-FP, methyl linoleate Std-FP, methyl linolenate Std-FP and methyl oleate Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of methyl arachidonate, methyl linoleate, methyl linolenate and methyl oleate should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of methyl arachidonate, methyl linoleate, methyl linolenate and methyl oleate peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from methyl arachidonate, methyl linoleate, methyl linolenate and methyl oleate peaks should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 10 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"17","text":"Separately inject methyl arachidonate Std-FP, methyl linoleate Std-FP, methyl linolenate Std-FP, methyl oleate Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of methyl arachidonate, methyl linoleate, methyl linolenate and methyl oleate peaks in the chromatograms of methyl arachidonate Std-FP, methyl linoleate Std-FP, methyl linolenate Std-FP and the retention times of the eleven characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify methyl arachidonate, methyl linoleate, methyl linolenate and methyl oleate peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of methyl arachidonate Std-FP, methyl linoleate Std-FP, methyl linolenate Std-FP and methyl oleate Std-FP. The retention times of methyl arachidonate, methyl linoleate, methyl linolenate and methyl oleate peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eleven characteristic peaks of Platycladi Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[29, 33, 1940, 263])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the eleven characteristic peaks of Platycladi Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.69</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(methyl oleate)</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(methyl linoleate)</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,methyl linolenate)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>1.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>10(methyl arachidonate)</td><td>1.20</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>1.24</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[322, 1218, 1826, 2051])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Platycladi Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above eleven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"17","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"17","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 6.0%.\n\n5. 8 Acid Value (Appendix XIV): not more than 40.0."},{"name":"17","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/platycladi-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Platycladi Semen","la":"Platycladi Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/plumbaginis-zeylanicae-radix-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[383, 820, 1487, 1768])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix A. Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix B. Magnified image of root C. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[383, 820, 1487, 1768])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix A. Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix B. Magnified image of root C. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 白花丹\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baihuadan"},{"name":"12","text":"Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix is the dried root of Plumbago zeylanica L. (Plumbaginaceae). The root is collected all the year round, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Main root cylindrical, texture hard, usually branched, slightly curved, with numerous rootlets. Externally greyish-brown to brownish-yellow, smooth or rough, with longitudinal wrinkles and rootlet scars. Wood yellowish. Fracture showed several concentric rings and closely arranged radial striations. Ray white and distinct. Odour slight; taste pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells, containing brownish-red contents. Cortex consists of several layers of polygonal parenchymatous cells, filled with starch granules and brownish-red contents. Cortical fibres visible, scattered singly or in bundles. Phloem distinct. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad. Vessels scattered singly or in groups, arranged radially. Xylem ray consists of 1-2 rows of cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellow to brown. Xylem fibres scattered or in bundles, walls slightly thickened, with distinct pits and pit canals; white under the polarized microscope. Cortical fibres long, walls thickened; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Cork cells subpolygonal or subrectangular, walls thickened. Stone cells usually scattered singly, 30-175 $ \\mu $ m long, 24-85 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, walls 5-18 $ \\mu $ m thick, with striations, distinct pits and pit canals; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Bordered-pitted vessels visible, 13-77 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Starch granules numerous, mainly simple starch granules, subrounded, 4-24 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1267, 194, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 470, 1692, 2023])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cortical fibres\n\nD. Brownish-red contents ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) and starch granules ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cortical fibres 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[255, 536, 1711, 2122])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix\n\n1. Xylem fibre 2. Cortical fibre 3. Cork cells 4. Stone cell\n\n5. Bordered-pitted vessel 6. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Plumbagin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of plumbagin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and glacial acetic acid (10:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 6 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately plumbagin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[374, 1816, 656, 2111])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of plumbagin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 374, 868, 1242])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Plumbagin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of plumbagin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Plumbagin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (150 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of plumbagin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 45 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with alkyl reversed-phase bonded silica gel with diisopropyl side chain (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-22</td><td>65</td><td>35</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>22-27</td><td>65→50</td><td>35→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>27-45</td><td>50→45</td><td>50→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>45→40</td><td>55→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of plumbagin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of plumbagin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of plumbagin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from plumbagin peak should not be less than 8500 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject plumbagin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of plumbagin peak in the chromatogram of plumbagin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify plumbagin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of plumbagin Std-FP. The retention times of plumbagin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[38, 33, 1938, 263])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, plumbagin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.76</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[342, 848, 1802, 1650])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/plumbaginis-zeylanicae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix","la":"Plumbaginis Zeylanicae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/pogostemonis-herba-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pogostemonis Herba v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[364, 798, 1585, 1973])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Pogostemonis Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Pogostemonis Herba B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaves D. Magnified image of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section of stem"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[364, 798, 1585, 1973])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Pogostemonis Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Pogostemonis Herba B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaves D. Magnified image of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section of stem"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Pogostemonis Herba\n\nChinese name: 廣藿香\n\nChinese phonetic name: Guanghuoxiang"},{"name":"14","text":"Pogostemonis Herba is the dried aerial part of Pogostemon cablin (Blanco) Benth. (Lamiaceae). The aerial part is collected when foliage and branches grow luxuriantly, repeatedly exposed to the sun and tightly piled at night until completely dried to obtain Pogostemonis Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem somewhat square, frequently branched, branches slightly curved, length varied, up to 60 cm in length, 2-7 mm in diameter. Externally pubescent. Texture fragile, easily broken, pith present in the centre of fracture surface. Leaves opposite, crumpled into masses, when whole, ovate or elliptical, 4-9 cm in length, 3-7 cm in width, greenish-brown to brown, whitish pubescent on both surfaces, apex short-acute or obtuse-rounded, base cuneate or obtusely rounded, margin irregularly serrate, petioles slender, 2-5 cm in length, pubescent. Odour aromatic; taste slight bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Non-glandular hair sometimes visible, mostly fallen off during staining procedure. Sometimes several layers of the suberized cells under epidermis can be observed. The outer part of cortex consists of several layers of sclerenchymatous cells, and the inner part of cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells with large intercellular spaces. Pericycle fibres in bundle. Phloem narrow. Xylem well developed in the angular region. Pith relatively large, mainly consisting of parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nLeaf: Non-glandular hairs and glandular hairs found on both the upper and lower epidermis, mostly fallen off during staining procedure. Upper epidermis and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of cells. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of irregularly shaped cells. Collenchyma consists of 1-9 layers of cells located in the inner side of the upper epidermis of the midrib. Spongy tissue mainly consists of subrounded parenchymatous cells, loosely arranged. Vascular bundles collateral, xylem vessels lignified, orderly arranged. Phloem narrow and shrunken. Fibre bundles nonlignified or slightly lignified [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour pale brown. Lower epidermal cells irregular in shape. Non-glandular hairs 1 to 6-celled, straight or curved at the apex, length varied, up to 600 $ \\mu m $ in length, walls warty, some cells containing yellowish-brown masses. Glandular scales each with 8-celled head and with a very short unicellular stalk, 35-80 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Intercellular glandular hairs each with an unicellular head, irregularly saccular, 13-50 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, length varied, the longer one up to about 113 $ \\mu m $ long, and with a short unicellular stalk. Small glandular hairs each with a bicellular head and a very short 1 to 3-celled stalk. Stomata diacytic. Needle crystals of calcium oxalate tiny, scattered in mesophyll cells, 1-27 $ \\mu m $ in length (Fig. 3).\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 411, 1732, 2120])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Pogostemonis Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle fibre 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n6. Pith 7. Non-glandular hair\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[234, 426, 1726, 2112])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Pogostemonis Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Collenchyma\n\n5. Spongy tissue 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Fibre bundle 9. Lower epidermis\n\n10. Glandular hair\n\n明黛參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n枸杞子\n\n紅豆蔻\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\nStorax蘇合香\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[229, 432, 1753, 2137])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Lower epidermal cells 2. Glandular scales 3. Non-glandular hairs\n\n4. Intercellular glandular hairs 5. Small glandular hairs 6. Diacytic stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n7. Needle crystals of calcium oxalate ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[28, 32, 1942, 255])"},{"name":"14","text":"Patchouli alcohol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of patchouli alcohol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nPogostone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of pogostone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, acetone, methanol and formic acid (18:3:2:1, v/v). Use the upper layer."},{"name":"14","text":"Mix 0.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde into 10 mL of acetic acid. Add 85 mL of methanol. Add cautiously 5 mL of sulphuric acid."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 12 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Evaporate the filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of ethanol (95%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately patchouli alcohol standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) pogostone standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 437, 759, 734])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 860, 932, 1151])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) patchouli alcohol and (ii) pogostone\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 1318, 1055, 2145])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Pogostemonis Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Patchouli alcohol standard solution 2. Pogostone standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 23, 1948, 258])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of patchouli alcohol and pogostone (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Patchouli alcohol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (150 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of patchouli alcohol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (95%).\n\nPogostone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (150 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of pogostone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 12 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Wash the residue with ethanol (95%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethanol (95%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (HP-5MS, $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{~ m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{~ m} $ ) of which the internal wall is covered with (5%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. Nitrogen is used as the carrier gas at 1.5 mL/min. The injection temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 20:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>120</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>15-52</td><td>120→230</td><td>3</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of patchouli alcohol Std-FP and pogostone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of patchouli alcohol and pogostone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of patchouli alcohol and pogostone peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from patchouli alcohol and pogostone peaks should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject patchouli alcohol Std-FP, pogostone Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of patchouli alcohol and pogostone peaks in the chromatograms of patchouli alcohol Std-FP, pogostone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify patchouli alcohol and pogostone peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of patchouli alcohol Std-FP and pogostone Std-FP. The retention times of patchouli alcohol and pogostone peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Pogostemonis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Pogostemonis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, patchouli alcohol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(pogostone)</td><td>1.11</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.59</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.70</td><td>$\\pm$0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[294, 372, 1824, 1026])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Pogostemonis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.4%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.75%."},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 8.3%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.8%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.9%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/pogostemonis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Pogostemonis Herba","la":"Pogostemonis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygalae-japonicae-herba-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygalae Japonicae Herba v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Polygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[646, 923, 1294, 1360])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[637, 1413, 734, 1780])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[845, 1410, 951, 1778])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1022, 1402, 1158, 1780])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1186, 1410, 1425, 1769])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Polygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[646, 923, 1294, 1360])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[637, 1413, 734, 1780])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[845, 1410, 951, 1778])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1022, 1402, 1158, 1780])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1186, 1410, 1425, 1769])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Polygalae Japonicae Herba\n\nB. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of flowers\n\nE. Magnified image of root"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Polygalae Japonicae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 瓜子金\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Guazijin"},{"name":"14","text":"Polygalae Japonicae Herba is the dried whole plant of Polygala japonica Houtt. (Polygalaceae). The whole plant is collected at flowering period in late spring, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Polygalae Japonicae Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Roots cylindrical, slightly curved, up to 4 mm in diameter; externally yellowish-brown, with longitudinal wrinkles; texture hard, fracture yellowish-white. Stems a few branched, 9-30 cm long, pale brown, pubescent. Leaves alternate, greyish-green on both surface, when whole ovate to ovate-lanceolate, 1-3 cm long, 5-10 mm wide; lateral veins distinct, apex short-acute, base rounded or cuneate, margin entire; petiole short or sessile, pubescent. Racemes axillary, the uppermost racemes lower than the top of stems; flowers butterfly-shaped. Odour slight; taste slightly pungent and bitter (Fig.1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Root: Cork consists several layers of cells. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells. Phloem relatively broad. Cambium consists of 3-5 layers of cells, arranged in a ring. Xylem well developed, lignified; xylem rays consist of 1-3 rows of cells; vessels single or 2-3 in groups [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[36, 33, 1950, 255])\n\nStem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle, unicellular non-glandular hairs occasionally found. Cortex consists of 5-7 layers of cells, the cells subrounded, arranged loosely. Endodermis consists of 1 layer of cells, the cells long-rounded, arranged densely, wall thickened, lignified. Phloem relatively narrow, the cells relatively small. Cambium indistinct. Xylem vessels mostly 2-5 in groups; xylem fibres in bundles, lignified. Pith consists of subrounded parenchymatous cells, arranged loosely, usually hollow in the centre [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermal cells oblong, lower epidermal cells subsquare, both covered with cuticle. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of oblong parenchymatous cells, arranged densely. Spongy tissue consists of several layers of subrounded parenchymatous cells, arranged loosely, with intercellular spaces. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregates, scattered in mesophyll. Vascular bundle of midrib collateral. Collenchymatous cells located on the inner side of lower epidermis of midrib. Unicellular non-glandular hairs found on both surfaces [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour yellowish-green. Upper epidermal cell polygonal, anticlinal walls relatively straight, stomata anomocytic or anisocytic. Lower epidermal cells polygonal or irregular in shape, anticlinal wall sinuate, with dense stomata, most of the stomata anomocytic, anisocytic occasionally found. Non-glandular hairs numerous, unicellular, walls thin, with fine warty surface. Vessels mostly reticulate and spiral, 5-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate few, in rosette aggregates with obtuse angles, 8-30 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ m in diameter, bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains subspherical or oblong, 30-65 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with striated sculptures. Fibres in bundle; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 241])\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 447, 1711, 2137])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Polygalae Japonicae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem 6. Xylem ray\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1025, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[244, 505, 1718, 2079])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Polygalae Japonicae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis 5. Phloem 6. Cambium\n\n7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[104, 188, 202, 239])\n\n天葵子\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[239, 399, 1740, 2119])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Polygalae Japonicae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Vascular bundle 6. Collenchyma 7. Lower epidermis 8. Non-glandular hair\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[197, 146, 289, 188])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[925, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 109])\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[246, 406, 1751, 2108])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Polygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n1. Upper epidermal cell with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ ） 2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ ）\n\n3. Non-glandular hairs 4. Reticulate vessels 5. Spiral vessel 6. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n7. Pollen grains 8. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"14","text":"Polygalasaponin F standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of polygalasaponin F CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of water, n-butanol and glacial acetic acid (5:4:1, v/v). Shake well and use the upper layer."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Collect the supernatant."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately polygalasaponin F standard solution and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n天冬\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[925, 87, 1028, 138])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n毛冬青 Ilicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[369, 368, 1519, 916])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of polygalasaponin F\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 1038, 849, 1838])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Polygalae Japonicae Herba extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Polygalasaponin F standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of polygalasaponin F (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Polygalasaponin F standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of polygalasaponin F CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ) pressure: 3.5 bar] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>80→60</td><td>20→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of polygalasaponin F Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of polygalasaponin F should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of polygalasaponin F peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from polygalasaponin F peak should not be less than 90000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject polygalasaponin F Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of polygalasaponin F peak in the chromatogram of polygalasaponin F Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify polygalasaponin F peak in the\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[925, 86, 1028, 138])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1751, 51, 1913, 125])\n\nchromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of polygalasaponin F Std-FP. The retention times of polygalasaponin F peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Polygalae Japonicae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Polygalae Japonicae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.85</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.98</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, polygalasaponin F)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[362, 1216, 1808, 2040])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Polygalae Japonicae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[1042, 149, 1134, 208])"},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygalae-japonicae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygalae Japonicae Herba","la":"Polygalae Japonicae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygonati-rhizoma-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygonati Rhizoma v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[417, 1127, 1101, 1659])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1101, 1131, 1493, 1365])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1151, 1411, 1519, 1707])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Polygonati Rhizoma\n\nA. Polygonati Rhizoma B. Magnified image of rhizome (stem scar $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of fracture surface\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[417, 1127, 1101, 1659])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1101, 1131, 1493, 1365])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1151, 1411, 1519, 1707])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Polygonati Rhizoma\n\nA. Polygonati Rhizoma B. Magnified image of rhizome (stem scar $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nC. Magnified image of fracture surface\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Polygonati Rhizoma\n\nChinese name: 黄精\n\nChinese phonetic name: Huangjing"},{"name":"12","text":"Polygonati Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Polygonatum sibiricum Red. (Liliaceae). The rhizome is collected in autumn, foreign matter and fibrous roots removed, washed clean, steamed thoroughly, or immersed in boiling water for a moment, then dried under the sun or baked to dryness to obtain Polygonati Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Elongated, nodular, varying in length, 2.7-15.2 cm long, 3-25 mm in diameter, often tapered at one end, branched, with protruded yellowish-white stem scars. Externally yellowish, greyish-yellow to yellowish-brown, with nodes, sometimes indistinct, internode 1-14 mm long. Heavy in weight, texture hard, fracture flat, horny, yellowish-white to yellowish-brown, slightly translucent, with numerous yellowish-white vein dots scattered. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle. Cortex relatively narrow. Stele broad, endodermis indistinct, with vascular bundles scattered; vascular bundles mainly collateral, relatively smaller and denser on the outer side. Mucilage cells scattered in parenchyma, some containing raphides of calcium oxalate, relatively denser on the outer side (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Raphides of calcium oxalate in bundles; scattered or present in mucilage cells, 30-220 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells subolygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls slightly beaded, covered with yellow cuticle. Vessels mainly scalariform and reticulate, 8-70 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Parenchymatous cells numerous, large, subpolygonal or subrounded, some with faintly visible pits (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1059, 91, 1142, 133])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1692, 55, 1938, 138])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[265, 547, 1739, 2068])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Polygonati Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Mucilage cell D. Vascular bundle 1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Stele 4. Vascular bundle 5. Mucilage cell 6. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 42, 1966, 255])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[299, 636, 1686, 2086])"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Raphides of calcium oxalate (1-1 scattered, 1-2 in mucilage cell)\n\n2. Epidermal cells (2-1 surface view, 2-2 lateral view) (cuticle $ \\rightarrow $)\n\n3. Vessels (3-1 scalariform vessel, 3-2 reticulate vessels) 4. Parenchymatous cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized light microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 39, 1945, 241])"},{"name":"12","text":"Trigonelline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of trigonelline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, formic acid and ethyl acetate (8: 8: 1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample (through a No. 5 sieve) and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 50 mL of methanol with 0.1% formic acid. Reflux the mixture for 45 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the combined filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in ethanol (50%). Transfer the extract to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Pre-condition a solid phase extraction (SPE) column (Neutral aluminium oxide, 20 mL, 5 g) with 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Load 5 mL of the extract to the pre-conditioned SPE column. Add 50 mL of ethanol (50%) to the column. Collect the eluate and transfer to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the eluate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in ethanol (50%). Transfer the extract to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.22- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately trigonelline standard solution ( $ 2 \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution ( $ 5 \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Expose the plate to iodine vapour in a chamber for about 20 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n京大戟 Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[374, 367, 644, 680])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of trigonelline\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[378, 852, 839, 1689])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Polygonati Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Trigonelline standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of trigonelline (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[32, 45, 1946, 134])"},{"name":"12","text":"0. 2M Ammonium acetate solution\n\nWeigh 7.71 g of ammonium acetate and dissolve in 500 mL water.\n\n0. 01M Ammonium acetate solution\n\nPipette 50 mL of 0.2M ammonium acetate solution and make up to 1000 mL of water.\n\n0. 2% Acetic acid in 0.01M ammonium acetate solution\n\nPipette 2 mL of acetic acid into 1000 mL of 0.01M ammonium acetate solution.\n\nMixture of acetic acid and 0.2M ammonium acetate in acetonitrile\n\nPipette 2 mL of acetic acid into 50 mL of 0.2M ammonium acetate solution. Add the solution of acetic acid in ammonium acetate into 950 mL of acetonitrile."},{"name":"12","text":"Trigonelline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of trigonelline CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample (through a No. 5 sieve) and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 50 mL of methanol with 0.1% formic acid. Reflux the mixture for 45 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction two more times. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the combined filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in ethanol (50%). Transfer the extract to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Pre-condition a solid phase extraction (SPE) column (Neutral aluminium oxide, 20 mL, 5 g) with 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Load 5 mL of the extract to the pre-conditioned SPE column. Add 50 mL of ethanol (50%) to the column. Collect the eluate and transfer to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the eluate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in ethanol (50%). Transfer the extract to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.22- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a Hydrophilic Interaction Chromatography (HILIC) column $ (2. 1 \\times1 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}, 1. 7 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 130 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 185 $ \\mathrm{m^{2}/g} $ surface area). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.4 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[30, 43, 1965, 257])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Mixture of acetic acid and 0.2M ammonium acetate in acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Acetic acid in 0.01M ammonium acetate solution(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>100→93</td><td>0→7</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>3-10</td><td>93→88</td><td>7→12</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-15</td><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of trigonelline Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of trigonelline should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of trigonelline peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from trigonelline peak should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject trigonelline Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of trigonelline peak in the chromatogram of trigonelline Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify trigonelline peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of trigonelline Std-FP. The retention times of trigonelline peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Polygonati Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Polygonati Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, trigonelline)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[338, 387, 1816, 1000])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Polygonati Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 11.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 259])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygonati-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygonati Rhizoma","la":"Polygonati Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-avicularis-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Avicularis Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[581, 853, 1578, 1960])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Polygoni Avicularis Herba A. Polygoni Avicularis Herba (flower $ \\rightarrow $ ） B. Stem with nodes and ocrea C. Upper (left) and lower (right) surface of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1899, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[581, 853, 1578, 1960])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Polygoni Avicularis Herba A. Polygoni Avicularis Herba (flower $ \\rightarrow $ ） B. Stem with nodes and ocrea C. Upper (left) and lower (right) surface of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 10, 1899, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Polygoni Avicularis Herba\n\nChinese Name: 萹蓄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Bianxu"},{"name":"13","text":"Polygoni Avicularis Herba is the dried aerial part of Polygonum aviculare L. (Polygonaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer when leaves flourish, roots and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Polygoni Avicularis Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Stems cylindrical and somewhat flattened, branched, 1-4 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-green to reddish-brown, with fine and slightly prominent longitudinal striations, nodes slightly swollen, with pale brown membranous ocrea, internodes 0.4-5 cm long. Texture hard and brittle; fracture whitish in the pith. Leaves alternate, almost sessile or short petiole; the lamina usually fallen off, crumpled and broken, but when intact and flattened, lanceolate-elliptic, 0.5-3.8 cm long, 1-7 mm wide, margins entire, both surfaces greyish-green to yellowish-green or brownish-green. Flowers small, fasciculate at leaf axil. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subrectangular cells covered with cuticle, sometimes with brown to yellowish-brown contents. Hypodermal fibre bundles arranged in an interrupted ring. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells, some cells containing clusters of calcium oxalate. Pericyclic fibre bundles arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. In xylem, vessels lined up radially. Pith large, consisting of large parenchymatous cells, scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[67, 9, 1969, 259])\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis each consists of 1 layer of cells. Palisade tissue present inside the upper and lower epidermis, with some parenchymatous cells containing clusters of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundle of midrib collateral. Sclerenchyma cells present outside the midrib. Collenchyma present on the inner sides of the upper and lower epidermis of the midrib [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green to brownish-green. Clusters of calcium oxalate 9-59 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains yellow to yellowish-white, ellipsoid, subspherical or obtusely triangular, 19-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with 3 germinal pores. Epidermal cells of leaf polygonal in surface view, anticlinal wall beaded, stomata anisocytic. Fibres slender, 6-28 $ \\mu $m in diameter; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of stem polygonal or subsquare in surface view, stomata sometimes visible. Vessels mainly spiral and reticulate, 3-51 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[39, 101, 203, 160])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 570, 1713, 1987])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Polygoni Avicularis Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate and fibre bundles\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermal fibre bundle 3. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Cortex 5. Pericyclic fibre bundle 6. Phloem 7. Cambium\n\n8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 403, 1729, 1991])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Polygoni Avicularis Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Collenchyma\n\n4. Cluster of calcium oxalate 5. Sclerenchyma 6. Xylem\n\n7. Phloem 8. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[539, 19, 701, 76])\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\n桃仁 Persicae Semen\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[278, 452, 1708, 1832])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Polygoni Avicularis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Pollen grains 3. Epidermal cells of leaf 4. Fibres\n\n5. Epidermal cells of stem 6. Vessels (6-1 spiral vessels, 6-2 reticulate vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[68, 14, 1964, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Myricitrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of myricitrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid, methanol and water (25:1:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately myricitrin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 4, 1904, 263])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of myricitrin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 511, 928, 1086])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of myricitrin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Myricitrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of myricitrin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (60%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 45 mL of methanol (60%). Soak the mixture for 1 h. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Centrifuge at about $ 3 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (60%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 190 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 12% carbon loading). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 6, 1968, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>80→40</td><td>20→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of myricitrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of myricitrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of myricitrin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from myricitrin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject myricitrin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of myricitrin peak in the chromatogram of myricitrin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify myricitrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of myricitrin Std-FP. The retention times of myricitrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Avicularis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[35, 4, 1904, 266])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Avicularis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, myricitrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.12</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.25</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(quercitrin)</td><td>1.30</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[297, 843, 1844, 1419])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Polygoni Avicularis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[66, 14, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 10.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-avicularis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Avicularis Herba","la":"Polygoni Avicularis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-chinensis-herba-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Chinensis Herba v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[648, 881, 1327, 1437])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[657, 1440, 836, 1823])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[890, 1456, 1070, 1814])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1085, 1505, 1345, 1599])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1160, 1676, 1289, 1808])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[648, 881, 1327, 1437])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[657, 1440, 836, 1823])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[890, 1456, 1070, 1814])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1085, 1505, 1345, 1599])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1160, 1676, 1289, 1808])"},{"name":"13","text":"A. Polygoni Chinensis Herba\n\nB. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of stem\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section of stem"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Polygoni Chinensis Herba\n\nChinese Name: 火炭母\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Huotanmu"},{"name":"13","text":"Polygoni Chinensis Herba is the dried aerial part of Polygonum chinense L. (Polygonaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Polygoni Chinensis Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem compressed-cylindrical, branched, 20-40 cm long, nodes slightly swollen, the lower part of nodes with rootlets; externally pale green to purplish-brown, glabrous, with ridges; texture fragile, easily broken, fracture greyish-yellow, usually hollowed. Leaves simple, alternate, mostly crumpled and broken, when whole, ovate-oblong in shape, 5-10 cm long, 2-5 cm wide; Apex short-acute, base truncate or slightly rounded, margins entire; the upper surface dark green with a purplish-black or greyish-white V shaped pattern on it and the lower surface lighter in colour; membranaceous ochrea tubular, apex oblique. Flowers small, orange-yellow in colour. Odour slight; taste sour, slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells tangentially elongated, some containing reddish-brown pigment, covered with thin cuticle. Collenchyma consists of 1-2 layers of cells, underneath the epidermis. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells. Pericycle fibres consist of 1-5 layers of cells, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem relatively broad. Xylem vessels 2-6 arranged. Pith large, the cells polygonal or irregular in shape. Some parenchymatous cells contain clusters of calcium oxalate [Fig 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[40, 33, 1937, 260])\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of rectangular cells. Mesophyll consists of 1 layer of palisade tissue and several layers of spongy tissue. Collenchyma consists of several layers of cells, located underneath the upper and lower epidermis of the midrib. Fibres in group, lignified. Vascular bundle of midrib 2-6, collateral, arranged in an interrupted ring. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in the parenchymatous cells. Non-glandular hairs raised from upper and lower epidermis [Fig 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-green. Epidermal cells of leaf subpolygonal, some with cuticle striation; anomocytic stomata relatively dense. Epidermal cells of stem subrectangular, some with cuticle striation. Non-glandular hairs multicellular and multiseriate, arranged imbricately. Glandular hairs unicellular or multicellular. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, 20-80 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with acute angles; pale white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains subspherical, with distinct reticulate-polygonal sculptures, 26-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Vessels mainly reticulate, 36-70 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 891, 117])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[736, 185, 853, 228])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1268, 194, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[346, 546, 865, 1089])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[281, 1118, 930, 1483])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[283, 1570, 920, 1906])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[984, 573, 1706, 1902])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Polygoni Chinensis Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Cortex 4. Pericycle fibre\n\n5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1295, 69, 1443, 118])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 420, 1740, 2131])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Polygoni Chinensis Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) D. Cluster of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Mesophyll 4. Fibre 5. Vascular bundle\n\n6. Cluster of calcium oxalate 7. Non-glandular hair 8. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[370, 409, 980, 804])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[993, 412, 1604, 809])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[369, 820, 980, 1222])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[990, 820, 1303, 1221])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1325, 826, 1604, 1206])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[370, 1233, 981, 1622])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[994, 1229, 1606, 1626])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[369, 1637, 984, 2033])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[995, 1645, 1605, 2037])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Polygoni Chinensis Herba\n\n1. Epidermal cells of leaf 2. Epidermal cells of stem 3. Non-glandular hairs\n\n4. Glandular hairs (4-1 surface view $ \\rightarrow $ , 4-2 lateral view) 5. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n6. Pollen grains 7. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 40, 1938, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Ellagic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of ellagic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (4:4:2:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 25 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Collect the supernatant."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ellagic acid standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1267, 193, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[371, 369, 829, 743])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of ellagic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[376, 910, 813, 1724])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Polygoni Chinensis Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Ellagic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of ellagic acid (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 37, 1940, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Ellagic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of ellagic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (365 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time</td><td>0.2% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>85</td><td>15</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>70→50</td><td>30→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ellagic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ellagic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ellagic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ellagic acid peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject ellagic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of ellagic acid peak in the chromatogram of ellagic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ellagic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[59, 34, 1909, 259])\n\nsolution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of ellagic acid Std-FP. The retention times of ellagic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Chinensis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Chinensis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,ellagic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.59</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[342, 1286, 1805, 2119])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Polygoni Chinensis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-chinensis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Chinensis Herba","la":"Polygoni Chinensis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-cuspidati-rhizoma-et-radix-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[567, 1022, 1411, 1743])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 7, 1926, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[567, 1022, 1411, 1743])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 7, 1926, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix\n\nChinese Name: 虎杖\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Huzhang"},{"name":"12","text":"Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix is the dried rhizome and root of Polygonum cuspidatum Sieb. et Zucc. (Polygonaceae). The rhizome and root are collected in spring and autumn, the rootlets removed, washed clean, cut into short sections or thick slices when fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Mostly irregular thick slices, 0.5-9 cm long, 5-27 mm in diameter or pieces in short, cylindrical sections. Externally yellowish-brown, showing longitudinal wrinkles and rootlet scars. In transversely cut surface, bark relatively thin, easily separated from wood, wood broad, yellowish-brown with radial rays. Pith in the rhizome septated or hollowed. Texture hard. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Rhizome: Cork consists of several layers of cells, brownish-red. Cortex narrow, scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate. Pericycle fibre bundles arranged in the outer part of phloem. Phloem scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate and fibre bundles. Cambium in a ring. Xylem relatively broad. Phloem rays and xylem rays, 2-9 rows of cells, arranged radially, originated from the pith parenchyma. Pith parenchymatous cells contain starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\nRoot (8 mm in diameter): without pith compared with rhizome (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 10, 1983, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette shape, abundant, 25-100 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells somewhat cylindrical in shape, 25-45 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, pale yellow, with pits, branched at one end. Xylem fibres mostly in bundles, 25-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with pits. Cork cells brownish-yellow, subpolygonal to subsquare. Xylem ray parenchymatous cells subsquare to rectangular, the cell walls thickened, bead-like, with pits. Vessel elements mostly bordered-pitted, 30-150 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Starch granules abundant, simple granules rounded to ellipsoid, 3-22 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum dotted, cruciate or V-shaped; compound granules composed of 2-4 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"12","text":"Polydatin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.3 mg of polydatin CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of water, ethanol, ethyl acetate and acetic acid (4:2:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC polyamide plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately polydatin standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the TLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the TLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of polydatin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[42, 7, 1932, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 434, 1726, 2018])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericycle fibre bundle D. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle fibre bundle 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n7. Pith 8. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[879, 43, 984, 128])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 46, 1984, 86])\n\nGynostemmatis Herba\n\n絞股藍\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1621, 69, 1785, 123])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 403, 1735, 2022])\n\n50 μm\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericycle fibre bundle D. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle fibre bundle 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n7. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[22, 5, 1935, 258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[238, 435, 1706, 2124])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Microscopic features of powder of Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cluster of calcium oxalate 2. Branched stone cell 3. Xylem fibres 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Xylem ray parenchymatous cells 6. Bordered-pitted vessels 7. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[876, 42, 984, 131])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1892, 40, 1984, 86])\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子 Cnidii Fructus\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\nPolygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[467, 375, 1160, 689])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[470, 830, 980, 1117])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 Chemical structures of (i) polydatin and (ii) resveratrol\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Polydatin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (120 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.2 mg of polydatin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol.\n\nResveratrol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (130 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.3 mg of resveratrol CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (240 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[19, 3, 1930, 258])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>84</td><td>16</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>84→74</td><td>16→26</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>74</td><td>26</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of polydatin Std-FP and resveratrol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of polydatin and resveratrol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of polydatin and resveratrol peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from polydatin and resveratrol peaks should not be less than 8000 and 70000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject polydatin Std-FP, resveratrol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of polydatin and resveratrol peaks in the chromatograms of polydatin Std-FP, resveratrol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify polydatin and resveratrol peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of polydatin Std-FP and resveratrol Std-FP. The retention times of polydatin and resveratrol peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[20, 15, 1984, 266])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (polydatin)</td><td>0.43</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, resveratrol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.21</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[379, 885, 1788, 1404])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 6, 1928, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0% Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed polydatin and resveratrol standard stock solution, Std-Stock (600 mg/L for polydatin and 660 mg/L for resveratrol)\n\nWeigh accurately 3.0 mg of polydatin CRS and 3.3 mg of resveratrol CRS, and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol.\n\nMixed polydatin and resveratrol standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed polydatin and resveratrol Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 4.8, 9.6, 36, 60, 108 mg/L for polydatin and 5.28, 10.56, 39.6, 66, 118.8 mg/L for resveratrol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh accurately 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Add 5 mL of ethanol to the residue. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to the 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-cuspidati-rhizoma-et-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix","la":"Polygoni Cuspidati Rhizoma et Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-perfoliati-herba-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Perfoliati Herba v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[384, 890, 1527, 1712])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\nA. Polygoni Perfoliati Herba B. Magnified image of aerial part\n\nC. Upper surface of leaf D. Lower surface of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\nF. Magnified image of hooks"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[384, 890, 1527, 1712])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\nA. Polygoni Perfoliati Herba B. Magnified image of aerial part\n\nC. Upper surface of leaf D. Lower surface of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\nF. Magnified image of hooks"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Polygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\nChinese Name: 杠板歸\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gangbangui"},{"name":"14","text":"Polygoni Perfoliati Herba is the dried aerial part of Polygonum perfoliatum L. (Polygonaceae). The aerial part is collected during flowering period in summer, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Polygoni Perfoliati Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Stems slightly square, with ridges, mostly branched, up to 2.3 mm in diameter; externally purplish-red to purplish-brown, bearing reversed hooks on the ridges, nodes slightly swollen, internodes 2-6 cm long, fracture fibrous, yellowish-white, medullated or hollowed. Leaves simple, alternate, long petioled, peltate; lamina mostly crumpled, when whole, subequilateral triangle-shaped, greyish-green to reddish-brown, veins on the lower surface and petiole bear reversed hooks; stipular sheaths tightly surrounding the nodes or fallen off. Short spikes terminal or axillary at the upper part of stem, bracts rounded, flowers small, mostly withered or fallen off. Odour slight; taste bland for stem, and sour for leaf (Fig.1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Collenchyma consists of multiple layers of rhombic, rectangular or subsquare cells, sometimes broken. Cortex narrow, consisting of 3-5 layers of cells. Epidermis and cortical cells sometimes contain reddish-brown contents. Pericyclic fibres arranged in a ring, wall thickened and lignified. Phloem consists of several layers of cells. Cambium visible. Xylem vessels large, singly or 3-5 in groups. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells. Pith cells large, sometimes hollowed in the centre [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[31, 32, 1938, 261])\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells. Several layers of collenchymatous cells located under upper epidermis in the midrib and sometimes under lower epidermis. Phloem fibres in bundles. Clusters of calcium oxalate visible. Phloem consists of several layers of small subsquare parenchymatous cells. Xylem vessels slightly lignified. Reddish-brown contents scattered in mesophyll [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour yellowish-green to brownish-green. Clusters of calcium oxalate sometimes scattered, subrounded to rounded, 17-62 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Lower epidermal cells with sinuous anticlinal walls in surface view, stomata mostly paracytic or sometimes anisocytic, subsidiary cells 2-3. Vessels mainly spiral and bordered-pitted, 4-80 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Hook mostly broken, consisting of rhombic, rectangular or subsquare cells (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 891, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[736, 185, 853, 229])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 391, 1736, 2028])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Reddish-brown content\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Hook 4. Cortex 5. Pericyclic fibres 6. Phloem\n\n7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Reddish-brown content 10. Cluster of calcium oxalate 11. Pith\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[231, 396, 1599, 2112])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration (midrib with blade) C. Magnified section of blade 1. Upper epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Phloem fibres 4. Cluster of calcium oxalate 5. Spongy tissue 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Lower epidermis 9. Reddish-brown content\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[735, 185, 854, 229])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1267, 194, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[247, 455, 1739, 2142])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Lower epidermal cells with stomata\n\n3. Vessels (3-1 spiral vessels, 3-2 bordered-pitted vessels) 4. Fragment of hook\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 40, 1940, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, 2-butanone, methanol, water and formic acid (6:2:1:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[419, 367, 906, 682])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[434, 749, 904, 1207])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) quercetin and (ii) quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[375, 1372, 821, 2014])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 37, 1939, 263])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L) Weigh 2.5 mg of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (305 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>0.5% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-45</td><td>30→68</td><td>70→32</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peak should not be less than 18000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peak in the chromatogram of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP. The retention times of quercetin 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, quercetin3-O-β-D-glucuronide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.00</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[332, 367, 1790, 1283])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Polygoni Perfoliati Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[62, 31, 1930, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/polygoni-perfoliati-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Polygoni Perfoliati Herba","la":"Polygoni Perfoliati Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/poria-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Poria v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[416, 876, 1572, 1912])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried whole Poria (Fulingge)\n\n</div>\n\nA. Whole Poria (Fulingge) B. Fracture surface of Poria\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n龍脷葉\n\nSauropi Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸棗仁\n\n1 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried and cut Poria (Fulingkuai)\n\n</div>\n\nCaryophylli Flos丁香\n\nMenthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\n薄荷\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n龍脷葉\n\nSauropi Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[365, 533, 1651, 1787])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried and slices Poria (Fulingpian)\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"17","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[416, 876, 1572, 1912])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried whole Poria (Fulingge)\n\n</div>\n\nA. Whole Poria (Fulingge) B. Fracture surface of Poria\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n龍脷葉\n\nSauropi Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸棗仁\n\n1 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried and cut Poria (Fulingkuai)\n\n</div>\n\nCaryophylli Flos丁香\n\nMenthae Haplocalycis Herba\n\n薄荷\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n龍脷葉\n\nSauropi Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[365, 533, 1651, 1787])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (iii) A photograph of dried and slices Poria (Fulingpian)\n\n</div>"},{"name":"17","text":"Official name: Poria\n\nChinese name: 茯苓\n\nChinese phonetic name: Fuling"},{"name":"17","text":"Poria is the dried sclerotium of the fungus, Poria cocos (Schw.) Wolf (Polyporaceae). The sclerotium is collected mostly in July to September with soil removed, piled up to allow evaporation of moisture onto the surface, then spread to dry. Repeat this operation for several times until wrinkles appears on the surface and most of the water inside evaporates, then dried in the shade to obtain \"Fulingge\" (whole Poria). The fresh sclerotium is peeled and cut into pieces, then dried in the shade, the cubic pieces and sliced pieces are known as \"Fulingkuai\" (cubic Poria) and \"Fulingpian\" (sliced Poria) respectively."},{"name":"17","text":"Fulingge (whole Poria) Subglobose, ellipsoid, oblate or irregular in shape, varying in size, 45-200 mm in length. The outer skin thin and rough, brown to blackish-brown, conspicuously shriveled and striated. Texture hard and compact, fracture surface granular, with cracks, the outer layer pale brown to brown, inner part white, rarely reddish. Odour slight; taste bland and sticky when chewed [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nFulingkuai (cubic Poria) Occurring in cubic pieces, varying in size, 8-14 mm in thickness. White, pale red or pale brown [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\nFulingpian (sliced Poria) Occurring in irregular thick slices, 2-6 mm in thickness. White, pale red or pale brown [Fig. 1 (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Powder\n\nColour greyish-white. Irregularly granular masses and branched masses colourless, dissolved gradually on mounting in chloral hydrate solution. Hyphae colourless or pale brown, slender, slightly curved, branched, 2-9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 2).\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸棗仁\n\nPoria\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[297, 448, 1663, 2097])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Poria (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Masses (1-1 granular masses, 1-2 branched masses)\n\n2. Hyphae (2-1 hyphae colourless, 2-2 hyphae pale brown)\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[18, 16, 1980, 255])"},{"name":"17","text":"Dehydrotumulosic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dehydrotumulosic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nPachymic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of pachymic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Prepare a mixture of toluene, ethyl acetate and formic acid (20:6:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"17","text":"Add slowly 80 mL of sulphuric acid to 20 mL of ethanol and dissolve 2 g of vanillin. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dehydrotumulosic acid standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), pachymic acid standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots and bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n明黛参 Changii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 437, 933, 814])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 1056, 979, 1423])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) dehydrotumulosic acid and (ii) pachymic acid\n\n</div>\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nPoria\n\n沒藥\n\n茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 375, 1128, 1212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Poria extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Pachymic acid standard solution 2. Dehydrotumulosic acid standard solution\n\n3. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried whole Poria (Fulingge)\n\n(ii) dried and cut Poria (Fulingkuai)\n\n(iii) dried and slices Poria (Fulingpian)\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of dehydrotumulosic acid and pachymic acid (Fig. 4).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 17, 1963, 255])"},{"name":"17","text":"Dehydrotumulosic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of dehydrotumulosic acid CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"17","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 20-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"17","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (242 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.05% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>60→65</td><td>40→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>65→95</td><td>35→5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-15</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"17","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dehydrotumulosic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dehydrotumulosic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dehydrotumulosic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dehydrotumulosic acid peak should not be less than 5000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"Separately inject dehydrotumulosic acid Std-FP and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dehydrotumulosic acid peak in the chromatogram of dehydrotumulosic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dehydrotumulosic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dehydrotumulosic acid Std-FP. The retention times of dehydrotumulosic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Poria extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Poria extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, dehydrotumulosic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.20</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.43</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.64</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.58</td><td>$\\pm$0.10</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>3.19</td><td>$\\pm$0.16</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[329, 1568, 1821, 2486])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried whole Poria (Fulingge) extract\n\n</div>\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[326, 354, 1838, 1297])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried and cut Poria (Fulingkuai) extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[329, 1470, 1823, 2412])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried and slices Poria (Fulingpian) extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[37, 28, 1954, 256])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"17","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 0.50%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"17","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 18%."},{"name":"17","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 1.8%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 1.9%."},{"name":"17","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"17","text":"Mixed dehydrotumulosic acid and pachymic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (250 mg/L each) Weigh accurately 2.5 mg of dehydrotumulosic acid CRS and 2.5 mg of pachymic acid CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed dehydrotumulosic acid and pachymic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed dehydrotumulosic acid and pachymic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 5,10,25,50,100 mg/L for both dehydrotumulosic acid and pachymic acid."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/poria","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Poria","la":"Poria"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/potentillae-chinensis-herba-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Potentillae Chinensis Herba v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[15, 783, 1567, 1919])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nA. Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nB. The upper surface of leaves\n\nC. The lower surface of leaves\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[1268, 194, 1388, 239])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[15, 783, 1567, 1919])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nA. Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nB. The upper surface of leaves\n\nC. The lower surface of leaves\n\nD. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[1268, 194, 1388, 239])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nChinese Name: 委陵菜\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Weilingcai"},{"name":"14","text":"Potentillae Chinensis Herba is the dried herb of Potentilla chinensis Ser. (Rosaceae). The whole plant is collected in spring before sprouting of stems, soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Potentillae Chinensis Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Roots cylindrical or subconical, slightly twisted, sometimes branched, occasionally broken, 3-14 cm long, up to 10 mm in diameter; externally dark brown to dark purplish-red, striated longitudinally, the outer bark coarse, easily exfoliated, root vertex slightly swollen; texture hard, easily broken; transverse section of root bark thin, dark brown, easily separated from wood, rays arranged radially. Compound leaves basal, odd-pinnate, petiolate; leaflets narrowly long-elliptic, margins pinnatipartite, the lower surface and petiole densely covered with greyish-white pubescence. Odour slight; taste astringent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Root: Cork consists of 4 to more than 10 layers of tangentially elongated cells, outer layer heavily stained. Cortical cells subrounded or irregular, arranged loosely, not obvious. Cleft frequently located on the outer part of phloem. Phloem narrow. Cambium distinct, arranged in undulate ring. Xylem broad, vessels radially arranged. Rays distinct, broad. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, mainly scattered in xylem rays [Fig. 2(i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[31, 33, 1938, 262])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare cells, covered with cuticle. Palisade tissue consists of 2-3 layers of cells, sometimes scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate. Spongy tissue consists of several layers of subrounded cells. Collenchyma consists of 2-4 layers of cells underneath phloem, and below the upper and lower epidermis. Vascular bundles collateral, xylem half-moon shaped, phloem crescent-shaped. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found in palisade tissue and parenchyma. Lower epidermal cells relatively small, tangentially elongated. Non-glandular hair unicellular mostly curved, raised from the upper and lower epidermis, abundant on the lower epidermis [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Non-glandular hairs numerous, unicellular, 2 types, first type with thinner cell wall, long and slender, 4-7 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, always twisted into mass; second type with thicker cell wall, varing in length, short one bended, in hook-shaped or straight; long one mostly broken, straight or slightly bended, 4-45 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette shape, singly scattered or distributed among parenchymatous cell, 4-52 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, and small prisms of calcium oxalate occasionally visible; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Lower epidermal cells with stomata mostly covered with non-glandular hair, occasionally visible; stomata anomocytic. Cork cells subpolygonal or flat-rectangular, containing yellowish-brown contents. Xylem fibres long fusiform, 7-20 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls slightly thickened and pit canals distinct. Vessels mainly spiral, reticulate or bordered-pitted (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[796, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[300, 597, 948, 1246])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[380, 1324, 945, 1828])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1002, 616, 1656, 1820])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Phloem 5. Cambium\n\n6. Xylem 7. Ray 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[243, 586, 1643, 1878])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Potentillae Chinensis Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Collenchyma 5. Xylem\n\n6. Phloem 7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Lower epidermis 9. Non-glandular hair\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[796, 75, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[735, 185, 854, 229])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1267, 194, 1387, 240])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[304, 521, 1275, 821])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1294, 521, 1685, 816])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[296, 849, 789, 1368])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[803, 848, 1294, 1366])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1314, 848, 1494, 1061])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1512, 847, 1687, 1062])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1318, 1078, 1686, 1369])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[297, 1394, 961, 1929])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[990, 1394, 1686, 1663])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[991, 1685, 1686, 1932])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Non-glandular hairs (1-1 with thinner cell wall, 1-2 with thicker cell wall)\n\n2. Crystals of calcium oxalate (2-1 clusters of calcium oxalate, 2-2 prisms of calcium oxalate)\n\n3. Lower epidermal cells with stomata 4. Cork cells 5. Xylem fibres 6. Spiral vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[39, 41, 1937, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nLuteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol, water and formic acid (40:5:5:2, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[430, 369, 1082, 670])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[437, 740, 1079, 1087])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and (ii) luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 1309, 968, 2199])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Potentillae Chinensis Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution\n\n2. Apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution 3. Test solution\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 38, 1939, 262])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nLuteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 3 3 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>85→75</td><td>15→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>75→70</td><td>25→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks should not be less than 45000 and 30000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks in the chromatograms of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP, luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP. The retention times of apigenin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide and luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Potentillae Chinensis Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Potentillae Chinensis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, luteolin 7-O-β-D-glucuronide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(apigenin 7-O-β-D-glucuronide)</td><td>1.30</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(luteolin)</td><td>2.08</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(tiliroside)</td><td>2.18</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[339, 369, 1805, 910])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Potentillae Chinensis Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/potentillae-chinensis-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Potentillae Chinensis Herba","la":"Potentillae Chinensis Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/potentillae-discoloris-herba-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Potentillae Discoloris Herba v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Potentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[397, 844, 1591, 1919])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Potentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[397, 844, 1591, 1919])"},{"name":"15","text":"A. Potentillae Discoloris Herba\n\nB. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of fracture of tuberous root"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Potentillae Discoloris Herba\n\nChinese Name: 翻白草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Fanbaicao"},{"name":"15","text":"Potentillae Discoloris Herba is the dried whole plant of Potentilla discolor Bge. (Rosaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer and autumn before flowering, soil and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Potentillae Discoloris Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Tuberous root spindle-shaped or cylindrical, 2-13 cm long, 3-15 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish brown to dark brown, irregularly twisted-furrowed; texture hard and brittle. Fracture flat, greyish-white to yellowish-white. Stem straight and brittle, the surface covered with white pubescence. Basal leaves tufted, odd-pinnate, petiole varies in length, mainly crumpled and bent; leaflets 5-9, petiole varies in length or sessile, oblong to long-elliptic, apical leaflets relatively larger, upper surface greyish-green to dark green, lower surface densely covered with white pubescence, margin coarse. Odour slight; taste sweet and slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Root: Rhytidome broken, cell walls slightly thickened, suberized. Cork consists of 4 to more than 10 layers of flattened cells, cells subrounded or irregular in shape, loosely arranged. Phloem narrow, with clusters of calcium oxalate inside the cells of the outer layer. Cambium arranged in a wavy ring. Xylem broad, occupying about 4/5 of the central portion of the root, vessels arranged radially. Rays distinct, broad. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, mainly distributed in the xylem rays [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[38, 33, 1954, 244])\n\nStem: Non-glandular hairs commonly found on the surface of the epidermis. Epidermis consists of 1-2 layers of cells, cells subrounded to subsquare. Cortex consists of several layers of parenchymatous cells. Pericycle fibres consist of 4-6 layers of cells, arranged in a ring; fibres small, cell walls thickened. Phloem relatively narrow, arranged in a ring. Cambium indistinct. Xylem relatively narrow, arranged radially. Pith broad, usually hollow in the centre, occupying 3/5 of the central portion of the stem, clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found [Fig. 2 (ii)].\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare to subrounded parenchymatous cells. Palisade tissue consists of 2-3 layers of cells, sometimes with clusters of calcium oxalate. Spongy tissue narrow, with clusters of calcium oxalate. Non-glandular hairs unicellular and mostly curved, found on the surfaces of epidermis, but more abundant on the lower epidermis. Vascular bundles collateral, xylem half-moon-shaped, phloem crescent-shaped. Lower epidermal cells relatively small, tangentially elongated [Fig. 2 (iii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Non-glandular hairs numerous, found in stem and largely from lower epidermis of leaf, two types: one type with a thin cell wall, long, sinuous and slender, 4-17 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, always twisted together; another type with a thicker cell wall, straight or slightly bent, 6-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate aggregated in rosette shape and singly scattered, distributed in parenchymatous cells, 3-53 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; small prisms of calcium oxalate occasionally found; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Upper epidermal cells of leaf polygonal in surface view, anticlinal wall nearly straight, always showing cicatrix of non-glandular hairs. Fibres mostly in bundles; single fibres 5-38 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, relatively long, walls thickened; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly of bordered-pitted and spiral; reticulate vessels occasionally found. Starch granules mostly single, spherical or oblong in shape; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 240])\n\n天葵子\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 403, 1737, 2073])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of tuberous root of Potentillae Discoloris Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope) D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Rhytidome 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Ray\n\n8. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1025, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 430, 1727, 2126])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Potentillae Discoloris Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope) D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Pericycle fibres 5. Phloem 6. Xylem\n\n7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Pith\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 239])\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\nPotentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[237, 399, 1732, 2101])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Potentillae Discoloris Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Non-glandular hair 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[197, 146, 288, 188])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma 白及\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[926, 87, 1025, 137])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1757, 52, 1911, 108])\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[254, 436, 1729, 2098])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Potentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n1. Non-glandular hairs with thin cell wall (1-1 single, 1-2 twisted together)\n\n2. Non-glandular hair with a thick cell wall 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Upper epidermal cells of leaf with a cicatrix of non-glandular hair (->) 5. Fibre bundle\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 spiral vessel, 6-2 bordered-pitted vessel) 7. Starch granule\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"15","text":"Ellagic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of ellagic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nKaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, formic acid, ethyl acetate and water (12:6:4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ellagic acid standard solution, kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 30 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[924, 86, 1028, 139])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[444, 366, 880, 719])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[468, 783, 983, 1239])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) ellagic acid and (ii) kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[376, 1401, 846, 2187])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Potentillae Discoloris Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Ellagic acid standard solution\n\n2. Kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of ellagic acid and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Ellagic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of ellagic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nKaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 80 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Transfer the residue to a 50-mL conical flask, then add 15 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue for two times each with 2 mL of methanol (70%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>82</td><td>18</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>82→75</td><td>18→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ellagic acid Std-FP and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of ellagic acid and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide should not be more\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[38, 36, 1949, 246])\n\nthan 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of ellagic acid and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from ellagic acid and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks should not be less than 30000 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject ellagic acid Std-FP, kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of ellagic acid and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks in the chromatograms of ellagic acid Std-FP, kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ellagic acid and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of ellagic acid Std-FP and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide Std-FP. The retention times of ellagic acid and kaempferol 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucuronide peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Potentillae Discoloris Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Potentillae Discoloris Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.82</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(quercetin3-O-β-D-glucuronide)</td><td>0.97</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,ellagic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(kaempferol3-O-β-D-glucuronide)</td><td>1.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[335, 364, 1821, 775])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Potentillae Discoloris Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[48, 42, 234, 122])\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[925, 86, 1028, 138])"},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/potentillae-discoloris-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Potentillae Discoloris Herba","la":"Potentillae Discoloris Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/psidii-guajavae-folium-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Psidii Guajavae Folium v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[622, 1016, 948, 1402])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[953, 1017, 1165, 1414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1200, 1016, 1406, 1452])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[629, 1484, 954, 1727])\n\nD\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1057, 1485, 1379, 1726])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Psidii Guajavae Folium\n\nA. Psidii Guajavae Folium B. Upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Lower surface of leaf D. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[622, 1016, 948, 1402])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[953, 1017, 1165, 1414])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1200, 1016, 1406, 1452])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[629, 1484, 954, 1727])\n\nD\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1057, 1485, 1379, 1726])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n1 mm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Psidii Guajavae Folium\n\nA. Psidii Guajavae Folium B. Upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Lower surface of leaf D. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nE. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Psidii Guajavae Folium\n\nChinese Name: 番石榴葉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Fanshiliuye"},{"name":"13","text":"Psidii Guajavae Folium is the dried leaf of Psidium guajava L. (Myrtaceae). The leaf is collected in spring and summer, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Psidii Guajavae Folium."},{"name":"13","text":"Mostly crumpled, rolled or broken. When intact, oblong to elliptic, 3-14 cm long, 2-7 cm wide, apex rounded or acuminate, base obtuse to rounded, margin entire. The upper surface yellowish-green to pale brown, glabrous or nearly glabrous, sparsely dark brown glandular-punctate. The lower surface greyish-brown to dark green, densely covered with pubescence, principal and lateral veins slightly protuberant. Petioles 2-10 mm long. Texture leathery. Odour delicately aromatic; taste astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of flat polygonal cells, covered with cuticle; with unicellular non-glandular hairs on the surface. Lower epidermal cells slightly small, sparsely covered with non-glandular hairs. Hypodermis consists of 2-3 layers of collenchymatous cells, orderly arranged, with secretory glands. Palisade tissue consists of 2 layers of columnar cells, discontinue at the midrib. Spongy tissue consists of slightly small cells, with secretory glands. Vascular bundle of the midrib bicollateral, surrounded by pericycle fibres. Xylem vessels radially arranged. Phloem narrow. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate and prisms of calcium oxalate scattered in the mesophyll. Collenchymatous cells subrounded, located beneath the lower epidermis (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 27, 1956, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to yellowish-green. Non-glandular hairs unicellular. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, in rosette aggregate, scattered or embedded in parenchymatous cells, 6-44 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Lower epidermal cells polygonal, with paracytic stomata, with 2 subsidiary cells. Secretory glands usually broken, the intact ones subrounded. Calcium oxalate prism sheath occasionally visible, Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered or embedded in parenchymatous cells, sometimes arranged in rows; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres slender, some with the walls slightly undulantly curved; white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mostly spiral, 6-40 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 202, 241])\n\n天葵子\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 398, 1746, 2123])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Psidii Guajavae Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prism and clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Prism and clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Non-glandular hair F. Secretory gland\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Hypodermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Non-glandular hair 6. Pericycle fiber 7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n10. Cluster of calcium oxalate 11. Collenchyma 12. Secretory gland 13. Lower epidermis\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n天冬\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma 白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 714, 1703, 2128])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Psidii Guajavae Folium\n\n</div>\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate 3. Lower epidermis cells with paracytic stomata\n\n4. Secretory gland 5. Calcium oxalate prisms sheath 6. Pericycle fibres 7. Spiral vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"13","text":"Guaijaverin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of guaijaverin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 50% ).\n\nHyperoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of hyperoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate, formic acid, ethanol and water (6.5:3.5:3:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (350 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately guaijaverin standard solution, hyperoside standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[439, 363, 866, 806])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[448, 874, 872, 1309])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) guaijaverin and (ii) hyperoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 1457, 995, 2327])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Psidii Guajavae Folium extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Hyperoside standard solution 2. Guaijaverin standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of guaijaverin and hyperoside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Guaijaverin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of guaijaverin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nHyperoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of hyperoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>2.5mM Sodium acetate solution(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>86</td><td>14</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-60</td><td>86→82</td><td>14→18</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of guaijaverin Std-FP and hyperoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of guaijaverin and hyperoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of guaijaverin and hyperoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from guaijaverin and hyperoside peaks should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])"},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject guaijaverin Std-FP, hyperoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of guaijaverin and hyperoside peaks in the chromatograms of guaijaverin Std-FP, hyperoside Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify guaijaverin and hyperoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of guaijaverin Std-FP and hyperoside Std-FP. The retention times of guaijaverin and hyperoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Psidii Guajavae Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Psidii Guajavae Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(ellagic acid)</td><td>0.91</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,hyperoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(isoquercitrin)</td><td>1.06</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4(reynoutrin)</td><td>1.23</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5(guaijaverin)</td><td>1.32</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>6(avicularin)</td><td>1.43</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[348, 374, 1825, 1167])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Psidii Guajavae Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 5.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[40, 38, 1952, 252])"},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 22.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/psidii-guajavae-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Psidii Guajavae Folium","la":"Psidii Guajavae Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-achyranthis-bidentatae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[9, 771, 1566, 1953])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[9, 771, 1566, 1953])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\nChinese Name: 牛膝\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Niuxi"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae is the dried root of Achyranthes bidentata Bl. (Amaranthaceae). The root is collected in the winter time when the aerial part has withered. Following removal of rootlets and soil, the roots are tied up into a small bundle, sun-dried until they become wrinkled externally, cut evenly at the summit, then dried under the sun thoroughly to obtain Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae."},{"name":"11","text":"Slender and cylindrical, straight or slightly curved, 13-90 cm long, 4-10 mm in diameter. Outer surface greyish-yellow or pale brown, with slightly twisted and fine longitudinal wrinkles, transverse lenticels, and sparsely distributed rootlet scars. Texture hard and fragile, softened when moistened, easily broken; fracture even, pale yellowish-brown, slightly keratose and oily. Xylem of vascular bundles in the center relatively large, yellowish-white; xylem in the outside small, in many scattered and spotted vascular bundles, arranged in 2-4 whorls. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet to somewhat bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"The cork consisting of several rows of cells. Cortex parenchyma cells elongated and arranged in rows tangentially. Vascular bundles arranged in 2-4 interrupted whorls, relatively small in the outermost whorl, but relatively large inward, sometimes having only one to several vessels. Cambium nearly in a ring in the outermost whorl. Xylem mainly consists of vessels and small\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1956, 253])\n\nxylem fibres; the central xylem aggregated into 2-3 groups. Parenchyma cells contain crystal sand of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale brown to greyish-white. Crystal sand of calcium oxalate triangular, arrow-pointed, subsquare or irregular in shape, scattered in the parenchyma cells, polychrome when observed under a polarized microscope. Bordered-pitted and reticulate vessels 8-93 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fibres mostly in bundles, walls slightly thickened, pits sparse, pit apertures oblique-porous, crisscross or V-shaped. Cork cells subsquare, subrectangular, subround or polygonal (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter. Transfer 0.5 mL of the filtrate to a test tube. Cautiously add about 0.5 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for 20 min. A reddish-brown or yellowish-brown ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"11","text":"Oleanolic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of oleanolic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and ethyl acetate (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Mix 1 mL of dilute sulphuric acid ( 50% , v/v) and 10 mL of p-hydroxybenzaldehyde in methanol ( 2% , w/v ). Freshly prepare the reagent.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[44, 0, 1955, 252])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[233, 429, 1712, 2269])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundles in the outermost whorl D. Crystal sand of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cambium 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[14, 10, 1961, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[344, 465, 720, 802])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[726, 462, 1172, 799])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1179, 459, 1642, 798])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[352, 819, 941, 1147])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[950, 812, 1642, 1213])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[341, 1217, 944, 1753])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[951, 1224, 1644, 1750])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\n</div>\n\n1. Crystal sand of calcium oxalate 2. Bordered-pitted vessels 3. Reticulate vessel 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Xylem fibres in a bundle 6. Xylem fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[52, 0, 1964, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (75%) and 3 mL of hydrochloric acid. Reflux the mixture for 1.5 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of water. Transfer the solution to a separatory funnel. Extract with 100 mL of dichloromethane. Collect the dichloromethane layer and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately oleanolic acid standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of oleanolic acid.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(1) \n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[717, 1500, 1375, 1901])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[694, 1949, 1396, 2453])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) oleanolic acid and (ii) ecdysterone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Ecdysterone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of ecdysterone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (270 nm) and a column $ (3.9\\times 300\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-40</td><td>95→55</td><td>5→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ecdysterone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ecdysterone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ecdysterone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ecdysterone peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject ecdysterone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of ecdysterone peak in the chromatogram of ecdysterone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify ecdysterone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[43, 0, 1967, 251])\n\nchromatogram of ecdysterone Std-FP. The retention times of ecdysterone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,ecdysterone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.03</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.30</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.32</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[396, 1336, 1754, 1794])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 16.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 59.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 54.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-achyranthis-bidentatae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae 地黄\n\n王不留行 Radix Rehmanniae\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Monograph - Supplementary Information of HKCMM Standards, Volume 2\n\n</div>\n\nAssay in Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae 地黄\n\n王不留行 Radix Rehmanniae\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Monograph - Supplementary Information of HKCMM Standards, Volume 2\n\n</div>\n\nAssay in Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae"},{"name":"15","text":"![](page=2,bbox=[32, 2, 1943, 251])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\nChinese Name: 牛膝\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Niuxi"},{"name":"15","text":"Radix Achyranthis Bidentata is the dried root of Achyranthes bidentata Bl. (Amaranthaceae). The root is collected in the winter time when the aerial part has withered. Following removal of rootlets and soil, the roots are tied up into a small bundle, sun-dried until they become wrinkled externally, cut evenly at the summit, then dried under the sun thoroughly to obtain Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae."},{"name":"15","text":"Slender and cylindrical, straight or slightly curved, 13-90 cm long, 4-10 mm in diameter. Outer surface greyish-yellow or pale brown, with slightly twisted and fine longitudinal wrinkles, transverse lenticels, and sparsely distributed rootlet scars. Texture hard and fragile, softened when moistened, easily broken; fracture even, pale yellowish-brown, slightly keratose and oily. Xylem of vascular bundles in the center relatively large, yellowish-white; xylem in the outside small, in many scattered and spotted vascular bundles, arranged in 2-4 whorls. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet to somewhat bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"The cork consisting of several rows of cells. Cortex parenchyma cells elongated and arranged in rows tangentially. Vascular bundles arranged in 2-4 interrupted whorls, relatively small in the outermost whorl, but relatively large inward, sometimes having only one to several vessels. Cambium nearly in a ring in the outermost whorl. Xylem mainly consists of vessels and small xylem fibres; the central xylem aggregated into 2-3 groups. Parenchyma cells contain crystal sand of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 3, 1966, 251])"},{"name":"15","text":"Colour pale brown to greyish-white. Crystal sand of calcium oxalate triangular, arrow-pointed, subsquare or irregular in shape, scattered in the parenchyma cells, polychrome when observed under a polarized microscope. Bordered-pitted and reticulate vessels 8-93 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fibres mostly in bundles, walls slightly thickened, pits sparse, pit apertures oblique-porous, crisscross or V-shaped. Cork cells subsquare, subrectangular, subround or polygonal (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter. Transfer 0.5 mL of the filtrate to a test tube. Cautiously add about 0.5 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for 20 min. A reddish-brown or yellowish-brown ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"15","text":"Oleanolic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of oleanolic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and ethyl acetate (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Mix 1 mL of dilute sulphuric acid ( 50% , v/v) and 10 mL of p-hydroxybenzaldehyde in methanol ( 2% , w/v ). Freshly prepare the reagent.\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[31, 2, 1946, 250])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[233, 439, 1729, 2272])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundles in the outermost whorl\n\nD. Crystal sand of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cambium 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n</div>\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n銀杏葉\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1362, 64, 1446, 105])\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[250, 458, 1724, 1862])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\n1. Crystal sand of calcium oxalate 2. Bordered-pitted vessels 3. Reticulate vessel 4. Cork cells\n\n5. Xylem fibres in a bundle 6. Xylem fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[41, 0, 1937, 251])"},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (75%) and 3 mL of hydrochloric acid. Reflux the mixture for 1.5 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of water. Transfer the solution to a separatory funnel. Extract with 100 mL of dichloromethane. Collect the dichloromethane layer and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately oleanolic acid standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of oleanolic acid.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[663, 1503, 1248, 1865])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[688, 1928, 1326, 2388])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) oleanolic acid and (ii) ecdysterone\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[27, 0, 1961, 247])"},{"name":"15","text":"Ecdysterone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of ecdysterone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ RC filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (270 nm) and a column $ (3.9\\times 300\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-40</td><td>95→55</td><td>5→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ecdysterone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ecdysterone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ecdysterone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ecdysterone peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject ecdysterone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of ecdysterone peak in the chromatogram of ecdysterone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify ecdysterone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of ecdysterone Std-FP. The retention times of ecdysterone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[37, 0, 1941, 252])\n\npeaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,ecdysterone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.03</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.30</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.32</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[391, 1437, 1753, 1909])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 3, 1964, 244])\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 16.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 59.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 54.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-achyranthis-bidentatae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae","la":"Radix Achyranthis Bidentatae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-aconiti-praeparata-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Aconiti Praeparata v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[489, 855, 1503, 1882])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Aconiti Praeparata\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1968, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[489, 855, 1503, 1882])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Aconiti Praeparata\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1968, 256])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Radix Aconiti Praeparata\n\nChinese Name: 製川烏\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhichuanwu"},{"name":"10","text":"Radix Aconiti Praeparata is the dried and processed tuberous parent root of Aconitum carmichaeli Debx. (Ranunculaceae). The parent root is collected in the summer, then dried to obtain Radix Aconiti. Clean Radix Aconiti is first graded according to the size, then macerated in water until there is no more dry core, then taken out, boiled in water for 4 to 6 hours (or steamed for 6 to 8 hours) until there is no more white core in the relatively large and solid root, and the taste becomes slightly tongue-numbing. After removal from the boiling water, it is air-dried as appropriate, cut into slices, and dried to obtain Radix Aconiti Praeparata."},{"name":"10","text":"Radix Aconiti Praeparata consists of irregular or elongated triangular slices. The outer part dark brown or yellowish-brown, the inner part with a greyish-brown cambium ring. Texture light and fragile, fracture lustrous. Odourless; the taste slightly tongue-numbing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour greyish-yellow. Stone cells nearly colourless or pale yellowish-green, subrectangular, subsquare, polygonal, or oblique at one side, 36-125 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, 75-204 $ \\mu $ m long, walls 5-17 $ \\mu $ m thick, striations of the thick-walled stone cells distinct, with relatively sparse pits. Cells of metaderm brown, some with walls irregularly thickened into a bulge and intruding into the lumen. Starch grains mostly gelatinized, but ungelatinized ones occasionally visible. Bordered\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 3, 1955, 253])\n\npitted vessels pale yellow, 18-67 $ \\mu $m in diameter, the ends truncate or shortly pointed, perforated at the end or the lateral walls, some vessel elements thick and short, tortuous or connected in a crisscross pattern (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{1} $\n\nDissolve 0.85 g of bismuth subnitrate in 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water, then add 20 mL of aqueous potassium iodide solution (40%, w/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Transfer the solution to a test tube. Add 2 drops of potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{1}. $ An orange or orangish-brown precipitate is observed."},{"name":"10","text":"Benzoylmesaconine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of benzoylmesaconine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of toluene, ethyl acetate and diethylamine (6:4:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add 2 mL of hydrogen hexachloroplatinate (IV) solution to 48 mL of water."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 50 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent\n\nMix 50 mL of solution A and 50 mL of solution B. Freshly prepare the reagent.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 260])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[402, 471, 973, 905])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[998, 471, 1563, 903])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[404, 921, 973, 1369])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[998, 931, 1558, 1368])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[404, 1385, 976, 1796])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1002, 1383, 1546, 1805])\n\n100 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Aconiti Praeparata\n\n</div>\n\n1. Polygonal stone cell 2. Subrectangular stone cell 3. Cells of metaderm 4. Gelatinized starch grains\n\n5. Vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 1, 1940, 250])"},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 2 mL of ammonium solution and 12 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 2 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to another tube. Evaporate the solvent to dryness with a gentle stream of nitrogen. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of dichloromethane."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately benzoylmesaconine standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of benzoylmesaconine.\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[723, 1341, 1330, 1832])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of benzoylmesaconine\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Benzoylmesaconine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of benzoylmesaconine CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of 0.01 M hydrochloric acid."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 10-mL centrifugal tube, then add 5 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[44, 0, 1966, 252])"},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (240 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, pH: 1-12). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Ammonium bicarbonate solution* (%v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile (%v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-50</td><td>95→40</td><td>5→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>40→0</td><td>60→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n* Ammonium bicarbonate solution\n\nDissolve 0.79 g of ammonium bicarbonate in 1 L of water and adjust the pH to 10 with 4 mL of ammonia solution."},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of benzoylmesaconine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of benzoylmesaconine should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of benzoylmesaconine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from benzoylmesaconine peak should not be less than 60000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject benzoylmesaconine Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of benzoylmesaconine peak in the chromatogram of the benzoylmesaconine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify benzoylmesaconine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of benzoylmesaconine Std-FP. The retention times of benzoylmesaconine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Aconiti Praeparata extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[40, 0, 1952, 253])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Aconiti Praeparata extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.30</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.32</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,benzoylmesaconine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.69</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[398, 831, 1758, 1363])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Aconiti Praeparata extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 1, 1975, 255])\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"The sample contains not more than 0.022% of the total content of aconitine $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{3 4} \\mathrm{H}_{4 7} \\mathrm{N O}_{1 1} \\right) $ hypaconitine $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{3 3} \\mathrm{H}_{4 5} \\mathrm{N O}_{1 0} \\right) $ and mesaconitine $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{3 3} \\mathrm{H}_{4 5} \\mathrm{N O}_{1 1} \\right) $ calculated with reference to the dried substance."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-aconiti-praeparata","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Aconiti Praeparata","la":"Radix Aconiti Praeparata"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-angelicae-pubescentis-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Angelicae Pubescentis v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[571, 802, 1585, 1897])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Angelicae Pubescentis\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[571, 802, 1585, 1897])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Angelicae Pubescentis\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Radix Angelicae Pubescentis\n\nChinese Name: 獨活\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Duhuo"},{"name":"13","text":"Radix Angelicae Pubescentis is the dried root of Angelica pubescens Maxim. f. biserrata Shan et Yuan (Apiaceae/Umbelliferae). The root is collected in early spring before sprouting or in late autumn when stem and leaves wither. After removal of stem remnant, rootlets and soil, the root is baked to half dry, piled for 2-3 days to soften, then baked to dryness to obtain Radix Angelicae Pubescentis."},{"name":"13","text":"Somewhat cylindrical, with 2-3 branches or more at the lower part, 4.5-23 cm long. The upper part bulgy, marked with transverse wrinkles, 6-45 mm in diameter, the apex exhibiting remains of stems and leaves or sunken spots. Outer surface greyish-brown or dark brown, marked with longitudinal wrinkles, protuberant elongate lenticels and slightly protuberant rootlet scars. Texture relatively hard, softening when moistened; fracture showing bark greyish-white, scattered with numerous brown oil dots; cambium ring brown; wood greyish-yellow to yellowish-brown. Odour with distinctive fragrance; taste bitteracrid, slightly tongue-numbing (Fig.1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Cork consisting of 3-12 layers of cells. Cortex narrow, with a few oil ducts. Phloem broad, occupying about 1/2 the radius of root, often with clefts; oil ducts relatively abundant, arranged in several rings, subround or elliptical, surrounded by 6-22 secretory cells, smaller near the cambium. Cambium in a ring. Rays 1-4 rows of cells wide. Vessels singly or in group, arranged radially in the xylem. The parenchyma cells contain starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[25, 0, 1951, 248])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale brown or brown. Oil ducts mostly broken, in transverse view the secretory cells appear elongated-subround, 5-30 $ \\mu $m in diameter, mostly containing yellowish-green or pale yellowish-brown secretions and oily drops. Phloem parenchyma cells colourless or pale yellowish, spindle-shaped, wall slightly thickened, with very fine oblique crisscross striations, 7-38 $ \\mu $m in diameter, thin and sinuous, transverse septa visible. Starch granules abundant, small; simple granules subround or elliptical, the hilum sometimes visible in large ones, punctate or herringbone-shaped, 2-13 $ \\mu $m in diameter; compound granules frequent, consisting of 2 to about 10 units, somewhat discrete. Vessels mainly reticulated or spiral, 5-90 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Cork cells yellowish-brown, polygonal or elongated-polygonal in surface view (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (95%). Allow to stand for 15 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to another conical flask. Concentrate the filtrate to about 1 mL on a water bath at $ 50^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ , then transfer the solution to a test tube. Add hydroxylamine hydrochloride solution (7%, w/v) and potassium hydroxide solution (20%, w/v), 3 drops each, to the test tube. Mix well and heat the mixture on a water bath at $ 50^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for about 10 min. Cool to room temperature. Adjust the pH to about 3-4 with dilute hydrochloric acid (10%, v/v). Add 2 drops of iron(III) chloride solution (1%, w/v) and mix well. A light brown to orange red solution is observed."},{"name":"13","text":"Columbianetin acetate standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of columbianetin acetate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of dichloromethane.\n\nOsthole standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of osthole CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of dichloromethane.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[42, 0, 1982, 252])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[327, 459, 943, 871])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[359, 1042, 935, 1478])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[360, 1591, 928, 2083])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n50μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[975, 443, 1683, 1563])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[971, 1572, 1686, 2097])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100μm\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Angelicae Pubescentis A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vessels D. Oil ducts 1. Cork 2. Phelloderm 3. Oil duct 4. Clefts 5. Phloem 6. Ray 7. Cambium 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 0, 1973, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[369, 452, 1019, 1328])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1031, 450, 1612, 867])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1025, 878, 1616, 1338])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[370, 1334, 1020, 1791])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1030, 1347, 1614, 1785])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Angelicae Pubescentis\n\n1. Oil duct 2. Fragments of oil duct 3. Oil duct with yellowish-brown secretions\n\n4. Parenchyma cells with crisscross striations 5. Simple starch granule 6. Compound starch granules\n\n7. Reticulated vessel 8. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[51, 0, 1965, 249])"},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, toluene and ethyl acetate (2:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of diethyl ether. Allow to stand overnight. Filter and transfer the filtrate to another conical flask. Evaporate to dryness on a water bath at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of dichloromethane."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately columbianetin acetate standard solution, osthole standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 9 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm and 365 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of columbianetin acetate and osthole.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[819, 1546, 1287, 1888])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[803, 1977, 1305, 2404])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) osthole and (ii) columbianetin acetate\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Osthole standard stock solution, Std-Stock (44 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.2 mg of osthole CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nOsthole standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (8.8 mg/L)\n\nPipette 0.4 mL of osthole Std-Stock into a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and put into a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (320 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-20</td><td>70→55</td><td>30→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>55→35</td><td>45→65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of osthole Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of osthole should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of osthole peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from osthole peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject osthole Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of osthole peak in the chromatogram of osthole Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[41, 0, 1967, 252])\n\nof the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify osthole peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of osthole Std FP. The retention times of osthole peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Angelicae Pubescentis extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Angelicae Pubescentis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.51</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(columbianetin acetate)</td><td>0.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,osthole)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.08</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[397, 1622, 1750, 2192])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Angelicae Pubescentis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 48.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 44.0%.\n\nEther-soluble extractives (carry out the method as indicated below):\n\nPulverize CMM sample, pass through a No. 4 sieve and mix well. Weigh accurately about 2.0 g of the powdered sample, previously dried over phosphorous pentoxide for 48 h, and transfer to a 100-mL conical flask. Add 70 mL of diethyl ether and several glass beads. Heat under reflux to slightly boil for 4 h, cool and filter. Wash the flask and residue with appropriate amount of ether. Combine the washings and filtrate, transfer to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ether, mix well. Accurately transfer 50 mL of the solution to an evaporating dish, previously dried to constant weight. Expel the ether and dry it over phosphorous pentoxide for 24 h. Weigh immediately and accurately. The sample contains not less than 3.0% of ether-soluble extractives, calculated with reference to the dried substance."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"13","text":"Osthole standard stock solution, Std-Stock (44 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.2 mg of osthole CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nOsthole standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the osthole Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 0.66, 4.40, 8.80, 13.20, 17.60 mg/L for osthole."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-angelicae-pubescentis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Angelicae Pubescentis","la":"Radix Angelicae Pubescentis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-angelicae-sinensis-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Angelicae Sinensis v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-angelicae-sinensis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Angelicae Sinensis","la":"Radix Angelicae Sinensis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-astragali-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Astragali v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-astragali","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Astragali","la":"Radix Astragali"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-aucklandiae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Aucklandiae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[477, 806, 1556, 1849])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Aucklandiae\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[477, 806, 1556, 1849])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Aucklandiae\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Radix Aucklandiae\n\nChinese Name: 木香\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Muxiang"},{"name":"12","text":"Radix Aucklandiae is the dried root of Aucklandia lappa Decne. (Asteraceae/Compositae). The root is collected in autumn and winter, after removal of rootlets and soil, it is cut into sections. The larger section is further cut into pieces longitudinally, dried, then the rough outer bark removed by dashing to obtain Radix Aucklandiae."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical or semicylindrical, 5-20 cm long, 5-50 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown to greyishbrown, showing distinct wrinkles, longitudinal furrows, and lateral root scars. Texture hard, not easy to break; fracture greyish-brown to dark brown; the outer layer greyish-yellow or pale brownish-yellow; cambium ring brown, having radial lines and scattered brown dotted oil cavities. Odour aromatic and characteristic; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"The cork consisting of several layers of cork cells. Phloem broad; phloem fibres in bundles, sparsely distributed or arranged in 1-3 interrupted whorls. Cambium in a ring. Xylem fibres mostly distributed near the cambium and the root center. Oil secretory cavities round or elliptic in shape, varying in size, up to 400 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to brown. Inulin fairly abundant, with radial striations. Reticulate vessels\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[23, 2, 1955, 252])\n\nvery frequent, bordered-pitted vessels also present, 11-110 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Xylem fibres mostly in bundles, long-fusiform, 11-40 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, pit apertures horizontal-porous, crisscross or V-shaped. Some parenchyma cells with very fine oblique crisscross striatons. Cork cells subsquare, subrectangular or polygonal in shape. Fragments of oil secretory cavity sometimes visible, containing yellow or brown secretion (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 15-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 5 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer 1 mL of the supernatant to a test tube, add 4 mL of ethanol and mix. Transfer 1 mL of the solution to another test tube. Cautiously add about 0.5 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for 10 min. A reddish-purple to purple ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"12","text":"Costunolide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of costunolide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nDehydrocostus lactone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dehydrocostus lactone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and cyclohexane (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mix 1 mL of dilute sulphuric acid ( 50% , v/v) and 10 mL of p-hydroxybenzaldehyde in methanol ( 2% , w/v ). Freshly prepare the reagent.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[233, 402, 1738, 2274])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Aucklandiae\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil secretory cavity\n\n1. Cork 2.Cortex 3. Oil secretory cavity 4. Phloem 5. Phloem fibres 6.Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Vessels\n\n9. Xylem fibres\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[13, 12, 1963, 253])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[239, 445, 1743, 2173])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Aucklandiae (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Inulin 2. Fragment of oil secretory cavity 3. Reticulate vessels 4. Bordered-pitted vessel 5. Xylem fibres\n\n6. Xylem fibres in a bundle 7. Parenchyma cells 8. Cork cells\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 248])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately costunolide standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), dehydrocostus lactone standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[852, 1451, 1233, 1854])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[807, 2003, 1274, 2281])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) dehydrocostus lactone and (ii) costunolide\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Costunolide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of costunolide CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>100→70</td><td>0→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-28</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>28-45</td><td>70→0</td><td>30→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of costunolide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of costunolide should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of costunolide peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from costunolide peak should not be less than 600000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and peak 5 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject costunolide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of costunolide peak in the chromatogram of costunolide Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify costunolide peak in\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1968, 253])\n\nthe chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of costunolide Std-FP. The retention times of costunolide peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Radix Aucklandiae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Radix Aucklandiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.37</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.58</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,costunolide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(dehydrocostus lactone)</td><td>1.01</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[404, 1526, 1752, 2187])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Aucklandiae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 65.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-aucklandiae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Aucklandiae","la":"Radix Aucklandiae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-bupleuri-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Bupleuri v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[551, 848, 1440, 1777])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Bupleuri\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1965, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[551, 848, 1440, 1777])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Bupleuri\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1965, 252])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Radix Bupleuri\n\nChinese Name: 柴胡\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chaihu"},{"name":"12","text":"Radix Bupleuri is the dried root of Bupleurum chinense DC. (Apiaceae/Umbelliferae). The root is collected in the spring or autumn. After removal of the aerial part and the soil, the root is dried to obtain Radix Bupleuri."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical, or long and conical; 3.5-16 cm long and 3-12 mm in diameter. The top part bulgy; the apex shows 3-15 stem-bases or short fibrous leaf-bases, and branched at the lower part. Outer surface dull blackish-brown or light brown, marked with longitudinal wrinkles, rootlet scars and lenticels. Texture hard and tenacious, not easy to break; fracture laminated-fibrous, bark light brown, wood yellowishwhite. Odour slightly fragrant; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consisting of several layers of flat cells. Cortex narrow, containing a few oil canals. Phloem shows scattered oil canals and, often with clefts; oil canals surrounded by secretory cells and contain yellowish-brown or greenish-yellow secretions or oily drops in canals. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad, occupying more than half of the radius of the root; vessels single or grouped; xylem rays visible. Xylem fibres well developed and arranged with xylem parenchyma cells in several discontinuous rings (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 0, 1955, 255])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Oil canals mostly fractured, containing yellowish-brown or greenish-yellow strip-shaped secretions, 10-50 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Xylem fibres frequent, single or in bundles colourless or slightly yellow, long fusiform, 7-25 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, 2-8 $ \\mu $ m in wall thickness, lignified. Reticulated and spiral vessels predominant, 5-80 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Cork cells yellowish-brown, polygonal (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Shake the mixture for 2 h. Allow to stand for about 30 min. Filter and transfer 100 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of filtrate to a test tube, add 3 mL of dichloromethane with slight shaking. Cautiously add 1.5 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. A red to reddish-brown ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"12","text":"Saikosaponin A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of saikosaponin A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nSaikosaponin C standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of saikosaponin C CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nSaikosaponin D standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of saikosaponin D CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and water (8:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Dissolve 2.0 g of 4-(dimethylamino)benzaldehyde in 100 mL of dilute sulphuric acid (40%, v/v). Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add about 20 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL conical flask. Concentrate the filtrate to about 5 mL on a water bath, and filter.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[349, 472, 919, 858])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[380, 896, 892, 1257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[945, 463, 1604, 941])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[945, 949, 1601, 1285])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[384, 1325, 890, 1625])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n50μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[944, 1286, 1609, 1621])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Bupleuri A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil canal D. Xylem fibres\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Oil canal 4. Cleft 5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Xylem fibres 10. Vessels\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 1, 1984, 258])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 449, 1675, 1522])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Bupleuri (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Oil canal 2. Xylem fibre 3. Xylem fibres in a bundle 4. Reticulated vessels 5. Spiral vessels\n\n6. Cork cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 0, 1965, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately saikosaponin A standard solution, saikosaponin C standard solution, saikosaponin D standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at about $ 60^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of saikosaponin A, saikosaponin C and saikosaponin D.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[664, 1160, 1405, 1497])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[664, 1584, 1410, 2022])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[666, 2098, 1408, 2431])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) saikosaponin A (ii) saikosaponin C and (iii) saikosaponin D\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 0, 1954, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"Saikosaponin D standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of saikosaponin D CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 20 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution in methanol [add 1 volume of ammonium hydroxide (25%, v/v) to 4 volumes of methanol] and mix well. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>70→50</td><td>30→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>50→40</td><td>50→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>40→10</td><td>60→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>10→0</td><td>90→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of saikosaponin D Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of saikosaponin D should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of saikosaponin D peak should not be more than 5.0%; the column efficiency determined from saikosaponin D peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and peak 4 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 0, 1967, 251])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject saikosaponin D Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of saikosaponin D peak in the chromatogram of saikosaponin D Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify saikosaponin D peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of saikosaponin D Std-FP. The retention times of saikosaponin D peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 3.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Bupleuri extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Bupleuri extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(saikosaponin A)</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,saikosaponin D)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.34</td><td>±0.09</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.37</td><td>±0.09</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[392, 1689, 1750, 2358])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Bupleuri extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 0, 1954, 253])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII):\n\nNon-medicinal part: not more than 8.0%\n\nOther foreign matter: not more than 2.0%\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[38, 0, 1966, 248])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-bupleuri","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Bupleuri","la":"Radix Bupleuri"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-codonopsis-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Codonopsis v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[560, 585, 1420, 1430])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[126, 1622, 952, 2435])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1039, 1614, 1892, 2438])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of dried root of Codonopsis tangshen Oliv.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1966, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"19","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[560, 585, 1420, 1430])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[126, 1622, 952, 2435])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1039, 1614, 1892, 2438])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of dried root of Codonopsis tangshen Oliv.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"19","text":"Official Name: Radix Codonopsis\n\nChinese Name: 黨參\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Dangshen"},{"name":"19","text":"Radix Codonopsis is the dried root of Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf., Codonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen or Codonopsis tangshen Oliv. (Campanulaceae). The root is collected in autumn, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Radix Codonopsis."},{"name":"19","text":"Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf. : The root is long and cylindrical, slightly curved, occasionally branched, 11-34 cm in length, 3-18 mm in diameter, outer surface yellowish-brown to greyish-yellow. The upper portion with numerous verruciform (wart-shaped), protuberant stem scars, each stem scar dented and dotted, commonly known as \"shizi-pantou\", dense transverse annulations occur below the root stock, gradually becoming sparse towards the lower part, some up to half the length of the root, few or no transverse annulations on cultivated roots. Whole root marked with longitudinal wrinkles and scattered transverse lenticels, scars of branch roots often with gelatinous, dull blackish-brown substances. Texture slightly hard or tenacious, fracture somewhat even, cleft or with striated radial markings, bark pale yellowish-white to pale brown, slightly thick, xylem pale yellow, in the shape of a chrysanthemum. Odour, with a distinctive fragrance; taste, sweet [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nCodonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen : The root is 6-32 cm in length, 6-25 mm in diameter, outer surface yellowish-brown to greyish-yellow, with dense transverse annulations occurring below the root stock, frequently up to over half the length of the root. Fracture shows more cleft [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\nCodonopsis tangshen Oliv.: The root is 10-45 cm in length, 5-18 mm in diameter, outer surface yellowishbrown to greyish-brown with distinctly longitudinal wrinkles. Texture slightly soft and pliable, fracture shows less cleft [Fig. 1(iii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[25, 3, 1963, 250])"},{"name":"19","text":"Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf. : Cork consists of 3-10 layers of cork cells, stone cells present in the outer layer of the cork tissue, single or grouped. Cortex narrow. Phloem broad, often with cleft, with groups of laticiferous tubes frequently alternated with sieve tubes. Cambium in a ring. Xylem vessels scattered singly or aggregated in groups, arranged radially. Inulin and starch grains contained in parenchyma cells [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nCodonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen : Stone cells present in a complete ring in the outer layer of cork tissue, composed of 2-5 layers of cells. Group of laticiferous tubes arranged radially in the inner part of the phloem, curved in the outer part, sometimes arranged tangentially in an interrupted ring [Fig. 2(ii)].\n\nCodonopsis tangshen Oliv.: Stone cells present in the outer layer of the cork tissue, singly scattered or several groups arranged in an interrupted ring. Groups of laticiferous tubes arranged irregularly [Fig. 2(iii)]."},{"name":"19","text":"Colour, yellowish-white, pale yellow or greyish-yellow. Stone cells frequent, in polygonal, rhombic, rectangular or spindle shapes, occurring scattered singly or aggregated in groups, some attached to the cork cells, 8-68 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, 15-152 $ \\mu $ m long, cells wall with occasional pits and pit traps. Cork cells frequent, subrectangular and polygonal in surface view. Vessels mostly of reticulate and scalariform bordered-pitted types, 8-142 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Inulin abundant, fan-shaped or irregular, with radial striation; showing bright mass with radial striation when examined under the polarized microscope. Groups of laticiferous tubes frequent, consisting yellowish-brown matter in granules or oily drops. Starch grains infrequent [Fig. 3(i), (ii) and (iii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[252, 447, 1665, 1707])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cell D. Laticiferous tube group\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Cleft 5. Phloem 6. Laticiferous tube group 7. Cambium 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[24, 7, 1961, 254])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[298, 473, 1584, 1728])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cell D. Laticiferous tube group\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Cleft 5. Phloem 6. Laticiferous tube group 7. Cambium 8. Xylem\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 257])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[244, 436, 1734, 1882])\n\nFigure 2(iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Codonopsis tangshen Oliv.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cell D. Laticiferous tube group\n\n1. Stone cell 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Cleft 5. Phloem 6. Laticiferous tube group 7. Cambium 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 2, 1973, 255])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[301, 491, 1723, 2162])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Laticiferous tube 2. Starch grain 3. Inulin 4. Stone cell 5. Reticulate bordered pits vessels 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 258])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[339, 485, 1250, 1011])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1280, 488, 1643, 732])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1278, 758, 1645, 1017])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[341, 1042, 758, 1337])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[783, 1039, 1222, 1342])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1245, 1044, 1642, 1338])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[341, 1375, 861, 2016])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[898, 1372, 1644, 2020])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[12, 1, 1973, 252])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[357, 479, 1247, 1080])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1297, 483, 1588, 749])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1296, 787, 1589, 1054])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[360, 1133, 738, 1484])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[780, 1129, 1166, 1498])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[1200, 1114, 1622, 1486])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[359, 1532, 971, 2040])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[989, 1524, 1623, 2045])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(iii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Codonopsis tangshen Oliv.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Laticiferous tube 2. Starch grain 3. Inulin 4. Stone cells 5. Scalariform bordered pits vessel\n\n6. Cork cells\n\n</div>\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[39, 0, 1974, 249])"},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Filter and transfer 1 mL of the filtrate to a test tube. Cautiously add about 1 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for about 20 min. An orange-brown ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"19","text":"Lobetyolin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of lobetyolin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"19","text":"Prepare a mixture of 1-butanol, acetic acid and water (7:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"19","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer 25 mL of the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of water. Load the water extract onto a Hydrophilic-Lipophilic Balance (HLB) extraction cartridge (3 mL, 60 mg). Wash the cartridge with 6 mL of water, then elute with 6 mL of methanol. Transfer the methanol solution to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"19","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately lobetyolin standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[24, 2, 1956, 253])\n\n10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of lobetyolin.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[683, 746, 1309, 1153])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of lobetyolin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"19","text":"Lobetyolin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (3600 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.6 mg of lobetyolin CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nLobetyolin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (360 mg/L)\n\nPipette 1 mL of the lobetyolin Std-Stock into a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 1.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 250-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of 0.1 M HCl-methanol (1:1, v/v). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"19","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (267 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[42, 0, 1966, 251])\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% of Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>90→80</td><td>10→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>80→0</td><td>20→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"19","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of lobetyolin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of lobetyolin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of lobetyolin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from lobetyolin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"19","text":"Separately inject lobetyolin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of lobetyolin peak in the chromatogram of lobetyolin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify lobetyolin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of lobetyolin Std-FP. The retention times of lobetyolin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Codonopsis extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Codonopsis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.64</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, lobetyolin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n牛膝\n\nRadix Achyranthis Bidentatae\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[391, 362, 1755, 860])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula (Franch.) Nannf. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[389, 1079, 1757, 1573])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Codonopsis pilosula Nannf. var. modesta (Nannf.) L.T. Shen extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[389, 1827, 1754, 2393])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Codonopsis tangshen Oliv. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[42, 0, 1964, 243])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"19","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"19","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 41.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"19","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-codonopsis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Codonopsis","la":"Radix Codonopsis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-et-rhizoma-gentianae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[561, 583, 1424, 1398])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana scabra Bge. 1. Rhizome 2. Root\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[605, 1642, 1381, 2431])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana rigescens Franch. 1. Rhizome 2. Root\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1965, 246])"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[561, 583, 1424, 1398])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana scabra Bge. 1. Rhizome 2. Root\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[605, 1642, 1381, 2431])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana rigescens Franch. 1. Rhizome 2. Root\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1965, 246])"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae\n\nChinese Name: 龍膽\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Longdan"},{"name":"16","text":"Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae is the dried root and rhizome of Gentiana scabra Bge. or Gentiana rigescens Franch. (Gentianaceae). The roots with the rhizome is collected in the spring and autumn. After removal of remnants of the above-ground stems. The root and rhizome were dried under the sun to obtain Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae."},{"name":"16","text":"Gentiana scabra Bge. : Rhizomes irregularly bundle-shaped, 1-3 cm in length, 3-10 mm in diameter. Outer surface dull dark greyish-brown or dark brown, the top end marked by stem scars or remnants of stems, all sides and the lower end with numerous slender roots. Root cylindrical, slightly twisted, 4-21 cm in length, 1-6 mm in diameter. Outer surface pale yellow or yellowish-brown, upper part usually with conspicuous transverse wrinkles, lower part thinner and marked with longitudinal wrinkles and scars of branch roots. Texture brittle, easily broken. Broken surface slightly even. Bark yellowish-white or pale yellowish-brown. Xylem paler in colour. Odour, faint; taste, quite bitter [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nGentiana rigescens Franch. : Rhizomes in the form of irregular pieces, smaller than that of Gentiana scabra Bge. Root 3.5-23 cm in length, 1-4 mm in diameter, externally yellowish-brown or yellowish-red without transverse wrinkles. The outer layer membranaceous, easily falling off. Wood yellowish-white, easily separated from the bark [Fig. 1(ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 3, 1956, 250])"},{"name":"16","text":"Gentiana scabra Bge. : Exodermis consists of a layer of cells, the outer walls relatively thicker. Cortex narrow, often with clefts. Endodermis distinct, cells elongated tangentially, each cell divided by longitudinal walls into several subsquare small cells. Phloem broad with cleft, occupying 1/3 of the radius. Cambium indistinct. Xylem bundles 5-10 grouped with vessels. Pith distinct. Parenchyma cells containing minute needles of calcium oxalate crystals are occasionally found [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nGentiana rigescens Franch. : The tissues outside the endodermis mostly falling off. Endodermis distinct. Phloem occupying 2/3 of the radius. Vessels in the xylem densely distributed with radial orientation. Pith absent [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Gentiana scabra Bge. : Colour pale yellowish-brown. Exodermis cells easily observed externally spindle-shaped or subrectangular, 42-536 $ \\mu $ m in length, 16-134 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, each cell divided by longitudinal wall into several small rectangular cells. Endodermis cells can be observed easily, rectangular in surface view and somewhat larger in size, 36-558 $ \\mu $ m in length, 34-255 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Periclinal wall shows minute transverse striations, each cell divided by longitudinal septum into 5-36 small palisade-like cells, with the longitudinal septum beaded. Vessels reticulate and scalariform, 7-52 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Some parenchyma cells contain minute needles of calcium oxalate crystals which appear as firework polychrome under the polarized microscope [Fig. 3(i)].\n\nGentiana rigescens Franch. : Colour reddish-brown to yellowish-brown. Exodermis cells absent. Endodermis cells 67-381 $ \\mu $ m in length, 36-305 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, transverse striations of the periclinal wall thicker and dense, each cell divided by longitudinal wall into 3-34 subsquare, palisade-like cells. Vessels reticulate, scalariform and spiral, 9-28 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fibre-tracheid easily observed, short, spindle-shaped or rectangular. Some parenchymatous cells containing minute needles of calcium oxalate which showing firework polychrome under a polarizing microscope [Fig. 3(ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 253])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 430, 1737, 1800])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana scabra Bge.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cortex\n\n1. Exodermis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Cleft 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[15, 9, 1962, 252])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[240, 439, 1731, 1798])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana rigescens Franch.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Endodermis\n\n1. Endodermis 2. Phloem 3. Sieve tube group 4. Xylem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 0, 1984, 253])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[320, 442, 1691, 1846])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana scabra Bge.\n\n1. Exodermis 2. Endodermis 3. Reticulate vessel 4. Needles of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[0, 4, 1984, 254])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[243, 410, 1742, 1990])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana rigescens Franch.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Endodermis 2. Reticulate, scalariform and spiral vessels 3. Needles of calcium oxalate 4. Fibre-tracheid a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[41, 0, 1966, 247])"},{"name":"16","text":"Potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{1} $\n\nDissolve 3.8 g of potassium iodide and 0.85 g of bismuth subnitrate in 35 mL of acetic acid (14% v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a test tube. Evaporate the solvent to dryness on a water bath. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Transfer 250 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the solution to another test tube and add 1 drop of acetic acid (14%, v/v). Add 2 drops of potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{1} $ into the mixture. Allow to stand for about 1 min. An orange precipitate is observed."},{"name":"16","text":"Gentiopicrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of gentiopicrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol and water (20:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 45 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Concentrate the mixture to about 1.5 mL at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Transfer the mixture into a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately gentiopicrin standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber. After drying in air, place the plate in the chamber and redevelop over a path of about 7 cm in the same mobile phase. After the development, remove the\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[28, 0, 1949, 250])\n\nplate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of gentiopicrin.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[612, 727, 1360, 1153])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of gentiopicrin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"16","text":"Gentiopicrin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of gentiopicrin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nGentiopicrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nPipette 2 mL of gentiopicrin Std-Stock into a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with mixed solvent of 0.4% phosphoric acid and methanol (1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 25 mL of mixed solvent of 0.4% phosphoric acid and methanol (1:1, v/v). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 45 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (230 nm) and a column $ (3.9\\times150\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% of Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>95→94</td><td>5→6</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-40</td><td>94→40</td><td>6→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-55</td><td>40→0</td><td>60→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-60</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of gentiopicrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of gentiopicrin should not be more than 2.0%; the RSD of the retention time of gentiopicrin peak should not be more than 3.0%; the column efficiency determined from gentiopicrin peak should not be less than 3000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject gentiopicrin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of gentiopicrin peak in the chromatogram of gentiopicrin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify gentiopicrin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of gentiopicrin Std-FP. The retention times of gentiopicrin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.93</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,gentiopicrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[401, 374, 1753, 1118])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana scabra Bge. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[394, 1452, 1752, 2188])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Gentiana rigescens Franch. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[46, 0, 1963, 245])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 37.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 36.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B).\n\nStandard solution\n\nGentiopicrin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of gentiopicrin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-et-rhizoma-gentianae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae","la":"Radix et Rhizoma Gentianae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-et-rhizoma-glycyrrhizae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[581, 618, 1419, 1367])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza uralensis Fisch.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[591, 1666, 1417, 2424])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza inflata Bat.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 254])"},"sections":[{"name":"18","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[581, 618, 1419, 1367])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza uralensis Fisch.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[591, 1666, 1417, 2424])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza inflata Bat.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 254])"},{"name":"18","text":"Official Name: Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae\n\nChinese Name: 甘草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gancao"},{"name":"18","text":"Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae is the dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza uralensis Fisch. or Glycyrrhiza inflata Bat. (Leguminosae). The root together with the rhizome is collected in the spring and autumn. After removal of the rootlets, the root and rhizome are dried under the sun to obtain Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae."},{"name":"18","text":"Glycyrrhiza uralensis Fisch. : Roots cylindrical, 12-56 cm long, 5-36 mm in diameter, some branched. The outer bark loose or tight. Externally reddish-brown or greyish-brown, prominently longitudinally wrinkled, lenticellate, and with sparse rootlet scars. Texture compact, fracture slightly fibrous, yellowish white to yellow, starchy, cambium ring distinct, rays radial, some with clefts. Rhizome cylindrical, externally with bud scars, pith present in the centre of fracture. Odour slight; taste sweet and characteristic [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nGlycyrrhiza inflata Bat. : Roots and rhizomes woody and stout, 6-60 cm long, and 6-30 mm in diameter. The outer bark rough, mostly greyish-brown. Texture compact, pale yellow to yellowish-brown, with many lignified fibres, and not too starchy. The rhizome has many large adventitious buds [Fig. 1(ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"The cork consists of several or more than ten layers of brown cells. The cortex relatively narrow. Phloem rays broad, mostly curved outside, and frequently with clefts; most phloem fibres in\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[35, 0, 1950, 250])\n\nbundles, unlignified or slightly lignified, and surrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate; sieve tube tissues often being distorted by compression. Fascicular cambium distinct. Xylem rays 2-5 cells wide, vessels frequent, up to 170 $ \\mu $m in diameter, xylem fibres in bundles, surrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate. Root without, but rhizome with a pith at the centre [Fig. 2(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Colour brownish-yellow. Fibres in bundles, 5-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, thick-walled, slightly lignified, surrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres. Crystals of calcium oxalate double-conical, rectangular or cubical, 4-18 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, up to 40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length. Vessels mostly bordered-pitted, up to 170 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, rarely reticulated, both lignified. Starch grains frequent, single grains subspheroidal, ellipsoidal or elongated-ovoid, hilum pointed or slit-shaped, 2-23 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; compound grains rare. Cork cells reddishbrown, polygonal in top view, slightly lignified. Pigmented masses rare, yellowish-brown or reddish-brown [Fig. 3(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a test tube. Evaporate to dryness on a water bath at about $ 7 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . Cool to room temperature. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of methanol. Transfer 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the solution to a test tube, add 2 mL of dichloromethane with slight shaking. Cautiously add 1 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for about 30 min. A yellow to orange-red ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"18","text":"Glycyrrhizic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of glycyrrhizic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of methanol.\n\nLiquiritin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of liquiritin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Prepare a mixture of 1-butanol, glacial acetic acid and water (7:1:12, v/v) in a separating funnel. Shake well and allow to stand for 30 min. Use the upper layer.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 0, 1983, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[402, 465, 777, 857])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[381, 1001, 794, 1329])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[382, 1438, 794, 1767])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n50μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[821, 446, 1620, 1768])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza uralensis Fisch.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vessels D. Fibres\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Clefts 4. Phloem rays 5. Phloem fibres 6. Cambium 7. Vessels 8. Xylem rays\n\n9. Xylem fibres\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[367, 560, 752, 947])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[804, 453, 1625, 1193])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[348, 1141, 776, 1468])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[350, 1637, 769, 1963])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n50μm\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[800, 1198, 1629, 1967])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 $ \\mu m $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza inflata Bat.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vessels D. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Clefts 4. Phloem rays 5. Phloem fibres 6. Cambium 7. Vessels 8. Xylem rays\n\n9. Xylem fibres 10. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[12, 0, 1984, 259])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[356, 428, 1716, 2180])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza uralensis Fisch.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Single fibre 2. Crystal fibre bundle 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Bordered-pitted vessels\n\n5. Starch granule 6. Cork cells 7. Pigmented mass\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 5, 1963, 257])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 470, 1677, 1938])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza inflata Bat.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Crystal fibres of bundle 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Bordered-pitted vessel 4. Starch granules\n\n5. Starch granules in parenchyma cells 6. Cork cells 7. Pigmented mass\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[44, 0, 1966, 246])"},{"name":"18","text":"Add slowly 20 mL of sulphuric acid to 80 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 40 mL of diethyl ether. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool to room temperature. Filter and discard the filtrate. Transfer the residue to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, add 30 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool to room temperature and filter. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 40 mL of water. Extract thrice each with 20 mL of 1-butanol. Combine the 1-butanol extracts and wash thrice each with 20 mL of water. Evaporate the 1-butanol extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"18","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately glycyrrhizic acid standard solution, liquiritin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm), and then spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent. Heat the plate at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of glycyrrhizic acid and liquiritin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[689, 2044, 1453, 2507])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[773, 409, 1373, 701])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) glycyrrhizic acid and (ii) liquiritin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"18","text":"Glycyrrhizic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of glycyrrhizic acid CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\nGlycyrrhizic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nPipette 1.0 mL of glycyrrhizic acid Std-Stock into a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 25 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"18","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (230 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.03% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>5→25</td><td>95→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>25</td><td>75</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-36</td><td>25→50</td><td>75→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>36-60</td><td>50→95</td><td>50→5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[40, 0, 1968, 250])"},{"name":"18","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of glycyrrhizic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of glycyrrhizic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of glycyrrhizic acid peak should not be more than 5.0%; the column efficiency determined from glycyrrhizic acid peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Separately inject glycyrrhizic acid Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of glycyrrhizic acid peak in the chromatogram of the glycyrrhizic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify glycyrrhizic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of glycyrrhizic acid Std-FP. The retention times of glycyrrhizic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae extract\n\n</div>\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.29</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(liquiritin)</td><td>0.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,glycyrrhizic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[398, 369, 1757, 967])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza uralensis Fisch. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[400, 1235, 1752, 1882])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Glycyrrhiza inflata Bat. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"![](page=11,bbox=[44, 39, 260, 138])\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"18","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 17.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18.0%."},{"name":"18","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-et-rhizoma-glycyrrhizae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae","la":"Radix et Rhizoma Glycyrrhizae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-et-rhizoma-rhei-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix et Rhizoma Rhei v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[608, 667, 1407, 1217])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Rheum palmatum L.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[180, 1707, 923, 2382])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Rheum tanguticum Maxim. ex Balf.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1055, 1838, 1857, 2425])\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Rheum officinale Baill.\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 254])"},"sections":[{"name":"20","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[608, 667, 1407, 1217])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Rheum palmatum L.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[180, 1707, 923, 2382])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Rheum tanguticum Maxim. ex Balf.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1055, 1838, 1857, 2425])\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of dried root and rhizome of Rheum officinale Baill.\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 254])"},{"name":"20","text":"Official Name: Radix et Rhizoma Rhei\n\nChinese Name: 大黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Dahuang"},{"name":"20","text":"Radix et Rhizoma Rhei is the dried root and rhizome of Rheum palmatum L., Rheum tanguticum Maxim. ex Balf. or Rheum officinale Baill. (Polygonaceae). The root and rhizome are collected in late autumn when stem and leaves wither or in the following spring before germination. After removal of soil, rootlets or the outer bark, the root and rhizome are cut into segments or sections or processed into egg-shaped or cylindrical-shaped products, either kept in a string together to be dried or dried directly to obtain Radix et Rhizoma Rhei."},{"name":"20","text":"Subcylindrical, conical, ovoid, hippocrepiform, or irregular slices or sections, 0.5-40 cm long or thick, 6-200 mm in diameter. Outer surface yellowish-brown to reddish-brown when peeled, sometimes the surface marked with whitish reticulations and scattered star spots (anomalous vascular bundles) or with remnants of brownish-black cork, mostly with coarse wrinkles. Hard and solid, sometimes loose and soft in the centre. When broken, the surface pale reddish-brown to yellowish-brown, granular. Pith of the rhizome broad; star spots in cross-section, appearing in rings or scattered. Cambium ring of root distinct; xylem well developed, with radial rays and no star spots. Odour delicately aromatic; taste bitter and slightly astringent, sticky and gritty on the chewing [Fig. 1(i), (ii) and (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"The cork and cortex have been mostly removed. The cork consists of several layers of flat cells;\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[35, 0, 1937, 251])\n\nwhile the remaining phelloderm contains a number of subround parenchyma cells, often with clefts. In the phloem, the parenchyma is well developed, with large mucilage cavities; phloem rays relatively dense, sometimes with clefts. Cambium distinct, in a ring. Xylem with relatively dense rays, 1-10 cells wide; vessels usually single or in group, sparsely arranged, unlignified. Parenchyma cells contain cluster crystals of calcium oxalate and abundant starch granules. The pith is broad, containing anomalous vascular bundles arranged in a more or less continuous ring(s) or scattered irregularly. The anomalous vascular bundles show a cambium in a ring, with the phloem inside and xylem outside, sometimes mucilage cavities visible near the cambium; the rays running radially from the centre of the ring towards the outside, forming whirls of tissues [Fig. 2(i), (ii) and (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to brown. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate frequent, 12-200 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Reticulated vessels frequent, but bordered-pitted and spiral vessels are also visible, unlignified, 12-180 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Starch granules abundant, single granules rounded, subround or polygonal, 2-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum stellate, punctuate, herringbone-shaped, slit-shaped or crossed; compound granules consisting of 2-15 units [Fig. 3(i), (ii) and (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of water. Heat the mixture on a water bath for 30 min. Cool to room temperature and then filter. Add 2 drops of hydrochloric acid to the filtrate, then extract twice each with 20 mL of diethyl ether and discard the ether layer. To the aqueous extract, add 5 mL of hydrochloric acid. Heat the mixture on a water bath for 30 min. Cool to room temperature. Extract with 20 mL of diethyl ether. Transfer 2 mL of the ether extract to a test tube, add 0.5 mL of sodium hydrogen carbonate solution (10%, w/v) and shake vigorously. A reddish-purple or reddish-brown colouration is observed in the lower layer.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 0, 1983, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[256, 461, 1686, 2094])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root and rhizome of Rheum palmatum L.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Phloem 5. Phloem ray 6. Mucilage cavities 7. Cambium 8. Xylem\n\n9. Xylem ray 10. Vessels 11. Cluster of calcium oxalate 12. Pith 13. Anomalous vascular bundle\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[39, 14, 1949, 255])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[258, 473, 1673, 2181])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root and rhizome of Rheum tanguticum Maxim. ex Balf.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Phloem 2. Phloem ray 3. Mucilage cavities 4. Cleft 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray 8. Vessels 9. Cluster of calcium oxalate 10. Pith 11. Anomalous vascular bundle\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 0, 1983, 257])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[260, 455, 1723, 2074])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root and rhizome of Rheum officinale Baill. A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Phloem 5. Phloem ray 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Vessels\n\n10. Pith 11. Cluster of calcium oxalate 12. Anomalous vascular bundle\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[10, 1, 1973, 256])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[245, 445, 1744, 1798])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Rheum palmatum L.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Reticulated vessels 3. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 0, 1983, 257])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[242, 410, 1749, 2018])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Rheum tanguticum Maxim. ex Balf.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Several clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Single cluster of calcium oxalate 3. Several reticulated vessels\n\n4. Single reticulated vessel 5. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[23, 8, 1961, 253])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[509, 434, 1743, 2082])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(iii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root and rhizome of Rheum officinale Baill.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cluster of calcium oxalate 2. Reticulated vessel and spiral vessel 3. Reticulated vessels\n\n4. Compound starch granule 5. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[39, 4, 1970, 240])"},{"name":"20","text":"Aloe-emodin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of aloe-emodin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nChrysophanol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chrysophanol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nEmodin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of emodin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nRhein standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rhein CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"20","text":"Prepare a mixture of toluene, ethyl acetate and glacial acetic acid (15:5:0.3, v/v)."},{"name":"20","text":"Dissolve 10 g of potassium hydroxide in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Allow to stand for 1 h with occasional shaking. Filter and transfer 5 mL of filtrate to a 25-mL evaporating dish. Evaporate to dryness on a water bath at about $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . Add 10 mL of water, followed by 1 mL of hydrochloric acid. Heat the mixture on a water bath for 30 min. Cool immediately. Extract twice each with 20 mL of diethyl ether. Combine the ether extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of dichloromethane."},{"name":"20","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately aloe-emodin standard solution, chrysophanol standard solution, emodin standard solution and rhein standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[28, 0, 1944, 253])\n\ncharacteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of aloe-emodin chrysophanol, emodin and rhein.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[439, 604, 963, 903])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1211, 607, 1609, 908])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[453, 1016, 945, 1320])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1124, 1006, 1672, 1319])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n( v )\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[446, 1416, 948, 1722])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) aloe-emodin (ii) chrysophanol (iii) emodin (iv) physcion and (v) rhein\n\n</div>"},{"name":"20","text":"Emodin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of emodin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) to dissolve the standard."},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 2 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 249])\n\nFilter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction twice. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Add 33 mL of hydrochloric acid (3.4% , v/v). Reflux the mixture for 45 min. Cool immediately. Extract thrice each with 60 mL of diethyl ether. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of methanol. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"20","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>35→80</td><td>65→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"20","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of emodin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of emodin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of emodin peak should not be more than 5.0%; the column efficiency determined from emodin peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"Separately inject emodin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of emodin peak in the chromatogram of emodin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify emodin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of emodin Std-FP. The retention times of emodin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 5.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix et Rhizoma Rhei extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix et Rhizoma Rhei extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(aloe-emodin)</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(rhein)</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,emodin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(chrysophanol)</td><td>1.34</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(physcion)</td><td>1.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[400, 877, 1752, 1574])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Rheum palmatum L. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[394, 1743, 1754, 2405])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Rheum tanguticum Maxim. ex Balf. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[40, 0, 1967, 251])\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[395, 370, 1754, 1037])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root and rhizome of Rheum officinale Baill. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"20","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 13.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[22, 1, 1953, 251])"},{"name":"20","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of powdered sample and put into a 20-mL test tube, then add 2 mL of methanol. Immerse the mixture in a water bath at about $ 4 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 10 min. Cool to room temperature. Pipette 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of supernatant and spot onto a filter paper. Dry in air. Allow to stand for 10 min. Apply 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ethanol (45%) to the dried spot. Examine the spot under UV light (365 nm). No persistent bright violet fluorescence should be observed."},{"name":"20","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 27.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-et-rhizoma-rhei","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix et Rhizoma Rhei","la":"Radix et Rhizoma Rhei"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-ginseng-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Ginseng v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-ginseng","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Ginseng","la":"Radix Ginseng"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-glehniae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Glehniae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 761, 1983, 2085])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1940, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 761, 1983, 2085])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1940, 253])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Glehniae\n\nChinese Name: 北沙参\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Beishashen"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Glehniae is the dried peeled root of Glehnia littoralis Fr. Schmidt ex Miq. (Apiaceae). The root is collected in summer and autumn, the rootlet removed, washed clean, treated with boiling water for 2-3 min, afterwards the cork is peeled, then dried under the sun to obtain Radix Glehniae."},{"name":"11","text":"Slender-cylindrical, 9-30 cm long, 2-15 mm in diameter. Outer surface pale yellowish-white to yellowish-brown, somewhat rough, the cork often peeled; fine longitudinal wrinkles and grooves, brownish-yellow punctiform protuberances scars of rootlets cover the surface throughout. The top slender, often marked with remnants of yellowish-brown rhizome base, the greater portion of the middle section of the root is thick, then becoming slender again towards the distal end. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken; fracture yellowish-white in the bark and brown in the wood. Odour distinctively fragrant; taste slightly sweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cortex consists of several rows of parenchyma cells. Phloem broad, cleft; phloem rays and groups of sieve tubes in the inner part are encrusted with densely arranged secretory canals, each surrounded by 5-8 secretory cells containing yellowish-brown secretions. Cambium prominent, in a ring. Xylem rays broad, vessels occur singly and scattered or arranged in a V-shape configuration. Parenchyma cells contain gelatinous starch masses (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Fragments of secretory canals containing yellowish-brown secretion are found frequently. Yellowish-brown secretion and gelatinous starch masses, their shape irregular, are also found. Vessel elements appear singly or in groups, 16-88 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with reticulate wall thickening, long and wide pits. Parenchyma cells sub-rectangular, abundant (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 0, 1966, 250])"},{"name":"11","text":"Falcarinol standard solution\n\nCarefully open the sealed brown ampoule containing 2 mg/mL of falcarinol CRS (Fig. 4). Pipette 500 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of falcarinol CRS solution into a 2-mL brown volumetric flask and make up to mark with methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately falcarinol standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of falcarinol.\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[860, 22, 1087, 116])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1063, 239])\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 419, 1734, 1833])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Glehniae\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory canals\n\n1. Cortex 2. Cleft 3. Secretory canal 4. Phloem 5. Phloem ray\n\n6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Secretory cell\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n燈心草\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n太子參\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n夏枯草\n\nSpica Prunellae\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[326, 440, 1209, 983])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1263, 439, 1657, 705])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1265, 748, 1656, 986])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[328, 1033, 819, 1796])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[879, 1042, 1659, 1794])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Glehniae (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Secretory canals 2. Yellowish-brown secretion 3. Starch gelatinous masses\n\n4. Reticulate vessels 5. Parenchyma cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[35, 0, 1946, 249])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[559, 491, 1281, 725])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of falcarinol\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Falcarinol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nCarefully open the sealed brown ampoule containing 2 mg/mL of falcarinol CRS. Pipette 1 mL of falcarinol CRS solution into a 10-mL brown volumetric flask and make up to mark with methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 150-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatant. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (260 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>53→40</td><td>47→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-60</td><td>40→15</td><td>60→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of falcarinol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of falcarinol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of falcarinol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from falcarinol peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject falcarinol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of falcarinol peak in the chromatogram of falcarinol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify falcarinol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of falcarinol Std-FP. The retention times of falcarinol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Glehniae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Glehniae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.67</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.79</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, falcarinol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[946, 180, 1064, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[382, 498, 1750, 1004])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Glehniae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 22.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 0, 1957, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Falcarinol standard stock solution, Std-Stock (400 mg/L)\n\nCarefully open the sealed brown ampoule containing 2 mg/mL of falcarinol CRS. Pipette 2 mL of falcarinol CRS solution into a 10-mL brown volumetric flask and make up to mark with methanol.\n\nFalcarinol standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the falcarinol Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 40,100,150,200,300 mg/L for falcarinol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-glehniae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Glehniae","la":"Radix Glehniae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-notoginseng-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Notoginseng v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-notoginseng","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Notoginseng","la":"Radix Notoginseng"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-ophiopogonis-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Ophiopogonis v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# \n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[37, 0, 1944, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# \n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[37, 0, 1944, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Ophiopogonis\n\nChinese Name: 麥冬\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Maidong"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Ophiopogonis is the dried root tuber of Ophiopogon japonicus (Thunb.) Ker-Gawl. (Liliaceae). The tuber is collected in the summer, washed clean, dried under the sun and piled up until almost dry. After removal of the fibrous roots, the tuber is finally dried to obtain Radix Ophiopogonis."},{"name":"11","text":"Spindle-shaped, both ends slightly pointed, 0.8-4.5 cm long, 2-9 mm in diameter in the middle part. Externally pale yellowish-white to yellowish-white or yellowish-brown; translucent and marked by fine, irregular and relatively deep longitudinal striae; one end often with a small exposed stele. Texture pliable; fracture yellowish-white to yellowish-brown, cuticular, translucent, with a small cylindrical stele in the centre. Odour slightly aromatic; taste sweet with a slight bitterness. Sticky when chewed (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Transverse section\n\nVelamen consists of 3-5 rows of lignified cells of subsquare, subrectangular or polygonal shape. Exodermal cells wall slightly thickened. Cortex broad, scattered with mucilage cells containing raphides of calcium oxalate of 2 types: one type is thin and short, the other type is thicker and longer. Stone cell layer consisting of 1-2 rows of cells is located outside of the endodermis, the shape of the cells long polygonal to subpolygonal, with the inner and lateral wall thickened, and having dense pits. Endodermis consists of cells with evenly thickened and lignified wall, as well as passage cells. Pericycle relatively small, consisting of 1-2 rows of parenchyma cells. Phloem bundles 16-22, located between two star-angles of the xylem bundles. Xylem consists of vessels, tracheids, xylem fibres and lignified cells in the inner side, linking up to a ring. Pith small, with suborbicular parenchyma cells (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[18, 1, 1967, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Raphides of calcium oxalate abundant, scattered or in bundles located in suborbicular to elliptic mucilage cells; the thin type 12-65 $ \\mu $ m long, some are thicker and longer, up to 129 $ \\mu $ m in length. Bright orange or polychromatic observed under the polarized microscope. Stone cells usually in groups; colourless; subsquare, rectangular or polygonal, 21-57 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; walls thickened unevenly; pit canals distinct, pits dense, flat-ellipsoid or short slit-shaped. Bright white in colour is observed under the polarized microscope. Cells of the endodermis rectangular or band-shaped; the pit canals distinct; pits dotted. Xylem fibres slender, their ends oblique or straight, the wall slightly thickened; pits oblique slit-shaped, cross-shaped or V-shaped (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Ophiopogonin D standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ophiopogonin D CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (8:2:0.3, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 60 min. Cool down to room temperature and filter. Wash the residue with 10 mL of methanol. Combine the filtrate and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 25 mL of water. Transfer the mixture to a 100-mL separatory funnel. Extract with 25 mL of water-saturated 1-butanol. Collect the 1-butanol extract and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of methanol and then filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ophiopogonin D standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 1, 1946, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 444, 1726, 2127])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Ophiopogonis A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Endodermis and stele D. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Velamen 2. Exodermis 3. Cortex 4. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Stone cells layer 6. Endodermis 7. Pericycle 8. Phloem 9. Xylem 10. Pith\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Radix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[347, 452, 971, 898])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1011, 452, 1634, 898])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[348, 929, 971, 1324])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1011, 929, 1634, 1328])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[347, 1363, 972, 1810])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1010, 1363, 1633, 1809])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[348, 1833, 970, 2217])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1012, 1837, 1635, 2220])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Raphides of calcium oxalate (thin) 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate (thick and long)\n\n3. Stone cells 4. Endodermal cells 5. Xylem fibres\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 0, 1945, 255])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of ophiopogonin D.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[577, 508, 1503, 933])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of ophiopogonin D\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Ophiopogonin D standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (300 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of ophiopogonin D CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 60 min. Cool down to room temperature and filter. Wash the residue with 10 mL of methanol. Combine the filtrate and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 25 mL of water. Transfer the mixture to a 100-mL separatory funnel. Extract with 25 mL of water-saturated 1-butanol. Collect the 1-butanol extract and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $; nebulizer gas $ \\mathrm{N_{2}} $ ) flow: 2.0 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>75→60</td><td>25→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-50</td><td>60→50</td><td>40→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>50→35</td><td>50→65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 0, 1966, 250])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ophiopogonin D Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ophiopogonin D should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ophiopogonin D peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ophiopogonin D peak should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject ophiopogonin D Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of ophiopogonin D peak in the chromatogram of ophiopogonin D Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify ophiopogonin D peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of ophiopogonin D Std-FP. The retention times of ophiopogonin D peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Ophiopogonis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Ophiopogonis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.51</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.64</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, ophiopogonin D)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[857, 23, 1090, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[946, 180, 1062, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[386, 430, 1751, 1002])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Ophiopogonis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 18.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 60.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 43.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[25, 0, 1955, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Ophiopogonin D standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of ophiopogonin D CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nOphiopogonin D standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the ophiopogonin D Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 100,200,300,400,500 mg/L for ophiopogonin D."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-ophiopogonis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Ophiopogonis","la":"Radix Ophiopogonis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-paeoniae-alba-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Paeoniae Alba v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[634, 885, 1310, 1714])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Paeoniae Alba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 1, 1966, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[634, 885, 1310, 1714])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Paeoniae Alba\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 1, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Paeoniae Alba\n\nChinese Name: 白芍\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baishao"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Paeoniae Alba is the dried processed root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall. (Ranunculaceae). The root is collected in the summer and autumn; after washing it, the two ends and the rootlets are removed. The rootbark is peeled either before or after boiling the root in water, followed by drying the root under the sun to obtain Radix Paeoniae Alba."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical, straight or slightly curved, the two ends truncate, 5-27 cm long, 8-34 mm in diameter. Externally whitish or pale reddish-brown, smooth and glossy or with longitudinal wrinkles, with rootlet scars and occasional remains of brown cork. Texture compact, not easily broken; fracture relatively even, whitish or pale brownish-red; cambium ring distinct, the rays radial. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and sour (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Parenchyma cells in the cortex elongated tangentially. Cambium in a ring. Xylem rays consist of 6 to more than 30 rows of cells. Vessels arranged radially. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate and masses of gelatinized starch grains are found in parenchyma cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-white to pale brown. Masses of gelatinized starch grains fairly abundant. Cluster\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 2, 1955, 251])\n\ncrystals of calcium oxalate 8-37 $ \\mu $m in diameter, crystal cells sometimes joined, arranged in rows or one to several cluster crystals in one cell, showing polychrome when examined under the polarized microscope. Bordered-pitted or reticulate vessels 13-66 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Xylem fibres long-fusiform with thickened and slightly lignified wall, which is large round-pitted or oblique-pitted (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of water. Heat the mixture to boil, then cool down to room temperature. Transfer the mixture to a 15-mL centrifuge tube and centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer 1 mL of the supernatant to a test tube. Add 1 drop of iron (III) chloride solution (5% ,w/v) to the mixture and mix well. A dark blue or dark green solution is observed (make appropriate dilution with water to observe where necessary)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 mg of paeoniflorin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate, methanol and formic acid (250:25:50:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Mix 1 mL of dilute sulphuric acid ( 50% , v/v) and 10 mL of p-hydroxybenzaldehyde in methanol ( 2% , w/v ). Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[12, 0, 1984, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[348, 498, 871, 980])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[399, 1064, 932, 1616])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n50μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[401, 1698, 929, 2099])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n25μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[987, 464, 1734, 2117])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Paeoniae Alba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Part of xylem D. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cortex 2. Phloem 3. Cambium 4. Xylem 5. Xylem rays 6. Vessels 7. Xylem fibres\n\n8. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 5, 1973, 253])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[261, 429, 1735, 2022])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Paeoniae Alba\n\n1. Starch masses 2. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate 3. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Xylem fibres 5. Reticulate vessels 6. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately paeoniflorin standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of paeoniflorin.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[799, 1101, 1262, 1643])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of paeoniflorin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Paeoniflorin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of paeoniflorin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (273 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[53, 0, 1924, 251])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\npacked with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>100→90</td><td>0→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>90</td><td>10</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>90→75</td><td>10→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of paeoniflorin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of paeoniflorin should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of paeoniflorin peak should not be more than 1.0%; the column efficiency determined from paeoniflorin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject paeoniflorin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of paeoniflorin peak in the chromatogram of paeoniflorin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify paeoniflorin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of paeoniflorin Std-FP. The retention times of paeoniflorin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Paeoniae Alba extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1968, 249])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Paeoniae Alba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,paeoniflorin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.36</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[402, 879, 1750, 1540])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Paeoniae Alba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[26, 9, 1939, 242])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 16.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-paeoniae-alba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Paeoniae Alba","la":"Radix Paeoniae Alba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-paeoniae-rubra-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Paeoniae Rubra v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[719, 625, 1379, 1329])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[701, 1683, 1305, 2405])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root of Paeonia veitchii Lynch\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1967, 251])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[719, 625, 1379, 1329])\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[701, 1683, 1305, 2405])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried root of Paeonia veitchii Lynch\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1967, 251])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Radix Paeoniae Rubra\n\nChinese Name: 赤芍\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chishao"},{"name":"15","text":"Radix Paeoniae Rubra is the dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall. or Paeonia veitchii Lynch (Ranunculaceae). The root is collected in the spring and autumn; after removal of the rhizomes, rootlets and soil, the root is dried under the sun to obtain Radix Paeoniae Rubra."},{"name":"15","text":"Cylindrical, slightly curved, 4-40 cm long, 5-30 mm in diameter. Externally brown or reddish-brown, rough, longitudinally furrowed and wrinkled, and showing rootlet scars and transversely prominent lenticels, sometimes the outer bark easily exfoliated. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken; fracture chalk-white or pink, bark narrow, wood with distinct radial striations, sometimes with clefts. Odour slightly fragrant; taste slightly bitter, sour and astringent [Fig. 1(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Paeonia lactiflora Pall. : The cork consisting of several layers of brown cells. Parenchyma cells in the cortex elongated tangentially. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem rays consist of 7 to more than 30 rows of cells; vessels, xylem fibres and parenchyma cells tangentially and alternately arranged; central xylem mostly appearing as diarch to polyarch. Parenchyma cells contain cluster crystals of calcium oxalate and starch grains [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nPaeonia veitchii Lynch: Cork consisting of several layers of brown cells. Parenchyma cells in the cortex elongated tangentially. Cambium in a ring. Xylem rays consist of 3-21 rows of cells;\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[28, 0, 1952, 252])\n\nvessels arranged radially, accompanied by fewer xylem fibres. Parenchyma cells contain cluster crystals of calcium oxalate and starch grains [Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour greyish-white to pale brown. Simple starch granules subglobular or ellipsoid, showing a black, cross shape when examined under the polarized microscope. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate 5-46 $ \\mu $m in diameter, crystal cells sometimes joined, arranged in rows or one to several cluster crystals in one cell, showing polychrome when examined under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres long-fusiform with thickened and slightly lignified wall, with oblique or cross pits. Bordered-pitted or reticulate vessels 11-80 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Cork cells brown or reddish-brown, striped, rectangular or polygonal, the wall slightly thickened, straight or slightly sinuous [Fig. 3(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 25-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of water. Heat the mixture to boil, then cool down to room temperature. Transfer the mixture to a 15-mL centrifuge tube and centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer 1 mL of the supernatant to a test tube. Add 1 drop of iron (III) chloride solution (5%, w/v) to the mixture and mix well. A dark blue or dark green solution is observed (make appropriate dilution with water to observe where necessary)."},{"name":"15","text":"Paeoniflorin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of paeoniflorin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate, methanol and formic acid (250:25:50:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Mix 1 mL of dilute sulphuric acid ( 50% , v/v) and 10 mL of p-hydroxybenzaldehyde in methanol ( 2% , w/v ). Freshly prepare the reagent.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1, 0, 1984, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[244, 405, 1744, 2190])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Part of xylem D. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem 6. Rays 7. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n8. Vessels 9. Xylem fibres\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[44, 6, 1961, 256])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[334, 480, 935, 969])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[368, 1088, 915, 1685])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[374, 1792, 916, 2138])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[941, 429, 1733, 2140])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Paeonia veitchii Lynch\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Part of xylem D. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem 6. Rays 7. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n8. Vessels 9. Xylem fibres\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[12, 0, 1984, 257])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[256, 460, 1741, 2057])\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall.\n\n1. Starch grains 2. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate 3. Xylem fibres 4. Reticulate vessels\n\n5. Bordered-pitted vessel 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 1, 1974, 256])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[245, 447, 1741, 2008])\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Paeonia veitchii Lynch\n\n1. Starch grains 2. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate 3. Xylem fibres 4. Reticulate vessels\n\n5. Bordered-pitted vessel 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1967, 249])"},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately paeoniflorin standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of paeoniflorin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[807, 1457, 1293, 2031])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of paeoniflorin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"15","text":"Paeoniflorin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L) Weigh 2.0 mg of paeoniflorin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (273 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>100→90</td><td>0→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>90</td><td>10</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>90→75</td><td>10→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of paeoniflorin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of paeoniflorin should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of paeoniflorin peak should not be more than 1.0%; the column efficiency determined from paeoniflorin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates."},{"name":"15","text":"The R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 5(i)]."},{"name":"15","text":"The R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 5(ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject paeoniflorin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of paeoniflorin peak in the chromatogram of paeoniflorin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify paeoniflorin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of paeoniflorin Std-FP. The retention times of paeoniflorin peaks from the two\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[44, 40, 261, 143])\n\nchromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall. extract and Paeonia veitchii Lynch extract are listed in Table 1(i) and (ii) respectively.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1(i) The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.69</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,paeoniflorin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.46</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[402, 1237, 1727, 1844])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Paeonia lactiflora Pall. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1(ii) The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of dried root of Paeonia veitchii Lynch extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.64</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,paeoniflorin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.46</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[399, 381, 1755, 1013])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Paeonia veitchii Lynch extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[39, 1, 1967, 244])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-paeoniae-rubra","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Paeoniae Rubra","la":"Radix Paeoniae Rubra"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-panacis-quinquefolii-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Panacis Quinquefolii v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[180, 727, 908, 1414])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1029, 718, 1727, 1418])\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>1cm</td><td>1cm</td></tr><tr><td>Figure 1(i)A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii(from Ji Lin, China)</td><td>Figure 1(ii)A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii(from Ontario, Canada)</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[84, 1716, 923, 2493])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii (from British Columbia, Canada)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1000, 1705, 1887, 2489])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iv) A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii (from Wisconsin, USA)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1945, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"19","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[180, 727, 908, 1414])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1029, 718, 1727, 1418])\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>1cm</td><td>1cm</td></tr><tr><td>Figure 1(i)A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii(from Ji Lin, China)</td><td>Figure 1(ii)A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii(from Ontario, Canada)</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[84, 1716, 923, 2493])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iii) A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii (from British Columbia, Canada)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1000, 1705, 1887, 2489])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(iv) A photograph of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii (from Wisconsin, USA)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1945, 249])"},{"name":"19","text":"Official Name: Radix Panacis Quinquefolii\n\nChinese Name: 西洋参\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xiyangshen"},{"name":"19","text":"Radix Panacis Quinquefolii is the dried root of Panax quinquefolium L. (Araliaceae). All the commercial supplies are obtained from cultivated plants. The root is collected in autumn, washed clean and dried under the sun or at a temperature below $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to obtain Radix Panacis Quinquefolii."},{"name":"19","text":"Fusiform, cylindrical or conical, 3-20 cm (occasionally up to 24 cm) long, 4-28 mm (occasionally up to 34 mm) in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown, yellowish-white, pale yellowish-brown or pale yellowish-white, exhibiting transverse annulations and linear lenticels, and showing shallow, fine and dense longitudinal wrinkles, and scars of rootlets. The middle and lower part of the main root, with 1 to several lateral roots, are mostly broken off. In some cases, the upper end presents remains of a rhizome (Lutou), with prominent annular nodes. Stem scars (Luwan) rounded or semirounded, with adventitious roots (Ding) remaining or already broken off. Texture heavy and hard, uneasily broken; fracture even, yellowish-white, horny or slightly starchy, with the bark exhibiting yellowishbrown to reddish-brown dotted resin canals, a brownish-yellow cambium ring, and wood arranged roughly in a radial pattern. Odour slight but characteristic; taste slightly bitter, gradually becoming sweet [Fig. 1(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)].\n\nRadix Panacis Quinquefolii from the four production areas show some differences from one another, as follows-\n\nSamples from China (Ji Lin): pale yellowish-brown to pale yellowish-white, plump, with apparent longitudinal wrinkles. They are relatively pale, plump and light in texture than those from USA and Canada.\n\nSamples from Canada (Ontario and British Columbia): pale yellowish-white, larger, with distinct longitudinal wrinkles. Their textures are heavier than that from China.\n\nSamples from USA (Wisconsin): yellowish-brown, smaller, with distinct transverse striations. Their textures are the heaviest among samples from the four areas.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[10, 2, 1965, 246])"},{"name":"19","text":"Cork consists of 6-8 rows of elongated tangential cells, with several layers often peeled off at the outer side. Cortex consists of more than 10 rows of parenchyma cells, some containing cluster of calcium oxalate. Resin canals scattered, surrounded by 5-11 secretory cells. In the phloem, resin canals are frequent and usually arranged in 1-3 concentric rings, the rays in the outer part with clefts. Cambium ring distinct. Vessels scattered singly or in groups of 2-10, with interrupted radial arrangement, and lignified or slightly lignified. Rays consist of 1-4 rows of cells wide. Parenchyma cells contain starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"19","text":"Colour pale brown or pale yellowish-white. Resin canals in longitudinal view appearing pipeline-shaped, containing a large quantity of yellow oil secretion in the shape of a drop, as well as a jacinth secretion in the shape of a strip. Cluster of calcium oxalate numerous, 8-91 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, bright polychromatic observed under a polarized microscope. Cork cells colourless, pale yellow to pale yellowish-brown, subpolygonal or subsquare, anticlinal wall thin and undulated curved. Vessels mainly reticulate and scalariform, but annular and spiral vessels can also be observed. Simple starch granules subglobose to ovoid, 2-28 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, hilum V-shaped, dotted or slit-shaped, the striations distinct; compound granules few, composed of 2-9 components; black, cruciate shape is observed under a polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"19","text":"Ginsenoside Rb standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinsenoside Rc standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of ginsenoside Rc CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nGinsenoside $ R g_{l} $ standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{11} $ standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{1 1} $ CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[860, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1671, 12, 1872, 84])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1132, 54, 1246, 106])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1063, 239])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii Radix Panacis Quinquefolli\n\na Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[233, 452, 1733, 1959])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Panacis Quinquefoli A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Resin canal D. Cluster of calcium oxalate 1. Cork 2. Cluster of calcium oxalate 3. Cortex 4. Resin canal 5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Cleft 9. Starch granules\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n燈心草\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\nRadix Panacis Quinquefolii\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[347, 445, 951, 837])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1030, 444, 1637, 835])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[347, 849, 952, 1220])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1031, 852, 1638, 1220])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[346, 1241, 952, 1595])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[346, 1616, 953, 2012])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1029, 1240, 1640, 1596])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1030, 1620, 1641, 2010])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii\n\n1. Resin canal 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n3. Cork cells 4. Vessel 5. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 0, 1939, 253])"},{"name":"19","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (13:7:2, v/v). Keep in a refrigerator at a temperature below $ 6^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for at least 10 h. Use the lower layer."},{"name":"19","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 10-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min and then filter."},{"name":"19","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ standard solution, ginsenoside Rc standard solution, ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ standard solution and 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{1 1} $ standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $ ginsenoside Rc, ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ and 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{11}. $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[548, 1812, 1517, 2456])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of (i) ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{1} $\n\n</div>\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\n白术\n\nFolium Ginkgo 銀杏\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n燈心草\n\n北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nSemen Cassiae明子\n\n決明子\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n遠志\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n黄芩 Radix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\n地黄\n\nRadix Panacis Quinquefolii\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[576, 386, 1465, 1044])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[554, 1103, 1478, 1757])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[528, 1811, 1419, 2464])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (ii) ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R b}_{2} $ (iii) ginsenoside Rc (iv) ginsenoside Rd\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[38, 9, 256, 96])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\n吳茱萸\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n( v )\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[604, 386, 1316, 1036])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vi)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(vii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[647, 1153, 1278, 1749])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[588, 1802, 1182, 2458])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (v) ginsenoside Re (vi) ginsenoside $ \\mathrm{R g}_{1} $ (vii) 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{11} $\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[19, 0, 1965, 250])"},{"name":"19","text":"Ginsenoside Rc standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of ginsenoside Rc CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol ( 70% ).\n\n24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{11} $ standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{1 1} $ CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol (70%)."},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue with 20 mL of methanol (70%). Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Combine the extracts and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (70%). Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"19","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $; nebulizer gas $ \\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right) $ flow: 2.4 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>68</td><td>32</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-50</td><td>68→57</td><td>32→43</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"19","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{1 1} $ Std-FP and ginsenoside Rc Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{1 1} $ and ginsenoside Rc should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention times of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{1 1} $ peak and ginsenoside Rc peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ \\mathrm{F}_{1 1} $ and ginsenoside Rc peaks should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 2, 1940, 256])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)]."},{"name":"19","text":"Separately inject 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{1 1} $ Std-FP, ginsenoside Rc Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{1 1} $ and ginsenoside Rc peaks in the chromatogram of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{1 1} $ Std-FP, ginsenoside Rc Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{1 1} $ and ginsenoside Rc peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{1 1} $ Std-FP and ginsenoside Rc Std-FP. The retention times of 24-(R)-pseudoginsenoside $ F_{1 1} $ and ginsenoside Rc peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1[marker1,24-(R)-pseudoginsenosideF11]</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2[marker2,ginsenosideRc)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(ginsenosideRb2)</td><td>1.12(vs peak2)</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.17(vs peak2)</td><td>$\\pm0.06$</td></tr><tr><td>5(ginsenosideRd)</td><td>1.41(vs peak2)</td><td>$\\pm0.07$</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.57(vs peak2)</td><td>$\\pm0.09$</td></tr></table>\n\n北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n遠志\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRadix Panacis Quinquefolii\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[394, 475, 1743, 1006])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii extract (from Shandong, China)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[390, 1252, 1745, 1788])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii extract (from Ontario, Canada)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[382, 1925, 1752, 2543])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii extract (from British Columbia, Canada)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[30, 0, 1926, 247])\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[381, 457, 1750, 1007])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(iv) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Panacis Quinquefolii extract (from Wisconsin, USA)\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram [Fig. 5(i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)]."},{"name":"19","text":"Ginsenoside Re standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (250 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of ginsenoside Re CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 0.4 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 20 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for three more times. Wash the residue with 10 mL of methanol (70%). Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Combine the extracts and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (70%). Transfer the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"19","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 2 2^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 3) -\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[20, 0, 1960, 251])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 3 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.002M Potassium dihydrogen phosphate(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>79</td><td>21</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>15-69</td><td>79→62</td><td>21→38</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>69-75</td><td>62</td><td>38</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"19","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of ginsenoside Re Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of ginsenoside Re should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of ginsenoside Re peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from ginsenoside Re peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 6(i), (ii), (iii) or (iv)]."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-panacis-quinquefolii","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Panacis Quinquefolii","la":"Radix Panacis Quinquefolii"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-platycodi-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Platycodi v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[648, 820, 1388, 1869])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Platycodi\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[648, 820, 1388, 1869])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Platycodi\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1968, 255])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Radix Platycodi\n\nChinese Name: 桔梗\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jiegeng"},{"name":"12","text":"Radix Platycodi is the dried root of Platycodon grandiflorum (Jacq.) A. DC. (Campanulaceae). The root is collected in the spring or autumn after removal of the rootlets, it is peeled or unpeeled, then dried thoroughly."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical, fusiform, or somewhat thin and long, slightly twisted, gradually tapering, often branched, main root 7-26 cm in length, 5-25 mm in diameter at the upper end. Sometimes the apex showing a shorter rhizome with round or crescent-shaped stem scars. Externally white or yellowish-brown, covered with coarse longitudinal wrinkles, lateral furrows and lenticel-like lateral lines. Texture fragile, fracture surface, even or uneven, cortex white or somewhat yellowish, xylem yellowish-white or pale brown, cambium ring brown, pale brown or not clear. Odourless or slightly scented of caramel smell. Tasteless at first, later bittersweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork cells occasionally remain, consisting of several layers of suberized cells. Cortex narrow, often with clefts. Groups of laticiferous tubes scattered in a radial fashion in the phloem, containing yellowish-brown granules. Cambium in a ring. Xylem vessels scattered singly or in groups, arranged radially (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[24, 1, 1953, 252])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour light greyish-yellow to light greyish-brown. Cork cells occasionally observed, polygonal in shape. Fragments of groups of laticiferous tubes easily observed, containing yellowish or yellowish-brown granules in the cells. Inulin abundant, fan-shaped or irregular, with radial striations, scattered or contained within parenchyma cells. Vessels reticulate, scalariform, or bordered-pitted, 13-78 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Filter and transfer 1 mL of the filtrate to a test tube. Cautiously add about 1 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for about 20 min. An orange-brown ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"12","text":"Platycoside E standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of platycoside E CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and acetic acid (5:8:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 3 mL of water. Load the water extract onto a Hydrophilic-Lipophilic Balance (HLB) extraction cartridge (3 mL, 60 mg). Wash the cartridge with 6 mL of water. Elute with 2 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[309, 457, 915, 1105])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[360, 1156, 952, 1554])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[351, 1592, 882, 2021])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[993, 479, 1745, 2031])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Platycodi A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cork cell D. Laticiferous tube group 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 0, 1974, 256])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[362, 460, 1729, 1991])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Platycodi\n\n</div>\n\n1. Laticiferous tube 2. Cork cells 3. Scalariform vessel 4. Inulin\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1974, 252])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately platycoside E standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and leave for about 30 min for equilibrium. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5- 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of platycoside E.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[391, 1388, 1759, 1876])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of platycoside E\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Platycoside E standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of platycoside E CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nPlatycoside E standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nPipette 1 mL of platycoside E Std-Stock into a 2-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 50 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction once. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of methanol. Add 50 mL of diethyl ether and shake for 1 min. Allow to stand for 12 h. Collect the precipitate and dissolve in 3 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (201 nm) and a column $ (3.9\\times 300\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>90→70</td><td>10→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of platycoside E Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of platycoside E should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of platycoside E peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from platycoside E peak should not be less than 5000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 and the R value between peak 4 and peak 5 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 0.7 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject platycoside E Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of platycoside E peak in the chromatogram of platycoside E Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify platycoside E peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of platycoside E Std-FP. The retention times of platycoside E peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1975, 254])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Platycodi extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Platycodi extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.95</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, platycoside E)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.56</td><td>±0.08</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.59</td><td>±0.08</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[391, 1148, 1754, 1675])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Platycodi extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[29, 1, 1940, 248])\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 49.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 25.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-platycodi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Platycodi","la":"Radix Platycodi"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-polygalae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Polygalae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[585, 849, 1525, 1809])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1941, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[585, 849, 1525, 1809])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1941, 253])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Polygalae\n\nChinese Name: 遠志\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Yuanzhi"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Polygalae is the dried root of Polygala tenuifolia Willd. (Polygalaceae). The root is collected in spring and autumn, washed clean, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Radix Polygalae."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical, somewhat hollow and curved, 0.5-8 cm long, 1-8 mm in diameter. Externally pale yellow to light greyish-brown, marked by dense and dented transverse wrinkles, longitudinal striations and fissures, and by somewhat nodulated scars of rootlet. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken; fracture shows a yellowish bark enclosing a pale yellow central woody portion, which can be separated easily from the bark. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet, subsequently acrid and irritating to the throat (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several orderly rows of pale brown cork cells. Cortex consists of parenchyma cells, with clefts. Phloem relatively broad. Cambium prominent, in a ring. Xylem well developed, rays present in 1-2 rows. Parenchyma cells in the phloem contain droplets of oil, some contain cluster of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour greyish-yellow to yellowish-brown. Parenchyma cells observed, containing oil droplets. Xylem fibres 6-13 $ \\mu $m in diameter, slightly lignified. Pitted fibre tracheids abundant. Cork cells subpolygonal, slightly lignified. Bordered-pitted, reticulated or spiral vessels are found, 9-42 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Parenchyma cells abundant. Cluster of calcium oxalate scattered, sometimes in the parenchyma cells, 10-26 $ \\mu $m in diameter, relatively rare (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[27, 0, 1960, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Tenuifolin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of tenuifolin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and water (10:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Transfer the mixture to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer 25 mL of the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 50 mL of sodium hydroxide (10%, w/v). Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool to room temperature. Add about 10 mL of hydrochloric acid (37%, v/v) to adjust the pH of the solution to 4-5. Transfer the solution to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract with 50 mL of water-saturated 1-butanol. Shake the solution for about 1 min. Allow to stand for about 20 min until the two layers are separated. Transfer the 1-butanol extract to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the 1-butanol extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 25 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately tenuifolin standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for above 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[859, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1063, 239])\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草 Herba Leonuri 平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis 吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[268, 431, 1701, 1830])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Polygalae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cluster of calcium oxalate 1. Cork 2. Clefts 3. Cortex 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Xylem rays\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[326, 447, 1025, 928])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1069, 450, 1393, 937])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[327, 983, 1392, 1619])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1455, 446, 1659, 1617])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[326, 1672, 976, 2129])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1025, 1672, 1659, 2122])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Polygalae\n\n</div>\n\n1. Oil droplets 2. Cluster of calcium oxalate 3. Xylem fibres\n\n4. Fibre tracheids 5. Reticulate vessels 6. Cork cells 7. Parenchyma cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1944, 253])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of tenuifolin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[699, 559, 1396, 951])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[675, 1041, 1368, 1699])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) tenuifolin and (ii) 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Allow to stand for 30 min. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[24, 1, 1959, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-25</td><td>80→75</td><td>20→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-35</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-60</td><td>75→70</td><td>25→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>60-70</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 3'，6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 3'，6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 3'，6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 3'，6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester peak should not be less than 3000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester peak in the chromatogram of 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester Std-FP. The retention times of 3',6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Polygalae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[36, 0, 1938, 254])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Polygalae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,3’,6-di-O-sinapoyl-sucrose ester)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.11(vs peak1)</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.22(vs peak2)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.29(vs peak2)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.69(vs peak2)</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[387, 956, 1753, 1491])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Polygalae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 1, 1959, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 32.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 35.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Tenuifolin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (2000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of tenuifolin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nTenuifolin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the tenuifolin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 200,400,600,800,1000 mg/L for tenuifolin."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatant. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 50 mL of sodium hydroxide (10%, w/v). Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool to room temperature. Add about 10 mL of hydrochloric acid (37%, v/v) to adjust the pH of the solution to 4-5. Transfer the solution to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract with 50 mL of water-saturated 1-butanol. Shake the solution for about 1 min. Allow to stand for about 20 min until the two layers are separated. Transfer the 1-butanol extract to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction twice. Combine the 1-butanol extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at about $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-polygalae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Polygalae","la":"Radix Polygalae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-polygoni-multiflori-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Polygoni Multiflori v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[595, 829, 1385, 1909])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (a) A photograph of Radix Polygoni Multiflori (b) A cross-section of Radix Polygoni Multiflori\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1965, 250])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[595, 829, 1385, 1909])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (a) A photograph of Radix Polygoni Multiflori (b) A cross-section of Radix Polygoni Multiflori\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1965, 250])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Radix Polygoni Multiflori\n\nChinese Name: 何首烏\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Heshouwu"},{"name":"12","text":"Radix Polygoni Multiflori is the dried tuberous root of Polygonum multiflorum Thunb. (Polygonaceae). The tuberous root is collected in autumn and winter when the leaves wither. Both pointed ends are cut off, washed clean, and the large ones are cut into pieces, then dried to obtain Radix Polygoni Multiflori."},{"name":"12","text":"The tuberous root is lump-shaped or irregularly fusiform in shape, 7-17 cm long, 2.5-11 cm in diameter, externally reddish-brown, shrunken and uneven, shallowly grooved, with transverse elongated lenticels and fine rootlet scars. The texture is heavy, compact, not easily broken, with the fracture pale yellowishbrown or pale reddish-brown, and starchy. The phloem exhibits 4-11 subround allotype vascular bundles arranged in a ring, forming brocaded patterns, the xylem in the central part relatively large, some having a woody core. Odourless; the taste bitterish, sweetish and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"The cork consists of several layers of cells filled with brown contents. Phloem relatively broad, scattered with 4-11 subround allotype vascular bundles of collateral type, vessels few. The central cambium of the tuberous root is in the form of a ring, few vessels in the xylem, surrounded by some tracheids and a few xylem fibres. The parenchyma cells contain cluster crystals of calcium oxalate and starch grains (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[28, 1, 1954, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Simple starch grains numerous, subround, 3-81 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum V-shaped, stellate or Y-shaped, striations of large ones fairly distinct; compound grains consist of 2-9 units, black and cruciate in shape when observed under the polarized microscope. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate 14-129 $ \\mu $m in diameter, jointed with prisms occasionally found. Colour bright orange when observed under the polarized microscope. Surface view of cork cells polygonal, filled with yellowish-brown contents in the lumina. Bordered pitted vessels 12-118 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 10 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (10%, w/v). Cautiously heat the mixture in a hot water bath for 3 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer 5 mL of the filtrate to another test tube, acidify the filtrate with hydrochloric acid (6.2%, v/v) until a pale pink colour solution is observed (about 8 mL). Add 5 mL of diethyl ether. Vortex. Transfer 4 mL of the upper layer to another test tube. Add 2 mL of ammonia solution. Vortex. A red or reddish-brown coloration is observed in the lower layer."},{"name":"12","text":"2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nEmodin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of emodin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nPhyscion standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of physcion CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Developing solvent system 1\n\nPrepare a mixture of toluene, ethanol and glacial acetic acid (4:3:0.5, v/v).\n\nDeveloping solvent system 2\n\nPrepare a mixture of toluene and ethanol (4:1, v/v).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[13, 1, 1984, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[267, 438, 1742, 2043])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Polygoni Multiflori A. Sketch B. Transverse section illustration C. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate D. Allotype vascular bundles\n\n1. Cork 2. Parenchyma cells 3. Allotype vascular bundles 4. Central cambium of the tuberous root\n\n5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 1, 1973, 252])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[346, 438, 1001, 926])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1009, 444, 1644, 930])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[344, 943, 1003, 1411])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1011, 947, 1645, 1414])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[352, 1429, 1001, 1908])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1018, 1433, 1642, 1913])\n\n100μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Polygoni Multiflori\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate 2. Starch grains 3. Vessels 4. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[42, 0, 1972, 247])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min and then filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent systems as described above. Apply separately 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), emodin standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), physcion standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 3.5 cm in developing solvent system 1, after the development, remove the plate from the chamber and dry in air. Redevelop over a path of 6 cm in developing solvent system 2. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of 2,3,5,4'- tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside, emodin and physcion.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[748, 1617, 1318, 2094])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[830, 2247, 1234, 2496])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[35, 3, 1938, 252])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[802, 408, 1248, 660])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside (ii) emodin and (iii) physcion\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol.\n\nEmodin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of emodin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 25 mL of acetone. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with acetone. Transfer 5 mL of the solution to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (290 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>90→60</td><td>10→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-55</td><td>60→0</td><td>40→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-70</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[45, 0, 1964, 249])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene -2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene -2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP, emodin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak and emodin peak in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak and emodin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing their retention times with those in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP. The retention times of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks and emodin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ from their counterparts by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Polygoni Multiflori extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Polygoni Multiflori extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker 1,2,3,5,4’-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O-β-D-glucoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.56 (vs peak 1)</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3 (marker 2, emodin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4 (physcion)</td><td>1.12 (vs peak 3)</td><td>±0.02</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[407, 369, 1757, 1041])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Polygoni Multiflori extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[43, 1, 1960, 237])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\n2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the 2,3,5,4'-tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 10, 50, 100, 200, 400 mg/L for 2,3,5,4'- tetrahydroxystilbene-2-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-polygoni-multiflori","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Polygoni Multiflori","la":"Radix Polygoni Multiflori"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-pseudostellariae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Pseudostellariae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[461, 846, 1523, 1849])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 1, 1944, 247])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[461, 846, 1523, 1849])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 1, 1944, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Pseudostellariae\n\nChinese Name: 太子參\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Taizishen"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Pseudostellariae is the dried root tuber of Pseudostellaria heterophylla (Miq.) Pax ex Pax et Hoffm. (Caryophyllaceae). The tuber is collected in the summer when most of the stems and leaves wither, washed clean, the rootlets and branch root removed; then put in boiling water for 1-3 min and dried thoroughly under the sun, or put it under the sun until dry to obtain Radix Pseudostellariae."},{"name":"11","text":"Slender fusiform or slat-shaped, slightly curved; 1.0-9.8 cm long, 2-7 mm in diameter. The top end broader, bearing stem scars, becoming slender towards the distal end. Externally yellowish-white to yellowish-brown, relatively smooth, with light longitudinal wrinkles; the dented parts of the surface are marked with scars of rootlets. Texture hard and fragile, breaking easily; fracture smooth, either pale yellowish-white and cuticular or off-white and starchy. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet (Fig.1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of 2-4 rows of subsquare and suberized cells. Cortex thin, consisting only of several rows of tangentially elongated parenchyma cells. Phloem narrow, rays broad. Cambium in a ring. The major portion of the root is occupied by xylem tissue, with vessels radially and distantly disposed, the primary xylem triarch or tetrarch. Parenchyma cells contain starch granules and cluster of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale yellowish-white. Starch granules numerous, mostly of the simple type, subglobose; 2-29 (occasionally up to 35) $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; hilum asteroidal, Y-shaped, V-shaped or cleft-shaped, obscure in the small granules, but with indistinct striations in the large ones. Compound granules consist of 2-4 components, 5 components may be seen occasionally, 6-42 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Black cruciate shape observed under the polarized microscope. Cluster\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 0, 1966, 249])\n\nof calcium oxalate 6-66 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, brightly polychromatic observed under the polarized microscope. Vessels are mostly with spiral wall thickening, 4-39 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; reticulate, scalariform and bordered-pitted vessels are also observed. Cork cells pale brownish-yellow, irregularly long-polygonal to subsquare, wall thin and slightly undulated and curved (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"$ \\Delta^{7} $ -Stigmasterol-3-O-$ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (8:2:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100 mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of water. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separatory funnel. Extract for three times each with 10 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and discard the petroleum ether layer. Extract the aqueous layer for three times each with 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol and then filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[861, 21, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1064, 239])\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[235, 408, 1735, 1943])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Parenchyma cells (containing starch granules and cluster of calcium oxalate)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem 6. Primary xylem\n\n7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Starch granules\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n北沙参\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[237, 436, 1666, 2037])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate 3. Vessel 4. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 10, 256, 94])\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi女貞子\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 23, 1089, 118])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[946, 177, 1068, 241])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1614, 11, 1922, 128])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[603, 371, 1410, 870])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"$ \\Delta^{7} $ -Stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L) Weigh 2.5 mg of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>8→0</td><td>92→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[38, 0, 1960, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmasterol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak should not be less than 12000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmastenol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmastenol- 3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak in the chromatogram of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmastenol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmastenol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmastenol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The retention times of $ \\Delta^{7} $ -stigmastenol-3-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Pseudostellariae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Pseudostellariae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.61</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker，$\\Delta^{7}$-stigmasterol-3-O-β-D-glucoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.62</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[946, 180, 1062, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[391, 484, 1758, 1005])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Pseudostellariae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 28.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[28, 0, 1959, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XIII."},{"name":"11","text":"Anthrone sulfuric acid solution\n\nWeigh accurately 0.1 g of anthrone and dissolve in 100 mL of sulphuric acid (80%)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-pseudostellariae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Pseudostellariae","la":"Radix Pseudostellariae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-puerariae-lobatae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Puerariae Lobatae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[12, 803, 1562, 1813])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[52, 0, 1948, 250])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[12, 803, 1562, 1813])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[52, 0, 1948, 250])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Radix Puerariae Lobatae\n\nChinese Name: 葛根\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gegen"},{"name":"10","text":"Radix Puerariae Lobatae is the dried root of Pueraria lobata (Willd.) Ohwi (Fabaceae). The root is collected in autumn or winter, cut into thick slices or into small cubes when fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Radix Puerariae Lobatae."},{"name":"10","text":"Usually in thick, longitudinally cut slice, or in small irregular cube, variable in size. Longitudinally cut slice 0.4-36.2 cm in length, 0.4-10 cm in width, 0.2-5.4 cm in thickness. Externally brown, with longitudinal wrinkles. Transverse section rough, pale yellowish-brown, several concentric annular rings indistinctly visible. Texture tough, pliable and strongly fibrous. Odourless; taste, slightly sweetish (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Cork broad, consisting of several rows of densely arranged cork cells. Cortex narrow. Fibre bundles are the major tissue elements, associated with the vessels, surrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, appearing as crystal fibres. Phloem and xylem are arranged alternately as hetero-vascular bundles, forming 1-3 concentric rings; vessels relatively large, numerous, densely and alternately arranged with fibre bundles, rays narrow, the parenchyma cells containing a few starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour pale brown to yellowish-white. Starch granules numerous, mainly of the simple type, but compound granules are sometimes found. Simple granules spherical, hemispherical or ellipsoidal, 2-53 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with dotted, cleft, or asteroidal hilum; compound granules composed of 2-12 units, 8-87 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fibre mostly in bundles, with thickened and lignified walls,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1966, 250])\n\nsurrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, parenchyma cells with thickened and lignified walls appear as crystal fibres. Bordered-pitted vessels large and numerous, densely packed. Pigment cells yellowish-brown, more-or-less oblong, usually scattered near fibres and vessels (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"Puerarin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of puerarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and water (12:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter through a filter paper."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately puerarin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of puerarin.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[861, 21, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 12, 1872, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1064, 239])\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心莲\n\n益母草 Herba Leonuri 平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis 吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[253, 493, 1713, 2240])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Puerariae Lobatae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibre bundle\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Fibre bundle 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Xylem ray\n\n7. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n銀杏葉\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1613, 150, 1692, 199])\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[244, 434, 1659, 2063])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"10","text":"1. Compound starch granules 2. Simple starch granules 3. Crystal fibres 4. Vessels\n\n5. Cork cells 6. Pigment cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[38, 10, 255, 94])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 23, 1089, 119])\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n女貞子\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[946, 177, 1068, 240])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[735, 373, 1311, 832])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of puerarin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Puerarin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of puerarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>90→65</td><td>10→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of puerarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of puerarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of puerarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from puerarin peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 0, 1966, 249])\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject puerarin Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of puerarin peak in the chromatogram of puerarin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify puerarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of puerarin Std-FP. The retention times of puerarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Puerariae Lobatae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Radix Puerariae Lobatae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.56</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2 (marker, puerarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4 (Daidzin)</td><td>1.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.86</td><td>±0.08</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[396, 1669, 1754, 2457])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Puerariae Lobatae extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[34, 1, 1942, 254])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 46.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 22.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"10","text":"Puerarin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (800 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 8.0 mg of puerarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (30%).\n\nPuerarin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the puerarin Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (30%) to produce a series of solutions of 16,40,80,160,240 mg/L for puerarin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-puerariae-lobatae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Puerariae Lobatae","la":"Radix Puerariae Lobatae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-puerariae-thomsonii-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Puerariae Thomsonii v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1943, 248])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1943, 248])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Radix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\nChinese Name: 粉葛\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Fenge"},{"name":"10","text":"Radix Puerariae Thomsonii is the dried root of Pueraria thomsonii Benth. (Fabaceae). The root is collected in autumn or winter, washed clean, the outer bark removed, dried slightly and cut into oblique thick slices or small cubes, then fully dried under the sun to obtain Radix Puerariae Thomsonii."},{"name":"10","text":"Intact roots cylindrical to subfusiform and conical, 0.4-39.3 cm in length, 27-84 mm in diameter; cut slices are oblique or transversely oriented or in the form of small cubes, variable in size. Externally yellowish-white, with remnant outer bark pale-brown. Texture heavy, hard, and starchy; transverse section pale yellowish-brown, appearing as concentric annular rings composed of fibres, the colour distinctly pale brown. Odourless; taste slightly sweetish (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Residual cork broad, consisting of several rows of densely arranged cork cells. Cortex narrow. Fibre bundles are arranged alternately and aligned with the vessels, surrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, which appear as crystal fibres. Phloem and xylem are arranged alternately into hetero-vascular bundles, forming 3-5 concentric rings; relatively large vessels in the xylem densely packed and alternately disposed with fibre bundles; rays narrow, parenchyma cells filled with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Numerous starch granules, mostly of the compound type, and a few simple ones, are observed; simple granules spheroidal, subglobose, 4-38 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with dotted, cleft or asteroidal hilum; compound granules composed of 2-15 simple granules, 8-78 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fibres mostly in bundles, with thickened walls, surrounded by parenchyma cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate. Vessels mostly bordered-pitted (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[23, 0, 1963, 247])"},{"name":"10","text":"Puerarin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of puerarin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and water (12:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter through a filter paper."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately puerarin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of puerarin.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[860, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1671, 12, 1872, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1064, 239])\n\nRadix Puerariae Thomsonii Bulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心莲\n\n益母草 Herba Leonuri 平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis 吴茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[258, 430, 1719, 2072])\n\n50μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Puerariae Thomsonii A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibre bundle\n\n</div>\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Fibre bundle 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Xylem ray\n\n7. Starch granules 8. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1363, 63, 1446, 105])\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n地黄\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\nRadix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[239, 415, 1740, 2086])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\n</div>\n\n1. Compound starch granules 2. Simple starch granules 3. Crystal fibres 4. Vessels\n\n5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 10, 255, 94])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 23, 1089, 118])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[946, 177, 1067, 240])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[698, 407, 1285, 868])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of puerarin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Puerarin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of puerarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (70 %)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>90→65</td><td>10→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of puerarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of puerarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of puerarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from puerarin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject puerarin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of puerarin peak in the chromatogram of puerarin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify puerarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of puerarin Std-FP. The retention times of puerarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Puerariae Thomsonii extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Radix Puerariae Thomsonii extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, puerarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.06</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3 (daidzin)</td><td>1.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.54</td><td>±0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[386, 1510, 1752, 2280])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Puerariae Thomsonii extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[858, 23, 1088, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[947, 180, 1061, 240])"},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 57.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 11.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"10","text":"Puerarin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (800 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 8.0 mg of puerarin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (30%).\n\nPuerarin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the puerarin Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (30%) to produce a series of solutions of 16, 40, 80, 160, 240 mg/L for puerarin."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh accurately 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethanol ( 70% ). Mix and filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[19, 1, 1966, 248])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-puerariae-thomsonii","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Puerariae Thomsonii","la":"Radix Puerariae Thomsonii"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-rehmanniae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Rehmanniae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[483, 1114, 1476, 1601])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Rehmanniae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1941, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[483, 1114, 1476, 1601])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Rehmanniae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 0, 1941, 252])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Rehmanniae\n\nChinese Name: 地黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Dihuang"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Rehmanniae is the dried root tuber of Rehmannia glutinosa Libosch. (Scrophulariaceae). The tuber is collected in autumn, root stocks, rootlets, soil and sand are removed, then slowly baked or dried under the sun until the inside turns black and almost dry to obtain Radix Rehmanniae, which is sometimes compressed into a mass, commonly known as \"Sheng Dihuang\"."},{"name":"11","text":"Mostly in irregular masses or oblong, swollen in the centre, slightly tapering at both ends. Some small pieces, slat-shaped, slightly compressed or twisted, 4.2-15.0 cm long, 17-65 mm in diameter. Externally brownish-black or greyish-brown, heavily shrunken, with irregular transverse wavy lines. Texture heavy, soft and pliable, broken uneasily. Fracture brownish-black or jet-black, lustrous, viscous. Odourless; taste slightly sweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of elongated tangential cells. In the cortex, parenchyma cells are loosely arranged; many scattered secretory cells are found, containing oily masses of orange to orange-red colour when unstained, but dark in colour when stained. Phloem relatively broad, containing much less number of secretory cells. Cambium in a ring. Xylem rays broad, vessels arranged radially and distantly spaced (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour dark brown. Parenchyma cells suborbicular or irregularly shaped, containing suborbicular nucleiform contents. Secretory cells mostly similar to ordinary parenchyma cells in shape, containing orange to orange-red oil droplets or oily masses. Vessels bordered-pitted and reticulate, up to 97 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bordered pits fairly dense. Cork cells yellowish-brown; in surface view subsquare, in transverse-section view subrectangular; arranged orderly; wall slightly curved (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[28, 0, 1964, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Catalpol standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of catalpol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (26:14, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL beaker, then add 100 mL of methanol. Disperse the sample with a mechanical mixer for 5 min. Shake the mixture for 30 min at room temperature. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of water. Transfer the aqueous solution to a separatory funnel. Extract for three times each with 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Combine the ethyl acetate extracts and evaporate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol and then filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately catalpol standard solution (2.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of catalpol.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[859, 21, 1088, 117])\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1064, 239])\n\npa Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 443, 1740, 2026])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Rehmanniae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Parenchyma cells with secretory cell 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Secretory cell 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem\n\n</div>\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Radix Rehmanniae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[331, 434, 956, 894])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1021, 434, 1646, 892])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[333, 930, 958, 1408])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1021, 930, 1647, 1406])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[332, 1425, 958, 1851])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1022, 1433, 1647, 1848])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Rehmanniae\n\n</div>\n\n1. Parenchyma cells 2. Secretory cells 3. Vessels\n\n4. Cork cells (surface view) 5. Cork cells (transverse section view)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[38, 10, 256, 95])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[857, 23, 1089, 119])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[946, 177, 1068, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[792, 377, 1267, 853])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of catalpol\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Catalpol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of catalpol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (20%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of methanol (20%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 60 min and avoid overheating. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol (20%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.6 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>99</td><td>1</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>99→95.5</td><td>1→4.5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>95.5→90</td><td>4.5→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[22, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of catalpol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of catalpol should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of catalpol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from catalpol peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject catalpol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of catalpol peak in the chromatogram of catalpol Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify catalpol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of catalpol Std-FP. The retention times of catalpol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Rehmanniae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Rehmanniae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.85</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,catalpol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[946, 180, 1062, 240])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[388, 451, 1754, 990])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Rehmanniae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 17.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 65.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 48.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[24, 0, 1957, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Catalpol standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of catalpol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (20%).\n\nCatalpol standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the catalpol Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (20%) to produce a series of solutions of 20, 50, 100, 150, 200 mg/L for catalpol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (20%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with 3 mL of methanol (20%). Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol (20%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-rehmanniae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Rehmanniae","la":"Radix Rehmanniae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-salviae-miltiorrhizae-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Salviae Miltiorrhizae v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-salviae-miltiorrhizae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Salviae Miltiorrhizae","la":"Radix Salviae Miltiorrhizae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-saposhnikoviae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Saposhnikoviae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[584, 779, 1563, 1928])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Saposhnikoviae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1966, 248])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[584, 779, 1563, 1928])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Radix Saposhnikoviae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 0, 1966, 248])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Radix Saposhnikoviae\n\nChinese Name: 防風\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Fangfeng"},{"name":"12","text":"Radix Saposhnikoviae is the dried root of Saposhnikovia divaricata (Turcz.) Schischk. (Apiaceae/ Umbelliferae). The root is collected in the spring and autumn before the growth of flowering stem. After removal of the rootlets and soil, the root is dried under the sun to obtain Radix Saposhnikoviae."},{"name":"12","text":"Long-conical or long-cylindrical, gradually tapering towards the lower part, some slightly tortuous, 5-30 cm long, 4-20 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown or yellowish-brown, rugged, with longitudinal wrinkles, numerous transverse, elongated lenticels, and dotted with raised rootlet scars. Rootstock with obvious dense annulations, some annulations marked by brown, hair-like remains of leaf bases. Texture light, easily broken; fracture uneven, bark brownish and cracked, wood yellowish. Odour characteristic; taste sweetish (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"The cork consisting of several rows of cells. Cortex parenchyma cells arrange in rows tangentially, and show large elliptical vittae. Phloem scattered with numerous subround vittae surrounded by 4-8 secretory cells; phloem rays, mostly curved and becoming cleft in the outer part. Cambium distinct. Xylem vessels fairly abundant, arranged radially. Pith present at the root stock. Stone cells scattered in the parenchyma occasionally (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[14, 0, 1946, 250])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brownish-yellow to pale brown. Vittae 13-74 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, filled with golden yellow or orangish-brown secretion. Reticulate and bordered-pitted vessels 5-98 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Fibres mostly in bundles, elongated. Stone cells infrequent, yellowish-brown or yellowish-green in colour, subrectangular or irregular in shape, with thick wall, showing polychrome when examined under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 15-mL centrifugal tube, then add 2 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Apply 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the supernatant onto a filter paper. Spray the filter paper evenly with aluminum (III) chloride solution (1%, w/v) and heat at about $ 8 0 \\mathrm{~}^\\circ \\mathrm{C} $ for 5 min. A blue fluorescent spot is observed under UV light (365 nm)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prim-O-glucosylcimifugin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of prim- $ O $ -glucosylcimifugin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[12, 0, 1984, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 416, 1739, 2222])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Saposhnikoviae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vitta 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cleft 4. Phloem 5. Vitta 6. Ray 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Vessels\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 2, 1973, 252])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[379, 429, 1622, 2181])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Saposhnikoviae\n\n</div>\n\n1. Vitta 2. Reticulate vessel 3. Bordered-pitted vessel 4. Fibre bundle 5. Cork cells 6. Stone cells a. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[41, 0, 1964, 248])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately prim-O-glucosylcimifugin standard solution, 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O- methylvisamminol standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of prim-O-glucosylcimifugin and 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[625, 1137, 1449, 1444])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[584, 1569, 1482, 1978])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) 4'- $ O $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5- $ O $ -methylvisamminol and (ii) prim- $ O $ -glucosylcimifugi\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[27, 0, 1942, 249])\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>100→40</td><td>0→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>40→0</td><td>60→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O methylvisamminol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O methylvisamminol peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and peak 3 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 0.95 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol peak in the chromatogram of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl -5-O-methylvisamminol Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol Std-FP. The retention times of 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol peaks from the two\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 252])\n\nchromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Radix Saposhnikoviae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Radix Saposhnikoviae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(prim-O-glucosylcimifugin)</td><td>0.85(vs peak2)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,4’-O-β-D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.01(vs peak2)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.22(vs peak2)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.84(vs peak6)</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2.70(vs peak2)</td><td>±0.09</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.17(vs peak6)</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[393, 1419, 1749, 2068])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Saposhnikoviae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 22.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 19.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed prim-O-glucosylcimifugin and 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of prim- $ O $ -glucosylcimifugin CRS and 10.0 mg of 4'- $ O $ - $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5- $ O $ - methylvisamminol CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol.\n\nMixed prim-O-glucosylcimifugin and 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed prim-O-glucosylcimifugin and 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 1, 5, 25, 50, 100 mg/L for both prim-O-glucosylcimifugin and 4'-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucosyl-5-O-methylvisamminol."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-saposhnikoviae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Saposhnikoviae","la":"Radix Saposhnikoviae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-scutellariae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Scutellariae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[435, 828, 1492, 1768])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[435, 828, 1492, 1768])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Radix Scutellariae\n\nChinese Name: 黄芩\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Huangqin"},{"name":"11","text":"Radix Scutellariae is the dried root of Scutellaria baicalensis Georgi (Lamiaceae). The root is collected in spring or autumn, the outer cork and rootlets removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Radix Scutellariae."},{"name":"11","text":"Conical, the broader part at the top, tapering towards the distal end, twisted, 8-25 cm long, 5-25 mm in diameter. Externally brownish-yellow or dark yellow, marked by sparse warty traces of rootlets; the upper portion rough, with twisted longitudinal wrinkles or irregular reticula, the lower distal portion with longitudinal striations and fine wrinkles. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Fracture yellow, reddish-brown in the centre, dark brown to brownish-black in the old root, withered or hollowed. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells, mostly broken. Cortex narrow, scattered with stone cells. Phloem broad, scattered with single or groups of stone cells. Cambium distinct. Xylem vessels occur singly or grouped, surrounded by xylem fibres bundles, with xylem rays relatively broad. Interxylary cork tissue is found in the xylem of roots of plant older than 2-year old, with several layers of well ordered cork cells enclosing the vessels. Parenchyma cells replete with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellow. Phloem fibres fusiform, scattered singly or in bundles, 60-250 $ \\mu m $ long, 9-33 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, thick-walled, with fine pit-canals. Stone cells suborbicular, subsquare or rectangular, relatively thick-walled or heavily thick-walled. Xylem fibres mostly broken,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 0, 1967, 249])\n\nwith sparse oblique pits. Cork cells brownish-yellow, polygonal in surface view. Vessels mainly reticulated, 24-72 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Starch granules abundant, 2-10 $ \\mu $m, simple granules spheroidal, hilum distinct, compound granules composed of 2-3 units (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Baicalein standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of baicalein CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nBaicalin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of baicalin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nWogonin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of wogonin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of toluene, ethyl acetate, methanol and formic acid (10:3:1:2, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1 g of iron(III) chloride hexahydrate and dissolve in 100 mL of absolute ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 30 mL of a mixture of ethyl acetate and methanol (3:1, v/v). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool to room temperature. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately baicalein standard solution, baicalin standard solution, wogonin standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 10 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of baicalein, baicalin and wogonin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[22, 0, 1955, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 422, 1743, 2127])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Radix Scutellariae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Interxylary cork tissue exists in xylem 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem rays 7. Xylem 8. Interxylary cork tissue\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[21, 0, 1973, 256])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[225, 436, 1688, 2264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Radix Scutellariae\n\n</div>\n\n1. Phloem fibres 2. Stone cells 3. Xylem fibres 4. Starch granules 5. Cork cells\n\n6. Reticulate vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 0, 1941, 251])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[735, 452, 1182, 744])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[646, 962, 1291, 1258])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[733, 1514, 1179, 1794])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) baicalein (ii) baicalin and (iii) wogonin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Baicalein standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of baicalein CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 40 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[28, 0, 1960, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (276 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>40</td><td>60</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>25-45</td><td>40→60</td><td>60→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of baicalein Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of baicalein should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of baicalein peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from baicalein peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject baicalein Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of baicalein peak in the chromatogram of baicalein Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify baicalein peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of baicalein Std-FP. The retention times of baicalein peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Scutellariae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[35, 1, 1930, 256])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Radix Scutellariae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(baicalin)</td><td>0.54</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.85</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,baicalein)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6(wogonin)</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 1044, 1748, 1519])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Radix Scutellariae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[26, 4, 1955, 245])"},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 30.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 46.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/radix-scutellariae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Radix Scutellariae","la":"Radix Scutellariae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ranunculi-ternati-radix-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ranunculi Ternati Radix v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[359, 827, 1587, 1884])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ranunculi Ternati Radix\n\nA. Ranunculi Ternati Radix\n\nB. A single root tuber (a piece of claw)\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface of Ranunculi Ternati Radix\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[359, 827, 1587, 1884])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ranunculi Ternati Radix\n\nA. Ranunculi Ternati Radix\n\nB. A single root tuber (a piece of claw)\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface of Ranunculi Ternati Radix\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 257])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Ranunculi Ternati Radix\n\nChinese name: 貓爪草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Maozhaocao"},{"name":"14","text":"Ranunculi Ternati Radix is the dried root tuber of Ranunculus ternatus Thunb. (Ranunculaceae). The root tuber is collected in spring and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Ranunculi Ternati Radix."},{"name":"14","text":"Single root tubers fusiform, 2.7-10.4 mm long, 1.3-5.9 mm in diameter, several to dozens of fusiform root tubers fascicle like a cat's claw, apex with yellowish-brown remains of stem or stem scars. Externally brown to greyish-brown, gradually darken on long term storage, with slight longitudinal wrinkles and some dotted rootlets scars or residual rootlets. Texture compact, cut surface whitish to yellowish-white, mostly solid, rarely hollow at the centre, powderly. Odour slight; taste slightly sweet (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Epidermal hairs slightly curved, slender, unicellular, sometimes broken, walls relatively thick, lumen clear. Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, tangentially elongated, yellowish-brown, some differentiated into epidermal hairs, slightly lignified. Cortex consists of 20-30 layers of parenchymatous cells, subpolygonal or irregular in shape, walls slightly thickened. Endodermis indistinct, containing 2-3 strands each of xylem and phloem. Xylem and phloem arranged alternatively. Starch granules numerously found in parenchymatous cells of cortex (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 35, 1954, 245])"},{"name":"14","text":"Colour brown. Epidermal cells brownish-yellow to brown; subsquare, subpolygonal or irregular in shape on the surface view; walls relatively thick, cells contain subrounded or elliptical nucleus-like masses. Epidermal hairs slender, slightly curved, unicellular, 8-32 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; walls relatively thick and lumen clear. Starch granules numerous; simple starch granules subrounded, subtriangular or irregular in shape, 4-20 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; hilum unclear; black and cruciate shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-3 units. Vessels mainly scalariform and spiral vessels, 7-37 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Parenchymatous cells subpolygonal or irregular in shape, slightly thickened, containing numerous starch granules (Fig. 3).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[366, 811, 956, 1446])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1025, 813, 1702, 1241])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[301, 1524, 636, 1700])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[658, 1525, 992, 1698])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1026, 1271, 1704, 1703])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Ranunculi Ternati Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Starch granules in parenchymatous cells (under the light microscope) D. Starch granules in parenchymatous cells (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermal hair 2. Epidermis 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Starch granules\n\n西河柳\n\n野老鸛草\n\nPolygonati Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[750, 145, 906, 187])\n\n黄精\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1691, 55, 1938, 138])\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[414, 433, 977, 840])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[997, 433, 1571, 836])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[414, 864, 979, 1204])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[994, 863, 1572, 1206])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[414, 1227, 980, 1561])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[997, 1230, 1568, 1555])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[414, 1582, 980, 2028])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[998, 1582, 1567, 2031])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Epidermal cells 2. Epidermal hair (attached to epidermal cells)\n\n3. Starch granules 4. Vessels (4-1 scalariform vessels, 4-2 spiral vessel)\n\n5. Parenchymatous cells containing starch granules ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 45, 1966, 256])"},{"name":"14","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of linoleic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nPalmitic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of palmitic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate, acetonitrile and glacial acetic acid (6:2.5:0.25:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of 2',7'-dichlorofluorescein and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of n-hexane. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC plate of silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ with 15% silver nitrate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately linoleic acid standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), palmitic acid standard solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 423, 900, 833])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 951, 907, 1362])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 1488, 1110, 1617])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 1726, 1115, 1856])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) linoleic acid (ii) methyl linoleate (iii) methyl palmitate and (iv) palmitic acid\n\n</div>\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n京大戟 Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n苦木 Picrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 377, 904, 1204])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ranunculi Ternati Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Linoleic acid standard solution 2. Palmitic acid standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of linoleic acid and palmitic acid (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Methyl linoleate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of methyl linoleate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of n-hexane.\n\nMethyl palmitate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of methyl palmitate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of n-hexane.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[19, 37, 1948, 248])"},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 100 mL of methanol and 1 mL of hydrochloric acid. Reflux the mixture for 1.5 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue with methanol. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of n-hexane. Transfer the extract to a 250-mL separating funnel. Extract with 10 mL of water. Collect the n-hexane extract. Extract the aqueous layer for four times each with 20 mL of n-hexane. Combine the n-hexane extracts and add 1.0 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue with n-hexane. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the combined extracts to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in n-hexane. Transfer the extract to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with n-hexane. Pipette 1 mL of the extract to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with n-hexane. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (DB-WAX, $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{~ m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{~ m} $ ) of which the internal wall is covered with polyethylene glycol in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. Nitrogen is used as carrier gas at 1.0 mL/min. The injection temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 2 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The split injection mode at a ratio of 5:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>110→120</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>120→160</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>20-60</td><td>160→180</td><td>0.5</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of methyl linoleate Std-FP and methyl palmitate Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of methyl linoleate and methyl palmitate should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of methyl linoleate and methyl palmitate peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from methyl linoleate and methyl palmitate peaks should not be less than 100000 and 150000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject methyl linoleate Std-FP, methyl palmitate Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of methyl linoleate and methyl palmitate peaks in the chromatograms of methyl linoleate Std-FP, methyl palmitate Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify methyl linoleate and methyl palmitate peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of methyl linoleate Std-FP and methyl palmitate Std-FP. The retention times of methyl linoleate and methyl palmitate peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Ranunculi Ternati Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Ranunculi Ternati Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.38</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(methyl palmitate)</td><td>0.64</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(methyl stearate)</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(methyl oleate)</td><td>0.93</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,methyl linoleate)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6(methyl linolenate)</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[275, 1831, 1752, 2432])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Ranunculi Ternati Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[21, 40, 1944, 252])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 38.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 34.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[21, 43, 1965, 258])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ranunculi-ternati-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ranunculi Ternati Radix","la":"Ranunculi Ternati Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/realgar-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Realgar v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[21, 5, 1927, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"9","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[21, 5, 1927, 255])"},{"name":"9","text":"Official Name: Realgar\n\nChinese Name: 雄黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xionghuang"},{"name":"9","text":"Realgar is a mineral of the sulphides of realgar group, containing mainly arsenic(II) sulphide $ \\mathrm{(A s_{2} S_{2})} $ The ore is collected, foreign matter removed to obtain Realgar."},{"name":"9","text":"Aggregates of lumpy or granular masses, irregular. Orange-red to dark red, streak pale orange-red, crystal surface showing diamond-like lustre. Texture fragile, easily broken, fracture with resin-like lustre. Powder refined ore: In powders or powder aggregates, orange-yellow to orange-red. Texture lax, powdered on kneading, lustreless. Odour slightly stinking (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"9","text":"Powder\n\nColour yellow. Irregular flakes or aggregates, golden-yellow to orange-yellow, lustrous, slightly dull in colour at the margin; minute granules dull in colour, translucent or non-transparent. Polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"9","text":"(I) Chemical test of arsenic salt"},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 8 mL of sulphuric acid. Heat the mixture on a hot plate and boil for about 15 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer 1 mL of the mixture to a 250-mL conical flask. Add 20 mL of water and 2 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (40%, w/v). Add 2.0 g of sodium hydrogen carbonate and\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 6, 1984, 265])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[235, 405, 1713, 1579])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of powder of Realgar\n\n</div>\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 4, 1927, 257])\n\nmix well. Transfer 1 mL of upper layer to a test tube. Add 1 drop of silver nitrate solution (1.7% , w/v) and mix well. Yellow precipitate can be observed."},{"name":"9","text":"Potassium chlorate-nitric acid solution\n\nDissolve excess amount of potassium chlorate in nitric acid."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.01 g of the powdered sample and place it in a test tube, then add 1 drop of water. Dissolve the sample in 2 mL of potassium chlorate-nitric acid solution. Add 3 drops of barium chloride solution ( 5% , w/v ). Allow the white precipitate to settle. Discard the supernatant. Add 2 mL of water to the precipitate and mix well. White precipitate cannot be dissolved."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix XVI."},{"name":"9","text":"Finely powdered $ \\alpha $ - Realgar (0.5 g) and $ \\beta $ - Realgar (0.5 g)."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of finely powdered sample onto a glass slide or other appropriate holder. Press and smear uniformly the sample until a flat and dense solid surface is obtained."},{"name":"9","text":"Check the accuracy of the zero shift error (in 20) of the X-ray diffractometer by using certified materials (lanthanum hexaboride $ \\mathrm{L a B}_{6} $ or other equivalent) at the beginning of analysis. Compare the 20 values of characteristic diffraction peaks of such certified material with the X-ray Powder Diffraction (XRPD) pattern found in scientific standard database. The instrument is in good condition to use when each of these diffraction peaks of the certified material has a $ 2\\theta $ discrepancy less than $ \\pm 0. 0 5^{\\circ} $ when compared to the corresponding 20 values of the XRPD pattern found in scientific standard database."},{"name":"9","text":"Separately place the glass slide containing the finely powdered standard materials and test sample onto the diffractometer platform and record the XRPD pattern. Measure the 20\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 4, 1977, 265])\n\nvalues of the diffraction peaks of the standard materials and test sample. Compare the 20 values of the i) characteristic diffraction peaks of the $ \\alpha $ - Realgar standard material and test sample or ii) characteristic diffraction peaks of the $ \\beta $ - Realgar standard material and test sample as listed in Table 1 (i) and (ii) respectively.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 (i) The 20 values of the five characteristic diffaction peaks of $ \\alpha $ - Realgar\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>14.707</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>15.451</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>16.441</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>29.297</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>30.544</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[378, 1137, 1629, 1706])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) A reference XRPD pattern of $ \\alpha $ - Realgar\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 (ii) The 2 $ \\theta $ values of the five characteristic diffraction peaks of $ \\beta $ - Realgar\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>20/°</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>15.415</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>17.803</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>22.660</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>29.820</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>31.140</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 2, 1929, 256])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[379, 367, 1604, 888])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) A reference XRPD pattern of $ \\beta $ -Realgar\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic diffraction peaks [Fig. 3(i) or (ii)] each has an angular discrepancy $ (\\Delta2\\theta) $ less than $ \\pm 0.2^{\\circ} $ , compared with the values stated in Table 1 (i) or (ii).\n\n</div>"},{"name":"9","text":"Lead (II) acetate absorbent\n\nWeigh 5.0 g of lead (II) acetate and place it in a 100-mL volumetric flask, then dissolve in water. Add acetic acid until the solution becomes clear and make up to the mark with water. Soak thoroughly 1.0 g of cotton wool in 12 mL of lead (II) acetate solution. Extrude excess solution and dry at a temperature below $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ Mercuric bromide test paper\n\nWeigh 2.5 g of mercuric bromide and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol. Soak filter paper in mercuric bromide solution for 1 h. Dry the paper and store in the dark.\n\nAcidic tin (II) chloride solution\n\nWeigh 20.0 g of tin (II) chloride dihydrate and dissolve in 50 mL of hydrochloric acid."},{"name":"9","text":"A is a 100 mL conical flask with standard ground joint; B is a standard hollow ground glass stopper connected to glass conduit C (external diameter 8.0 mm, internal diameter 6.0 mm), the total length of B and C is about 180 mm, D is a plastic screw, the upper part of which has an aperture 6.0 mm in diameter and the lower part of which has an aperture 8.0 mm in diameter; E is a plastic screw cap which has an aperture 6.0 mm in diameter. A wad of lead acetate cotton wool weighing about 60 mg is packed into tube C to a depth of about 60-80 mm. A disc of mercuric bromide test paper is placed between the contacting surface of D and E.\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1893, 41, 1984, 87])\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba 半枝蓮\n\n五味子\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\nSchisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[804, 358, 1238, 1345])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Apparatus for the test of arsenic (III) oxide in CMM samples\n\n</div>"},{"name":"9","text":"Arsenic (III) oxide standard stock solution, Std-Stock\n\nWeigh 0.132 g of arsenic (III) oxide and place it in a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then add 5 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (20%, w/v). Neutralize the solution with sulphuric acid (10%, w/v) and further add 10 mL of sulphuric acid (10%, w/v). Make up to the mark with water.\n\nArsenic (III) oxide standard solution\n\nPipette 10 mL of the Std-Stock to a 1000-mL volumetric flask, then add 10 mL of sulphuric acid (10%, w/v). Make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the solution."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of hydrochloric acid (28%, w/v) and stir for 30 min. Filter the mixture. Wash the residue for two times, each with 10 mL of hydrochloric acid (28%, w/v) by stirring for 10 min. Combine the extracts to a 500-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Transfer 10 mL of solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"9","text":"(a) Transfer 2 mL of test solution to the flask (Fig. 4), add 5 mL of hydrochloric acid, 21 mL of water, 5 mL of potassium iodide solution (16.5% , w/v) and 5 drops of acidic tin (II) chloride solution.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 4, 1926, 257])\n\nAllow the mixture to stand for about 10 min.Add 2.0 g of zinc granule and heat the mixture on a water bath at about $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 45 min. Collect and examine the mercuric bromide test paper.\n\n(b) Transfer 2 mL of standard solution to the flask (Fig. 4), carry out the procedure as stated in (a). Collect and examine the mercuric bromide test paper. Less intense orange spot should be observed in (a) when compared to (b)."},{"name":"9","text":"The reaction product (arsine) is flammable, pyrophoric and poisonous."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/realgar","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Realgar","la":"Realgar"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhapontici-radix-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhapontici Radix v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[409, 883, 1504, 1666])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhapontici Radix A. Rhapontici Radix B. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[409, 883, 1504, 1666])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhapontici Radix A. Rhapontici Radix B. Magnified image of transverse section of root"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Rhapontici Radix\n\nChinese Name: 漏蘆\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Loulu"},{"name":"12","text":"Rhapontici Radix is the dried root of Rhaponticum uniflorum (L.) DC. (Asteraceae). The root is collected in spring and autumn, rootlets and soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Rhapontici Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Conical or flatted lumps, mostly twisted, varying in length, 10-25 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown to dark brown, rough, with longitudinal furrows and rhombic, netted clefts. Outer bark easily exfoliated. Root stock swollen, with the remains of stems and scaly leaf bases, top with greyish-white hairs. Light in weight, texture fragile, easily broken, fracture irregular, greyish-white to greyish-yellow, with greyish-black to brownish-black cleft in the centre. Odour characteristic; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Epidermis mostly fallen off. Metaderm consists of several to more than 20 layers of brown cell, cell walls slightly thickened, lignified and suberized. Secretory canals mainly scattered in parenchyma, varying in size, containing reddish-brown secretion, elliptical and larger secretory canals occasionally located near metaderm and arranged in irregularly interrupted ring. Phloem relatively broad, phloem rays wide. Cambium in a ring. Xylem vessels abundant, the large vessel groups often alternated with the small vessel groups. Xylem rays often scattered with radial clefts (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 17, 1940, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brown. Non-glandular hairs 10-31 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, lignified. Metaderm cells subsquare or rectangular, walls slightly thickened, reddish-brown, lignified and suberized. Secretory canals 20-85 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, containing reddish-brown secretions. Reticulate and bordered-pitted vessels abundant, 8-93 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 402, 1742, 2067])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhapontici Radix\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory canal\n\n1. Metaderm 2. Secretory canal 3. Phloem 4. Phloem ray 5. Cambium\n\n6. Xylem ray 7. Cleft 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[31, 38, 1946, 262])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[259, 435, 1755, 2108])"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Non-glandular hair (1-1 upper part, 1-2 middle part, 1-3 lower part)\n\n2. Metaderm cells 3. Secretory canal\n\n4. Vessels (4-1 bordered-pitted vessel, 4-2 reticulate vessel)"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\beta $ -Ecdysterone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol (95%) and water (8:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol (30%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (70%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[370, 1795, 996, 2248])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone\n\n</div>\n\nLonicerae Flos山銀花\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 376, 860, 1024])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Rhapontici Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. $ \\beta $ -Ecdysterone standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\beta $ -Ecdysterone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (30%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (30%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (30%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (247 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>90→80</td><td>10→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>80→60</td><td>20→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone peak should not be less than 70000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone peak in the chromatogram of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone Std-FP. The retention times of $ \\beta $ -ecdysterone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhapontici Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhapontici Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.98</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,β-ecdysterone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.27</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.48</td><td>±0.06</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[332, 365, 1794, 1160])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhapontici Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 18.5%\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 14.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[63, 34, 1894, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhapontici-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhapontici Radix","la":"Rhapontici Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-alismatis-v1-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Alismatis v1 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"},"sections":[{"name":"error","text":"OCR failed: GLM-OCR HTTP 429: {\"error\":{\"code\":\"1302\",\"message\":\"Rate limit reached for requests\"}}"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-alismatis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Alismatis","la":"Rhizoma Alismatis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-anemarrhenae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Anemarrhenae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[418, 848, 1535, 1741])\n\n1cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Anemarrhenae (A: Maozhimu B: Guangzhimu)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[44, 1, 1946, 248])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[418, 848, 1535, 1741])\n\n1cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Anemarrhenae (A: Maozhimu B: Guangzhimu)\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[44, 1, 1946, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Anemarrhenae\n\nChinese Name: 知母\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zhimu"},{"name":"11","text":"Rhizoma Anemarrhena is the dried rhizome of Anemarrhena asphodeloides Bge. (Liliaceae). The rhizome is collected in spring or autumn, remnants of the aerial stem and fibrous roots removed, then dried under the sun (to produce 'Maozhimu') or alternatively, the cork is peeled off when the rhizome is still fresh, then dried under the sun (to produce 'Guangzhimu') to obtain Rhizoma Anemarrhenae."},{"name":"11","text":"Maozhimu: Cork intact. Slat-shaped, slightly curved, somewhat compressed, occasionally branched, 3-15 cm long, 8-20 mm in diameter, with pale yellowish stem and leaf scars. Externally yellowishbrown to brown, the upper part exhibiting a concave groove and closely arranged annular nodes with dense yellowish-brown remains of leaf bases arising upward laterally; the lower part is raised and somewhat shrivelled, exhibiting depressions or protruding dotted root scars. Texture hard, easily broken, fracture yellowish-white. Odour slight; taste slightly sweetish, bitterish and viscous on chewing (Fig. 1A).\n\nGuangzhimu: Outer cork removed, 5-15 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white, with twisted grooves, sometimes with leaf scars and root scars (Fig. 1B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Phelloderm or cork mostly remains in Maozhimu, absent (due to removal) in Guangzhimu consists of several layers of polygonal cork cells and over 10 layers of sub-rectangular cork cells, slightly lignified. Leaf-trace vascular bundles scattered in suberized and non-suberized cortex occasionally. Mucilage cells numerous, containing raphides of calcium oxalate. Endodermis invisible. Stele broad, root-trace vascular bundles tangentially arranged along the pericycle; collateral or amphivasal vascular bundles small and scattered. Some parenchyma cells are mucilaginous and contain raphides of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale yellow. Raphides of calcium oxalate easily observed, in bundles or scattered, 20-110 $ \\mu m $ long. Cork cells yellowish-brown, varying in shape in surface view, anticlinal walls thin, several cells appearing overlapped when viewed from the top view. Vessels bordered-pitted or spiral, 6-25 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Fibres very slender, 6-15 $ \\mu m $ in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Sarsasapogenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of sarsasapogenin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of dichloromethane.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of ethyl acetate and cyclohexane (1:4, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Mix cautiously 25 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v) into 25 mL of ice-cold glacial acetic acid. Add 2.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde. Add further 50 mL of sulphuric acid (20%, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of absolute ethanol. Shake the mixture for about 15 h. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 40 min. Filter and transfer 10 mL of filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Add 11 mL of 1.1 M hydrochloric acid. Reflux the solution for 2 h. Cool in an ice bath. Add dropwise about 1 mL of sodium hydroxide solution (40%, w/v) with shaking. Transfer the solution to a separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 50 mL of dichloromethane. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 4 mL of dichloromethane."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately sarsasapogenin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[861, 21, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii Rhizoma Anemarrhenae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 177, 1063, 239])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 450, 1704, 2193])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhizoma Anemarrhenae\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundles D. Root-trace vascular bundle\n\n1. Suberized cortex 2. Cortex 3. Mucilage cells 4. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Root-trace vascular bundles 6. Vascular bundles 7. Phloem 8. Xylem\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n銀杏葉\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[60, 171, 262, 237])\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1653, 6, 1784, 59])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1044, 25, 1175, 89])\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[956, 151, 1046, 202])\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n王不留行\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1614, 150, 1693, 199])\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Rhizoma Anemarrhenae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[244, 415, 1705, 1729])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Rhizoma Anemarrhenae\n\n1. Mucilage cells 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate (broken bundle)\n\n3. Raphides of calcium oxalate (in bundles) 4. Bordered-pitted vessels\n\n5. Spiral vessels 6. Cork cells 7. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[37, 3, 1943, 255])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of sarsasapogenin.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[711, 572, 1341, 940])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[702, 1094, 1368, 1455])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) sarsasapogenin and (ii) mangiferin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Mangiferin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (30 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of mangiferin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (257 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.5 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[38, 0, 1961, 251])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.03% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>100→90</td><td>0→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-35</td><td>90→50</td><td>10→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-40</td><td>50→20</td><td>50→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>20</td><td>80</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of mangiferin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of mangiferin should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of mangiferin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from mangiferin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject mangiferin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of mangiferin peak in the chromatogram of mangiferin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify mangiferin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of mangiferin Std-FP. The retention times of mangiferin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Anemarrhenae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Anemarrhenae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,mangiferin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[857, 24, 1090, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[946, 180, 1062, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[395, 521, 1747, 1187])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhizoma Anemarrhenae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 51.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 49.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[23, 0, 1964, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Sarsasapogenin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of sarsasapogenin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nSarsasapogenin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the sarsasapogenin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 30, 50, 100, 200, 300 mg/L for sarsasapogenin."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh accurately 0.25 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of ethanol (95%). Shake the mixture for 15 h. Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 40 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 3 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 250-mL conical flask. Repeat the sonication extraction for two more times each with 40 mL of ethanol (95%). Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Add 20 mL of water and then 2 mL of hydrochloric acid to the residue. Reflux the mixture for 2 h. Cool to room temperature. Adjust the pH to neutral by adding dropwise of sodium hydroxide solution (10%, w/v) (about 8 mL) with shaking in an ice bath. Transfer the solution to a separating funnel. Extract for three times each with 50 mL of dichloromethane. Combine the extracts. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-anemarrhenae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Anemarrhenae","la":"Rhizoma Anemarrhenae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-atractylodis-macrocephalae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[48, 1, 1952, 250])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"Figure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[48, 1, 1952, 250])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nChinese Name: 白术\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Baizhu"},{"name":"10","text":"Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephala is the dried rhizome of Atractylodes macrocephala Koidz. (Asteraceae). The rhizome is collected in winter, when the leaves on the lower part of the plant turn yellow and those on the upper part become fragile, soil removed and baked over low heat or dried under the sun, the fibrous roots are removed to obtain Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae."},{"name":"10","text":"Stout masses of irregular shape and size, 3-12 cm in length, 10-70 mm in diameter. Externally greyish yellow to greyish-brown, with warty protuberances, discontinuous longitudinal wrinkles and grooves, and scars of rootlets; the top end is marked by stem remnants and bud scars. Texture hard, uneasily broken; fracture uneven, scattered with brownish-yellow, dotted oil cavities. Odour fresh and fragrant; taste sweetish and slightly pungent, viscous on chewing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Cork consists of several rows of polygonal cells; stone cells present in groups or separately in the inner part of the cork, arranged in a discontinuous ring. Oil cavities, round to elongatedrounded in shape, containing yellow oil, numerous and scattered in the cortex, phloem and xylem. Cambium ring distinct. Vessels arranged radially, lignified, those in the internal part of the xylem surrounded by fibre bundles (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Raphides of calcium oxalate 10-36 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in length, irregularly scattered or found in bundles in the parenchyma cells. Inulins numerous, scattered inside or outside parenchyma cells, with radial striations. Fibre cells yellow, mostly in bundles long-fusiform, 10-62 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, the walls fairly thickened, lignified, showing distinct\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[24, 0, 1960, 248])\n\npit canals. Stone cells subglobose to polygonal or subcubical, 21-100 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, with distinct pit canals. Vessels, reticulated and bordered-pitted, about 10-66 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Cork cells, polygonal, sometimes with yellowish contents (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"Atractylenolide III standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of atractylenolide III CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and isopropanol (8:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately attractylenolide III standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 10 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of atractylenolide III.\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[860, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1871, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 240])\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nAndrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\n益母草 Herba Leonuri 平贝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis 吴茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 471, 1743, 2155])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells D. Xylem 1. Cork 2. Groups of stone cells 3. Cortex 4. Oil cavity 5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem\n\n</div>\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\n銀杏葉\n\n燈心草\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n北沙参\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 89])\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\n夏枯草\n\nSpica Prunellae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1362, 64, 1446, 104])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n地黄\n\n王不留行 Rhizoma\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[317, 435, 757, 767])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[777, 434, 1219, 762])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1237, 439, 1664, 753])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[319, 820, 756, 1141])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[779, 820, 1218, 1141])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1238, 821, 1665, 1141])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[317, 1195, 868, 1546])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[895, 1200, 1267, 1538])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1290, 1201, 1665, 1542])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[318, 1590, 889, 1897])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1163, 1588, 1665, 1900])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"10","text":"1. Irregularly scattered raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate in bundles\n\n3. Inulin 4. Fibres 5. Stone cells 6. Bordered-pitted vessel 7. Reticulate vessel 8. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 11, 254, 94])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 23, 1087, 118])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[948, 177, 1064, 240])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1727, 176, 1858, 228])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[821, 381, 1182, 598])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of atractylenolide III\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Atractylenolide III standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of atractylenolide III CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>80→40</td><td>20→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-25</td><td>40→0</td><td>60→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-40</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of atractylenolide III Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of atractylenolide III should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of atractylenolide III peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from atractylenolide III peak should not be less than 80000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 0, 1966, 248])"},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject atractylenolide III Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of atractylenolide III peak in the chromatogram of atractylenolide III Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify atractylenolide III peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of atractylenolide III Std-FP. The retention times of atractylenolide III peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,atractylenolide III)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(atractylone)</td><td>1.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[388, 1594, 1752, 2379])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[42, 0, 1948, 252])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 60.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-atractylodis-macrocephalae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae","la":"Rhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-belamcandae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Belamcandae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[440, 868, 1533, 1704])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 0, 1943, 250])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[440, 868, 1533, 1704])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 0, 1943, 250])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nChinese Name: 射干\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shegan"},{"name":"11","text":"Rhizoma Belamcandae is the dried rhizome of Belamcanda chinensis (L.) DC. (Iridaceae). The rhizome is collected in early spring when the plant is budding, or in late autumn when the aerial part is withering. Fibrous roots and soil removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Rhizoma Belamcandae."},{"name":"11","text":"Irregularly nodulated, 2-7 cm long, 10-20 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown, dark brown to blackish-brown, shrunken, with profuse annular striations. Several dish-shaped and sunken stem scars are found on the upper part, occasionally with remains of stem bases, while remains of thin roots and root scars are found on the lower part. Texture hard; fracture yellow, granular. Odour slight; taste bitter and slightly pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork consists of several layers of irregularly shaped cells. Cortex marked by sparsely scattered leaf-trace vascular bundles; endodermis indistinct. Vascular bundles in the stele are of amphivasal and collateral types, densely arranged near the outer edge. Parenchyma cells contain columnar crystals of calcium oxalate, as well as starch granules and oil drops (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Columnar crystals of calcium oxalate abundant, mostly broken; intact crystals 49-388 $ \\mu $m long. Starch granules simple, globose to ellipsoid, 2-14 $ \\mu $m in diameter, the hilum dotted; compound granules rare. Reticulate, spiral and bordered-pitted vessels frequent, 9-39 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fibres mostly in bundles, slender, the end truncated.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 0, 1966, 250])\n\nParenchyma cells subglobose to ellipsoid, their wall slightly thickened or beaded, with simple pits. Cork cells brown, polygonal in surface view, the wall slightly sinuous, some cells contain brown masses (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Irisflorentin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of irisflorentin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Collect the supernatant."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately irisflorentin standard solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of irisflorentin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[32, 0, 1945, 255])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[240, 423, 1746, 2021])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Columnar crystals of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Starch granules 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Collateral vascular bundles\n\n9. Amphivasal vascular bundles 10. Leaf-trace vascular bundles\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1175, 90])\n\n決明子\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[956, 151, 1047, 202])\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n射干 Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\nRadix Rehmanniae Rhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[262, 433, 1679, 2197])"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Columnar crystals of calcium oxalate 2. Starch granules 3. Reticulate vessels 4. Fibres\n\n5. Parenchyma cells 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[35, 0, 1944, 248])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[794, 463, 1351, 765])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[688, 905, 1417, 1326])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) irisflorentin (ii) tectoridin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Tectoridin standard solution for fingerprinting Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of tectoridin CRS (Fig.4) and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nIrisflorentin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of irisflorentin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatant. Make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (266 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[26, 0, 1966, 250])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>82→80</td><td>18→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-25</td><td>80→67</td><td>20→33</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-45</td><td>67→60</td><td>33→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>60→47</td><td>40→53</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>60-65</td><td>47</td><td>53</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of tectoridin Std-FP and irisflorentin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of tectoridin and irisflorentin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of tectoridin and irisflorentin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from tectoridin and irisflorentin peaks should not be less than 3500 and 20000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject tectoridin Std-FP, irisflorentin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of tectoridin and irisflorentin peaks in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify tectoridin and irisflorentin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing their retention times with those in the chromatograms of the corresponding Std-FP. The retention times of tectoridin and irisflorentin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Belamcandae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[32, 1, 1937, 255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Belamcandae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker 1, tectoridin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.44 (vs peak 4)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.49 (vs peak 4)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4 (marker 2, irisflorentin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 952, 1740, 1404])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhizoma Belamcandae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 7.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 10.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[23, 0, 1961, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 21.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 22.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Mixed tectoridin and irisflorentin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (20 mg/L for tectoridin and 10 mg/L for irisflorentin)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of tectoridin CRS and 2.5 mg of irisflorentin CRS and dissolve in 250 mL of ethanol (70%)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-belamcandae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Belamcandae","la":"Rhizoma Belamcandae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-chuanxiong-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Chuanxiong v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[670, 806, 1310, 1900])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Chuanxiong\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1967, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[670, 806, 1310, 1900])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Chuanxiong\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 1, 1967, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Chuanxiong\n\nChinese Name: 川芎\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Chuanxiong"},{"name":"11","text":"Rhizoma Chuanxiong is the dried rhizome of Ligusticum chuanxiong Hort. (Apiaceae/Umbelliferae). The rhizome of the cultivated plant is harvested in the summer of the second year of cultivation, when bulging nodes on the stem become prominent and slight purplish in colour. Dig up the whole plant, get the rhizome and removed the soil, then dried in a shaded area or by baking; once dried, the rootlets are removed to obtain Rhizoma Chuanxiong."},{"name":"11","text":"In irregular knotty and fist-like masses, 2-7 cm in diameter. Externally dark brown, greyish-brown or yellowish-brown, rough and shrunken, with numerous parallel and raised annulations, showing dent and subround stem scars on the summit, and numerous warty rootlet scars beneath and at the annulations. Texture compact, not easily broken. When broken, the surface is yellowish-white or greyish-yellow, scattered with yellowish-brown oil cavities, the cambium forming an undulate ring. Odour, strongly fragrant; taste, bitter, acrid, slightly sweet afterwards, giving a slight numbing sensation (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"The cork consists of over 10 rows of flat cells, the cortex narrow, scattered with root-trace vascular bundles. Phloem broad. Cambium undulate or irregularly polygonal. Xylem vessels mostly uniseriate or arranged in a V shape; xylem fibres in bundles are occasionally found. Pith broad. Oil cavities scattered in parenchyma, subround, ellipsoid or irregular, pale yellowish-brown, smaller\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 0, 1955, 257])\n\nnear the cambium, gradually becoming larger outwards. The parenchyma cells contain abundant starch grains (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown or greyish-brown. Starch grains abundant; simple grains ellipsoid, elongated-rounded, oval or kidney-shaped, 3-19 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum pointed, long cleft or V-shaped; compound grains few, consisting of 2-4 units, black cruciate-shaped as seen under the polarized microscope. Oil cavities mostly broken, secretory cells containing numerous droplets of oil. Cork cells closely arrayed, sub-polygonal in surface view, thin-walled, sinuately crooked, well-arranged in lateral view. Spiral vessels frequent, 14-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, the thickened wall of some spiral vessels interconnected to form reticulated vessels. Clusters of calcium oxalate, in subround masses, rarely observed, intensely white in colour under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 60 min. Allow the solid residue to settle. Spot the supernatant onto a filter paper with a capillary tube. Examine the spot under UV light (254 nm), a fluorescent blue spot is observed."},{"name":"11","text":"Z-Ligustilide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of Z-ligustilide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nE-Ferulic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of E-ferulic acid CRS (Fig.4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and diethyl ether (2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 0, 1984, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 423, 1749, 2230])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhizoma Chuanxiong\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil cavity D. Starch grains\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Oil cavities 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Pith 8. Xylem fibres\n\n9. Root-trace vascular bundles\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[10, 6, 1973, 259])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 441, 1745, 2232])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Rhizoma Chuanxiong\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch grains 2. Fragments of oil cavities 3. Cork cells in surface view 4. Cork cells in lateral view\n\n5. Spiral vessels 6. Reticulated vessel 7. Cluster crystals of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 258])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to another tube. Evaporate the solvent to dryness with a gentle stream of nitrogen. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately Z-ligustilide standard solution, E-ferulic acid standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent, then heat at about $ 70^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of Z-ligustilide and E-ferulic acid.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(1) \n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[834, 1535, 1242, 1883])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[845, 2012, 1224, 2293])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) E-ferulic acid and (ii) Z-ligustilide\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Z-ligustilide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of Z-ligustilide CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol. Store at about $ - 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ in the dark."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.25 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 90 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (293 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>85→80</td><td>15→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-40</td><td>80→47</td><td>20→53</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>47→0</td><td>53→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of Z-ligustilide Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of Z-ligustilide should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of Z-ligustilide peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from Z-ligustilide peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 7 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject Z-ligustilide Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of Z-ligustilide peak in the chromatogram\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1967, 258])\n\nof the Z-ligustilide Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify Z-ligustilide peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of Z-ligustilide Std-FP. The retention times of Z-ligustilide peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Chuanxiong extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Chuanxiong extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.23</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(E-ferulic acid)</td><td>0.36</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.46</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.91</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7(marker,Z-ligustilide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[402, 1629, 1753, 2211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhizoma Chuanxiong extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 27.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 23.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-chuanxiong","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Chuanxiong","la":"Rhizoma Chuanxiong"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-cimicifugae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Cimicifugae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[427, 819, 1587, 1782])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Cimicifugae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[38, 0, 1967, 252])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[427, 819, 1587, 1782])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Cimicifugae\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[38, 0, 1967, 252])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Cimicifugae\n\nChinese Name: 升麻\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Shengma"},{"name":"11","text":"Rhizoma Cimicifugae is the dried rhizome of Cimicifuga heracleifolia Kom. (Ranunculaceae). The rhizome is collected in the autumn, after removal of soil and fibrous root, it is dried under the sun to obtain Rhizoma Cimicifugae."},{"name":"11","text":"Irregular long pieces, frequently branched, nodular, 6-30 cm long, 12-40 mm in diameter. Externally blackish-brown or brown, rough, showing remains of many wiry fibrous roots; the upper part shows several round and hollow remains of stems, the inner wall with reticulate furrows, the lower part lumpy with fibrous root scars. Texture light and hard, not easily broken; fracture uneven, cracked, fibrous, yellowish-green or pale yellowish-white. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Part of outer metaderm cells walls thickened by suberization. Sometimes the outer and anticlinal wall of some metaderm cells nipple-shaped thickened, and stretched into the cell cavities. Cortex mainly consists of parenchyma cells. Phloem fibre bundles crescent-shaped, 5-110 fibres in one bundle. Vascular bundles up to 60, arranged in a ring. Pith broad (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-brown or brown. Metaderm cells yellowish-brown, rectangular or subpolygonal,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[25, 0, 1955, 254])\n\nwall unevenly thickened; some cells have nipple-shaped, thickened wall stretched into the cell cavities. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted and reticulate. Most of the xylem fibres are fibre-tracheids, either singles or in bundles, slightly shuttle-shaped, the end elongated, sharp or blunt, some branched, with oblique, crisscross or V-shaped pits. Phloem fibres mostly in bundles, narrow and elongated in shape, the end blunt or slightly sharp, lignified, and with distinct pit-canals. Xylem parenchyma cells single or in groups, rectangular or subround in shape, with round or shortly slit-shaped pits (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of dichloromethane. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer 0.5 mL of the supernatant to a test tube. Cautiously add about 0.5 mL of sulphuric acid along the inner wall of the test tube. Allow to stand for 20 min. A reddish-brown or yellowishbrown ring is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers."},{"name":"11","text":"Isoferulic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isoferulic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of toluene, dichloromethane and glacial acetic acid (6:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 50 mL of ethanol. Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[12, 0, 1984, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[247, 448, 1741, 2127])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhizoma Cimicifugae A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Metaderm (phellem) 1. Metaderm 2. Cortex 3. Phloem fibre bundles 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6.Xylem rays 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[11, 0, 1974, 256])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[297, 447, 1682, 2072])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Rhizoma Cimicifugae (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Metaderm cells 2. Bordered-pitted vessels 3. Reticulate vessel 4. Xylem fibres 5. Phloem fibres\n\n6. Xylem parenchyma cells\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[40, 0, 1973, 251])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately isoferulic acid standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of isoferulic acid.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[800, 999, 1293, 1357])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of isoferulic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Isoferulic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isoferulic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (325 nm) and a column $ (3.9\\times 300\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.9 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-50</td><td>95→75</td><td>5→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-70</td><td>75→50</td><td>25→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of isoferulic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of isoferulic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of isoferulic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from isoferulic acid peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and peak 5 in the chromatogram of test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject isoferulic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of isoferulic acid peak in the chromatogram of isoferulic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify isoferulic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of isoferulic acid Std-FP. The retention times of isoferulic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Cimicifugae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Cimicifugae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(ferulic acid)</td><td>0.93</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, isoferulic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.42</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.52</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[44, 0, 1965, 248])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[397, 385, 1754, 839])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhizoma Cimicifugae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 12.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 19.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Isoferulic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of isoferulic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nIsoferulic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the isoferulic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 10,20,30,40,50 mg/L for isoferulic acid."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-cimicifugae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Cimicifugae","la":"Rhizoma Cimicifugae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-coptidis-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Coptidis v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[534, 603, 1448, 1397])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried rhizome of Coptis chinensis Franch.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[594, 1717, 1414, 2400])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried rhizome of Coptis deltoidea C. Y. Cheng et Hsiao\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1966, 249])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[534, 603, 1448, 1397])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried rhizome of Coptis chinensis Franch.\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[594, 1717, 1414, 2400])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1(ii) A photograph of dried rhizome of Coptis deltoidea C. Y. Cheng et Hsiao\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[39, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Coptidis\n\nChinese Name: 黄連\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Huanglian"},{"name":"14","text":"Rhizoma Coptidis is the dried rhizome of Coptis chinensis Franch. or Coptis deltoidea C. Y. Cheng et Hsiao (Ranunculaceae), commonly known as \"Wei-lian\" or \"Ya-lian\", respectively. The rhizome is collected in the autumn, the rootlets and soil removed, then dried to obtain Rhizoma Coptidis."},{"name":"14","text":"Coptis chinensis Franch. : Mostly gathered as clusters of curved rhizomes, resembling \"chicken feet\"; single rhizome 3-7 cm long, 3-9 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-yellow or yellowish-brown, rough, bearing irregular nodular protrusions, rootlets, and remains of rootlets; some internodes smooth as the aerial stem. The upper part bearing remains of brown scale leaves, the apex often bearing remains of stems or petioles. Texture hard; fracture uneven; bark orange-red or dark brown, wood bright yellow or orange-yellow, radially arranged; pith sometimes hollowed. Odour slight; taste very bitter [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nCoptis deltoidea C. Y. Cheng et Hsiao : Mostly single rhizomes; somewhat cylindrical, slightly curved, 3-9 cm long, 3-10 mm in diameter. Internodes smooth and relatively long. Apex with some remains of stems [Fig. 1(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"The cork consisting of several layers of cells; sometimes scale leaf epidermis outside of the cork is observed. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups in the cortex. Phloem fibres occur in bundles\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[24, 1, 1956, 253])\n\nand may be accompanied by a few stone cells. Xylem lignified. Pith consists of parenchyma cells sometimes with stone cells [Fig. 2(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour yellowish-brown to brown. Stone cells yellow or yellowish-brown, subsquare, subrectangular, subround or polygonal in shape, 13-90 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; the wall fairly thickened and has pits, pit canals and striations; polychrome can be observed under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres slightly thick-walled, 8-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Phloem fibres occur in bundles, the wall fairly thickened and has cleft or pointed pits. Cork cells yellowish-brown, subsquare, subrectangular or polygonal in shape. Scale leaf epidermal cells greenish-yellow or yellowish-brown, the wall sinuous or with beaded-thickening. Vessels occasionally found [Fig. 3(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{2} $\n\nDissolve 0.4 g of bismuth subnitrate in 5 mL of acetic acid and 20 mL of water, then add 25 mL of aqueous potassium iodide solution (40%, w/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer 1 mL of the supernatant to a test tube. Add 2 drops of potassium iodobismuthate solution $ \\mathrm{R}_{2} $ and allow the precipitate to settle. Reddish-orange precipitate is observed."},{"name":"14","text":"Berberine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of berberine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nPalmatine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of palmatine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of toluene, ethyl acetate, methanol, 2-propanol and ammonia solution (12:6:3: 3:1, v/v).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 255])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[244, 443, 1737, 2120])\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried rhizome of Coptis chinensis Franch.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells D. Phloem fibres\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells 4. Phloem fibres 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[16, 7, 1962, 252])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[246, 416, 1737, 2212])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried rhizome of Coptis deltoidea C. Y. Cheng et Hsiao\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells D. Phloem fibres\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells 4. Phloem fibres 5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Pith 8. Stone cells in pith\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 0, 1983, 253])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[431, 442, 1749, 2067])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried rhizome of Coptis chinensis Franch.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells in a group 2. Stone cell 3. Xylem fibres 4. Phloem fibres 5. Vessels 6. Cork cells\n\n7. Scale leaf epidermal cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[445, 447, 800, 913])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[810, 446, 1177, 911])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1188, 449, 1540, 655])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1187, 666, 1540, 910])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[441, 931, 895, 1435])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 939, 1248, 1414])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1261, 928, 1541, 1432])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[441, 1444, 993, 1904])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1004, 1443, 1540, 1904])\n\n50μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried rhizome of Coptis deltoidea C. Y. Cheng et Hsiao\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells in a group 2. Stone cell 3. Xylem fibres 4. Phloem fibres 5. Vessels 6. Cork cells\n\n7. Scale leaf epidermal cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 250])"},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 100-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 4 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and add the same volume of ammonia solution to the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and leave for 15 min for equilibrium. Apply separately berberine chloride standard solution, palmatine chloride standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Place the plate into the trough of the chamber containing the developing solvent. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (365 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of berberine chloride and palmatine chloride.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[724, 1565, 1363, 1973])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[682, 2037, 1402, 2445])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) berberine chloride and (ii) palmatine chloride\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"Berberine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of berberine chloride CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (346 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-48</td><td>100→50</td><td>0→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>48-55</td><td>50→0</td><td>50→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-60</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of berberine chloride Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of berberine chloride should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of berberine chloride peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from berberine chloride peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and peak 4 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject berberine chloride Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of berberine chloride peak in the chromatogram of berberine chloride Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions,\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 252])\n\nidentify berberine chloride peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of berberine chloride Std-FP. The retention times of berberine chloride peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Coptidis extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Coptidis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(jatrorrhizine chloride)</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.90</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(coptisine chloride)</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(palmatine chloride)</td><td>0.98</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7(marker,berberine chloride)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[402, 1502, 1756, 2143])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Coptis chinensis Franch. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[404, 368, 1753, 1015])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Coptis deltoidea C. Y. Cheng et Hsiao extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[42, 0, 1961, 242])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 17.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-coptidis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Coptidis","la":"Rhizoma Coptidis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-curcumae-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Curcumae v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[461, 681, 1589, 2042])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Curcumae (i) Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. (ii) Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang (iii) Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 0, 1965, 246])"},"sections":[{"name":"19","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[461, 681, 1589, 2042])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma Curcumae (i) Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. (ii) Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang (iii) Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 0, 1965, 246])"},{"name":"19","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Curcumae\n\nChinese Name: 莪朮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Ezhu"},{"name":"19","text":"Rhizoma Curcumae is the dried rhizome of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val., Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang or Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling (Zingiberaceae). The rhizome is collected in winter when stem and leaves wither. After washing it clean, it is steamed or boiled thoroughly, then dried under the sun or in a shady and cool place, and once dried, fibrous root and foreign matter removed to obtain Rhizoma Curcumae."},{"name":"19","text":"Ovoid, elongate-ovoid, conical or elongate-fusiform, 1.5-8.5 cm in length, 8-40 mm in diameter, the apex frequently obtuse, the base obtuse and rounded. Externally yellowish-white, greyish-yellow or pale brown, rough or slightly smooth. The upper part with conspicuous, raised-annulations, and with rounded and slightly dented rootlet scars, or with remaining rootlets, some exhibiting a row of concave bud scars and subrounded lateral rhizome scars on each of the two sides, respectively. Heavy, texture hard, fracture waxy, sometimes covered with pale yellow powder; cortex and stele easily detachable, endodermal ring distinct, dot-shaped or strip-shaped of vascular bundles scattered inside the endodermal ring. Odour slightly aromatic; taste slightly bitter and pungent.\n\nCurcuma phaeocaulis Val. : Rhizome 1.5-5.5 cm in length, 15-35 mm in diameter. Externally pale brown to greyish-yellow, slightly smooth. Fracture markedly waxy, dark green to green; endodermal ring pale yellow [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nCurcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang: Rhizome 2-8.5 cm in length, 12-40 mm in diameter. Externally pale brown, mostly rough. Fracture slightly waxy, dark brown; endodermal ring whitish [Fig. 1(ii)].\n\nCurcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling: Rhizome 1.8-6.8 cm in length, 8-35 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown, mostly rough. Slightly light, fracture slightly waxy, yellow to pale brown; endodermal ring dark brown [Fig. 1(iii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[24, 2, 1956, 249])"},{"name":"19","text":"Epidermal cells sometimes remains, mostly lignified, non-glandular hairs visible occasionally; cork consists of a number of layers of cells, strongly or slightly lignified or non-lignified; cortex occupying about 1/3-1/4 of the radius, showing scattered leaf-trace bundles; endodermis distinct, cells often crimped. Stele broad, occupying about 2/3-3/4 of the radius, vascular bundles collateral and scattered, those along the pericycle relatively small and tightly arranged. Parenchyma cells filled with gelatinous starch masses; oil cells scattered in the parenchyma, containing golden oily matter [Fig. 2(i), (ii) and (iii)]."},{"name":"19","text":"Colour pale yellow, yellowish-brown or pale brown. Gelatinous starch masses greyish-white, making up the major portion of the powder, the shape in rounded-ovate, oblong, subround or irregular. Oil cells elongated-subround, subround or rounded-ovate, containing brownish-yellow or brownish-red oily matter, 23-154 $ \\mu $m in length, 21-85 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Vessels of the spiral type frequent, scalariform or reticulate types also present, 18-95 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Cork cells brownishyellow, consisting of several layers of cells, frequently overlapping, polygonal. Fibres occasional, obliquely sharp at the top, usually broken. Non-glandular hairs unicellular or 2-3 celled, some with sinuous wall, 11-55 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall 2-17 $ \\mu $m thick [Fig. 3(i), (ii) and (iii)]."},{"name":"19","text":"Vanillin-sulphuric acid solution (0.5% , w/v)\n\nDissolve 0.5 g of vanillin in 100 mL of sulphuric acid."},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL conical flask, then add 6 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and transfer 0.5 mL of the filtrate to a test tube. Cautiously add 0.5 mL of vanillin-sulphuric acid solution (0.5% ,w/v) along the inner wall of the tube. Allow to stand for 1 min. A dark purple layer is observed at the interface of the two solvent layers.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[346, 560, 917, 1245])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[327, 1421, 930, 1842])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[939, 461, 1671, 1854])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried rhizome of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Leaf-trace bundle 5. Endodermis 6. Stele 7. Phloem 8. Xylem\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[24, 12, 1952, 251])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[392, 510, 933, 1250])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[416, 1364, 934, 1726])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[949, 439, 1623, 1744])\n\n50 μm\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried rhizome of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Leaf-trace bundle 5. Endodermis 6. Stele 7. Phloem 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[41, 0, 1983, 251])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[362, 501, 908, 1199])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[360, 1335, 982, 1795])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[987, 454, 1644, 1820])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2(iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried rhizome of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Leaf-trace bundle 5. Endodermis 6. Stele 7. Phloem 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[14, 9, 1962, 252])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[255, 432, 1738, 2237])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(i) Microscopic features of powder of dried rhizome of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch gelatinous masses 2. Oil cell 3. Scalariform vessels 4. Spiral vessels 5. Cork cells\n\n6. Non-glandular hairs 7. Fibres\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 0, 1983, 253])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[375, 460, 830, 971])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[858, 459, 1603, 945])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[375, 1002, 902, 1467])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[930, 966, 1606, 1465])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 1487, 768, 2136])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[792, 1488, 1179, 2138])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1206, 1485, 1604, 2139])\n\n50μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried rhizome of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch gelatinous masses 2. Oil cell 3. Scalariform vessels 4. Spiral vessels 5. Cork cells\n\n6. Non-glandular hairs 7. Fibres\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[13, 9, 1962, 255])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[245, 458, 1750, 2085])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3(iii) Microscopic features of powder of dried rhizome of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch gelatinous masses 2. Oil cell 3. Scalariform vessels 4. Spiral vessels 5. Cork cells\n\n6. Non-glandular hairs 7. Fibres\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[40, 0, 1966, 247])"},{"name":"19","text":"Germacrone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of germacrone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"19","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (96:4, v/v)."},{"name":"19","text":"Transfer 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of the volatile oil (see 7.2 Assay of Volatile Oil) to a test tube and dissolve in 0.4 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"19","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately germacrone standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of germacrone.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[774, 1773, 1194, 2062])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of germacrone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"19","text":"Germacrone standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of germacrone CRS and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nGermacrone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nPipette 1.0 mL of germacrone Std-Stock into a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol."},{"name":"19","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter the supernatant through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"19","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (214 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>3-30</td><td>55→35</td><td>45→65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-38</td><td>35</td><td>65</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>38-45</td><td>35→10</td><td>65→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-55</td><td>10→0</td><td>90→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-65</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"19","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of germacrone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of germacrone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of germacrone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from germacrone peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and peak 2 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)].\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[40, 0, 1967, 252])"},{"name":"19","text":"Separately inject germacrone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of germacrone peak in the chromatogram of germacrone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify germacrone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of germacrone Std-FP. The retention times of germacrone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Curcumae extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Curcumae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, germacrone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.32</td><td>±0.09</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[399, 1664, 1753, 2279])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Curcuma phaeocaulis Val. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[397, 367, 1756, 951])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Curcuma kwangsiensis S. G. Lee et C. F. Liang extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[396, 1264, 1759, 1846])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(iii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried rhizome of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i), (ii) or (iii)]."},{"name":"19","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 16.0%."},{"name":"19","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%."},{"name":"19","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B).\n\nStandard solution\n\nGermacrone standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of germacrone CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol.\n\nGermacrone standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the germacrone Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 10,50,100,200,300 mg/L for germacrone.\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[27, 0, 1939, 249])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-curcumae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Curcumae","la":"Rhizoma Curcumae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-et-radix-notopterygii-v2-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii v2 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[523, 815, 1476, 1721])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 2, 1967, 246])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[523, 815, 1476, 1721])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[40, 2, 1967, 246])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii\n\nChinese Name: 羌活\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qianghuo"},{"name":"11","text":"Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii is the dried rhizome and root of Notopterygium incisum Ting ex H. T. Chang (Apiaceae/Umbelliferae). The rhizome and root is collected in the spring and autumn. After removal of the clay and rootlets, the rhizomes and roots are dried under the sun or by heat to obtain Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii."},{"name":"11","text":"The rhizomes are cylindrical and somewhat curved, showing scars of aerial stem at the apices, 4-13 cm long, 6-25 mm in diameter. Internodes short, forming dense raised annulations like a silkworm (known as Canqiang); or elongated like the nodes of a bamboo (known as Zhujieqiang). Externally reddishbrown to brownish-black in colour, the point where the outer bark had fallen off appears brownish yellow. Nodes show numerous dotted or tuberculate root scars and brown scales. Texture light and fragile, easily broken. Fracture uneven, with numerous radial clefts, bark brownish-yellow to dark brown, oily, with brown oil dots, wood yellowish-white with distinct rays, pith yellow to yellowish-brown. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter and acrid (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"The cork consists of over 10 rows of flat cells, sometimes a rhytidome is present. Cortex narrow. Phloem broad, ray cells mostly broken, forming many large clefts. Cambium in a ring. Xylem rays usually broken as clefts, with many vessels present. Phloem and pith with numerous secretory\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 1, 1955, 250])\n\ncanals, which are sub-rounded or irregularly long-rounded, varying in size, up to 450 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour brownish-yellow. Fragments of secretory canals easily visible, usually containing yellowish brown, linear secretion in the canals. Parenchyma cells mainly elongated, mostly containing pale yellowish secretion and oily droplets. Mainly reticulate vessels, with dense pits; spiral and bordered pitted vessels are also observed. Cork cells in lateral view multiseriate, flat, sometimes with a rhytidome outside, containing yellowish-brown matter; surface view polygonal or irregular in shape, thin-walled, crookedly sinuate. Secretory masses yellowish-brown, varying in size and up to 100 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and put into a test tube, then add 5 mL of diethyl ether. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 60 min. Allow the solid residues to settle. Transfer 2 mL of the supernatant to another test tube. Add 3 drops of hydroxylamines hydrochloride solution (7%, v/v) and mix. Adjust the pH to about 9 with sodium hydroxide solution (10%, w/v). Heat the mixture in a water bath for 1 min. Cool down to room temperature. Adjust the pH to about 3 with hydrochloric acid (6.2%, v/v), then add 1 drop of ferric chloride solution (1%, w/v). A red colouration is observed in the lower layer."},{"name":"11","text":"Isoimperatorin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isoimperatorin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nNotopterol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of notopterol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of toluene and ethyl acetate (4:1, v/v).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[11, 0, 1984, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 426, 1739, 2190])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Secretory canal [（i）smaller size and (ii) larger size] 1. Cork 2. Clefts 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem 6. Pith 7. Secretory canals\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[444, 467, 987, 930])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1046, 468, 1535, 927])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[457, 957, 964, 1389])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1048, 958, 1535, 1387])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[456, 1441, 971, 1902])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1023, 1438, 1541, 1904])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii (under the light microscope\n\n1. Secretory canal fragments 2. Secretory masses 3. Parenchyma cells 4. Reticulate vessel\n\n5. Bordered-pitted vessels 6. Cork cells\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[43, 0, 1973, 248])"},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min and then filter."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately isoimperatorin standard solution, notopterol standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of isoimperatorin and notopterol.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[774, 1379, 1345, 2198])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) isoimperatorin and (ii) notopterol\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Isoimperatorin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of isoimperatorin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and put into a 50-mL centrifugal tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (490 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a detector (255 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows-\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-45</td><td>60→30</td><td>40→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-50</td><td>30→0</td><td>70→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections each with 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of isoimperatorin Std-FP.The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of isoimperatorin should not be more than 3.0%; the RSD of the retention time of isoimperatorin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from isoimperatorin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and peak 4 in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject isoimperatorin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of isoimperatorin peak in the chromatogram of the isoimperatorin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Under the same HPLC conditions, identify\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[45, 0, 1967, 248])\n\nisoimperatorin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of isoimperatorin Std-FP. The retention times of isoimperatorin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(bergapten)</td><td>0.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(notopterol)</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,isoimperatorin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[406, 1440, 1756, 2116])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 19.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 22.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"11","text":"Isoimperatorin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of isoimperatorin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nIsoimperatorin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the isoimperatorin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series\n\nof solutions of 5,20,60,100,200 mg/L for isoimperatorin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-et-radix-notopterygii","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii","la":"Rhizoma et Radix Notopterygii"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-gastrodiae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Gastrodiae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 748, 1983, 2091])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[37, 1, 1943, 247])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 748, 1983, 2091])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[37, 1, 1943, 247])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Rhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nChinese Name: 天麻\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tianma"},{"name":"10","text":"Rhizoma Gastrodiae is the dried tuber of Gastrodia elata Bl. (Orchidaceae).The tuber is collected from winter to spring, washed clean immediately, steamed thoroughly, spread out and dried at a temperature not more than $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to obtain Rhizoma Gastrodiae."},{"name":"10","text":"Ellipsoid or slat-shaped, slightly compressed, shrunken and somewhat curved, 5-17 cm long, 14-43 mm wide, 5-26 mm thick. Externally yellowish-white to pale yellowish-brown, with longitudinal wrinkles and many transverse annulations arranged along latent buds, brown thread sometimes visible. Apex with reddish-brown to deep brown parrot-beak-shaped buds or remains of stem; the lower end with a rounded scar. Texture hard and uneasily broken, fracture fairly even, yellowish-white to brownish, horny. Odour slight; taste sweetish (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"The transverse section sometimes shows remains of epidermis; the hypodermis consists of 2-3 rows of suberized and tangentially elongated cells. Cortex cells are elongated tangentially, some contain raphides of calcium oxalate. In older tubers, one to several rows of sclerenchyma cells present in cortex adjacent to the hypodermis. Stele large, with numerous small amphicribral or collateral vascular bundles, plenty of parenchyma cells contained gelatinized polysaccharides were visible, raphides of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchyma cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour yellowish-white to yellowish-brown. Needle crystals of calcium oxalate are found in bundles or scattered, their length varied, the longer one up to 95 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ , showing a polychrome when examined under a polarized microscope. When mounted in glycerol-acetic acid test solution,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 1, 1966, 250])\n\nparenchyma cells containing gelatinized polysaccharides appear colourless, while some cells containing long-ellipsoid or subglobose granules appear as a brown or brownish-purple colour when iodine solution is added. Sclerenchyma cells are sometimes visible, ellipsoid to subpolygonal, the wall 3-9 $ \\mu m $ thick, lignified, distinctly pitted; pitted walls showing a black, cross shape when examined under a polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"Gastrodin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of gastrodin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and water (45:10:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Mix 1 mL of dilute sulphuric acid ( 50% , v/v) and 10 mL of p-hydroxybenzaldehyde in ethanol ( 2% , w/v ). Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 60 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately gastrodin standard solution and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of gastrodin.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 0, 1940, 252])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[249, 429, 1771, 2208])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Rhizoma Gastrodiae\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphides of calcium oxalate D. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermis 3. Sclerenchyma 4. Cortex 5. Stele\n\n6. Raphides of calcium oxalate 7. Vascular bundle 8. Gelatinized polysaccharides in parenchyma cells\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n北沙参\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1362, 63, 1446, 105])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n王不留行\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1614, 150, 1694, 199])\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[275, 454, 1680, 1555])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Rhizoma Gastrodiae\n\n1. Raphides of calcium oxalate 2. Parenchyma cells containing gelatinized polysaccharides\n\n3. Sclerenchyma cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 10, 255, 95])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[858, 23, 1089, 119])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[947, 177, 1067, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[745, 482, 1289, 772])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of gastrodin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Gastrodin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of gastrodin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL test tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (230 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05%Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Trifluoroacetic acid：Acetonitrile(0.05:99.95，v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>100→70</td><td>0→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of gastrodin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of gastrodin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of gastrodin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from gastrodin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject gastrodin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of gastrodin peak in the chromatogram of gastrodin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify gastrodin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of gastrodin Std-FP. The retention times of gastrodin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Gastrodiae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Rhizoma Gastrodiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,gastrodin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.31(vs peak1)</td><td>±0.06</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.71(vs peak1)</td><td>±0.09</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.28(vs peak3)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.36(vs peak3)</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.50(vs peak3)</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[394, 1651, 1749, 2346])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhizoma Gastrodiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[38, 0, 1943, 255])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"10","text":"Gastrodin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of gastrodin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nGastrodin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the gastrodin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 5, 50, 100, 150, 200 mg/L for gastrodin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhizoma-gastrodiae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhizoma Gastrodiae","la":"Rhizoma Gastrodiae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhododendri-daurici-folium-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhododendri Daurici Folium v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[519, 985, 960, 1750])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[960, 1109, 1156, 1635])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1192, 1122, 1416, 1623])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhododendri Daurici Folium\n\nA. Rhododendri Daurici Folium B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf C. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[519, 985, 960, 1750])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[960, 1109, 1156, 1635])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1192, 1122, 1416, 1623])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhododendri Daurici Folium\n\nA. Rhododendri Daurici Folium B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf C. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Rhododendri Daurici Folium\n\nChinese name: 满山红\n\nChinese phonetic name: Manshanhong"},{"name":"12","text":"Rhododendri Daurici Folium is the dried leaf of Rhododendron dauricum L. (Ericaceae). The leaf is collected in summer, foreign matter removed, then dried in shaded area to obtain Rhododendri Daurici Folium."},{"name":"12","text":"Leaf blade rolled, torn or crumpled, elliptic or oblanceolate, 4-8 cm long, 1-3 cm wide; apex mucronate, margin slightly revolute, base obtuse; both surfaces greenish-brown to brown; papery to almost leathery. Petiole 3-7 mm, glabrescent. Odour aromatic; taste bitter, slightly acrid (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Upper epidermal cells rectangular, covered with cuticle, with unicellular non-glandular hair on the surface near the midrib. Palisade tissue consists of 2-3 layers of cells, discontinuous at the midrib. Cells of spongy tissue subrounded. Vascular bundle of midrib bicolateral, pericycle fibres arranged in an interrupted ring. Collenchymatous cells occurring inside the upper and lower epidermis at the midrib. Parenchymatous cells containing clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate. Lower epidermal cells subrounded and peltate hairs at sunken spaces (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-green, greenish-brown to brown. Upper epidermal cells polygonal to irregular in shape. Lower epidermal cells with sinuous walls, paracytic stomata with 2 subsidiary cells. Non-glandular hair unicellular. Reticulate and bordered-pitted vessels visible, 5-137 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Stone cell numerous, 25-65 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Cluster of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, scattered singly, 9-72 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibre occasionally found, 9-18 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Peltate hair multicellular, branched, plate-like or shield-like, occasionally found (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 45, 1953, 244])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[234, 398, 1737, 2116])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of the transverse section of Rhododendri Daurici Folium\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Peltate hair\n\nD. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nE. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Non-glandular hair 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Phloem 6. Peltate hair 7. Xylem 8. Pericycle fibre 9. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n10. Collenchyma 11. Lower epidermis\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[645, 74, 765, 114])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 49, 1851, 96])\n\n京大戟\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\nNigellae Semen\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1043, 162, 1127, 206])\n\n石斛\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[300, 494, 1728, 2088])"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Upper epidermal cells 2. Lower epidermal cells with paracytic stomata\n\n3. Non-glandular hair 4. Bordered-pitted vessel 5. Reticulate vessel 6. Stone cell\n\n7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Fibres 9. Peltate hair\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 40, 1946, 236])"},{"name":"12","text":"Farrerol standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of farrerol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nHyperoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of hyperoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of methanol, water, tetrahydrofuran and formic acid (3.5:2.5:1.5:0.3, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC RP-18 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4 \\mathrm{S}} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately farrerol standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), hyperoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[380, 422, 879, 724])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[382, 839, 849, 1357])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) farrerol and (ii) hyperoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[19, 41, 1949, 240])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 376, 877, 1207])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Rhododendri Daurici Folium extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Farrerol standard solution 2. Hyperoside standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of farrerol and hyperoside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Farrerol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of farrerol CRS and dissolve in 100 mL ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nHyperoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of hyperoside CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (120 W) the mixture for 40 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (310 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>80→62</td><td>20→38</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-45</td><td>62→55</td><td>38→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>55→25</td><td>45→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of farrerol Std-FP and hyperoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of farrerol and hyperoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of farrerol and hyperoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from farrerol and hyperoside peaks should not be less than 800000 and 50000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject farrerol Std-FP, hyperoside Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of farrerol and hyperoside peaks in the chromatograms of farrerol Std-FP, hyperoside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify farrerol and hyperoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of farrerol Std-FP and hyperoside Std-FP. The retention times of farrerol and hyperoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhododendri Daurici Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Rhododendri Daurici Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, hyperoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.18</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5 (farrerol)</td><td>2.23</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[333, 1695, 1816, 2320])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhododendri Daurici Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 23.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhododendri-daurici-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhododendri Daurici Folium","la":"Rhododendri Daurici Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhododendri-mollis-flos-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhododendri Mollis Flos v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[526, 1049, 1014, 1614])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1062, 1023, 1476, 1653])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhododendri Mollis Flos A. Rhododendri Mollis Flos B. Magnified image of flower (spread view after soaking)"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[526, 1049, 1014, 1614])\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1062, 1023, 1476, 1653])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rhododendri Mollis Flos A. Rhododendri Mollis Flos B. Magnified image of flower (spread view after soaking)"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Rhododendri Mollis Flos\n\nChinese Name: 鬧羊花\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Naoyanghua"},{"name":"11","text":"Rhododendri Mollis Flos is the dried flower of Rhododendron molle G. Don (Ericaceae). The flower is collected at the beginning of flowering period in April and May, dried in a shaded area or under the sun to obtain Rhododendri Mollis Flos."},{"name":"11","text":"Mostly one or two flowers remaining on peduncle, greyish-yellow to yellowish-brown, shrunken. Sepals 5-lobed, semirounded to triangular, margins with slender hairs. Corolla campanulate, tube relatively long, 1-2.5 cm long, with contorted 5-lobed apex. Petals broadly ovate, with obtuse or retuse apex. Stamens 5, filaments curved, as long as or slightly longer than corolla, pubescent below the middle part, anthers reddish-brown, poricidal. Pistil 1, stigma cephaloid. Pedicel 1-2.8 cm long brown, pubescent. Odour slight (Fig.1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Pollen grains tetrahedral, 58-97 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with 3 germinal pores. Non-glandular hairs of calyx multicellular, arranged in several rows, 29-68 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Non-glandular hairs of corolla unicellular, 10-20 $ \\mu $m in diameter, up to 400 $ \\mu $m long or longer, wall thin, some with visible warty protuberance. Epidermal cells of pollen sac subpolygonal to subrounded, 13-31 $ \\mu $m in diameter, arranged orderly and tightly, wall slightly thickened, some with distinct pits, cells contain yellowish-brown contents. Epidermal cells of corolla rectangular, subsquare or irregular in shape, 26-78 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with thin and sinuous walls (Fig. 2).\n\nSaururi Herba 三白草\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[319, 455, 763, 898])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[804, 456, 1250, 899])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[320, 954, 772, 1403])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[800, 953, 1254, 1405])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[317, 1456, 779, 1916])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[801, 1452, 1253, 1918])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1308, 450, 1667, 1935])\n\n250 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Pollen grains (1-1 with germinal pores, 1-2 tetrahedron)\n\n2. Non-glandular hair of calyx (2-1 in full view, 2-2 in partial view)\n\n3. Non-glandular hair of corolla 4. Epidermal cells of pollen sac\n\n5. Epidermal cells of corolla"},{"name":"11","text":"Rhodojaponin II standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rhodojaponin II CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nRhodojaponin III standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rhodojaponin III CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol, acetone and formic acid (10:1:1:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately rhodojaponin II standard solution, rhodojaponin III standard solution and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 20 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[371, 365, 855, 707])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[381, 778, 857, 1038])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structures of (i) rhodojaponin II and (ii) rhodojaponin III\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[378, 1201, 928, 1920])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Rhododendri Mollis Flos extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Rhodojaponin II standard solution 2. Rhodojaponin III standard solution 3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of rhodojaponin II and rhodojaponin III (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"11","text":"Rhodojaponin II standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of rhodojaponin II CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nRhodojaponin III standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of rhodojaponin III CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 4.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 40 mL of ethanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with ethanol (70%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Pipette 25 mL of the solution to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in ethanol (70%). Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 8 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas (air) flow: 3.0 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>0.5% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-16</td><td>38→58</td><td>62→42</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>16-22</td><td>58→80</td><td>42→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of rhodojaponin II Std-FP and rhodojaponin III Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of rhodojaponin II and rhodojaponin III should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of rhodojaponin II and rhodojaponin III peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from rhodojaponin II and rhodojaponin III peaks should not be less than 25000 and 6000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[47, 31, 1935, 262])\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject rhodojaponin II Std-FP, rhodojaponin III Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of rhodojaponin II and rhodojaponin III peaks in the chromatograms of rhodojaponin II Std-FP, rhodojaponin III Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify rhodojaponin II and rhodojaponin III peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of rhodojaponin II Std-FP and rhodojaponin III Std-FP. The retention times of rhodojaponin II and rhodojaponin III peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rhododendri Mollis Flos extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rhododendri Mollis Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(rhodojaponin III)</td><td>0.60</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(hyperoside)</td><td>0.70</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(quercitrin)</td><td>0.87</td><td>$\\pm$0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,rhodojaponin II)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[332, 1828, 1793, 2334])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rhododendri Mollis Flos extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[62, 31, 1932, 260])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 18.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 14.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rhododendri-mollis-flos","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rhododendri Mollis Flos","la":"Rhododendri Mollis Flos"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rubiae-radix-et-rhizoma-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[368, 849, 1558, 1937])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified transverse section of roots\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[368, 849, 1558, 1937])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\nA. Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified transverse section of roots\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 茜草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Qiancao"},{"name":"13","text":"Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma is the dried root and rhizome of Rubia cordifolia L. (Rubiaceae). The root and rhizome are collected in spring and autumn, soil removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma."},{"name":"13","text":"Rhizomes nodular, with numerous fascicled roots varying in thickness. Roots cylindrical, slightly curved, 3.5-24 cm long, 1-10 mm in diameter, externally reddish-brown or dark brown, with fine longitudinal wrinkles and a few rootlet scars; the area where the outer bark has exfoliated appearing yellowish-red. Texture fragile, easily broken. Fracture even, bark narrow and purplish-red; xylem broad, pale yellowish-red, showing numerous pores of vessels. Odour slight; taste bitter and irritative to tongue when chewed continually (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Cork consists of 6-12 layers of cells, containing brown contents. Cortex narrow consisting of 4-5 layers of cells. Phloem cells relatively small. Cambium in a ring, less indistinct. Xylem occupies the major portion of the root, completely lignified; xylem rays indistinct. Parenchymatous cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 9, 1974, 259])\n\nRhizome: Cork consists of 6-12 layers of cells, containing brown contents. Cortex narrow consisting of 4-5 layers of cells. Phloem cells relatively small. Cambium in a ring, indistinct. Xylem occupies the greater portion of the rhizome, completely lignified, xylem rays indistinct. Parenchymatous cells contain raphides of calcium oxalate. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells, scattered with raphides of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Cork cells polygonal to irregular, wall thick, containing brown contents. Raphides of calcium oxalate numerous, scattered singly or in bundles, 18-97 $ \\mu $ m long; bright polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres colourless, slender, slightly curved. Vessels scattered singly or in bundles, mostly bordered-pitted vessels and few spiral vessels, 4-84 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Tracheids numerous, mostly in bundles, wall slightly thick and pits clear (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 6, 1914, 264])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[238, 442, 1744, 1966])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Raphides of calcium oxalate 4. Phloem 5. Cambium\n\n6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n紫苑\n\n績斷 Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 424, 1727, 1971])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Raphides of calcium oxalate 4. Phloem 5. Cambium\n\n6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray 8. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[35, 99, 210, 197])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[539, 19, 700, 75])\n\n桃仁 Persicae Semen\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\nSpatholobi Caulis\n\n羅布麻葉\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[293, 453, 738, 792])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[764, 453, 1216, 793])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1245, 455, 1689, 780])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[291, 807, 740, 1149])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[759, 814, 1210, 1145])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1241, 802, 1692, 1146])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[292, 1170, 741, 1506])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[758, 1169, 1208, 1500])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1240, 1174, 1689, 1505])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n1. Cork cells 2. Raphides of calcium oxalate in bundles\n\n3. Scattered raphides of calcium oxalate 4. Fibres 5. Bordered-pitted vessels\n\n6. Tracheids\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[68, 13, 1962, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Mollugin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of mollugin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (95%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (10:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (95%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately mollugin standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of mollugin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 2, 1915, 263])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 367, 750, 651])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[415, 724, 779, 961])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) mollugin and (ii) purpurin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Mollugin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (12 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.12 mg of mollugin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL conical flask, then add 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Trifluoroacetic acidand0.2% triethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>20→100</td><td>80→0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of mollugin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of mollugin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of mollugin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from mollugin peak should not be less than 200000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[65, 13, 1962, 259])\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject mollugin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of mollugin peak in the chromatogram of mollugin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify mollugin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of mollugin Std-FP. The retention times of mollugin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.19</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,mollugin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[294, 1738, 1824, 2430])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[45, 8, 1901, 262])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 15.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nOven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"13","text":"Mixed mollugin and purpurin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of mollugin CRS and 1.0 mg of purpurin CRS (Fig. 5), and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nMixed mollugin and purpurin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed mollugin and purpurin Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (70%) to produce a series of solutions of 1,2,8,16,30 mg/L for both mollugin and purpurin.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[66, 13, 1964, 260])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/rubiae-radix-et-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma","la":"Rubiae Radix et Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/salviae-plebeiae-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Salviae Plebeiae Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[348, 825, 1493, 1887])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Salviae Plebeiae Herba\n\nA. Salviae Plebeiae Herba B. Branch and leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of inflorescence D. Magnified image of leaves\n\nE. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf F. Magnified image of flowers\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[348, 825, 1493, 1887])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Salviae Plebeiae Herba\n\nA. Salviae Plebeiae Herba B. Branch and leaves\n\nC. Magnified image of inflorescence D. Magnified image of leaves\n\nE. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf F. Magnified image of flowers\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Salviae Plebeiae Herba\n\nChinese name: 荔枝草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Lizhicao"},{"name":"13","text":"Salviae Plebeia Herba is the dried aerial part of Salvia plebeia R. Br. (Lamiaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn while flowering and leaf being green, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Salviae Plebeia Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem square-columnar, frequently branched, 22-97 cm long, 1-7 mm in diameter; externally greyishgreen to brown, covered with short pubescence; fracture pale white, hollow in the centre. Leaves opposite, lamina usually crumpled, when whole, elliptic-ovate to elliptic-lanceolate, 1.5-9.7 cm long, 0.4-3.5 cm wide, margin dentate, both surfaces pubescent; petiole 0.4-6.8 cm long. Verticillaster spicate, apical or altar, corolla easily dropped; calyx campanulate, persistent, around 2-5.5 mm long, greyish-green to pale brown. Texture fragile, slight. Odour aromatic; taste bitter, pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare or rectangular cells; remnants of glandular hairs and non-glandular hairs sometimes present on epidermis. Collenchyma locate on the outer side of cortex in arrises, cells subpolygonal or irregular polygonal, thickened at corners. Cortex very narrow, sometimes indistinct. Pericycle fibre bundles locate in cortex, arranged in an interrupted ring, abundant near arrises. Phloem very narrow, cells relatively small. Xylem radially arranged, broad in arrises; medullary rays distinct. Pith large, usually hollow in the centre, consisting of large parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 38, 1948, 249])\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of flat rectangular cells. Non-glandular hair frequently observed on the surface of upper and lower epidermis. Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells. Spongy tissue consists of loosely arranged irregular cells, extended into both sides of the main vein. Collenchyma locate underneath the upper and lower epidermis of midrib, cells thickened at corners. Vascular bundles of midrib collateral; vessels 2-6 cells, radially arranged. Phloem parenchymatous cells polygonal. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of flat rounded or irregular cells, cells relativelly small [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Non-glandular hairs numerous, composed of 2-5 cells, 7-59 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; basal cells usually enlarged, apical cells acute. Head of glandular hairs unicellular, ovate or spherical, 7-27 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, sometimes with yellowish-brown contents, stalk unicellular or multicellular. Glandular scales numerous, greyish, yellowish-brown or colourless, composed of 4 cells, subglobose, 18-64 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Upper epidermal cells irregular polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls nearly straight, stomata diacytic. Lower epidermal cells irregular polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls sinuous, stomata diacytic or anomocytic. Pericycle fibres mostly in bundles, septate, lignified. Vessels numerous, mainly bordered-pitted and reticulate type, 6-60 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Raphides of calcium oxalates scattered in mesophyll cells, fine, 2-11 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; bright white under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalates abundantly exist in tracts, scattered in lower epidermal cells, 2-8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic or bright white under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains occasionally observed, 20-39 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; two types in shape, one subspherical, with spiny exine and germinal pores; another elliptic, with furrows, surface bearing reticulate striations (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 44, 1967, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[246, 417, 1711, 2109])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Salviae Plebeiae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericycle fibre bundle\n\n1. Glandular hair 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Epidermis 4. Collenchyma\n\n5. Cortex 6. Pericycle fibre bundle 7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[21, 45, 1953, 249])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[235, 394, 1741, 2128])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Salviae Plebeiae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Palisade tissue 3. Spongy tissue 4. Collenchyma\n\n5. Phloem 6. Xylem 7. Non-glandular hair 8. Lower epidermis\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[645, 75, 764, 114])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1722, 48, 1852, 96])\n\n京大戟\n\nNigellae Semen\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1042, 161, 1127, 206])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\nPicrasmae Ramulus et Folium\n\n貓爪草\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[302, 518, 1210, 906])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1238, 521, 1682, 898])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[301, 920, 750, 1261])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[772, 917, 1211, 1253])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1239, 920, 1683, 1260])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[303, 1289, 749, 1623])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[773, 1289, 1214, 1624])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1240, 1288, 1685, 1622])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[305, 1654, 744, 1988])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[771, 1657, 1216, 1985])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1242, 1659, 1684, 1985])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Non-glandular hairs 2. Glandular hairs and glandular scale\n\n3. Upper epidermal cells with diacytic stoma ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n4. Lower epidermal cells with anomocytic stoma ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) 5. Fibres\n\n6. Reticulate and bordered-pitted vessels 7. Raphides of calcium oxalate ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) in mesophyll cells\n\n8. Prisms of calcium oxalate ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) in lower epidermal cells 9. Pollen grains\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n---\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 39, 1945, 243])"},{"name":"13","text":"Homoplantaginin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of homoplantaginin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol, formic acid and water (11:1.4:0.1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC polyamide plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately homoplantaginin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the TLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the TLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 70^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[382, 424, 857, 699])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[382, 832, 1013, 1114])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) hispidulin and (ii) homoplantaginin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 1284, 790, 2118])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference TLC chromatogram of Salviae Plebeiae Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Homoplantaginin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of homoplantaginin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 41, 1948, 240])"},{"name":"13","text":"Hispidulin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of hispidulin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nHomoplantaginin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of homoplantaginin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction two more times. Wash the residue with ethanol (50%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (342 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>65</td><td>35</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>65→55</td><td>35→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>55→45</td><td>45→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>45→30</td><td>55→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of hispidulin Std-FP and homoplantaginin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of hispidulin and homoplantaginin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of hispidulin and homoplantaginin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from hispidulin and homoplantaginin peaks should not be less than 140000 and 24000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject hispidulin Std-FP, homoplantaginin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of hispidulin and homoplantaginin peaks in the chromatograms of hispidulin Std-FP, homoplantaginin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify hispidulin and homoplantaginin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of hispidulin Std-FP and homoplantaginin Std-FP. The retention times of hispidulin and homoplantaginin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Salviae Plebeiae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Salviae Plebeiae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.76</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,homoplantaginin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(hispidulin)</td><td>1.72</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[335, 1699, 1814, 2298])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Salviae Plebeiae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 13.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/salviae-plebeiae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Salviae Plebeiae Herba","la":"Salviae Plebeiae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sambuci-williamsii-ramulus-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[387, 808, 1578, 2056])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus\n\nA. Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus\n\nB. Magnified image of surface (upper: stem, lower: branch)\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface (left: stem, right: branch)"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[387, 808, 1578, 2056])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus\n\nA. Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus\n\nB. Magnified image of surface (upper: stem, lower: branch)\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface (left: stem, right: branch)"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus\n\nChinese name: 接骨木\n\nChinese phonetic name: Jiegumu"},{"name":"14","text":"Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus is the dried stem and branch of Sambucus williamsii Hance (Caprifoliaceae $ ^{*} $ ). The stem and branch are collected all year round, cut into section when fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus."},{"name":"14","text":"Cylindrical, some cut into half, 7-83 mm in diameter. Surface brown to dark brown, with longitudinal cracks, lenticel elliptical, protruding; old stem with deep cracks, young one and branch relatively smooth. Light in weight, texture very hard. Bark narrow in cut surface, brown, easily stripped off, wood yellowish-white, pith pale brown; annual rings distinct on smooth cut surface. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Cork consists of dozens to hundreds layers of cells, varying in thickness. Cortex narrow, cells with slightly thickened walls. Phloem with phloem fibres scattered singly or in groups. Xylem broad, consisting of vessels and fibres; xylem ray consists of 1-4 rows of cells. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells, some containing reddish-brown masses. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells of phloem and pith [Fig. 2(i) and (ii)].\n\nFootnote: Microscopic features of transverse section have no significant differences between the stem and branch.\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 36, 1955, 248])"},{"name":"14","text":"Colour pale brown. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate scattered or present in parenchymatous cells, prismatic or triangular; polychromatic or bright white under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibre numerous, usually in bundles, 11-47 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, wall thickened, with sparse oblique pits; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Phloem fibre singly scattered or in bundles, 11-52 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, pale yellow or yellow, wall always extremely thickened, lumen linear, margin sometimes uneven; orange or bright white under the polarized microscope. Xylem parenchymatous cells square or rectangular, with distinct pit canals and pit, wall slightly thickened. Cork cells usually broken, brown or yellowish-brown, subpolygonal or subrectangular in surface view. Mainly bordered-pitted vessels, usually broken, 13-133 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Reddish-brown mass irregular in shape (Fig. 3).\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 48, 1852, 97])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[246, 411, 1735, 2118])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate and phloem fibres (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate and phloem fibres (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Magnified image of pith\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Xylem ray 5. Xylem 6. Pith\n\n7. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 8. Phloem fibre 9. Reddish-brown mass\n\n---\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 46, 1961, 248])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[280, 445, 1721, 2085])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of branch of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate and phloem fibres (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate and phloem fibres (under the polarized microscope)\n\nE. Magnified image of pith\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Xylem ray 5. Xylem 6. Pith\n\n7. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 8. Phloem fibre 9. Reddish-brown mass\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 43, 1966, 254])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[265, 626, 1697, 2109])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 2. Xylem fibres 3. Phloem fibre\n\n4. Xylem parenchymatous cells 5. Cork cells 6. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n7. Reddish-brown mass\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 41, 1945, 236])"},{"name":"14","text":"Lariciresinol standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of lariciresinol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (24:7:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of water. Transfer the extract to a 15-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Pre-condition a solid phase extraction (SPE) column containing OS packing (12 mL, 2 g) with 30 mL of ethanol and then followed by 30 mL of water. Load 3 mL of the supernatant to the pre-conditioned SPE column. Add 10 mL of water to the column and discard the eluant. Add 10 mL of ethanol (30%) to the column. Collect the eluant and transfer to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the eluant to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (30%). Filter through a 0.22- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately lariciresinol standard solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 431, 689, 741])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 860, 913, 1108])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) (E)-ferulic acid and (ii) lariciresinol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[376, 1279, 768, 2113])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Lariciresinol standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of lariciresinol (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 40, 1946, 241])"},{"name":"14","text":"(E)-Ferulic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of (E)-ferulic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nLariciresinol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of lariciresinol CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the extract to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction two more times. Combine the filtrates. Evaporate the combined filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in ethanol (70%). Transfer the extract to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.22- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with OS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 40^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.35 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile:Isopropanol:Formic acid(9:1:0.01,v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-2</td><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>2-4</td><td>88→85</td><td>12→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>4-8</td><td>85→78</td><td>15→22</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>8-15</td><td>78→75</td><td>22→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-20</td><td>75→65</td><td>25→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 45, 1965, 255])"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of (E)-ferulic acid Std-FP and lariciresinol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of (E)-ferulic acid and lariciresinol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of (E)-ferulic acid and lariciresinol peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from (E)-ferulic acid and lariciresinol peaks should not be less than 3000 and 40000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject (E)-ferulic acid Std-FP, lariciresinol Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of (E)-ferulic acid and lariciresinol peaks in the chromatograms of (E)-ferulic acid Std-FP, lariciresinol Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify (E)-ferulic acid and lariciresinol peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of (E)-ferulic acid Std-FP and lariciresinol Std-FP. The retention times of (E)-ferulic acid and lariciresinol peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1[(E)-ferulic acid]</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.06</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,lariciresinol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.09</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[330, 376, 1818, 1001])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 9.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[29, 44, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 2.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%"},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sambuci-williamsii-ramulus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus","la":"Sambuci Williamsii Ramulus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sargentodoxae-caulis-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sargentodoxae Caulis v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[352, 741, 1587, 2044])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sargentodoxae Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sargentodoxae Caulis B. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[352, 741, 1587, 2044])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sargentodoxae Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sargentodoxae Caulis B. Magnified image of transverse section of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Sargentodoxae Caulis\n\nChinese name: 大血藤\n\nChinese phonetic name: Daxueteng"},{"name":"13","text":"Sargentodoxae Caulis is the dried lianoid stem of Sargentodoxa cuneata (Oliv.) Rehd. et Wils. (Lardizabalaceae). The stem is collected in autumn and winter. Removed from lateral branch, cut into sections, then dried under the sun to obtain Sargentodoxae Caulis."},{"name":"13","text":"Cylindrical, slightly curved, up to 60 cm long, 10-30 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown, rough, the outer layer often scaling off, the exposed surface dark reddish-brown, sometimes showing inflated nodes and slightly dented branch scars or leaf scars. Texture hard, in fracture, bark reddishbrown, some inlaid into the wood, wood yellowish-white, with numerous fine pores of vessels and rays arranged radially. Odour slight, taste slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells containing brown to brownish-red contents. Stone cells in cortex usually in groups, some of them containing prisms of calcium oxalate. Group of sieve tubes scattered in phloem. Secretory cells contain brown to brownish-red content, arranged tangentially and alternated with sieve tubes. Vascular bundles collateral. Fascicular cambium obvious. Xylem ray broad. Vessels mostly single and scattered, subrounded, up to about 400 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, surrounded by xylem fibres. Stone cells in pith sometimes visible. Paranchymatous cells containing brown to brownish-red contents and prisms of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 36, 1955, 249])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour reddish-brown. Cork cells brown, polygonal in surface view. Stone cells elliptical, subtriangular, sub-rectangular, fusiform or irregular in shape, some containing prisms of calcium oxalate; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered or present in parenchymatous cells, up to 39 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Secretory cells elliptical or irregular in shape, containing brown to brownish-red content. Diameter of bordered-pitted vessels varying, up to 400 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ . Fibres pit distinct, 20-42 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 45, 1967, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 408, 1736, 2109])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Sargentodoxae Caulis A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope) D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Stone cell 4. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n5. Secretory cell 6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Ray 9. Xylem 10. Pith\n\n西河柳\n\n大血藤\n\nSargentodoxae Caulis\n\n野老鸛草\n\nPolygonati Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n巴豆（生）\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\n黄精\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1691, 55, 1938, 138])\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\nSargentodoxae Caulis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[256, 416, 1750, 2114])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Sargentodoxae Caulis\n\n1. Cork cells 2. Stone cells and stone cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Secretory cells 5. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n6. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 45, 1965, 257])"},{"name":"13","text":"Liriodendrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of liriodendrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (7:1.5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol and dissolve 1 g of vanillin."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of water. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately liriodendrin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[36, 37, 1963, 245])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 430, 982, 643])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 768, 962, 1465])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside (salidroside) and (ii) liriodendrin\n\n</div>\n\n京大戟\n\nEuphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\n满山紅\n\nRhododendri Daurici Folium\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 374, 873, 1203])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Sargentodoxae Caulis extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1. Liriodendrin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of liriodendrin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"p-Hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside (salidroside) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 100 mL of water.\n\nLiriodendrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (5 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of liriodendrin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of water."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of water. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with water. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[32, 37, 1958, 246])"},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times 50 \\mathrm{~mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 130 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and $ 1 8 5 \\mathrm{~m}^{2} / \\mathrm{g} $ surface area). The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>Isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-20</td><td>95→85</td><td>5→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside Std-FP and liriodendrin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside and liriodendrin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside and liriodendrin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside and liriodendrin peaks should not be less than 8000 and 100000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside Std-FP, liriodendrin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside and liriodendrin peaks in the chromatograms of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside Std-FP, liriodendrin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside and liriodendrin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside Std-FP and liriodendrin Std-FP. The retention times of p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside and liriodendrin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 261])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sargentodoxae Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sargentodoxae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.46</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,p-hydroxyphenethyl glucopyranoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.76</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>5(liriodendrin)</td><td>3.88</td><td>±0.09</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[326, 1082, 1814, 1702])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Sargentodoxae Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sargentodoxae-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sargentodoxae Caulis","la":"Sargentodoxae Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sauropi-folium-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sauropi Folium v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[393, 837, 1404, 1946])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sauropi Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sauropi Folium\n\nB. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[393, 837, 1404, 1946])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sauropi Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sauropi Folium\n\nB. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Sauropi Folium\n\nChinese name: 龍脷葉\n\nChinese phonetic name: Longliye"},{"name":"12","text":"Sauropi Folium is the dried leaf of Breynia spatulifolia (Beille) Welzen & Pruesapan (syn. Sauropus spatulifolius Beille) (Euphorbiaceae*). The leaf is collected in summer and autumn, and then dried under the sun to obtain Sauropi Folium."},{"name":"12","text":"Crumpled into masses or long pieces, when whole, long-ovate, ovate-lanceolate or obovate-lanceolate; Upper surface yellowish-brown, yellowish-green or greenish-brown; lower surface lighter in colour, 5.9-14.9 cm in length, 2.1-5.1 cm in width, apex rounded-obtuse, somewhat pitted inwards, mucronulate; base cuneated or somewhat rounded, margin flattened or somewhat crumpled to undulate. Lower surface, midrib prominent, base occasionally pubescent, lateral veins pinnate, 4-8 opposite, and combined to marginal veins near outer margin; short-petioled. Odour slight; taste mild, slightly sweet (Fig.1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 12, 1972, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, subrectangular or subsquare. Collenchyma located under both upper and lower epidermis of the midrib; upper part consists of 2-3 layers of collenchymatous cells, subrounded or oblate; lower part consists of 2-4 layers of relatively small collenchymatous cells, subrounded or oblate. Palisade tissue under the upper epidermis, consisting of 1 layer of cylindrical cells, arranged in row, discontinue at midrib, containing clusters of calcium oxalate. Spongy tissue occupied about 1/2 of leaf, loosely packed, consisting of around 10 layers of irregularly shaped cells, scattered with clusters of calcium oxalate. Xylem occupied 2/3 of vascular bundle, vessels arranged radially in a row. Phloem occupied 1/3 of vascular bundle, containing of clusters of calcium oxalate in parenchymatous cells. Vascular bundle located in the middle of midrib, collateral. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, found in palisade tissue, spongy tissue and phloem; those in phloem are relatively small in size; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Non-glandular hair found on both upper and lower epidermis and more abundant in lower epidermis; unicellular, conical, sometimes with brown content (red after staining). Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, subrectangular to subsquare, cells are relatively smaller than those in upper epidermis (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Crystal sheath contains rosette aggregate clusters of calcium oxalate arranged in a row of 2-18 clusters, 9-42 $ \\mu $m in diameter, found in parenchyma of the vein; crystals polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Palisade tissue and spongy tissue both contain clusters of calcium oxalate; palisade cells cylindrical to subrectangular; spongy cells rounded to subrounded. Upper epidermal cells with sinuous anticlinal walls, containing numerous oil droplets. Lower epidermal cells with sinuous anticlinal walls, containing oil droplets; stomata abundant and paracytic, oblong to subrounded, with their subsidiary cells size varied. Non-glandular hair detached, unicellular, sub-cylindrical or nipple-like, 23-68 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Vessels mostly bordered-pitted and spiral, some reticulate, 6-38 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fibre occasionally found, cell wall thickened, long and slender, 9-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸隶仁\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[235, 386, 1747, 2124])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Sauropi Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Cluster of calcium oxalate 8. Non-glandular hair\n\n9. Lower epidermis\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[258, 635, 1687, 2129])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Sauropi Folium\n\n1. Crystal sheath 2. Palisade tissue 3. Upper epidermal cells\n\n4. Lower epidermal cells with paracytic stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) 5. Non-glandular hairs\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 spiral vessel, 6-2 bordered-pitted vessels, 6-3 reticulate vessels)\n\n7. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[26, 21, 1956, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol (70%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol, water and formic acid (25:5:5:2, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 45 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with methanol (70%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately kaempferol- 3-O-gentiobioside standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 5 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 366, 1058, 910])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 1083, 794, 1915])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Sauropi Folium extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[25, 20, 1962, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 45 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with methanol (70%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times 50 \\mathrm{~mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>95</td><td>5</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-30</td><td>95→55</td><td>5→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside peak in the chromatogram of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside Std-FP. The retention times of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Sauropi Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Sauropi Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(2-acetylpyrrole)</td><td>0.45</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[335, 1528, 1820, 2019])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Sauropi Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.2%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.3%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 15%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 27%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 18%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol (70%). Kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (70%) to produce a series of solutions of 2, 5, 10, 20, 50 mg/L for kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sauropi-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sauropi Folium","la":"Sauropi Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/saururi-herba-v8-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Saururi Herba v8 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[668, 965, 836, 1764])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[884, 985, 1051, 1425])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1081, 975, 1252, 1445])\n\n2cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Saururi Herba\n\nA. Saururi Herba\n\nB. Magnified image of leaves (left: upper surface, right: lower surface)\n\nC. Racemes"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[668, 965, 836, 1764])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[884, 985, 1051, 1425])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1081, 975, 1252, 1445])\n\n2cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Saururi Herba\n\nA. Saururi Herba\n\nB. Magnified image of leaves (left: upper surface, right: lower surface)\n\nC. Racemes"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Saururi Herba\n\nChinese Name: 三白草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Sanbaicao"},{"name":"14","text":"Saururi Herba is the dried aerial part of Saururus chinensis (Lour.) Baill. (Saururaceae). The aerial part is collected in summer and autumn, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Saururi Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Stems cylindrical, with 4 longitudinal furrows, one of them relatively broad. Fracture yellowish-brown to brown, fibrous and hollow. Leaves simple, alternate, lamina ovate to ovate-lanceolate, 3-12 cm long, 2-12 cm wide; acuminate at apex, cordate at base, margins entire, 5-basinerved; petiole relatively long, with longitudinal wrinkles. Racemes at the apex of branch, opposite to leaves, 6-12 cm long, flowers small, brown. Capsules subspheroidal. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of subsquare cells. Subrounded parenchymatous cells located beneath the epidermis, arranged in reticulate pattern with large intercellular spaces, form the cortex, with large cellular space. Oil cells and secretory ducts scattered in cortex, oil cells contain yellow oil droplets, while secretory ducts contain pale brown contents. Pericyclic fibre 3-7 rows, arranged in a ring or an interrupted ring lining the outerside of the vascular bundle. Vascular bundles collateral, fibre bundle located beneath the xylem. Pith broad, scattered with oil cells and clusters of calcium oxalate, aerenchyma occasionally found [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[32, 15, 1938, 261])\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis each consists of 1 layer of cells. 1 layer of large parenchymatous cells located beneath the upper epidermis. Palisade tissue consists of 1-2 layers of cells, containing brown contents. Cells of spongy tissue arranged loosely. Vascular bundle of midvein bicollateral, with the xylem made up of vessels and fibres. Vascular bundle sheath consists of several layers of sclerenchymatous cells, forming a ring enclosing the bicollateral vascular bundle. Several layers of collenchymatous cells exist between the midrib and lower epidermis. Glandular hairs present on the surface of lower epidermis. Oil cells and clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchyma [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour green. Secretory ducts 35-42 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide, containing brown or reddish-brown substances, brown substances 5-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, surrounding by parenchymatous cells with beaded-thickened walls occasionally, some with visible pits. Oil cells contain yellow oil droplets. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, scattered in parenchymatous cells, 10-20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres straight or curved, 12-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls relatively thick, some with visible pits and pit canals. Upper and lower epidermal cells slightly polygonal, stomata absent in upper epidermis and numerous in lower epidermis. Glandular hairs 2-3 celled. Vessels mainly reticulate, scalariform and spiral vessels also visible (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[797, 74, 892, 118])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[333, 681, 908, 1092])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1031, 638, 1584, 1168])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1028, 1173, 1595, 1476])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[335, 1360, 857, 1786])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n4\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n500 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[893, 1584, 1130, 1789])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1162, 1585, 1372, 1786])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1408, 1583, 1625, 1795])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Saururi Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Pericyclic fibres D. Oil cell\n\nE. Clusters of calcium oxalate F. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Oil cell 4. Pericyclic fibre 5. Phloem\n\n6. Xylem 7. Fibre bundle 8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Secretory duct 10. Pith\n\nPolygoni Perfoliati Herba\n\n杠板歸\n\n北豆根 Menispermi Rhizoma\n\nLonicerae Flos 山銀花\n\nSaururi Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[321, 808, 910, 1283])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[348, 1359, 614, 1620])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[661, 1365, 930, 1625])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[993, 809, 1679, 1498])\n\nD 50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Saururi Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate D. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Large parenchymatous cell 3. Palisade tissue 4. Oil cell\n\n5. Spongy tissue 6. Lower epidermis 7. Xylem 8. Phloem 9. Vascular bundle sheath\n\n10. Cluster of calcium oxalate 11. Collenchyma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[796, 74, 893, 118])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[300, 625, 859, 1055])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[873, 629, 1276, 1047])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1287, 634, 1682, 1049])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[303, 1078, 698, 1437])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[712, 1075, 1109, 1436])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1122, 1076, 1347, 1431])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1355, 1078, 1682, 1436])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[308, 1463, 702, 1811])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[720, 1466, 1120, 1809])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1136, 1461, 1685, 1813])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Saururi Herba\n\n1. Secretory duct (containing brown or reddish-brown substances) 2. Parenchymatous cells\n\n3. Oil cell 4. Cluster of calcium oxalate 5. Fibres 6. Upper epidermal cells\n\n7. Lower epidermal cells with stoma (->) 8. Glandular hair 9. Vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[37, 39, 1938, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Sauchinone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of sauchinone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), acetone and ethyl acetate (5:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 150-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of methanol. Sonicate (250 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 150-mL round-bottomed flask. Concentrate the filtrate to about 2 mL at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Transfer the solution to the neutral alumina and activated charcoal column [a glass column with internal diameter of 1.0 cm, dry packing the column with mixture of 0.4 g of activated charcoal (mesh number of 20-40) and 4 g of neutral alumina (mesh number of 100-200)]. Add 60 mL of methanol to the column. Collect the eluant and transfer to a 150-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately sauchinone standard solution and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 6 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1266, 193, 1388, 240])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[373, 371, 916, 775])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of sauchinone\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 939, 811, 1698])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Saururi Herba extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Sauchinone standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of sauchinone (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[39, 37, 1939, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Sauchinone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of sauchinone CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 150-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture in a boiling water bath for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (250 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>17</td><td>83</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-25</td><td>17→26</td><td>83→74</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-45</td><td>26→60</td><td>74→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of sauchinone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of sauchinone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of sauchinone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from sauchinone peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject sauchinone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of sauchinone peak in the chromatogram of sauchinone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify sauchinone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of sauchinone Std-FP. The retention times of sauchinone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Saururi Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Saururi Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.26</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.32</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,sauchinone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[330, 1434, 1793, 1940])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Saururi Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 11.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 11.0% Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/saururi-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Saururi Herba","la":"Saururi Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/saxifragae-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Saxifragae Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[395, 823, 1583, 1950])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Saxifragae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Saxifragae Herba B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of stolons E. Magnified image of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 43, 1965, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[395, 823, 1583, 1950])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Saxifragae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Saxifragae Herba B. Magnified image of upper surface of leaf\n\nC. Magnified image of lower surface of leaf\n\nD. Magnified image of stolons E. Magnified image of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 43, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Saxifragae Herba\n\nChinese name: 虎耳草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Huercao"},{"name":"14","text":"Saxifragae Herba is the dried whole plant of Saxifraga stolonifera Curtis (Saxifragaceae). The whole plant is collected all year round, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Saxifragae Herba."},{"name":"14","text":"Basal leaves with petiole 3-15 cm; leaf blade subcordate or reniform to subrounded, 2-8 cm long, 3-10 cm wide, apex obtuse, base rounded to cordate, margin irregularly dentate or undulated. Upper surface of the leaf greenish-brown to brown, lower surface pale greenish-brown to brown. Stolons densely crisped. Root fibrous. Odour slightly pungent; taste acrid (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Root: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of thin and flat cells, covered with cuticle. Cortex broad, consisting of irregularly shaped cells, sinuous walls. Clusters of calcium oxalate numerous, in rosette aggregate, scattered in the cortex and pith. Endodermis distinct. Stele cambium visible, in interrupted ring within the vascular cylinder. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells, clusters of calcium oxalate numerous [Fig. 2(i)].\n\nStolon: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells sub-rectangular to polygonal. Cortex broad, parenchymatous cells irregular in shape, walls sinuous. Endodermis distinct. Vascular bundle closed collateral. Pith consists of parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2(ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 38, 1946, 252])\n\nLeaf: Unicellular or multicellular non-glandular hair found on the upper epidermis, maybe broken or fallen off. Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cells subrectangular. Palisade tissue consists of 3-4 layers of cells, cells oval in shape. Vascular bundle amphicribral. Cells of spongy tissue loosely arranged, irregular in shape, tile-like, sinuous walls. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in mesophyll, in rosette aggregate. Cells of lower epidermis polygonal, walls thin, flattened. Multicellular non-glandular hair found on the lower epidermis, broken or fallen off [Fig. 2(iii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour brownish. Upper epidermal cells of leaf tile-like, flat with non-glandular multicellular hair or non-glandular unicellular hairs. Lower epidermal cells with anisocytic stomata, 3 unequal subsidiary walls. Multicellular non-glandular hairs occasionally found, fragmented. Simple starch granules spherical; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells of stolons with wavy, undulated cell wall. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregate, 20-50 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Unicellular non-glandular hairs occasionally found. Fibres appears singly or in bundles. Spiral vessels occasionally found, 8-87 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 43, 1966, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 395, 1742, 2132])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Saxifragae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate in cortex (under the light microscope) D. Clusters of calcium oxalate in cortex (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cluster of calcium oxalate 3. Cortex 4. Endodermis\n\n5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n野老鸛草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1939, 138])\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[236, 461, 1729, 2119])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of stolon of Saxifragae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Pith 6. Xylem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 45, 1967, 253])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[255, 415, 1744, 2091])\n\nFigure 2 (iii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Saxifragae Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) D. Clusters of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Unicellular non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Palisade tissue\n\n4. Vascular bundle 5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Spongy tissue\n\n8. Cluster of calcium oxalate 9. Lower epidermis 10. Multicellular non-glandular hair\n\n野老鸛草\n\nPolygonati Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1057, 91, 1144, 134])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1691, 55, 1938, 138])\n\nGeranii Caroliniani Herba\n\nCrotonis Fructus (unprocessed)\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[305, 563, 1690, 1986])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Saxifragae Herba\n\n2. Lower epidermal cells with anisocytic stomata ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Multi-cellular non-glandular hair 4. Starch granules 5. Epidermal cells of stolon\n\n6. Cluster of calcium oxalate 7. Unicellular non-glandular hair 8. Fibre\n\n9. Spiral vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 45, 1965, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Bergenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of bergenin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 30% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, ethyl acetate, isopropanol, formic acid and methanol (5:1.5:1:0.5:0.3, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of ethanol (30%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately bergenin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[20, 41, 1952, 246])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[384, 364, 822, 753])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of bergenin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[376, 936, 832, 1743])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Saxifragae Herba extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Bergenin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of bergenin (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 43, 1965, 257])"},{"name":"14","text":"Bergenin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (60 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.0 mg of bergenin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol (30%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol (30%). Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 $ mm) packed with alkyl reversed-phase bonded silica gel with diisopropyl side chain (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>100→90</td><td>0→10</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>90→70</td><td>10→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of bergenin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of bergenin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of bergenin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from bergenin peak should not be less than 120000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject bergenin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of bergenin peak in the chromatogram of bergenin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify bergenin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of bergenin Std-FP. The retention times of bergenin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Saxifragae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Saxifragae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(gallic acid)</td><td>0.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(protocatechuic acid)</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,bergenin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[332, 1584, 1818, 2213])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Saxifragae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[20, 45, 1958, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 15.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 4.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 14.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 4.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"14","text":"Bergenin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of bergenin CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of ethanol (30%).\n\nBergenin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the bergenin Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (30%) to produce a series of solutions of 5,10,20,40,80 mg/L for bergenin.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[19, 40, 1948, 244])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/saxifragae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Saxifragae Herba","la":"Saxifragae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/schisandrae-chinensis-fructus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[622, 1097, 1356, 1809])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1928, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[622, 1097, 1356, 1809])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1928, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 五味子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Wuweizi"},{"name":"12","text":"Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Schisandra chinensis (Turcz.) Baill. (Magnoliaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in autumn, stalk and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun or after steamed to obtain Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregularly spheroidal to compressed-spheroidal, 5-8 mm in diameter. Externally red to dull red, shrunken, oily, with soft pulp, sometimes externally blackish-red or covered with \"white frost\". Seeds 1-2, kidney-shaped, externally brownish-yellow, lustrous, testa thin and fragile. Odour of pulp slight; taste slightly sour. Odour of seeds fragrant when broken; taste pungent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of square to rectangular epidermal cells, walls relatively thickened, covered with striated cuticle, oil cells scattered. Mesocarp parenchymatous cells shrunken, with indistinct boundaries, containing scattered starch granules, with small collateral vascular bundles. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of small, square parenchymatous cells. The outermost layer of testa consists of radially elongated stone cells, thick-walled, with fine and dense pits and pit canals; inside showing several layers of stone cells, polygonal to subrounded or irregular in shape, with slightly thickened walls and distinct pits and pit canals. The cells in the oil cell layer square to rectangular, containing oil drops. Inner epidermal cells of testa flat and shrunken. Endosperm contains oil droplets and aleurone grains (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[23, 10, 1982, 262])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brown to dark brown. Stone cells of the outer layer of testa 18-40 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, polygonal to elongated-polygonal in surface view, rectangular in longitudinal section view; walls thickened with very fine and dense pit canals; lumen contains dark brown contents. Inner layer stone cells of testa vary in size, up to 83 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, polygonal to subrounded or irregular in surface view, with slightly thickened walls and distinct pits and pit canals. Epidermal cells of exocarp polygonal in surface view, anticlinal walls slightly beaded, with striated cuticle and scattered oil cells. Mesocarp cells shrivelled, containing brown contents and starch granules. Endosperm cells contain oil droplets and aleurone grains (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Schisandrin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of schisandrin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nSchisandrin B standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of schisandrin B CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (15:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately schisandrin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), schisandrin B standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of schisandrin and schisandrin B.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 3, 1936, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[254, 412, 1746, 2001])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Exocarp, oil cell and mesocarp D. Mesocarp, endocarp and testa\n\n6. Testa (6a. The outermost layer of testa 6b. Inner layers of testa with several layers of stone cells)\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱莲\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 45, 1984, 87])\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[257, 517, 1717, 1940])\n\n1. Stone cells of outer layer of testa in surface view"},{"name":"12","text":"2. Stone cells of outer layer of testa and stone cells of inner layer in longitudinal section view\n\n3. Inner layer of stone cells of testa 4. Epidermal cells of exocarp 5. Mesocarp cells\n\n6. Oil cell layer and endosperm cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 3, 1930, 260])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[391, 366, 895, 717])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[393, 876, 830, 1222])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) schisandrin and (ii) schisandrin B\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Schisandrin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of schisandrin CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol.\n\nSchisandrin B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of schisandrin B CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.4 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates. Make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[13, 3, 1979, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-45</td><td>50→15</td><td>50→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-50</td><td>15→0</td><td>85→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of schisandrin Std-FP and schisandrin B Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of schisandrin and schisandrin B should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of schisandrin and schisandrin B peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from schisandrin and schisandrin B peaks should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject schisandrin Std-FP, schisandrin B Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of schisandrin and schisandrin B peaks in the chromatograms of schisandrin Std-FP, schisandrin B Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify schisandrin and schisandrin B peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of schisandrin Std-FP and schisandrin B Std-FP. The retention times of schisandrin and schisandrin B peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 3, 1929, 259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker1,schisandrin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.27(vs peak1)</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.52(vs peak1)</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.90(vs peak1)</td><td>$\\pm0.05$</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker2,schisandrinB)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[384, 950, 1816, 1684])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[30, 20, 1976, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 16.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/schisandrae-chinensis-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus","la":"Schisandrae Chinensis Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/schisandrae-sphenantherae-fructus-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[1004, 887, 1281, 1107])\n\n0. 5cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[637, 1195, 1336, 1757])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[1004, 887, 1281, 1107])\n\n0. 5cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[637, 1195, 1336, 1757])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[20, 6, 1927, 259])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 南五味子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Nanwuweizi"},{"name":"11","text":"Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Schisandra sphenanthera Rehd. et Wils. (Magnoliaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in autumn, stalk and foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus."},{"name":"11","text":"Spheroidal to compressed-spheroidal, 4-7 mm in diameter. Externally brownish-red to dark brown, shrunken and shrivelled; pulp usually adhered closely to seeds. Seeds 1-2, kidney-shaped, externally brownish-yellow, the surface minutely granular, testa thin and fragile. Odour of pulp slight; taste slightly sour. Odour of seeds fragrant when broken; taste pungent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of square to rectangular epidermal cells, covered with cuticle; oil cells scattered. Mesocarp parenchymatous cells shrunken, with indistinct boundaries, containing scattered starch granules, with small collateral vascular bundles. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of small, square parenchymatous cells. The outermost layer of testa consists of radially elongated stone cells, thick-walled, with fine and dense pits and pit canals; inside showing several layers of stone cells, polygonal to subrounded or irregular in shape, with slightly thickened walls and distinct pits and pit canals. The cells in oil cell layer square to rectangular, containing oil droplets. Inner epidermal cells of testa flat and shrunken. Endosperm contains oil droplets and aleurone grains (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[13, 11, 1983, 261])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour brown. Stone cells of outer layer of testa 18-30 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, polygonal to elongated-polygonal in surface view; rectangular in longitudinal section view; walls thickened with very fine and dense pit canals; lumen contains dark brown contents. Inner layer stone cells of testa vary in size, up to 60 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, polygonal to subrounded or irregular in surface view, with slightly thickened walls and distinct pits and pit canals. Epidermal cells of exocarp polygonal in surface view, with striated cuticle, scattered with oil cells. Mesocarp cells shrivelled, containing brown contents and starch granules. Endosperm cells contain oil droplets and aleurone grains (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Schisandrin A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of schisandrin A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nSchisantherin A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of schisantherin A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and formic acid (15:5:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately schisandrin A standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), schisantherin A standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of schisandrin A and schisantherin A.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 1, 1929, 259])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 416, 1751, 2037])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus\n\n</div>\n\n6. Testa (6a. The outermost layer of testa 6b. Inner layers of testa with several layers of stone cells)\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[22, 8, 1984, 263])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[290, 558, 1699, 1790])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Stone cells of outer layer of testa in surface view\n\n2. Stone cells of outer layer of testa and stone cells of inner layer in longitudinal section view\n\n3. Inner layer stone cells of testa 4. Epidermal cells of exocarp 5. Mesocarp cells\n\n6. Oil cell layer and endosperm cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 3, 1929, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[452, 367, 885, 715])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[456, 867, 1014, 1208])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) schisandrin A and (ii) schisantherin A\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Schisandrin A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of schisandrin A CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 0.4 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (560 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates. Make up to the mark with ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.8 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[26, 4, 1966, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-45</td><td>50→15</td><td>50→85</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-50</td><td>15→0</td><td>85→100</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>0</td><td>100</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of schisandrin A Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of schisandrin A should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of schisandrin A peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from schisandrin A peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject schisandrin A Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of schisandrin A peak in the chromatogram of schisandrin A Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify schisandrin A peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of schisandrin A Std-FP. The retention times of schisandrin A peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(schisantherin A)</td><td>0.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, schisandrin A)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 2, 1929, 261])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[387, 399, 1842, 1100])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"11","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 14, 1976, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 21.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/schisandrae-sphenantherae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus","la":"Schisandrae Sphenantherae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/schizonepetae-spica-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Schizonepetae Spica v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[28, 5, 1928, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[28, 5, 1928, 260])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Schizonepetae Spica\n\nChinese Name: 荆芥穗\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jingjiesui"},{"name":"10","text":"Schizonepetae Spica is the dried fruit-spike of Schizonepeta tenuifolia Briq. (Lamiaceae). The spike is collected in summer and autumn when flowers bloom to the top and spikes become green. After foreign matter removed, the spike is dried under the sun to obtain Schizonepetae Spica."},{"name":"10","text":"Cylindrical, 2-24 cm long and 2-9 mm in diameter. Corolla usually missing, persistent calyx yellowish green to green, campanulate, fragile. Nutlets brownish-black, small. Odour aromatic; taste pungent and slightly astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Colour yellowish-green to green. Glandular scales scattered, each with a subrounded head in top view, 8-celled, 30-124 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; stalk very short, unicellular, containing yellow to yellowish-brown secretion. Glandular hairs small, each with a 1-2 celled subspherical head, 11-29 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; stalk unicellular. Non-glandular hairs usually broken, 1-6 celled when whole, 45-711 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 8-69 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter at base, walls warty. Epidermal cells of persistent calyx rectangular or subrectangular, with wavy anticlinal walls. Cells of exocarp polygonal in surface view, with mucilaginous walls, lumen small, with yellowish-brown content; subsquare or subrectangular in lateral view, usually covered with a yellow to brown pigment layer; exocarp cells greyish-white, dull orange-yellow if connected to pigment layer under the polarized microscope. Stone cells of endocarp colourless, yellow to pale brown, with deeply wavy anticlinal walls and densely pitted; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Pollen grains subspherical, with six canals, 16-37 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Fibres slender, 3-31 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[21, 14, 1975, 265])\n\nwith slightly wavy or straight walls. Oil droplets rounded, pale yellow to yellow. Vessels mainly spiral, 2-32 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"10","text":"Pulegone standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of pulegone CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 1 mL of petroleum ether $ ( 6 0-8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}) $"},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (37:3, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1 g of vanillin and dissolve in 100 mL of sulphuric acid in ethanol (5%, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.8 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 15 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter the supernatant."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately pulegone standard solution (0.1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-7 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of pulegone.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 3, 1934, 256])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[282, 447, 1703, 2034])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"10","text":"1. Glandular scale (1-1 and 1-2 in top view, 1-3 in lateral view) 2. Glandular hair 3. Non-glandular hair\n\n4. Epidermal cells of persistent calyx 5. Cells of exocarp (5-1 in surface view, 5-2 in lateral view)\n\n6. Stone cells of endocarp 7. Pollen grain 8. Fibres 9. Oil droplets 10. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[12, 9, 1984, 265])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[388, 370, 677, 593])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structure of pulegone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Pulegone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of pulegone CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL centrifuge tube, then add 50 mL of methanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.5%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>84</td><td>16</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-40</td><td>84→60</td><td>16→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-50</td><td>60→48</td><td>40→52</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>48→20</td><td>52→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of pulegone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of pulegone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of pulegone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from pulegone peak should not be less than 300000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 5, 1927, 260])\n\nThe R value between peak 7 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject pulegone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of pulegone peak in the chromatogram of pulegone Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify pulegone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of pulegone Std-FP. The retention times of pulegone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Schizonepetae Spica extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of Schizonepetae Spica extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.84</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7(marker,pulegone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 370, 1857, 974])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Schizonepetae Spica extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 9.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 11.0%.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[27, 4, 1930, 259])"},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"10","text":"Pulegone standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 10.0 mg of pulegone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nPulegone standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the pulegone Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 10, 50, 100, 150, 200 mg/L for pulegone."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/schizonepetae-spica","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Schizonepetae Spica","la":"Schizonepetae Spica"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/scrophulariae-radix-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Scrophulariae Radix v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[677, 897, 1318, 1861])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 5, 1928, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[677, 897, 1318, 1861])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 5, 1928, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Scrophulariae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 玄參\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xuanshen"},{"name":"12","text":"Scrophulariae Radix is the dried root of Scrophularia ningpoensis Hemsl. (Scrophulariaceae). The root is collected in winter, removed rhizome, rootlets, root buds, soil and sand, slowly baked or dried under the sun until semi-dried, then piled for 3-6 days; repeat several times to dry, then obtain Scrophulariae Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Subcylindrical, slightly thick at the middle part or thick at the upper part and slender at the lower part; sometimes slightly curved, 6-28 cm long, 6-40 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-yellow or greyish-brown, with distinct, irregular longitudinal grooves and latitudinal lenticels; scars of rootlet occasionally observed. Texture tough, pliable, transverse section black and slightly lustrous. Odour caramel-like; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Metaderm slightly suberized. Cortex relatively broad; stone cells numerous, polygonal, subrounded or subsquare in shape; cell walls thick, mostly singly scattered or 2-5 in groups. Phloem slightly narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem ray broad, vessels arranged intermittently and radially, those in the inter part of xylem surrounded with xylem fibres. Metaxylem observed in the centre. Parenchymatous cells contain nucleus-like contents (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-yellow to greyish-brown. Stone cells numerous, mostly singly scattered or 2-5 in groups; rectangular, subsquare or subrounded, triangular or fusiform in shape, 23-132 $ \\mu $ m in diameter or 47-250 $ \\mu $ m long, cell walls 4-22 $ \\mu $ m thick; pits small, pit canals mostly ramified,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 7, 1984, 263])\n\nlumina relatively large. Parenchymatous cells numerous, mostly containing dark nucleus-like contents. Fibres slender, slightly lignified, with small pits. Reticulate and bordered-pitted vessels, 4-125 $ \\mu $ m. Metaderm cells brownish-yellow, subrectangular on the surface view; walls slightly thickened and suberized (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Harpagide standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of harpagide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nHarpagoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of harpagoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, methanol and formic acid (4:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately harpagide standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), harpagoside standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat it at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 1 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of harpagide and harpagoside.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[598, 15, 695, 68])\n\n湖北貝母\n\n砒霜 Arsenicum\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[246, 453, 1683, 2046])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Scrophulariae Radix\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells D. Parenchymatous cells\n\n1. Metaderm 2. Cortex 3. Stone cells 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Ray\n\n8. Metaxylem 9. Nucleus-like content\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n墨旱莲\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1891, 43, 1984, 87])\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[281, 419, 1720, 2117])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Scrophulariae Radix (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Parenchymatous cells 3. Fibres 4. Bordered-pitted vessels\n\n5. Reticulate-scalariform vessels 6. Metaderm cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[28, 4, 1935, 259])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[382, 367, 778, 560])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[388, 600, 867, 1044])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[439, 1040, 1104, 1468])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) cinnamic acid (ii) harpagide and (iii) harpagoside\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Cinnamic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of cinnamic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nHarpagoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of harpagoside CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[13, 4, 1978, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>0.05%Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>40</td><td>60</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-30</td><td>40→70</td><td>60→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>70</td><td>30</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of cinnamic acid Std-FP and harpagoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of cinnamic acid and harpagoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of cinnamic acid and harpagoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from cinnamic acid and harpagoside peaks should not be less than 80000 and 110000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject cinnamic acid Std-FP, harpagoside Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of cinnamic acid and harpagoside peaks in the chromatograms of cinnamic acid Std-FP, harpagoside Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify cinnamic acid and harpagoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of cinnamic acid Std-FP and harpagoside Std-FP. The retention times of cinnamic acid and harpagoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Scrophulariae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Scrophulariae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.76</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,cinnamic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(harpagoside)</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[385, 422, 1810, 1004])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Scrophulariae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 16.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 8, 1984, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 60.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 57.0 %."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/scrophulariae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Scrophulariae Radix","la":"Scrophulariae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/scutellariae-barbatae-herba-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Scutellariae Barbatae Herba v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[404, 834, 1561, 1713])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[404, 834, 1561, 1713])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 258])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Scutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 半枝蓮\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Banzhilian"},{"name":"12","text":"Scutellariae Barbatae Herba is the dried whole plant of Scutellaria barbata D. Don (Lamiaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer and autumn when foliage branch growing luxuriantly, foreign matter removed, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Scutellariae Barbatae Herba."},{"name":"12","text":"Root slender. Stem square, dark purple or brownish-green, frequently branched. Leaves opposite, short petiole or near sessile; lamina crumpled and easily broken, when whole, lanceolate or ovate lanceolate, 0.7-3.6 cm long and 0.4-2.5 cm wide, obtuse at the apex, broadly cuneate near the base, entire or indistinctly crenate, the upper surface dark green, the lower surface greyish-green. Flowers solitary in the leaf axils of the upper part of the branches, corolla 2-lipped, brown or pale blue-purple, frequently fallen off, calyx lobes blunt, 1.8-4 mm long, pubescent. Nutlets oblate. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem: Epidermal cells 1 layer, subrectangular, arranged in order, covered with cuticle, occasionally with glandular hairs. Cortex relatively thin, cell elongated tangentially, collenchyma occurs at the outside of cortex, more frequently at the edge and corner. Endodermis distinct, 1 layer of cells, subrectangular. Vascular bundles collateral, developed at the four edges. Phloem narrow, cambium distinct, xylem relatively broad, linked up into a ring, vessels radially arranged. Pith broad, parenchymatous cells subrounded, generally hollow in mid-part (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 7, 1984, 262])\n\nRoot: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, cell subsquare or rectangular. Cortex broad. Endodermis distinct, consisting of 1 layer of cells, cell subrounded, arranged in order. Phloem narrow, cambium in a ring, xylem vessels scattered (Fig. 3).\n\nLeaf: Upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, with glandular scales, non-glandular hairs, glandular hairs and sunken stoma. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of palisade cells, arranged in order and spongy tissue consists of subrounded parenchymatous cells, arranged loosely. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, subrounded, relatively small. The inner side of the upper and lower epidermis had collenchyma (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-green. Epidermal cells of stem irregular rectangular or ovate in shape, stoma and glandular scale visible, stoma in diacytic type, with 4-7 subsidiary cells. Epidermal cells of leaf walls slightly curved, stoma in diacytic type, with 2-7 subsidiary cells. Glandular scales 4 to 8-celled, subrounded or elliptical, 24-47 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Glandular hairs consist of a fewcelled head, stalk 1 cell. Non-glandular hairs consist of 1-4 cells, apical cell relatively long, with fine warty protuberance on the surface. Mainly reticulate vessels, 8-46 $ \\mu $m in diameter, bordered-pitted and spiral vessels occasionally visible. Fibres often in bundles, 8-36 $ \\mu $m in diameter, usually broken, and relative thick walls; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Apigenin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of apigenin CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and formic acid (10:0.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[34, 4, 1928, 254])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 425, 1705, 1737])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Scutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Glandular hair 4. Cortex 5. Endodermis 6. Phloem 7. Cambium\n\n8. Xylem 9. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[29, 20, 1977, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 407, 1741, 1815])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf and root of Scutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch of leaf B. Section illustration of leaf C. Sketch of root D. Section illustration of root\n\nE. Glandular hair\n\n1. Non-glandular hair 2. Upper epidermis 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue 5. Glandular hair\n\n6. Vascular bundle 7. Collenchyma 8. Lower epidermis 9. Epidermis 10. Cortex 11. Endodermis\n\n12. Phloem 13. Cambium 14. Xylem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[171, 14, 326, 70])\n\nDictamni Cortex\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[598, 15, 695, 68])\n\nArctii Fructus牛蒡子\n\nAurantii Fructus Immaturus\n\n湖北貝母\n\n延胡索\n\nCorydalis Rhizoma\n\nArsenicum\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1693, 26, 1901, 108])\n\nIsatidis Folium\n\nSchizonepetae Spica\n\n荊芥穗\n\nCurcumae Longae Rhizoma\n\n蒼朮 薑黃\n\nAtractylodis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[260, 436, 1708, 1786])\n\nFigure 4 Microscopic features of powder of Scutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n1. Epidermal cells of stem 2. Epidermal cells of leaf 3. Glandular scale 4. Glandular hairs\n\n5. Non-glandular hair 6. Reticulate vessel 7. Bordered-pitted vessel 8. Spiral vessel 9. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[11, 5, 1984, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately apigenin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 10 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of apigenin.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 1260, 1176, 1963])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 Chemical structures of (i) apigenin and (ii) scutellarin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Scutellarin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (24 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.24 mg of scutellarin CRS (Fig. 5) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 3, 1929, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol (70%). Immediately reflux the mixture for 15 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (270 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>1% Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>83→75</td><td>17→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-30</td><td>75</td><td>25</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of scutellarin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of scutellarin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of scutellarin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from scutellarin peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject scutellarin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of scutellarin peak in the chromatogram of scutellarin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify scutellarin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of scutellarin Std-FP. The retention times of scutellarin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 4, 1978, 266])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Scutellariae Barbatae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Scutellariae Barbatae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.93</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, scutellarin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.67</td><td>±0.08</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[380, 966, 1731, 1667])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Scutellariae Barbatae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[19, 6, 1931, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 10.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 26.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/scutellariae-barbatae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Scutellariae Barbatae Herba","la":"Scutellariae Barbatae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sedi-herba-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sedi Herba v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[545, 833, 1507, 1926])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sedi Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sedi Herba B. Magnified image of herb\n\nC. Magnified image of leaves and adventitious roots D. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[22, 43, 1964, 256])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[545, 833, 1507, 1926])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sedi Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sedi Herba B. Magnified image of herb\n\nC. Magnified image of leaves and adventitious roots D. Magnified image of leaf\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[22, 43, 1964, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Sedi Herba\n\nChinese name: 垂盆草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Chuipencao"},{"name":"12","text":"Sedi Herba is the dried whole plant of Sedum sarmentosum Bunge (Crassulaceae). The whole plant is collected in summer and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Sedi Herba."},{"name":"12","text":"Stems slender, length varies up to over 20 cm long, some nodes bearing slender adventitious roots. Roots small and short. Leaves whorled in 3, blade oblanceolate to oblong, yellowish-green to green, 0.8-2.7 cm long, 3-9 mm wide, nearly acute at the apex, sharply narrow at the base. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Stem: Epidermal cells rectangular, the outer walls thickened. Cortex broad and consists of 10 layers of parenchymatous cells, some of cells containing brown to reddish-brown masses. Vascular cylinder small. Vascular bundles collateral. Phloem located outside xylem, arranged in a ring. Xylem vessels subrounded. Pith triangular, cells polygonal, walls thickened [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nLeaf: Blade cells usually shrunken. Upper and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of cells, walls thickened. Mesophyll cells usually shrunken. Vascular bundles collateral, with the phloem cells usually shrunken, vessels subrounded. Stomata sometimes visible on the epidermis [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale brown. Upper and lower epidermal cells show a wavy and curved anticlinal wall, with stomata present on both surfaces, mostly of anisocytic type. Cells containing brown to reddish-brown masses occasionally found. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells; bright white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly spiral and bordered-pitted vessels (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1059, 91, 1142, 133])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[1692, 55, 1937, 137])\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[237, 414, 1731, 2103])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Sedi Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Cells containing brown to reddish-brown masses\n\n4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Pith\n\n溪黄草\n\nIsodonis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[631, 40, 774, 115])\n\n京大戟 Euphorbiae Pekinensis Radix\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 395, 1748, 2128])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Sedi Herba\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration (midrib with blade) C. Upper epidermis with stoma D. Lower epidermis with stoma\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Mesophyll 3. Vessel 4. Phloem 5. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1058, 91, 1143, 134])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1692, 55, 1938, 138])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[275, 534, 1691, 2003])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Upper epidermal cells of leaf with stomata 2. Lower epidermal cells of leaf with stomata\n\n3. Cells containing reddish-brown to brown masses 4. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 5. Spiral vessels\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 44, 1964, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 75% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, acetone, formic acid and water (8:1:0.5:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 3 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (75%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol (75%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 367, 1052, 678])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 838, 874, 1669])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Sedi Herba extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 44, 1965, 253])"},{"name":"12","text":"Luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (25 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol ( 75% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol ( 75% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol ( 75% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (348 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.5 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>95→86</td><td>5→14</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-55</td><td>86→77</td><td>14→23</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>55-60</td><td>77→55</td><td>23→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[19, 40, 1949, 246])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu $ L each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak in the chromatogram of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside Std-FP. The retention times of luteolin 7-O- $ \\beta $ -D-glucoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sedi Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sedi Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker,luteolin7-O-β-D-glucoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[340, 1646, 1815, 2265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Sedi Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 45, 1958, 257])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 5.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 17.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 6.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 19.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sedi-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sedi Herba","la":"Sedi Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/selaginellae-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Selaginellae Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[728, 799, 1226, 1431])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried herb of Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring A. Selaginellae Herba B. Small branches\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[751, 1722, 1272, 2406])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried herb of Selaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim. A. Selaginellae Herba B. Small branches\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1900, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[728, 799, 1226, 1431])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried herb of Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring A. Selaginellae Herba B. Small branches\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[751, 1722, 1272, 2406])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried herb of Selaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim. A. Selaginellae Herba B. Small branches\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1900, 263])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Selaginellae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 卷柏\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Juanbai"},{"name":"15","text":"Selaginellae Herba is the dried herb of Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring or Selaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim. (Selaginellaceae). The whole plant is collected throughout the year, fibrous root and soil removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Selaginellae Herba."},{"name":"15","text":"Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring: Crumpled into a fisted mass, 3-10 cm long. Branches fascicled, flat and branched, green or brownish-yellow, curved inward, scaly leaflets densely matted, each leaflet long-aristate at the apex. Central leaves (ventral leaves) bear 2 lines, ovate-oblong, arranged obliquely upward, margins membranous, irregularly serrulate. In dorsal leaves (lateral leaves), membranous margin of dorsal surface frequently brownish-black. Fibrous roots remain attached at the base, brown to dark brown, scattered or clustered in short-rod shape. Odour slight; taste weak [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nSelaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim.: Crumpled into a globular or fisted mass, 2-9.5 cm long. Central leaves (ventral leaves) bear 2 lines, ovate-lanceolate. Lamina asymmetrical, the inner side relatively straight, the outer side frequently thickened by inward folding, margins entire. Fibrous roots scattered and remain attached at the base. Odour slight; taste weak [Fig. 1 (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[60, 11, 1964, 258])"},{"name":"15","text":"Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, with thickened wall, covered with cuticle. Cortex mainly consists of sclerenchyma and parenchyma; 2-3 layers of sclerenchymatous cells located on the outer side of the cortex, with unlignified wall; more than 10 layers of sclerechymatous cells located in the middle part, with thickened wall, heavily lignified; several layers of parenchymatous cells located on the inner part, arranged densely. 2-4 leaf-trace vascular bundles scattered in cortex. 1 or 2 long-ellipsoid or ovoid-rounded air cavities present in the inner part of cortex, with elongated-ellipsoid or elongated strip-shaped stele in the centre of air cavity; all (of these) connected with the endodermis through several transverse bridges, transverse bridges arranged radially. Stele sheath distinct, xylem cells completely lignified [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nSelaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim.: Air cavity 1, ovate-triangular [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour yellowish-brown or brown. Margin cells of leaf long and narrow, convex, denticulate or long hairy. Epidermal cells of leaf subsquare or subrectangular, stomata anomocytic, subsidiary cells 5-7. Spores yellowish-brown, subrounded, subtriangular or subellipsoid, 17-77 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with irregular protuberance in the surface, exine smooth after protuberance falling off. Scalariform tracheids yellowish-brown or grey, 4-21 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 3 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[40, 7, 1904, 266])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 452, 1706, 1578])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of main stem of Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Leaf-trace vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Leaf-trace vascular bundle 4. Endodermis 5. Air cavity 6. Stele\n\n7. Transverse bridge 8. Xylem\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n繢断 Selaginellae Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[261, 464, 966, 808])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[289, 947, 871, 1533])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1009, 746, 1681, 1534])\n\nB\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of main stem of Selaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Leaf-trace vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Leaf-trace vascular bundle 4. Endodermis 5. Air cavity 6. Stele\n\n7. Transverse bridge 8. Xylem\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[34, 99, 209, 197])\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[292, 454, 989, 920])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1016, 452, 1690, 930])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[294, 947, 988, 1416])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1003, 946, 1692, 1419])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried herb of Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Margin cells of leaf 2. Epidermal cells of leaf with stomata 3. Spores 4. Scalariform tracheids\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Selaginellae Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[255, 450, 1711, 1558])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried herb of Selaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Margin cells of leaf 2. Epidermal cells of leaf with stomata 3. Spores 4. Scalariform tracheids\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 6, 1910, 261])"},{"name":"15","text":"Amentoflavone standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of amentoflavone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Developing solvent system 1\n\nPrepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, cyclohexane and formic acid (5:2:0.5, v/v).\n\nDeveloping solvent system 2\n\nPrepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (5:2, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, two twin trough chambers and the freshly prepared developing solvent systems as described above. Apply separately amentoflavone standard solution and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent system 1 to one of the troughs of the first chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 3 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber and dry in air. Add the developing solvent system 2 to one of the troughs of the second chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[70, 10, 1968, 260])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of amentoflavone.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[380, 505, 992, 1006])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of amentoflavone\n\n</div>"},{"name":"15","text":"Amentoflavone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of amentoflavone CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 250-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 50 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 5 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (330 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>40→44</td><td>60→56</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-45</td><td>44→65</td><td>56→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>65→70</td><td>35→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[47, 4, 1909, 264])"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of amentoflavone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of amentoflavone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of amentoflavone peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from amentoflavone peak should not be less than 12000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject amentoflavone Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of amentoflavone peak in the chromatogram of amentoflavone Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify amentoflavone peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of amentoflavone Std-FP. The retention times of amentoflavone peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Selaginellae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Selaginellae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,amentoflavone)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.16</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.15</td><td>$\\pm0.06$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.78</td><td>$\\pm0.11$</td></tr></table>\n\nCyperi Rhizoma\n\n香附\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[286, 372, 1797, 810])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried herb of Selaginella tamariscina (Beauv.) Spring extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[296, 1002, 1791, 1452])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried herb of Selaginella pulvinata (Hook. et Grev.) Maxim. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[66, 15, 1903, 265])"},{"name":"15","text":"Total ash: not more than 13.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5 %."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 2.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/selaginellae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Selaginellae Herba","la":"Selaginellae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/semen-cassiae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Semen Cassiae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[651, 904, 1065, 1210])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1122, 953, 1321, 1208])\n\n5mm\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Cassia obtusifolia L.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[681, 1918, 1099, 2240])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1135, 1936, 1321, 2205])\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1943, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[651, 904, 1065, 1210])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1122, 953, 1321, 1208])\n\n5mm\n\nFigure 1(i) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Cassia obtusifolia L.\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[681, 1918, 1099, 2240])\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1135, 1936, 1321, 2205])\n\n5mm\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1943, 253])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Semen Cassiae\n\nChinese Name: 決明子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Juemingzi"},{"name":"13","text":"Semen Cassiae is the dried ripe seed of Cassia obtusifolia L. or Cassia tora L. (Fabaceae). The ripe legume is collected in autumn and dried under the sun, then the seed is tapped out and foreign matter removed to obtain Semen Cassiae."},{"name":"13","text":"Cassia obtusifolia L.: Slightly rhomboid-cuboidal, short-cylindrical or tapered-cuboidal in shape, 3-7 mm in length, 1.5-4 mm in width, both ends parallel and slanting, one end truncate, the other acuminate. Externally greenish-brown to dark brown, smooth and lustrous, with a light yellowishbrown longitudinal line or band on each side. Texture hard, not easily broken; testa thin. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1(i)].\n\nCassia tora L.: Short-cylindrical to tapered-cuboidal in shape, externally usually brown, 2-6 mm in length, and 1-3 mm in width. In general, the size is somewhat smaller than that of C. obtusifolia L. [Fig. 1(ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Testa thin. Cuticle smooth, transparent and thick. Palisade cells in 1 layer, palisade-shaped, showing 2 distinct luciferous bands. Brace or prop cells in 1 layer, nearly dumbbell-shaped, wall thick, with large intercellular spaces. Nutritive layer consists of 6-8 rows of parenchyma cells. Perisperm tissue greyish-white, translucent, rich in aleurone granules. Cotyledonous tissue is made up of 2 patches, S-shaped, with the parenchyma cells rich in cluster of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2(i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 1, 1967, 250])"},{"name":"13","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Palisade cells in groups, colourless or pale yellowish, arranged in 1 row, when viewed laterally narrow-rectangular in shape, 50-113 $ \\mu $m in length, with thick walls, the cell lumina minute, in surface view sub-polygonal. Brace cells dumbbell-shaped laterally, suborbicular or polygonal in surface view, with 2 concentric circles, 13-84 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Cluster of calcium oxalate numerous, scattered in the parenchyma cells, 9-44 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Fig. 3(i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Aurantio-obtusin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of aurantio-obtusin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, isopropanol and formic acid (5:1:0.1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Dissolve 3.0 g sodium hydroxide in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter through a filter paper."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately aurantio-obtusin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of aurantio-obtusin.\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[860, 21, 1087, 117])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1871, 83])\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 239])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草 Herba Leonuri 平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis 吴茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[237, 449, 1734, 1904])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Cassia obtusifolia L.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B-C. Section illustration\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Palisade cells 3. Brace cells 4. Nutritive layer 5. Endosperm\n\n6. Cotyledon 7. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n決明子\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1363, 64, 1446, 105])\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1614, 150, 1694, 199])\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[236, 416, 1736, 1811])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Cassia tora L.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B-C. Section illustration\n\n1. Cuticle 2. Palisade cells 3. Brace cells 4. Nutritive layer 5. Endosperm\n\n6. Cotyledon 7. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[102, 6, 207, 60])\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[861, 22, 1087, 116])\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1670, 12, 1870, 83])\n\nRadix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[948, 178, 1063, 239])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[325, 462, 755, 934])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[775, 459, 1225, 938])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1254, 464, 1658, 931])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[325, 988, 625, 1461])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[660, 989, 931, 1462])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1057, 988, 1659, 1460])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[325, 1526, 975, 2002])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1001, 1529, 1660, 1999])\n\n100 $ \\mu m $\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Cassia obtusifolia L.\n\n1. Palisade cells (lateral view) 2. Palisade cells (surface view)\n\n3. Palisade cells and brace cells 4. Brace cells (surface view) 5. Brace cells (lateral view)\n\n6. A fragment of cotyledon 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\nRhizoma Atractylodis Macrocephalae\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n銀杏葉\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 89])\n\n北沙参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\n太子参\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1362, 64, 1446, 105])\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1614, 150, 1694, 199])\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[329, 463, 831, 898])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[848, 460, 1647, 898])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[331, 935, 738, 1373])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[750, 942, 1060, 1378])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1065, 936, 1657, 1369])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[328, 1424, 857, 1866])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1110, 1426, 1659, 1865])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Cassia tora L.\n\n1. Palisade cells (lateral view) 2. Palisade cells (surface view)\n\n3. Palisade cells and brace cells 4. Brace cells (surface view) 5. Brace cells (lateral view)\n\n6. A fragment of cotyledon 7. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[39, 11, 255, 94])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[858, 23, 1089, 118])\n\n![](page=7,bbo"},{"name":"13","text":"Aurantio-obtusin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of aurantio-obtusin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (285 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1%Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>82→70</td><td>18→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-40</td><td>70→60</td><td>30→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>60→40</td><td>40→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of aurantio-obtusin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of aurantio-obtusin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of aurantio-obtusin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from aurantio-obtusin peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[11, 2, 1966, 251])\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject aurantio-obtusin Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of aurantio-obtusin peak in the chromatogram of aurantio-obtusin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify aurantio-obtusin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of aurantio-obtusin Std-FP. The retention times of aurantio-obtusin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Semen Cassiae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Semen Cassiae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.35</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.40</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.50</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,aurantio-obtusin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[395, 1732, 1751, 2480])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Cassia obtusifolia L. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[856, 24, 1090, 119])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[946, 179, 1064, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[396, 446, 1749, 1187])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5(ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Cassia tora L. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5(i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 29.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[22, 0, 1965, 248])"},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"13","text":"Aurantio-obtusin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 2.0 mg of aurantio-obtusin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nAurantio-obtusin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the aurantio-obtusin Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol ( 70% ) to produce a series of solutions of 1,4,10,15,20 mg/L for aurantio-obtusin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/semen-cassiae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Semen Cassiae","la":"Semen Cassiae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/semen-vaccariae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Semen Vaccariae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[457, 898, 1535, 1727])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[457, 898, 1535, 1727])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1940, 253])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Semen Vaccariae\n\nChinese Name: 王不留行\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Wangbuliuxing"},{"name":"11","text":"Semen Vaccariae is the dried seed of the ripe fruit of Vaccaria segetalis (Neck.) Garcke (Caryophyllaceae). The plant is collected in summer when the fruit is ripe but before dehiscing, dried under the sun, the seeds gathered and dried again under the sun, then foreign matter removed to obtain Semen Vaccariae."},{"name":"11","text":"Spheroidal, 1.4-2.4 mm in diameter. Externally black or chestnut-brown, slightly lustrous. Fine, dense and evenly distributed granular protuberances visible on testa surface under $ 1 0 \\times $ magnification; hilum suborbicular and dented, with a shallow taenioid furrow on one side. Texture hard; milky white endosperm exposed when cracked open; embryo curved like a ring; cotyledons 2. Odour slight; taste slightly astringent and bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Epidermal cells of testa are arranged in 1 row, reddish-brown, outer wall distinctly thickened with nipple-like protuberances, striations barely perceptible; inner epidermis of testa shrunken, yellowish-brown to brown. Endosperm consists of parenchyma cells containing aleurone granules. Cotyledons and radicle composed of parenchyma cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale greyish-brown. Epidermal cells of testa reddish-brown or yellowish-brown, stellate in shape, 48-135 $ \\mu $m in diameter, outer wall striations thickened; inner epidermis of testa pale yellowish-brown, subsquare, rectangular to polygonal, anticlinal wall densely beaded, striations visible on the surface. Endosperm cells polygonal, subsquare or subrectangular, lumina full of aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells contain fatty oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[22, 1, 1965, 246])"},{"name":"11","text":"Segetalin A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of segetalin A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (10:1, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (4:1, v/v). Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately segetalin A standard solution and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for about 1 min. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of segetalin A.\n\nHerba Taxilli 桑寄生\n\n葛根\n\nRadix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[859, 22, 1087, 117])\n\n女貞子\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1871, 83])\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 239])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n吳茱萸\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[274, 441, 866, 899])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[318, 1100, 914, 1524])\n\n100 $ \\mu m $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[975, 456, 1679, 1521])\n\nB\n\n100 $ \\mu m $"},{"name":"11","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermal cell of testa\n\n1. Epidermis of testa 2. Inner epidermis of testa 3. Endosperm\n\n4. Radicle 5. Cotyledon\n\nNote: The radicle and cotyledon are composed of parenchyma cells and contain no characteristic features for authentication. As they are located at two sides of the seed, they were not shown in detail.\n\nFolium Ginkgo\n\nMedulla Junci\n\n銀杏葉\n\n燈心草\n\nFructus Forsythiae\n\n連翹\n\nSemen Cassiae\n\n北沙參\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n決明子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1043, 24, 1176, 90])\n\n太子参\n\nRhizoma Gastrodiae\n\nRadix Pseudostellariae\n\nRadix Polygalae\n\n夏枯草\n\nSpica Prunellae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\nRhizoma Anemarrhenae 知母\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1613, 150, 1694, 199])\n\nRadix Rehmanniae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\nRhizoma Belamcandae\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[333, 449, 961, 936])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1014, 448, 1647, 935])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[334, 994, 963, 1482])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1018, 994, 1647, 1484])\n\n50μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Semen Vaccariae (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Epidermal cells of testa 2. Inner epidermal cells of testa 3. Endosperm cells\n\n4. Cotyledon cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 11, 255, 95])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[857, 23, 1089, 119])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[946, 175, 1072, 241])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[739, 404, 1265, 837])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of segetalin A\n\n</div>"},{"name":"11","text":"Segetalin A standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of segetalin A CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (240 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (219 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-20</td><td>90→85</td><td>10→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>70→30</td><td>30→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 0, 1966, 249])"},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of segetalin A Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of segetalin A should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of segetalin A peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from segetalin A peak should not be less than 150000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject segetalin A Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of segetalin A peak in the chromatogram of segetalin A Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify segetalin A peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of segetalin A Std-FP. The retention times of segetalin A peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Semen Vaccariae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Semen Vaccariae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(Hypaphorine)</td><td>0.24</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.59</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.92</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(marker, segetalin A)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[857, 24, 1090, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[947, 180, 1062, 240])\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平貝母 Bulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[380, 534, 1752, 1001])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Semen Vaccariae extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[22, 0, 1959, 247])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/semen-vaccariae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Semen Vaccariae","la":"Semen Vaccariae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/semiaquilegiae-radix-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Semiaquilegiae Radix v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[419, 1101, 1078, 1654])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1125, 1163, 1548, 1616])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Semiaquilegiae Radix A. Semiaquilegiae Radix (rootlet scar $ \\rightarrow $ B. Magnified image of cut surface of root tuber"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[419, 1101, 1078, 1654])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1125, 1163, 1548, 1616])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 cm\n\n</div>\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Semiaquilegiae Radix A. Semiaquilegiae Radix (rootlet scar $ \\rightarrow $ B. Magnified image of cut surface of root tuber"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Semiaquilegiae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 天葵子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tiankuizi"},{"name":"12","text":"Semiaquilegiae Radix is the dried root tuber of Semiaquilegia adoxoides (DC.) Makino (Ranunculaceae). The root tuber is collected in early summer, washed clean, rootlets removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Semiaquilegiae Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregularly short-cylindrical, fusiform or lump-shaped, slightly curved, 1.0-4.7 cm long, 3-15 mm in diameter. Externally dark brown, blackish-brown to greyish-black, with irregular longitudinal wrinkles and rootlet scars. Apex usually with remains of stems or leaves, covered with several layers of yellowish-brown sheath-like scales. Texture relatively soft, easily broken, transversely cut surface whitish or pale yellowish-white in the bark and yellowish-white to yellowish-brown and slightly radially striated in the wood. Odour slight; taste sweet, slightly bitter and pungent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex broad. Phloem arranged radially in a narrow-conical shape, the tip reaching nearly to the outer edge of the cortex. Cambium in a ring. Xylem rays consist of over 20 rows of cells at the interphase with the cambium; vessels arranged radially, sparsely scattered in the centre of root (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 29, 1956, 256])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-brown to blackish-brown. Starch granules rare, mainly simple, broadly ovoid, long-spheroidal or irregularly spheroidal, 4-32 $ \\mu $m in diameter; striations mostly indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Xylem ray cells subsquare, subrectangular or subpolygonal, with slightly thickened walls. Vessels mostly reticulate, 8-41 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Cork cells subsquare, subrectangular or irregular in shape, with thickened walls, usually containing yellowish-brown to reddish-brown contents (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 239])\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nSemiaquilegiae Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 404, 1742, 2048])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Semiaquilegiae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vessels 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Cambium 5. Xylem 6. Xylem ray\n\n苦地丁\n\nCorydalis Bungeanae Herba\n\n天冬\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\nSemiaquilegiae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[243, 477, 1709, 2121])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Semiaquilegiae Radix\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Xylem ray cells 3. Reticulate vessel 4. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Griffonilide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of griffonilide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nLithospermoside (griffonin) standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of lithospermoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (7:3:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately griffonilide standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), lithospermoside standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[47, 42, 235, 121])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[925, 86, 1029, 138])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 363, 803, 569])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[454, 618, 970, 987])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) griffonilide and (ii) lithospermoside (griffonin)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 1152, 896, 1976])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Semiaquilegiae Radix extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Griffonilide standard solution 2. Lithospermoside standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of griffonilide and lithospermoside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Griffonilide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (3 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.15 mg of griffonilide CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nLithospermoside (griffonin) standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (6 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of lithospermoside CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 15 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with ethanol (50%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (258 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.6 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>100</td><td>0</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>5-30</td><td>100→50</td><td>0→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>50→10</td><td>50→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of griffonilide Std-FP and lithospermoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of griffonilide and lithospermoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of griffonilide and lithospermoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from griffonilide and lithospermoside peaks should not be less than 150000 and 100000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[47, 41, 236, 122])"},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject griffonilide Std-FP, lithospermoside Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of griffonilide and lithospermoside peaks in the chromatograms of griffonilide Std-FP, lithospermoside Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify griffonilide and lithospermoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of griffonilide Std-FP and lithospermoside Std-FP. The retention times of griffonilide and lithospermoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Semiaquilegiae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Semiaquilegiae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, lithospermoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(griffonilide)</td><td>1.04</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.12</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.31</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.43</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[341, 1774, 1823, 2216])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Semiaquilegiae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 48.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 27.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed griffonilide and lithospermoside (griffonin) standard stock solution, Std-Stock (200 mg/L each) Weigh accurately 5.0 mg of griffonilide CRS and 5.0 mg of lithospermoside CRS, and dissolve in 25 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nMixed griffonilide and lithospermoside standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed griffonilide and lithospermoside Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.4, 0.8, 1.5, 3, 6 mg/L for griffonilide and 1.5, 3, 6, 12, 24 mg/L for lithospermoside.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[36, 36, 1950, 254])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/semiaquilegiae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Semiaquilegiae Radix","la":"Semiaquilegiae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sinapis-semen-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sinapis Semen v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[621, 691, 1350, 1235])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified image of seed C. Magnified image of cut surface of seed\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[642, 1696, 1337, 2279])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Brassica juncea (L.) Czern. et Coss.\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified image of seed C. Magnified image of cut surface of seed\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"22","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[621, 691, 1350, 1235])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified image of seed C. Magnified image of cut surface of seed\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[642, 1696, 1337, 2279])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried ripe seed of Brassica juncea (L.) Czern. et Coss.\n\nA. Seeds B. Magnified image of seed C. Magnified image of cut surface of seed\n\n</div>"},{"name":"22","text":"Official Name: Sinapis Semen\n\nChinese Name: 芥子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jiezi"},{"name":"22","text":"Sinapis Semen is the dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L. or Brassica juncea (L.) Czern. et Coss. (Brassicaceae). The former is known as \"Bai Jiezi\". The latter is known as \"Huang Jiezi\". The plant is collected in late summer and early autumn when the fruit is ripe, afterwards the harvested plant is dried under the sun; the seeds tapped out, foreign matter removed, then the seeds are gathered to obtain Sinapis Semen.\n\nPart I Dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L."},{"name":"22","text":"Spheroidal, 1.5-2.6 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-white to pale yellow, finely reticulated, with an obvious dot-like hilum. Testa thin and brittle; after sectioning, pale yellow folded cotyledons visible, oily. Odour slight; taste pungent [Fig. 1 (i)]."},{"name":"22","text":"Epidermal cells of testa subsquare or slightly radially elongated, containing mucilage with mucilaginous striations. Hypodermis consists of 2 layers of collenchymatous cells. Palisade cells of testa consist of 1 layer of cells with thickened inner and lateral walls and thin outer walls. Endosperm consists of 1 layer of subsquare cells, containing fatty oil droplets (before staining) and aleurone grains. Parenchymatous cells of radicle and cotyledons contain fatty oil droplets (before staining) and aleurone grains (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 24, 1956, 253])"},{"name":"22","text":"Colour yellow. Epidermal cells of testa colourless, subrounded, subsquare, polygonal or subpolygonal in surface view, cellulose column visible at centre in some cells, mucilage striations visible in periphery. Palisade cells of testa with thickened inner and lateral walls and thin outer walls in lateral view, polygonal to slightly elongated-polygonal in surface view, 6-14 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, walls thickened. Endosperm cells polygonal or rectangular in surface view, oblate to rectangular in lateral view, containing fatty oil droplets and aleurone grains. Cotyledon cells contain fatty oil droplets and aleurone grains (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\nLini Semen亞麻子\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\nSemiagilegiae Radix\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[228, 420, 1740, 2084])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermal cells containing mucilage (mucilaginous striations $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n1. Epidermis of testa 2. Hypodermis 3. Palisade cells of testa 4. Endosperm\n\n5. Radicle 6. Cotyledon\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 108])\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[295, 557, 1697, 2042])"},{"name":"22","text":"1. Epidermal cells of testa in surface view\n\n2. Palisade cells of testa (2-1 in lateral view, 2-2 in surface view)\n\n3. Endosperm cells (3-1 in surface view, 3-2 in lateral view) 4. Cotyledon cells"},{"name":"22","text":"Sinapine thiocyanate standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of sinapine thiocyanate CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"22","text":"Prepare a mixture of acetone, ethyl acetate, formic acid and water (5:3.5:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"22","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"22","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately sinapine thiocyanate standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[376, 1629, 887, 1868])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of sinapine thiocyanate\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 374, 820, 1174])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L. extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Sinapine thiocyanate standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of sinapine (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"22","text":"Sinapine thiocyanate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of sinapine thiocyanate CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"22","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"22","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (225 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>95→30</td><td>5→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"22","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of sinapine thiocyanate Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of sinapine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of sinapine peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from sinapine peak should not be less than 43000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"22","text":"Separately inject sinapine thiocyanate Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of sinapine peak in the chromatogram of sinapine thiocyanate Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify sinapine peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of sinapine thiocyanate Std-FP. The retention times of sinapine peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L. extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[37, 33, 1949, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,sinapine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.87</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[339, 930, 1802, 1660])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Sinapis alba L. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"22","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"22","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"22","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%."},{"name":"22","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sinapis-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sinapis Semen","la":"Sinapis Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sinomenii-caulis-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sinomenii Caulis v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[561, 909, 1407, 1400])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[556, 1435, 1091, 1716])\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1145, 1485, 1367, 1699])\n\nC 5mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\nA. Lianoid stem\n\nB. Magnified image of lianoid stem\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[369, 505, 1646, 1797])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\nA. Lianoid stem\n\nB. Magnified image of lianoid stem\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 16, 1959, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"18","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[561, 909, 1407, 1400])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[556, 1435, 1091, 1716])\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1145, 1485, 1367, 1699])\n\nC 5mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\nA. Lianoid stem\n\nB. Magnified image of lianoid stem\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[369, 505, 1646, 1797])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\nA. Lianoid stem\n\nB. Magnified image of lianoid stem\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of lianoid stem\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[41, 16, 1959, 255])"},{"name":"18","text":"Official name: Sinomenii Caulis\n\nChinese name: 青風藤\n\nChinese phonetic name: Qingfengteng"},{"name":"18","text":"Sinomenii Caulis is the dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson or Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson (Menispermaceae). The stem is collected in late autumn and early winter, leaves removed, cut into long sections, dried under the sun to obtain Sinomenii Caulis."},{"name":"18","text":"Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson: Long-cylindrical, somewhat curved, 21-79 cm in length, 3-21 mm in diameter. Externally greenish-brown, greyish-brown to brown or even dark green, with longitudinal striations and prominent verrucose lenticels. Nodes slightly swollen and branched. Light in weight, texture hard and fragile, easily broken; fracture surface uneven, greyish-yellow to pale greyish-brown; bark narrow, medullary rays conspicuous, arranged radially. Pith pale yellowish-white to yellowish-brown. Odour slight; taste bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nSinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson: 21-83 cm in length, 2-23 mm in diameter [Fig. 1(ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[34, 23, 1935, 258])"},{"name":"18","text":"Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of flattened rectangular cells, covered with thick cuticle. Cork sometimes existing. Cortex narrow, scattered with fibres and stone cells. Pericyclic fibre bundles in crescent shape. Stone cells several layers, inside of the pericyclic fibre bundles, tangentially elongated and linked with those of rays to a ring. Phloem narrow. Cambium distinct. Xylem broad, vessels numerous and variable in size, singly scattered or several linked up tangentially. Rays conspicuous, phloem rays gradually widened outwards, cells at the middle decadent into cracks. Cells surrounding pith rounded, walls slightly thickened, starch granules visible. Pith distinct, parenchymatous cells polygonal, walls relatively thin [Figure 2 (i)].\n\nSinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson: There is no significant difference in features as compared with Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson [Figure 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson: Colour yellowish-brown or greyish-brown. Cortex fibres pale yellow to yellow, with heavily thickened walls and narrow lumen; weak white under the polarized microscope. Stone cells singly or in groups with various sizes, 45-725 $ \\mu m $ in length, 18-121 $ \\mu m $ in width; subsquare, fusiform, elliptical or irregular in shape; with thickened and pitted walls; pit canals distinct; weak white under the polarized microscope. Cork cells polygonal, slightly thickened and lignified. Epidermal cells sub-rectangular or oblong in surface view, covered with a thick granular cuticle. Pericyclic fibres singly or in bundles, 3-30 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with slightly thickened walls, lumen narrow; bright polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Xylem fibres numerous, 7-49 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, mostly broken, with heavily thickened walls, lignified; weak white under the polarized microscope. Medullary ray cells square or rectangular, with thick, lignified and pitted walls. Vessels large, mostly bordered-pitted. Raphides of calcium oxalate numerous, fine, 1-12 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope [Figure 3 (i)].\n\nSinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson: Stone cells 43-770 $ \\mu $m in length, 24-129 $ \\mu $m in width. Pericyclic fibres 4-31 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Xylem fibres 7-52 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Raphides of calcium oxalate 1-11 $ \\mu $m in diameter [Figure 3 (ii)].\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁 菊花\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 445, 1690, 2074])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. D. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Pericyclic fibre 5. Stone cell\n\n6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Ray 10. Cells surrounding pith\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[240, 455, 1684, 2056])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H. Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. D. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cork 3. Cortex 4. Pericyclic fibre 5. Stone cell\n\n6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Ray 10. Cells surrounding pith\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷\n\nAngelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁 菊花\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[244, 386, 1736, 2119])"},{"name":"18","text":"1. Cortex fibre 2. Stone cells 3. Cork cells\n\n4. Epidermal cells (in surface view) 5. Pericyclic fibres 6. Xylem fibres\n\n7. Medullary ray cells 8. Bordered-pitted vessel 9. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[252, 409, 1729, 2105])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"18","text":"1. Cortex fibre 2. Stone cells 3. Cork cells 4. Epidermal cells (in surface view)\n\n5. Pericyclic fibres 6. Xylem fibres 7. Medullary ray cells\n\n8. Bordered-pitted vessel 9. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[44, 33, 1944, 254])"},{"name":"18","text":"Magnoflorine iodide standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of magnoflorine iodide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nSinomenine standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of sinomenine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 50% )."},{"name":"18","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (7:4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"18","text":"Iodine."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"18","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately magnoflorine iodide standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), sinomenine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Expose the plate to iodine vapor in a chamber for about 20-30 min until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤 Sinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum苦杏仁\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[376, 378, 733, 723])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[379, 845, 690, 1196])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) magnoflorine iodide and (ii) sinomenine\n\n</div>\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤 Sinomenii Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[1401, 198, 1477, 256])\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[380, 376, 1009, 1192])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Sinomenii Caulis extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Sinomenine standard solution 2. Magnoflorine iodide standard solution\n\n3. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\n(ii) dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of magnoflorine and sinomenine (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[39, 35, 1940, 258])"},{"name":"18","text":"0. 06 M Sodium 1-heptanesulphonate solution\n\nWeigh 6.06 g of sodium 1-heptanesulphonate and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\n0. 06 M Potassium dihydrogen phosphate solution\n\nWeigh 4.08 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate and dissolve in 500 mL of water.\n\nSodium 1-heptanesulphonate-potassium dihydrogen phosphate buffer solution (pH3)\n\nTransfer 500 mL of 0.06 M sodium 1-heptanesulphonate solution and 500 mL of 0.06 M potassium dihydrogen phosphate solution to a 1000-mL conical flask. Adjust the pH to 3 with phosphoric acid."},{"name":"18","text":"Magnoflorine iodide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of magnoflorine iodide CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nSinomenine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of sinomenine CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"18","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 7 mL of ethanol (50%). Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Transfer the extract to a 10-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction two more times. Wash the residue with ethanol (50%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"18","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (221 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min.\n\nProgramme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[28, 24, 1911, 255])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Sodium1-heptanesulphonate-potassium dihydrogenphosphate buffer solution(pH3)(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-8</td><td>84</td><td>16</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>8-12</td><td>84→83</td><td>16→17</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>12-15</td><td>83→80</td><td>17→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-20</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>20-21</td><td>80→78</td><td>20→22</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>21-25</td><td>78→40</td><td>22→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"18","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of magnoflorine iodide Std-FP and sinomenine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of magnoflorine and sinomenine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of magnoflorine and sinomenine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from magnoflorine and sinomenine peaks should not be less than 8000 and 7000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"18","text":"Separately inject magnoflorine iodide Std-FP, sinomenine Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of magnoflorine and sinomenine peaks in the chromatograms of magnoflorine iodide Std-FP, sinomenine Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify magnoflorine and sinomenine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of magnoflorine iodide Std-FP and sinomenine Std-FP. The retention times of magnoflorine and sinomenine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Sinomenii Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[39, 9, 1924, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Sinomenii Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, sinomenine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(magnoflorine)</td><td>1.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.99</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[332, 822, 1821, 1369])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[337, 1596, 1821, 2149])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried lianoid stem of Sinomenium acutum (Thunb.) Rehder & E.H.Wilson var. cinereum (Diels) Rehder & E.H.Wilson extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[45, 39, 1917, 251])"},{"name":"18","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 6.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sinomenii-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sinomenii Caulis","la":"Sinomenii Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/smilacis-glabrae-rhizoma-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[475, 844, 1522, 1741])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[475, 844, 1522, 1741])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 6, 1928, 258])"},{"name":"10","text":"Official Name: Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 土茯苓\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tufuling"},{"name":"10","text":"Smilacis Glabra Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Smilax glabra Roxb. (Liliaceae). The rhizome is collected in summer or autumn. After fibrous roots have been removed, the rhizome is washed clean, then dried to yield the intact form. Alternatively, the rhizome is sliced while fresh, and dried immediately to obtain the sliced form of Smilacis Glabra Rhizoma."},{"name":"10","text":"Subcylindrical, slightly flattened or irregularly strip-shaped, with knob-like outgrowths and short branches, 2-26 cm long, 20-80 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown, uneven, with stiff remains of fibrous roots, and with rounded bud scars at the top. Some outer bark irregularly fissured, fallen off easily and exhibiting remains of scales. Texture hard, uneasily broken, fracture whitish to pale reddish-brown, starchy; dotted vascular bundles and numerous small light spots visible, frequently distributed near the centre. Fracture texture slightly tough, dusting on breaking, viscous and slippery when moistened. Odourless; taste slightly sweet and astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Epidermis consists of 3-10 layers of cells, yellowish-brown, arranged densely, the wall relatively thick, slightly lignified. Cortex scattered large mucilage cells, containing raphides of calcium oxalate in mucilage cells. Parenchymatous cells radially elongated in vascular cylinder, collateral vascular bundles scattered, relatively densely near centre. Xylem usually contains 2 large vessels and numbers of small vessels; fibres occasionally visible outside of phloem. Parenchymatous cells contain numerous starch granules (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 8, 1983, 263])"},{"name":"10","text":"Colour pale brown. Starch granules abundant, simple granules subrounded, polygonal or subsquare, 9-49 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, hilum cleft, stellate, Y-shaped or pointed, striations visible in large granules; compound granules composed of 2-4 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Stone cells abundant, subsquare, subellipitic or triangular, 24-126 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with fine and close pit canals; deep brown stone cell long-striped, walls heavily thickened on the three sides and thin on one side. Raphides of calcium oxalate clustered in bundles or scattered singly, 40-133 $ \\mu m $ long; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Mainly bordered-pitted vessels and tracheids, pits mostly horizontal extension, reticulate vessels occasionally visible. Fibres few, scattered singly, with extremely thick walls, pit distinct, lumina narrow to form slit-shaped, 11-53 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"10","text":"Astilbin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of astilbin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nDeveloping solvent system\n\nPrepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, water and formic acid (10:0.8:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 15 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately astilbin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[23, 1, 1929, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[246, 412, 1745, 1836])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Raphides of calcium oxalate in mucilage cell 1. Epidermis 2. Mucilage cells 3. Starch granules 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Raphides of calcium oxalate\n\n</div>\n\nOrpiment\n\n輕粉 Calomelas\n\nCurculiginis Rhizoma\n\n雄黄\n\nRealgar\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 47, 1984, 88])\n\n墨旱蓮\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba 半枝蓮\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n五味子\n\nSchisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\nSmilacis Glabrae Rhizoma\n\n板蓝根\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[249, 419, 1721, 1898])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Compound starch granules 3. Subsquare stone cell 4. Dark brown stone cell\n\n5. Subellipitic stone cell 6. Raphides of calcium oxalate bundles 7. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n8. Reticulate vessel 9. Fibre\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[39, 0, 1930, 262])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of astilbin.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[380, 558, 968, 918])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of astilbin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"10","text":"Astilbin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 5.0 mg of astilbin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (70%)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (320 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>82→78</td><td>18→22</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-40</td><td>78→60</td><td>22→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of astilbin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of astilbin should\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 5, 1978, 264])\n\nnot be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of astilbin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from astilbin peak should not be less than 15000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject astilbin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of astilbin peak in the chromatogram of astilbin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify astilbin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of astilbin Std-FP. The retention times of astilbin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,astilbin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.77</td><td>±0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 1910, 1323, 2449])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[21, 7, 1927, 260])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Total ash: not more than 2.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"10","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/smilacis-glabrae-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma","la":"Smilacis Glabrae Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sophorae-flavescentis-radix-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sophorae Flavescentis Radix v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=1,bbox=[26, 6, 1929, 259])"},"sections":[{"name":"11","text":"![](page=1,bbox=[26, 6, 1929, 259])"},{"name":"11","text":"Official Name: Sophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\nChinese Name: 苦参\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Kushen"},{"name":"11","text":"Sophora Flavescentis Radix is the dried root of Sophora flavescens Ait. (Fabaceae). The root is collected in spring or autumn, removed the top parts, rootlets and soil, washed with water, then dried to yield the intact form. Alternatively, the root is sliced while fresh, and dried immediately to obtain the sliced form of Sophora Flavescentis Radix."},{"name":"11","text":"Cylindrical, usually branched on the lower part, 10-60 cm long, 5-25 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown to brown, with distinct longitudinal wrinkles and laterally extended lenticels; bark thin, usually cracked, rolled outwards and fallen off. Texture hard, uneasily broken, with somewhat fibrous surface. Transversely cut slice rounded or oblique in shape, 2-10 mm thick, 10-60 mm in diameter, externally pale-yellow, with radial striations and clefts; sometimes hetero-vascular bundle outline appears as a concentric annular ring or irregularly scattered. Odour slight; taste very bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"11","text":"Cork occasionally removed, consisting of several layers of cells. Cortex narrow. Fibre bundles scattered in phloem, surrounded by parenchymatous cells with prisms of calcium oxalate, appearing as crystal fibres. Phloem rays broad. Cambium distinct, arranged in an interrupted ring. Xylem branches into 2-4 lines outwards; vessels arranged radially; crystal fibres in bundles. Xylem ray distinct and broad. Parenchymatous cells contain numerous starch granules and prisms of calcium oxalate (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[12, 13, 1984, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"Colour pale brown. Simple starch granules subglobular or ellipsoid, 0.5-35 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with cleft hilum; compound granules mostly composed of 2-5 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Fibres numerous, mostly in bundles, with thickened and slightly lignified wall, surrounded by parenchymatous cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate, appearing as crystal fibres. Prisms of calcium oxalate biconical, double rhombic or polyhedral; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels bordered-pitted, occasionally reticulate, 4-109 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Parenchymatous cells subrounded or subrectangular, sometimes lignified in uneven beaded shape; containing starch granules. Cork cells subpolygonal, with irregular slight cracks on the surface (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"11","text":"Matrine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of matrine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nOxymatrine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of oxymatrine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol.\n\nSophoridine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of sophoridine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"11","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), water, ethanol and ethyl acetate (1.5:1:3.5:15, v/v)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of iodine and 10.0 g of potassium iodide, and dissolve in 50 mL of water. Heat and add 2 mL of glacial acetic acid. Transfer the solution to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 4.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 15 mL of ethyl acetate and 0.3 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 1, 1928, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[241, 440, 1732, 1870])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Sophorae Flavescentis Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Crystal fibres bundle\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Phloem ray 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Xylem ray\n\n8. Vessels 9. Fibre bundles\n\nCurculiginis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[878, 43, 984, 129])\n\nHouttuyniae Herba\n\n魚腥草\n\n前胡 Peucedani Radix\n\n墨旱莲\n\nEcliptae Herba\n\n蛇床子\n\nCnidii Fructus\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1892, 46, 1984, 86])\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\nScutellariae Barbatae Herba\n\n半枝蓮\n\nSchisandrae Chinensis Fructus\n\nSophorae Flavescentis Radix\n\n板蓝根\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[297, 501, 825, 893])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[847, 505, 1368, 893])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1396, 502, 1677, 890])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[297, 941, 825, 1329])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[849, 942, 1369, 1334])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1398, 941, 1687, 1331])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[301, 1365, 823, 1753])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[860, 1362, 1258, 1754])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1291, 1364, 1692, 1758])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"11","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Crystal fibres 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Bordered-pitted vessels\n\n5. Lignified parenchymatous cells 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[20, 3, 1926, 259])"},{"name":"11","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately matrine standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), oxymatrine standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), sophoridine standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of matrine, oxymatrine and sophoridine.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[461, 1328, 662, 1614])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[458, 1723, 663, 2087])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[395, 2185, 660, 2472])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) matrine (ii) oxymatrine and (iii) sophoridine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[12, 16, 1975, 263])"},{"name":"11","text":"Matrine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (40 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of matrine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (30%).\n\nOxymatrine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (800 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 8.0 mg of oxymatrine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (30%)."},{"name":"11","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 15 mL of ethanol (30%) and 0.5 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for two more times. Wash the residue with ethanol (30%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with ethanol (30%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"11","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of acetonitrile and 0.3% phosphoric acid with 0.3% triethylamine (4:96, v/v). The elution time is about 20 min."},{"name":"11","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of matrine Std-FP and oxymatrine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of matrine and oxymatrine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of matrine and oxymatrine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from matrine and oxymatrine peaks should not be less than 6500 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"Separately inject matrine Std-FP, oxymatrine Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of matrine and oxymatrine peaks in the chromatograms of matrine Std-FP, oxymatrine Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify matrine and oxymatrine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of matrine Std-FP and oxymatrine Std-FP.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 3, 1930, 260])\n\nThe retention times of matrine and oxymatrine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Sophorae Flavescentis Radix extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Sophorae Flavescentis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(matrine)</td><td>0.57</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(oxysophoridine)</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker, oxymatrine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 1198, 1798, 1765])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Sophorae Flavescentis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"11","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[29, 20, 1976, 264])"},{"name":"11","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"11","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sophorae-flavescentis-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sophorae Flavescentis Radix","la":"Sophorae Flavescentis Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sophorae-fructus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sophorae Fructus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"B 0.5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[520, 1193, 1428, 1846])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sophorae Fructus A. Sophorae Fructus B. Seeds and cotyledons\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1899, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"B 0.5 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[520, 1193, 1428, 1846])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sophorae Fructus A. Sophorae Fructus B. Seeds and cotyledons\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1899, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Sophorae Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 槐角\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Huaijiao"},{"name":"12","text":"Sophora Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Sophora japonica L. (Fabaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in winter, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Sophorae Fructus."},{"name":"12","text":"Beaded, 1-8 cm long, 6-10 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-green to yellowish-brown, shrunken and rough, the side of one dorsal suture yellow. Texture soft, shrunken after drying, easily broken at the collapsed part; fracture yellowish-green, viscous. Seeds 1-6, reniform, 6-11 mm long, surface smooth, black to brownish-black; hilum at the concave side round, greyish-white; texture hard, cotyledons 2, yellow to yellowish-green. Odour of pericarp slight; taste bitter. Seeds taste bean-like on chewing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of rectangular epidermal cells, covered with cuticle. Mesocarp consists of parenchymatous cells in various layers, occasionally more than 30 layers, scattered with prisms of calcium oxalate. The cells of the outer layers and inner layers of mesocarp arranged closely, parenchymatous cells in the middle part relatively large, with small groups of stone cells and vascular bundles found scattered opposite the seed hilum. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of small subrectangular parenchymatous cells, prolonged tangentially. Testa consists of 1 layer of palisade cells in outer side, with strongly lignified wall, beneath showing 1 layer of beaker cells, and several layers of parenchymatous cells in inner side. Oval tracheid island located inside the cavity of the seed hilum, consisting of many scalariform and reticulate tracheids. Stellate tissue present in both sides of the tracheid island. Cotyledon surrounded by endosperm cells (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-brown to brown. Epidermal cells of exocarp polygonal in the surface view, wall slightly thickened, stomata actinocytic visible. Mesocarp cells elliptical or elongated. Prisms of calcium oxalate usually present in mesocarp and parenchymatous cells, diamond-shaped or columnar, 4-24 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Palisade cells of testa colourless to brown, columnar and neatly arranged in lateral view, subrounded in bottom or surface view. Beaker cells of testa dumbbell-shaped and subrectangular in lateral view, subrounded in surface view. Stone cells yellow to greenish-yellow, varied in shape, subrectangular, subrounded, triangular or shell-shaped. Cotyledon cells contain oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[34, 99, 209, 198])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[341, 449, 877, 958])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[356, 988, 866, 1330])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[981, 441, 1656, 950])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[356, 1329, 863, 1640])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[356, 1644, 866, 1989])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[984, 1122, 1664, 1615])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[979, 1758, 1281, 1984])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nE\n\n</div>\n\n25 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1310, 1782, 1516, 1985])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nF\n\n</div>\n\n25 μm\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Sophorae Fructus\n\nA. Sketch B. Pericarp section illustration C. Pericarp section ( near seed hilum ) illustration D. Seed section illustration E. Prisms of calcium oxalate F. Stellate tissue\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Mesocarp 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Endocarp 6. Testa 7. Beaker cells 8. Parenchyma of testa\n\n9. Endosperm 10. Cotyledon 11. Tracheid island 12. Stellate tissue 13. Stone cells\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Sophorae Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[398, 451, 1729, 2031])"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Exocarp with an actinocytic type stoma\n\n2. Mesocarp with prisms of calcium oxalate 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate in parenchymatous cells\n\n4. Palisade cells and beaker cells of testa (in lateral view)\n\n5. Palisade cells, beaker cells and parenchymatous cells of testa (in lateral view)\n\n6. Palisade cells of testa (in surface view)\n\n7. Beaker cells (in surface view) 8. Stone cells 9. Cotyledon cells\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 8, 1902, 261])"},{"name":"12","text":"Sophoricoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of sophoricoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, ethanol and acetic acid (7.5:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately sophoricoside standard solution and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of sophoricoside.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[377, 1673, 1173, 1996])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of sophoricoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[68, 12, 1965, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Sophoricoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of sophoricoside CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Pipette 1 mL of the solution to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (260 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile:Methanol(50:50，v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-60</td><td>85→70</td><td>15→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of sophoricoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of sophoricoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of sophoricoside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from sophoricoside peak should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject sophoricoside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of sophoricoside peak in the chromatogram of sophoricoside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[33, 4, 1907, 266])\n\npeaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify sophoricoside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of sophoricoside Std-FP. The retention times of sophoricoside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sophorae Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sophorae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.62</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(rutin)</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,sophoricoside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[393, 1376, 1720, 2004])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Sophorae Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 14, 1964, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 43.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 45.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sophorae-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sophorae Fructus","la":"Sophorae Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sparganii-rhizoma-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sparganii Rhizoma v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[623, 847, 1342, 1899])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sparganii Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sparganii Rhizoma\n\nB. Cut surface of rhizome"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[623, 847, 1342, 1899])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Sparganii Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sparganii Rhizoma\n\nB. Cut surface of rhizome"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Sparganii Rhizoma\n\nChinese Name: 三棱\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Sanleng"},{"name":"12","text":"Sparganii Rhizoma is the dried rhizome of Sparganium stoloniferum Buch.-Ham. (Typhaceae). The rhizome is collected from winter to the next spring, washed clean, partly peeled, then dried under the sun to obtain Sparganii Rhizoma."},{"name":"12","text":"Conical or obovate, slightly flattened, 2-8 cm long, 20-45 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to greyish-yellow, with marks pared with a knife, fibrous root scars small, densely spotted, slightly annulated arranged transversely. Texture hard, compact and heavy in weight. Fracture greyish-yellow to pale brown, slightly flattened, with numerous scattered spots. Odour slight; taste bland, slightly numb and pungent on chewing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cortex broken, the remnants of the cortex consists of irregular parenchymatous cells, intercellular spaces relatively large. Endodermis consists of 1 layer of tangentially elongated cells, densely arranged, inner and lateral walls of some cells thickened. Parenchymatous cells of stele subrounded or subpolygonal, walls slightly thickened, containing starch granules. Secretory cells scattered in cortex and stele, containing brownish-red contents. Collateral and amphivasal vascular bundles scattered (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 29, 1957, 252])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-white. Starch granules numerous, simple starch granules subrounded, subpolygonal or elliptic, 2-10 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, dotted or slit-shaped hilum slightly visible in bigger granules; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules mostly composed of 2-3 units. Secretory cells contain reddish-brown contents. Parenchymatous cells subrectangular, long elliptical or irregular in shape, walls beaded, slightly lignified. Fibres usually in bundles, walls relatively thick, slightly lignified or lignified, with sparse and oblique pits. Asteroidal parenchymatous cells occasionally visible, usually connected with sclerenchymatous cells, irregular in shape (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 239])\n\n天葵子\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[245, 417, 1735, 2120])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Sparganii Rhizoma\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle (collateral type)\n\n1. Remnants of cortex 2. Endodermis 3. Secretory cell 4. Vascular bundle (amphivasal type)\n\n5. Vascular bundle (collateral type) 6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Starch granule\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1025, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1757, 52, 1911, 107])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[293, 477, 987, 998])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1005, 480, 1685, 995])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[297, 1018, 987, 1473])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1007, 1022, 1685, 1473])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[297, 1501, 986, 1958])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1006, 1494, 1685, 1956])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Secretory cells (->) 3. Lignified parenchymatous cells 4. Fibres\n\n5. Asteroidal parenchymatous cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\beta $ - Sitosterol standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.5 mg of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ $ \\beta $ -Sitosterol glucoside standard solution\n\nWeigh 4.5 mg of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $"},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $) and methanol (5:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately $ \\beta $ -sitosterol standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 105^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[442, 366, 1006, 746])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[453, 854, 1167, 1301])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and (ii) $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 1422, 994, 2215])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n1 2 3\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Sparganii Rhizoma extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. $ \\beta $ -Sitosterol glucoside standard solution 2. $ \\beta $ -Sitosterol standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"$ \\beta $ -Sitosterol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (130 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.3 mg of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $\n\n$ \\beta $ -Sitosterol glucoside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (110 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.1 mg of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol. Keep at about $ 4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 5.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 250-mL round-bottomed flask. Wash the residue with methanol. Combine the solutions. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the solutions. Evaporate the solvent to dryness below $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol. Transfer the solution to a 5-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $; nebulizer gas $ \\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right) $ pressure: 3.5 bar] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with OS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-50</td><td>85→100</td><td>15→0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>100</td><td>0</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol Std-FP and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside peaks should not be less than 30000 and 15000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[36, 36, 1950, 256])\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject $ \\beta $ -sitosterol Std-FP, $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol Std-FP, $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol Std-FP and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside Std-FP. The retention times of $ \\beta $ -sitosterol and $ \\beta $ -sitosterol glucoside peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sparganii Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Sparganii Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.68</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(β-sitosterol glucoside)</td><td>0.73</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.94</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker,β-sitosterol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.49</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[358, 1828, 1795, 2520])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Sparganii Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[45, 34, 1958, 260])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/sparganii-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sparganii Rhizoma","la":"Sparganii Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/spatholobi-caulis-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Spatholobi Caulis v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Figure 1 A photograph of Spatholobi Caulis A. Spatholobi Caulis B. Oblique slices C. Magnified transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1901, 264])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"Figure 1 A photograph of Spatholobi Caulis A. Spatholobi Caulis B. Oblique slices C. Magnified transverse section\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1901, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Spatholobi Caulis\n\nChinese Name: 雞血藤\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Jixueteng"},{"name":"12","text":"Spatholobi Caulis is the dried lianoid stem of Spatholobus suberectus Dunn (Fabaceae). The stem is collected in autumn and winter. After branches and leaves removed, the stem is cut into slices and dried under the sun to obtain Spatholobi Caulis."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical, slightly twisted, 30-90 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-white to greyish-brown, appearing reddish-brown when the cork exfoliated. Texture compact and hard. Oblique slices elliptical, oblong or irregular, 2-5 mm thick, xylem brownish-yellow to reddish-brown, showing numerous pores; phloem with resinous secretion, reddish-brown to blackish-brown, arranged alternately with xylem, forming 2-10 eccentric semi-circular or circular rings; pith inclined to one side. Odour slight; taste astringent (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of rectangular cells. Cortex relatively narrow; groups of stone cells present in cortex, with brownish-red contents in the lumen; some parenchymatous cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Phloem arranged alternately with xylem in several whorls forming abnormal vascular bundles. Sclerenchymatous cell layer present at the outer side of\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 9, 1974, 260])\n\nphloem, consisting of 1 to several layers of stone cells. Phloem rays often compressed; secretory cells numerous, often several in groups, containing brownish-red contents; phloem fibre bundles abundant, surrounded by cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres. In the xylem, the vessels usually scattered singly or 2-3 in groups; bundles of xylem fibres also form crystal fibres, and cells of xylem rays often filled with brownish-red contents. Pith relatively small, consisting of parenchymatous cells, some of which are filled with brownish-red contents (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brownish-red. Stone cells subsquare, subrectangular, suborbicular or polygonal, 14-81 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with distinct pits, pit canals and striations, sometimes filled with brownish-red contents; bright yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered or present in sclerenchymatous cells, 4-26 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres mainly in bundles, surrounded by cells containing prisms of calcium oxalate, forming crystal fibres; single fibres slender, 4-31 $ \\mu m $ in diameter; yellowishwhite or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Secretory cells contain brownish-red contents. Cork cells polygonal in surface view, with slit-shaped pits. Clumps of resinous secretion yellowish-brown to reddish-brown, stick-shaped or irregular in shape. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 18-394 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, often broken into fragments (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[31, 3, 1908, 266])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 402, 1749, 2170])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Spatholobi Caulis\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells in cortex D. Crystal fibres in xylem\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Prism of calcium oxalate 4. Stone cell 5. Sclerenchymatous cell layer\n\n6. Phloem 7. Phloem fibre 8. Secretory cell 9. Xylem 10. Pith 11. Phloem ray\n\n12. Xylem fibre 13. Xylem ray\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[271, 577, 1706, 2180])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Spatholobi Caulis\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Prisms of calcium oxalate in sclerenchymatous cells\n\n3. Fibres (3-1 crystal fibres, 3-2 single fibre) 4. Secretory cell 5. Cork cells\n\n6. Clump of resinous secretion 7. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 6, 1903, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Formonetin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of formonetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane and methanol (30:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 40 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 10 mL of water. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL centrifuge tube. Add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{254} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately formonetin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of formonetin.\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nSpatholobi Caulis Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[382, 369, 989, 738])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[378, 766, 992, 1015])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) (-)-epicatechin and (ii) formonetin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Formonetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (2 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of formonetin CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (60%). Centrifuge at about $ 3 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with methanol (60%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[28, 3, 1906, 266])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>10-60</td><td>60→40</td><td>40→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of formonetin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of formonetin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of formonetin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from formonetin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject formonetin Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of formonetin peak in the chromatogram of formonetin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify formonetin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of formonetin Std-FP. The retention times of formonetin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Spatholobi Caulis extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 6, 1968, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Spatholobi Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.40</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.74</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(marker,formonetin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[290, 972, 1843, 1590])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Spatholobi Caulis extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[39, 13, 1899, 266])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%.\n\nWater-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/spatholobi-caulis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Spatholobi Caulis","la":"Spatholobi Caulis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/spica-prunellae-v3-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Spica Prunellae v3 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[469, 1014, 1506, 1680])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1939, 253])"},"sections":[{"name":"9","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[469, 1014, 1506, 1680])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 0, 1939, 253])"},{"name":"9","text":"Official Name: Spica Prunellae\n\nChinese Name: 夏枯草\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Xiakucao"},{"name":"9","text":"Spica Prunellae is the dried fruiting spike (infructescence) of Prunella vulgaris L. (Lamiaceae). The herb is collected in summer when the spike becomes brownish-red, the infructescence cut off, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Spica Prunellae."},{"name":"9","text":"Baculiform or rod-shaped, slightly flattened, 1.5-7.5 cm in length, 8-13 mm in diameter, externally pale brown to brownish-red, at base subtended by remnant of peduncle of various lengths. The spike is composed of up to 10 or more whorls of calyces and bracts, each whorl with two opposite bracts. Bract fan-shaped, acuminate apically, vein-striations distinct, the outer surface with white hairs; each bract subtends three flowers, their corollas often fallen off. Calyces bilabiate, enclosing four small brown ovoid nutlets, white-convex at the acute end. Texture light, odour slight, taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"9","text":"Colour brownish-red. Numerous multicellular, non-glandular hairs, usually broken into fragments; unbroken ones consist of 2-12 cells, 74-1250 $ \\mu $ m in length, 31-160 $ \\mu $ m in width at base. Unicellular non-glandular hairs abundant, subuliform, 23-84 $ \\mu $ m in length, 18-66 $ \\mu $ m in width at base. Glandular hairs are found in two forms; bicellular head with unicellular stalk and unicellular head with bicellular stalk. Head of glandular scale 44-74 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, consisting of 4 secretory cells. Upper epidermis of calyx heterocellular, the anticlinal cell wall deeply and undulately curved, and thickened, with distinct pits, the cell contents brown. Lower epidermis of calyx heterocellular, the cell wall slightly and undulately curved, with scattered diacytic stomata. Mesocarp cells polygonal, containing sandy crystals of calcium oxalate. Epidermal cells of testa polygonal, some cell wall with thickened reticular vein (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[23, 1, 1965, 247])"},{"name":"9","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS (Fig. 3) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"9","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate, isopropanol and formic acid (7:3:2:0.3, v/v)."},{"name":"9","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 2.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 30 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 5 mL of ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately rosmarinic acid standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of rosmarinic acid.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[102, 7, 207, 60])\n\nHerba Taxilli\n\n葛根 Radix Puerariae Lobatae\n\n補骨脂\n\nFructus Psoraleae\n\nFructus Ligustri Lucidi\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[861, 23, 1087, 116])\n\n女貞子\n\n浙貝母\n\nRadix Ophiopogonis\n\nSpica Prunellae\n\nRadix Puerariae Thomsonii\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Thunbergii\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1670, 11, 1870, 83])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[948, 178, 1064, 239])\n\nHerba Andrographidis\n\n穿心蓮\n\n益母草\n\nHerba Leonuri\n\n平贝母\n\nBulbus Fritillariae Ussuriensis\n\nFructus Evodiae\n\n吴茱萸\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[336, 455, 952, 937])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[992, 452, 1343, 689])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1393, 451, 1645, 685])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[991, 715, 1343, 937])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1392, 716, 1640, 931])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[335, 965, 951, 1417])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[981, 964, 1644, 1420])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[333, 1443, 756, 1837])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[769, 1448, 1207, 1833])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1224, 1440, 1647, 1838])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"9","text":"1. Multicellular non-glandular hair 2. Unicellular non-glandular hair\n\n3. Glandular hair: bicellular head with unicellular stalk\n\n4. Glandular hair: unicellular head with bicellular stalk\n\n5. Glandular scale 6. Upper epidermal cells of caylx\n\n7. Stomata embedded in a lower epidermal cells of calyx\n\n8. Mesocarp cells 9. Epidermal cells of testa\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n北沙参\n\nRadix Glehniae\n\n太子参 Radix Pseudostellariae\n\n黄芩\n\nRadix Scutellariae\n\nSemen Vaccariae\n\n王不留行\n\n地黄\n\nRadix Rehmannia\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[600, 465, 1366, 707])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Chemical structure of rosmarinic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"9","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"9","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"9","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (280 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.05%Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>90→70</td><td>10→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-35</td><td>70→65</td><td>30→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>35-50</td><td>65→25</td><td>35→75</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>25→10</td><td>75→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"9","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of rosmarinic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of rosmarinic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from rosmarinic acid peak should not be less than 70000 theoretical plates.\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 0, 1939, 254])\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"9","text":"Separately inject rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of rosmarinic acid peak in the chromatogram of rosmarinic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify rosmarinic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of rosmarinic acid Std-FP. The retention times of rosmarinic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Spica Prunellae extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Spica Prunellae extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.55</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,rosmarinic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[395, 1669, 1752, 2488])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Spica Prunellae extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[30, 0, 1965, 251])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"9","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XV): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 10.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.5%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X): not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 10.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"9","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"9","text":"Rosmarinic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 5.0 mg of rosmarinic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nRosmarinic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the rosmarinic acid Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (50%) to produce a series of solutions of 0.1, 1, 10, 50, 200 mg/L for rosmarinic acid.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[38, 0, 1938, 252])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/spica-prunellae","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Spica Prunellae","la":"Spica Prunellae"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/stellariae-radix-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Stellariae Radix v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[375, 821, 1587, 1948])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Stellariae Radix\n\nA. Stellariae Radix\n\nD. Magnified image of \"Zhenzhupan\" E. Magnified image of \"Shayan\" ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nF. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\nG. Magnified image of transverse section of root (cultivar)"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[375, 821, 1587, 1948])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Stellariae Radix\n\nA. Stellariae Radix\n\nD. Magnified image of \"Zhenzhupan\" E. Magnified image of \"Shayan\" ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nF. Magnified image of transverse section of root\n\nG. Magnified image of transverse section of root (cultivar)"},{"name":"12","text":"Official name: Stellariae Radix\n\nChinese name: 銀柴胡\n\nChinese phonetic name: Yinchaihu"},{"name":"12","text":"Stellariae Radix is the dried root of Stellaria dichotoma L. var. lanceolata Bunge. (Caryophyllaceae). The root is collected in spring and summer when the plant sprouts, or after autumn when stem and leaves wither; cultivated Stellariae Radix is collected in the middle of September of the third year, or in the middle of April of the fourth year of cultivation. The remnant stem, rootlets and soil are removed from the root, then dried under the sun to obtain Stellariae Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Cylindrical to subcylindrical, occasionally branched but broken, 8-26 cm in length, 4-16 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown with occasionally twisted longitudinal wrinkles, scattered with pored depressions of lateral rootlets scars customarily called \"Shayan\" (sand hole). The rootstock slightly swollen, with dense warty protuberances of bud, stem or rhizome traces customarily called \"Zhenzhupan\" (pearl disk). Texture hard and fragile, easily broken, fracture uneven, cracked and relatively loose, bark fairly thin, with alternating radial striations of yellow xylem and white rays. Odour slight; taste sweet.\n\nCultivars usually branched, mostly twisted in the lower part. Externally pale yellowish-brown to pale brownish-yellow, with fine and distinct longitudinal wrinkles, rootlets scars \"Shayan\" mostly punctiform dented, small and hardly observed. Rootstock warty protuberance \"Zhenzhupan\" relatively small. Fracture relatively compact, almost without clefts, slightly powderly, yellow-and-white alternating radial striations indistinct. Odour slight, taste sweetish (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[20, 12, 1973, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several to more than 10 layers of cells, yellowish-brown, flat-rectangular or subsquare in shape. Cortex relatively narrow, consisting of 4-8 layers of tangentially elongated, subrectangular or round-rectangular parenchymatous cells. Phloem narrow, occupying about 1/10 of the root; parenchamatous cells polygonal or subsquare in shape. Sieve tube groups sparsely scattered in phloem. Cambium with indistinct interfascicular cambium and distinct intrafascicular cambium; cells small and rectangular in shape, arranged tangentially in an interrupted ring. Xylem developed, occupying most of the root; vessels somehow arranged radially. Rays composed of 5-20 rows of parenchymatous cells. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate abundant, scattered in parenchymatous cells, mainly in rays. Clusters of calcium oxalate in rosette aggregated, occurring in parenchymatous cells, mainly in rays (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour brownish-yellow. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate abundant, scattered or densely filled inside parenchymatous cells; triangular, arrow-headed, subsquare, cuneate, rhombic or irregular in shape, 2-9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; whitish or yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Clusters of calcium oxalate abundant, usually scattered, occasionally found with microcrystals in parenchymatous cells, 11-74 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, in rosette aggregate form with numerous fine angles; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Reticulate vessels pale yellow to yellow, relatively slender, with large pits and perforations on the lateral walls, 9-99 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Cork cells pale yellow, rectangular in lateral view, polygonal in surface view, walls slightly lignified and thin, somewhat sinuous (Fig. 3).\n\nRemarks: All samples share the same features described above irrespective of being wild or cultivar samples.\n\n枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸隶仁\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[300, 674, 869, 1132])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[315, 1201, 868, 1486])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[318, 1557, 868, 1833])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[967, 676, 1679, 1825])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Stellariae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Section magnified (under the light microscope) D. Section magnified (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Sieve tube group 5. Cambium\n\n6. Xylem 7. Ray 8. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate\n\n9. Cluster of calcium oxalate\n\n---\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n酸隶仁\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[303, 547, 965, 985])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1023, 550, 1686, 983])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[304, 1044, 966, 1480])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1017, 1042, 1676, 1473])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[305, 1533, 965, 1967])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1019, 1536, 1677, 1967])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 2. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n3. Reticulate vessels 4. Cork cells (4-1 lateral view, 4-2 surface view)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[23, 26, 1952, 259])"},{"name":"12","text":"Dichotomine B standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of dichotomine B CRS (Fig. 4) and place it in a 5-mL volumetric flask, then add 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Sonicate (220 W) the solution for 5 min. Make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% ). Pipette 0.5 mL of the solution to a 1-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, water, ethanol and formic acid (5:2:1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 45 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Wash the residue with methanol (70%) and combine the extracts. Make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately dichotomine B standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 368, 767, 657])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of dichotomine B\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[377, 894, 753, 1730])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Stellariae Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm) 1. Dichotomine B standard solution 2. Test solution\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of dichotomine B (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 18, 1958, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Dichotomine B standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of dichotomine B CRS and place it in a 5-mL volumetric flask, then add 0.1 mL of dimethyl sulphoxide. Sonicate (220 W) the solution for 5 min. Make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Pipette 0.1 mL of the solution to a 1-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (70%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 45 mL of methanol (70%). Reflux the mixture for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Wash the residue with methanol (70%) and combine the extracts. Make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (275 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times 50 \\mathrm{~mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-2</td><td>88→85</td><td>12→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>2-8</td><td>85</td><td>15</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>8-20</td><td>85→40</td><td>15→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of dichotomine B Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of dichotomine B should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of dichotomine B peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from dichotomine B peak should not be less than 2500 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject dichotomine B Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of dichotomine B peak in the chromatogram of dichotomine B Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify dichotomine B peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of dichotomine B Std-FP. The retention times of dichotomine B peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Stellariae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Stellariae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,dichotomine B)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.15</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.79</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.71</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[331, 1530, 1820, 2020])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Stellariae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.4%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.7%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.1%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 18%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 39%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 35%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/stellariae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Stellariae Radix","la":"Stellariae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/stephaniae-tetrandrae-radix-v4-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix v4 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[550, 1033, 1403, 1722])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 5, 1929, 260])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[550, 1033, 1403, 1722])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[27, 5, 1929, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 防己\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Fangji"},{"name":"12","text":"Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix is the dried root of Stephania tetrandra S. Moore (Menispermaceae). The root is collected in autumn, washed clean, and removed the outer coarse bark, half-dried under the sun, then cut into small stick, the large ones further cut into longitudinal slice, then thoroughly dried under the sun to obtain Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Irregularly cylindrical, subcylindrical or thick slices, mostly curved, 1.4-12.5 cm long, 6-46 mm in diameter. Externally pale greyish-yellow, with remnants of greyish-brown outer bark visible on the curved part, usually with deeply depressed lateral grooves and knotty in appearance. Texture heavy and hard, fracture even, greyish-white, starchy, with sparsely arranged radial striations. Odour slight; taste bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork usually removed, sometimes remnant. Cortex narrow, stone cells scattered singly or 2-5 in a group, usually laterally arranged. Phloem narrow. Cambium in a ring. Xylem broad, vessels arranged continually and radially; surrounded by xylem fibres. Ray distinct and broad. Parenchymatous cells filled with starch granules; small rod-shaped crystals of calcium oxalate visible (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-white or yellowish-white. Starch granules numerous, simple granules spherical, helmet-shaped or polygonal, 1-49 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum dotted, slit-shaped, V-shaped or stellate,\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[11, 8, 1984, 262])\n\nstriations distinct; compound granules usually composed of 2-5 units; black and cruciate in shape under the polarized microscope. Vessels mostly bordered-pitted, 4.5-175 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Stone cells subrounded, subsquare, or oblong, 20-97 $ \\mu $ m in diameter or 49-111 $ \\mu $ m long, wall 2-20 $ \\mu $ m thick and with distinct pit canals. Fibre rare, long-shuttle, lignified. Cork cells pale yellow and polygonal. Prisms of calcium oxalate rare, fine short rod in shape, 0.5-7.9 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; brightly white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3)."},{"name":"12","text":"Fangchinoline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of fangchinoline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nTetrandrine standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of tetrandrine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v), ethanol and ethyl acetate (0.2:2:5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth subnitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 8 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 30 mL of water."},{"name":"12","text":"Transfer 5 mL of Solution A, 5 mL of Solution B and 20 mL of glacial acetic acid to a 100-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 3.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 15 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture.\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[24, 1, 1927, 258])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 399, 1736, 2143])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Cortex\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell 3. Cortex 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Ray\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[29, 14, 1984, 264])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[235, 414, 1753, 2248])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix\n\n</div>\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Bordered-pitted vessel 3. Stone cells 4. Fibre 5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 3, 1927, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel G60 plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately fangchinoline standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), tetrandrine standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (4 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and dry in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of fangchinoline and tetrandrine.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[458, 1361, 1196, 1673])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[459, 1822, 1197, 2130])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) fangchinoline and (ii) tetrandrine\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 13, 1977, 265])"},{"name":"12","text":"Fangchinoline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of fangchinoline CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%).\n\nTetrandrine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (400 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.0 mg of tetrandrine CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (90 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.3%Trifluoroacetic acid和0.5%Triethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>20→40</td><td>80→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-40</td><td>40→100</td><td>60→0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of fangchinoline Std-FP and tetrandrine Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of fangchinoline and tetrandrine should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of fangchinoline and tetrandrine peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from fangchinoline and tetrandrine peaks should not be less than 40000 and 50000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject fangchinoline Std-FP, tetrandrine Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of fangchinoline and tetrandrine peaks in the chromatograms of fangchinoline Std-FP, tetrandrine Std-FP and the\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 3, 1928, 261])\n\nretention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify fangchinoline and tetrandrine peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of fangchinoline Std-FP and tetrandrine Std-FP. The retention times of fangchinoline and tetrandrine peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,fangchinoline)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(tetrandrine)</td><td>1.17</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.86</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[391, 1475, 1807, 2099])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[29, 21, 1976, 267])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVIII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%.\n\n5. 8 Detection of Aristolochic Acid I (Appendix XIII): meet the requirements."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/stephaniae-tetrandrae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix","la":"Stephaniae Tetrandrae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/storax-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Storax v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 755, 1964, 2022])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Storax (from Honduras)\n\n</div>\n\nA. Storax (brownish-yellow) B. Storax (dark brown)"},"sections":[{"name":"10","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[0, 755, 1964, 2022])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Storax (from Honduras)\n\n</div>\n\nA. Storax (brownish-yellow) B. Storax (dark brown)"},{"name":"10","text":"Official name: Storax\n\nChinese name: 蘇合香\n\nChinese phonetic name: Suhexiang"},{"name":"10","text":"Storax (from Honduras) is the purified balsam obtained from the trunk of Liquidambar styraciflua L. (Hamamelidaceae*). The bark on the tree is chipped to expose the underlying wood during summer and autumn, and the exudate is collected from the bark wound. The collected crude Storax is heated up until it is thinner and then foreign matters are filtered to obtain cleaner Storax."},{"name":"10","text":"Semifluid viscous liquid, brownish-yellow or dark brown, translucent. Odour aromatic (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"10","text":"Not applicable for Storax."},{"name":"10","text":"(E)-Cinnamic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 10.0 mg of (E)-cinnamic acid CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"10","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl formate, petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and formic acid (15:15:1, v/v)."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Transfer the extract to a 20-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel 60 $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately (E)-cinnamic acid standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ). Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[382, 1130, 740, 1334])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[382, 1452, 977, 1652])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[384, 1773, 976, 1962])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Chemical structures of (i) (E)-cinnamic acid (ii) (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and (iii) 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate\n\n</div>\n\n明黛参 Changii Radix\n\nHippophae Fructus沙棘Lycii Fructus枸杞子\n\n紫蘇葉 Perillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n沒藥 茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[375, 369, 758, 1187])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Storax extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. (E)-Cinnamic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of (E)-cinnamic acid (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[18, 17, 1953, 255])"},{"name":"10","text":"(E)-Cinnamic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of (E)-cinnamic acid CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of absolute ethanol.\n\n(E,E)-Cinnamyl cinnamate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 10 mL of absolute ethanol.\n\n3-Phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate CRS (Fig. 2) and dissolve in 10 mL of absolute ethanol."},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the sample and place it in a 100-mL conical flask, then add 40 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Transfer the solution to a 50-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Pipette 1 mL of the mixture into a 25-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with absolute ethanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"10","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (277 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.7 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 35^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>1%Acetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>80→75</td><td>20→25</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>75→45</td><td>25→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-16</td><td>45→42</td><td>55→58</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>16-20</td><td>42→35</td><td>58→65</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[33, 14, 1954, 258])"},{"name":"10","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of (E)-cinnamic acid Std-FP, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate Std-FP and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate peaks should not be less than 25000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"10","text":"Separately inject (E)-cinnamic acid Std-FP, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate Std-FP, 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate peaks in the chromatograms of (E)-cinnamic acid Std-FP, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate Std-FP, 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 4) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of (E)-cinnamic acid Std-FP, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate Std-FP and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate Std-FP. The retention times of (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Storax extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Storax extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,(E)-cinnamic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2(benzyl cinnamate)</td><td>2.13</td><td>$\\pm$0.06</td></tr><tr><td>3((E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate)</td><td>2.52</td><td>$\\pm$0.07</td></tr><tr><td>4(3-phenylpropyl(E)-cinnamate)</td><td>2.63</td><td>$\\pm$0.07</td></tr></table>\n\n明黛参\n\nChangii Radix\n\n沒藥 茯苓\n\nMyrrha\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸棗仁\n\nStorax\n\n蘇合香\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[324, 365, 1821, 1112])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Storax extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 4)."},{"name":"10","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): Test not required for this balsam.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: Test not required for this balsam.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: Test not required for this balsam."},{"name":"10","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 2.9%.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[20, 12, 1971, 259])"},{"name":"10","text":"Weigh 10.0 g of the sample and heat the sample in a beaker at $ 1 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 30 min. Take up the residue in 100 mL of hot ethanol (95%) , pass through counter-balanced filters or a tared filter crucible, and wash the residue with small portions of hot ethanol (95%) until the last washing is colourless or practically so. Dry the residue at $ 1 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 h. Weigh accurately and calculate the percentage of ethanol-insoluble matter with reference to the dried substance. The amount of ethanol-insoluble matter in the sample should not be more than 4.4%.\n\n5. 9 Acid Value (Appendix XIV): 51-54."},{"name":"10","text":"Water-soluble extractives: Test not required for this balsam.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 55%."},{"name":"10","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."},{"name":"10","text":"Mixed (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate standard stock solution, Std-Stock (120 mg/L for (E)-cinnamic acid, 1320 mg/L for (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 1160 mg/L for 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate)\n\nWeigh accurately 3.0 mg of (E)-cinnamic acid CRS, 33.0 mg of (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate CRS and 29.0 mg of 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate CRS, and dissolve in 25 mL of absolute ethanol.\n\nMixed (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed (E)-cinnamic acid, (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate Std-Stock, dilute with absolute ethanol to produce a series of solutions of 2.5, 5, 10, 25, 50 mg/L for (E)-cinnamic acid, 25, 50, 100, 250, 500 mg/L for (E,E)-cinnamyl cinnamate and 10, 25, 50, 100, 250 mg/L for 3-phenylpropyl (E)-cinnamate."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/storax","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Storax","la":"Storax"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/tamaricis-cacumen-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tamaricis Cacumen v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[362, 826, 1587, 1924])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Tamaricis Cacumen\n\n</div>\n\nA. Tamaricis Cacumen B. Magnified image of Tamaricis Cacumen C. Magnified image of alternated leaves D. Magnified image of fracture of young twig\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1964, 257])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[362, 826, 1587, 1924])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Tamaricis Cacumen\n\n</div>\n\nA. Tamaricis Cacumen B. Magnified image of Tamaricis Cacumen C. Magnified image of alternated leaves D. Magnified image of fracture of young twig\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1964, 257])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official name: Tamaricis Cacumen\n\nChinese name: 西河柳\n\nChinese phonetic name: Xiheliu"},{"name":"14","text":"Tamaricis Cacumen is the dried young twig and leaf of Tamarix chinensis Lour. (Tamaricaceae). The young twig and leaf are collected in summer before flowering, then dried under the sun to obtain Tamaricis Cacumen."},{"name":"14","text":"The upper part of young twig slender-cylindrical, 0.3-1.3 mm in diameter, externally greyish-green to yellowish-green; texture fragile, easily broken, fracture flat, yellowish-white, arranged in radial striations, with distinct ray and pith in the centre. The lower part of young twig 0.7-2.5 mm in diameter, externally yellowish-brown to reddish-brown. Leaves alternate, scaly, ovate-lanceolate, apex acute, sheath-like base. Leaves usually fallen off and the leaf-bases protruded. Odour slight, taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Cork contains reddish-brown pigment, the upper part of young twig consists of 1 layer of flattened cells; the lower part of young twig consists of 4-7 layers of flattened cells, with outer walls lignified. Cortex relatively broad, consisting of irregularly arranged parenchymatous cells, with cells subsquare or irregular in shape. Pericycle fibres arranged in an interrupted ring, walls of fibre markedly thickened and lignified, with surrounding cells containing crystals of calcium sulphate. Groups of stone cells occasionally exist among fibre bundles. Phloem thin. Cambium indistinct. Xylem consists of fibres and vessels. Ray 2-3 rows of cells wide, lignified. Pith relatively small, containing subrounded parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2(i) and Fig. 2(ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour reddish-brown, brownish-green or yellowish-green. Crystals of calcium sulphate numerous, 5-28 $ \\mu $m in diameter, some corner angles distinct; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Stone cells several in group, subrounded or elliptic. Leaf base fibre slender, with relatively thickened walls and thorn-like projections surface. Fibres present in bundles, 4-16 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls slightly thick, lignified. Epidermal cells of leaf subsquared, subpolygonal or rectangular in surface view, slightly thickened, occasionally semilunar cuticle convex; sunken stoma with 4-6 subsidiary cells appeared in both upper and lower epidermis layer of cells. Vessels mostly reticulate and spiral vessels, 6-19 $ \\mu $m and 3-14 $ \\mu $m in diameter respectively (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 41, 1967, 257])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[242, 391, 1742, 2117])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of upper part of young twig of Tamaricis Cacumen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells D. Crystals of calcium sulphate (under the light microscope $ \\rightarrow $ ) E. Crystals of calcium sulphate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle fibre 4. Stone cell 5. Phloem 6. Ray 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[20, 42, 1954, 245])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[260, 594, 1732, 1945])"},{"name":"14","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells\n\nD. Crystals of calcium sulphate (under the light microscope $ \\rightarrow $)\n\nE. Crystals of calcium sulphate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Pericycle fibre 4. Stone cell 5. Phloem 6. Ray 7. Xylem 8. Pith\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 43, 1966, 253])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[250, 604, 1688, 2112])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of the powder of Tamaricis Cacumen\n\n1. Crystals of calcium sulphate 2. Stone cells 3. Leaf base fibre 4. Fibres\n\n5. Epidermal cells of leaf (5-1 upper epidermal cells, 5-2 lower epidermal cells, anomocytic stomata $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n6. Vessels (6-1 reticulate vessel, 6-2 spiral vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[20, 40, 1946, 238])"},{"name":"14","text":"Quercetin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.25 mg of quercetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, n-hexane, formic acid and water (11:7:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1 g of aluminium trichloride and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of 2% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately quercetin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 2 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[368, 441, 857, 779])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[366, 1040, 888, 1335])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[369, 1653, 858, 1978])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) isorhamnetin (ii) kaempferol and (iii) quercetin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[20, 40, 1948, 246])\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[376, 372, 793, 1209])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Tamaricis Cacumen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Quercetin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of quercetin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Isorhamnetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (6 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of isorhamnetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nKaempferol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (3 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.15 mg of kaempferol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol.\n\nQuercetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (4 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of quercetin CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 20 mL of 2% hydrochloric acid in methanol. Reflux the mixture for 30 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with 3 mL of methanol. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (364 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.5 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>0.2% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-12</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>12-20</td><td>60→65</td><td>40→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>65→70</td><td>35→30</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>70→74</td><td>30→26</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of isorhamnetin Std-FP, kaempferol Std-FP and quercetin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of isorhamnetin, kaempferol and quercetin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of isorhamnetin, kaempferol and quercetin peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from isorhamnetin, kaempferol and quercetin peaks should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject isorhamnetin Std-FP, kaempferol Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of isorhamnetin, kaempferol and quercetin peaks in the chromatograms of isorhamnetin Std-FP, kaempferol Std-FP, quercetin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify isorhamnetin, kaempferol and quercetin peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of isorhamnetin Std-FP, kaempferol Std-FP and quercetin Std-FP. The retention times of isorhamnetin, kaempferol and quercetin peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Tamaricis Cacumen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Tamaricis Cacumen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(quercetin)</td><td>0.72</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,isorhamnetin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(kaempferol)</td><td>1.08</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.13</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[333, 1723, 1819, 2179])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Tamaricis Cacumen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 15.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 11.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 20.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/tamaricis-cacumen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tamaricis Cacumen","la":"Tamaricis Cacumen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trachelospermi-caulis-et-folium-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[486, 869, 1478, 1896])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium A. Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium B. Leaf C. Transverse section of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 10, 1900, 261])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[486, 869, 1478, 1896])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium A. Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium B. Leaf C. Transverse section of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 10, 1900, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\nChinese Name: 絡石藤\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Luoshiteng"},{"name":"14","text":"Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium is the dried stem with leaf of Trachelospermum jasminoides (Lindl.) Lem. (Apocynaceae). The stem with leaf is collected in winter to next spring, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem cylindrical, branched, varying in length, 1-5 mm in diameter. Externally reddish-brown, with dotted lenticels and adventitious roots; texture hard; fracture pale yellow and frequently hollow. Leaves opposite, slightly curled and with short petiole; lamina elliptical to ovate-lanceolate, 2.1-6.9 cm long, 0.7-3.4 cm wide; upper surface dark green or brownish-green, glossy; lower surface greyish-green, margin entire; texture coriaceous. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Stem: Cork consists of several layers of brownish-red cells. Stone cells located inside the cork layers, arranged in an interrupted ring, prisms of calcium oxalate scattered between the cork and the stone cells band. Cortex narrow. Phloem narrow, with non-lignified fibre bundles on the outer side, arranged in an interrupted ring. Cambium in a ring. Xylem consists of lignified cells; the vessels subrounded, arranged radially. On the inner part of the xylem, internal cambium and internal phloem situated. Pith relatively small, containing large clefts, fibres and prisms of calcium oxalate [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[70, 8, 1967, 260])\n\nLeaf: The upper epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, subsquare or rectangular. Palisade tissue consists of 1 layer of palisade cells, arranged in compact order, going through the upper part of the midrib. Spongy cells subrounded, arranged loosely, clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally found. Vascular bundles collateral, xylem vessels radially arranged, with non-lignified fibres lining up the outer side of the phloem. Lower epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, the cells subrounded to subrectangular. Non-lignified fibres and prisms of calcium oxalate present in the inner side of the epidermis. Non-glandular hairs occasionally visible [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-yellow to brown. Epidermal cells with curved anticlinal wall, stomata anomocytic, subsidiary cells 4-8. Non-glandular hairs unicellular or multicellular, scattered, wall thick, with warty protuberance. Stone cells scattered singly or in groups, subrectangular, oblong ovate or triangular, 14-98 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall thick, striations distinct. Prisms of calcium oxalate relatively numerous, scattered or occasionally present in stone cells, 6-51 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Clusters of calcium oxalate occasionally visible, subrounded, 13-52 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with short and obtuse angles; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 7-134 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Fibres scattered singly or in bundles, relatively long, 6-56 $ \\mu $m in diameter, wall relatively thick, with distinct pit canals, polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells yellowish-brown, subpolygonal, rectangular or triangular (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[39, 6, 1901, 262])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 396, 1744, 1434])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Stone cells\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cells 3. Cortex 4. Phloem fibres 5. Phloem 6. Cambium 7. Xylem\n\n8. Pith 9. Internal cambium 10. Internal phloem 11. Fibres of pith\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1682, 173, 1758, 216])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 413, 1746, 1494])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate 1. Upper epidermis 2. Fibres 3. Palisade tissue 4. Sponge tissue 5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Fibres 8. Prisms of calcium oxalate 9. Lower epidermis 10.Non-glandular hair\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[36, 99, 209, 199])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[540, 19, 700, 75])\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[288, 453, 1696, 1555])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\n1. Epidermal cells of leaf 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Stone cell\n\n4. Prisms of calcium oxalate 5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Bordered-pitted vessel\n\n7. Fibres 8. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[76, 13, 1965, 257])"},{"name":"14","text":"Tracheloside standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.2 mg of tracheloside CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of ethyl acetate, water, formic acid and glacial acetic acid (8:1.5:0.8:0.8, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 100-mL round-bottomed flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Reflux the mixture for 15 min. Cool down to room temperature. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately tracheloside standard solution (15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (6 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8.5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 4 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV(A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of tracheloside.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 5, 1913, 260])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[446, 376, 1086, 769])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[444, 850, 997, 1073])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) tracheloside and (ii) trachelogenin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"Tracheloside standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of tracheloside CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (200 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (228 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[68, 4, 1966, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>75→65</td><td>25→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>65→60</td><td>35→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-40</td><td>60→40</td><td>40→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-50</td><td>40</td><td>60</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of tracheloside Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of tracheloside should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of tracheloside peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from tracheloside peak should not be less than 40000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject tracheloside Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of tracheloside peak in the chromatogram of tracheloside Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify tracheloside peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of tracheloside Std-FP. The retention times of tracheloside peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[36, 5, 1907, 264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.70</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, tracheloside)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(trachelogenin)</td><td>1.41</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[379, 824, 1735, 1388])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[65, 14, 1963, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Total ash: not more than 8.0 %.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 3.5%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 8.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 11.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 9.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trachelospermi-caulis-et-folium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium","la":"Trachelospermi Caulis et Folium"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trichosanthis-radix-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trichosanthis Radix v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[613, 1001, 1370, 1379])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n2 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[639, 1500, 894, 1696])\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[915, 1491, 1168, 1701])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1206, 1486, 1372, 1700])\n\n5 mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried root of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.\n\nA. Roots B. Magnified image of outer appearance\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section\n\nD. Magnified image of longitudinal section\n\nPotentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n翻白草\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nLini Semen\n\n亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n白花蛇舌草\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n三棱\n\n天葵子\n\nSemiaquilaria Radix\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\n天花粉\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[373, 497, 1652, 1794])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried root of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms\n\nA. Roots B. Magnified image of outer appearance\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section\n\nD. Magnified image of longitudinal section\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[35, 43, 1921, 133])"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[613, 1001, 1370, 1379])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n2 cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[639, 1500, 894, 1696])\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[915, 1491, 1168, 1701])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1206, 1486, 1372, 1700])\n\n5 mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried root of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.\n\nA. Roots B. Magnified image of outer appearance\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section\n\nD. Magnified image of longitudinal section\n\nPotentillae Discoloris Herba\n\n翻白草\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\nLini Semen\n\n亞麻子\n\nHedyotidis Diffusae Herba\n\n白花蛇舌草\n\nSparganii Rhizoma\n\n三棱\n\n天葵子\n\nSemiaquilaria Radix\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nPsidii Guajavae Folium\n\n番石榴葉\n\n天花粉\n\nTrichosanthis Radix\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum片藍黄\n\n半邊蓮\n\nLobeliae Chinensis Herba\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[373, 497, 1652, 1794])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried root of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms\n\nA. Roots B. Magnified image of outer appearance\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section\n\nD. Magnified image of longitudinal section\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[35, 43, 1921, 133])"},{"name":"15","text":"Official Name: Trichosanthis Radix\n\nChinese Name: 天花粉\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Tianhuafen"},{"name":"15","text":"Trichosanthis Radix is the dried root of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim. or Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms (Cucurbitaceae). The root is collected in autumn and winter, washed clean, cork of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim. mostly peeled, cork of Trichosanths rosthornii Harms mostly unpeeled, then cut into segments or sliced longitudinally to obtain Trichosanthis Radix."},{"name":"15","text":"Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.: Irregularly cylindrical, spindle-shaped, or in pieces, 5-24 cm long, 5-39 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-white to pale brownish-yellow, with longitudinal wrinkles, rootlet scars, and a slightly impressed transverse lenticel. Remnants of yellowish-brown outer bark scattered. Texture compact, fracture white to yellowish-white, starchy, a radially arranged yellow xylem visible in transverse section, striated yellow xylem visible in longitudinal section. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nTrichosanthes rosthornii Harms: Irregularly cylindrical, spindle-shaped, or in pieces, 4-21 cm long, 9-49 mm in diameter. Externally pale greyish-yellow to brownish-yellow, with reticulate wrinkles. Remnants of brownish-yellow outer bark visible [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.: Cork completely broken or peeled off, 2-12 layers of cork cells visible occasionally. Stone cells occasionally found, irregular in shape, several cells in groups arranged in an interrupted ring. Fibres located close to stone cells, mainly in groups. Phloem narrow. Cambium indistinct. Xylem broad, vessels singly scattered or in groups, 22-432 $ \\mu $m in diameter, surrounded by xylem fibres, radially arranged starting near the cambium or irregularly. Starch granules numerous, scattered in parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[34, 14, 1949, 261])\n\nTrichosanthes rosthornii Harms: Lower layer of cork visible, consisting of 2-10 layers of cork cells. Stone cells numerous, irregular in shape, several cells in groups, arranged in an interrupted ring. Vessels 18-473 $ \\mu $m in diameter, surrounded by xylem fibres, radially arranged starting near the cambium or irregularly [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.: Colour yellowish-white. Starch granules numerous, simple starch granules relatively less abundant, subspherical, globose, subglobose or helmeted, 3-36 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, the hilum pointed, shortly cleft or forming a V shape, with striations faintly visible in the small granules, but clear and sharp in the large ones; compound starch granules more abundant, composed of 2-14 units, frequently composed of one large unit and several small ones; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Stone cells greenish-yellow, singly scattered or occasionally in groups, the cells rectangular, elliptic, subsquare, polygonal or fusiform, 11-79 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, but may be as long as 131 $ \\mu m $ , densely fine-pitted, walls 4-14 $ \\mu m $ in thickness. Bordered-pitted vessel large, mostly broken, densely and clearly pitted, tightly arranged, two types: one type with the pits hexagonal, pentagonal or square; the other type with the pits oblong to oval. Fibres 5-56 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, two types: one type tracheidlike fibres and broken; the other type frequently single, straight and thick walled; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cell polygonal, tightly packed [Fig. 3 (i)].\n\nTrichosanthes rosthornii Harms: Starch granules 3-45 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; compound granules composed of 2-15 units. Stone cells relatively abundant and easily observed, singly scattered or in groups, the cells rectangular, elliptic, subsquare, polygonal or fusiform, 14-107 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, but may be as long as 192 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ , densely fine-pitted, walls 3-21 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in thickness. Fibres 6-59 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter [Fig.3 (ii)].\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 86, 1025, 137])\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[234, 497, 1670, 2041])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Starch granules (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Starch granules (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell 3. Fibre 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Starch granule\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[102, 188, 203, 241])\n\n天葵子\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[236, 436, 1750, 2100])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried root of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Starch granules (under the light microscope) D. Starch granules (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Cork 2. Stone cell 3. Fibre 4. Phloem 5. Cambium 6. Xylem 7. Starch granule\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[197, 146, 288, 188])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1757, 51, 1911, 107])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[699, 141, 828, 191])\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[270, 539, 1678, 2018])\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried root of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.\n\n1. Starch granules 2. Stone cells (2-1 single stone cell, 2-2 cluster of stone cells)\n\n3. Bordered-pitted vessels (3-1 bordered pits polygonal, 3-2 bordered pits oblong)\n\n4. Fibres (4-1 tracheid-like fibres, 4-2 straight fibre with a thick cell wall) 5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[673, 44, 815, 119])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[103, 188, 202, 238])\n\n天葵子\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[667, 146, 791, 187])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1038, 148, 1133, 201])\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[334, 437, 819, 824])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[838, 435, 1326, 825])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1341, 435, 1656, 826])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[332, 849, 820, 1261])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[847, 851, 1294, 1262])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1310, 845, 1657, 1258])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[334, 1286, 1090, 1587])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[332, 1609, 1092, 2007])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[1111, 1291, 1658, 2002])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"15","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Stone cells (2-1 single cell, 2-2 cluster of stone cells)\n\n3. Bordered-pitted vessels (3-1 bordered pits polygonal, 3-2 bordered pits oblong)\n\n4. Fibres (4-1 tracheid-like fibre, 4-2 curved fibre with a thick cell wall) 5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[37, 37, 1947, 251])"},{"name":"15","text":"L-Citrulline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of L-citrulline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol (50%)."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, ethanol, glacial acetic acid and water (3:2:2:2, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1 g of ninhydrin and dissolve in 50 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (220 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately L-citrulline standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[370, 2015, 999, 2253])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of L-citrulline\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[103, 185, 204, 248])\n\n瓜子金\n\nPolygalae Japonicae Herba\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum片蘁黄\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[378, 377, 957, 1375])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Trichosanthis Radix extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. L-Citrulline standard solution\n\n2. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried root of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.\n\n(ii) dried root of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of L-citrulline (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"L-Citrulline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (200 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of L-citrulline CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[36, 36, 1950, 257])"},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 150-mL conical flask, then add 90 mL of methanol (50%). Sonicate (180 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue for three times each with 3 mL of methanol (50%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with an ELSD [drift tube temperature: $ 1 1 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ; nebulizer gas $ \\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right) $ flow rate: 3.0 L/min] and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.7 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of 0.3% trifluoroacetic acid and 0.15% heptafluorobutyric acid in water. The elution time is about 20 min."},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of L-citrulline Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of L-citrulline should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of L-citrulline peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from L-citrulline peak should not be less than 10000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject L-citrulline Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of L-citrulline peak in the chromatogram of L-citrulline Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify L-citrulline peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of L-citrulline Std-FP. The retention times of L-citrulline peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Trichosanthis Radix extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Trichosanthis Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.66</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,L-citrulline)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[320, 774, 1822, 1240])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[320, 1404, 1821, 1882])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried root of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[48, 44, 235, 122])\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[925, 88, 1026, 137])"},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): not more than 400 mg/kg.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 15.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trichosanthis-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trichosanthis Radix","la":"Trichosanthis Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trichosanthis-semen-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trichosanthis Semen v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[602, 903, 1382, 1399])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[638, 1751, 1384, 2413])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of seed of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms\n\n</div>\n\nA. Trichosanthis Semen B. Outer surface of cotyledon C. Inner surface of cotyledon D. Magnified seed\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 10, 1901, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"16","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[602, 903, 1382, 1399])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[638, 1751, 1384, 2413])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of seed of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms\n\n</div>\n\nA. Trichosanthis Semen B. Outer surface of cotyledon C. Inner surface of cotyledon D. Magnified seed\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 10, 1901, 262])"},{"name":"16","text":"Official Name: Trichosanthis Semen\n\nChinese Name: 瓜萋子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Gualouzi"},{"name":"16","text":"Trichosanthis Semen is the dried ripe seed of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim. or Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms (Cucurbitaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in autumn and dissected. The seed is gathered, washed clean, then dried under the sun to obtain Trichosanthis Semen."},{"name":"16","text":"Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.: Flattish-ellipsoidal, 1-1.8 cm long, 8-12 mm wide, 2-3.5 mm thick. Externally yellowish-green to brown, smooth, with a circle of furrow along the edge. Apex relatively acute, with a hilum, base rounded-obtuse or relatively narrow. Testa hard; tegmen membranous, greyish-green; cotyledons 2, yellowish-white, oily. Odour slight; taste weak [Fig. 1 (i)].\n\nTrichosanthes rosthornii Harms: Flattish-ellipsoidal to elliptic-rectangular, 1.4-1.9 cm long, 6-10 mm wide, 1.5-3 mm thick. Externally brown, the circular furrow around the edge prominent and somewhat closer to the central part. Apex even and truncated [Fig. 1 (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Testa consists of 1 layer of epidermal cells, the cells subrectangular, tangentially elongated externally covered with cuticle. Sclerenchyma beneath the epidermis, consisting of 6-14 layers of lignified cells, the cells of the 1-3 layers on the outside relatively small, long-rounded or subrounded, elongated tangentially; the cells of the outer to inner 3-6 layers varied in size, arranged irregularly and densely, with relatively small lumen; the innermost 1-3 layers of the\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 9, 1974, 260])\n\nstone cells subsquare or polygonal, with prominently thickened wall, arranged densely. Inside the stone cell layer, the lacunar parenchyma situated, consisting of 1-3 layers of cells relatively small at the outside, elongated-rounded, subrounded or elliptical, those increased in size or dilapidated towards inner side, with gradually thickened wall, forming sclerenchyma near the joint of cotyledon in the two ends of seed. Vascular bundle located in the lacunar parenchyma of the two ends of the seed, with the vessels relatively small. Endosperm consists of several layers of flatten cells. Parenchyma of cotyledon occupied the most part of seed, filled with oil droplets and aleurone grains [Fig. 2 (i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Colour dark reddish-brown or pale brown. Stone cells relatively small, scattered singly or in groups, brown, pale yellowish-brown or greyish-green, irregularly squared or rounded, edges indistinct, wall sinuous or branched, some with striations, pits relatively sparse or with indistinct pit canals at one side, lumen occasionally containing dark brown substances. Sclerenchymatous cells mostly scattered singly or in groups, some attached to epidermal cells, brownishyellow, orange or greyish-green, irregularly rectangular, elongated-rounded or subtriangular 22-79 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, 42-108 $ \\mu m $ long, wall 3-16 $ \\mu m $ thick, sinuous, often with several branches or protuberance, tips obtuse. Epidermal cells of testa subpolygonal or irregular in surface view, periclinal wall slightly curved or straight, with cuticled striations. Parenchymatous cells of cotyledons polygonal, subpolygonal or subrounded, filled with oil droplets and aleurone grains. Fragments of endosperm seldom found, yellowish-green, polygonal or subpolygonal, containing aleurone grains [Fig. 3 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[20, 3, 1932, 264])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[247, 443, 1725, 2063])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim.\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis of testa 2. Sclerenchyma 3. Stone cells 4. Lacunar parenchyma\n\n5. Endosperm 6. Cotyledon 7. Vascular bundle\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Trichosanthis Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[245, 432, 1737, 2057])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of dried ripe seed of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Vascular bundle\n\n1. Epidermis of testa 2. Sclerenchyma 3. Stone cells 4. Lacunar parenchyma\n\n5. Endosperm 6. Cotyledon 7. Vascular bundle\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[35, 99, 210, 197])\n\n西紅花 Croci Stigma\n\nEupatorii Herba\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\n桃仁 Persicae Semen\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\n雞血藤 Spatholobi Caulis\n\n羅布麻葉\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[293, 452, 674, 770])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[697, 453, 1013, 762])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1032, 454, 1353, 758])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1374, 453, 1690, 766])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[295, 796, 672, 1117])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[695, 793, 1013, 1112])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1032, 793, 1362, 1120])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1374, 796, 1692, 1125])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[288, 1146, 801, 1688])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[812, 1150, 1283, 1687])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[1292, 1146, 1692, 1686])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Sclerenchymatous cells 3. Epidermal cells of testa\n\n4. Cotyledon cells 5. Endosperm cells\n\nRubiae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n茜草\n\n餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nLophatheri Herba淡竹葉\n\n桂枝\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n繢斷 Trichosanthis Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[297, 456, 672, 753])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[706, 459, 1006, 737])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1033, 462, 1345, 751])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1372, 450, 1688, 755])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[295, 785, 669, 1083])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[711, 787, 1005, 1107])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1108, 792, 1342, 1010])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1456, 791, 1687, 1091])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[291, 1129, 793, 1643])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[804, 1127, 1231, 1647])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[1244, 1129, 1692, 1629])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried ripe seed of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Stone cells 2. Sclerenchymatous cells 3. Epidermal cells of testa\n\n4. Cotyledon cells 5. Endosperm cells\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[27, 5, 1903, 261])"},{"name":"16","text":"3, 29-Dibenzoyl-karounitriol standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ )."},{"name":"16","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"16","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 20 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 20 mL of ethyl acetate. Transfer the solution to a separating funnel. Extract with a mixture of 16 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) and 24 mL of water. Transfer the upper layer to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of cyclohexane."},{"name":"16","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[68, 9, 1968, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[380, 598, 1080, 1045])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol\n\n</div>"},{"name":"16","text":"3, 29-Dibenzoyl-karounitriol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L) Weigh 0.5 mg of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"16","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the freshly powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 25 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"16","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (230 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[20, 4, 1904, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>30→100</td><td>70→0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-60</td><td>100</td><td>0</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"16","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol peak should not be less than 90000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"Separately inject 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol peak in the chromatogram of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol Std-FP. The retention times of 3, 29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Trichosanthis Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[68, 11, 1966, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Trichosanthis Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.54</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,3,29-dibenzoyl-karounitriol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.14</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.18</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[385, 848, 1695, 1545])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Trichosanthes kirilowii Maxim. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[384, 1714, 1742, 2463])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe seed of Trichosanthes rosthornii Harms extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[38, 7, 1905, 263])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"16","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Total ash: not more than 3.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"16","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10.0%."},{"name":"16","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 3.0%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[66, 9, 1963, 259])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trichosanthis-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trichosanthis Semen","la":"Trichosanthis Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trigonellae-semen-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trigonellae Semen v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[666, 927, 806, 1094])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[801, 905, 967, 1094])\n\n2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[991, 893, 1092, 1023])\n\n2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1193, 1010, 1391, 1117])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[520, 1190, 1438, 1873])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Trigonellae Semen A. Seeds B. Magnified seeds C. Transverse section of seeds D. Cotyledons\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 10, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[666, 927, 806, 1094])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[801, 905, 967, 1094])\n\n2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[991, 893, 1092, 1023])\n\n2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1193, 1010, 1391, 1117])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n2mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[520, 1190, 1438, 1873])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Trigonellae Semen A. Seeds B. Magnified seeds C. Transverse section of seeds D. Cotyledons\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[34, 10, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Trigonellae Semen\n\nChinese Name: 胡蘆巴\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Huluba"},{"name":"12","text":"Trigonellae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Trigonella foenum-graecum L. (Fabaceae). The plant is collected in summer when the fruit is ripe. The fruit dried under the sun, the seed tapped out and foreign matter removed to obtain Trigonellae Semen."},{"name":"12","text":"Slightly rhomboid or rectangular, 3-4 mm long, 2-3 mm wide, about 2 mm thick. Externally yellowish green to yellowish-brown, smooth, with a deep oblique furrow on each of the two sides, and a dotlike hilum at the intersection of the two furrows. Texture hard, uneasily broken. Testa thin, endosperm translucent and viscous; cotyledons 2, pale yellow to brown, radicle curved, plump and long. Odour aromatic; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"The outermost layer of the testa consists of palisade cells, covered with cuticle, thick wall, apex cusp, striations distinct. Radicle cells polygonal to subrounded. Beaker cells 1 layer, somewhat dumbbell-shaped, narrow at the upper end but broader at the lower end with indistinct linear striations on the anticlinal walls. Beneath beaker cells, 2-4 layers of parenchymatous cells visible. Aleurone layer located in the outer side of the endosperm, consisting of 1 layer of cells, the cells subsquare or elliptical. Endosperm mostly consists of mucilage cells. Cotyledon cells polygonal or elongated in transverse section view."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellow to brown. Palisade cells of testa with fine and close longitudinal pit canals in lateral view, polygonal in surface view, 40-85 $ \\mu $m long, wall thickened, lumen small. Beaker cells somewhat dumbbell-shaped, narrow at the upper end but broader at lower end with linear striations on anticlinal walls in lateral view; subrounded or polygonal, with dense radial striations in surface view. Parenchymatous cells and aleurone grains sometimes visible in seed fragments. Cotyledon cells oval or elongated, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[78, 6, 1918, 263])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[244, 430, 1744, 2137])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Trigonellae Semen\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Part of section magnified\n\n1. Palisade cells of testa 2. Radicle 3. Beaker cells\n\n4. Aleurone layer 5. Endosperm 6. Cotyledon 7. Parenchyma\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Trigonellae Semen Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[259, 674, 1690, 2144])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Trigonellae Semen (under the light microscope)\n\n1. Palisade cells of testa (in lateral view) 2. Palisade cells of testa (in surface view)\n\n3. A fragment of the seed's outer portion, showing cell types of the palisade layer, beaker layer, parenchymatous layer and aleurone layer\n\n4. Beaker cells (in surface view) 5. Cotyledon cells with aleurone grains and fatty oil droplets\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[27, 7, 1904, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Trigonelline standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of trigonelline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, hydrochloric acid and ethyl acetate (8:3:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Solution A\n\nWeigh 0.85 g of bismuth oxynitrate and dissolve in a mixture of 10 mL of glacial acetic acid and 40 mL of water. Place it in a water bath at about $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to dissolve the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 4 g of potassium iodide and dissolve in 10 mL of water.\n\nSolution C\n\nTransfer 1 mL of Solution A, 1 mL of Solution B and 4 mL of glacial acetic acid into a 20-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with water."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 4.5 g of ferric trichloride and dissolve in 50 mL of water.\n\nSpray reagent\n\nMix 10 mL of Solution C and 5 mL of Solution D in a 50-mL conical flask. Freshly prepare all the reagents."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 30 mL of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ). Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Discard the supernatant. Add 30 mL of ethanol to the residue. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately trigonelline standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 4 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Heat the plate at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ for 1 h. Cool down to room temperature. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[67, 9, 1964, 259])\n\nand dry it in air until the spots or bands become visible. Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of trigonelline.\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 687, 710, 1059])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of trigonelline\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Trigonelline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of trigonelline CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (50%)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (50%). Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (50%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (265 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>95→71</td><td>5→29</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[31, 4, 1905, 264])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of trigonelline Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of trigonelline should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of trigonelline peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from trigonelline peak should not be less than 5000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject trigonelline Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of trigonelline peak in the chromatogram of trigonelline Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify trigonelline peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of trigonelline Std-FP. The retention times of trigonelline peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Trigonellae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Trigonellae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, trigonelline)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>8.93</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>10.78</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>11.02</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[300, 347, 1863, 1370])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Trigonellae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[32, 10, 1904, 266])"},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/trigonellae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trigonellae Semen","la":"Trigonellae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/tritici-levis-fructus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tritici Levis Fructus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[837, 877, 1354, 1179])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[588, 1212, 1337, 1875])\n\nA. Tritici Levis Fructus B. Magnified Tritici Levis Fructus\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[35, 9, 1900, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[837, 877, 1354, 1179])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[588, 1212, 1337, 1875])\n\nA. Tritici Levis Fructus B. Magnified Tritici Levis Fructus\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[35, 9, 1900, 262])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Tritici Levis Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 浮小麥\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Fuxiaomai"},{"name":"13","text":"Tritici Levis Fructus is the dried, shriveled and light caryopsis of Triticum aestivum L. (Poaceae). The caryopsis is collected around the summer solstice, the dust is sifted and rinsed with water, then dried under the sun to obtain Tritici Levis Fructus."},{"name":"13","text":"Elongated, oblong-ellipsoid, both ends slightly acute, 4-8 mm long, 1.5-3 mm in diameter. Externally pale yellowish-brown to yellow, crumpled, with a deep crease on the centre part of abdomen; an indistinct embryo at the base of the back, with beard-like and pale yellow hairs at the apex on the dorsal side. Texture hard and fragile, easily broken. Fracture white to yellowish-white, starchy. Odour slight; taste weak (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Pericarp fused with testa, the epidermal cells of the pericarp consist of 1 layer, with relatively thickened wall. Transverse cell 1 layer, oblong, arranged regularly, located inside the pericarp, with a relatively thickened wall. Aleurone layer located in the outermost side of endosperm, the cells square, filled with aleurone grains. Vascular tissue small, located deep in the crease. The embryo consists of parenchymatous cells. The endosperm embraces the embryo, filled with starch granules (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour whitish. Starch granules varied in shape, globular to broadly ovoid, cucullate or reniform, 6-42 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum slit-shaped, striations indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-4 (even more) units. Cells of aleurone layer subsquare or subrounded, with intercellular space, wall slightly thickened, filled with aleurone grains. Transverse cells mostly in groups, arranged regularly, slender cylindrical, wall beaded-thickened. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, apex acute and pointed, base slightly constricted and rounded, 143-653 $ \\mu $m long. Epidermal cells of pericarp subrectangular, elongated or long polygonal in surface view, anticlinal wall beaded (Fig. 3).\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 424, 1737, 2166])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Tritici Levis Fructus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Aleurone layer and endosperm\n\n1. Pericarp 2. Testa 3. Aleurone layer 4. Vascular tissue 5. Embryo\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Transverse cells\n\n香附\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[254, 437, 1713, 2176])"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Aleurone layer cells (in surface view) 3. Transverse cells 4. Non-glandular hairs\n\n5. Epidermal cells of pericarp\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 8, 1902, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"5-Heneicosylresorcinol standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of 5-heneicosylresorcinol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and acetone (5:3, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 5-heneicosylresorcinol standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5-10 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of 5-heneicosylresorcinol.\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix 紫苑\n\n績斷 Tritici Levis Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 375, 1453, 571])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[429, 637, 935, 1011])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) 5-heneicosylresorcinol and (ii) linoleic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Linoleic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (350 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 3.5 mg of linoleic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\n5-Heneicosylresorcinol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (12 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of 5-heneicosylresorcinol CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (100 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[28, 4, 1905, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-25</td><td>30→100</td><td>70→0</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-60</td><td>100</td><td>0</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of linoleic acid Std-FP and 5-heneicosylresorcinol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of linoleic acid and 5-heneicosylresorcinol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of linoleic acid and 5-heneicosylresorcinol peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from linoleic acid and 5-heneicosylresorcinol peaks should not be less than 160000 and 40000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject linoleic acid Std-FP, 5-heneicosylresorcinol Std-FP and the test solution (20 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of linoleic acid and 5-heneicosylresorcinol peaks in the chromatograms of linoleic acid Std-FP, 5-heneicosylresorcinol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify linoleic acid and 5-heneicosylresorcinol peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of linoleic acid Std-FP and 5-heneicosylresorcinol Std-FP. The retention times of linoleic acid and 5-heneicosylresorcinol peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Tritici Levis Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 11, 1965, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Tritici Levis Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.93</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, linoleic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.48</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(5-heneicosylresorcinol)</td><td>1.81</td><td>±0.05</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[382, 885, 1764, 1731])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Tritici Levis Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[38, 17, 1902, 264])"},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 15.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 10.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 1.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/tritici-levis-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tritici Levis Fructus","la":"Tritici Levis Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/tsaoko-fructus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tsaoko Fructus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[576, 969, 814, 1141])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[893, 981, 1106, 1119])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n0. 5cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[532, 1231, 1409, 1866])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Tsaoko Fructus\n\nA. Tsaoko Fructus B. Transverse section C. Groups of seeds D. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1904, 262])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[576, 969, 814, 1141])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[893, 981, 1106, 1119])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n1cm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n0. 5cm\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[532, 1231, 1409, 1866])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Tsaoko Fructus\n\nA. Tsaoko Fructus B. Transverse section C. Groups of seeds D. Seeds\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1904, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Tsaoko Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 草果\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Caoguo"},{"name":"14","text":"Tsaoko Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Amomum tsao-ko Crevost et Lemarie (Zingiberaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Tsaoko Fructus."},{"name":"14","text":"Narrowly ellipsoid, obtusely 3-ridged, 1.4-5 cm long, 10-24 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-brown to reddish-brown, with longitudinal grooves and ridges. A rounded and protuberant stylopodium at the apex, and a fruit stalk or its scar at the base. Pericarp tough, easily stripped off longitudinally. Upon removal of the pericarp, 3 groups of seeds visible and separated by yellowish-brown septa in the middle, each group contains 8-20 seeds. Seeds conical to polyhedral, about 5 mm in diameter, externally reddish-brown, covered with greyish-white to yellowish-white membranous aril, a longitudinal furrowed raphe and a dented hilum are located at the apex; texture hard; endosperm greyish-white to yellowish-white. Odour characteristically aromatic; taste pungent and slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Pericarp: Exocarp consists of 1 layer of rectangular cells. Mesocarp broad, consisting of several layers of parenchymatous cells, some cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundles scattered in the mesocarp, with fibre bundles on the outer side. Endocarp consists of 1 layer of tangentially elongated parenchymatous cells [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[68, 8, 1972, 260])\n\nSeed: Aril consists of several layers of irregularly shaped parenchymatous cells. Epidermal cells of testa rectangular, with relatively thick walls. Hypodermis consists of 1 layer of flat parenchymatous cells, tangentially elongated. Oil cell layer consists of 1 layer of subsquare or rectangular oil cells. Pigment layer consists of several layers of densely packed cells with indistinct boundaries, containing pigments. Endotesta consists of 1 layer of palisade sclerenchymatous cells, with heavily thickened inner and lateral walls, lumen small, containing silica bodies. Perisperm cells palisade-like on the outer layers, filled with small starch granules, some cells contain prisms of calcium oxalate. Endosperm cells contains aleurone grains, surrounding the embryo in the central portion [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-white to brownish-white. Epidermal cells of testa bright yellow, strip-shaped in surface view, wall thickened, covered with cuticle; bright yellow under the polarized microscope. Endotesta cells yellow to orange-yellow, polygonal or subrounded in surface view, containing silica bodies, wall thickened; cells palisaded in lateral view, with lumen on one side containing silica bodies inside; yellow to orange-yellow under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate 1-16 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; bright white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Oil cells subrounded, 11-71 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Perisperm cells narrowly rectangular or polygonal, filled with tiny starch granules, some containing prisms of calcium oxalate; pale yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Exocarp cells rectangular or polygonal in surface view, containing brownish-red pigment. Fibres 9-56 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly scalariform, 5-58 $ \\mu $ m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[36, 7, 1904, 265])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[248, 521, 1705, 1728])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of pericarp of Tsaoko Fructus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate in parenchyma\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Fibre bundle 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Prism of calcium oxalate 6. Endocarp\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nTsaoko Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[241, 456, 1734, 1734])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of seed of Tsaoko Fructus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate in perisperm\n\n1. Aril 2. Epidermis of testa 3. Hypodermis 4. Oil cell layer\n\n5. Pigment layer 6. Endotesta 7. Perisperm 8. Endosperm\n\n9. Embryo 10. Silica body 11. Prism of calcium oxalate\n\n西紅花 Croci Stigma\n\nEupatorii Herba佩蘭\n\nTsaoko Fructus\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\n桃仁 Persicae Semen\n\nTrachelospermi Caulis et Folium\n\nSpatholobi Caulis\n\n羅布麻葉\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[287, 448, 1700, 2125])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Tsaoko Fructus\n\n</div>\n\n1. Epidermal cells of testa 2. Endotesta cells with silica bodies (2-1 in surface view, 2-2 in lateral view)\n\n3. Prisms of calcium oxalate 4. Oil cell 5. Perisperm cell 6. Exocarp cells 7. Fibre 8. Scalariform vessel\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[70, 13, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"1,8-Cineole standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of 1,8-cineole CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, acetone and ethyl acetate (30:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 1 mL of sulphuric acid to 50 mL of glacial acetic acid and then add 0.5 mL of 4-methoxybenzaldehyde. Freshly prepare the reagent."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter the supernatant."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately 1,8-cineole standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3-5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of 1,8-cineole.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 4, 1912, 264])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 372, 582, 566])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of 1,8-cineole\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"1,8-Cineole standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 1,8-cineole CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethyl acetate."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with ethyl acetate. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethyl acetate. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The gas chromatograph is equipped with a FID and a capillary column (DB-17, $ 0. 2 5 \\mathrm{~ m m} \\times 3 0 \\mathrm{~ m} $ ) of which the internal wall is covered with (50%-phenyl)-methylpolysiloxane in a layer about $ 0. 2 5 \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. The injection temperature is at $ 2 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The detector temperature is at $ 3 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ The split injection mode at a ratio of 5:1 is used. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Temperature(℃)</td><td>Rate(℃/min)</td></tr><tr><td>0-5</td><td>50</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5-19</td><td>50→120</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>19-31.5</td><td>120→170</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>31.5-39.5</td><td>170→250</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>39.5-41.5</td><td>250</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 13, 1965, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of 1,8-cineole Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of 1,8-cineole should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of 1,8-cineole peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from 1,8-cineole peak should not be less than 90000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject 1,8-cineole Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the GC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of 1,8-cineole peak in the chromatogram of 1,8-cineole Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify 1,8-cineole peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of 1,8-cineole Std-FP. The retention times of 1,8-cineole peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Tsaoko Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Tsaoko Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(α-phellandrene)</td><td>0.88</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker,1,8-cineole)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3(3,7-dimethyl-6-octenal)</td><td>1.59</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(3,7-dimethyl-2,6-octadienal)</td><td>1.69</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(4-propylbenzaldehyde)</td><td>1.86</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[35, 100, 211, 199])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[260, 371, 1830, 1003])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference GC fingerprint chromatogram of Tsaoko Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference GC fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 8.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.5%."},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[71, 12, 1965, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 12.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 10.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(C)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/tsaoko-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tsaoko Fructus","la":"Tsaoko Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/uncariae-ramulus-cum-uncis-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[604, 1049, 813, 1303])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1125, 1050, 1308, 1297])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[583, 1530, 917, 1750])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1133, 1481, 1424, 1700])\n\n1 mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil.\n\nA. Anterior view\n\nB. Lateral view\n\nC. Upper view\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nChrysanthemi Flos菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[375, 554, 1637, 1787])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.\n\nA. Anterior view\n\nB. Lateral view\n\nC. Upper view\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[50, 32, 1898, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"<div align=\"center\">\n\n# Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[604, 1049, 813, 1303])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n1 cm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1125, 1050, 1308, 1297])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[583, 1530, 917, 1750])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nC\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1133, 1481, 1424, 1700])\n\n1 mm\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil.\n\nA. Anterior view\n\nB. Lateral view\n\nC. Upper view\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nChrysanthemi Flos菊花\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[375, 554, 1637, 1787])\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.\n\nA. Anterior view\n\nB. Lateral view\n\nC. Upper view\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[50, 32, 1898, 263])"},{"name":"53","text":"Official name: Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\n\nChinese name: 钩藤\n\nChinese phonetic name: Gouteng"},{"name":"53","text":"Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis is the dried hook-bearing branch of multiple species of Uncaria, among which are Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil., Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil., Uncaria macrophylla Wall., Uncaria sessilifructus Roxb. or Uncaria hirsuta Havil. (Rubiaceae). The hook-bearing branch is collected in autumn and winter, freed from leaves and cut into sections, dried under the sun to obtain Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis.\n\nBased on the chemical profile differences among the 5 species, they are separated into 4 parts under the collective name \"Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\".\n\nPart I Dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil. and Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil."},{"name":"53","text":"I. Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil.: Branch cylindrical or subsquare, 1-4 cm in length, 1-5 mm in diameter. Externally reddish-brown to purplish-brown or brown, glabrous with fine longitudinal striations. Nodes slightly raised and most bearing with two opposite downward curved hooks, sometimes with a hook at one side only; hook 1-4 cm in length, shaped like an anchor, with tapered apex and slightly rounded base. Dotted scars of falling petiole and ring-shaped scars of stipule visible on the branch connected with the hook base. Texture hard, light in weight. Fracture surface yellowish-brown in wood part, light brown to light yellow in pith. Odour slight; taste bland [Fig 1 (i)].\n\nII. Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.: Branch subsquare, 1-5 cm in length, 1-5 mm in diameter. Externally reddish-brown to purplish-brown or brown. Hook 1-5 cm in length, shaped like an anchor. Hook base slightly flat. Texture loose, light in weight. Fracture surface yellowish-brown in wood part and yellowish-white in pith [Fig 1 (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[45, 30, 1923, 261])"},{"name":"53","text":"I. Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil.: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle. Cortex cells in various shapes, arranged loosely in several layers, sometimes containing brown inclusions. Pericyclic fibres with thick walls, arranged in an interrupted ring. Phloem parenchymatous cells loosely arranged, various shapes. Xylem vessels arranged radially. Parenchymatous cells contain microcrystals of calcium oxalate. Pith broad, accounting for about half of the diameter of the transverse section [Fig 2 (i)].\n\nII. Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.: Pith broad, accounting for about half to two-thirds of the diameter of the transverse section [Fig 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"53","text":"I. Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil.: Colour light yellowish-brown to reddishbrown. Fibres in bundles or scattered, often broken, 9-28 $ \\mu $m in diameter, walls 3-13 $ \\mu $m thick; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate densely occurring in parenchymatous cells, sometimes lined in rows; bright white under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells brownish-yellow, polygonal or slightly elongated in surface view, 10-38 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Bordered-pitted vessels mostly broken, 11-62 $ \\mu $m in diameter, bordered pits arranged relatively closely. Starch granules occasionally found, simple starch granules subglobose to ovoid, 5-16 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with dotted or Y-shaped hilum; compound starch granules mostly composed of 2-6 units, 7-28 $ \\mu $m in diameter; black cruciate shape observed under the polarized microscope [Fig 3 (i)].\n\nII. Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.: Fibres 15-42 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, walls 5-15 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ thick; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Epidermal cells 10-37 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Bordered-pitted vessels 11-53 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Starch granules occasionally found, simple starch granules 5-16 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; compound starch granules 5-21 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter [Fig 3 (ii)].\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\nUncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\n\nUncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.\n\n菊花\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[383, 555, 896, 974])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[349, 1064, 901, 1445])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[351, 1542, 898, 1920])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[935, 521, 1652, 1916])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $"},{"name":"53","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Pericyclic fibres 4. Phloem 5. Xylem\n\n6. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 7. Pith\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nChrysanthemi Flos菊花\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[252, 535, 1726, 2010])"},{"name":"53","text":"A. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\nC. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope)\n\nD. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Cortex 3. Pericyclic fibres 4. Phloem\n\n5. Xylem 6. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate 7. Pith\n\n---\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[46, 24, 1942, 261])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[236, 390, 1749, 2126])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Fibres 2. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate in parenchymatous cells\n\n3. Epidermal cells 4. Bordered-pitted vessels 5. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[37, 28, 1941, 258])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[203, 414, 1749, 2122])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.\n\n</div>\n\n1. Fibres 2. Microcrystals of calcium oxalate in parenchymatous cells\n\n3. Epidermal cells 4. Bordered-pitted vessel 5. Starch granules\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[49, 38, 1942, 258])"},{"name":"53","text":"Corynoxeine standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of corynoxeine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nIsocorynoxeine standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of isocorynoxeine CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\nIsorhynchophylline standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of isorhynchophylline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol.\n\nRhynchophylline standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of rhynchophylline CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"53","text":"Prepare a mixture of dichloromethane, methanol and water (220:17:1, v/v)."},{"name":"53","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample (through a No.4 sieve) and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 1 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (32%, v/v) and 25 mL of ethyl acetate. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 20 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the filtrate to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 4 mL of 0.66 M hydrochloric acid. Transfer the extract to a 15-mL centrifuge tube. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Collect the supernatant. Adjust the pH to 9 with ammonium hydroxide solution (32%, v/v). Transfer the extract to a 25-mL separating funnel. Extract with 4 mL of ethyl acetate. Collect the ethyl acetate extract. Evaporate the extract to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"53","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately corynoxeine standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) isocorynoxeine standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), isorhynchophylline standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), rhynchophylline standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (5-18 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[458, 422, 936, 711])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[457, 790, 935, 1072])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[456, 1168, 934, 1451])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iv)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[458, 1586, 934, 1863])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) corynoxeine (ii) isocorynoxeine (iii) isorhynchophylline and (iv) rhynchophylline\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[377, 376, 1049, 1136])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis. extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Corynoxeine standard solution 2. Isorhynchophylline standard solution\n\n3. Isocorynoxeine standard solution 4. Rhynchophylline standard solution\n\n5. Test solution of\n\n(i) dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil.\n\n(ii) dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil.\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of corynoxeine, isocorynoxeine, isorhynchophylline and rhynchophylline (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"53","text":"Corynoxeine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.3 mg of corynoxeine CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol (90%).\n\nIsocorynoxeine standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of isocorynoxeine CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol (90%).\n\nIsorhynchophylline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (18 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.9 mg of isorhynchophylline CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (90%).\n\nRhynchophylline standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.4 mg of rhynchophylline CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol (90%).\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[47, 24, 1937, 263])"},{"name":"53","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample (through a No.4 sieve) and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 2 mL of ammonium hydroxide solution (32%, v/v). Allow the mixture to stand for 10 min. Add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 20 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the supernatants. Wash the residue with 10 mL of methanol. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants. Evaporate the combined supernatants to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in methanol (90%). Transfer the extract to a 10-mL volumetric flask and make up to the mark with methanol (90%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"53","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (245 nm) and a column $ (2.1\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.9 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 30^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 0.5 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.015%Diethylamine(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>88→84</td><td>12→16</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>3-9</td><td>84→72</td><td>16→28</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>9-11</td><td>72→67</td><td>28→33</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>11-13</td><td>67→65</td><td>33→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>13-17</td><td>65→55</td><td>35→45</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>17-20</td><td>55</td><td>45</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"53","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of corynoxeine Std-FP, isocorynoxeine Std-FP, isorhynchophylline Std-FP and rhynchophylline Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of corynoxeine, isocorynoxeine, isorhynchophylline and rhynchophylline should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of corynoxeine, isocorynoxeine, isorhynchophylline and rhynchophylline peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from corynoxeine, isocorynoxeine, isorhynchophylline and rhynchophylline peaks should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=12,bbox=[39, 25, 1933, 262])"},{"name":"53","text":"Separately inject corynoxeine Std-FP, isocorynoxeine Std-FP, isorhynchophylline Std-FP, rhynchophylline Std-FP and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of corynoxeine, isocorynoxeine, isorhynchophylline and rhynchophylline peaks in the chromatograms of corynoxeine Std-FP, isocorynoxeine Std-FP, isorhynchophylline Std-FP, rhynchophylline Std-FP and the retention times of the seven characteristic peaks [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify corynoxeine, isocorynoxeine, isorhynchophylline and rhynchophylline peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of corynoxeine Std-FP, isocorynoxeine Std-FP, isorhynchophylline Std-FP and rhynchophylline Std-FP. The retention times of corynoxeine, isocorynoxeine, isorhynchophylline and rhynchophylline peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil. extract and Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil. extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the seven characteristic peaks of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil. extract and Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil. extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.63</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.79</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,corynoxeine)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4(isorhynchophylline)</td><td>1.06</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(isocorynoxeine)</td><td>1.08</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>6(rhynchophylline)</td><td>1.16</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1.28</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[39, 29, 1939, 258])\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[332, 376, 1823, 913])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria rhynchophylla (Miq.) Miq. ex Havil. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=13,bbox=[333, 1121, 1821, 1657])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried hook-bearing branch of Uncaria sinensis (Oliv.) Havil. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above seven characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 6 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=14,bbox=[45, 39, 1928, 258])"},{"name":"53","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%.\n\nTotal ash: not more than 2.7%."},{"name":"53","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 10%."},{"name":"53","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 9.6%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 11%.\n\n![](page=15,bbox=[53, 33, 1937, 261])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/uncariae-ramulus-cum-uncis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis","la":"Uncariae Ramulus cum Uncis"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/valerianae-radix-et-rhizoma-v10-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma v10 e"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[681, 869, 1345, 1413])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[681, 1416, 1340, 1659])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[755, 1664, 984, 1854])\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1084, 1694, 1215, 1851])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 1mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified image of root\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface of rhizome\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 255])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[681, 869, 1345, 1413])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nA\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[681, 1416, 1340, 1659])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nB\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[755, 1664, 984, 1854])\n\n5 mm\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[1084, 1694, 1215, 1851])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nD 1mm\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma B. Magnified image of root\n\nC. Magnified image of cut surface of rhizome\n\nD. Magnified image of cut surface of root\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[21, 44, 1965, 255])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official name: Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma\n\nChinese name: 纈草\n\nChinese phonetic name: Xiecao"},{"name":"13","text":"Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma is the dried root and rhizome of Valeriana officinalis L. (Valerianaceae). The root and rhizome are collected during September to October, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma."},{"name":"13","text":"Rhizome obconical or cylindrical, short and horny, 4-22 mm in diameter; externally dull yellowishbrown to dark brown, with remnants of stem bases and leaf scars; usually entirely covered by numerous roots; fracture surface greyish-brown, irregular, sometimes hollow. Root almost cylindrical, longitudinally striated, 2-14 cm long, 1-3 mm in diameter; externally almost the same colour of the rhizome, slender, with fibrous lateral rootlets; fracture surface greyish-white, showing a narrow central stele. Texture brittle. Odour strong and characteristic; taste sweetish initially, becoming camphoraceous and somewhat bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Epidermis consists of 1 layer of cells, outer cell walls convex. Hypodermal cells 1 layer, comparatively large, polygonal or subrounded, walls slightly suberized; cortex broad, parenchymatous cells spheroidal or polygonal. Oil cells numerous, scattered in cortex, smaller than cortex parenchymatous cells. Endodermis consists of 1 layer of tangentially elongated cells. Sclerenchymatous cells occasionally singly scattered or several arranged in an interrupted ring outside endodermis. Vascular bundle collateral, arranged in a ring and surrounding the pith; phloem cells irregular in shape; cambium indistinct; vessels and fibres lignified. Pith small, clefts occasionally present [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[19, 38, 1947, 248])\n\nRhizome: Cork consists of several layers of cells, cells polygonal, comparatively large, and slightly suberized. Cortex cells several layers, spheroidal, polygonal or irregular in shape, walls slightly thickened. Endodermis consists of 1 layer of tangentially elongated cells. Wedge-shaped vascular bundles collateral, circularly arranged; phloem cells irregular in shape, loosely arranged; cambium indistinct; vessels and fibres lignified. Oil cells occasionally scattered in parenchyma. Pith relatively large, occasionally with clefts; stone cells present in the centre, with thick pitted walls and narrow lumen [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-yellow or greyish-brown. Starch granules abundant, 4-16 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, with cleft or stellate hilum, the compound granules composed of 2-5 units; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Stone cells single or in groups, polygonal, with thick walls and narrow lumen; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Cork cells polygonal, with irregularly thickened walls. Oil cells infrequently observed, scattered among parenchymatous cells. Hypodermal cells rectangular to polygonal, slightly thickened and elongated. Sclerenchymatous cells occasionally observed, rectangular, with moderately thickened wall and large lumen; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres mostly in bundles, pale yellow, with lignified cell walls and distinct pits, slender, 6-26 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Endodermis cells rectangular, showing sinuous tangential walls. Bordered-pitted or reticulate vessels 6-66 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[632, 42, 771, 114])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[1722, 48, 1852, 97])\n\nRanunculi Ternati Radix\n\n貓爪草\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 406, 1741, 2094])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C-D. Section magnified\n\n1. Epidermis 2. Hypodermis 3. Cortex 4. Oil cell 5. Endodermis\n\n6. Sclerenchymatous cell 7. Phloem 8. Xylem 9. Pith 10. Cleft\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1059, 91, 1143, 133])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[751, 145, 906, 186])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1693, 55, 1938, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[238, 411, 1741, 2123])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of rhizome of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil cell\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Endodermis 4. Phloem 5. Xylem 6. Oil cell 7. Pith\n\n8. Cleft 9. Stone cell\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[21, 43, 1966, 254])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[253, 636, 1686, 2100])"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Stone cells 3. Cork cells 4. Oil cell ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n5. Hypodermal cells (5-1 lateral view, 5-2 surface view)\n\n6. Sclerenchymatous cells 7. Fibres 8. Endodermal cells\n\n9. Vessels (9-1 bordered-pitted vessel, 9-2 reticulate vessels)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[21, 41, 1945, 234])"},{"name":"13","text":"Valerenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of valerenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, ethyl acetate and formic acid (8:2:0.2, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Spray reagent 1\n\nAdd slowly 10 mL of glacial acetic acid to 40 mL of hydrochloric acid.\n\nSpray reagent 2\n\nMix 0.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde into 10 mL of glacial acetic acid. Add 85 mL of methanol. Add cautiously 5 mL of sulphuric acid."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (400 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately valerenic acid standard solution and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent 1 and heat at about $ 1 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent 2 and heat at about $ 1 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 3 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[383, 367, 631, 677])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of valerenic acid\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[378, 847, 756, 1679])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Valerenic acid standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of valerenic acid (Fig. 5).\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[21, 41, 1944, 240])"},{"name":"13","text":"Valerenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (15 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.5 mg of valerenic acid CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (300 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 4 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Wash the residue with ethanol (70%). Combine the extracts and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (220 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>50→40</td><td>50→60</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-25</td><td>40→30</td><td>60→70</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>25-35</td><td>30</td><td>70</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of valerenic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of valerenic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of valerenic acid peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from valerenic acid peak should not be less than 24000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject valerenic acid Std-FP and the test solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of valerenic acid peak in the chromatogram of valerenic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify valerenic acid peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of valerenic acid Std-FP. The retention times of valerenic acid peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n</div>\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.52</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, valerenic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.11</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.47</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[334, 1589, 1812, 2209])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 11.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 5.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 9.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 25.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 25.0%."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/valerianae-radix-et-rhizoma","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma","la":"Valerianae Radix et Rhizoma"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/violae-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Violae Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[694, 922, 1326, 1274])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[589, 1319, 1353, 1852])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[694, 922, 1326, 1274])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[589, 1319, 1353, 1852])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Violae Herba\n\nChinese Name: 紫花地丁\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Zihuadiding"},{"name":"13","text":"Violae Herba is the dried herb of Viola yedoensis Makino (Violaceae). The whole plant is collected in spring and autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Violae Herba."},{"name":"13","text":"Usually crumpled into masses. The main roots long-conical, 1-3.5 mm in diameter, pale yellowish brown, with fine longitudinal wrinkles. Leaves basal, greyish-green to brownish-green, lanceolate or ovate-lanceolate when intact and flattened, 1.6-8.6 cm long, 0.6-4.7 cm wide; apex obtuse, base truncate or somewhat cordate, margin obtusely serrate, both surfaces pubescent; petioles slender, 2.2-13.5 cm long, with distinct narrow wings at the upper part. Capsules loculicidally 3-valved; seeds numerous, pale brownish-yellow. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and sticky (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"13","text":"Root: Cork consists of several layers of cells. Cortex broad, parenchymatous cells subrounded, filled with starch granules; scattered with mucilage cells and clusters of calcium oxalate. Phloem relatively broad. In the xylem, vessels usually scattered singly or in groups, arranged radially [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nLeaf: The upper epidermal cells relatively large, tangentially elongated. The lower epidermal cells relatively small. The mesophyll consists of 1 to 2 layers of palisade cells and several layers of spongy cells, some cells contain clusters of calcium oxalate. Vascular bundle of the midrib collateral. Collenchyma present on the inner sides of the upper and lower epidermis of the midrib [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour greyish-green to brownish-green. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4-68 $ \\mu $m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Non-glandular hairs unicellular, 56-366 $ \\mu $m long (occasionally up to 408 $ \\mu $m), 11-65 $ \\mu $m in diameter, with short cuticular striations. Upper epidermal cells relatively large, subpolygonal in surface view, anticlinal wall beaded. Lower epidermal cells subpolygonal in surface view, anticlinal wall slightly wavy, sometimes beaded, stomata anisocytic, numerous. Sclerenchymatous cells of testa slender, pits distinct. Cork cells pale brownish-yellow, subsquare or subpolygonal in surface view. Starch granules scattered, simple starch granules subrounded, 1-25 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum distinct, dotted, slit-shaped, V-shaped or stellate, striations indistinct; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-9 units. Vessels mainly spiral and reticulate, 4-41 $ \\mu $m in diameter (Fig. 3).\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[249, 456, 1736, 1952])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of root of Violae Herba\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Clusters of calcium oxalate, mucilage cell and starch granules in cortex\n\n1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Cluster of calcium oxalate 4. Mucilage cell 5. Phloem\n\n6. Xylem 7. Starch granule\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizon\n\n香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nViolae Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[234, 408, 1693, 1964])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Violae Herba\n\nA. Sketch of midvein B. Section illustration of midvein\n\n1. Upper epidermis 2. Collenchyma 3. Palisade tissue 4. Spongy tissue\n\n5. Cluster of calcium oxalate 6. Xylem 7. Phloem 8. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[30, 9, 1909, 263])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[296, 488, 1691, 1743])\n\n100 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Violae Herba\n\n1. Clusters of calcium oxalate 2. Non-glandular hair 3. Upper epidermal cells\n\n4. Lower epidermal cells 5. Sclerenchymatous cells of testa 6. Cork cells\n\n7. Starch granules 8. Vessels (8-1 spiral vessel, 8-2 reticulate vessel)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[69, 13, 1963, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Esculetin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of esculetin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butyl acetate, methanol and formic acid (15:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ nylon filter."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately esculetin standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of esculetin.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[33, 5, 1904, 265])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[376, 362, 827, 559])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of esculetin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Esculetin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of esculetin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with ethanol (70%). Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (340 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>95→85</td><td>5→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-40</td><td>85→74</td><td>15→26</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>74→95</td><td>26→5</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[66, 9, 1964, 260])"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of esculetin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of esculetin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of esculetin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from esculetin peak should not be less than 50000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.0 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject esculetin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of esculetin peak in the chromatogram of esculetin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify esculetin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of esculetin Std-FP. The retention times of esculetin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Violae Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Violae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, esculetin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.26</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.29</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.49</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[23, 1, 1912, 266])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[301, 369, 1862, 1031])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Violae Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins-Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 4.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 24.0% Acid-insoluble ash: not more than 8.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 13.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[66, 12, 1963, 261])"},{"name":"13","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 16.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 13.0%."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"13","text":"Esculetin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (1000 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of esculetin CRS and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nEsculetin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the esculetin Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol ( 70% ) to produce a series of solutions of 5,10,20,50,100 mg/L for esculetin."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh accurately 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Wash the residue with ethanol ( 70% ). Centrifuge at about $ 3 5 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/violae-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Violae Herba","la":"Violae Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/visci-herba-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Visci Herba v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[997, 913, 1368, 1159])"},"sections":[{"name":"13","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[997, 913, 1368, 1159])"},{"name":"13","text":"1 cm\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Visci Herba\n\nA. Visci Herba B. Magnified transverse section of stem\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[35, 8, 1900, 263])"},{"name":"13","text":"Official Name: Visci Herba\n\nChinese Name: 槲寄生\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Hujisheng"},{"name":"13","text":"Visci Herba is the dried stem and branch, together with the leaf of Viscum coloratum (Komar.) Nakai (Loranthaceae). They are collected in winter to next spring. The thick stem (over 10 mm in diameter) removed, then cut into sections, then dried under the sun or in shaded area; or dried after steaming to obtain Visci Herba. This plant mainly parasitizes on the host plant of Ulmus pumila L. and Pyrus spp.."},{"name":"13","text":"Stems cylindrical, normally 2-branched in fork shape, mostly broken at the nodes into segments of varying lengths, each 2-10 mm in diameter; externally yellowish-green, golden-yellow or yellowishbrown, with longitudinal wrinkles; the nodes swollen, with branches or scars of branches. Texture light and fragile, easily broken; fracture uneven, in transverse section the bark appears yellow, wood pale yellow, with distinct annular rings and radical rays, pith often inclined to one side. Leaves opposite on the tips of branches, easily fallen off, sessile; lamina narrowly elliptic-lanceolate, asymmetrical, 2-7 cm long, 0.5-1.5 cm wide; apex obtusely rounded, base cuneate, margin entire; externally yellowish-green, with fine wrinkles, quinquenerved, the middle 3 distinct. Texture coriaceous. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter and sticky on chewing (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[67, 12, 1965, 258])"},{"name":"13","text":"Stem: Epidermal cells rectangular, covered with orange cuticle, 19-80 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $ thick. Cortex consists of 8-30 layers of parenchymatous cells. Pericycle fibres arranged in bundles of several dozens of cells, strongly lignified. Phloem consists of 5-9 layers of parenchymatous cells. Cambium indistinct. Vessels and xylem rays arranged radially, scattered with fibre bundles; vessels surrounded by fibres. Pith distinct. Parenchymatous cells contains clusters of calcium oxalate and a few prisms. Stem scattered with numerous fibres, stone cells are sometimes visible [Fig. 2 (i)].\n\nLeaf: Upper and lower epidermis consist of 1 layer of cells, covered with cuticle. Clusters of calcium oxalate scattered in parenchymatous cells. Vascular bundles collateral, each with fibre bundles above and below it [Fig. 2 (ii)]."},{"name":"13","text":"Colour pale yellow to brown. Epidermal cells yellowish-green, subsquare, stomata paracytic. Stone cells subsquare, subpolygonal or irregular, 15-110 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Vessels mainly reticulate and spiral. Heterogeneous sclerenchymatous cells irregular, wall relatively thickened, slightly lignified, lumen large. Clusters of calcium oxalate 14-50 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Prisms of calcium oxalate relatively few, 4-20 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres arranged in bundles, wall relatively thickened and straight, somewhat sinuous and pitted (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[30, 4, 1908, 267])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[254, 446, 1730, 2088])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of stem of Visci Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Crystals of calcium oxalate D. Stone cells 1. Cuticle 2. Epidermis 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4. Cortex 5. Pericycle fibre bundles 6. Phloem 7. Cambium 8. Xylem 9. Pith 10. Xylem fibres 11. Xylem rays 12. Prisms of calcium oxalate 13. Stone cells\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\nVisci Herba Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[248, 393, 1739, 2175])\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of leaf of Visci Herba A. Sketch B. Section illustration 1. Cuticle 2. Upper epidermis 3. Clusters of calcium oxalate 4. Xylem fibres 5. Xylem 6. Phloem 7. Pericycle fibre bundles 8. Lower epidermis\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[31, 7, 1915, 264])\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[383, 459, 1612, 2060])"},{"name":"13","text":"1. Epidermal cells (in lateral view)\n\n2. Epidermal cells with stomata (in surface view) 3. Stone cells\n\n4. Vessels 5. Heterogeneous sclerenchymatous cells 6. Clusters of calcium oxalate\n\n7. Prisms of calcium oxalate 8. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[69, 14, 1965, 259])"},{"name":"13","text":"Oleanolic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of oleanolic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 0.5 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-hexane, ethyl acetate and acetic acid (20:6:1, v/v)."},{"name":"13","text":"Add slowly 10 mL of sulphuric acid to 90 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of ethanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 1 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"13","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately oleanolic acid standard solution and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 80^{\\circ}\\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 15 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of oleanolic acid.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[23, 2, 1915, 266])\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[377, 364, 1187, 1264])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) oleanolic acid and (ii) syringin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"13","text":"Syringin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)"},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 0.2 mg of syringin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol ( 75% )."},{"name":"13","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of ethanol (75%). Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with ethanol (75%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"13","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (264 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The internal diameter of the inlet column tubing is about 0.5 mm. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[67, 5, 1969, 262])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-30</td><td>95→85</td><td>5→15</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-60</td><td>85→65</td><td>15→35</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"13","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of syringin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of syringin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of syringin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from syringin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"Separately inject syringin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of syringin peak in the chromatogram of syringin Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify syringin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of syringin Std-FP. The retention times of syringin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Visci Herba extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 7, 1911, 265])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Visci Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker,syringin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.08</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.56</td><td>$\\pm0.05$</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.22</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2.33</td><td>$\\pm0.03$</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[297, 877, 1823, 1579])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Visci Herba extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"13","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[67, 10, 1965, 256])"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/visci-herba","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Visci Herba","la":"Visci Herba"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/viticis-fructus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Viticis Fructus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[617, 801, 1346, 1461])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Vitex trifolia L. var. simplicifolia Cham.\n\nA. Viticis Fructus B. Magnified fruits and transverse section\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[658, 1712, 1384, 2411])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Vitex trifolia L. A. Viticis Fructus B. Magnified fruits and transverse section\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[617, 801, 1346, 1461])\n\nFigure 1 (i) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Vitex trifolia L. var. simplicifolia Cham.\n\nA. Viticis Fructus B. Magnified fruits and transverse section\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[658, 1712, 1384, 2411])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 (ii) A photograph of dried ripe fruit of Vitex trifolia L. A. Viticis Fructus B. Magnified fruits and transverse section\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[32, 9, 1901, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Viticis Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 蔓荊子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Manjingzi"},{"name":"14","text":"Viticis Fructus is the dried ripe fruit of Vitex trifolia L. var. simplicifolia Cham. or Vitex trifolia L. (Verbenaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in autumn, foreign matter removed, then dried under the sun to obtain Viticis Fructus."},{"name":"14","text":"Spheroidal, 4-6 mm in diameter. Externally greyish-black to blackish-brown, bearing 4 longitudinal shallow furrows, covered with greyish-white frost-like hairs; apex slightly concave, with greyish-white persistent calyx and a short fruit stalk at base. Calyx 1/3 to 2/3 in length of fruit, 5-lobed, 2 of the lobes relatively deep and covered with densely pubescence. Texture hard and light in weight, uneasily broken. Transverse section shows 4 locules, each with 1 seed. Odour characteristic and aromatic; taste weak and slightly pungent [Fig. 1 (i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Exocarp consists of 1 layer of flat cells with brown content inclusions, covered with cuticle, with glandular hairs. Mesocarp broad, cells subsquare to subrounded, walls slightly lignified, with distinct pits; small vascular bundles arranged in a ring. Endocarp consists of 3-8 layers of stone cells. The outer layer of testa consists of 1 layer of small flat parenchymatous cells, with 2-6 layers of larger cells beneath them, with reticulate striations [Fig. 2 (i) and (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-brown. Exocarp cells polygonal, with cuticular striations and scars of glandular and non-glandular hairs. Epidermal cells of calyx subrounded to rectangle, wall sinuous. Testa cells rounded to subrounded, 20-70 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with reticulate striation and lignified wall. Non-glandular hairs of calyx 2- to 3-celled, apical cell relatively wide with warty protrusions. Most of glandular hairs consist of 2- to 6-celled head and unicellular stalk, sometimes with unicellular head and 1- to 2-celled stalk. Mesocarp cells subsquare to subpolygonal to subrounded, wall slightly lignified, and with distinct pits. Stone cells of endocarp elliptical, rectangle or subsquare, 10-66 $ \\mu m $ in diameter [Fig. 3 (i) and (ii)].\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[80, 4, 1928, 263])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[236, 430, 1732, 2055])\n\nFigure 2 (i) Microscopic features of transverse section of fruit of Vitex trifolia L. var. simplicifolia Cham.\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Exocarp epidermal cells with glandular hair D. Vascular bundle E. Endocarp stone cells and testa\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Endocarp 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Testa (5a. Parenchymatous cells in outer layer of testa, 5b. Inner layers of testa cells with reticulate striation)\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon 8. Glandular hair\n\n桂枝 Cinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nViticis Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[319, 443, 899, 951])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[335, 1178, 896, 1431])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1013, 738, 1662, 1423])\n\n200 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[340, 1603, 797, 1942])\n\n50 μm\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1016, 1571, 1582, 1947])\n\n50μm\n\nFigure 2 (ii) Microscopic features of transverse section of fruit of Vitex trifolia I\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Epidermal cells and glandular hairs\n\nD. Vascular bundle E. Endocarp stone cells and testa\n\n1. Exocarp 2. Mesocarp 3. Endocarp 4. Vascular bundle\n\n5. Testa (5a. Parenchymatous cells in outer layer of testa, 5b. Inner layers of testa cells with reticulate striation)\n\n6. Endosperm 7. Cotyledon 8. Glandular hair\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[283, 464, 1681, 1643])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 (i) Microscopic features of powder of fruit of Vitex trifolia L. var. simplicifolia Cham. (under the light microscope)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Exocarp cells 2. Epidermal cells of calyx\n\n3. Testa cells with reticulate striation 4. Non-glandular hairs\n\n5. Glandular hairs 6. Mesocarp cells 7. Stone cells\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\nCinnamomi Ramulus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nViticis Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[280, 460, 1709, 1632])\n\nFigure 3 (ii) Microscopic features of powder of fruit of Vitex trifolia L. (under the light microscope)\n\n1. Exocarp cells 2. Epidermal cells of calyx\n\n3. Testa cells with reticulate striation 4. Non-glandular hairs\n\n5. Glandular hairs 6. Mesocarp cells 7. Stone cells\n\nFootnote: Microscopic features of powder have no significant differences between the fruit of Vitex trifolia L. var. simplicifolia Cham. and Vitex trifolia L.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[29, 8, 1904, 261])"},{"name":"14","text":"Casticin standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of casticin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ) and ethyl acetate (1:2, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 0.5 mL of ethanol (70%)."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately casticin standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2-3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 5 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of casticin.\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nViticis Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[420, 369, 1053, 762])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of casticin\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"Casticin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (35 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.7 mg of casticin CRS and dissolve in 20 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (160 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m RC filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (350 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.4% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-40</td><td>75→20</td><td>25→80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>40-60</td><td>20</td><td>80</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[30, 6, 1904, 264])"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of casticin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of casticin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of casticin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from casticin peak should not be less than 100000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)]."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject casticin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of casticin peak in the chromatogram of casticin Std-FP and the retention times of the three characteristic peaks [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)] in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify casticin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of casticin Std-FP. The retention times of casticin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Viticis Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the three characteristic peaks of Viticis Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.46</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.89</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,casticin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr></table>\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\nLophatheri Herba淡竹葉\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n槐角\n\nSophorae Fructus\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\n紫菀\n\nViticis Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[290, 362, 1824, 1056])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (i) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe fruit of Vitex trifolia L. var. simplicifolia Cham. extract\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[289, 1203, 1827, 1762])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 (ii) A reference fingerprint chromatogram of dried ripe fruit of Vitex trifolia L. extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above three characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the respective reference fingerprint chromatograms [Fig. 5 (i) or (ii)].\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[48, 16, 1899, 264])"},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 12.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 7.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"14","text":"Casticin standard stock solution, Std-Stock (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh accurately 1.0 mg of casticin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nCasticin standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the casticin Std-Stock, dilute with methanol to produce a series of solutions of 1,10,20,35,50 mg/L for casticin."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/viticis-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Viticis Fructus","la":"Viticis Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/vladimiriae-radix-v12-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vladimiriae Radix v12 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[392, 844, 1442, 1885])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Vladimiriae Radix\n\nA. Vladimiriae Radix\n\nB. Root stock with black viscous gelatinous residue\n\nC. Magnified image of fracture surface\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[49, 20, 1922, 258])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[392, 844, 1442, 1885])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Vladimiriae Radix\n\nA. Vladimiriae Radix\n\nB. Root stock with black viscous gelatinous residue\n\nC. Magnified image of fracture surface\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[49, 20, 1922, 258])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Vladimiriae Radix\n\nChinese name: 川木香\n\nChinese phonetic name: Chuanmuxiang"},{"name":"14","text":"Vladimiriae Radix is the dried root of Dolomiaea souliei (Franch.) C.Shih [syn. Vladimiria souliei (Franch.) Ling] (Asteraceae). The root is collected in autumn, freed from rootlet, soil and gelatinous matter on root stock, dried under the sun to obtain Vladimiriae Radix."},{"name":"14","text":"Cylindrical or semi-cylindrical with longitudinal furrows, slightly curved, 10-30 cm in length, 10-30 mm in diameter. Externally yellowish-brown or deep brown, longitudinally wrinkled, showing loofah-like fine veins at the place where the outer bark fallen off, root stocks occasionally with black viscous gelatinous residue, commonly known as \"oil head\". Relatively light in weight, texture hard and fragile, easily broken, fracture surface yellowish-white or yellow, with sparsely scattered deep yellow oil dots and some clefts, wood broad, with radial striations, sometimes the central part rotten-wood-like. Odour slightly aromatic, taste bitter, sticky on chewing (Fig. 1).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[39, 22, 1932, 257])"},{"name":"14","text":"Cork consists of several layers of brown cells. Oil cavities scattered in parenchyma of rays and pith. Phloem: Phloem rays relatively broad; phloem sieve tube groups alternately arranged with phloem fibre bundles. Cambium ring undulated. Xylem vessels alternately arranged with xylem fibre bundles. Phloem fibres and xylem fibres mostly in bundles, lignified, sometimes accompanied by stone cells. Pith intact or withered. Inulin observed in parenchymatous cells (before staining process) (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Inulin abundant, in fan-shaped or irregular in shaped; faint bluish-white under the polarized microscope. Stone cells quite few, sometimes visible, oblong or irregular in shaped, 25-40 $ \\mu $ m in diameter, up to 127 $ \\mu $ m in length, with distinct pits and pit-canals; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Fibres mostly in bundle, long fusiform, ends oblique, or slightly truncated, lignified, 14-37 $ \\mu $ m in diameter; polychromatic under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate, sometimes bordered-pitted visible, 13-142 $ \\mu $ m in diameter. Fragments of oil cavities sometimes visible. Cork cells subsquare, irregular or polygonal in surface of view (Fig. 3).\n\nCampsis Flos\n\n凌霄花\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos苦杏仁菊花\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 425, 1740, 2037])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Vladimiriae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified image of phloem and xylem D. Stone cell\n\n1. Cork 2. Oil cavity 3. Phloem fibre 4. Phloem ray 5. Phloem sieve tube\n\n6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray 9. Xylem fibre 10. Pith 11. Stone cell ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\nCampsis Flos凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\nAilanthi Cortex\n\n椿皮\n\nUncariae Ramulus cum Uncis\n\n重樓\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma珠子参\n\n鈎藤\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤\n\nSinomenii Caulis\n\n川木香\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum Chrysanthemi Flos\n\n苦杏仁 菊花\n\nVladimiriae Radix\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[242, 423, 1746, 2094])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Vladimiriae Radix\n\n1. Inulins 2. Stone cell 3. Fibres 4. Reticulate vessels\n\n5. Fragments of oil cavity 6. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[41, 35, 1943, 256])"},{"name":"14","text":"Costunolide standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of costunolide CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of absolute ethanol.\n\nDehydrocostus lactone standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of dehydrocostus lactone CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of absolute ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane, acetone and ethyl acetate (16:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"14","text":"Add slowly 5 mL of sulphuric acid to 95 mL of ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of absolute ethanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 1 h. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 100-mL round-bottomed flask. Repeat the extraction one more time and combine the filtrates. Evaporate the combined filtrates to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of absolute ethanol. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately costunolide standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), dehydrocostus lactone standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 7 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ until the spots or bands become visible (about 5 min). Examine the plate under visible light. Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nAgastaches Herba\n\n藿香\n\nCampsis Flos\n\n凌霄花\n\n乳香\n\nOlibanum\n\n重楼\n\nParidis Rhizoma\n\nAilanthi Cortex Uncariae\n\n椿皮\n\nPanacis Majoris Rhizoma\n\n珠子参\n\n木通 Akebiae Caulis\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n青風藤 Sinomenii Caulis\n\nArmeniacae Semen Amarum\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[380, 427, 820, 788])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[383, 921, 757, 1328])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) costunolide and (ii) dehydrocostus lactone\n\n</div>\n\n白芷 Angelicae Dahuricae Radix\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[381, 380, 1057, 1215])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Vladimiriae Radix extract observed under visible light after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Costunolide standard solution 2. Dehydrocostus lactone standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Costunolide standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (500 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.5 mg of costunolide CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of absolute ethanol.\n\nDehydrocostus lactone standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (900 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 4.5 mg of dehydrocostus lactone CRS and dissolve in 5 mL of absolute ethanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.3 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 10-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of absolute ethanol. Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 1 h. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with absolute ethanol. Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $ m nylon filter.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[40, 23, 1940, 255])"},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (203 nm) and a column $ ( 2. 1 \\times1 0 0 \\mathrm{~ m m} ) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel $ ( 1. 7 \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.2 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>65</td><td>35</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>3-7</td><td>65→50</td><td>35→50</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>7-15</td><td>50→45</td><td>50→55</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-16</td><td>45→10</td><td>55→90</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of costunolide Std-FP and dehydrocostus lactone Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone peaks should not be less than 30000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 5 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 6 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject costunolide Std-FP, dehydrocostus lactone Std-FP and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone peaks in the chromatograms of costunolide Std-FP, dehydrocostus lactone Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of costunolide Std-FP and dehydrocostus lactone Std-FP. The retention times of costunolide and dehydrocostus lactone peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[35, 7, 1937, 261])\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Vladimiriae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Vladimiriae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.20</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.27</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.77</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.80</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(marker, costunolide)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6(dehydrocostus lactone)</td><td>1.06</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[332, 1102, 1821, 1633])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Vladimiriae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6).\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[45, 38, 1941, 250])"},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 3.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.65%."},{"name":"14","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 11%."},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 28%. Ethanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 22%.\n\n![](page=11,bbox=[50, 28, 1938, 259])"},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/vladimiriae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vladimiriae Radix","la":"Vladimiriae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/wenyujin-rhizoma-concisum-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[442, 858, 1516, 1804])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n</div>"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[442, 858, 1516, 1804])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\nChinese Name: 片藍黄\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Pianjianghuang"},{"name":"12","text":"Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum is the dried rhizome of Curcuma wenyujin Y. H. Chen et C. Ling (Zingiberaceae). The rhizome is collected in winter after stem and leaf withered, washed clean, foreign matter removed, cut into thick longitudinal slices while fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum."},{"name":"12","text":"Elliptic or irregular slices, 1.6-10.8 cm long, 0.9-4.5 cm wide, 1-9 mm thick. The outer bark greyish-yellow, rough and uneven with numerous annular nodes and rootlets scars, cut surface yellowish-white to brownish-yellow, with an annular ring and numerous dotted vascular bundles. Texture hard and fragile, fracture greyish-white to brownish-yellow, slightly starchy. Odour characteristic and aromatic; taste slightly bitter, pungent and cool (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of irregularly shaped cells, cell flattened, regularly arranged, cell walls suberized. Cortex broad, scattered with leaf-trace vascular bundles. Endodermis distinct, cells small. Oil cells scattered in cortex and stele. Vascular bundles collateral, tightly arranged, relatively small on the outer part of stele, xylem vessels few, sometimes 1-2 only. Starch granules numerous, gelatinized, elliptic to orbicular-ovate or short-rod-like in shape (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[30, 21, 1955, 252])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour greyish-yellow. Oil cells elliptic to rounded-ovate, relatively large, 34-124 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, full of yellow or orange oily masses. Starch granules abundant, orbicular-ovate, elliptic or short-rod-like in shape, 13-69 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 5-52 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter, hilum usually eccentric, locates on the narrow end, striations visible; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly spiral, 10-94 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Non-glandular hair unicellular, usually broken, with pointed tip. Cork cells polygonal, with thickened wall (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[103, 188, 203, 239])\n\n天葵子\n\n芥子\n\nSinapis Semen\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片藍黄\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 400, 1752, 2121])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Oil cell D. Vascular bundle E. Starch granules (under the light microscope) F. Starch granules (under the polarized microscope)\n\n</div>\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1026, 137])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1756, 51, 1911, 108])\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\nGlechomae Herba\n\n連錢草\n\nHoveniae Semen枳椇子\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[327, 457, 980, 951])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1009, 455, 1667, 946])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[326, 978, 974, 1464])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1012, 983, 1665, 1476])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[322, 1513, 971, 2001])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1008, 1510, 1661, 2001])\n\n50 μm\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Wenyujin Rhizome Concisum\n\n</div>\n\n1. Oil cells 2. Starch granules 3. Spiral vessel 4. Non-glandular hair 5. Cork cells\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Curcumenol standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of curcumenol CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of petroleum ether (60-80 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ), ethyl acetate and methanol (9:1:0.5, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 5 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately curcumenol standard solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (254 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[375, 1759, 760, 2092])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of curcumenol\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[925, 86, 1028, 139])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[376, 370, 791, 1215])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum extract observed under UV light (254 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Curcumenol standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of curcumenol (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Curcumenol standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (50 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of curcumenol CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 20 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (210 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.04%Trifluoroacetic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Trifluoroacetic acid:Acetonitrile(0.04:99.96,v/v)(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-35</td><td>62</td><td>38</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>35-45</td><td>62→47</td><td>38→53</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>47→38</td><td>53→62</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of curcumenol Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of curcumenol should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of curcumenol peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from curcumenol peak should not be less than 9000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject curcumenol Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of curcumenol peak in the chromatogram of curcumenol Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify curcumenol peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of curcumenol Std-FP. The retention times of curcumenol peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[34, 39, 1948, 256])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1(marker, curcumenol)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.05</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.30</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.74</td><td>±0.04</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[347, 873, 1819, 1500])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[104, 186, 203, 244])"},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.5%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 2.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Toluene distillation method: not more than 17.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 8.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/wenyujin-rhizoma-concisum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum","la":"Wenyujin Rhizoma Concisum"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/wikstroemiae-radix-v9-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Wikstroemiae Radix v9 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[625, 840, 1342, 1920])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Wikstroemiae Radix A. Oblique slices of Wikstroemiae Radix B. Short pieces of Wikstroemiae Radix"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[625, 840, 1342, 1920])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Wikstroemiae Radix A. Oblique slices of Wikstroemiae Radix B. Short pieces of Wikstroemiae Radix"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Wikstroemiae Radix\n\nChinese Name: 了哥王\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Liaogewang"},{"name":"12","text":"Wikstroemia Radix is the dried root of Wikstroemia indica (L.) C. A. Mey. (Thymelaeaceae). The root is collected in autumn to early spring, washed clean, foreign matter removed, cut into short pieces or oblique slices while fresh, then dried under the sun to obtain Wikstroemia Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Short pieces or oblique slices. Short pieces cylindrical, often branched, 1-13 cm long, 4-50 mm in diameter; oblique slices 1-12.5 cm long, 0.5-7.5 cm wide, 0.2-0.4 cm thick. Externally yellowishbrown to pale brown, with longitudinal wrinkles, transverse elongated lenticels and small protruding rootlet scars. Texture hard and flexible, fracture greyish-yellow, the root bark easily stripped from the wood; Odour slight; taste slightly bitter, pungent on chewing (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of several layers of rectangular suberized cells. Cortex scattered with abundant fibres. Phloem broad, scattered with abundant fibres. Phloem ray consists of 1-3 rows of cells, radially elongated and arranged radially. Cambium consists of 1 layer of cells, arranged in a ring. Xylem vessels singly scattered or several in groups. Xylem ray consists of 1-3 rows of cells, radially elongated and arranged radially (Fig. 2).\n\n![](page=2,bbox=[29, 19, 1956, 254])"},{"name":"12","text":"Colour pale yellowish-brown. Fibres abundant, mostly scattered singly, long fusiform, easily broken, 8-45 $ \\mu $m in diameter; bluish-white under the polarized microscope. Starch granules few, mostly simple starch granules, subrounded to elliptic, 4-35 $ \\mu $m in diameter, hilum dotted and locate on a side; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly reticulate, easily broken. Cork cells polygonal in surface view, sometimes contain yellowish-brown or brownish-red resinous contents. Crystals of calcium oxalate few, singly scattered, varying in shape; yellowish-white or polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n了哥王\n\nWikstroemiae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[102, 188, 203, 241])\n\n天葵子\n\nWenyujin Rhizoma Concisum\n\n片蘴黄\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[243, 453, 1691, 2063])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Wikstroemiae Radix A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Fibres 1. Cork 2. Cortex 3. Phloem 4. Fibres 5. Phloem ray 6. Cambium 7. Xylem 8. Xylem ray\n\nAsparagi Radix\n\nBletillae Rhizoma白及\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[926, 87, 1025, 137])\n\n毛冬青\n\nIlicis Pubescentis Radix et Caulis\n\nElephantopi Herba地膽草\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[438, 418, 961, 813])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[991, 419, 1517, 813])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[436, 830, 964, 1217])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[988, 827, 1519, 1222])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[438, 1232, 967, 1631])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[992, 1235, 1518, 1618])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[436, 1643, 962, 2034])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[996, 1638, 1514, 2038])\n\n50 $ \\mu \\mathrm{m} $\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Wikstroemiae Radix\n\n1. Fibre 2. Starch granules 3. Reticulate vessels 4. Cork cells 5. Crystals of calcium oxalate (->)\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"12","text":"Daphnoretin standard solution\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of daphnoretin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol.\n\nPrepare a mixture of methanol and water (4:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol. Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a TLC polyamide plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately daphnoretin standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (2 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the TLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the TLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[377, 1804, 941, 1942])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structure of daphnoretin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[924, 86, 1029, 139])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[375, 372, 799, 1216])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference TLC chromatogram of Wikstroemiae Radix extract observed under UV light (366 nm)\n\n</div>\n\n1. Daphnoretin standard solution 2. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spot or band with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value, corresponding to that of daphnoretin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Daphnoretin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (10 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.1 mg of daphnoretin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of acetonitrile."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.2 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 25 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (320 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size, 190 $ \\mathrm{\\AA} $ pore size and 12% carbon loading). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>1% Formic acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-10</td><td>80→77</td><td>20→23</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>10-20</td><td>77→71</td><td>23→29</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>20-30</td><td>71</td><td>29</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>71→60</td><td>29→40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>45-50</td><td>60→58</td><td>40→42</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>50-60</td><td>58</td><td>42</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of daphnoretin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of daphnoretin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of daphnoretin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from daphnoretin peak should not be less than 20000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 3 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject daphnoretin Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of daphnoretin peak in the chromatogram of daphnoretin Std-FP and the retention times of the six characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify daphnoretin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of daphnoretin Std-FP. The retention times of daphnoretin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Wikstroemiae Radix extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[35, 38, 1949, 261])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the six characteristic peaks of Wikstroemiae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.44</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.67</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3(marker,daphnoretin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.39</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1.61</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1.75</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[346, 953, 1817, 1591])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Wikstroemiae Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above six characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): The CMM shall meet the requirements for arsenic, lead and mercury as specified in Appendix V. For cadmium, Wikstroemiae Radix should meet the specified limit of not more than 13.5 mg/kg, when the CMM will be processed as a decoction in the final consumption form; otherwise, the limit for cadmium specified in Appendix V shall be applied.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[105, 184, 202, 245])\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[1040, 149, 1134, 202])"},{"name":"12","text":"5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Total ash: not more than 5.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 13.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (hot extraction method): not less than 8.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 5.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/wikstroemiae-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Wikstroemiae Radix","la":"Wikstroemiae Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/xanthii-fructus-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Xanthii Fructus v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[715, 974, 1308, 1330])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[738, 1405, 1276, 1773])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1900, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"14","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[715, 974, 1308, 1330])\n\n![](page=0,bbox=[738, 1405, 1276, 1773])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[33, 9, 1900, 263])"},{"name":"14","text":"Official Name: Xanthii Fructus\n\nChinese Name: 蒼耳子\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Cang'erzi"},{"name":"14","text":"Xanthii Fructus is the dried ripe fruit with involucre of Xanthium sibiricum Patr. (Asteraceae). The plant is collected in autumn when the fruit is ripe, and dried under the sun. The fruit with involucre gathered, foreign matter removed to obtain Xanthii Fructus."},{"name":"14","text":"Fusiform to ovoid, 0.6-1.8 cm long, 3-9 mm in diameter. Externally greenish-yellow to brownish yellow, with hooked spines throughout, and with 2 relatively thick spines at the apex and a fruit stalk scar at the base. Texture hard and tough. At the centre of transverse section, a septum separating 2 loculi clearly visible; each locule contains an achene. Achene ellipsoid to slightly fusiform, relatively even at one side, pericarp thin and fragile, greyish-black, with longitudinal wrinkles. Seed somewhat ellipsoid, beaked at the point of attachment to the pericarp. Testa membranous, yellowish-grey, cotyledons 2, oily. Odour slight; taste slightly bitter (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"14","text":"Epidermal cells of involucre subrectangular or subrounded. Parenchyma of involucre consists of several layers of cells. The outer involucre fibres arranged longitudinally in the lengthwise direction of the fruit, the inner involucre fibres arranged vertically, with vascular bundles scattered among them. Epidermis of pericarp consists of 1 layer of cells. A dark brown-pigment layer beneath pericarp. Several layers of pericarp fibres are arranged in a compact manner. Epidermis of testa consists of 1 layer of flat cells, with vascular bundles scattered underneath it. Endotesta consists of 1 layer of flat cells. Cotyledon cells filled with aleurone grains (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"14","text":"Colour greyish-green to pale yellowish-brown. Epidermal cells of pericarp rectangular, often with adherent pigment and pericarp fibres on the lower layer. Epidermal cells of testa subrectangular or subrounded, wall relatively thick. Endotesta cells polygonal, with papillae. Involucre fibres often present in bundles, arranged in a crisscross pattern; single fibres fusiform, 5-25 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, wall relatively thick, with pits visible; yellowish-white under the polarized microscope. Pericarp fibres mostly in bundles, single fibres fusiform, 5-24 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, pits distinct; pale white under the polarized microscope. Cotyledon cells subrectangular, subrounded or polygonal, containing aleurone grains and oil droplets. Lignified parenchymatous cells subrectangular, with distinct pits. Vessels mainly spiral and reticulate, 3-20 $ \\mu m $ in diameter. Nonglandular hairs occasionally found, multicellular (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[21, 6, 1915, 264])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[234, 414, 1722, 2173])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Xanthii Fructus\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration\n\n1. Epidermis of involucre 2. Involucre parenchyma 3. Involucre fibre\n\n4. Vascular bundle 5. Epidermis of pericarp 6. Pericarp fibre 7. Epidermis of testa\n\n8. Endotesta 9. Cotyledon 10. Pigment layer\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\nXanthii Fructus Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[285, 446, 1717, 2166])"},{"name":"14","text":"1. Epidermal cells of pericarp 2. Epidermal cells of testa 3. Endotesta cells 4. Involucre fibres\n\n5. Pericarp fibres 6. Cotyledon cells 7. Lignified parenchymatous cell\n\n8. Vessels (8-1 spiral vessel, 8-2 reticulate vessel) 9. Non-glandular hair\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[29, 6, 1904, 262])"},{"name":"14","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol.\n\n1,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol."},{"name":"14","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butyl acetate, water and formic acid (7:2.5:2.5, v/v). Freshly prepare and use the upper layer."},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 15-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (140 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 2 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL round-bottomed flask. Evaporate the solvent to dryness at reduced pressure in a rotary evaporator. Dissolve the residue in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chlorogenic acid standard solution, 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and place the HPTLC plate in the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully tilt the chamber to allow sufficient solvent to pass from the trough containing the solvent to the other containing the HPTLC plate for development. Develop over a path of about 6 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[67, 9, 1967, 259])\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[423, 612, 1019, 1014])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[411, 1103, 1232, 1556])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) chlorogenic acid and (ii) 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid\n\n</div>"},{"name":"14","text":"Chlorogenic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (80 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 2.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (60%).\n\n1,5-Dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (20 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS and dissolve in 50 mL of methanol (60%).\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[22, 6, 1903, 264])"},{"name":"14","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 10 mL of methanol (60%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 1 8 0 0 \\times g $ for 10 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 25-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Wash the residue with methanol (60%). Combine the solutions and make up to the mark with methanol (60%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"14","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (254 nm) and a column $ (4. 6 \\times 2 5 0 \\mathrm{~ m m}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The column temperature is maintained at $ 4 0 \\mathrm{~ o C} $ during the separation. The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>0.1% Phosphoric acid(%，v/v)</td><td>Methanol(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-15</td><td>90-80</td><td>10-20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>15-30</td><td>80-60</td><td>20-40</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>30-45</td><td>60</td><td>40</td><td>isocratic</td></tr><tr><td>45-60</td><td>60-20</td><td>40-80</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"14","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peaks should not be less than 70000 and 90000 theoretical plates respectively.\n\nThe R value between peak 2 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"Separately inject chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peaks in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP, 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chlorogenic acid Std-FP and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-FP. The retention times of chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Xanthii Fructus extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Xanthii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.62</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>2(marker, chlorogenic acid)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1.07</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1.53</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>5(1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid)</td><td>1.58</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[36, 99, 209, 194])\n\n巴戟天\n\nMorindae Officinalis Radix\n\nApocyni Veneti Folium\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[294, 380, 1835, 910])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Xanthii Fructus extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"14","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 5.0%\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.5%\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 9.0%.\n\n![](page=10,bbox=[66, 12, 1963, 260])"},{"name":"14","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 7.0%."},{"name":"14","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"14","text":"Mixed chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid standard stock solution, Std-Stock (240 mg/L for chlorogenic acid and 80 mg/L for1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid)\n\nWeigh accurately 6.0 mg of chlorogenic acid CRS and 2.0 mg of 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid CRS, and dissolve in 25 mL of methanol (60%).\n\nMixed chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed chlorogenic acid and 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid Std-Stock, dilute with methanol (60%) to produce a series of solutions of 15,30,60,120,240 mg/L for chlorogenic acid and 5,10,20,40,80 mg/L for 1,5-dicaffeoylquinic acid."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/xanthii-fructus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Xanthii Fructus","la":"Xanthii Fructus"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/zanthoxyli-radix-v5-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zanthoxyli Radix v5 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[542, 876, 1575, 1808])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 10, 1902, 263])"},"sections":[{"name":"12","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[542, 876, 1575, 1808])\n\n![](page=1,bbox=[31, 10, 1902, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Official Name: Zanthoxyli Radix\n\nChinese Name: 兩面針\n\nChinese Phonetic Name: Liangmianzhen"},{"name":"12","text":"Zanthoxyli Radix is the dried root of Zanthoxylum nitidum (Roxb.) DC. (Rutaceae). The root is collected throughout the year, washed clean, sliced or sectioned, then dried under the sun to obtain Zanthoxyli Radix."},{"name":"12","text":"Thick slices or cylindrical and curved sections, 0.4-11 cm long, 0.4-5 cm wide, 1-34 mm thick. Externally pale brownish-yellow or pale yellow, with thick longitudinal wrinkles, sometimes with transverse clefts; lenticels protuberant, bright yellow or yellowish-brown, suborbicular. Cut surface rather smooth; bark pale brown, xylem pale yellow, concentric annulations and dense pores visible. Texture hard. Odour slightly aromatic; taste pungent, tongue-numb and bitter."},{"name":"12","text":"Cork consists of 10-15 layers of cells. Oil cells scattered at phloem, 28-122 $ \\mu $m in diameter. Lignified fibres outside of phloem, single or 2-5 in groups. Stone cells present at phloem of stout root. Phloem scatters with few prisms of calcium oxalate and oil cells. Xylem vessels scattered singly or 2-4 in groups, arranged radially; xylem rays 1-3 rows of cells wide (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"12","text":"Colour yellowish-brown. Simple starch granules broadly ovate or subrounded, 3-25 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter; black and cruciate-shaped under the polarized microscope; compound starch granules composed of 2-3 units. Stone cells pale yellowish-green, subsquare, subrectangular, subrounded, fusiform or strip-shaped, relatively large, 16-225 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ long, 12-131 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ wide, wall varying in thickness, cells with thick wall had distinct striations, cells with thin wall had fine pits. Prisms of calcium oxalate scattered singly or presented in parenchymatous cells, shapes various, short rod-shaped, rhombic, or irregularly polygonal; bright orange under the polarized microscope. Vessels mainly bordered-pitted, 14-182 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ in diameter. Cork cells brown, polygonal or long polygonal in surface view, wall slightly thickened, some lumen containing brown masses. Fibres relatively long, wall relatively thick, lignified; polychromatic under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[67, 7, 1916, 263])\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[239, 517, 1686, 2164])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Zanthoxyli Radix\n\n</div>\n\nA. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n1. Cork 2. Oil cell 3. Stone cells 4. Cortex 5. Fibre\n\n6. Phloem 7. Xylem 8. Xylem rays 9. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\nma 餘甘子\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\n地膺子\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom\n\n香附\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nZanthoxyli Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 714, 742, 1035])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[769, 710, 1222, 1040])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1243, 714, 1691, 1034])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 1066, 743, 1413])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[770, 1065, 1226, 1411])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1245, 1064, 1692, 1405])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[292, 1432, 740, 1770])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[771, 1432, 1231, 1769])\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[1248, 1424, 1693, 1771])\n\n50 μm"},{"name":"12","text":"1. Starch granules 2. Stone cell 3. Prisms of calcium oxalate\n\n4. Bordered-pitted vessel 5. Cork cells 6. Fibres\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope\n\n![](page=5,bbox=[32, 7, 1904, 260])"},{"name":"12","text":"Chelerythrine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of chelerythrine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% ).\n\nNitidine chloride standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of nitidine chloride CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 20 mL of ethanol ( 70% )."},{"name":"12","text":"Prepare a mixture of cyclohexane and ethyl acetate (1:1, v/v)."},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.5 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of ethanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 10 min. Filter the mixture."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate, a twin trough chamber and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately chelerythrine standard solution (0.5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), nitidine standard solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (1 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Before the development, add the developing solvent to one of the troughs of the chamber and add 10 mL of ammonia hydroxide solution (25%, v/v) to the other trough. Cover the chamber with a lid and let equilibrate for about 15 min. Carefully place the HPTLC plate in the trough containing the developing solvent for development. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride.\n\nPhyllanthi Fructus\n\nKochiae Fructus\n\n木蝴蝶\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nCyperi Rhizom香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n繢斷\n\nZanthoxyli Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[392, 365, 1035, 723])\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[402, 779, 1159, 1095])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) chelerythrine chloride and (ii) nitidine chloride\n\n</div>"},{"name":"12","text":"Chelerythrine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (6 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of chelerythrine chloride CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (70%)\n\nNitidine chloride standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (6 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 0.6 mg of nitidine chloride CRS and dissolve in 100 mL of ethanol (70%)"},{"name":"12","text":"Weigh 0.1 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL centrifuge tube, then add 20 mL of ethanol (70%). Sonicate (150 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 3 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Transfer the supernatant to a 50-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction for one more time. Combine the supernatants and make up to the mark with ethanol (70%). Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m PTFE filter."},{"name":"12","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (273 nm) and a column $ (4.6\\times 250\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 1.0 mL/min. The mobile phase is a mixture of acetonitrile and 0.05% trifluoroacetic acid with 0.05% triethylamine (27:73, v/v). The elution time is about 55 min.\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[38, 5, 1908, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of chelerythrine chloride Std-FP and nitidine chloride Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak areas of chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention times of chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride peaks should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiencies determined from chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride peaks should not be less than 8700 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 4 and the closest peak; and the R value between peak 5 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"Separately inject chelerythrine chloride Std-FP, nitidine chloride Std-FP and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the HPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention times of chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride peaks in the chromatograms of chelerythrine chloride Std-FP, nitidine chloride Std-FP and the retention times of the five characteristic peaks (Fig. 5) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride peaks in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatograms of chelerythrine chloride Std-FP and nitidine chloride Std-FP. The retention times of chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride peaks in the chromatograms of the test solution and the corresponding Std-FP should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Zanthoxyli Radix extract are listed in Table 1.\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the five characteristic peaks of Zanthoxyli Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.60</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.81</td><td>±0.03</td></tr><tr><td>4(marker, nitidine chloride)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>5(chelerythrine chloride)</td><td>1.33</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n地膺子\n\nFarfarae Flos款冬花 Visci Herba 槲寄生\n\nCyperi Rhizoma香附\n\nPlantaginis Semen\n\n田基黄 Hyperici Japonici Herba\n\nOroxyli Semen\n\nDipsaci Radix\n\n績斷\n\nZanthoxyli Radix Rhizoma\n\n![](page=8,bbox=[303, 366, 1772, 1029])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Zanthoxyli Radix extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above five characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"12","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 4.0%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 1.0%.\n\n5. 7 Water Content (Appendix X)\n\nOven dried method: not more than 10.0%.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[29, 7, 1903, 263])"},{"name":"12","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 6.0%."},{"name":"12","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV(B)."},{"name":"12","text":"Mixed chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride standard stock solution, Std-Stock (50 mg/L each)\n\nWeigh accurately 0.5 mg of chelerythrine chloride CRS and 0.5 mg of nitidine chloride CRS, and dissolve in 10 mL of ethanol (70%).\n\nMixed chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride standard solution for assay, Std-AS\n\nMeasure accurately the volume of the mixed chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride Std-Stock, dilute with ethanol (70%) to produce a series of solutions of 1,2,4,10,16 mg/L for both chelerythrine chloride and nitidine chloride."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/zanthoxyli-radix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zanthoxyli Radix","la":"Zanthoxyli Radix"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ziziphi-spinosae-semen-v11-e","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ziziphi Spinosae Semen v11 e"},"edition":"HKCMSS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"![](page=0,bbox=[392, 800, 1582, 2008])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen\n\nA. Ziziphi Spinosae Semen\n\nB. Magnified image of seeds (B-1 surface with 1 raised longitudinal line in the centre,\n\nB-2 relatively even surface)\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of seed"},"sections":[{"name":"15","text":"![](page=0,bbox=[392, 800, 1582, 2008])\n\nFigure 1 A photograph of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen\n\nA. Ziziphi Spinosae Semen\n\nB. Magnified image of seeds (B-1 surface with 1 raised longitudinal line in the centre,\n\nB-2 relatively even surface)\n\nC. Magnified image of transverse section of seed"},{"name":"15","text":"Official name: Ziziphi Spinosae Semen\n\nChinese name: 酸棗仁\n\nChinese phonetic name: Suanzaoren"},{"name":"15","text":"Ziziphi Spinosae Semen is the dried ripe seed of Ziziphus jujuba Mill. var. spinosa (Bunge) Hu ex H.F. Chou (Rhamnaceae). The ripe fruit is collected in late autumn and early winter. The seed is freed from pulp and shell, then dried under the sun to obtain Ziziphi Spinosae Semen."},{"name":"15","text":"Oblate or flattened ellipsoidal, 5-9 mm in length, 4-7 mm in width. About 3 mm thick. Externally purplish-red to purplish-brown, smooth, lustrous, some fissured. Some showing rounded protruding in two surfaces, some relatively even in one surface, with 1 raised longitudinal line in the centre, the other surface slightly raised. One end dented, showing a linear hilum, the other end having a raised small chalaza. Testa fragile, after sectioning endosperm white, cotyledons 2, pale yellow, oily. Odour slight; taste bland (Fig. 1)."},{"name":"15","text":"Epidermal cells of testa consist of 1 layer of palisade cells, outer walls thickened, close to surface bearing a light line, the upper and middle parts of walls heavily thickened, the lower part gradually thin. The cells of nutrient layer decadent and some of them brown. The cells of endopleura rectangular or subsquare, anticlinal walls slightly thickened and lignified. Cells of endosperm subpolygonal, containing oil droplets (before stained) and aleurone granules. Epidermal cells of cotyledons containing fine clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate. Mucilage layer 20-30 $ \\mu m $ thick (Fig. 2)."},{"name":"15","text":"Colour brownish-red. Palisade cells of testa brownish-red, polygonal in surface view, varying in size and up to 15 $ \\mu m $ in diameter, with thickened and lignified walls, lumen small; long slat-shaped in lateral view, with thickened outer wall and the walls much thickened in upper and middle parts and gradually thinned towards lower parts; subpolygonal or rounded polygonal in bottom surface view. Cells of endosperm subpolygonal, containing oil droplets and aleurone granules. Cells of endopleura brownish-yellow, rectangular or subsquare with beaded and lignified anticlinal walls in surface view. Fine clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate visible, mainly present in cells of cotyledon; bluish-white under the polarized microscope (Fig. 3).\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n沒藥\n\nMyrrha\n\nPoria\n\n茯苓\n\nZiziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n酸隶仁\n\n銀柴胡\n\nStellariae Radix\n\n![](page=3,bbox=[219, 398, 1748, 2097])\n\nFigure 2 Microscopic features of transverse section of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen A. Sketch B. Section illustration C. Magnified section D. Fine clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate (under the light microscope) E. Fine clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate (under the polarized microscope)\n\n1. Epidermis of testa (1-1 light line, 1-2 palisade cells) 2. Nutritive layer\n\n3. Endopleura 4. Endosperm 5. Cotyledons\n\n6. Fine clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate 7. Mucilage layer\n\n明黨參\n\nChangii Radix\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n廣藿香\n\nPogostemonis Herba\n\n![](page=4,bbox=[273, 657, 1687, 2128])\n\nFigure 3 Microscopic features of powder of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen\n\n1. Palisade cells of testa (1-1 in surface view, 1-2 in lateral view, 1-3 in bottom view)\n\n2. Endosperm cells with aleurone grains ( $ \\rightarrow $ ) and oil droplets ( $ \\rightarrow $ )\n\n3. Cells of endopleura 4. Clusters and prisms of calcium oxalate\n\na. Features under the light microscope b. Features under the polarized microscope"},{"name":"15","text":"Jujuboside A standard solution\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of jujuboside A CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 1 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate to dissolve the standard.\n\nSpinosin standard solution\n\nWeigh 0.5 mg of spinosin CRS (Fig. 4) and dissolve in 2 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate to dissolve the standard."},{"name":"15","text":"Prepare a mixture of n-butanol, water and acetic acid (7:2:1, v/v)."},{"name":"15","text":"Mix 0.5 mL of p-anisaldehyde into 10 mL of acetic acid. Add 85 mL of methanol. Add cautiously 5 mL of sulphuric acid."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 1.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 50-mL conical flask, then add 10 mL of methanol ( 70% ). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Filter through a 0.45- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method by using a HPTLC silica gel $ \\mathrm{F}_{2 5 4} $ plate and a freshly prepared developing solvent system as described above. Apply separately jujuboside A standard solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ), spinosin standard solution (5 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) and the test solution (10 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ ) to the plate. Develop over a path of about 8 cm. After the development, remove the plate from the chamber, mark the solvent front and dry in air. Spray the plate evenly with the spray reagent and heat at about $ 1 0 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (about 5 min). Examine the plate under UV light (366 nm). Calculate the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values by using the equation as indicated in Appendix IV (A).\n\n紫蘇葉\n\nPerillae Folium\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(i)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[381, 433, 1134, 1074])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(ii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[379, 1204, 1144, 1736])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\n(iii)\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=6,bbox=[384, 1860, 1031, 2302])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 4 Chemical structures of (i) jujuboside A (ii) jujuboside B and (iii) spinosin\n\n</div>\n\n![](page=7,bbox=[379, 377, 995, 1211])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 5 A reference HPTLC chromatogram of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen extract observed under UV light (366 nm) after staining\n\n</div>\n\n1. Jujuboside A standard solution 2. Spinosin standard solution\n\n3. Test solution\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give spots or bands with chromatographic characteristics, including the colour and the $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values, corresponding to those of jujuboside A and spinosin (Fig. 5)."},{"name":"15","text":"Spinosin standard solution for fingerprinting, Std-FP (100 mg/L)\n\nWeigh 1.0 mg of spinosin CRS and dissolve in 10 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate to dissolve the standard."},{"name":"15","text":"Weigh 2.0 g of the powdered sample and place it in a 10-mL centrifuge tube, then add 5 mL of methanol (70%). Sonicate (270 W) the mixture for 30 min. Centrifuge at about $ 5 0 0 0 \\times g $ for 5 min. Filter and transfer the filtrate to a 10-mL volumetric flask. Repeat the extraction one more time. Combine the filtrates and make up to the mark with methanol (70%). Filter through a 0.2- $ \\mu $m nylon filter."},{"name":"15","text":"The liquid chromatograph is equipped with a DAD (336 nm) and a column $ (2.0\\times100\\mathrm{mm}) $ packed with ODS bonded silica gel (1.8 $ \\mu\\mathrm{m} $ particle size). The flow rate is about 0.3 mL/min. Programme the chromatographic system as follows (Table 1) -\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 1 Chromatographic system conditions\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Time(min)</td><td>Water(%，v/v)</td><td>Acetonitrile(%，v/v)</td><td>Elution</td></tr><tr><td>0-3</td><td>84→81</td><td>16→19</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>3-5</td><td>81→80</td><td>19→20</td><td>linear gradient</td></tr><tr><td>5-10</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>isocratic</td></tr></table>"},{"name":"15","text":"Perform at least five replicate injections, each using 3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ of spinosin Std-FP. The requirements of the system suitability parameters are as follows: the RSD of the peak area of spinosin should not be more than 5.0%; the RSD of the retention time of spinosin peak should not be more than 2.0%; the column efficiency determined from spinosin peak should not be less than 3000 theoretical plates.\n\nThe R value between peak 1 and the closest peak in the chromatogram of the test solution should not be less than 1.5 (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"Separately inject spinosin Std-FP and the test solution (3 $ \\mu\\mathrm{L} $ each) into the UHPLC system and record the chromatograms. Measure the retention time of spinosin peak in the chromatogram of spinosin Std-FP and the retention times of the four characteristic peaks (Fig. 6) in the chromatogram of the test solution. Identify spinosin peak in the chromatogram of the test solution by comparing its retention time with that in the chromatogram of spinosin Std-FP. The retention times of spinosin peaks from the two chromatograms should not differ by more than 2.0%. Calculate the RRTs of the characteristic peaks by using the equation as indicated in Appendix XII.\n\nThe RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen extract are listed in Table 2.\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[21, 9, 1951, 259])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nTable 2 The RRTs and acceptable ranges of the four characteristic peaks of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\n<table border=\"1\"><tr><td>Peak No.</td><td>RRT</td><td>Acceptable Range</td></tr><tr><td>1 (marker, spinosin)</td><td>1.00</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2.04</td><td>±0.05</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.51</td><td>±0.04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2.65</td><td>±0.03</td></tr></table>\n\n![](page=9,bbox=[328, 858, 1826, 1385])\n\n<div align=\"center\">\n\nFigure 6 A reference fingerprint chromatogram of Ziziphi Spinosae Semen extract\n\n</div>\n\nFor positive identification, the sample must give the above four characteristic peaks with RRTs falling within the acceptable range of the corresponding peaks in the reference fingerprint chromatogram (Fig. 6)."},{"name":"15","text":"5. 1 Heavy Metals (Appendix V): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 2 Pesticide Residues (Appendix VI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 3 Mycotoxins - Aflatoxins (Appendix VII): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 4 Sulphur Dioxide Residues (Appendix XVI): meet the requirements.\n\n5. 5 Foreign Matter (Appendix VIII): not more than 2.0%.\n\n5. 6 Ash (Appendix IX)\n\nTotal ash: not more than 3.1%.\n\nAcid-insoluble ash: not more than 0.50%."},{"name":"15","text":"Oven dried method: not more than 8.3%."},{"name":"15","text":"Water-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 14%.\n\nEthanol-soluble extractives (cold extraction method): not less than 25%."},{"name":"15","text":"Carry out the method as directed in Appendix IV (B)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/hk/monographs/ziziphi-spinosae-semen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ziziphi Spinosae Semen","la":"Ziziphi Spinosae Semen"},"edition":"HKCMMS-1","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"pending"},"sections":[{"name":"opening","text":"pending"}]}]}